var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/","page":"Examples","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Forced-HIT-—-building-a-pseudospectral-DNS-on-GBGPeriodicFields","page":"Examples","text":"This is a small, demonstration pseudospectral DNS of forced homogeneous-isotropic turbulence, written the way a real consumer would use GBGPeriodicFields. It plots the Eulerian and Lagrangian second-order velocity structure functions — each compensated by its Kolmogorov (K41) inertial scaling, side by side — alongside the energy spectrum, the stationarity history, and the velocity autocorrelations. The goal is a readable, performance-first end-to-end workflow: the time loop is fully in-place over a preallocated workspace (no per-step allocation) — both how a real DNS is written and what lets the GPU twin, a device copy of this script, run with no manual garbage collection.\n\nThis is a demonstration, not the production solver. The full pseudospectral DNS built on this package is GBGHomogeneousTurbulence.jl — that is where the complete physics, the tested solver machinery, and the real runs live. This page exists to show how the package, the field layer a solver builds on, is driven end to end, small enough to read in one sitting.\n\nMethod. The demo uses the rotational form. We write hatcdot for the spectral (rfft) representation, u for the velocity, omega = nablatimes u for the vorticity, mathbfk for the wavenumber vector and kappa = lvertmathbfkrvert for its magnitude, nu for the kinematic viscosity, and mathbbP for the Helmholtz (Leray) projection onto divergence-free fields. The wavenumber vector is set bold here, where the package pages set it plain, because this page follows the turbulence convention in which italic k denotes the turbulent kinetic energy; the two never collide as a result. With that notation the demo advances\n\nfracpartial hatupartial t\n  = mathbbP widehatutimesomega - nu kappa^2 hatu + hatf\n\nwhere widehatutimesomega is the transform of minus the Lamb vector omega times u (the rotational form of the nonlinearity) and hatf is a deterministic large-scale forcing. The viscous term is integrated exactly through the integrating factor exp(-nu kappa^2 Delta t) and the nonlinear term explicitly (RK2); the 23 rule de-aliases the product, and the forcing holds the lowest shells' band energy fixed (statistical stationarity, no tuning). The time loop is the consumer's — the package has no concept of time, by design.\n\nThis page is generated from the runnable script HIST_CPU.jl with Literate.jl (it is not executed during the docs build); the figures are from the GPU twin gpu/rocm/HIST_ROCm.jl (320^3 Float32, mathrmRe_lambda approx 130 on a 24 GB RX 7900 XTX). The run is split into simulate, dump and plot stages — every plotted curve is written to an HDF5 file, so the figures can be re-styled without re-running the DNS:\n\njulia --project=examples examples/HIST_CPU.jl            # full run (default 32³, ~1 min)\njulia --project=examples examples/HIST_CPU.jl --smoke    # tiny end-to-end check\njulia --project=examples examples/HIST_CPU.jl --fast     # 64³, nicer statistics\njulia --project=examples examples/HIST_CPU.jl --huge     # 192³, Re_λ≈93 (heavy)\njulia --project=examples examples/HIST_CPU.jl --mega     # 256³, Re_λ≈109 (very heavy)\njulia --project=examples examples/plot_hist.jl examples/output/HIST_CPU.h5  # re-plot only\n\nThe analysis, the HDF5 dump, and the plotting live in the shared hist_postprocess.jl; this script stays focused on the package. Let's build it.\n\nusing GBGPeriodicFields\nusing GBGPeriodicFields: field_data, field_grid          # raw-array / grid accessors\nusing Random, Statistics, Printf, LinearAlgebra\nimport FFTW  # for the `MEASURE` planner flag (see the plan setup)\n\nconst OUTDIR = joinpath(@__DIR__, \"output\")\ninclude(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"hist_postprocess.jl\"))  # statistics, HDF5 I/O, plots (shared)","title":"Forced HIT — building a pseudospectral DNS on GBGPeriodicFields"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Parameters","page":"Examples","text":"These are the options of the run. The smoke() / fast() / huge() / mega() configurations select 32^3 / 64^3 / 192^3 / 256^3.\n\nBase.@kwdef struct Params\n  N::Int          = 32          # grid points per side\n  L::Float64      = 2π          # box size (so wavenumbers are integers)\n  ν::Float64      = 5.0e-2      # kinematic viscosity (tuned for k_max·η ≳ 1 at 32^3)\n  k_force::Int    = 2           # force shells 1 ≤ |k| ≤ k_force\n  E_force::Float64 = 0.6        # energy held in the forced band (sets the velocity scale)\n  cfl::Float64    = 0.5         # advective CFL number\n  n_spinup::Int   = 1500        # steps to reach stationarity (statistics discarded)\n  n_stats::Int    = 2000        # steps over which statistics are collected\n  n_particles::Int = 2000       # Lagrangian tracers\n  rec_stride::Int = 3           # record particle velocity every this many stats steps\n  snap_stride::Int = 100        # take an Eulerian SF snapshot every this many stats steps\n  seed::Int       = 42\nend\n\nThe tiny configuration --smoke gives a quick end-to-end check.\n\nsmoke() = Params(n_spinup = 15, n_stats = 40, n_particles = 300,\n                 rec_stride = 1, snap_stride = 10)\n\nThe larger, still quick --fast gives nicer statistics: 64^3 and more tracers.\n\nfast() = Params(N = 64, n_spinup = 400, n_stats = 800, n_particles = 4000,\n                rec_stride = 2, snap_stride = 40)\n\nThe high-Reynolds configuration --huge runs 192^3 at a lower viscosity, so the Reynolds number (mathrmRe_lambda approx 93, built on the Taylor microscale lambda) and the scale separation lambda ll L, tau_eta ll tau_u (the box size L; the Kolmogorov time tau_eta against the integral time tau_u) are large enough to read off the plots; the grid resolves it (kappa_maxeta approx 113 — the highest resolved wavenumber times the Kolmogorov length eta). At fixed forcing mathrmRe_lambda is set by nu, not the grid, so 192^3 already gives the separation; it is also a comfortable fit on a 12 GB GPU. The CFL is conservative (0.3) because the statistics-window Delta t is frozen (for a uniform Lagrangian axis): over a long averaging window a CFL-tight, marginally-resolved run can hit a velocity peak the frozen Delta t cannot handle and blow up. The n_spinup steps clear the initial energy overshoot; n_stats time-averages over a few of the forcing's energy-oscillation periods (the band-rescaling forcing makes the turbulent kinetic energy k(t) oscillate about its mean rather than settle flat). Heavy: minutes on a GPU, much longer on the CPU.\n\nhuge() = Params(N = 192, ν = 3.2e-3, cfl = 0.3, n_spinup = 3300, n_stats = 4700,\n                n_particles = 8000, rec_stride = 4, snap_stride = 60)\n\nThe largest CPU-practical configuration, --mega, runs 256^3 at nu = 25times10^-3, mathrmRe_lambda approx 109 with kappa_maxeta approx 13 (comfortably resolved — a lower nu would raise mathrmRe_lambda but drop kappa_maxeta below 11, where the marginally-resolved flow blows up over a long run). Same recipe as --huge, one grid level up. On the CPU it is reproducible but very heavy — hours of wall-clock and gtrsim 12 GB of RAM (Float64 doubles the GPU twin's footprint). The documented figures go one level further still — the GPU twin's --giant at 320^3 (mathrmRe_lambda approx 130), which a 24 GB card's headroom unlocks; 256^3 was the ceiling of the 12 GB card this scheme was first characterized on.\n\nmega() = Params(N = 256, ν = 2.5e-3, cfl = 0.3, n_spinup = 4500, n_stats = 4500,\n                n_particles = 8000, rec_stride = 4, snap_stride = 100)","title":"Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Spectral-utilities-and-the-preallocated-workspace","page":"Examples","text":"The diagonal-in-Fourier operators — de-aliasing, the integrating factor exp(-nu kappa^2Delta t), the initial-condition envelope — are applied with the package's convolve (periodic convolution being a coefficientwise multiply by a transfer function hatg). The de-alias filter is the package's spectral_cutoff; the physics kernels (carrying nu, Delta t, the envelope width) are radial symbols, functions of lvertmathbfkrvert^2 alone: the viscous integrating factor goes through exponential_convolve!, which evaluates exp(-nulvertmathbfkrvert^2Delta t) per mode from the wavenumber arrays, and the initial envelope through radial_kernel, which stores a closure. Neither builds a field of coefficients.\n\nThe make_workspace helper preallocates every scratch field the time loop needs, so a step allocates nothing — this is the heart of the performance-first design (and what keeps the GPU's memory pool from growing).\n\nThe workspace preallocates scratch fields for the in-place hot loop, so a step allocates nothing (this is how a real DNS is written — performance first). It holds the RK stages and the velocity/vorticity work fields; the loop and step! write into these instead of allocating.\n\nfunction make_workspace(physical_reference, spectral_reference)\n  # `similar_field` copies a reference field's representation, grid and storage backend and\n  # takes the tensor order to carry, so the scratch matches the fields it works on and the\n  # GPU twin needs no change. Its storage is uninitialized, hence the zeroing.\n  scratch(reference, order) = (field = similar_field(reference, order); field .= 0; field)\n  spectral_vector() = scratch(spectral_reference, Val(1))\n  physical_vector() = scratch(physical_reference, Val(1))\n  return (ωh = spectral_vector(), k1 = spectral_vector(), k2 = spectral_vector(),\n          ustar = spectral_vector(), uhc = spectral_vector(), tmp = spectral_vector(),\n          Nt = spectral_vector(),  # (u×ω)^ scratch (distinct helmholtz source)\n          u = physical_vector(), ω0 = physical_vector(), ω = physical_vector(),\n          uω = physical_vector(), uxω = physical_vector())\nend","title":"Spectral utilities and the preallocated workspace"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Diagnostics","page":"Examples","text":"Kinetic energy, dissipation and the energy spectrum are all package reductions: inner(f,g) equiv langle fcdot grangle and radial_spectrum of the spectral velocity uh with the multipliers m (the wavenumber arrays the derivatives use) the inner resolved over wavenumber shells, so the shell sum recovers it — sum_kappa E(kappa) = tfrac12langlelvert urvert^2rangle = k, the turbulent kinetic energy. No field_data loops. The CFL speed uses maximum(abs, field_data(u)) — an allocation-free reduction; lpnorm(u, Inf) would be the true suplvert urvert but allocates a whole magnitude field, so we avoid it in the hot loop.\n\nkinetic_energy(u) = 0.5 * inner(u, u)  # ½⟨|u|²⟩   (Reductions.inner)\ndissipation(ω, p) = p.ν * inner(ω, ω)  # ν⟨|ω|²⟩   (homogeneous)\n\nThe CFL speed is max_i lvert u_irvert, the largest velocity component in absolute value — an allocation-free reduction over the raw array. (lpnorm(u, Inf) is the true suplvert urvert but allocates a whole magnitude field every call — avoided so the hot loop allocates nothing; the max component under-estimates the magnitude by at most sqrt3, so the effective CFL number is at most that much larger than the nominal one — absorbed by the conservative cfl the long runs use.)\n\nmax_speed(u)      = maximum(abs, field_data(u))\n\nThe spectrum is half the shell-resolved Parseval inner, E(kappa) = tfrac12 P(kappa), so that its shells sum to the kinetic energy: sum_kappa E(kappa) = tfrac12langlelvert urvert^2rangle = k.\n\nfunction energy_spectrum(uh, m)\n  shells, spectrum = radial_spectrum(uh, m)  # (|k| bins, Σ_{shell} |û|²)  (package)\n  return shells, 0.5 .* spectrum  # E(κ): physics ½ stays consumer-side\nend","title":"Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Forcing","page":"Examples","text":"Hold the kinetic energy in the shells from lvertmathbfkrvert = 1 up to k_force at E_force, built entirely from package ops: band is a spectral_band mask kernel; the band energy is tfrac12langlelvert u_mathrmbandrvert^2rangle, computed as 0.5 * inner(ws.tmp, ws.tmp) on the workspace's scratch field ws.tmp (Parseval; the same-argument inner(f, f) is allocation-free); rescaling the in-band modes is one more convolve (scaling preserves nablacdot u = 0). Applying convolve! into the scratch ws.tmp keeps it allocation-free, no scalar indexing.\n\nfunction force!(uh, band, ws, p)\n  convolve!(ws.tmp, uh, band)  # band-pass copy into scratch (no allocation)\n  # ½⟨|u_band|²⟩ = ½ inner(u_band,u_band): f===f ⇒ allocation-free\n  band_kinetic = 0.5 * inner(ws.tmp, ws.tmp)\n  band_kinetic < 1e-12 && return uh                     # guard (the initial condition seeds the band)\n  scale = sqrt(p.E_force / band_kinetic)\n  # rescale in-band modes: one convolve (α/β, alloc-free)\n  convolve!(uh, uh, band, scale - 1, 1)\n  return uh\nend","title":"Forcing"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Right-hand-side-and-time-integration-—-in-place","page":"Examples","text":"The heart of the performance-first design: no per-step allocation. The work fields all live in the preallocated ws; the package's in-place operators write into them (curl!, to_physical!, cross!, to_spectral!, helmholtz_projection!, convolve!), and the linear combinations are in-place broadcasts on the raw arrays. Nothing here allocates, so there is no garbage-collection pressure — which is exactly what lets the GPU twin run unattended (an allocating style would grow the device memory pool every step until it wedges).\n\nThis helper writes the physical velocity and vorticity of a field in spectral representation into the given buffers. The uh buffer is preserved by transforming a COPY of it — to_physical! may overwrite its spectral source as scratch.\n\nfunction to_velocity_vorticity!(u_out, ω_out, uh, ws, m, plans)\n  curl!(ws.ωh, uh, m)                          # ω̂ = ∇×û  (û preserved)\n  to_physical!(ω_out, ws.ωh, plans)            # physical vorticity (consumes ω̂)\n  copyto!(field_data(ws.uhc), field_data(uh))  # keep û: transform a scratch copy\n  to_physical!(u_out, ws.uhc, plans)           # physical velocity (consumes the copy)\n  return u_out, ω_out\nend\n\nThe buffer Nh holds hatN, the de-aliased Leray projection of widehatutimesomega formed from physical u and omega.\n\nfunction rhs!(Nh, u, ω, ws, m, plans, dealias)\n  cross!(ws.uxω, u, ω)                # u × ω  (physical)\n  to_spectral!(ws.Nt, ws.uxω, plans)  # (u×ω)^ into scratch\n  # Leray projection (alias-safe and allocation-free either way)\n  helmholtz_projection!(Nh, ws.Nt, m)\n  convolve!(Nh, Nh, dealias)          # 2/3 de-alias (coefficientwise multiply)\n  return Nh\nend\n\nThis function takes one integrating-factor RK2 (Heun) step, fully in place. The integrating factor mathcalE = exp(-nu kappa^2Delta t) (nu, Delta t are physics the package never sees) is applied with exponential_convolve!, which evaluates it per mode from the wavenumber arrays, so no kernel field is built, stored, or read. It assumes ws.u, ws.ω0 already hold the START-of-step physical velocity & vorticity (the loop fills them and uses them for the diagnostics), so the first stage reuses them. With hatN the de-aliased projected nonlinearity (the buffers ws.k1, ws.k2 hold hatN_1, hatN_2):\n\nbeginaligned\nhatN_1 = hatN(hatu_n) \nhatu^* = mathcalE (hatu_n + Delta t hatN_1) \nhatN_2 = hatN(hatu^*) \nhatu_n+1 = mathcalE hatu_n + tfracDelta t2 (mathcalE hatN_1 + hatN_2)\nendaligned\n\nthen force.\n\nfunction step!(uh, ws, m, plans, dealias, band, p, dt)\n  # k₁ = N̂(ûₙ)  (cached start-of-step u, ω)\n  rhs!(ws.k1, ws.u, ws.ω0, ws, m, plans, dealias)\n  field_data(ws.ustar) .= field_data(uh) .+ dt .* field_data(ws.k1)\n  # the display-math form of this stage is in the block above; this factor is evaluated per\n  # mode from the wavenumber arrays, so no kernel field is built, stored, or read.\n  exponential_convolve!(ws.ustar, ws.ustar, m, -p.ν * dt)\n  # û*'s velocity & vorticity (scratch)\n  to_velocity_vorticity!(ws.uω, ws.ω, ws.ustar, ws, m, plans)\n  rhs!(ws.k2, ws.uω, ws.ω, ws, m, plans, dealias)    # k₂ = N̂(û*)\n  exponential_convolve!(ws.k1, ws.k1, m, -p.ν * dt)  # ℰ·k₁, the same lazy factor\n  exponential_convolve!(uh, uh, m, -p.ν * dt)        # ℰ·ûₙ\n  field_data(uh) .+= (dt / 2) .* (field_data(ws.k1) .+ field_data(ws.k2))   # ûₙ₊₁\n  force!(uh, band, ws, p)\n  return uh\nend","title":"Right-hand side and time integration — in place"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Particles","page":"Examples","text":"RK2 in space on the frozen start-of-step velocity field (the O(Delta t^2) field-staleness is fine for a demo), using the package's interpolate, the grid-to-point gather. Positions wrap into 0 L); the per-tracer velocity is recorded for the Lagrangian statistics.\n\nfunction advect!(X, u, dt, p)\n  v1 = point_values(interpolate(u, PointSet(X)))          # CatmullRom gather (package)\n  Xmid = mod.(X .+ dt .* v1, p.L)\n  v2 = point_values(interpolate(u, PointSet(Xmid)))\n  X .= mod.(X .+ (dt / 2) .* (v1 .+ v2), p.L)\n  return v1\nend","title":"Particles"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Initial-condition","page":"Examples","text":"The initial condition is a random field, concentrated at large scales by an envelope, projected solenoidal, then band-energy normalized. (The package deliberately ships no random-field generator; the example builds its own.)\n\nfunction random_solenoidal(grid, m, plans, ws, band, p; kp = 2.0)\n  rng = MersenneTwister(p.seed)\n  uh = to_spectral(VectorFieldPhysical(randn(rng, p.N, p.N, p.N, 3), grid), plans)\n  # concentrate at large scales (a closure)\n  envelope = radial_kernel(k2 -> exp(-k2 / kp^2), m)\n  convolve!(uh, uh, envelope)       # apply it (alias-safe, coefficientwise)\n  helmholtz_projection!(uh, uh, m)  # make solenoidal (alias-safe, one pass)\n  force!(uh, band, ws, p)           # set the forced-band energy\n  return uh\nend","title":"Initial condition"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Statistics","page":"Examples","text":"The Lagrangian per-component structure function (lagrangian_stats), the Lagrangian velocity autocorrelation, and the integral-scale reduction (integral_scale) live in the shared hist_postprocess.jl. The Eulerian integral length comes from the package's radial_correlation (in the driver below); the Lagrangian integral time from lagrangian_stats.","title":"Statistics"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#The-driver","page":"Examples","text":"Setup, spin-up to stationarity, then the statistics window — advancing the field in place, advecting the tracers, and accumulating the diagnostics — then dump everything to HDF5 and plot.\n\nfunction run_hist(p::Params)\n  isdir(OUTDIR) || mkpath(OUTDIR)\n  @printf(\"HIST_CPU: N=%d  ν=%.3g  forcing |k|≤%d @ E=%.2g  (%d spinup + %d stats steps)\\n\",\n          p.N, p.ν, p.k_force, p.E_force, p.n_spinup, p.n_stats)\n  @printf(\"  threads: Julia=%d  (package CPU threading %s — launch with `-tN` to use more cores)\\n\",\n          Threads.nthreads(), Threads.nthreads() > 1 ? \"ON\" : \"off\")\n\nThe setup builds the grid, transform plans, spectral multipliers, the de-alias filter and forcing band, and the preallocated workspace.\n\n  grid   = Grid3D((p.N, p.N, p.N), (p.L, p.L, p.L))\n  layout = RealFourierLayout()\n  uphys0 = VectorFieldPhysical(zeros(p.N, p.N, p.N, 3), grid)\n  spectral_shape = field_spectral_shape(grid, layout, Val(1))\n  uspec0 = VectorFieldSpectral(zeros(ComplexF64, spectral_shape), grid)\n\nPlan the transforms with FFTW's MEASURE planner instead of the default ESTIMATE: over a long run the one-time planning cost amortizes over thousands of transforms. (The sharper case for MEASURE is single precision, where ESTIMATE can mis-plan badly on some CPUs — up to sim5times slower at 256^3 — but this run is Float64 throughout, so amortization is the reason here.) MEASURE overwrites its input arrays while planning, so we plan on the zero-filled uphys0 / uspec0 scaffolds before seeding the initial condition below. One plan pair serves scalars, vectors and tensors alike: a transform is a property of the box, and a field whose value has R indices is that transform per component, so the scalar divergence diagnostic reuses these vector plans rather than planning a second pair of its own.\n\n  # physical ↔ spectral, any rank\n  vplans = FieldTransformPlans(uphys0, uspec0; fft_flags = FFTW.MEASURE)\n  m    = FieldSpectralMultipliers(uspec0)\n\nThe two-thirds-rule de-alias filter and the forcing band are both package shell-mask kernels.\n\n  dealias = spectral_cutoff(m, (2 / 3) * (p.N / 2) * (2π / p.L))\n  band    = spectral_band(m, 0.5 * (2π / p.L), (p.k_force + 0.5) * (2π / p.L))\n  ws   = make_workspace(uphys0, uspec0)  # preallocated scratch (a step allocates nothing)\n  Δx   = p.L / p.N\n\nBuild the initial condition, then de-alias it.\n\n  uh = random_solenoidal(grid, m, vplans, ws, band, p)\n  convolve!(uh, uh, dealias)\n\nSpin up to stationarity (adaptive Delta t; these statistics are discarded). Each step fills the cached start-of-step velocity/vorticity (to_velocity_vorticity!), records k(t) and varepsilon(t), then advances uh.\n\n  t_hist = Float64[]; E_hist = Float64[]; ε_hist = Float64[]\n  t = 0.0\n  sim_start = time()  # wall-clock of the simulation (the time loops)\n  for n in 1:p.n_spinup\n    # start-of-step velocity & vorticity\n    to_velocity_vorticity!(ws.u, ws.ω0, uh, ws, m, vplans)\n    dt = p.cfl * Δx / max(max_speed(ws.u), 1e-6)\n    push!(t_hist, t); push!(E_hist, kinetic_energy(ws.u))\n    push!(ε_hist, dissipation(ws.ω0, p))\n    step!(uh, ws, m, vplans, dealias, band, p, dt)\n    t += dt\n  end\n\nFreeze Delta t for the statistics window so the axis of Lagrangian time lags tau is uniform.\n\n  to_velocity_vorticity!(ws.u, ws.ω0, uh, ws, m, vplans)\n  dt = p.cfl * Δx / max(max_speed(ws.u), 1e-6)\n\nIn the statistics window (Delta t frozen so the time-lag axis tau is uniform), advance the field, advect the tracers on the start-of-step velocity, record their velocities, and accumulate the autocorrelation.\n\n  X = p.L .* rand(MersenneTwister(p.seed + 1), p.n_particles, 3)   # seed tracers\n  n_rec = p.n_stats ÷ p.rec_stride\n  V = zeros(p.n_particles, 3, n_rec)                              # recorded velocities\n  # time-averaged velocity autocorrelation C(r)\n  Cacc = Float64[]; sep = Float64[]; n_snap = 0\n  rec = 0\n  for n in 1:p.n_stats\n    # start-of-step velocity & vorticity\n    to_velocity_vorticity!(ws.u, ws.ω0, uh, ws, m, vplans)\n    push!(t_hist, t); push!(E_hist, kinetic_energy(ws.u))\n    push!(ε_hist, dissipation(ws.ω0, p))\n    # advances ûₙ (reuses ws.u, ws.ω0 for k₁)\n    step!(uh, ws, m, vplans, dealias, band, p, dt)\n    v = advect!(X, ws.u, dt, p)  # advect on the start-of-step velocity\n    if n % p.rec_stride == 0 && rec < n_rec\n      rec += 1\n      V[:, :, rec] .= v\n    end\n    if n % p.snap_stride == 0\n      # Eulerian statistics from the package's shell-averaged FFT autocorrelation C(r): the\n      # per-component S⁽ᴱ⁾(r) = (2/3)(C(0)−C(r)) and the integral length ℒ = ∫ρ follow from\n      # it, with no hand-rolled shift. C is accumulated over snapshots.\n      s, Csnap = radial_correlation(uh, vplans)\n      isempty(Cacc) ? (sep = s; Cacc = copy(Csnap)) : (Cacc .+= Csnap)\n      n_snap += 1\n    end\n    t += dt\n  end\n  if n_snap == 0  # ensure ≥1 sample (if snap_stride > n_stats)\n    sep, Cacc = radial_correlation(uh, vplans); n_snap = 1\n  end\n  C = Cacc ./ n_snap                # time-averaged autocorrelation\n  sim_seconds = time() - sim_start  # full simulation wall-clock (spin-up + statistics)\n\nThe final diagnostics are the energetics, the Kolmogorov/Taylor scales, the divergence check, the spectrum.\n\n  u  = to_physical(uh, vplans)\n  K  = kinetic_energy(u)\n  ε  = dissipation(to_physical(curl(uh, m), vplans), p)\n  uprime = sqrt(2K / 3)\n  Reλ = sqrt(15 * uprime^4 / (p.ν * ε))\n  η   = (p.ν^3 / ε)^(1 / 4)                 # Kolmogorov length\n  τ_η = sqrt(p.ν / ε)                        # Kolmogorov time\n  λ   = uprime * sqrt(15 * p.ν / ε)          # Taylor microscale (length), λ = u'√(15ν/ε)\n  kmaxη = (2 / 3) * (p.N / 2) * (2π / p.L) * η  # the 2π/L makes the wavenumber physical\n\nThe divergence check, at round-off, is lVertnablacdot urVert  (kappa_mathrmrms lVert urVert) with the energy-weighted rms wavenumber, via l2norm.\n\n  divp = to_physical(divergence(uh, m), vplans)\n  κ_rms = sqrt(weighted_inner(uh, radial_kernel(identity, m)) / max(inner(uh, uh), 1e-300))\n  divrel = l2norm(divp) / (κ_rms * l2norm(u) + 1e-30)\n  ek_k, ek_E = energy_spectrum(uh, m)\n\nThe length and time scales come from the velocity autocorrelations. On the Eulerian side the time-averaged C(r) = langle u(x)cdot u(x+r)rangle (with C(0) = langlelvert urvert^2rangle) gives the trace-correlation integral length, over separations of the normalized correlation, mathcalL = int_0^infty rho(r)mathrmdr — written mathcalL because L is already the box size on this page — which is tfrac23L_11 of the longitudinal scale the literature usually plots, since C dots the velocity at the two points and the per-component structure function S^(E)(r) = tfrac23 (C(0) - C(r)). On the Lagrangian side the particle-velocity autocorrelation R(tau) (from the recorded V) gives the integral time over time lags of its normalized form, tau_u = int_0^infty rho_L(tau)mathrmdtau.\n\n  rho_E = C ./ C[1]\n  L_int = integral_scale(sep, C)\n  # Eulerian per-component structure function (homogeneous identity)\n  S2_E  = (2 / 3) .* (C[1] .- C)\n  τ_l, R_l, S2l = lagrangian_stats(V, p.rec_stride * dt)\n  rho_L = R_l ./ R_l[1]\n  τ_u   = integral_scale(τ_l, R_l)\n\n  @printf(\"  K=%.4g  ε=%.4g  u'=%.4g  Re_λ=%.1f  η=%.3g  k_max·η=%.2f  ‖∇·u‖_rel=%.1e\\n\",\n          K, ε, uprime, Reλ, η, kmaxη, divrel)\n  @printf(\"  simulation: %.1f s wall-clock for %d steps at %d³  (%.1f ms/step)\\n\",\n          sim_seconds, p.n_spinup + p.n_stats, p.N,\n          1e3 * sim_seconds / (p.n_spinup + p.n_stats))\n  @printf(\"  scales: λ/η=%.2f  L/η=%.2f  |  τ_u/τ_η=%.2f  √(τ_η·τ_u)/τ_η=%.2f\\n\",\n          λ / η, L_int / η, τ_u / τ_η, sqrt(τ_η * τ_u) / τ_η)\n\nBy Parseval, the package's spectral inner (rfft half-spectrum weighting) reproduces the physical tfrac12langlelvert urvert^2rangle exactly — same energy, computed in spectral representation.\n\n  @printf(\"  ½⟨|u|²⟩: physical=%.6g  spectral inner(û,û)=%.6g  |  Σ E(k)=%.4g  (Parseval)\\n\",\n          K, 0.5 * inner(uh, uh), sum(ek_E))\n  @assert isfinite(K) && isfinite(ε) && divrel < 1e-6 \"non-finite or non-solenoidal state\"\n\nDump every plotted curve to HDF5, then plot (re-plot later via plot_hist.jl, no re-run).\n\n  bundle = Dict{String, Any}(\n    \"backend\" => \"CPU\", \"eltype\" => \"Float64\",\n    \"N\" => p.N, \"L_box\" => p.L, \"nu\" => p.ν, \"dt\" => dt, \"n_spinup\" => p.n_spinup,\n    \"K\" => K, \"epsilon\" => ε, \"uprime\" => uprime, \"Re_lambda\" => Reλ,\n    \"eta\" => η, \"tau_eta\" => τ_η, \"lambda\" => λ, \"L_int\" => L_int, \"tau_u\" => τ_u,\n    \"kmax_eta\" => kmaxη, \"div_rel\" => divrel, \"C2\" => 2.1, \"C0\" => 6.0, \"CK\" => 1.6,\n    \"sim_seconds\" => sim_seconds,\n    \"t\" => t_hist, \"E\" => E_hist, \"eps_t\" => ε_hist,\n    \"ek_k\" => collect(ek_k), \"ek_E\" => collect(ek_E),\n    \"r\" => sep[2:end], \"S2_E\" => S2_E[2:end],  # structure-function panel (excludes r = 0)\n    \"tau\" => τ_l, \"S2_L\" => S2l, \"rho_L\" => rho_L,\n    \"sep\" => collect(sep), \"rho_E\" => rho_E,  # autocorrelation panel (includes r = 0)\n  )\n  save_hist(joinpath(OUTDIR, \"HIST_CPU.h5\"), bundle)\n  make_hist_plots(bundle; outdir = OUTDIR, prefix = \"HIST_CPU\")\n  return nothing\nend","title":"The driver"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Entry-point","page":"Examples","text":"Select a configuration from the command line, and run.\n\nfunction main()\n  p = (\"--smoke\" in ARGS) ? smoke() :\n      (\"--fast\"  in ARGS) ? fast()  :\n      (\"--huge\"  in ARGS) ? huge()  :\n      (\"--mega\"  in ARGS) ? mega()  : Params()\n  run_hist(p)\nend\n\nmain()","title":"Entry point"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#Results","page":"Examples","text":"The figures below are from the GPU twin (320^3 Float32, mathrmRe_lambda approx 130 on a 24 GB RX 7900 XTX, time-averaged over several forcing-oscillation periods). The headline is the pair of compensated structure functions: the per-component second-order velocity structure functions (one third of the trace langlelvertdelta urvert^2rangle), Eulerian and Lagrangian, each normalized by its Kolmogorov (K41) inertial scaling — a true inertial range shows as a plateau at 1. With langlecdotrangle the average (over space/time origins), u the Eulerian velocity and U a tracer's velocity,\n\nbeginaligned\nS^(E)(Delta x) = tfrac13bigllangle lvert u(x+Delta x) - u(x)rvert^2bigrrangle\n                   = tfrac23bigl(C(0) - C(r)bigr) \nS^(L)(Delta t) = tfrac13bigllangle lvert U(t+Delta t) - U(t)rvert^2bigrrangle\n                   = tfrac23bigl(R(0) - R(Delta t)bigr)\nendaligned\n\nwhere r = lvertDelta xrvert, C(r) = langle u(x)cdot u(x+r)rangle is the spatial velocity autocorrelation — read straight off the package's radial_correlation (the shell-averaged C(r)) — and R(tau) = langle U(t)cdot U(t+tau)rangle is the Lagrangian one (over tracers and time origins). The plotted curves are the K41-normalized ratios\n\nbeginaligned\nfracS^(E)(Delta x)tfrac119 C_2 varepsilon^23 Delta x^23\n  quadtextversusquad fracDelta xeta \nfracS^(L)(Delta t)C_0 varepsilon Delta t\n  quadtextversusquad fracDelta ttau_eta\nendaligned\n\nwith varepsilon the mean dissipation rate, eta the Kolmogorov length, tau_eta = sqrtnuvarepsilon the Kolmogorov time, and C_2 approx 21, C_0 approx 6 the (adjustable, plot-time) Kolmogorov constants. The Taylor microscale lambda and the integral scales are marked, each the area under a normalized autocorrelation: the trace-correlation integral length mathcalL = int_0^infty rho(r)mathrmdr (= tfrac23L_11) and time tau_u = int_0^infty rho_L(tau)mathrmdtau, with rho = CC(0) and rho_L = RR(0) (taken to the first zero crossing).\n\n(Image: Eulerian and Lagrangian structure functions, normalized by their Kolmogorov scalings)\n\nThe velocity autocorrelations, from which the integral scales are read off:\n\n(Image: Spatial and Lagrangian velocity autocorrelations)\n\nThe energy spectrum E(kappa), compared against the K41 inertial-range form E(kappa) = C_K varepsilon^23 kappa^-53 with C_K approx 16 the Kolmogorov constant. The prefactor C_K varepsilon^23 is not cosmetic: E(kappa) has the dimensions of energy per unit wavenumber and kappa^-53 does not, so the guide must carry C_K varepsilon^23 (with the measured varepsilon) to be the full K41 prediction rather than a slope. Alongside it, the turbulent kinetic energy k(t) and the dissipation varepsilon(t): the band-rescaling forcing makes the energy oscillate slowly about its mean rather than settle flat, so the statistics are time-averaged over several oscillation periods (the autocorrelation accumulates over all snapshots) to keep the means representative.\n\n(Image: Energy spectrum)\n\n(Image: Stationarity history)","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/hist_cpu/#On-the-GPU","page":"Examples","text":"The device twin gpu/rocm/HIST_ROCm.jl runs this identical solver on ROCArrays (Float32, allowscalar(false)). Because the time loop is in-place and the package's differential operators are allocation-free, it needs no manual garbage collection — the device memory pool stays flat and it sustains approx 96 ms/step at 320^3 on a 24 GB RX 7900 XTX (peak approx 13 GB; the card reaches 384^3 at its approx 21 GB ceiling). That hassle-free GPU behaviour is the whole point of writing the solver preallocated and in-place.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"On the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FiberSections/#FiberSections","page":"FiberSections","text":"","title":"FiberSections"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FiberSections/#The-fiber","page":"FiberSections","text":"","title":"The fiber"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FiberSections/#Fiber-section-supertype","page":"FiberSections","text":"","title":"Fiber-section supertype"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FiberSections/#Threading-configuration","page":"FiberSections","text":"","title":"Threading configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FiberSections/#Internal-API","page":"FiberSections","text":"These symbols are not part of the public interface; they are documented for contributors — the CPU-threading primitives, the fused fiberwise executor that every stamped operation runs through, the fiberwise-algebra code generators, the section-introspection helpers, and the storage-ownership check that keeps aliasing safe by construction, for anyone extending the package's algebra.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections","page":"FiberSections","text":"FiberSections\n\nIt is the fiber-bundle model's base-agnostic core.\n\nA field (a section over a Grid3D) and a collection of off-grid point-values (a section over a PointSet) are both sections of a tensor bundle over a base. This module owns the part of the design that does not depend on which base is used. The parts are:\n\nthe fiber types — FiberVector, FiberTensor2, FiberTensor3 and FiberTensor4, StaticArrays aliases naming the value at one base point, together with fiber_shape. A fiber is base-agnostic by construction, so it belongs here and both the field and point layers take their fiber vocabulary from this module;\nthe AbstractFiberSection supertype, which both fields and point-values subtype;\nthe CPU-threading, fused-assignment primitive _assign! and its policy controls (with_threading, threading_cutoff, and set_threading_cutoff!);\nthe fiberwise-algebra code generators — the per-component builders behind operations such as dot, cross, and norm — written once here so that one set of generated kernels serves both field and point-value sections;\nthe fused fiberwise executor _fiberwise_pass! — one portable kernel and one threaded CPU loop, running every stamped values function, a pure scalar function taking one element per input and returning its outputs, the route for multi-output fiberwise operations;\nthe fiber adapter behind map!/map — the pure-fiber wrapper that assembles a base point's components into a fiber, applies the caller's function, and flattens the result back, so an arbitrary fiber map rides the same executor as the generated algebra.\n\nThe base-specific halves live elsewhere. Fields (grid) and Points (point set) hold the concrete section types, the section-interface accessors (field_data, _vector_component_data, and _tensor2_component_data) and the broadcast styles, each extending the accessors for its own types, while FieldAlgebra and Points stamp the method tables from the builders here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections"},{"category":"type","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.FiberVector","page":"FiberSections","text":"FiberVector{T}\n\nA FiberVector{T} is one static three-component Cartesian vector value.\n\nThis is an alias for SVector{3, T}, with scalar type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.FiberVector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.FiberTensor2","page":"FiberSections","text":"FiberTensor2{T}\n\nA FiberTensor2{T} is one static second-order Cartesian tensor value.\n\nThis is an alias for SMatrix{3, 3, T, 9}, with scalar type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.FiberTensor2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.FiberTensor3","page":"FiberSections","text":"FiberTensor3{T}\n\nA FiberTensor3{T} is one static third-order Cartesian tensor value.\n\nThis is an alias for SArray{Tuple{3, 3, 3}, T, 3, 27}, with scalar type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.FiberTensor3"},{"category":"type","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.FiberTensor4","page":"FiberSections","text":"FiberTensor4{T}\n\nA FiberTensor4{T} is one static fourth-order Cartesian tensor value.\n\nThis is an alias for SArray{Tuple{3, 3, 3, 3}, T, 4, 81}, with scalar type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.FiberTensor4"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.fiber_shape","page":"FiberSections","text":"fiber_shape(::Val{R}) where {R}\n\nReturn the tensor-fiber shape associated with tensor order R.\n\nFor scalar fields, R = 0 gives (). For higher tensor orders, each tensor index has length 3, so R = 1 gives (3,), R = 2 gives (3, 3), and so on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.fiber_shape"},{"category":"type","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.AbstractFiberSection","page":"FiberSections","text":"AbstractFiberSection{T, R}\n\nSupertype of every section of the package's tensor bundle, so both fields (sections over a Grid3D, in physical-space or spectral representation) and point-values (sections over a PointSet) subtype it.\n\nThe parameter T<:AbstractFloat is the real scalar type, and R is the tensor order (0 scalar, 1 vector, 2 second-order tensor, and so on). The fiberwise algebra is rooted at this supertype because it is base-independent: it applies the same operation to the same fiber type whether the base is a structured grid or an unstructured point set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.AbstractFiberSection"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.threading_cutoff","page":"FiberSections","text":"threading_cutoff()\n\nReturn the element count at or above which a CPU field assignment is split across Base.Threads workers rather than run serially. Here an assignment is a fused elementwise write into field storage — the _assign! primitive behind broadcasts, and the same gate guards the fused CPU loops behind the algebra and the differential operators, and the fixed-tree reductions (a reduction whose summation tree depends on the array length alone, so its value is the same at every thread count). Two groups of operations sit outside the gate and thread by Threads.nthreads() regardless (see the warning on with_threading): the grid↔point transfer and the radial reductions. The count is measured as the destination array's length (grid points times fiber components), and it applies on the CPU only, since GPU arrays are never threaded. The default is 16384, and set_threading_cutoff! changes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.threading_cutoff"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.set_threading_cutoff!","page":"FiberSections","text":"set_threading_cutoff!(n::Integer)\n\nSet threading_cutoff to n and return it. A CPU field assignment whose destination has fewer than n elements then runs serially instead of threaded. Raise n to keep small operations off the thread pool, or lower it to thread smaller writes.\n\nThe cutoff is a single process-wide setting, unlike with_threading, whose override is dynamically scoped and therefore task-local. Set it once at startup rather than around a call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.set_threading_cutoff!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.with_threading","page":"FiberSections","text":"with_threading(f; enabled::Union{Nothing, Bool} = true)\n\nRun f() with the CPU-threading decision forced to enabled, restore the previous setting, and return f()'s value. The override is dynamically scoped and task-local, affecting only f(), and is restored even on an exception.\n\nArguments\n\nf — the zero-argument function to run under the override.\nenabled — true or false to force the threaded or serial path, or nothing to follow the automatic nthreads()-based policy. Pass false around a region already parallel at a higher level, avoiding oversubscription.\n\nwarning: Not every operation is governed by this override\nThe override (and the cutoff) governs the fused broadcast assignments, the fused CPU loops behind the algebra and the differential operators (helmholtz_projection! included), and the fixed-tree reductions — a reduction whose summation tree is a function of the array length alone, so it returns the same value at every thread count. Two groups of operations run as portable device-style kernels on every backend and partition by Threads.nthreads() directly, ignoring this setting: the grid↔point transfer (interpolate!/anterpolate!) and the radial reductions (radial_spectrum/radial_correlation) — the kernels with atomic or irregular access, which a gated loop cannot serve — \"gated\" meaning a CPU loop that consults this setting before threading, as against a kernel that always partitions by thread count. To run those serially, start Julia with one thread.A third category is neither governed nor exempt: an operation that is not threaded at all, and so unaffected by this setting for a different reason. The weighted_inner reduction is the one to know about — its single fused pass is serial, which is why it diverges from inner as threads are added. Nothing is wrong there; it simply has no threaded route to override.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.with_threading"},{"category":"type","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._MapValues","page":"FiberSections","text":"_MapValues(φ, orders, stride)\n\nThe values function _fiberwise_pass! runs for map!/map. Its arrays are the inputs' whole storage arrays, one per input, and at each linear index it reads each input's fiber itself — component c of an input at n + (c - 1) * stride, the fiber-last layout — applies φ to the fibers, and flattens the result. Passing one array per input, rather than one per component, keeps the arrays tuple as short as the input count and a device kernel's argument list independent of the inputs' tensor orders; the component reads are unrolled by order, so no count the caller chooses reaches a splat.\n\nFields\n\nφ — the caller's fiber map, applied to the assembled fibers at each base point.\norders — the tensor orders of the inputs, a tuple of Val{R}, so the fiber assembly is resolved at compile time.\nstride — the number of base points, the distance between one component of an input and the next in its storage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._MapValues"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._SERIAL_CUTOFF","page":"FiberSections","text":"_SERIAL_CUTOFF\n\nBelow this element count an assignment runs serially regardless of thread count. Threading does not pay on small arrays, and the cutoff also exempts one-element views of the zero Fourier mode. The assignment path reads it, and set_threading_cutoff! sets it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._SERIAL_CUTOFF"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._THREADING_OVERRIDE","page":"FiberSections","text":"_THREADING_OVERRIDE\n\nThis task-local value overrides the threading decision. A value of nothing follows Threads.nthreads(), while true or false forces threaded or serial. The setter with_threading sets it in a dynamic scope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._THREADING_OVERRIDE"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._accumulate_values!-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{NTuple{N, Any}, NTuple{N, Any}, Any, Any, Any}} where N","page":"FiberSections","text":"_accumulate_values!(targets, values, α, β, n)\n\nThe beta ne 0 counterpart of _write_values!: write α * values[i] + β * targets[i][n], mirroring the accumulate branch of the broadcast kernel bodies expression for expression.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._accumulate_values!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assemble_fiber-Tuple{Val{0}, Any, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_assemble_fiber(::Val{R}, scalars, offset)\n\nAssemble one fiber of order R from the scalars tuple, reading _fiber_component_count(Val(R)) entries starting after offset, in column-major order (so the tensor case is a direct SMatrix constructor). The scalars tuple is homogeneous in the section scalar type, so its reads stay type-stable even when offset is not a literal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assemble_fiber"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assign!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_assign!(destination, source)\n\nHandle a non-Broadcasted right-hand side — for example a bare component view from a transpose, or a constant — by wrapping it as an identity broadcast so the same threaded assignment path applies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assign!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assign!-Tuple{Any, Base.Broadcast.Broadcasted}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_assign!(destination, broadcasted)\n\nMaterialize the lazy broadcasted expression into destination and return it, threading over chunks on the CPU when _use_threads allows. The generic method is plain Broadcast.materialize!, the safe default that keeps GPU arrays and unknown array wrappers on their broadcast path with no scalar indexing. Only dense Arrays and Array-backed SubArrays take the threaded path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assign!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assign_slabs!-Union{Tuple{A}, Tuple{Any, Any, Val{A}}} where A","page":"FiberSections","text":"_assign_slabs!(destination, instantiated, axis::Val)\n\nRun the slab tasks of _assign_threaded!: partition the given axis of destination with _index_ranges and materialize! each slab's sliced assignment in its own task. Separated behind a Val function barrier so every slice and kernel call compiles for the concrete axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assign_slabs!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assign_threaded!-Tuple{Any, Base.Broadcast.Broadcasted}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_assign_threaded!(destination, broadcasted)\n\nThread a fused broadcast assignment by splitting the _slab_axis of destination into contiguous slabs and running the standard fused Broadcast.materialize! on each slab in its own task — the destination viewed to the slab, the Broadcasted tree rebuilt over _slice_broadcast_argument-sliced operands. Each slab therefore executes the same SIMD-vectorized broadcast kernel the serial path uses, only over a sub-range: fusion is preserved (no temporaries), and the result is bitwise-identical to serial, since each element is written exactly once from the same fused expression and no value crosses a slab boundary. This replaced a per-element CartesianIndex loop over the whole Broadcasted, which does not vectorize — measured at ~4.7× slower per core than materialize!, so threading inverted at low thread counts (2026-07-21; the finding and the numbers live with the benchmarks). The method falls back to serial when the axes do not match or an argument type is not sliceable, which is defensive because the field kernels always match and only build sliceable trees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._assign_threaded!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._broadcast_sliceable-Tuple{Base.Broadcast.Broadcasted}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_broadcast_sliceable(argument)\n\nReturn whether every leaf of a broadcast argument tree is a type _slice_broadcast_argument knows how to slice or pass through — arrays, numbers, Refs, and nested Broadcasted trees over them. Anything else makes the threaded path bail out to the serial materialize! (the safe default; the field kernels only ever build trees of these types).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._broadcast_sliceable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._call_values-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Any, Any, Any}} where F","page":"FiberSections","text":"_call_values(f, arrays, n, constants)\n\nApply the values function f at linear index n: a generated values function takes one scalar per array, followed by the fiber constants, as positional arguments — a fixed and small count. The map! adapter reads its inputs itself instead (see _MapValues), since how many there are is the caller's to choose.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._call_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._check_owned_storage-Tuple{Any, AbstractString}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_check_owned_storage(data, what::AbstractString)\n\nReject storage that does not own its memory — a SubArray anywhere in the parent chain — so that two sections can share storage only by being backed by the same array.\n\nA section over a view is not a construction this package has a use for. Nothing in the library builds one; there is no verb that returns one; and on a periodic domain it is not even meaningful, since a sub-block of a periodic base is not itself periodic and a component slice of a vector field is a coordinate projection rather than a scalar section (a component is obtained by contracting with a basis vector, which allocates).\n\nWhat it did have was a silent wrong answer. A fused pass reads element n and writes element n, which is safe when the destination IS an input and unsafe when one section's element n is another's element n + m. Two overlapping views at different base offsets produced exactly that, in fourteen operations across three execution routes, with no error — and no contiguity test excludes it, since reshape(view(flat, 2:end), dims) is densely strided and still overlaps at an offset. Ownership is the property that does exclude it.\n\nThe check is on ownership, not on layout: Array, CuArray, ROCArray and a reshape of any of them all own their memory and are accepted, so the package's backend genericity is untouched. Allocate with similar_field or similar_point_values rather than slicing a larger buffer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._check_owned_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._cross_formula-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_cross_formula(left_symbol, left_kind, right_symbol, right_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function i -> expr for the cross product: with cyclic (j, k), component i is u_j v_k - u_k v_j. Its output feeds _vector_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._cross_formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._det_tensor_rhs-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_det_tensor_rhs(symbol, kind)\n\nBuild the right-hand side for the fiberwise determinant det(A) = varepsilon_ijk A_1i A_2j A_3k, the third principal invariant I_3 of the 3times3 tensor symbol (kind is :field or :fiber), expanded by cofactors along the first row:\n\nbeginaligned\ndet(A) =   A_11(A_22A_33 - A_23A_32) \n              - A_12(A_21A_33 - A_23A_31) \n              + A_13(A_21A_32 - A_22A_31)\nendaligned\n\nIt feeds _scalar_kernel_body. Unlike the linear _trace_tensor_rhs, the determinant is cubic in the components (a homogeneous degree-3 form), so it is a nonlinear operation and is generated for physical-space representation only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._det_tensor_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._dot_tensor_rhs-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_dot_tensor_rhs(A_symbol, A_kind, B_symbol, B_kind)\n\nBuild the right-hand-side expression for the tensor Frobenius inner product, sum_ij A_ij B_ij, as a single broadcasted expression fed to _scalar_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._dot_tensor_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._dot_vector_rhs-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_dot_vector_rhs(u_symbol, u_kind, v_symbol, v_kind)\n\nBuild the right-hand-side expression for a vector inner product, sum_i u_i v_i, as a single broadcasted expression fed to _scalar_kernel_body. Each operand kind is :field or :fiber, as in _vector_component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._dot_vector_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._element_values-Tuple{Tuple, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_element_values(arrays, n)\n\nReturn the tuple of every array's element at linear index n, written out with a literal index per array. The body is generated per tuple type, so it holds for any number of arrays and any mix of array types. A map over the tuple takes Base's generic path from 32 entries, the ceiling the multi-input map! hit at four second-order inputs; an ntuple over a closure indexes a mixed tuple through a runtime lookup whenever the compiler declines to inline the closure, which allocates per element on a CPU and does not compile on a device (a radial kernel beside plain arrays in a kernel-composed combine!).\n\nThe generated body carries an inline marker, @inline not applying to a @generated function: without it the read stayed a call inside the executor's @simd loop, which blocked vectorization and made mul! 3.2 times slower on a CPU and 13.5 times slower on a device.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._element_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiber_component_count-Tuple{Val{0}}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_fiber_component_count(::Val{R})\n\nCount the stored scalar components of a fiber of tensor order R (1, 3, 9).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiber_component_count"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiber_order-Tuple{Type{<:Number}}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_fiber_order(fiber_type)\n\nReturn a concrete fiber type's tensor order, 0, 1 or 2, or -1 when the type is not a recognized fiber (an un-inferrable φ return), on which the allocating map raises a clear error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiber_order"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiber_type-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Val{0}}} where T","page":"FiberSections","text":"_fiber_type(::Type{T}, ::Val{R})\n\nReturn the concrete fiber type of order R over scalar T: T, SVector{3, T}, or SMatrix{3, 3, T, 9}, for inferring φ's output order in the allocating map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiber_type"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiberwise_pass!-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{Any, F, Vararg{Any, 5}}} where F","page":"FiberSections","text":"_fiberwise_pass!(backend, f, targets, arrays, constants, α, β)\n\nRun one fused fiberwise pass — every target element receives alpha f(textelement values textconstants), plus β times its prior value when the accumulate is requested — over the common linear index range of the participating storage. Here f is a pure scalar values function: it takes one scalar per entry of arrays (that array's element at the running index) followed by the fiber constants verbatim, and returns the tuple of output values written to targets. Every array element is read once and every output written once, and all of an element's reads happen before its writes, so a target may alias a source array. The generic method launches the _fiberwise_values_kernel! device kernel on the backend; the KernelAbstractions.CPU method runs a @simd loop, threaded over slabs, over the linear range (component views are contiguous, so linear indexing is stride-1) through the same _use_threads gate as broadcasts, bitwise-identical to serial because each element is written once from the same expression. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiberwise_pass!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiberwise_slab!-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Vararg{Any, 6}}} where F","page":"FiberSections","text":"_fiberwise_slab!(f, targets, arrays, constants, α, β, elements)\n\nRun _fiberwise_pass!'s loop over one contiguous slab of the linear index range, the beta branch hoisted out of the loop (the package-wide overwrite-vs-accumulate convention: beta = 0 never reads the destination).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._fiberwise_slab!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._flatten_fiber-Tuple{Number}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_flatten_fiber(value)\n\nFlatten an output fiber into the column-major tuple written to the targets: a scalar becomes a one-tuple, a StaticArray its Tuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._flatten_fiber"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._foreach_outer_slab-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Any, Any}} where F","page":"FiberSections","text":"_foreach_outer_slab(slab_body!, outer_axis, element_count)\n\nRun slab_body! over the whole outer_axis serially, or — when _use_threads allows for element_count written elements — over contiguous slabs of it, one task per slab. The caller's slab_body! loops modes with the outer axis restricted to the given range, so each element is written by exactly one task and the threaded result is bitwise-identical to serial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._foreach_outer_slab"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._index_ranges-Tuple{AbstractUnitRange, Integer}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_index_ranges(axis::AbstractUnitRange, n::Integer)\n\nPartition the index range axis into at most n contiguous, near-equal UnitRanges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._index_ranges"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._nary_assign!-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Any, Tuple, D}} where {F, D}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_nary_assign!(reducer, destination, operands, duplicate)\n\nWrite the left fold of reducer over operands into destination and return it, in passes of at most _OPERANDS_PER_PASS operands: the first pass folds the leading operands, and each later one continues the fold with destination as its first operand. The order of the operations is the single pass's, so the result is bitwise the same. A later operand that is destination itself is copied by duplicate before the first pass overwrites it, which keeps an aliased call exact; that copy is the one allocation, and only an aliased call makes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._nary_assign!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._norm_scalar_rhs-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_norm_scalar_rhs(symbol)\n\nBuild, for _scalar_kernel_body, the scalar symbol's pointwise norm varphi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._norm_scalar_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._norm_tensor_rhs-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_norm_tensor_rhs(symbol, kind)\n\nBuild, for _scalar_kernel_body, the tensor symbol's Frobenius norm sqrtsum_ij A_ij^2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._norm_tensor_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._norm_vector_rhs-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_norm_vector_rhs(symbol, kind)\n\nBuild, for _scalar_kernel_body, the vector symbol's Euclidean norm sqrtsum_i u_i^2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._norm_vector_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._product-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_product(x, y)\n\nBuild the broadcasted product expression x .* y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._product"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._scalar_kernel_body-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_scalar_kernel_body(rhs)\n\nBuild the body of a scalar-field-valued kernel (a contraction with a scalar result), writing field_data(destination) .= rhs and ending in return destination.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._scalar_kernel_body"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._section_order-Union{Tuple{AbstractFiberSection{T, R}}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}} where {T, R}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_section_order(section)\n\nRead a section's tensor order as a Val from its AbstractFiberSection{T, R} type parameter — the compile-time quantity the map! adapter unrolls over.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._section_order"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._section_scalar_type-Union{Tuple{AbstractFiberSection{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"FiberSections","text":"_section_scalar_type(section::AbstractFiberSection)\n\nReturn the real scalar type T of any section. Because it is defined once on AbstractFiberSection{T}, it yields the real T for fields (including those in spectral representation, whose data is Complex{T}) and for point-values alike, which lets the shared sym/skew/dev kernels obtain T without dispatching on the concrete base.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._section_scalar_type"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._section_tensor_order-Union{Tuple{AbstractFiberSection{T, R}}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}} where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_section_tensor_order(section)\n\nReturn a section's tensor order R, read from its type. The peer of _section_scalar_type: both are type parameters, so a compatibility check written against them costs nothing at run time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._section_tensor_order"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._slab_axis-Tuple{NTuple{N, Int64} where N}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_slab_axis(dimensions)\n\nPick the axis a threaded assignment is split along: the last among the largest, so each slab is a large contiguous block and a trailing fiber axis of length 3 never caps the parallelism (a whole-vector-field write (N, N, N, 3) splits along the third axis, not the fourth).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._slab_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._slice_broadcast_argument-Tuple{Base.Broadcast.Broadcasted, Val, UnitRange{Int64}}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_slice_broadcast_argument(argument, axis::Val, slab)\n\nRestrict one broadcast argument to the slab range of broadcast axis axis. An array spanning that axis is viewed to the slab (a view re-bases its axes, so all sliced operands land on matching OneTo axes); an array with a singleton or missing axis passes through unchanged (broadcasting extrudes it exactly as in the full assignment); a nested Broadcasted is rebuilt over its sliced arguments; scalars and Refs are shape-free and pass through. The axis is a Val so the whole recursive slice specializes: with it a runtime Int would leave every view and rebuilt node un-inferred, boxing kilobytes per slab where the specialized form allocates only the small wrapper objects themselves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._slice_broadcast_argument"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._sum_terms-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_sum_terms(terms)\n\nCombine a non-empty collection of expressions into a broadcasted sum term_1 .+ term_2 .+ term_3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._sum_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tensor_component-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_tensor_component(symbol, kind, i, j)\n\nBuild the expression that accesses component (i, j) of a second-order tensor argument named symbol. With kind = :field it reads a field's component view (_tensor2_component_data); with kind = :fiber it indexes a constant SMatrix; with kind = :scalar it emits the positional scalar argument name symbol_ij for a fused values function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tensor_component"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tensor_kernel_body-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_tensor_kernel_body(formula; alpha_beta::Bool)\n\nBuild the body of a second-order tensor-valued kernel, writing the value formula(i, j) builds into component (ij) of destination, for i j = 1 2 3, with the same alpha_beta five-argument behaviour as _vector_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tensor_kernel_body"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tensor_tensor_formula-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_tensor_tensor_formula(A_symbol, A_kind, B_symbol, B_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function (i, j) -> expr for the tensor-tensor product, whose component (i, j) is A_ik B_kj (the repeated fiber index summed). Its output feeds _tensor_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tensor_tensor_formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tensor_vector_formula-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_tensor_vector_formula(A_symbol, A_kind, u_symbol, u_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function i -> expr for the tensor-on-vector left action, whose component i is A_ij u_j (the repeated fiber index summed). Its output feeds _vector_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tensor_vector_formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._to_broadcasted-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_to_broadcasted(expr)\n\nRewrite a dotted expression — an infix dotted operator such as a .* b .+ c, or a broadcast call such as f.(x) — into the lazy Base.Broadcast.broadcasted(f, args...) tree, recursing into operands and leaving non-broadcast subexpressions untouched. This codegen helper lets generated and hand-written kernels route a fused assignment through _assign! without materializing intermediates, so fusion is preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._to_broadcasted"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._trace_tensor_rhs-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_trace_tensor_rhs(symbol, kind)\n\nBuild the right-hand side for the fiberwise trace operatornametr(A) = A_ii = A_11 + A_22 + A_33, the sum of the diagonal components of the tensor symbol (kind is :field or :fiber). It feeds _scalar_kernel_body, and the fused _dev_values function reuses it for the trace it subtracts. The trace is linear, so the same body serves both physical-space and spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._trace_tensor_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._transpose_formula-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_transpose_formula(A_symbol, A_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function (i, j) -> expr for the transpose, whose component (i, j) is A_ji. Its output feeds _tensor_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._transpose_formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tuple_map-Union{Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Tuple}} where F","page":"FiberSections","text":"_tuple_map(f, operands)\n\nApply f to each element of the tuple operands and return the tuple of results, written out with a literal index per element and inlined, like _element_values. It replaces Base.map over a tuple whose length the caller chooses: Base.map takes its generic path from 32 elements, where the result type is no longer inferred, which made the allocating map over 33 sources return a Union (found by the exhaustive survey, decision 88).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._tuple_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._undot-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_undot(expr)\n\nLower a dotted formula expression to its per-element form: dotted operators (.+, .*, …) lose their dots and dotted calls f.(x) become plain calls, recursively. The formula builders above emit dotted (broadcast) expressions; the fused values functions evaluate the same tree one element at a time, so the arithmetic — and hence the result — is bitwise-identical.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._undot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._use_threads-Tuple{Integer}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_use_threads(element_count)\n\nReturn whether an assignment over element_count elements should be threaded. It is threaded when the count is at or above the serial cutoff and threading is either forced on or, under the automatic policy, Threads.nthreads() > 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._use_threads"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._vector_component-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_vector_component(symbol, kind, i)\n\nBuild the expression that accesses component i of a vector argument named symbol. With kind = :field it reads a field's component view (_vector_component_data); with kind = :fiber it indexes a constant SVector; with kind = :scalar it emits the positional scalar argument name symbol_i for a fused values function (_fiberwise_pass!). The builders splice the result into kernel formulas so that each per-component formula is written once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._vector_component"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._vector_kernel_body-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_vector_kernel_body(formula; alpha_beta::Bool)\n\nBuild the body of a vector-valued kernel, writing the value formula(i) builds into component i of destination, for i = 1 2 3, where formula(i) is the per-component expression. With alpha_beta = true it emits the five-argument destination = α * formula + β * destination form and branches on iszero(β_value), so the pure-overwrite path stays allocation-free. The body ends in return destination and is spliced into a function that binds destination (and, when alpha_beta is set, α_value and β_value).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._vector_kernel_body"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._vector_tensor_formula-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"_vector_tensor_formula(u_symbol, u_kind, A_symbol, A_kind)\n\nReturn a per-component function i -> expr for the vector-on-tensor right action, whose component i is u_j A_ji (the repeated fiber index summed). Its output feeds _vector_kernel_body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._vector_tensor_formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._write_values!-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{NTuple{N, Any}, NTuple{N, Any}, Any, Any}} where N","page":"FiberSections","text":"_write_values!(targets, values, α, n)\n\nWrite α * values[i] into targets[i][n] for every output, unrolled over the (static) output count, returning nothing. The real α keeps the multiply exact for alpha = 1, so the plain (non-alpha/beta) forms stay bitwise-identical to broadcasting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections._write_values!"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/FiberSections/#GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.@_assign!-Tuple{Any}","page":"FiberSections","text":"@_assign!(destination .= rhs)\n\nReplace a fused broadcast assignment with a threaded one: rewrite rhs to a lazy broadcast tree (via _to_broadcasted) and assign it through _assign!, so the write threads on the CPU. Use it in hand-written kernels in place of .=, and note that _assign! must be in scope at the call site.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections.@_assign!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#DifferentialOperators","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"","title":"DifferentialOperators"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#First-order-differential-operators","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"","title":"First-order differential operators"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#Second-order-differential-operators","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"","title":"Second-order differential operators"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#Projection-operators","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"","title":"Projection operators"},{"category":"section","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#Internal-API","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"These symbols are not part of the public interface; they are documented for contributors — the coefficientwise spectral kernels each operator applies per mode, and the gated CPU loops — \"gated\" meaning a loop that consults the threading setting before splitting — that mirror them expression for expression so a threaded run agrees with a device one bitwise.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"DifferentialOperators\n\nDifferential operators on scalar, vector, and tensor fields over three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains, applied as spectral multipliers. A field in this module is its finite Fourier representation, so each operator is the exact term-by-term derivative of that trigonometric polynomial on the resolved band — the band a grid's resolution actually represents, which excludes the Nyquist frequency of each even-length axis — realized as a coefficientwise multiply by a wavenumber symbol with no finite-difference truncation. The two symbol families treat that Nyquist term differently and deliberately so (see the Nyquist split below), so it is not simply zeroed everywhere. The wavenumber symbols are held in a FieldSpectralMultipliers, built from a field with FieldSpectralMultipliers(field) or from its six wavenumber arrays. Every operator provides an allocating form and an in-place ! form, and each per-operator docstring gives both the physical-space meaning and the spectral symbol it applies.\n\nThe parts are:\n\ngradient (nabla) raises the tensor order: a scalar field becomes a vector field, and a vector field becomes a second-order tensor field.\ndivergence (nablacdot) lowers the tensor order: a vector field becomes a scalar field, and a second-order tensor field becomes a vector field; an optional kernel argument composes a convolution into the same pass.\ncurl (nablatimes) returns the curl of a vector field, a measure of its local rotation, and preserves the tensor order.\ninverse_curl ((nablatimes)^-1) returns the solenoidal preimage of a curl — the divergence-free field whose curl is the given one — and preserves the tensor order.\nsymmetric_gradient (operatornamesymnabla) returns the symmetric part of a vector field's gradient as a second-order tensor field, formed in one pass without the full gradient.\nlaplacian (Delta) applies the second-derivative operator to every component and preserves the tensor order.\ninverse_laplacian (Delta^-1) returns the zero-mean solution of Poisson's equation and preserves the tensor order.\nhelmholtz_projection (mathbb P) projects a vector field onto its divergence-free part in one pass; an optional kernel argument composes a convolution into the same pass.\n\nThe Nyquist frequency is treated differently by symbol: first-derivative wavenumbers are zeroed at the Nyquist frequency on even-length axes, while squared wavenumbers keep the true squared wavenumber there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.gradient!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"gradient!(\n  destination::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  field::ScalarFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\ngradient!(\n  destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2},\n  field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the gradient (gradient, nabla) of a field and write it into destination, returning destination. Differentiating raises the tensor order by one: a scalar field varphi gives the vector field with components (nablavarphi)_i = partial_x_ivarphi, and a vector field u gives the second-order tensor field with components (nabla u)_ij = partial_x_j u_i.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating gradient returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field that receives the result; it shares the domain of field and holds one tensor order higher.\nfield — the field to differentiate, in spectral representation.\nmultipliers — the wavenumber symbols for the field's grid.\n\nEach output component is the source component multiplied coefficientwise by mathrmi times the derivative wavenumber of the differentiation axis. Below, k labels the mode and a is the derivative wavenumber family — the one zeroed at an even axis's Nyquist frequency, kept apart from the true squared wavenumber the Laplacian uses:\n\nbegingathered\nwidehat(nablavarphi)_i(k) = mathrmi  a_i  hatvarphi(k) \nwidehat(nabla u)_ij(k) = mathrmi  a_j  hat u_i(k)\nendgathered\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.gradient!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.gradient","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"gradient(field, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocating form of gradient!: allocate the result and return it. A scalar field varphi gives its gradient vector field nablavarphi; a vector field u gives the second-order tensor field nabla u. See gradient! for the componentwise definition and the spectral kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.divergence!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"divergence!(\n  destination::ScalarFieldSpectral,\n  field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\ndivergence!(\n  destination::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  field::TensorFieldSpectral{2},\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\ndivergence!(destination, field, multipliers, kernel::SpectralKernel)\ndivergence!(destination, field, multipliers, kernel::SpectralKernel, α, β)\n\nCompute the divergence (divergence, nablacdot) of a field and write it into destination, returning destination. Differentiating lowers the tensor order by one: a vector field u gives the scalar field nablacdot u = partial_x_i u_i, and a second-order tensor field A gives the vector field with components (nablacdot A)_i = partial_x_j A_ij.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating divergence returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field that receives the result; it shares the domain of field and holds one tensor order lower.\nfield — the field to differentiate, in spectral representation.\nmultipliers — the wavenumber symbols for the field's grid.\n\nThe result is the coefficientwise contraction of the source with mathrmi times the derivative wavenumber vector, summed over the three axes. Below, k labels the mode and a is the derivative wavenumber family — zeroed at an even axis's Nyquist frequency, unlike the true squared wavenumber the Laplacian uses:\n\nbegingathered\nwidehatnablacdot u(k) = mathrmi (a_1 hat u_1 + a_2 hat u_2 + a_3 hat u_3) \nwidehat(nablacdot A)_i(k) = mathrmi (a_1 hat A_i1 + a_2 hat A_i2 + a_3 hat A_i3)\nendgathered\n\nThe result has exactly zero mean, as the divergence of a periodic field must: every derivative wavenumber is exactly zero at the zero mode, so the mean coefficient is computed as exactly zero rather than as round-off. This holds for multipliers built from the grid, which the convenience constructor of FieldSpectralMultipliers does; wavenumbers supplied through its explicit six-array constructor are not checked at the zero mode. In the kernel-composed form below with beta ne 0, the mean is beta times the destination's.\n\nThe kernel-composed form\n\nPassing a kernel composes the divergence with a periodic convolution in the same fused pass. As a real physical object the result is the kernel-weighted local average of the divergence, g star (nablacdot u) — a single operator, because convolution and divergence both act coefficientwise in spectral representation, so their symbols simply multiply. The additional arguments are:\n\nkernel — the kernel's transfer function hat g, in any storage class of a SpectralKernel — a StoredKernel holding one coefficient per mode, or a compressed class evaluated per mode (radial, separable, or their product) costing O(N) for N points per axis, not O(N^3), exactly as convolve! takes it — including its reality condition, that hat g be Hermitian, hat g(-k) = overlinehat g(k), without which the result is the spectrum of no real field; it may alias destination, since the product is coefficientwise.\nα, β — real accumulation weights as in convolve!: the four-argument form overwrites (alpha = 1, beta = 0), and the six-argument form computes\n\nbeginaligned\nwidehattextttdestination(k) = alpha  hat g(k)  widehatnablacdot u(k) \nquad + beta  widehattextttdestination(k)\nendaligned\n\nand likewise per component for a second-order tensor field. The composed form replaces a divergence! followed by a convolve! — one fused pass instead of two, saving a full read and a full write of destination per call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.divergence!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.divergence","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"divergence(field, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\ndivergence(field, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n           kernel::SpectralKernel)\n\nAllocating form of divergence!: allocate the result and return it. A vector field u gives its scalar divergence nablacdot u; a second-order tensor field A gives the vector field nablacdot A. With a kernel the result is the divergence convolved with the kernel, g star (nablacdot u), in one pass. See divergence! for the componentwise definition, the spectral kernel, and the kernel-composed form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.divergence"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.curl!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"curl!(\n  destination::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the curl (curl, nablatimes) of a vector field u and write it into destination, returning destination. The curl is the vector field whose components are, with varepsilon_ijk the Levi-Civita symbol, (nablatimes u)_i = varepsilon_ijk  partial_x_j u_k; written out cyclically,\n\nbegingathered\n(nablatimes u)_1 = partial_x_2 u_3 - partial_x_3 u_2 \n(nablatimes u)_2 = partial_x_3 u_1 - partial_x_1 u_3 \n(nablatimes u)_3 = partial_x_1 u_2 - partial_x_2 u_1\nendgathered\n\nIt measures the field's local rotation at twice the angular rate — the rate itself is tfrac12nablatimes u. (A fluid-dynamics user calls nablatimes u the vorticity; the package keeps the neutral mathematical name, as it does for symmetric_gradient against \"strain rate\".)\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating curl returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the vector field that receives the result; it shares the domain of field, and it may alias field freely: the single pass reads all three source coefficients of a mode before writing any component of it, so the aliased call needs no temporary copy and is bitwise-identical to the non-aliased one.\nfield — the vector field to differentiate, in spectral representation.\nmultipliers — the wavenumber symbols for the field's grid.\n\nThe spectral symbol is a coefficientwise multiply by mathrmi times the cross product with the derivative wavenumber vector, widehatnablatimes u(k) = mathrmi  a times hat u(k), where k labels the mode and a is the derivative wavenumber family — zeroed at an even axis's Nyquist frequency, unlike the true squared wavenumber the Laplacian uses. Componentwise:\n\nbegingathered\nwidehat(nablatimes u)_1(k) = mathrmi (a_2 hat u_3 - a_3 hat u_2) \nwidehat(nablatimes u)_2(k) = mathrmi (a_3 hat u_1 - a_1 hat u_3) \nwidehat(nablatimes u)_3(k) = mathrmi (a_1 hat u_2 - a_2 hat u_1)\nendgathered\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.curl!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.curl","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"curl(field, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocating form of curl!: allocate the result and return it, the curl nablatimes u of a vector field. See curl! for the componentwise definition and the spectral kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.curl"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.symmetric_gradient!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"symmetric_gradient!(\n  destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2},\n  field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the symmetric gradient (symmetric_gradient, operatornamesymnabla) of a vector field u and write it into destination, returning destination. It is the symmetric part of the gradient nabla u, the second-order tensor field\n\nS_ij = tfrac12 (partial_x_j u_i + partial_x_i u_j)\n\nformed directly. It is the fused symmetric peer of curl, which extracts the antisymmetric part of nabla u as a vector: forming S this way skips the full nine-component gradient tensor that sym(gradient(u)) builds only to symmetrize, so reach for it whenever the symmetric part is all you need.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating symmetric_gradient returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the second-order tensor field that receives the result; it shares the domain of field and holds one tensor order higher.\nfield — the vector field to differentiate, in spectral representation.\nmultipliers — the wavenumber symbols for the field's grid.\n\nThe spectral symbol symmetrizes mathrmi a_j hat u_i in its two indices, with k labelling the mode and a the derivative wavenumber family — zeroed at an even axis's Nyquist frequency, unlike the true squared wavenumber the Laplacian uses:\n\nwidehatS_ij(k) = tfracmathrmi2 bigl(a_j hat u_i(k) + a_i hat u_j(k)bigr)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.symmetric_gradient!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.symmetric_gradient","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"symmetric_gradient(field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n                   multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocating form of symmetric_gradient!: allocate the result and return it, the symmetric gradient operatornamesymnabla u — the symmetric part of nabla u as a second-order tensor field, formed without the full gradient. See symmetric_gradient! for the componentwise definition and the spectral kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.symmetric_gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.laplacian!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"laplacian!(\n  destination::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the Laplacian (laplacian, Delta) of a field and write it into destination, returning destination. The Laplacian is a local operator: it sums the second partial derivatives of a scalar field f over the three axes, and it acts on each component of a vector or tensor field u alike, so it preserves the tensor order:\n\nbegingathered\nDelta f = partial^2_x_i x_i f  (Delta u)_j = partial^2_x_i x_i u_j\nendgathered\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating laplacian returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field receiving the result, on field's domain and tensor order.\nfield — the field to differentiate, in spectral representation.\nmultipliers — the wavenumber symbols for the field's grid.\n\nThe spectral symbol multiplies each mode's coefficient by -k^2, its true wavevector squared:\n\nbegingathered\nwidehatDelta f(k) = -k^2  hat f(k)  k^2 = k_1^2 + k_2^2 + k_3^2\nendgathered\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.laplacian!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.laplacian","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"laplacian(field::AbstractFieldSpectral, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocating form of laplacian!: allocate the result and return it, the Laplacian Delta f. It preserves the tensor order (scalar, vector or tensor field alike). See laplacian! for the spectral form -k^2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.laplacian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_laplacian!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"inverse_laplacian!(\n  destination::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nCompute the zero-mean inverse Laplacian (inverse_laplacian, Delta^-1) of a field f and write it into destination, returning destination. For a mean-free right-hand side the result is the zero-mean field psi solving Poisson's equation — on the periodic box, the integration domain, it is solvable only when int_Omega fmathrmdx = 0, since any periodic psi has int_Omega Deltapsimathrmdx = 0 — and because the Laplacian annihilates constants, a vanishing mean fixes the solution uniquely:\n\nbegingathered\nDeltapsi = f \nint_Omega fmathrmdx = 0 \nint_Omega psimathrmdx = 0\nendgathered\n\nA general input is projected onto that compatible part: the result's zero Fourier mode is set to zero unconditionally, so the operator returns the zero-mean solution of Deltapsi = f - langle frangle (langle frangle the input's mean); the per-mode form below is the unconditional statement.\n\nFor a vector or tensor field the same solve is applied to each component, so the tensor order is preserved.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating inverse_laplacian returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field that receives the result; it matches the domain and tensor order of field.\nfield — the field to invert, in spectral representation.\nmultipliers — the wavenumber symbols for the field's grid.\n\nThe spectral symbol divides by -k^2, each mode's true wavevector squared, and zeroes the zero mode:\n\nwidehatDelta^-1 f(k) =\nbegincases\n- hat f(k)  k^2  k ne 0 \n0  k = 0\nendcases\n\nwarning: The inverse Laplacian is nonlocal\nAs the inverse of a differential operator, Delta^-1 is nonlocal: unlike the Laplacian it has no local partial-derivative formula, and the value at any point depends on the whole field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_laplacian!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_laplacian","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"inverse_laplacian(field::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n                  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocating form of inverse_laplacian!: allocate the result and return it, the zero-mean inverse Laplacian Delta^-1 f (the zero Fourier mode is set to zero). It preserves the tensor order (scalar, vector or tensor field alike). See inverse_laplacian! for the spectral form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_laplacian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_curl!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"inverse_curl!(destination::VectorFieldSpectral, field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n              multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nRecover the divergence-free vector field whose curl is field, writing and returning destination — the inverse of curl! (nablatimes) where that inverse exists.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating inverse_curl returns a fresh result instead.\n\nGiven a vorticity w the equation nablatimes u = w fixes u only up to a gradient, since nablatimesnablaphi = 0. This returns the one representative that is itself divergence-free, nablacdot u = 0, which is the gauge the choice amounts to and the one a velocity field satisfies. It also requires a solvable right-hand side: a curl is divergence-free, so nablacdot w = 0 is a compatibility condition on the input, and a field violating it has its longitudinal part silently discarded rather than reported.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the vector field written with the result. It may alias field: every mode's three source coefficients are read before any of its components are written.\nfield — the vector field to invert, in spectral representation.\nmultipliers — the FieldSpectralMultipliers supplying the derivative wavenumbers, which must match the field's grid, layout, element type and storage family.\n\nBecause nablatimes(nablatimes u) = nabla(nablacdot u) - Delta u and the result is divergence-free, the operator is a Laplacian solve composed with a curl, and in spectral representation it is one coefficientwise pass,\n\nhat u(k) = fracmathrmi a(k) times hat w(k)lvert a(k)rvert^2 \n\nwith a the Nyquist-zeroed derivative wavenumbers and the k = 0 mode set to zero. Numerator and denominator share one family, so nablatimes(nablatimes)^-1 reproduces a divergence-free field exactly at every mode the warning does not zero, not only on the resolved band.\n\nwarning: The zero mode has no preimage and is returned as zero\nA constant field is the curl of nothing, so the k = 0 coefficient is written zero, as is each Nyquist corner where every derivative wavenumber vanishes. The recovered field has zero mean, the convention inverse_laplacian! uses for the same reason.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_curl!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_curl","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"inverse_curl(field, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nAllocating form of inverse_curl!: allocate the result and return it, the divergence-free vector field whose curl is field. See inverse_curl! for the componentwise definition, the gauge it picks, and the compatibility condition on the input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.inverse_curl"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.helmholtz_projection!","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"helmholtz_projection!(\n  destination::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n  multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n)\n\nhelmholtz_projection!(destination, field, multipliers, kernel::SpectralKernel)\nhelmholtz_projection!(destination, field, multipliers,\n                      kernel::SpectralKernel, α, β)\n\nCompute the Helmholtz (Leray) projection (helmholtz_projection, mathbb P) of a vector field u and write it into destination, returning destination. The projection removes the longitudinal (curl-free) part of the field and keeps the divergence-free part, subtracting the gradient of the pressure-like potential that solves the divergence:\n\nmathbb P u = u - nabla  Delta^-1(nablacdot u)\n\nan identity exact on the resolved band — the wavenumbers the grid resolves, excluding each even axis's Nyquist plane (the inverse Laplacian keeps the true squared wavevector k^2 while the projector uses the derivative family — the two coincide off the Nyquist planes). The implemented operator is the exact orthogonal projector onto the kernel of the discrete divergence: mathbb P^2 = mathbb P, and the divergence of the result vanishes, at every mode — the Nyquist planes included.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating helmholtz_projection returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the vector field that receives the result; it shares the domain of field, and it may alias field at no cost: the projection is computed in a single pass that reads all three components of a mode before writing any of them, so the aliased call needs no temporary.\nfield — the vector field to project, in spectral representation.\nmultipliers — the wavenumber symbols for the field's grid.\n\nThe spectral symbol subtracts the longitudinal part a_i (acdothat u)(a cdot a) from hat u_i, with a the derivative wavenumber — the Nyquist-zeroed family the gradient and divergence use, equal to k on the resolved band — and passes every mode with a = 0 unchanged (the zero mode, and the pure-Nyquist corners where the whole derivative wavenumber vanishes):\n\nbegingathered\nwidehat(mathbb P u)_i(k) = Bigl(delta_ij - fraca_i a_ja cdot aBigr)  hat u_j(k) \ntextwith  delta_ij text the Kronecker delta and  a ne 0\nendgathered\n\nThe kernel-composed form\n\nPassing a kernel composes the projection with a periodic convolution in the same fused pass. As a real physical object the result is the kernel-weighted local average of the projected field, g star (mathbb P u) — a single operator, because convolution and projection both act coefficientwise in spectral representation, so the scalar symbol hat g multiplies the projection symbol. The additional arguments are:\n\nkernel — the kernel's transfer function hat g, in any storage class of a SpectralKernel — a StoredKernel holding one coefficient per mode, or a compressed class evaluated per mode (radial, separable, or their product) costing O(N) for N points per axis, not O(N^3), exactly as convolve! takes it — including its reality condition, that hat g be Hermitian, hat g(-k) = overlinehat g(k), without which the result is the spectrum of no real field; one scalar kernel weights all three components.\nα, β — real accumulation weights as in convolve!: the four-argument form overwrites (alpha = 1, beta = 0), and the six-argument form computes\n\nbeginaligned\nwidehattextttdestination_i(k) = alpha  hat g(k)  widehat(mathbb P u)_i(k) \nquad + beta  widehattextttdestination_i(k)\nendaligned\n\nThe composed form replaces a helmholtz_projection! followed by a convolve! — one fused pass instead of two, saving a full read and a full write of destination per call.\n\nwarning: The projection is nonlocal in physical space\nThe inverse Laplacian Delta^-1 is nonlocal, so mathbb P is a nonlocal operator in physical space even though it acts coefficientwise in spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.helmholtz_projection!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.helmholtz_projection","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"helmholtz_projection(field::VectorFieldSpectral,\n                     multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\nhelmholtz_projection(field, multipliers, kernel::SpectralKernel)\n\nAllocating form of helmholtz_projection!: allocate the result and return it, the Helmholtz (Leray) projection mathbb P u — the divergence-free part of a vector field, with every mode whose derivative wavenumber vanishes left unchanged. With a kernel the result is the projection convolved with the kernel, g star (mathbb P u), in one pass. See helmholtz_projection! for the spectral form and the kernel-composed form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators.helmholtz_projection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_helmholtz_projection!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{VectorFieldSpectral{T, Â, G} where {Â<:AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4}, G<:Grid3D{T}}, VectorFieldSpectral{T, Â, G} where {Â<:AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4}, G<:Grid3D{T}}, FieldSpectralMultipliers, Any, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_apply_helmholtz_projection!(destination, field, multipliers,\n                             kernel_data, α, β)\n\nRun the projection over every spectral mode through _helmholtz_projection_modes!, which dispatches on the backend of destination's storage: a KernelAbstractions launch of _helmholtz_projection_kernel! on a device, or a gated loop over contiguous slabs on a CPU. It passes the three derivative-wavenumber arrays and the converted α/β — the projector's denominator is formed in the pass by squaring those values, so no second family is read. The kernel_data is a kernel's storage, or nothing for the plain projection. Every helmholtz_projection! method calls it; it returns destination.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_helmholtz_projection!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_divergence!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Type{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_apply_spectral_divergence!(destination_component,\n                            source_1, source_2, source_3,\n                            wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3, ::Type{T})\n\n_apply_spectral_divergence!(destination_component,\n                            source_1, source_2, source_3,\n                            wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3,\n                            kernel_data, α, β, ::Type{T})\n\nApply a coefficientwise contraction with the derivative wavenumber vector, computing mathrmi (a_1 hat s_1 + a_2 hat s_2 + a_3 hat s_3), where a_j is the derivative wavenumber component and hat s_j is the source field's spectral component along axis j = 1 2 3. The divergence operator uses this kernel for the vector-to-scalar case, and once per output component for the tensor-to-vector case.\n\nThe eleven-argument form composes the contraction with a coefficientwise kernel and the alpha/beta accumulate in the same fused pass, computing textttdestination_component mathrel= alpha hat g cdot mathrmi (a_j hat s_j) + beta textttdestination_component with hat g = kernel_data, branching on iszero(β) to overwrite or accumulate — the divergence analogue of _apply_convolution!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_divergence!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_inverse_laplacian!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Type{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_apply_spectral_inverse_laplacian!(destination_data, source_data,\n                                   squared_wavenumber, ::Type{T})\n\nApply the coefficientwise zero-mean inverse Laplacian, computing -source_data ./ squared_wavenumber at every nonzero wavevector and setting the zero Fourier mode to zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_inverse_laplacian!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_laplacian!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_apply_spectral_laplacian!(destination_data, source_data, squared_wavenumber)\n\nApply the coefficientwise Laplacian symbol -k^2 to each component, computing destination_data .= (-).(squared_wavenumber) .* source_data, where squared_wavenumber is the lazy _squared_wavenumber_broadcast. It returns destination_data, preserving the tensor order by acting on every component alike.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._apply_spectral_laplacian!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._curl_modes!-NTuple{11, Any}","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_curl_modes!(backend, destination_1, destination_2, destination_3,\n             source_1, source_2, source_3,\n             wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3, imaginary_unit)\n\nRun the fused curl pass over every mode: read the three source coefficients once, write the three components of mathrmi (a times hat u), with a the derivative wavenumber. The pass is alias-safe — a mode's reads precede its writes — so the destination may alias the source. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._curl_modes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._helmholtz_projection_modes!-Tuple{KernelAbstractions.CPU, Vararg{Any, 12}}","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_helmholtz_projection_modes!(backend, destination_1, destination_2, destination_3,\n                             source_1, source_2, source_3,\n                             wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3,\n                             kernel_data, α, β)\n\nApply the projector to every mode, dispatching on backend: a KernelAbstractions launch of _helmholtz_projection_kernel! on a device, and a gated loop over slabs on a CPU. The two arms carry the same per-mode arithmetic expression for expression, so a threaded run agrees with a device one bitwise — which is what the GPU parity suite compares. The kernel_data is a coefficientwise kernel's storage or nothing, and α/β arrive already converted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._helmholtz_projection_modes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._inverse_curl_modes!-NTuple{11, Any}","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_inverse_curl_modes!(backend, destination_1, destination_2, destination_3,\n                     source_1, source_2, source_3,\n                     wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3, imaginary_unit)\n\nApply the inverse curl to every mode, dispatching on backend: a KernelAbstractions launch of _inverse_curl_kernel! on a device, and a gated loop over slabs on a CPU. The two arms carry the same per-mode arithmetic expression for expression, so a threaded run agrees with a device one bitwise, which is what the GPU parity suite compares.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._inverse_curl_modes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._optional_kernel_value-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_optional_kernel_value(kernel_data, mode_1, mode_2, mode_3)\n\nRead the coefficientwise kernel value at one mode, or return the multiplicative identity true when no kernel is attached (kernel_data === nothing). Dispatch on Nothing lets one fused operator kernel serve both its plain and kernel-composed forms with the branch compiled away.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._optional_kernel_value"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._scalar_gradient_modes!-NTuple{9, Any}","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_scalar_gradient_modes!(backend, destination_1, destination_2, destination_3,\n                        source, wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3,\n                        imaginary_unit)\n\nRun the fused scalar-gradient pass over every mode: read the source coefficient once, write the three components of mathrmi a hat s, with a the derivative wavenumber. The generic method launches _scalar_gradient_kernel! on the backend; the CPU method runs the slab-threaded @simd loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._scalar_gradient_modes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._symmetric_gradient_modes!-Tuple{Any, NTuple{9, AbstractArray}, Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractArray, AbstractArray}, Vararg{Any, 5}}","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_symmetric_gradient_modes!(backend, destinations::NTuple{9}, sources::NTuple{3},\n                           wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3,\n                           imaginary_unit, half_imaginary)\n\nRun the fused symmetric-gradient pass over every mode: read the three source coefficients once, compute the six distinct components of tfracmathrmi2(a_j hat u_i + a_i hat u_j) once each, and write all nine entries. Destinations are ordered row-major, matching _tensor2_component_data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._symmetric_gradient_modes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/DifferentialOperators/#GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._vector_gradient_modes!-Tuple{Any, NTuple{9, AbstractArray}, Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractArray, AbstractArray}, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"DifferentialOperators","text":"_vector_gradient_modes!(backend, destinations::NTuple{9}, sources::NTuple{3},\n                        wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3, imaginary_unit)\n\nRun the fused vector-gradient pass over every mode: read the three source coefficients once, write the nine components of mathrmi a_j hat u_i, with a the derivative wavenumber. Destinations are ordered row-major, the first row's three before the second's, matching _tensor2_component_data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators._vector_gradient_modes!"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Running on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Running-on-a-GPU","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"The package runs on NVIDIA GPUs through CUDA and on AMD GPUs through AMDGPU, and depends on neither: add the one for your card to your own environment. The first section describes what holds on both vendors; each vendor section then starts with a complete example you can run as it stands.","title":"Running on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Both-vendors","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"","title":"Both vendors"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Fields-on-a-device","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"The kind of array holding a field's values is its storage family: Array on the host, CuArray on an NVIDIA GPU, ROCArray on an AMD GPU. Every field constructor accepts a family in place of an array and allocates uninitialized storage in it. Fields, their multipliers, kernels, point sets and transform plans move to another family with adapt, and adapt! copies into an existing field. Plans and multipliers built from device fields live there too.\n\nThe fields, multipliers and kernels one operation combines must share a storage family and an element type. Anything else throws an ArgumentError rather than being copied, so such an error usually means one object was left on the host.","title":"Fields on a device"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Writing-efficient-device-code","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"Four habits make the difference on either vendor:\n\nNever touch single elements. Reading or writing one element of a device array is a transfer between host and GPU. The package never does it, and CUDA.allowscalar(false) or AMDGPU.allowscalar(false) makes your own code throw where it would otherwise crawl.\nPreallocate, and call in-place operators. In-place ! operators other than norm! allocate no field-sized scratch, so a loop that calls only them needs no manual garbage collection. Allocating calls — to_spectral, lpnorm, radial_correlation — create a new device array each time.\nConsider Float32. It halves the memory of every field, which is usually what limits the grid on a GPU; Float64 works on both vendors.\nUse a GPU for large grids. Each GPU operation has a fixed start-up cost, so small grids gain little. Performance has timings for both vendors.","title":"Writing efficient device code"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Transforms-may-overwrite-their-input","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"Whether to_spectral! or to_physical! overwrites its input differs between transform libraries, so never rely on the input surviving. Use the allocating to_spectral or to_physical, or to_physical!(physical, spectral, plans, spectral_scratch), where spectral_scratch is a second field shaped like spectral and is overwritten instead.","title":"Transforms may overwrite their input"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#CUDA-(NVIDIA)","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"","title":"CUDA (NVIDIA)"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#A-first-run-on-CUDA","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"using CUDA, GBGPeriodicFields\nCUDA.functional() || error(\"no usable CUDA device\")\nCUDA.allowscalar(false)\n\nL = Float32(2π)\ngrid = Grid3D((256, 256, 256), (L, L, L))       # Float32 fields, set by the grid\nu = VectorFieldPhysical(CuArray, grid)          # uninitialized, on the GPU\nû = VectorFieldSpectral(CuArray, grid)\nplans = FieldTransformPlans(u, û)               # built from GPU fields, so on the GPU\nmultipliers = FieldSpectralMultipliers(û)\n\nθ = adapt(CuArray, ScalarFieldPhysical((x, y, z) -> sin(x), grid))  # host to GPU\nθ̂ = ScalarFieldSpectral(CuArray, grid)\nto_spectral!(θ̂, θ, plans)                       # a transform on the GPU\n∇θ̂ = gradient(θ̂, multipliers)                   # an operator on the GPU\nθ_host = adapt(Array, θ)                        # GPU to host\n\nThe package is tested on an RTX 5070 Ti. Transforms need the default RealFourierLayout, which the constructors above use when no layout is passed: cuFFT cannot plan one with its axes reordered. The turbulence simulation in the ROCm section has no CUDA version; its CPU version is in Examples.","title":"A first run on CUDA"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Fields-larger-than-GPU-memory","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"CUDA.jl can allocate every CuArray in unified memory, which the driver pages between the GPU and host RAM, so the package runs unchanged on fields that exceed the card, as long as the excess fits in host RAM. Turn it on once, from Julia started in the project the job runs in, then restart Julia:\n\nusing CUDA\nCUDA.CUDACore.Preferences.set_preferences!(CUDA.CUDACore,\n                                           \"default_memory\" => \"unified\"; force = true)\n\nThe call writes two entries, which can also be added by hand. The first goes in LocalPreferences.toml beside the project's Project.toml:\n\n[CUDACore]\ndefault_memory = \"unified\"\n\nThe second goes under the [extras] table of that Project.toml, created if it is absent. The call rewrites Project.toml to add it and drops any comments there, so add it by hand to keep them:\n\n[extras]\nCUDACore = \"bd0ed864-bdfe-4181-a5ed-ce625a5fdea2\"\n\nwarning: Without both entries nothing changes, and nothing says so\nSince CUDA.jl 6 this preference is read through its CUDACore subpackage, which Julia finds only when the project names it. A [CUDA] section, as CUDA.jl's own template shows, or a missing [extras] entry leaves every array in device memory without a warning.\n\nThe setting takes effect in a new Julia session, which recompiles CUDA.jl; the same call with \"device\" restores the default. The printed field says CuArray either way, so rerun the first example and check CUDA.is_unified(field_data(u)) on the array holding u's values: it returns true. The setting covers every array the process allocates, so keep it out of a shared environment such as @v1.12, which every project uses. Unified arrays also ignore allowscalar(false), so develop with device memory.\n\nOn a PCIe host with an RTX 5070 Ti, a 512^3 Float64 scalar transform round trip took about 8% longer in unified memory than in device memory while every field fit on the card, and about 2.9 times as long once the fields in use totalled 1.3 times the card's memory. Other access patterns and hosts, Grace Hopper included, are unmeasured; see Performance.","title":"Fields larger than GPU memory"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#ROCm-(AMD)","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"","title":"ROCm (AMD)"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#A-first-run-on-ROCm","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"using AMDGPU, GBGPeriodicFields\nAMDGPU.functional() || error(\"no usable ROCm device\")\nAMDGPU.allowscalar(false)\n\nL = Float32(2π)\ngrid = Grid3D((256, 256, 256), (L, L, L))       # Float32 fields, set by the grid\nu = VectorFieldPhysical(ROCArray, grid)         # uninitialized, on the GPU\nû = VectorFieldSpectral(ROCArray, grid)\nplans = FieldTransformPlans(u, û)               # built from GPU fields, so on the GPU\nmultipliers = FieldSpectralMultipliers(û)\n\nθ = adapt(ROCArray, ScalarFieldPhysical((x, y, z) -> sin(x), grid))  # host to GPU\nθ̂ = ScalarFieldSpectral(ROCArray, grid)\nto_spectral!(θ̂, θ, plans)                       # a transform on the GPU\n∇θ̂ = gradient(θ̂, multipliers)                   # an operator on the GPU\nθ_host = adapt(Array, θ)                        # GPU to host\n\nThe package is tested on an RX 7900 XTX. Transforms on a layout with its axes reordered are untested on ROCm, so use the default layout there too. AMDGPU.jl has no unified memory, so fields must fit on the card.","title":"A first run on ROCm"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Known-pitfalls-on-ROCm","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"warning: The first run after a reboot looks like a hang\nThe first GPU use after a reboot compiles kernels on the host for minutes, with no output, the GPU idle and the CPU busy. Let it finish: an interrupted compile is not saved, so the next run starts cold again. rocFFT also recompiles its own kernels in each new process unless ROCFFT_RTC_CACHE_PATH names a writable file for it to keep them in.\n\nOn the tested card the Float64 forms of radial_spectrum and its co-spectrum are exceptionally slow, about 48 times slower than an RTX 5070 Ti at 256^3 where Float32 is within about 10%, so a run that needs them is best done in Float32 throughout.","title":"Known pitfalls on ROCm"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#A-turbulence-simulation-on-ROCm","page":"Running on a GPU","text":"A forced isotropic-turbulence simulation runs on an AMD GPU in Float32, writing its results and figures to examples/gpu/rocm/output; the figures need a LaTeX installation. From the root of a clone of the repository:\n\njulia --project=examples/gpu/rocm -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=examples/gpu/rocm examples/gpu/rocm/HIST_ROCm.jl --smoke","title":"A turbulence simulation on ROCm"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-overview","page":"Overview","text":"The package is an umbrella module (GBGPeriodicFields) that re-exports a set of submodules. The include order reflects the dependency layering: FiberSections → Fields → FieldAlgebra → DifferentialOperators → Convolution → Points → Interpolation → Reductions.\n\nWhat each module is for is summarised on the Home page; the pages below are its reference.\n\nPages = [\n  \"api/GBGPeriodicFields.md\",\n  \"api/FiberSections.md\",\n  \"api/Fields.md\",\n  \"api/FieldAlgebra.md\",\n  \"api/DifferentialOperators.md\",\n  \"api/Convolution.md\",\n  \"api/Points.md\",\n  \"api/Interpolation.md\",\n  \"api/Reductions.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"API overview"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/","page":"Performance","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Performance-—-GBGPeriodicFields","page":"Performance","text":"This is the third rationale companion to ARCHITECTURE.md and TESTING.md: where those explain why the package is built as it is and how its correctness is checked, this one explains how performance is reasoned about, measured, and tracked. Read it before optimizing anything or adding a benchmark. The throughline is the same as for tests: a performance claim is only as good as the independent measurement behind it — and on the platform that matters.","title":"Performance — GBGPeriodicFields"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#1.-Philosophy","page":"Performance","text":"Memory-bound, GPU-first, measure-don't-guess. Four principles set everything below.\n\nThe baseline is memory bandwidth, not flops. Every operation here has low arithmetic intensity — a handful of floating-point operations per element moved (§2). So \"fast\" means \"moving the necessary bytes efficiently,\" and adding cores or tuning arithmetic helps only until the memory system saturates. The roofline ceiling for this package is the bandwidth roof, not the compute roof (roofline).\nGPU-first, and the CPU path targets a whole cluster node. Production runs on the GPU, and where an optimization helps one platform and hurts the other — which happens, and is proven below — the GPU wins across the range both can run. One carve-out, exercised once: the rule ranks platforms, not the particular card a number came from, so where the measured GPU disagrees with the production one the production target decides. §4(c) is that case — an option ≈6% slower in Float64 on the test card was kept, the reversal tracing to that card's Float64 throughput rather than to the code. A carve-out claimed without a measured reason the test hardware is unrepresentative would just be the rule being ignored. The CPU path is not, however, a convenience for toy problems. It carries two roles that must not be confused. Development and small tests run on the CPU because that is where a workstation is, and at those grids performance rarely decides anything. The role the CPU path is tuned for is the other one: a single large CPU node, running the resolutions that do not fit one GPU's memory. Distributed and multi-GPU execution are out of scope (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §1), so the ceiling is whatever one node holds — and on a CPU node that ceiling is high. A NAISS Arrhenius thin CPU node pairs two 128-core AMD EPYC 9755 processors with 768 GB of DDR5, a fat node carries 3 TB, and one GH200 in the GPU partition offers 96 GB of HBM. Counting one scalar field in either representation as 8 bytes per grid point in Float64 and a solver's live set at order 25 such fields, a single GH200 runs out near 768^3 while a thin CPU node reaches roughly 1536^3 and a fat node beyond it. The CPU therefore owns a band of resolutions no GPU's memory can hold, and inside that band it is the production platform, not a fallback. CPU multithreading (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §9) is the mechanism that band rests on.\nMeasure before you optimize, and measure on the production platform. No optimization is kept without a same-session side-by-side comparison showing it helps the GPU. This session's record is instructive: of the candidates that looked like wins, polynomial-eval tuning was already free, the periodic-wrap hoist made the default scheme slower, and point sorting — the textbook locality optimization — regressed the GPU gather. Only the one the measurement pointed at (loop order) survived (§4). The lesson is the method, not those verdicts: both negative results were re-measured on current hardware in 2026-08-07 and reversed sign for the default scheme — which is the same principle applied twice, since a verdict is only ever about the platform it was taken on (§4(c)).\nA benchmark run owns the machine, and a number is not a result until it is explained. Two obligations, both learned the hard way. Run benchmarks alone — no test suite, no documentation build, no second lane, nothing else on the host, and especially not while a GPU sweep is in flight. A device benchmark is host-bound at small grids (the synchronisation and, on the GPU path, the per-sample GC), so competing work inflates it by a roughly fixed amount per case; that alone invented a 2.2× \"regression\" at 64^3, an inflated canary baseline (the frozen per-host regression-guard suite benchmark/regress.jl judges against; §7), and a phantom \"environment shift\" attributed to a driver, all in one session. Check uptime and the GPU's utilisation before starting, and wait rather than measure into a busy box. Then read what came back. Running a sweep and reporting that it completed is not a measurement: compare against the previous archive\n\n(the saved per-sweep result file a characterization run writes; §6),   state which operations moved and in which direction, and account for every systematic movement   before naming a cause. The cheapest discriminator is whether a penalty is additive or   proportional — a fixed offset that is constant in absolute time while its ratio collapses with   grid size is overhead, not arithmetic — and the second is whether the same commit range moved the   other backend, since a real code regression is not confined to one device.\n\nA scope note carried from the architecture: platform optima diverge, so every result below is reported per platform, never as a single number. (The L²/measure reductions — integral, inner, l2norm, mean, …; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md decision 18 — are bandwidth-bound streaming reductions whose only subtlety is floating-point summation order, tolerance-tested rather than bitwise; they are now part of the suite, §5.)","title":"1. Philosophy"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#2.-The-performance-model","page":"Performance","text":"What bounds each public operation class — the mental model a benchmark either confirms or refutes.\n\nFFT transforms (to_physical/to_spectral) → bound by FFTW (CPU) / rocFFT/cuFFT (GPU). The package adds only the normalization convention and the fiber-last layout (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §3–4); the transform itself is the library's. Not something we tune — but the dominant cost of any spectral step, so it is benchmarked to track the libraries and our plan/layout overhead. The GPU libraries have no FFTW-style planner search, and the tuning surfaces they do have are currently out of reach from Julia — recorded here so nobody re-discovers them: cuFFT offers a patient-JIT plan property (LTO-finalized kernels — slower planning for faster execution) and LTO load/store callbacks that fuse user ops into the transform's own passes, none of it wrapped by CUDA.jl's FFT interface (cuFFT docs); rocFFT has an offline tuner producing per-size/per-architecture solution maps, not surfaced by AMDGPU.jl. The one reachable lever is rocFFT's ROCFFT_RTC_CACHE_PATH kernel cache (an environment variable; see §7 gap 6).\nField algebra (M2 + M3), the differential operators, and convolution → memory-bandwidth-bound. A fiberwise add, contraction, wavenumber multiply, kernel multiply (convolve), or arbitrary fiber map (map! — its adapter assembles the fiber in registers, adding no memory traffic over the named ops, so it tracks det!) reads and writes O(1) numbers per grid point and does O(1) flops on them — arithmetic intensity well below the roofline ridge. Consequence: on CPU, thread scaling saturates once the cores jointly saturate DRAM bandwidth (the plateau in §4); on GPU, the figure of merit is achieved bandwidth, not occupancy.\nThe grid↔point transfer (interpolate/anterpolate), local-stencil spline schemes → the subtle one. Each point gathers (or scatters) a (2n+2)^3 stencil (n the spline half-width) with data-dependent indices, so it is bound by irregular memory access, and the binding differs by platform:\nGPU: maximize memory-level parallelism — many distinct cache lines and DRAM banks in flight at once to hide latency (coalescing/occupancy). Scattered (unsorted) points naturally spread access and feed that parallelism. The corollary once drawn here — that the field is largely cache-resident at the grids we run, so little locality is left to win — is FALSIFIED. It reasoned from a retired card's 96 MB against a 67 MB 256^3 scalar Float32 field; decision 78 then won 306times by reordering for locality on that very grid (finding (ad)). A consumer's fields are often Float64 or rank-1, several times 67 MB, and no current card carries 96 MB.\nCPU: bound by the cache hierarchy + hardware prefetchers, which reward locality — the opposite pull.\nThis single fact — GPU wants spread, CPU wants locality — explains the platform-divergent results in §4 (loop order, sorting) and is the most important thing to internalize before touching the transfer. Hold it as a mechanism rather than a verdict: it explains why sorting can fail to pay on a GPU, and on the card that recorded that result it did. On the RX 7900 XTX the same experiment reversed for the default scheme (§4(c)), so the balance between the two pulls is a property of the card, not a constant.\nDeliberately unbenchmarked, so the absence reads as a decision rather than a gap. Two public surfaces carry no row on purpose. The allocating constructors (decision 74) have none because an allocator's cost is the backend allocator's, and finding (y) records what a per-call field-sized allocation does to a row: it lands in a process-history-selected regime and stops being comparable across process shapes, so the row would measure the harness's memory history. Scheme persistence (decision 75) has none because it writes four HDF5 attributes and there is no persistence class to put it in — inventing one to time a constant would measure the library rather than the package.\nBase-space motion (decision 73) → four rows in the transfer class (displace! one- and three-term, the three-term torus arity, and wrap!): O(M) streaming over (M 3) arrays, bandwidth-trivial beside a gather — 30–300× cheaper than the CatmullRom gather at the same point count, which is the boundary-costs-nothing claim the rows document. The fused wrap is free in traffic (the torus row's counts equal the cover row's, pinned by the pass audit) but not in serial arithmetic: floating-point mod is compute-visible where one core does all the work (256^3-derived points, Float64: torus 7.0 against cover 2.2 ms at t1) and vanishes into bandwidth where the pass is memory-bound (1.41 against 1.24 ms at t8; 91.5 against 64.6 µs on the RX 7900 XTX). The fusion itself pays exactly there: at t8 the fused torus pass beats cover-then-wrap! by 33% (1.41 against 2.12 ms), and on the device by 22%.\nThe spectral transfer (DirectDFT/NUFFT) → a different cost class. DirectDFT is the exact O(N M) double sum (compute-bound, no FFT, no window) — the oracle; NUFFT is its O(Nlog N + M) fast form, an oversampled FFT plus a windowed spread/gather, so it is FFT-bound in the grid size N and carries the same irregular-access spread/gather as the splines in the point count M.","title":"2. The performance model"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#3.-Best-practices","page":"Performance","text":"Short checklists, each grounded in an external reference. Most are already enforced by the architecture or the tests; they are collected here so an optimizer has them in one place.\n\nJulia (Julia Performance Tips; Modern Julia Workflows):\n\nType stability — non-concrete types force boxing and allocation. Checked package-wide by test/jet.jl (JET.@test_call).\nZero allocations in hot paths — an unexpected allocation almost always signals a type problem. Prefer the in-place ! forms; allocate scratch once and reuse. (@allocated/BenchmarkTools report it.)\n@inbounds in kernels, isbits / SVector kernel arguments (so the spline coefficient matrices pass into GPU kernels by value, no device array), and fused broadcasts through _assign! rather than a bare .= (the codegen + threading contract, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §9).\nAllocate via similar/similar_field so results inherit the input's backend (the one-line reason a CPU-written method runs on a ROCArray unchanged).\n\nGPU (ROCm/HIP performance guidelines):\n\nNo scalar indexing of device arrays (invariant 6) — checked under allowscalar(false) in both the test and benchmark GPU runs, but only when those are run: they need a physical device, they are opt-in, they are not part of Pkg.test(), and CI is manual-only. It is a guarantee the design makes and a check that confirms it on demand — not a gate a regression cannot get past. A scalar index introduced during CPU-only work stands until someone runs the GPU suite.\nFavour spread access for memory-level parallelism, and loop the contiguous (x) axis innermost so per-thread stencil access is stride-1 (§4 measured this at up to 3.9×).\nMinimize host↔device transfer; keep fields resident on the device. Allocate once, operate in place.\n\nMethodology (BenchmarkTools.jl manual):\n\nWarm up (compile) before timing; synchronize the backend — GPU work is asynchronous, so an un-synced timer measures launch latency, not the kernel (the suite's bench_sync).\nReport the minimum of many samples (least-noise estimator) alongside the median.\nSide-by-side comparison in one session: build both variants (baseline and candidate) and time them on identical data, back-to-back, in a single process — the only reliable way to resolve a difference smaller than the ~10% run-to-run variance (separate launches drift with thermal state, allocation layout, and cache).\nControl the thread count (-t1 vs -t4), and always confirm on the GPU before keeping an optimization (platform divergence is real, §4).","title":"3. Best practices"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#4.-What-we-have-found","page":"Performance","text":"The transfer-layer results below were measured on an Intel i7-12700K (8 P-cores) and on a since-retired AMD card, Float32, ~1 point per 27 grid cells. That card is gone from the fleet and from RESULTS.md; what is kept here is the qualitative finding in each case, not its absolute numbers, and anything re-measured on the current cards (RX 7900 XTX, RTX 5070 Ti) says so explicitly. These are the cumulative results of the transfer-layer optimization work; the benchmark suite (§5) regenerates the tracked subset on demand.\n\n(a) Memory-bound, and smoothness is free. Interpolation runs at ~12% of peak bandwidth at 256³; cost scales with the stencil volume (2n+2)^3, and the spline degree (m) is invisible — the weight polynomials hide under memory latency. So pick the smoothness for accuracy, never for speed.\n\ngather scheme stencil GPU 256³ (621k pts) throughput\nLalescuSpline(0,0) (trilinear) 2³ = 8 0.51 ms 1230 Mpts/s\nLalescuSpline(1,1) (Catmull–Rom) 4³ = 64 3.55 ms 175 Mpts/s\nLalescuSpline(3,m) (Lalescu 8-pt) 8³ = 512 28.0 ms (flat in m) 22 Mpts/s\n\n(b) Loop order — contiguous axis innermost (KEPT). Iterating the contiguous x axis innermost (stride-1 stencil access) vs z-innermost (stride N_x N_y, the grid's x,y dimensions):\n\n GPU 256³ GPU 512³ (Lalescu) CPU (Catmull–Rom / Lalescu)\nspeedup (x-inner / z-inner) 1.6–1.7× 3.9× (785→203 ms) 0.75× (i7-12700K, 2026-06 — superseded, see below)\n\nKept x-inner as a GPU-first choice — the CPU's large caches absorb the strided access and its z-inner form vectorizes better, but the GPU is the production platform. Re-measured (2026-07-22), and x-inner now stands on CPU evidence too: decision 37 reopened this tiebreak because its cache justification dies in the CPU production band, so the A/B was repeated at 512³ Float64 (≈5×10⁶ points, the harness's one-per-27-cells density, Ryzen 9800X3D) — and the old penalty does not reproduce there. The z-inner nest gains only ~9–10% on the 4³ Catmull–Rom stencil and loses 23% (serial; 5% at eight threads) on the 8³ Lalescu stencil, whose per-point working set no longer fits the reuse the old measurement enjoyed. A per-backend loop order would also break gather bit-identity across backends for at most a single-digit win on one scheme; the split is rejected and the x-inner nest is the recorded choice for both platforms.\n\n(c) Rejected by measurement. Two \"obvious\" optimizations that did not survive:\n\nWrap-hoist (precompute the 3(2n+2) periodic node offsets to kill inner-loop mods): bit-identical, but regressed the default Catmull–Rom by 10–14% — the extra registers cut occupancy. Proof the kernels are memory/occupancy-bound, not ALU-bound. RE-MEASURED 2026-08-07 and REOPENED: the sign is reversed for the default scheme, and the CPU was never tested. Two kernels differing only in where the wrap is computed, verified bitwise equal, ratios hoisted-over-base:\n\n(a table cannot render inside a list item, so the ratios are listed by scheme, in grid order   64^3, 128^3, 256^3, 512^3):\n\nGPU trilinear — 0.969, 0.990, 0.978, 0.992\nGPU CatmullRom — 0.698, 0.713, 0.829, 0.895\nGPU Lalescu(3,3) — 0.516, 1.122, 2.017, (not run)\nCPU trilinear — 0.897, 0.843, 0.884, 0.843\nCPU CatmullRom — 0.368, 0.433, 0.584, 0.607\nCPU Lalescu(3,3) — 0.276, 0.297, 0.359, (not run)\n\nThree things follow. The recorded occupancy mechanism is right, and R = 3 on the GPU is where   it shows: 24 registers per thread turns a 2times win at 64^3 into a 2times loss at   256^3 as the working set grows and occupancy becomes the binding constraint. But it does not   bite the default scheme on RDNA3 — CatmullRom hoisted is 10–30% faster at every grid, where the   the retired AMD card recorded 10–14% slower. And the CPU, never measured for this, wins hugely and   everywhere — up to 36times — which is what the mechanism predicts: a CPU thread has no   occupancy to lose, and mod by a runtime grid size is a genuine integer division rather than a   compiler-strength-reduced constant. That matters because the CPU path carries the beyond-VRAM grids   (ARCHITECTURE.md decision 37). On both backends the benefit shrinks as the grid grows and the   gather becomes more latency-bound, converging toward 1 without crossing it for W le 4.\n\nReopened, not adopted, and the caveat is load-bearing: these are standalone kernels sharing the   package's loop order but not its spline weights, and the hoist is written with ntuple, which need   not compile like the original attempt did. The ratios establish that the trade is worth re-examining   on current hardware, not what the package's own kernel would measure.\n\nMorton particle sorting (the textbook GPU-locality optimization): recorded as 0.5–1.0× on GPU (gather) — concentrating access kills the memory-level/DRAM-bank parallelism the GPU needs, and the field is cache-resident anyway — though 2.2–4.4× on CPU. That gather result does NOT reproduce on the RX 7900 XTX (re-measured 2026-08-07), and the question is reopened. Identical points, reordered only, at the same 1-per-27 density: Morton reaches 1.43× (Float32 128^3 CatmullRom, 105.7 → 73.6 µs) and 1.40× (Float32 256^3 trilinear, 116.8 → 83.4 µs), and helps in three of four cells for CatmullRom — the default scheme. One cell clearly loses, Float64 256^3 trilinear at 074times. The original was measured on a since-retired card, so this is plausibly an RDNA2-versus-RDNA3 difference rather than a bad measurement; either way the recorded range no longer describes the hardware the project runs on. It is reopened, not overturned, and three things must hold before it is adopted: the ordering must be rebuilt as points move (measured host cost 31.7 ms at 256^3, paying back after 377 gathers; an on-device radix sort at an estimated 1–2 ms would pay back after 12–24, which is the number that matters and is not yet measured), the benchmark compares perfectly-sorted against perfectly-random while a real point set drifts between the two, and the companion scatter result below is unaffected and remains the larger objection. The companion atomic-free node-centric scatter is correct, conserving and bit-deterministic, but 6–24× slower on GPU: at our sparse 1-per-27 density it traverses ~27× more (empty) reverse-stencil cells than the atomic scatter touches particles. Sorting + node-centric deposition is a dense-particle (PIC) technique; wrong for this regime. (If deterministic anterpolation ever becomes a hard requirement, it is the available — slow-at-sparse — answer.)\nStoring the Helmholtz denominator as a precomputed derivative-squared wavenumber family (rather than squaring a in-register — decision 42 dropped exactly these arrays, and after decision 47 restored the gated CPU loop the question was whether to bring them back). Measured head-to-head, in-register vs a stored a^2 array, same projection otherwise: CPU in-register wins everywhere — Float64 +4 (128^3/256^3), Float32 +10 — because a register multiply of the already-loaded a beats an extra stride-1 load, and the stored form keeps three more arrays resident. On the GPU in-register ties in Float32 but is approx 6 slower in Float64 — and that lone reversal is a test-hardware artifact, not a production signal. The kernel is memory-bound (the F64/F32 time ratio is approx 21times at 256^3, tracking the 2times byte ratio, not any compute ratio); the reason dropping three squaring-multiplies helps in F64 is that the RTX 5070 Ti's consumer-Blackwell FP64 runs at sim 164 rate, so its FP64 ALU becomes co-limiting alongside memory and the extra multiplies tip it over. The production GPU is GH200 (decision 37 — Grace-Hopper, full-rate 12 FP64), where that ALU has sim 32times the F64 throughput and cannot be co-limiting on a memory-bound kernel — so the F64 case is expected to behave like F32 (a tie), and the stored array's extra reads to help nowhere. That is an inference from the memory-bound measurement and the known FP64 rates, not measured on a GH200 (none on hand). Even taking the 6 at face value, capturing it needs a per-backend kernel split — re-introducing the two-bodies-in-sync coupling that produced the decision-47 dead-loop bug — for a win seen only on a card the production runs will not use. Kept in-register; the arrays stay dropped. This also retires the original (wrong) hypothesis that decision 42's in-register arithmetic cost the regression the canary flagged — it did not; the dead loop did (decision 47), and the arithmetic is free-to-favourable on every path that matters.\n\n(d) Anterpolation and contention. The scatter costs ≈2.2–2.7× the gather (atomic read-modify-write + a full-grid prescale). On that RDNA2 card Float32 atomic-add was native but Float64 is emulated (CAS loop) ⇒ ≈1.3–1.7× slower; contention is worst at moderate clustering (~2 pts/cell), not extreme (extreme stays cache-hot).\n\n(e) CPU vs GPU. The transfer threads on CPU through KernelAbstractions' CPU() backend with no code change, scaling ≈4.5–5.7× on 8 threads before the bandwidth plateau; the GPU is ≈9–22× the 8-thread CPU (≈ the 384-vs-~60 GB/s bandwidth ratio for the heavy schemes, more for the light ones via parallelism).\n\n(f) Allocation-free in-place operators — the GPU hot-loop discipline. A pseudospectral solver applies the differential operators thousands of times in its time loop, so any per-call scratch an operator allocates internally matters. On the GPU it matters a lot: such scratch escapes Julia's GC (which triggers on host memory, while the scratch is device memory), so the device pool grows every step until VRAM fills and the run wedges (allocator spinning, GPU idle) or hipMalloc aborts — observed at 192³/256³. The fix made the in-place differential operators allocate nothing: _squared_wavenumber_broadcast (the k^2 coordinate) is returned lazily (an unfused Broadcasted, no O(N^3) array), and the helmholtz_projection! / laplacian! / inverse_laplacian! kernels fuse their former temporaries into the component broadcasts — each now allocates sim 0 bytes in place (measured; guarded by test/allocations.jl), with identical results. Payoff: a preallocated, in-place pseudospectral DNS step allocates nothing per step → no manual GC.gc()/reclaim, flat VRAM. The forced-HIT example (examples/gpu/rocm/HIST_ROCm.jl, 192³ Float32, mathrmRe_lambda approx 93) sustains ≈ 34 ms/step (8000 steps, ≈ 270 s) on the retired card this way. Discipline for consumers: preallocate a workspace and use the ! forms with a destination distinct from the sources; the fiber norm/lpnorm deliberately materialize one field and are the exceptions (dev! left the list when decision 39 fused its trace into a register; use maximum(abs, …) for a CFL speed). [Decision 24; decisions 38/39 later retired the alias-guard copies — aliased in-place calls are now computed directly and stay zero-byte — and the decision-40 threaded reductions run on the same audit.]\n\n(g) Allocation-bounded radial diagnostics — the same GC trap, in the binning. The same device-GC hazard as (f) lived in the diagnostics. The radial_spectrum / radial_correlation reductions binned by looping over the k- (or r-) shells and reducing a masked broadcast per shell. The shell count scales with the grid (approx sqrt3 N_a2, approx 220 at N_a = 256, with N_a the per-axis point count), and each iteration allocates a full-grid mask — and, because a mapreduce over a Broadcasted materializes on this GPU stack rather than fusing, the masked product too: O(N) full-grid temporaries per call, which the host-triggered GC does not reclaim. A single 256^3 radial_correlation snapshot churned several GB and wedged a 12 GB card at the first snapshot. The fix bins in a single scatter-add pass — one portable KernelAbstractions + Atomix histogram kernel atomically accumulating each element into its shell — so per-call temporaries drop from O(N) to O(1). Correctness is unchanged (same per-shell sums; CPU test/reductions.jl + GPU parity hold). Payoff: the 256^3 Float32 forced-HIT example (approx 87 ms/step) now runs to completion at a flat sim6 GB (approx 8 GB peak) through its snapshots, where the per-shell form wedged at the first one. The scatter contends on the small accumulator set, acceptable for an occasional diagnostic (not a per-step call); a privatized histogram is a possible future optimization. [Decision 25.] Decision 34 later fused the remaining O(1) staging — the shell-index, magnitude, and weighted planes — into the binning kernels themselves (shell, fiber contraction, and Parseval weight derived per element from the index; the shell count analytic from the corner), taking radial_spectrum from three staged spectral planes per call to its sim14 KB accumulator and leaving radial_correlation only its substance (the power field and the allocating inverse transform).\n\n(h) FFTW ESTIMATE can badly mis-plan single-precision transforms — pass MEASURE for production. FieldTransformPlans builds its FFTW plans with the default ESTIMATE flag (instant, but it guesses the algorithm instead of timing candidates). On a Ryzen 9800X3D (Zen 5, stock FFTW 3.3.11) that guess is ~5× too slow for the 256³ Float32 transforms — in both directions, at every thread count — because its hardcoded single-precision codelet heuristics misjudge this CPU generation. Rebuilding with FFTW.MEASURE (a one-time, timed plan search) fixes it, and the planning cost (seconds) amortizes over a long run. Pure in-place transform, 256³ Float32, minimum time:\n\nthreads c2r (to_physical!), ESTIMATE→MEASURE r2c (to_spectral!), ESTIMATE→MEASURE\n1 170 → 33.3 ms (5.1×) 168 → 33.6 ms (5.0×)\n4 47.3 → 14.0 ms (3.4×) 46.3 → 12.9 ms (3.6×)\n8 27.5 → 12.0 ms (2.3×) 26.2 → 10.2 ms (2.6×)\n\nThe gain narrows as threads are added — the planner's advantage is largest where a single core is doing all of the work — so the headline is the single-threaded figure. (Read from the current RESULTS.md rather than re-measured for this table. The earlier printed figures were a one-off snapshot whose t4/t8 MEASURE times ran 35–75% faster than anything in the committed archive, and the surrounding prose promises this table tracks the live record.)\n\nIt is size- and CPU-specific (128³ is fine; the i7-12700K shows no such inversion) — a classic ESTIMATE pitfall, not a package issue.\n\nThe inversion is Zen 5's; the mis-planning is not — corrected 2026-08-23 from a corpus-wide sweep of the committed table. Reading ESTIMATE/MEASURE as a ratio rather than looking for a Float32-slower-than-Float64 ordering, the i7-12700K mis-plans single precision too, at 256^3/t1: 2.13× (to_physical scalar), 2.40× (to_spectral scalar) and 2.10× (to_physical vector), against 517/507/410 on the 9800X3D. Double precision is 120–146 on the i7 and 115–283 on the Ryzen. So the effect is precision-specific on both microarchitectures and merely three times worse on Zen 5. What is genuinely Zen-5-only is that it is severe enough to flip the ordering — on the i7 the ESTIMATE Float32 transform is still faster than its Float64 twin (880 against 1273 ms), so nothing looks wrong unless the measure twin is consulted, which is exactly why the narrower attribution was recorded. The sentence above stays true as written about the inversion; what changes is that \"misjudge this CPU generation\" must not be read as \"and only that generation\", and a consumer on any x86 CPU doing long single-precision runs should pass MEASURE.\n\nThis widens rather than narrows the ARM concern in §7 gap 8, which cites this finding as a Zen 5 artifact among the reasons no x86 CPU result transfers to aarch64. An ESTIMATE heuristic that mis-plans single precision on two unrelated x86 microarchitectures is more likely to do so on a third platform, not less — so MEASURE should be in the first Arrhenius measurement, not discovered there. The default stays ESTIMATE (cheap, deterministic, right for tests and short runs); the escape hatch already exists, FieldTransformPlans(phys, spec; fft_flags = FFTW.MEASURE) (or FFTW.PATIENT). MEASURE overwrites its input arrays while planning, so build the plans on scratch fields before seeding data. The forced-HIT CPU example (examples/HIST_CPU.jl) now plans with MEASURE. Update (2026-07-21, the 512³ sweep): the mis-plan persists into the CPU production band — at 512³ the ESTIMATEd scalar Float32 c2r ties the Float64 one to three digits (402.297 vs 402.195 ms, t8), single precision buying nothing — so the RESULTS.md 9800X3D Float32 FFT columns at 256³–512³ should be read as ESTIMATE-planned (the package default, deliberately what the suite measures), not as the CPU's Float32 capability. Update (2026-07-22): the pair is now a standing measurement — every CPU FFT row in the suite carries a measure twin planned with FFTW.MEASURE (built on scratch fields, per the overwrite caveat above), so RESULTS.md shows the ESTIMATE experience and the tuned production path side by side and this finding's delta is re-measured per host, size, and type on every sweep. GPU rows have no twin — the device FFT libraries take no planner flag. First sweep with the twins (12700K): even this host — no Float32/Float64 inversion — leaves 1.7–1.8× to the planner at 256^3 Float32 single-threaded (1.2–1.4× elsewhere at t1), while at t8 the pair is within jitter: the planner choice matters most exactly where a long serial production run lives, and threading largely hides it.\n\n(i) A fused reduction needs a different shape per backend — get that right and it wins on both. The mapped forms (decision 30) reduce a function of the fiber value at every base point without materializing it. A single formulation cannot serve both devices, and the first implementation shipped the GPU's shape to the CPU, where it ran slower than the materializing route it was meant to replace. Splitting it fixes that. Measured with the same map on both sides (mean(det, A) against mean(det(A)), N = 128, Float64):\n\nbackend fused materialize + reduce \nCPU (vectorized blocked loop) 5.1 ms, 0 B 13.0 ms, 16.8 MB fused 2.5× faster\nGPU, RX 7900 XTX (fused Broadcasted) 0.55 ms, 0 B 0.69 ms, 16.8 MB fused 1.3× faster\n\nDecomposing the CPU side shows why one shape could not do both, and it is not the layout:\n\nstep N = 128, Float64, 1 thread factor\nmaximum over a contiguous array 0.56 ms baseline\nreducing a lazy Broadcasted instead (one stream, no gather) 3.1 ms 5.6×\nthree strided streams instead of one 6.4 ms 2×\nbuilding the StaticArray per base point 5.6–6.4 ms free\nLinearAlgebra.norm instead of naive sqrtsum_i u_i^2 27.7 ms 4.3×\n\nThe dominant term is the reduction losing its vectorized blocked loop, which costs more than the extra section of traffic that materializing pays — so on a CPU the fix is simply not to reduce a lazy expression: the implementation runs its own blocked loop over the component arrays instead. A GPU has no such loop to forfeit (thread-parallel, bandwidth-bound), so index arithmetic hides behind memory latency and the skipped section write and read-back is pure profit; it keeps the fused Broadcasted. Blocking the CPU loop pairwise, as Base's own reduction does, additionally makes it the most accurate route (relative error 12times10^-16, against 37times10^-15 for the lazy form and 16times10^-16 for materializing). The strided gather that fiber-last layout (invariant 3) forces contributes only 2times, and the StaticArray build is free.\n\nThe last row is a measurement trap worth recording: LinearAlgebra.norm on a fiber uses the overflow-safe scaled 2-norm, 43times the plain magnitude the fieldwise norm evaluates. So comparing maximum(norm, u) against lpnorm(u, Inf) varies the algorithm and the route together and reads as a fusion penalty that is not the fusion — given the same formula the fused form is 1.8 ms against 4.2 ms. This analysis reported the confounded number twice before decomposing it. Vary one thing at a time here.\n\nThree formulations that look equivalent are not, and each silently reintroduces allocation: a multi-argument mapreduce (fused by GPUArrays, but materializing a full section on the CPU), an anonymous vararg closure (uninferrable component count), and a forwarded Function-typed argument left untyped in the signature (Julia declines to specialize on it). All three are pinned at zero bytes by test/allocations.jl. [Decision 30.]\n\n(j) A multi-output kernel beats broadcast recomputation — the one-pass Helmholtz projection. The projection writes three output components that share one intermediate (the longitudinal factor a_jhat u_j(a cdot a), with a the derivative wavenumber — decision 42's exact denominator), which a broadcast cannot express: the three-broadcast form recomputed the shared dot product per component — nine component reads and three divisions per mode for three writes. The one-pass KernelAbstractions kernel (decision 32) reads each source component once, computes the factor once, and writes all three components — and, reading a whole mode before writing it, serves the aliased in-place call with no guard temporary. Measured on the 12700K (64^3–128^3, Float64; the fused-vs-three-broadcast ratio), against the KA-on-CPU path this operation ran before decision 40 replaced it with a gated CPU loop of the same shape (decision 42 briefly severed that loop and decision 47 restored it — see the canary lane, §7 gap 3):\n\nthreads 64^3 128^3\n1 3.9× 4.1×\n4 6.5× 2.7×\n\nTwo costs worth recording. The KA launch allocates a small size-independent host-side bookkeeping (~1.5 KB serial, ~7 KB at four threads — task spawning), where the broadcast route allocated ~0.3 KB; the allocation audit's fraction-of-a-field threshold absorbs it, and the field-sized guard temporary of the aliased call (~0.84 MB at the audit size, IT's 3.19-unit transient at 256^3) is what is gone. And the kernel-composed form (helmholtz_projection!(…, kernel), decision 32) adds one scalar-field read to the same pass — measured +6–16% over the plain projection, against ~+50% for the separate convolve! pass it replaces.\n\n(k) A lazy kernel is a reuse question, not a free win — and the naive A/B forgets the build pass. The exponential family (decision 33) evaluates exp(ck^2) per mode instead of reading a stored kernel field. Whether that pays depends on what the stored route would actually do:\n\ncomparison (vector field) CPU 128^3 F64, 4 threads GPU 256^3 F32, RX 7900 XTX\nlazy vs stored kernel, kernel fixed 9.1 vs 5.4 ms — stored 1.7× 0.94 vs 0.87 ms — stored 1.08×\nlazy vs rebuild + apply, exponent varies comparable (the rebuild pays the same exp) 0.94 vs 0.46 + 0.87 ms — lazy 1.4×\ntwo-operand fused vs sequential combine! + convolve! 9.9 vs 11.3 ms — fused 1.14× 1.11 vs 1.68 ms — fused 1.5×\nkerneled combine! vs the sequential pair 7.4 vs 11.3 ms — fused 1.5× 1.13 vs 1.68 ms — fused 1.5×\n\nThree lessons. The fusion forms (the bottom two rows) win on both backends — they save a destination write and re-read, which no amount of exp cost eats. The lazy-vs-stored single-operand trade is governed by reuse: a fixed kernel amortizes its build and then wins on pure bandwidth (the per-mode exponential costs more than the field read it saves — dramatically so on CPU, where exp is expensive and the broadcast re-evaluates it once per fiber component); a varying exponent forces the stored route to pay a full build pass per change, and the lazy form then wins. And the measurement trap: an A/B of exponential_convolve! against a prebuilt convolve! silently compares against a route whose build pass happened outside the timer — state which reuse pattern is being measured before quoting a ratio. A possible future optimization — hoisting the exponential over the fiber in a mode-loop kernel, the decision-32 shape — would cut the CPU vector case's threefold exp re-evaluation; it waits for a CPU-bound consumer (the audits' consumer runs GPU-first). That deferral predates decision 37 and should be read against it: \"GPU-first\" no longer settles a CPU cost by itself, since the CPU path's tuning target is now a full cluster node running grids past one GPU's memory. A threefold exp re-evaluation per fiber is exactly the kind of per-component redundant work that is nearly free while a field is cache-resident and is not once it is not. The item stays deferred for want of a consumer, not because the cost is deemed unimportant — and it wants a node-scale CPU measurement before it is ranked.\n\n(l) The threaded _assign! inner loop runs at ~8.6 GB/s per thread — mid thread counts sit above serial on the streaming ops. Found by the first 512³ CPU sweep (2026-07-21) and visible, unnoticed, in every committed CPU column before it: on both measured CPUs, at every grid size, add!/ gradient!/symmetric_gradient! and the rest of the _assign!-routed streaming family run slower at 2 and 4 threads than at 1, with 8 threads only marginally recovering (9800X3D add! scalar Float64, t1→t2→t4→t8: 64³ 40 µs → 368 → 185 → 95; 512³ 83.9 ms → 187.6 → 94.2 → 73.6). The serial path is materialize! — SIMD, ~41 GB/s on one Zen 5 core, already near the desktop's DRAM ceiling — while the threaded path (_assign_threaded!) indexes the fused Broadcasted element by element through a CartesianIndex, which does not vectorize: measured single-threaded at 512³, 375 ms against materialize!'s 79 ms, a 4.7× per-core penalty. Every threaded lane is that loop divided by its thread count until the aggregate hits DRAM (375/2 = 187.5, 375/4 = 93.8 — reproducing the sweep cells to three digits). Two corollaries, both measured: linear indexing plus @simd — the alternative the implementation's own comment contemplates, legal now that the compat floor is 1.12 — does not help (365 ms; Broadcasted getindex stays scalar either way), so the fix is not an index-style swap but per-chunk materialize! over sliced broadcast arguments; and the ops that thread through other routes are unaffected (FFTW threads itself; the KernelAbstractions kernels partition natively — anterpolate! scales 3.4 s → 562 ms, t1→t8, in the same sweep). Consequences while the fix is pending: on a 2-channel desktop, JULIA_NUM_THREADS=1 is the fastest CPU configuration for the streaming ops below ~256³ and within ~15% of t8 at 512³; on the many-channel node of decision 37 (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md), where serial is ~1/12 of socket bandwidth, the slow loop still saturates the socket eventually but wastes ~4.7× the cores doing it. The threading tests are unaffected (bitwise identity holds — the loop is slow, not wrong). Fixed (2026-07-22, decision 38): the threaded path now runs the standard fused materialize! per contiguous slab of the largest axis, so each slab executes the same SIMD kernel as serial — measured on the probe, 512³ Float64 add!: t2 187.7 → 73.3 ms (the DRAM ceiling, previously reached only at t8), t8 unchanged at the ceiling, and 64³ t8 95 → 28 µs (threading now wins at small sizes). Both hosts' CPU columns in RESULTS.md are re-swept post-fix (Enteropia and Aiporetne, 2026-07-22); pre-fix t2–t8 numbers survive only in superseded archives and measure the retired loop.\n\n(m) exp producing a Float32 subnormal costs ~16× per element — and a fixed benchmark exponent measured exactly that. On x86 an arithmetic result in the subnormal band — between the smallest normal approx 12times10^-38 and the underflow-to-zero point approx 14times10^-45 for Float32 — is produced through a microcode assist: measured 37.1 ns/element, against 2.4 for a normal result and 1.2 for a full underflow to exact zero. For the lazy kernel exp(clvert krvert^2) the affected arguments, clvert krvert^2 in -103 -87, form a spherical shell in k-space; the benchmark's former fixed c = -01 put ~11% of a 64^3 grid's modes inside it, inflating the Float32 exponential_convolve! cell to 2.7–3.1× its Float64 twin (the blend 1 + 011 times 15 approx 26; the sublinear 64^3 to 128^3 size step was the tell), so the row measured libm underflow handling, not the operation. The benchmark coefficient is now scale-aware — c = -4lvert krvert^2_mathrmcorner, the kernel spanning e^-4 1 at every size — verified to close the 64^3 type gap from 3.0× to 1.05–1.08×; cells swept before 2026-07-23 carry the old exponent. The operation keeps exact semantics (no flush-to-zero guard): Float64's band (arguments below approx -708) is unreachable by physical kernels, GPUs flush subnormals natively, and a Float32 CPU consumer with a genuinely deep kernel — an aggressive Gaussian filter — pays the shell honestly; the exponential_convolve! docstring carries the caveat.\n\n(n) The shell-binning atomics contend on an accumulator smaller than a dozen cache lines — threading inverts at small grids, and Float32 bins double the collisions. The radial family (radial_spectrum, radial_correlation, the co-spectra) ends in one Atomix.@atomic add per stored mode into a packed bins array of 56 (64^3) to 223 (256^3) entries — on x86 a CAS loop that must own the bin's cache line exclusively, so true same-bin contention (serialized retries) stacks with false sharing (different bins on one 64-byte line). A model scatter (4M adds, eight threads) reproduces the table: serial 4.6 ns/element; threaded over a 64^3-scale packed bin array 17–19 ns — threading quadruples the per-add cost, the table's 64^3 inversion (t8 1.3–1.6× slower than t1); Float32 packed at 223 bins runs 1.51× Float64 (16 bins per line versus 8 — the table's t8 type reversal, 1.44–1.5×); one-bin-per-line padding equalizes the types and finally scales at 223 bins (2.7–2.8 ns) yet still trails serial at the 64^3 count (same-bin collisions, approx 856 per add). At production sizes the mode reads are DRAM-bound and the contention hides, so the anomaly is confined to small-grid diagnostics costing single-digit milliseconds — recorded, not fixed. Read the ≈48× Float64 radial gap at both of its levels, which are not in tension: its mechanism is vendor hardware — RDNA emulates the global Float64 atomic with a CAS loop where NVIDIA has it natively, which is why the gap collapses to 1.11× in Float32 (§6) — while its exposure is an addressable implementation gap, because the global atomic scatter has no per-workgroup pre-reduction, the standard technique for exactly this kernel shape, and with one the emulated atomic would be hit a few times per workgroup instead of once per mode. Every other cross-vendor ratio in this document sits in the 1.5–4× band a bandwidth ratio explains; this one is an order of magnitude larger because the un-pre-reduced kernel runs straight into the one operation the two vendors implement most differently. Candidates when a consumer's cadence makes it matter: bin padding (backend-uniform; kills the Float32 reversal, not the 64^3 inversion); per-thread private bins with an ordered merge on CPU (kills both and makes the CPU binning bitwise thread-invariant, retiring one of docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §9's two tolerance-tested accumulators, at the price of splitting the deliberately single-source KernelAbstractions kernel); workgroup-local bins on GPU — the same redesign's lever on the ~48× CUDA-over-ROCm Float64 radial gap (§6 and §7 measure it at 48–49× on the current archive), which is this kernel under RDNA3's CAS-loop Float64 atomics.\n\n(o) An axis-factored kernel is bandwidth-bound like the stored kernel it replaces, so what decides its speed is how the per-axis product is INDEXED and how the launch is SHAPED — not how few streams it reads. Decision 50 stores a separable kernel (SeparableKernel) as three O(N) per-axis factors instead of an O(N^3) field, which removes one field-sized stream from every application (three down to two). That alone predicts a speed-up; it does not deliver one, and the two failed attempts are the instructive part. Handing the lazy product to the linear-indexed fiberwise executor makes every element convert a linear index to a cartesian one — an integer division, emulated on a GPU — measured 0.29× a stored kernel (vector, 256^3, Float64, RTX 5070 Ti). Replacing that with a hand-written three-dimensional KernelAbstractions kernel removed the conversion and gained nothing (0.78× vector) while halving the scalar case to 0.55×: the folded axis is N2 + 1 — 129 at 256^3 — so a three-dimensional launch fragments warps against a row length that is not a multiple of the warp size, where the stored path's flat launch stays coalesced. What works is the plainest form, one flat broadcast over the whole section with the factors carrying singleton axes (they extend along the remaining mode axes and along the fiber): cartesian indexing and a flat, coalesced launch.\n\nThe CPU needs the opposite route, and the same measurement says so. That five-operand broadcast, three of whose operands carry singleton axes, does not vectorize: it ran at a fixed ~330 M elements/s — the same rate in Float32 as in Float64, the signature of an element-bound loop rather than a bandwidth-bound one. So the CPU takes a gated hand-written twin (§9's kernel-pair shape): the two outer factors hoisted to scalars, leaving an inner pass over contiguous storage for @simd. Loop order in that twin is worth stating, because the obvious choice is wrong — with the fiber components innermost the pass jumps between component blocks every row and the threaded case fell to 0.98×; walking each component's block start-to-end inside a thread's slab restored 1.14×.\n\nRatios against the stored kernel (vector field, 1 = axis-factored faster):\n\n GPU F64 GPU F32 CPU t1 F64 CPU t1 F32 CPU t8 F64 CPU t8 F32\n64^3 0.98 0.95 0.44 0.46 0.62 0.52\n128^3 0.79 0.83 0.66 0.45 1.44 0.50\n256^3 0.78 1.01 0.91 0.54 1.15 1.18\n512^3 — — 0.85 0.60 1.12 1.12\n\nThe trend is the point: the ratio improves with grid size on the CPU, because the saving is bytes not moved (two streams instead of three) while the cost is per-element arithmetic. The GPU column does not follow it — the ratio there sits near parity at 64^3 and drifts slightly down, a device being bandwidth-bound at every size in this range rather than crossing out of cache. Small grids sit in cache — the stored kernel's extra read is nearly free there, so the axis-factored form only pays for its arithmetic and loses. Past cache the pass becomes DRAM-bound and the missing stream wins: at 256^3–512^3 threaded, the CPU is 1.12–1.18× faster, and Float32 on the GPU reaches parity.\n\nThe Float64 GPU column is a consumer-card artifact, not the production case. The two extra complex multiplies are 12 FP64 flops per element; the gap at 256^3 is 363 µs for 305 MFLOP, an implied 0.84 TFLOP/s — precisely the RTX 5070 Ti's 1/64-rate FP64. On the decision-37 GH200 (full-rate FP64) the same work is ~9 µs, so Float64 there should track the Float32 column and land near 1.15×.\n\nThe same split applies to the kernel-composed combine!, which initially kept the linear-indexed route and so ran the rejected shape at R ge 1 — measured at roughly a third of the stored kernel's speed until it took the cartesian routes as well, after which it is 1.1× faster than the stored kernel at 256^3 threaded. A kernel storage class is only as fast as the slowest entry point that accepts it.\n\nThe radial class of decision 51 (RadialKernel) takes the same two routes, for the same reason. A kernel depending on the mode only through k^2 stores no coefficients at all — a symbol closure and three references to the squared-wavenumber rows — so it removes the same field-sized stream, and its arithmetic is one extra add plus the symbol evaluation. Everything above transfers: a device takes the flat broadcast, a CPU takes the gated component-major twin, and both index the wavenumber axes cartesian. The one addition is an ordering constraint the separable class does not have. The squared wavenumber must be summed (kappa_1^2 + kappa_2^2) + kappa_3^2, matching the left association the broadcast itself uses, or the compressed and stored paths agree only to rounding rather than bit for bit — and the suite asserts the stronger form.\n\nThe symbol is a closure, and it must capture values rather than a type. Capturing an element type — writing one(T) inside the closure with T closed over — makes the captured object a Type, which is not a bitstype, and a GPU launch rejects it outright. Hoist the constants to values before the closure is built.\n\nThree rules generalize. Reach for the broadcast route before a hand-written kernel on a device — §9's execution-route policy, here vindicated by measurement, since Julia's broadcast machinery chose a better launch than the hand-rolled kernel did. A broadcast over singleton-axis operands is the exception on a CPU, where it does not vectorize and a hand-written twin is worth its keep. And when a fused pass underperforms, measure the launch shape and the traversal order before blaming the arithmetic — both failed attempts here were layout problems wearing an arithmetic costume.\n\n(p) CPU FFT at the larger grids moved 20–50% between the 2026-07-30 and 2026-08-05 sweeps on Enteropia, and the 2026-08-07 Aiporetne control says the cause is specific to that host. The full re-sweep of the 9800X3D lanes found a cluster confined to the fft class: at 256^3 Float64, -t1 to_physical scalar went 83.8 → 116.0 ms and to_spectral scalar 171.9 → 207.4 ms; -t8 saw to_spectral vector at 128^3 go 11.3 → 16.7 ms. It is reproducible, not jitter — three standalone probes of the 256^3 Float64 rows returned 115.8 / 116.8 / 116.5 and 208.3 / 209.3 / 211.1 ms, a spread under 1%.\n\nWhat it is not, each checked rather than assumed: not a package change (the transform path has zero changed code lines between the two commits — the 136 insertions in Fields.jl are docstrings and decision 61's type parameter); not FFTW (the libfftw3 artifact dates from 2026-05-25 and benchmark/Manifest.toml from 2026-07-22, both predating the older archive); not Julia (1.12.6 for both sweeps); and not a machine-wide slowdown — the other 596 rows of the same lanes have a median drift of -012, so only the FFT rows moved. It is CPU-only: the cuFFT lane shows no fft row among its movers. The one difference the record does show is a reboot between the two sweeps (current kernel 7.0.0-28-generic), and no pre-reboot kernel or microcode version was recorded, so a platform change can be neither confirmed nor excluded on that host alone.\n\nThe control ran on Aiporetne (i7-12700K) on 2026-08-07, and at the larger grids the shift does not reproduce. That host's lanes were last measured 2026-07-22, so the comparison spans an even wider commit range than the one that produced the finding. Across its 192 fft rows (four thread lanes × 48), exactly one row at 256^3 moved slower, by +10, against Enteropia's +38 on the row the finding names; the per-lane medians run -45 to -68. State this precisely rather than as \"nothing got slower\", which a first pass over a single lane wrongly suggested: 23 of the 192 rows did move slower, but all except that one 256^3 row sit at 64^3 or 128^3, and the three exceeding 20% (+468, +235, +222) are all small-grid cells at -t4/-t8, where thread overhead dominates a workload of well under a millisecond and the scatter is largest. The finding is about the larger grids, and there the two hosts moved in opposite directions over overlapping periods. Aiporetne runs kernel 7.0.0-28-generic — the same version Enteropia recorded after its reboot — so if a kernel or microcode change were responsible, this host should show the slow vintage and instead shows the fast one. That exonerates the platform version specifically, which is stronger than a bare failure to reproduce, and leaves the cause in Enteropia's own machine state.\n\nThe control's own movement is equally unexplained, and saying so is the point. Aiporetne's fft rows did not merely fail to slow — they got materially faster, by 5–46% at the larger grids, with the FFTW.MEASURE twins moving most (256^3 Float32 to_spectral scalar measure, -t1: 64.8 → 35.0 ms, a ratio of 054). No package change touches the transform path over that range either. So both hosts show an fft-class-confined shift across a re-sweep, in opposite directions, with unrecorded platform state on both sides. The conservative reading is not that one host regressed and the other did not, but that fft timings are not stable across sweeps on either host while the conditions of a sweep go unrecorded — which is exactly what the vintage caveat exists to express. Whatever moves them is below the package, and neither direction has been traced. Treat fft rows as comparable only within a vintage, and treat a cross-vintage fft comparison as uninformative rather than as evidence.\n\nStudied on Aiporetne, 2026-08-07: five candidates eliminated by measurement, and the instrument gap closed. The fft class is the only one whose cost depends on a plan chosen at runtime, so it is the natural suspect, but each mechanism was tested rather than argued:\n\nCross-process variation is not it. The finding's own row (to_physical scalar, Float64, 256^3) measured in eight separate processes spans 156.1–165.0 ms — a spread of 106times, against the 20–50% to be explained.\nHybrid core placement is not it, but it IS a latent hazard. The 12700K is 8 P-cores plus 4 E-cores. Pinned to an E-core the same row measures 255.8–266.1 ms against 156.5–164.3 ms on a P-core — 163times — easily enough to produce the effect. It does not, because every unpinned sample landed in the P-core range: Linux reliably places a single compute-bound thread on a P-core. A sweep run under taskset, a cgroup, or a systemd slice that admits E-cores would read 1.6× slow with nothing else changed, which is worth knowing before anyone constrains one.\nTransparent huge pages are not it. THP is in madvise mode with AnonHugePages: 0, so the arrays are never huge-page backed on this host — the mode cannot vary between sweeps because nothing requests it.\nMemory fragmentation is not it. buddyinfo shows 26,625 free order-10 blocks after 22 hours of uptime, so large allocations are not falling back to scattered pages.\nPlan selection is not it. With fft_flags unset — what every plain row uses — plans are built with FFTW's default ESTIMATE, a deterministic heuristic, not the timed search MEASURE performs. The measure twins are the only rows whose plan choice can vary from run to run.\n\nWhat remains is state that persists across a session, and the finding could not be attributed because none of it was recorded. That is an instrument gap rather than a mystery, and it is now closed: characterize.jl stamps kernel, microcode, cpu, governor, no_turbo, smt, thp, julia and threads into every archive beside the commit and date, so the next occurrence is a diff rather than a reconstruction. Aiporetne's state at this sweep: kernel 7.0.0-28-generic, microcode 0x3e, governor powersave with turbo enabled, SMT on, THP madvise. Note the governor in particular — powersave on intel_pstate still boosts, but its ramp depends on recent load history, which is exactly the kind of cross-sweep state nothing was capturing.\n\nRESOLVED 2026-08-09 on the host that showed it: the shift was a ONE-OFF, not a persistent state change. Enteropia's CPU lanes were re-swept against the 2026-08-05 archive over an interval whose src/ edits are docstring text only — five files were touched by a documentation pass, and stripping docstrings and comments from both revisions leaves zero differing executable lines — so anything that moved would be platform or measurement. Nothing moved. The fft class is flat — per-lane medians -14, -12 and -11 at -t1, -t2 and -t4 — and at 256^3, where the event was +20–50, the medians are -37, -65 and -12. Every other class is flat on the same lanes, 600 rows each, with overall medians of +00, -02 and -02.\n\nWhat that licenses, and what it does not. The instrument reproduces itself across four days on this host, so the harness is sound and the 2026-08-05 fft reading was a transient of that particular sweep rather than a lasting change in the machine — which is also why the Aiporetne control found nothing to confirm. It does not identify the transient's cause, and it cannot: the state that would have named it was never recorded, and the episode is over. The finding therefore closes as unattributed but bounded — a single sweep's fft column, on one host, not reproduced before or since. The stamping is the durable outcome: these three lanes are the first archives in the tree carrying platform state, so a recurrence becomes a diff. The -t8 lane could not be re-measured at all, for the unrelated reason in (q).\n\n(q) FFTW's threaded executor segfaults at -t8 — REPRODUCIBLE on Enteropia, and the lane completes only when split across processes. The heading below and the 105-trial non-reproduction that follows it are kept as the record of how this looked before 2026-08-09; both are now superseded. On that date the -t8 characterization lane failed three times consecutively on Enteropia (9800X3D), taking the tally on that host to four failures in five attempts across two dates. Every crash carries the identical stack — signal 11 in spawn_apply inside libfftw3, reached through FFTW.jl's spawnloop (providers.jl:49) on a Julia task — and every one landed in the reductions class, whose radial_correlation takes an inverse transform. The crash point moves between attempts (64^3 Float64, then 128^3 Float64, at 2, 8 and 10 minutes in), which marks it a race rather than a bad cell, and the process had accumulated 289M and 1071M allocations with 5874 and 14598 collections when it died.\n\nThe 2026-08-07 non-reproduction was run on the wrong host. That attempt — 105 trials, ~50,500 transform executions, no crash — ran on Aiporetne. It closes as evidence about Aiporetne, where the fault has never appeared, and says nothing about Enteropia, where it now appears about four times in five. The finding already flagged that a host-specific trigger was not excluded; it is now the leading explanation. Two targeted repros on the failing host also came back clean — 2,400 radial_correlation calls at -t8, and twelve rounds of build → run → drop → GC.gc(true) across the fft, transfer and reductions classes — so the fault needs the full lane's duration and allocation pressure, not merely its operations. A short reproducer is not cheaply available.\n\nThe lane is obtainable anyway, by splitting it across processes. The mitigation below is unusable here — building plans under set_fft_threads!(1) removes the callback, but the -t8 lane exists precisely to measure threaded FFT, so it would corrupt the column it is meant to produce. What works instead is running the lane as one process per grid (CHAR_SIZES=64, then 128, 256, 512) and merging the four archives: 64³, 256³ and 512³ passed first time, 128³ on its second attempt, and the parts hold disjoint rows (150 each) so the union is what an uncrashed process would have written. RESULTS.md's CPU 9800X3D t8 column was rebuilt that way on 2026-08-10 and is flat against the 2026-08-05 one it replaces — median +00 over 600 rows, the largest movers being the measure twins whose MEASURE plan search legitimately re-decides between runs.\n\nThat is a workaround, and it sharpens the diagnosis rather than closing it. The fault is unchanged and a plain -t8 invocation still dies here. But a fresh process doing only 128^3 also crashed, 2.5 minutes in, so the trigger is not simply a long-lived process accumulating state; splitting helps because it shortens each exposure and makes a retry cheap, not because it removes the hazard. Combined with the crash point moving between attempts, the fault reads as a low-probability race whose rate rises with the number of threaded transform executions — which is why the small grids, with their enormous call counts per second, are where it keeps landing.\n\nThe conditions, tabulated from five crashes — and the four things that are NOT enough. The tally is five because the four failures of the single-process lane (three on 2026-08-09, one on 2026-08-05, against one success) are joined by a fifth from the split run of 2026-08-10, whose 128^3 process crashed on its first attempt before succeeding on its second. Every crash on record shares four properties: -t8 (FFTW's thread count tracks Julia's, so eight workers; -t1, -t2 and -t4 have never failed across many lanes), the host Enteropia, the reductions class — five times out of five, never fft, algebra, operators, convolution or transfer — and a small or middling grid (128^3 four times, 64^3 once, never 256^3 or 512^3, though that last is partly survivorship because the monolithic lanes died before reaching them). What it is not is an accumulation threshold: the allocation counter at death spans 158M to 1071M and the collection count 1753 to 14598, a sevenfold spread on both.\n\nFour reproduction attempts at -t8 on the failing host all came back clean, and each one narrows the cause: 2,400 radial_correlation calls; twelve rounds of build → run → drop → GC.gc(true) over fft, transfer and reductions; five rounds of the reductions class alone at the exact crashing cell; and the full Pkg.test() suite. So neither the operation, nor plan churn, nor the class, nor eight threads is sufficient on its own.\n\nWhat every real crash had that none of those had is the rest of the process. In each one, fft, algebra, operators, convolution and transfer had already run before reductions was reached — and fft is the class that builds the most plans, including the measure twins that invoke FFTW's timed planner. That is the exact window FFTW.jl documents, quoted below: the collector may run during the spawnloop callback while the planner is executing. Its deferred plan destruction and GC.safepoint() spin exist to stop that deadlocking; a segfault is the other failure, the shape of a plan freed by its finalizer while another thread is still executing it. The working description is therefore a low-probability race, per threaded transform execution, whose window is opened by earlier planner activity in the same process — which explains both why the small grids catch it (their call counts per second are enormous) and why splitting the lane helps without fixing anything.\n\nSeparately, the package itself is fine at eight threads. Pkg.test() under JULIA_NUM_THREADS=8 passes in full — 6182 tests across 92 testsets, zero failures, identical to -t1 and -t4 — so this is not a general -t8 hazard for a consumer, and the CI matrix's -t1/-t4 pair is not hiding a broken configuration. It is the sustained benchmark lane, with its millions of small threaded transforms, that finds the race.\n\n(q, historical) FFTW's threaded executor segfaulted once at -t8, and did not reproduce. The first -t8 characterization lane died with signal 11 inside spawn_apply in libfftw3, reached through FFTW.jl's spawnloop, while running the reductions class at 128^3 Float64 — which touches FFTW because radial_correlation takes an inverse transform. The package sets FFTW's thread count to match Julia's at load, so -t8 is the only lane driving FFTW with eight worker threads; -t1, -t2 and -t4 all completed. Re-running the identical lane succeeded (30m31s, rc=0), so the fault is intermittent. Nothing was corrupted: characterize.jl writes its JSON only at the end, so the crash left the previous archive intact rather than half-written — worth knowing, because a harness that wrote incrementally would have produced a silently mixed-vintage column instead.\n\nAttempted reproduction, 2026-08-07: 105 trials, ~50,500 transform executions, no crash — but the fault is now LOCATED rather than mysterious. Two stressors were built against the mechanism instead of re-running a 30-minute lane. Both drove eight FFTW worker threads at 128^3 Float64 through radial_correlation, which is what the original crash was running (it takes an inverse transform and materialises a power field), with plan churn so finalizers had work pending while the next round executed: v1 forced collection between rounds, v2 instead kept the heap near its threshold from a background allocating task, so the collector would fire inside the callback rather than between calls. Totals: 105 trials, ~50,500 transform executions, ~6,200 plan build-and-destroy cycles, on Aiporetne (i7-12700K) with FFTW.jl 1.10.0 and FFTW_jll 3.3.12+0. Every trial exited 0.\n\nWhat the attempt did establish. FFTW.jl's own source documents this interaction zone: \"the garbage-collector may run at unexpected times. For example, it may run during the spawnloop callback while the FFTW planner is executing\", and it carries deferred plan destruction plus a GC.safepoint() spin specifically so that case cannot deadlock on the fftwlock. A segfault is a different failure from the one that machinery guards, and the shape that produces one is a plan being destroyed by its finalizer while another thread is still executing it — a use-after-free reached through spawn_apply. That is consistent with every recorded detail: it needs plan lifetime to overlap execution, it needs the collector to run at an unlucky moment, and it appeared only in the -t8 lane, the one driving the callback with eight workers.\n\nWhat it does not establish. A non-reproduction bounds the rate, it does not clear the code: under adversarial conditions the fault is rarer than about 1 in 50,000 transforms on this host, against an original of one occurrence in one 30-minute lane. The crash was also seen on Enteropia (9800X3D) and this attempt ran on Aiporetne, so a host-specific trigger is not excluded.\n\nThe mitigation, if it ever bites a consumer, is known and is not free. Building plans under set_fft_threads!(1) removes the callback entirely — the thread count is fixed at plan-build time (finding (t)) — and also removes that finding's per-call allocation. The cost is real: FFTW threading is worth about 2times at 256^3 on the 12700K (to_physical scalar Float64, 153.4 ms at -t1 against 78.2 ms at -t8), so this is a robustness-for-throughput trade to make deliberately, not a default to change.\n\n(r) Every kernel-taking operation moved 20–45% on the CPU between the 2026-07-22 and 2026-08-07 sweeps, and the cause is decision 51 reaching them through finding (k). The re-sweep's slower rows were almost exactly the operations that take a kernel — divergence!∘kernel, helmholtz!∘kernel, convolve!, combine!∘kernel — across every thread count and both element types.\n\nThe GPU half of this finding is WITHDRAWN, not explained. Both archives it originally compared were measured in the broken regime of (s), so the device cluster was a difference between two collector-dominated measurements and says nothing about the operations. It cannot be recovered either, since answering it now would require the old code re-measured on the fixed harness, which no archive contains. The gcsample setting never applied to CPU lanes, so what follows is CPU-only and is real.\n\nBisected rather than argued. The archives were gone, so the same operations were re-measured at 48b6254a and at HEAD in adjacent worktrees sharing one dependency manifest, on an idle box, -t1. Three control rows taking no kernel (add!, laplacian!, the plain divergence!) came out flat at 093–101, which is what licenses reading anything into the rest:\n\nrow (64^3 / 128^3 Float64) HEAD / old\ncombine!∘kernel vector 228times / 165times\nconvolve! vector 198times / 102times\ndivergence!∘kernel 133times / 124times\nhelmholtz!∘kernel 127times / 123times\nconvolve! scalar 101times / 091times\nexponential_convolve! 100times / 100times\n\nThe mechanism is a per-fiber-component recompute of the kernel symbol. Decision 51 made spectral_cutoff and spectral_band return a RadialKernel, whose _kernel_data is a lazy sigma(lvert krvert^2) over the (N_k_x N_y N_z) mode grid. Against a scalar field that shape matches and the symbol is evaluated once per mode; against a vector field the trailing singleton is extruded over the fiber and the symbol is evaluated once per component. A stored kernel re-reads a cached array in the same place, which is far cheaper. Measured directly at HEAD, radial against its own materialized twin:\n\ntensor order 64^3 (compute-bound) 128^3 (bandwidth-bound)\nR = 0 102 088 — radial faster\nR = 1 199 102\nR = 2 152 106\n\nThe case R = 0 never pays it, which is the signature; exponential_convolve! takes multipliers rather than a kernel object and is untouched, which is the control. The case R = 2 sits below R = 1 because nine complex components are memory-heavy enough that bandwidth starts dominating even at 64^3, which is the same effect that removes the penalty entirely at 128^3.\n\nThis is finding (k)'s trade, arriving somewhere new. That finding already recorded a lazy kernel costing 17times a stored one on the CPU when the kernel is fixed; decision 51 then changed what spectral_cutoff returns, so every operation taking a cutoff kernel inherited the penalty without any of them changing. It also reconciles what looked like a contradiction: the report's own ∘ radial versus ∘ stored rows show radial faster, because they are measured at 128^3 and -t8, deep in the bandwidth-bound regime where the saved kernel stream wins. Both readings are correct; the regime decides.\n\nThe hoist was prototyped, measured, and DECLINED. The obvious fix is to evaluate the symbol once per mode and reuse it across the fiber — the decision-38/39 fused-pass pattern — and it is not materialization: the value lives in a row-sized buffer of N_k_x floats, a few hundred bytes, never an O(N^3) array. It was written that way (σ once per (i_2 i_3) row into a stack buffer, then applied across components with contiguous SIMD access in i_1), checked bitwise against convolve!, and timed against the current broadcast:\n\ntensor order 128^3 hoist / broadcast 256^3 hoist / broadcast\nR = 0 072 092\nR = 1 095 102 — slower\nR = 2 099 107 — slower\n\nIt buys nothing where it matters and costs a little. At 256^3 the pass is bandwidth-bound, so the recompute is already hidden behind memory latency while the buffer adds a store and a load that the memory-bound regime hides less well. The R = 0 column gains, but R = 0 never paid the fiber penalty in the first place — that gain is over the broadcast machinery itself, at a tensor order with no fiber to extrude over.\n\nThe premise behind wanting the fix was also wrong, and measuring is what showed it. A traffic count says the radial class should beat stored by 1(2R+1) — about 14% at R = 1 — and that the recompute was eating it. At 256^3 Float64 the radial broadcast measures 41.9 ms against stored's 45.0 ms, already 6.8% ahead: it is realizing its memory saving at production scale, and there was no forfeited 14% to recover. The 102 reading at 128^3 is the transition point between the two regimes, not evidence of a permanent loss.\n\nSo the penalty is real but confined to the regime nobody ships in. It appears at 64^3 and serial, where the pass is compute-bound; the CPU path's tuning target is the beyond-VRAM band (ARCHITECTURE.md decision 37), and there the radial class is already the faster of the two. Decision 51 stands as measured: O(1) builders, competitive application at production grids, and a small-grid compute penalty that no reasonable fix removes without costing more elsewhere. No change is warranted, and the pass audit's separate hat w extrusion holder should be re-priced against this result before anyone acts on it either.\n\n(s) Every GPU number this project has ever recorded is inflated 2–29× by gcsample, and the operations were never what was being timed. characterize.jl sets BenchmarkTools.DEFAULT_PARAMETERS.gcsample = true for device lanes, and regress.jl sets gcsample = USEGPU. That runs a full GC.gc() before every sample, whose finalizers free device buffers and synchronize the device — a cost that lands on a measurement whose operation is tens of microseconds. Measured A/B on the RX 7900 XTX, Float64, the whole convolution class run through the suite's own code with only that one parameter changed:\n\n64^3: inflation 184–292times (convolve! scalar 956 µs against 32.7 µs)\n128^3: inflation 58–198times (convolve! scalar 1.128 ms against 56.9 µs)\n256^3: inflation 19–425times (convolve! vector 2.494 ms against 1.218 ms)\n\nThe gcsample = true figures reproduce the committed archive to within a few percent, which is what identifies the archive as this regime rather than some other.\n\nThe tell was visible in the table all along. With the overhead in, convolve! scalar and convolve! vector at 128^3 read 1.094 and 1.177 ms — a 3× difference in bytes moved appearing as 1.08×, because both were dominated by a shared ~1 ms floor. With it out they read 57 and 182 µs, a ratio of 32 that matches the data volume. A device column whose scalar and vector rows nearly coincide is not measuring the device.\n\nIt is not a uniform scale factor, so it corrupts comparisons within a column too. The inflation varies per row (194–425times at 256^3 alone), and at least one documented conclusion reverses when it is removed: convolve! vector ∘ radial against ∘ stored reads 148times slower for the radial class at 256^3 with gcsample, and 090times — faster — without it, with the same flip at 128^3. Decision 51's radial kernel is therefore faster than the stored class on this card, in the same direction as CUDA. The claim recorded in bd1e4f30 that decision 51's trade \"has opposite sign on the two vendors\" is retracted: it rested on a probe that also ran gcsample = true, and the apparent sign came from two rows being inflated by different factors (95times against 58times), not from the kernels.\n\nScope. gcsample is gated on USEGPU in both harnesses, so CPU columns and CPU canary baselines are clean and every GPU column of RESULTS.md — all four cards, every grid — plus both hosts' GPU canary baselines are affected. Cross-card GPU comparisons are affected twice over, since each vendor's free-and-synchronize cost differs; the recorded \"RTX 5070 Ti is ≈1.8× the RX 7900 XTX\" was not safe to rely on until re-measured, and on re-measurement (both cards, fixed regime) it fell to 1.09× in Float64 at 256^3 — see the resolved cross-card entry in (v).\n\nWhy it was there, and why deleting it was not the fix. It was added deliberately (2026-06-11) because the reductions class wedged the device: lpnorm materializes a magnitude field per call, BenchmarkTools' sampling loop does almost no host allocation, so Julia's GC never fired, and the AMDGPU pool grew until the allocator spun. That failure is real, and a bare gcsample = false reinstates it.\n\nThe fix, landed and validated: gcsample is now decided PER ROW, by measurement. Only a row that creates device arrays fast enough to exhaust the pool within its own trial needs the per-sample collector, and _apply_gcsample_policy! in characterize.jl finds those rows rather than assuming them. Each row is warmed up, then run with the collector disabled while the device pool is watched; the growth is projected over the trial from the row's own per-call time, and a row projected to exceed 2 GiB keeps gcsample. Host bytes cannot serve as the signal — a KernelAbstractions launch allocates host bytes per call, so a first attempt using them classified every launching row as allocating and changed nothing.\n\nThe probe's own output is the evidence it works, because it independently reproduces invariant 13. At 128^3 it measures 0.0 MiB of pool growth for every in-place operation and finds exactly the documented materializers: lpnorm 144 MiB, radial_correlation and the cross-correlation 1310 MiB, the spectral inner's O(N^2) correction planes 13.5 MiB, materialize_kernel 146 MiB. That list was written from the code years before this probe existed, and the probe was not told it.\n\nValidated on the case the setting existed for. Running characterize.jl itself over convolution and reductions at 128^3 and 256^3 Float64: it completes without wedging, keeps gcsample on exactly six rows (the two materialize_kernel rows and the four documented reduction materializers), and the in-place rows recover their physics — convolve! scalar against vector reads 57.3 against 181.0 µs, a ratio of 316 matching the data volume, where the archive read 1.094 against 1.177 ms. The figures reproduce an independent gcsample = false measurement to within 1%. CPU lanes are untouched, the policy being gated on the backend.\n\nThe probe could wedge the card it was written to protect, and did. It runs GC-free, so its first version's fixed eight calls were themselves an unbounded allocation: transfer at 256^3, whose NUFFT allocates oversampled work arrays per call, filled the pool and hung a re-sweep for two and a half hours with VRAM at 99%, device activity at 0%, and one core spinning in the allocator. The catch-based fail-safe did not fire because a wedge is a hang, not an exception. The probe is now bounded by memory with an early exit — one call at a time, stopping the moment growth passes 512 MiB, which is already decisive — so its peak use is that ceiling plus one call rather than the pool. SIGTERM did not reach the spinning process; SIGKILL released the pool cleanly and the archive survived, characterize.jl writing its JSON only at the end.\n\nAn allocating row need not pay a GC per sample either. Since the probe measures per-call growth, samples is capped from it (a 4 GiB budget, evals pinned to 1) and the row runs GC-free with a bounded pool. This matters because the user-facing transforms allocate their result by definition, so the whole device fft column was otherwise still timed on the collector. Only a row whose single call is too large to repeat — the vector transforms, at ~400 MB each — keeps the per-sample GC. A capped row's parameters are deliberate and must not be passed to tune!, which would search evals upward and undo the cap.\n\nRe-swept 2026-08-07, and the correction is the size the A/B predicted. Against the pre-fix archive, the RX 7900 XTX column moved by a median of -816 over 467 rows: operators -920, convolution -850, algebra -829, reductions -811, transfer -806, fft -717. Individual rows fall 97%. The physics is back — convolve! scalar against vector at 128^3 reads 57.3 against 180.0 µs, a ratio of 314 matching the data volume, where the old column read 1.094 against 1.177 ms. The canary was refrozen on the fixed harness and is green with zero rows needing the per-sample GC, every canary being an in-place mechanism: that lane had been pure collector overhead.\n\nA defect in that fix suppressed tune! for the entire device suite, and is now repaired. The policy returns the rows whose parameters it set deliberately so they are not re-tuned, and it identified them with allocating || params.evals == 1. The second test was meant to catch a capped row, which reports allocating = false but has had evals pinned to 1 — except that 1 is also BenchmarkTools' untuned default, so the test matched every row in the suite and tune! ran on none of them. The flat rows are documented three paragraphs above as \"tuned normally\", so the code contradicted its own specification. A capped row now carries its own flag. The repair was verified by perturbation on a synthetic suite rather than by reading: a flat row must leave fixed empty, and a row whose stubbed pool grows 100 MiB per call must still appear there with no per-sample GC.\n\nIts footprint is bounded, confined to the fastest rows, and MEASURED — it is 6–8% on three of them. Tuning only raises evals above 1 when a call is short enough that per-sample overhead is comparable to the work. Rather than reason about which rows those are, the two disciplines were run against each other on the RTX 5070 Ti at 64^3 Float64: of the whole convolution class only spectral_cutoff, radial_kernel and spectral_band — the O(1) closure builders at approx 04 µs — tune above evals = 1 (to ≈ 207), and untuned they read 6–8% high (400.0 against 373.8 ns; 390.0 against 361.2; 400.0 against 376.5). Every other row stays at evals = 1 and ties to within 1%, including materialize_kernel radial at 11.4 µs — so the threshold sits far below the 20 µs a first estimate assumed, and the affected set is three builder mechanisms rather than every sub-20 µs row. A 6–8% bias on three closure-construction rows is inside the documented jitter and changes no conclusion, so the RX 7900 XTX column needs no re-sweep on this account; its builder rows are simply known to read a few percent high until that lane is next swept.\n\nScope, stated exactly. The policy is reached only under USEGPU; the CPU branch calls tune!(suite) and always did, so no CPU column is touched. Before the per-row policy existed tune!(suite) ran unconditionally for devices too, which makes the RX 7900 XTX re-sweep of 2026-08-07 the only archive in the tree measured with tuning suppressed — every other column, device and CPU alike, is tuned. The canary is unaffected: regress.jl carries its own standalone copy of the policy that neither caps samples nor returns a do-not-tune set, so no frozen baseline needs refreezing.\n\nRESULTS.md now holds both GPU columns in the fixed regime, the RX 7900 XTX re-swept 2026-08-07 and the RTX 5070 Ti 2026-08-09, so the two are comparable again — see the resolved cross-card entry in (v). Two further columns were dropped rather than carried: both belonged to cards since retired from the fleet, both were measured in the broken regime, and neither could be re-swept, so a reader could never correct or compare them — and a column like that invites exactly the comparison it cannot support.\n\n(t) A threaded FFTW plan allocates Julia-side task state on every call — size-independent, nondeterministic, and fixed when the plan is BUILT rather than when it is called. The 2026-08-04 survey run reported to_spectral! allocating 8–12 KB per step on odd grids under permuted layout folds, which invariant 13 would make a per-step leak. It is not one, and the package is allocation-free there.\n\nFFTW.jl reaches its worker threads through a Julia callback, so a plan built with more than one thread allocates task state per transform call. Three properties identify it as harness rather than scratch:\n\nSize-independent. The same 7008 B/step for a 12 KB field and for a 2.6 MB one, over grids from 8^3 to 48^3. Field-sized scratch would scale; this does not.\nNondeterministic. The same configuration measured 7008, 10464, 23173 and 48557 B/step on different runs, since the number of tasks actually spawned varies with scheduling.\nSet at plan-build time. A plan built under set_fft_threads!(1) measures exactly 0 B/step even when called from a four-thread session, while one built under four threads measures 7008 in the same session. Restoring the setting before the call changes nothing.\n\nThe value also depends on the layout fold — 7008 for the default and (213), 10464 for (312) — which is what made it look like a permuted-layout defect. It is the plan's stage count, not the package's code path.\n\nThe gate was already right and the survey was not. test/allocations.jl bounds this class with 4096 times the thread count, which is why it passes at -t4; the survey's sequences lane used a purely field-proportional bound, so a constant several-kilobyte cost overran it on the small fixtures. The lane now builds a dedicated single-threaded plan for the allocation measurement — the only place the cost can be removed — and reports 0 B/step deterministically across repeated -t4 runs, with the tight field-proportional bound kept for detection power. Raising the bound would have been the wrong fix: it would have set a threshold against a quantity that varies 7× run to run, which is how a harness stops measuring anything.\n\n(u) Jitter is not uniform across the table: a small grid at a high thread count carries up to 144times run-to-run variance, and the committed \"~10%\" was a blanket claim that does not hold there. Two mean(norm) vec cells were flagged as regressions by the 2026-08-07 re-sweep — GPU 128^3 Float32 at +1219 and CPU -t8 64^3 Float32 at +1112. Neither is one.\n\nThe GPU cell was measured on both sides in the broken gcsample regime of (s); re-swept on the fixed harness the column is monotone and physical again (Float32 87.3 → 241.0 → 849.0 µs across 64^3–256^3, Float64 97.7 → 236.8 → 1371 µs), and the old 538.9 µs reading — which sat below its own 64^3 row — is gone.\n\nThe CPU cell is real jitter, and measuring it is what shows the scale. Five repeats of the same measurement, Float32 mean(norm) vec:\n\ncell five minima (µs) spread\n64^3 -t8 31.2, 31.0, 31.3, 30.8, 44.4 144times\n64^3 -t1 192.1 – 193.8 101times\n256^3 -t8 5434 – 5931 109times\n256^3 -t1 13063 – 13295 102times\n\nThe variance is confined to small grid × high thread count, where ~31 µs of work is comparable to the cost of spawning eight threads and BenchmarkTools' minimum over a one-second budget does not suppress the tail. The mode at that cell is ~31 µs, which is what the old archive recorded (32.0); the re-sweep happened to catch the tail (67.5). Thread scaling there is in fact monotone when measured cleanly — 193.2, 100.4, 53.0, 43.8 µs at -t1/-t2/-t4/-t8.\n\nSo a movement of tens of percent in a small-grid, high-thread cell is not evidence. Compare those rows at 256^3 or above, or at low thread counts; the report header now says so rather than claiming a uniform ~10%.\n\n(v) Which recorded decisions the gcsample defect could have corrupted — audited, not assumed. Finding (s) invalidates GPU numbers, and several recorded decisions were justified by GPU numbers, so the question is which of them could now be wrong. The inflation was not a uniform factor (1.94–4.25× at 256^3, 18–29× at 64^3), and a uniform factor is the only kind that cancels in an A/B ratio — so the exposure is worst where a claimed ratio is near 1. That is exactly how decision 51's radial versus stored comparison flipped sign.\n\nThe blast radius is narrower than it first looks. BenchmarkTools' own default is gcsample = false, and only three entry points ever set it: characterize.jl, regress.jl, and the two runbenchmarks.jl drivers. An ad-hoc @benchmark head-to-head is therefore unaffected, which is how most of the decision-bearing comparisons in this document were taken. A useful corroborating test: the per-sample GC put a floor of roughly 1 ms on any device row at le 128^3, so a recorded GPU figure below that floor cannot have been measured under it.\n\nRe-checked on the fixed harness, and surviving:\n\nDecision 30's backend split (mean(det, A) fused against mean(det(A)) materialising, 128^3 Float64, RX 7900 XTX). Recorded: 0.55 vs 0.69 ms, fused 13times. Re-measured: 0.259 vs 0.486 ms, fused 187times — same direction, stronger, and both absolutes fell as the collector floor came off. The GPU branch of _reduce_fiber is pointed the right way. Its recorded figures also sit below the 1 ms floor, so they were never under gcsample to begin with.\nDecision 51 (radial against stored): re-measured on the fixed harness, radial is 9–12% faster at 128^3 and 256^3. The claim that its trade \"has opposite sign on the two vendors\", published in bd1e4f30, was retracted the same night.\nFinding (o) (an axis-factored kernel does not win): the AMD side agrees on the fixed harness — separable 241.9 µs against stored 181.1 µs at 128^3 Float64. The qualitative conclusion holds; its RTX 5070 Ti specifics need re-measuring on that host.\n\nAt risk and NOT resolvable here. These rest on GPU ratios small enough to sit inside the inflation's per-row variance, and none can be re-checked from this box:\n\nThe Morton-sorting rejection, gather half — RE-CHECKED, and it does not hold on the current card. This was first recorded here as unresolvable because the sorting dependency and the sorted gather were deleted. That was wrong on the part that matters: only the sort was deleted, and the sort is not what the measurement times. The interpolate! verb still exists and Morton order is just a permutation of the PointSet, which a host sortperm produces perfectly well for a benchmark. See §4(c), where the entry now carries the re-measurement.\nThe wrap-hoist rejection — RE-MEASURED, and reopened (see §4(c)). Filing it here as a gcsample casualty was probably wrong twice over: the record describes a \"same-session A/B\", i.e. an ad-hoc comparison that BenchmarkTools would have run with gcsample = false anyway, so the defect likely never touched it. What it was at risk from is the same thing as Morton — retired RDNA2 hardware. Re-measured on RDNA3 the sign reverses for the default scheme (CatmullRom hoisted 10–30% faster, against a recorded 10–14% slower) and the CPU, never tested for this, wins by up to 36times.\nDecision 42's GPU figure — RE-CHECKED on the RX 7900 XTX, and it is a tie. The recorded \"in-register ties in Float32, approx 6 slower in Float64\" was measured on the RTX 5070 Ti, so this is a different card rather than a direct re-run. Both variants written as standalone kernels differing only in the denominator — and verified bitwise equal — give stored-over- in-register ratios of 0955 (Float64 128^3), 0998 (Float64 256^3), 1015 (Float32 128^3) and 1004 (Float32 256^3). The recorded direction reproduces weakly — in-register is 4.5% slower in Float64 at 128^3 — while everything else ties inside 1.5%, so the effect is real but marginal and vanishes at the larger grid. The decision is untouched either way: it rests on the CPU result (in-register wins everywhere, +4/+10), and the CPU was never affected. Worth stating plainly because it is the natural worry: neither variant materialises anything field-sized. The stored family is three PER-AXIS vectors of length N_k_x, N_y, N_z — a few KB, the same O(N) shape as the derivative wavenumbers — so the trade is three register multiplies against three stride-1 loads from cache-resident vectors, not memory pressure. (The stored family must be the derivative-squared a^2, not the multipliers' true-lvert krvert^2 family: invariant 5 keeps the true value at Nyquist where the derivative wavenumbers are zeroed, so substituting it would change the answer there.)\nEvery cross-card ratio — RESOLVED 2026-08-09, and the headline one was wrong. Both sides were inflated by vendor-specific amounts, so nothing could be said until both cards were re-swept; they now are. The prediction that the large ratios keep their direction and none keeps its magnitude was half right. The approx 48times Float64 radial gap is confirmed almost exactly — 48.64× on co-spectrum vec.vec and 48.07× on radial_spectrum vec at 256^3, the atomic-scatter difference — and it survives because it is enormous. The \"≈1.8× median\" does not: at 256^3 the median over 71 comparable rows is 1.09× in Float64 and 1.28× in Float32, which is near parity, and the RX 7900 XTX in fact has the wider memory bus. The advantage the old figure described is real but concentrated where the work is small: 2.21× and 3.06× at 64^3, 1.35× and 1.65× at 128^3, decaying to parity as the rows become bandwidth-bound — the signature of a launch-latency edge, not a throughput one. And the AMD card wins seven of the transfer class's eight rows at 256^3 Float64, which no previous comparison could have shown: the four spline gathers and scatters run at 0.53–0.68× the NVIDIA time (so 1.5–1.9× the speed), the two NUFFT rows sit near parity at 0.85–0.95×, and the one NVIDIA win is anterpolate! Lalescu(3,3) at 1.11×. Treat \"the fleet's fastest card\" as true only at small grids and in Float64 atomics.\nThat transfer verdict is a 2026-08-09 reading and decision 78 has since overturned part of it; the paragraph above is kept as the record of that date. On the table as regenerated 2026-08-31 the AMD card wins five of the eight, not seven, at 125–179times, and it now LOSES interpolate! CatmullRom (104times to NVIDIA) and both NUFFT directions (123–125times). The gather reversal is finding (aj)'s AMD-only walk regression arriving in a vendor comparison: that row reads 1.048 ms in the 2026-08-09 archive against 1.540 ms now.\n\nStructurally safe: everything measured on the CPU. gcsample was gated on the backend throughout, so decisions 38, 39 and 40, the threading work, finding (k)'s and (l)'s CPU numbers, and the CPU half of every split decision are untouched by this. That is most of the tuning record.\n\n(w) The fix for (s) never ran on CUDA — it called a function that does not exist, and the catch-all reported the failure as a measurement. The per-row policy asks the device pool how much it grew, and on the NVIDIA side it asked with CUDA.total_memory() - CUDA.available_memory(). CUDA.available_memory is not a binding in the CUDA.jl this tree resolves (CUDACore 6.2.0); the call raised UndefVarError on every row. Each row's probe is wrapped in a try/catch whose documented intent is \"unclassifiable ⇒ keep the old, safe behaviour\", so every row on CUDA fell back to gcsample = true — the exact regime (s) exists to remove.\n\nIt announced itself as a result. The run printed per-sample GC kept for N allocating row(s): … and named them, which reads as a measurement of N allocating rows. Those lists were each class's complete row list: operators reported all nine of its rows — laplacian!, curl!, divergence! and the rest — every one of which test/allocations.jl pins at zero bytes. That is the tell, and it is the same shape as (s)'s own: a device column whose in-place operations look like allocators is not measuring allocation. The condition was caught three classes into the first Enteropia re-sweep (2026-08-09) by noticing that add! scalar and spectral_cutoff cannot possibly be allocating rows.\n\nRoot cause is unexercised code, and it was flagged in advance. The policy was written on a host with no NVIDIA card, and the note queuing this re-sweep said in as many words that the CUDA branch \"has never been exercised, unlike the AMD path\". The AMD path was correct throughout — AMDGPU.used_memory() exists — so finding (s)'s AMD re-sweep and every conclusion drawn from it are unaffected. What follows is narrower and worse: no CUDA archive has ever been measured under the per-row policy, so the RTX 5070 Ti column remained in the pre-fix regime even after the fix shipped.\n\nBoth halves repaired. The call is now CUDA.used_memory(), the same name the AMD path already used, which makes the two vendors symmetric. Its semantics were verified rather than assumed: a 16 MiB CuArray moves it by exactly 16 MiB, reclaim returns it to zero, and it reads zero at rest — and being pool-scoped it ignores other processes on the card, where the device-wide total - free reported 32 MiB for that same 16 MiB array and would drift with anything else using the GPU. The second repair matters more than the first: apply_gcsample_policy! now returns the rows whose probe threw separately from the rows it classified, and prints a !! banner with the count and the first exception, plus an unmissable line when every row failed. A fallback that cannot be told apart from a result is how a broken instrument reports success.\n\nVerified after the repair, Float64 64^3 on the RTX 5070 Ti: operators nine rows, zero probe failures, zero rows keeping the collector; algebra ten rows, the same. The probe reproduces invariant 13 on CUDA exactly as it did on AMD, having been told nothing about it.\n\n(x) The storage class, measured at last: the identity is free, and the two bus directions are not symmetric. The class decision 63 added was swept on the RTX 5070 Ti on 2026-08-17 (42 rows, 64^3 to 256^3, both element types) and says three things.\n\nAdapting to the family a section already lives in costs 118 ns, flat. It reads 118.2–122.1 ns at every grid and both types — five orders of magnitude below the 198 ms a 256^3 Float64 vector takes to actually cross. Decision 63 documents that this case is the identity; this measures it, and the flatness is the proof: a copy would scale with the grid and this does not.\n\nDevice-to-host is about 4times slower than host-to-device, at every size and both types. A 256^3 Float64 scalar goes out in 603 ms and comes back in 2372 ms; the vector, 1977 against 7218 ms; in Float32, 246 against 1141 ms and 900 against 3627 ms. The outbound rate is approx 22 GB/s, which is a healthy PCIe 4.0 figure; the inbound is approx 56 GB/s, far below it. The asymmetry is therefore not the bus — it is that the return trip allocates its destination, a fresh pageable host array, and pays for the pages as well as the transfer. That is evidence for the open design question the roadmap already carries: whether adapt should gain an in-place form writing into a caller's preallocated destination. A consumer pulling a field back every few steps is currently paying an allocation it has no way to hoist.\n\nAnd the asymmetry is now actionable. Decision 69 added adapt!, which moves into a destination that already exists; measured on the same card and field, copyto! into a preallocated host array runs at 190 GiB/s against 51 for the allocating form, a 375times recovery that brings the return trip to the outbound rate. The storage class carries adapt! rows beside the allocating ones, so the comparison stays measured rather than remembered.\n\nAnd it is now measured, not projected. Decision 69's adapt! rows sit beside the allocating ones in the table since 2026-08-23: on the return trip it is 3.48–3.66× at 256^3, and outbound it is a wash (10times) — which is this finding's own diagnosis confirming itself, the device allocation being pooled and cheap so there was never a page cost to avoid that way. The gain also has a size threshold: a 128^3 Float64 scalar (16.8 MB) shows 101times where the vector on the same grid (50 MB) shows 348times, which is what page faults on fresh host pages look like — they bite only once an allocation outgrows the allocator's reused blocks.\n\nThe two non-section rows behave as their structure predicts. adapt multipliers is approx 13 µs independent of the grid — six O(N) per-axis arrays, so the cost is six transfers' fixed overhead rather than bytes — and adapt plans (rebuild) grows 74 to 235 µs with the grid, which is the FFT planning it redoes rather than anything copied.\n\nThe RX 7900 XTX was swept 2026-08-23 and confirms every structural claim above, while disagreeing on one magnitude. The identity is free there too (146–159 ns, flat across grids and both element types, against the 118–122 ns NVIDIA reads); the outbound adapt! is a wash (101–109); the return trip recovers 279–299 at 256^3 against NVIDIA's 348–366; and the size threshold reproduces exactly — a 128^3 Float64 scalar shows 099, no gain at all, where the vector on the same grid shows 306. Two structural properties holding on an independent runtime is what promotes them from a CUDA observation to a property of the operation.\n\nThe magnitude that differs is the outbound bus, and it is the vendor rather than the package. A 256^3 Float64 scalar crosses to the AMD card in 1142 ms against NVIDIA's 603 — an outbound rate of approx 118 GB/s against approx 22 — while the return trip is near parity (2440 against 2372 ms). So the 4times asymmetry this finding is named for is approx 21times on AMD, not because the return is faster but because the outbound is slower. The package issues one copyto! per component in both cases, so nothing here is ours to fix; the consequence for a consumer is only that the outbound leg is the one worth hoisting on AMD, where on NVIDIA it is nearly free. The same latency signature shows in adapt multipliers, which is approx 147 µs on AMD against approx 13 µs on NVIDIA: six small O(N) transfers, so the row prices per-transfer overhead, and an 11times gap there is the launch-latency profile these two cards show throughout the small-grid rows rather than anything about bytes.\n\n(y) The two materialize_kernel rows cannot be read ACROSS thread counts: their t1 cell depends on what ran before it in the same process. A corpus sweep flagged one cell as unexplained — materialize_kernel radial, Float64 512^3, i7-12700K, t2 reading 143.0 ms against t1 93.2 and t4 76.7, i.e. slower than both neighbours. It was re-measured on the assumption it was a transient. It is not. Two independent processes returned 143.18 and 143.57 ms, agreeing with each other to 0.3% and with the archive to 100times, and the neighbours reproduced too (t1 91.7, t4 76.6). The table is right.\n\nWhat is wrong is the comparison, not the number. Measured standalone, the operation scales cleanly at every thread count — 296 to 145 to 79 to 54 ms for t1/t2/t4/t8, a clean halving — so there is no threading defect. The t2 and t4 cells agree with standalone measurement to within 2%; the t1 cell does not, and is 32times faster in the suite than alone. The difference was bisected to a single ingredient by elimination: constructing the row through build_suite gives 295.5 ms, adding tune! gives 298.0, but running the whole class in one process — what characterize.jl does — gives 91.6 ms, reproducing the archive. So the row is sensitive to the 17 rows that stream through multi-GB fields ahead of it, and only at one thread. materialize_kernel separable behaves identically, which is what identifies the class: these are the only two rows that allocate an O(N^3) result per call rather than writing into a preallocated destination.\n\nFive hypotheses were tested and refuted, and they are recorded so nobody re-runs them: first-touch page faults (a recycled page-warm block and freshly mapped pages measure 297.8 against 299.9 ms); the kernel symbol (spectral_band(1,3), spectral_cutoff(N/3) and spectral_cutoff(2) are within 1% of each other); dead-code elimination, the most plausible of them, since bench_sync reads only its argument's type and the result is otherwise dead — but the exact harness expression measured standalone is 297.3 ms and forcing the data to be observed adds only 114times; evals, which tune! sets to 1 in every lane; and heap history, since holding 6 GB of live fields first changes nothing (300.2 → 296.2 ms). A bimodal distribution was excluded by sampling 93 times at t1, where the minimum is still 300.4 ms. The mechanism is therefore not identified — what is established is that the effect is real, reproducible, confined to t1, and confined to the two allocating rows.\n\nThe follow-up sweep (2026-08-24) drew the boundary by measurement. Every suite row allocating a field-sized result per call was measured standalone at t1, 512^3 Float64 against its archived cell. Clean in that cell: all eight allocating fft rows — standalone agrees with the archive within 3%, so per-call allocation is not the discriminator, and finding (h)'s ESTIMATE/MEASURE ratios rest on uncontaminated cells. Finding (at) breaks that verdict at 256^3 Float32. Affected: exactly five rows — the two materialize_kernel rows (28–32times), lpnorm vec Inf (163times), and radial_correlation/cross-correlation (12–17times, at t1 and t2, worst in Float32 at 512^3). The five allocate their result inside the operation (via similar/broadcast) rather than through a transform plan; whether that is the mechanism or merely coextensive with it is unestablished.\n\nThe archive is internally consistent, and needs no re-measurement. Re-running the archive's exact process shape — all classes, one process, 512^3 Float64 — reproduces the archived cell for every affected row at t1 (098–100times: lpnorm 222.1 against 223.6 ms, radial_correlation 5458 against 5581, cross-correlation 5607 against 5734, both materialize_kernel rows exact) and for the correlations at t2 (101–103times). A class-only process is a third regime: sufficient history for materialize_kernel (the convolution class reproduces its archive cells) but not for the reductions three, whose class-only values sit with standalone. So the fast values are not stale and not wrong — they are what the full sweep reproducibly measures — and the rule is that a cell from one process shape must never be compared against a measurement from another. The four rows decisions 71/72 added were checked the same way and are regime-insensitive (reality_residual vector 47.5 against the merged 47.4 ms; weighted_inner x3 fused 2.020 against 2.065 s), so their class-only-merged cells are comparable with their full-sweep column.\n\nOne cell is bistable and should not be trusted at all: materialize_kernel separable's t2. The archive reads 63.4 ms; three same-day measurements in three process shapes — class-only twice, full-sweep once — all read 156.5, and within the archive itself the cell sits below its own t4 (82.0 ms), the same inversion signature. The 2026-08-07 sweep evidently landed the fast regime at t2 for this row where every 2026-08-24 process lands the slow one, so regime selection at t2 is not even deterministic across sweeps. Both materialize_kernel rows' t4/t8 cells reproduce everywhere (radial 76.7/47.9, separable 82.0/50.3, matched today to 1%).\n\nThe consequence is a reading rule. For the two materialize_kernel rows, read thread scaling from t4 upward — both t1 (bistable, approx 3times) and t2 (bistable for separable) sit in history-selected regimes — and for lpnorm and the two correlation rows, compare their t1/t2 cells only against sweeps of the same process shape. Within RESULTS.md every CPU column is a full sweep, so the table is self-consistent; what is invalid is comparing those cells against a standalone probe or a class-only lane. This is the same class of defect as finding (s), a row reporting the harness rather than the operation, and it was found the same way — by re-measuring a number that looked wrong instead of explaining it.\n\n(z) A fused multiply-add is not faster here, and writing one to force cross-backend agreement was declined (2026-08-26). The CUDA parity suite found decision 73's displace! disagreeing with its CPU twin in the last bit. The cause is compiler contraction, established rather than assumed: the device folds x + c * v into a fused multiply-add — one rounding — where the host multiplies and then adds, two. On CUDA the device result equals the host's muladd form exactly, in both precisions and for the two-term call, and the host's own plain-against-muladd mismatch count (151 of 900 in Float64, 136 in Float32) matches the device-against-host count element for element.\n\nThe obvious repair is to write muladd on the CPU side so both contract identically. It was measured on all three grounds and fails on each.\n\nSpeed: none. The pass reads three arrays and writes one, so it is bandwidth-bound and the instruction count of the innermost expression is invisible behind the traffic. On the 9800X3D the ratios are 0.998 at 3 times 10^5 elements and 1.010 at 17 times 10^7 in Float64, 1.006 and 1.051 in Float32; at 900 elements, amortized over a thousand inner repetitions to clear the timer floor, 0.964 and 0.970 — two nanoseconds of a forty-six nanosecond call. On the 5070 Ti it changes nothing at all (0.999 and 1.003 at 17 times 10^7), which is the expected result and worth stating: the device already emitted the fused instruction, so naming it in the source cannot move anything.\nAccuracy: a better bound, not a better answer. Against a BigFloat reference the maximum error improves from 899 times 10^-16 to 795 times 10^-16 in Float64 and from 509 times 10^-7 to 454 times 10^-7 in Float32 — one rounding per term rather than two. Element by element it is not uniformly better: over 30,000 elements per type it wins 3162 and loses 1800 in Float64, wins 3116 and loses 1765 in Float32. A luckily-cancelling double rounding can land closer than a single one.\nFooting: the agreement is a vendor coincidence. It holds because the device compiler contracts and associates exactly as muladd does, which is documented nowhere. A future release changing contraction policy would reopen the divergence and turn a bitwise assertion red with nothing anyone could act on. That is the objection invariant 14 raises against resting a package guarantee on a vendor's behaviour, and it applies here as squarely as it does to a method table.\n\nScope decides the rest. The package uses muladd nowhere, and the pattern is systemic — eight alpha_beta codegen sites plus the explicit alpha a + beta b expressions in FiberSections, Points and Convolution, since the accumulation of invariant 10 is two multiplies and an add. So adopting it in displace! alone would fix the line rather than the class, and adopting it across the class is a numerics-changing sweep of every in-place operator, invalidating pass-audit and benchmark baselines, in exchange for a bound. The comparison moved to a tolerance instead, which is what TESTING.md already prescribed across backends.\n\n(aa) Rank-free transform plans: neutral on a device, a large win on the CPU — and the win is the planner's, not the plan shape's (2026-08-26, decision 76). The fft class was measured on three lanes before the change and again after, in the same session on an idle host, so this is a before/after rather than a comparison against an archive.\n\nCUDA: flat. Median 1000 over 48 rows, worst cell 1014, scalar and vector alike. The device gives back its per-rank plan arrays and asks nothing for them.\nCPU scalar rows: flat, and this is the control. Median 1000 at t1 and 0999 at t8 over 32 rows each. A rank-0 field is one component either way, so any movement here would have been the harness rather than the change — there is none.\nCPU vector rows under ESTIMATE, which is what the package plans with by default: median 0621 at t1 and 0651 at t8, best cell 0146. A 1.5-fold median speedup, and nearly sevenfold in the best case.\nCPU vector rows under MEASURE: median 0974 and 0973. Neutral.\n\nThe last two rows together say what the win actually is. The plan shape is neutral — given a properly planned transform, three single-component passes cost what one batched pass costs. What changed is that ESTIMATE plans a three-dimensional transform far better than it plans a four-dimensional batched one, and the package ships ESTIMATE. So the speedup is real and reaches every consumer that does not pass its own planner flags, but it is a planner-quality effect and would mostly vanish for a consumer already planning with MEASURE.\n\nThat also sharpens finding (h). Under ESTIMATE a 256^3 Float32 forward transform measured 513 ms against Float64's 496 ms — single precision should be about twice as fast, not equal — where under MEASURE the same cells read 108 ms and 180 ms. The default planner effort is leaving a factor of 47 on the floor at that size, which is worth more than this decision was, and is not addressed by it.\n\n(ab) Every \"slowdown\" in the 2026-08-28 re-sweep was the MEASUREMENT, not the code — and the method that settled it is the one to reuse (2026-08-28). Re-sweeping transfer and convolution on three lanes after the odd-width stencil fix flagged twenty rows against the archives. All twenty were chased to a cause. None is a code regression.\n\nThe instrument that settles it is a back-to-back A/B, not a repeat. A git worktree at the archive's own commit, with the current dependency manifest copied in so only package source differs, measured alternately with the new tree in the same thermal and memory state. Re-running the identical lane — which I did first — establishes stability and says nothing about cause: the GPU group reproduced to 1.3% while being wrong about the code.\n\nCPU 512^3 convolution (five rows, times 116–135). Back-to-back, old against new reads times 1012. The same binary, same shape, run four times, alternates 178.1, 159.5, 177.8, 158.7 ms — two stable bands 12 apart, chosen per process. Within one process the rows agree to 003, so a sweep is self-consistent and two sweeps need not be.\n\nThe band is not what the obvious suspects say. Six stored kernels allocated in ONE process all time 160–166 ms, so it is not the kernel array's placement. Pinning to a single core still alternates (158.6, 177.5, 177.3, 158.5), so it is not scheduling. Both bands report 0 transparent huge pages. A minimal three-array probe sits in the slow band always, while the full class reaches the fast one — which places this in finding (y)'s family: sensitivity to the process's allocation history, here selecting between two bands rather than shifting one row.\n\nCPU 128^3/256^3 at t4 (five rows). Back-to-back, the new tree is equal or faster: interpolate! Lalescu(3,3) 88.1 against 95.0 ms, trilinear 3.72 against 3.79, CatmullRom 14.36 against 14.55. The row convolve! vector ∘ radial re-measures at 8.195 ms against the archive's 8.277.\n\nDevice small grids (ten rows, up to times 149). Two regimes were being compared, not two codes: the archive's vintage keeps per-sample GC on 8 device transfer rows where the current harness keeps it on 0. Measured directly on the real row, new code, GC-free, interpolate! Lalescu(3,3) 64^3 Float32 reads 214.9 µs against the archive's 215.1 — exact agreement. The 320 µs in the sweep is the sweep's context.\n\nSo the stencil fix is free, and the evidence for that is the A/B rather than the sweep. The sweep also showed it — NUFFT rows flat at 256^3 and 512^3, where a per-point cost would appear first — but that reading was only as trustworthy as the comparison it rested on.\n\nConsequence, and it is a rule rather than an observation: a CLASS-SKIPPED LANE MUST NOT BE MERGED INTO A FULL-SWEEP TABLE. Skipping fft, algebra, operators, reductions and storage changes the process history that finding (y) shows several rows depend on, and it changes which rows the per-row gcsample probe classifies. The whole re-sweep was therefore REVERTED from the archives and RESULTS.md regenerated back to its previous content, verified byte-identical outside the generation header. Re-measure a class by sweeping the lane whole, or measure it as an A/B and keep the result out of the table.\n\n(ac) The weighted reductions ran Base's serial broadcast fold on CPU — 70× inner at eight threads — and now run the fixed tree (2026-08-30, from IT's codegen report). The weighted_inner reduction was GPU-first: one fused sum over a lazy Broadcasted, which a device parallelizes and a CPU hands to Base's serial cartesian fold. It never met the decision-30/40 backend split, so it sat at 14.7 ms at 128^3 Float64 on the 9800X3D at one thread AND at eight, against inner's 0.2 ms — in the consumer's per-stage hot loop.\n\nThe CPU leg now runs the fixed tree over contiguous axis-1 columns — bitwise thread-count-invariant, asserted in test/threading.jl's battery, both weight classes and the tuple form. Measured after: 0.44 ms at -t8 (33×) and 2.43 ms serial (6×); the three-weight tuple 24.3 → 1.16 ms (21×). The GPU branch is byte-identical and the CUDA suite is green over it.\n\nTwo traps inside the fix, both measured rather than read. An accumulator that a map closure captures while the loop reassigns it is BOXED — 15 MB allocated per 32^3 call, 462 ms serial — so the tuple accumulation is recursion on first/tail, no closure, 432 B. And the generic per-element getindex(::Broadcasted, CartesianIndex) costs 8.6× the stored class, so each kernel class gets an element source reproducing its _kernel_data expression exactly — the radial one is sigma((s_1+s_2)+s_3), the three-argument broadcast's own left fold — keeping the storage classes bitwise-equal to each other, which the suite pins.\n\nCross-box after-record (i7-12700K, IT's own re-measurement after taking the bump): the kappa^2 radial weight at 128^3 Float64 reads 19.2 → 4.49 ms serial and 1.08 ms at -t8 (17.8×), the ratio to same-size inner falling from ~12 to 1.6 — and the consumer's whole CPU step 248 → 202 ms (1.23×). Their 300-step checksum is identical to the pre-fix run at print precision and their frozen audits stay green, so the pathology is dead on both CPU generations with values preserved.\n\n(ad) The transfer was spending most of its time on cache misses it did not need, and on integer division (2026-08-30, user-directed). Two independent changes, measured on an RTX 5070 Ti and a Ryzen 9800X3D at the regime a consumer actually runs — 256^3 grid, 10^6 points, CatmullRom.\n\nThe periodic wrap was an integer division per stencil NODE. Each node index came from mod, which LLVM shared down to 32 idiv instructions per point — around 200 cycles of a non-pipelined divider, for arithmetic that is an increment and a compare. Two qualifications an adversarial re-measurement added: the 32 is the count at four nodes per axis, rising to 41 at eight; and it is per POINT, not per point per fibre, because loop-invariant motion hoists the divisions out of the fibre loop — nine fibre components cost the same divisions as three.\n\nThe stencil walks consecutive nodes, so each index is its predecessor plus one, wrapped: _wrap_step replaces the division with a conditional zero and keeps three mods per point for the starts. It is bitwise identical — only the index arithmetic changed, the multiply and accumulate order did not — verified over 48 configurations including a stencil wider than the grid, where the walk wraps more than once. Measured: CPU gather 13–21times, GPU gather 11–12times.\n\nThis is NOT the wrap-hoist §3 rejected: that one precomputed index tuples with ntuple and paid registers for them; this adds three integers and no arrays.\n\nBut the walk has the SAME failure mode at the widest stencil, and the first version of this finding missed it because every figure above was taken at CatmullRom. Swept across the scheme axis — a stencil of 2, 4, 6 and 8 nodes per axis, i.e. 8 to 512 nodes per point — the CPU gather reads:\n\nstencil R = 0 R = 1\n2 (trilinear) 133times 163times\n4 (CatmullRom) 135times 170times\n6 (LalescuSpline(2,1)) 185times 176times\n8 (LalescuSpline(3,3), LagrangeSpline(3)) 114times 086times\n\nAt eight nodes per axis the walk can be SLOWER, and the first two versions of this finding both described it wrongly. A third correction landed 2026-09-01: the table below is the GATHER, and the width-8 SCATTER — which no version of this finding ever swept — is far worse, 145–148times at 512^3 Float64 on every thread lane. See finding (am); the qualifications immediately below describe the gather corner and do not carry over to it.\n\nWhat survives adversarial re-measurement, interleaved and across seeds:\n\nThe sign flips with thread count. At one thread the walk is 117–122times FASTER on every width-8 scheme; the loss appears only from about four threads (086–096times at eight and sixteen). The table above is a -t8 table, which was not stated.\nThe magnitude is 4–14%, not 14%. Only LalescuSpline(3,2) reaches 14%; LalescuSpline(3,3) and LagrangeSpline(3) reach 4–7%. The recorded 617 → 720 ms was one scheme at the machine's default sixteen threads, generalized to a corner it does not describe.\nIt is R = 1 specifically, not \"with a fibre\". R = 2 is LESS affected than R = 1 (0.92–0.99 against 0.87–0.95), which falsifies the \"width 8 × 3 components\" framing — nine components would have to be worse than three, and they are not.\nThe recorded MECHANISM was backwards. The claim was that mod blocks vectorization and that the walk's registers cost more. Measured AVX instruction counts say the opposite: the mod nest emits 983–1588 vector instructions against the walk's 133–138, so the mod form is the VECTORIZED one and the walk's index-to-index dependence is what prevents it — and neither variant spills, so the register half had no support at all. The consistent story is the memory roof: the walk's per-thread time is lower, so it saturates bandwidth first and then forfeits the memory-level parallelism the independent mod indices retain. That also explains the thread-count sign flip, which a per-thread register argument cannot.\nThe cost is attributable to the innermost-axis walk specifically, confirmed by two probe variants: walking only the innermost axis reproduces the regression, and leaving mod there recovers it. The mechanism is the one that sank the ntuple hoist:\n\nthe walk keeps six live indices across the nest and makes each index depend on its predecessor, where mod computes all of them independently, and at width 8 × 3 components that serial chain and its registers cost more than the divisions saved. The GPU does not show it: across six schemes and three ranks every one of 36 cells is at least 103times, the range running to 128times. An earlier 093times cell recorded at trilinear R = 1 did not reproduce and was noise.\n\nIt is kept, and the corner is stated rather than fixed. A hybrid — walking the outer axes and leaving mod on the innermost — recovers width 8 but gives back the width-6 win (291.9 → 453.5 ms at R = 1, independently reproduced at 344.5 → 543.4). It is not strictly worse, as first recorded: against the pre-walk kernel it was never behind in 60 measured cells. It is worse against the shipped walk where the walk wins, which is the common case and the default scheme. Making the choice conditional on scheme, rank and backend would mean a second kernel body, which is exactly what decision 47 records going silently dead. The default scheme is width 4, where the walk wins on both backends, and the affected corner is a wide stencil on a vector field on CPU.\n\nPoints arrived in an order unrelated to the grid. A solver seeds points in its own order, so consecutive work-items touched unrelated parts of the field and every stencil was a fresh set of misses. The point_traversal_order builder groups them by block of cells and the transfer verbs take it as a traversal option (decisions 78, 80). At 256^3 with 10^6 points:\n\n256^3, 10^6 points GPU gather GPU scatter CPU gather CPU scatter\nFloat64 scalar 340times 373times 201times 155times\nFloat64 vector 452times 406times 286times 226times\nFloat32 scalar 710times 310times 137times 112times\nFloat32 vector 1243times 406times 396times 194times\n\nThe ordering payoff RISES with stencil width, which the CatmullRom-only sweep could not show. Device gather at 256^3, 10^6 points, Float64: 186times at 2 nodes per axis, 339times at 4, 384times at 6, 369times at 8 — and on CPU it rises monotonically to 695times (Float64 vector, width 8). A wider stencil reads more nodes per point, so there is more traffic for locality to save. The CPU scatter moves the other way, 153times at width 4 down to 115times at width 8, as the atomic accumulation comes to dominate.\n\nAt grids past 256^3 the two separate. At 512^3 with 10^6 points the density falls eightfold and CatmullRom gets only 138–199times, while LalescuSpline(3,3) gets 459–568times; at 1024^3 (Float32, 4times 10^6 points, 8 GiB of fields on a 16 GB card) it is 121times against 429times. So the option matters most exactly where a run is most expensive — a wide stencil on a large grid — and least for a narrow stencil on a sparse large grid.\n\nAt 4times 10^6 points the device gains hold or grow — the Float64 vector scatter reaches 554times and the scalar 500times — because the unordered kernel degrades as the point set outgrows cache while the ordered one does not.\n\nBuilding the order cost 0.098–0.13 ms on device against a gather it saves 1.7 ms on, so it repays inside a SINGLE transfer — where the estimate it replaces guessed 12–24, having assumed a vendor sort. Validating the permutation (the adversarial review below) roughly doubled that to 0.24–0.27 ms, which still repays six times over in one transfer; the figure is quoted post-validation everywhere else, since that is the code that ships. It is not a sort: a counting pass over blocks of cells, three portable kernels, no new dependency. The vendor sortperm over the same keys costs 2.8 ms, 25times more.\n\nThree things were measured and NOT adopted, which is the more useful half.\n\nMorton order loses to plain lexicographic tile order (3.10 vs 3.49 on the 256^3 Float64 scatter), and the same result is published: Domínguez et al. (2011) find lexicographic beats Z-order in SPH and name the discriminator — Morton wins where cell access is RANDOM, lexicographic where it is a sequential sweep, which a sorted stencil transfer is. The recorded plan had assumed Morton.\n\nStability within a bucket buys nothing, within noise everywhere but one cell, so the atomic placement stands and no backend split was built for it. One bucket per cell is not better and costs 14times more to build (1.55 ms against 0.11 at 256^3), the 167 million buckets dominating the cumulative sum; including the build, the default 4 is optimal on both backends.\n\nWhere it does not pay, which the option exists for. At 64^3 with 10^5 points every backend is launch-bound and building an order for one transfer loses (09times including the build). On CPU at Float32 scalar the field is small enough to sit in a 96 MB V-Cache, so there is little to win and the order's own scattered reads of the coordinates cost more than they save.\n\nAnd the headline ratios measure the INPUT, not the method — the sharpest correction an adversarial re-measurement produced, since every figure above was taken with uniformly random point positions, which is the worst possible order. Re-seeding the same count four ways (Float32 vector gather, 256^3, 10^6 points): uniformly random 1228times, a lattice in sweep order 093times, a lattice jittered by one cell 098times, and a lattice advected far enough to decorrelate 509times. The ORDERED arm barely moves across all four (0.313–0.433 ms) — the traversal order normalises the access pattern — while the baseline varies eighteenfold. So the honest span for a consumer is 093–12times, and what the option repairs is a bad order rather than improving a good one: a lattice-seeded set runs the UNORDERED kernel faster than any ordered configuration. The docstring's original justification — that a solver's seeding order is usually unrelated to the grid — was backwards for a sweep-seeded set and has been corrected. The traversal order gives locality to the FIELD reads and takes it from the COORDINATE reads, which is why it grows with tensor order — a vector field reads three components per point against one coordinate triple.\n\n(au) An operand count the caller chooses now costs nothing extra to allocate, at any count, and its time is exactly the traffic the grouping adds (2026-09-26, measured 2026-09-27; decision 88).\n\nThe four operations whose operand count the caller chooses were measured at 9 and 33 operands on Enteropia, with every operand written out at the call site so the caller's own splat is not counted.\n\nAt one thread add! and subtract! over fields and over point values, displace! in all four forms, and map! over six tensor sources each allocate zero bytes. At four threads the field add! carries about 9.6 KB of Base.Threads task state per pass, so 13.7 KB at 9 operands and 48.2 KB at 33, where each pass is one threaded assignment. The point-value forms, below the threading cutoff at 4000 points, stay at zero. The tuple form of weighted_inner allocates exactly what one single-weight call does, 368 B, once per group of four weights: 1104 B for 9 weights and 3312 B for 33.\n\nThe same probe on the commit before the change read 775 MB per call for add! over 33 fields, 211 MB over 33 point-value sections, 139 MB for 33 weights, 0.6 MB for the torus displace! over 33 terms, and 57 MB for map! over six tensor fields. The field figures are tens to thousands of times the storage of the fields involved (332 KB for one vector field, 1 MB for one tensor field), so they were paid per element rather than once per call.\n\nThe time was measured on 2026-09-27 on Enteropia, with benchmark rows added to the suite for it (add! scalar x8/x9/x16, displace! 8/9/33-term, weighted_inner x4/x33 fused and x33 separate), Float64, minimum times.\n\nGrouping costs exactly the memory traffic it adds. Nine add! operands take two passes, the second re-reading and re-writing the partial sum, so 12 array transfers where one pass would need 10; sixteen take 21 where one pass would need 17. Priced per array from the eight-operand row, that model predicts CUDA at 256^3 to within 1%: 1976 us against 1984 measured for nine operands, 3458 against 3488 for sixteen. The CPU agrees to within about 10% at 256^3 and 512^3. Past eight operands add! therefore runs about 20–24% slower than a single pass would.\n\nThe displace! rows follow the same model: 33 terms cost 4.3 times the eight-term time on one CPU thread, as predicted, and 3.9 times on CUDA. The one outlier is eight threads at 256^3, 8.6 times, where the eight-term working set largely fits the 96 MB cache and the 33-term one does not; at 512^3, where neither fits, the ratio returns to 4.1.\n\nFor weighted_inner the grouped tuple still beats one call per weight at every point measured — 33 weights at 256^3 take 391 ms against 564 on one CPU thread, 57 against 188 at eight, 15.1 against 23.1 on CUDA, and at 64^3 on CUDA 0.51 against 1.12 ms — so the nine traversals cost less than the 33 they replace.\n\n(at) The 2026-09-20 Enteropia five-lane re-sweep: flat against 55 commits, and one apparent 175times win that belongs to the sweep rather than to the package (2026-09-20/21; the archives it replaces were stamped 6581b433 @ 2026-08-31). Five lanes ran green — CPU t1/t2/t4/t8 at 57, 49, 41 and 41 minutes, the t8 lane split one grid per process per §4(q) and needing no retry, and the CUDA lane at 57 minutes.\n\nAgainst the archives the medians are 0.9974, 0.9985, 0.9988 and 0.9989 over the 752 rows each CPU lane shares with its predecessor, and 1.0000 over 606 device rows. Asking which rows are slower than 110times on at least three of the four CPU lanes returns nothing, bar two kernel builders whose absolute times are 1.0 ns and 1.3 ns — one clock tick, and they move both ways. The canary is green on all three baselined lanes, with 36 mechanism rows judged invariant.\n\nEvery row the diff calls added or dropped reconciles, which is the check that would catch a class quietly emptying itself. The projector reality_projection! contributes 16 rows (decision 83), point_traversal_order! eight (decision 80), the eight transfer_order rows are those same rows renamed and report.jl's alias map already carries them, and decision 85's resolution verbs are measured for the first time — 72 rows on a CPU lane and 54 on the device.\n\nThe finding is that the Float32 256^3 vector transform depends on what ran before it in the process, and the dependence is worth 175times. The row reads 72.33 ms in the archive and 40.07 ms in the new sweep, which looks like a large win and is not one. Four measurements place it:\n\nmeasurement of to_spectral vector, Float32 256^3, t4 minimum time\narchive full sweep, 2026-08-31 72.33 ms\nnew full sweep, then a repeat of the whole lane 40.07 ms, 41.29 ms\nthe fft class alone, all four grids 72.17 ms\nthe fft class alone at this one grid and element type, twice 70.69 ms, 68.55 ms\n\nTwo candidate causes are excluded by evidence rather than by argument. The transform machinery is byte-identical across the two commits apart from identifier renames, so no package change can produce the movement; and FFTW_jll did not move, the benchmark manifest predating the archive it is compared against.\n\nThe FFTW-wisdom hypothesis was put as a falsifiable prediction, and it failed. Were planner state accumulated by the measure rows at the other grids the cause, running the fft class alone across all four grids would reproduce the fast number; it returns the slow one. The cause therefore lies outside that class, in what a full sweep runs before it, and it is unattributed. The kernel moved from 7.0.0-30-generic to -31 between the two sweeps and the older kernel is gone, so that candidate can no longer be tested — the terminal state of §4(p).\n\nOne reading of the table follows: this cell is comparable only against a same-shaped sweep. Every CPU column in RESULTS.md is a full sweep, so the table stays self-consistent.\n\nA claim worth not making is that a vector row reading below its scalar sibling is by itself impossible — the archived 512^3 Float32 cell shows that ratio below one too. What carries the finding is the disagreement between regimes, not the ratio.\n\n(as) CUDA unified memory runs the package unchanged, at about 8% when everything fits, and the benchmark harness cannot measure it (2026-09-16/17, Enteropia: RTX 5070 Ti 16 GB on an x86 PCIe host, CUDA.jl 6.3.0; decision 84). With CUDA.jl's default_memory preference set as Running on a GPU describes, every allocating route of the package lands in unified memory and the GPU suite passes there (494 of 494, with 44 further checks of the unified lane itself). The first attempt used the section name CUDA.jl's own template shows, [CUDA], and changed nothing: every array stayed in device memory, which only a residency check reveals.\n\nThe cost when everything fits, a 512^3 Float64 scalar transform round trip with five field pairs (10 GiB) resident: 54.3 ms on device memory, 58.4–59.0 ms on unified memory, reproduced — about 8%. The likely cause, not isolated, is CUDA.jl prefetching each unified array to the GPU at every kernel launch, a driver call that costs something even when the pages are already there.\n\nThe cost when the fields exceed the card, ten pairs (20 GiB) on the 15.5 GiB card, each round trip touching one pair in turn: 155–161 ms, 27times the unified run that fits and 29times device memory. No out-of-memory error, no hang, values intact. Pages migrate over PCIe here, so the figure describes this host's link and this one streaming pattern; it is not a prediction for another host, and for Grace Hopper in particular see gap 9.\n\nWhy the harness refuses the configuration. The per-row GC policy (§4(s)) reads CUDA.used_memory(), which does not count unified allocations: it reported 0.0 GiB with 10–20 GiB of unified fields live, against 13.03 GiB for the same fields in device memory. Every row would read as non-allocating and run GC-free, so characterize.jl, regress.jl and the CUDA runbenchmarks.jl now stop with an error when CUDA arrays are unified. Unified memory also serves element-by-element access through the host without honouring allowscalar(false), so a unified run cannot re-check invariant 6.\n\n(ar) A fresh real-to-complex transform satisfies the reality condition only to round-off, and which sizes are exact is the library's business, not the package's (2026-09-05, measured while deciding decision 83). The reality_residual of to_spectral of a random real field, in coefficient units, Float32 / Float64: FFTW reads exactly 0 at 32^3, 64^3 and 128^3 and 13times10^-4 / 25times10^-13 at 96^3, 74times10^-4 / 16times10^-12 at 256^3; rocFFT reads nonzero at every size, 20times10^-5 / 48times10^-14 at 32^3 rising to 82times10^-4 / 36times10^-12 at 256^3. The consumer measured cuFFT behaving like rocFFT.\n\nSo decision 71's \"satisfies the constraint by construction\" was a fact about FFTW's power-of-two codelets, and a forced solver turns that floor into the whole field at a rate of A - nukappa^2 per time unit (decision 83).\n\nThe projector's rows, measured once the box was idle (the same day; the other 80 operators rows reproduced the day-old archives at medians 0998–1001, every cell within 15%, which is the evidence of idleness). Scalar Float64 at one thread runs 20, 102, 449 and 3140 us across 64^3–512^3 against the residual's 80, 362, 1586 and 13258 — 42times faster at 512^3 while also writing, since the fused traversal allocates no plane temporaries; eight threads give 95times at 512^3. On the RX 7900 XTX the cells sit at 27–48 us, launch-bound, where the residual reads 238–244. The rows enter RESULTS.md at the next sweep; src/ moved with the decision, so a class splice is not licensed.\n\n(aq) The AMD-only gather regression has its mechanism: on gfx1100 the walk kernel spills, and CatmullRom runs at HALF the occupancy it had (2026-09-04, closing the question Enteropia's CUDA A/B left open in finding (al)). The evidence is the compiled kernel, read from the code object rather than inferred from timings: _interpolate_kernel! at 256^3 Float64, dumped through the compiler for the pre-decision-78 commit 7bbbc75a and for HEAD, for the regressed scheme and for the flat one.\n\nCatmullRom, the 174times regression: 91 VGPRs, no scratch and 16 waves per EU under mod; 192 VGPRs, 292 bytes of scratch and 8 waves per EU under the walk, with 72 scratch loads and stores in the emitted code against 14.\nLalescu(3,3), flat at 098–105times: 104 → 107 VGPRs, occupancy 12 → 12, scratch traffic 14 → 5. Nothing moved.\n\nSo the register allocator, given the walk's chained indices for the 4-node stencil, keeps roughly twice as many values live, crosses the RDNA3 allocation tier that halves the resident wavefronts, and spills the remainder into the hot loop; the 8-node stencil hands it a loop it treats differently and loses nothing. Decision 78's own record that \"neither variant spills\" was a HOST measurement and is false on this card for this scheme.\n\nBoth signs are now measured rather than argued. The walk executes about 25times fewer instructions (2150 against 5261 for CatmullRom, the per-node mod lowering to long integer-division sequences), which is the win wherever the resident waves already hide the latency — every 128^3 cell, both Lalescu grids, the whole CUDA table. The loss appears exactly where latency hiding is what the kernel needs and occupancy is what was halved: CatmullRom at 256^3 Float64, the one measured cell whose field exceeds the Infinity Cache. Finding (aj)'s cache boundary was therefore the place the mechanism becomes VISIBLE, not the mechanism, which is why the same boundary predicted nothing on the other vendor.\n\nA prediction the other host can falsify at no cost. If this is right, the walk kernel compiled for sm120 does NOT cross a register tier: its register count and occupancy for CatmullRom should sit where the mod kernel's did, which one `@devicecode_ptx/sass` dump would show. If the CUDA kernel also spilled and also halved its occupancy, this account is wrong and the vendor split has another cause.\n\nWhat to do about it, if anything, is a design question and is not taken here. The lever is register pressure at one scheme, not the walk's arithmetic — a kernel that kept the walk's instruction count and gave the allocator fewer simultaneously live indices for the 4-node stencil would keep the win everywhere, and decision 47 forbids a second body conditioned on scheme. The roadmap's route, advancing a small block of stencil indices independently, is also the shape that lowers live-range length, so the two goals point the same way. The corner stays documented, as decision 78 records.\n\n(ap) The 2026-09-04 Aiporetne re-sweep: five lanes flat to four decimals, the stale ordered rows repaired, and a report defect a hand edit had been hiding (the queue asked for a transfer-only splice; src/ had moved by nine files since these archives' b452fb17, so the splice tool's own rule applied and the lanes were re-swept whole). Medians against the 2026-08-31 archives: CPU t1/t2/t4/t8 09991/09999/10001/10000 over 752 rows, GPU 10005 over 663 — the harness reproduces itself, and a replication scan across the four thread lanes finds zero rows slower in all four at any threshold. Canaries green on all three lanes and refrozen.\n\nThe movers are finding (ak)'s repair and nothing else. Every ordered transfer row runs faster — 056–087times on the CPU lanes and down to 022times on the device, so interpolate! CatmullRom ordered at 256^3 Float64 on the 7900 XTX falls 1811.7 → 713.1 us, against 1545 us unordered — the 3–4times win this file recorded while RESULTS.md reported a loss. The transfer class medians sit at 0989–0994, the ordered rows being the only ones that moved.\n\nThe regeneration then exposed a defect in the REPORT, and it is worth the record because a hand edit had made it invisible. Decision 80 renamed the row transfer_order to point_traversal_order, and d828f406 renamed it in RESULTS.md by editing the table — eighteen lines — rather than in the tool that generates it. Every Enteropia archive still carries the old key, so the first regeneration after the rename split the row in two: this host's cells under the new name, Enteropia's under the old, and — opposite each.\n\nreport.jl now carries a row-alias map applied as an archive is READ, so a column keeps its honest commit= vintage and no archive is rewritten. The check that caught it is the one to run after any future rename: every Enteropia cell must be bit-identical after a regeneration that did not touch its archives, and it read eight differing until the alias landed.\n\nThe ROCm suite was run first, as the queue ordered: 491/491, exit 0 in 5m16s, the predicted count exactly, so decisions 80 and 82 are staged on AMD and _same_backend, invariant 14's predicate, has been exercised on every device row. Both benchmark gates report every transfer point set unmutated (CPU 192 rows, ROCm 206, 0 failed).\n\n(am) A SHIPPED REGRESSION nobody had found: decision 78's walk costs 145–148times on the width-8 CPU scatter at 512^3 Float64, and the corner (ad) documents is not that corner (2026-09-01, from a regression hunt over the committed history of RESULTS.md rather than over prose — the question being not whether a claim is worded well but whether anything shipped slower).\n\nThe measurement, and the archive step is NOT the evidence. anterpolate! Lalescu(3,3) at 512^3 Float64 on the 9800X3D sat at 3350 ms (t4) for twenty consecutive sweeps and stepped to 5135 ms at the first sweep carrying decisions 76–79. That step alone would not settle anything, because the harness changed in the same range: decision 78 added seven rows to this very group, three of them allocating 512^3 vector fields, so roughly 3 GB more sits live while the row runs — which can move a neighbour with no kernel changing.\n\nSo the claim rests on an A/B that bypasses the benchmark group entirely — a standalone script building one scalar field and one point set and timing anterpolate! directly, byte-identical in both arms, with no BenchmarkTools, no gcsample policy and no characterize.jl. Against 7bbbc75a, the last commit before decision 78, interleaved A-B-A-B:\n\n pre-78 HEAD ratio\nt4, repeat 1 3384.9 ms 4952.4 ms 1463times\nt4, repeat 2 3389.6 ms 4941.8 ms 1458times\nt1, single 12707 ms 18419 ms 1449times\n\nEach arm reproduces itself to 0.2% and its own archive to within 3%. Decision 79 cannot touch a scatter, so the walk is the only candidate in the range.\n\nIt is one cell, and the scoping is the useful part. Across scheme × grid × element type × thread count on this host, everything else moved to 073–111times — flat or faster:\n\nanterpolate!, after/before 9800X3D (96 MB V-Cache) 12700K (25 MB L3)\ntrilinear, 512^3 Float64 074–079times 076–095times\nCatmullRom, 512^3 Float64 095–097times 059–083times\nLalescu(3,3), 256^3 Float64 096–101times 061–065times\nLalescu(3,3), 512^3 Float32 100–111times 056–064times\nLalescu(3,3), 512^3 Float64 129–151times 057–065times\n\nSo the widest stencil, the largest grid, Float64, on ONE host — and on the other host that same cell is the largest WIN in the table. Nine smaller flags accompany it, all NUFFT on this host (117–129times), which spreads through the same kernels and is plausibly the same effect at a smaller grid; that is unconfirmed.\n\nWhy it was missed, and it is the session's own failure mode again. Finding (ad) swept the scheme axis and recorded a width-8 corner of \"4–14%, above four threads, R = 1\" — but it swept the gather. The scatter was left at CatmullRom, so the width-8 scatter was never measured at all, and the corner as documented understates this cell by an order of magnitude and gets its thread dependence backwards: here every lane regresses, including t1, where (ad) records the walk running 117–122times FASTER. A sweep that covers one verb is not a sweep of the axis.\n\nIt also supplies the mechanism (aj) and (al) were left without, as a hypothesis worth testing. The walk regresses on the 9800X3D (96 MB V-Cache) and the RX 7900 XTX (96 MB Infinity Cache), and helps on the i7-12700K (25 MB L3) and the RTX 5070 Ti (approx 48 MB L2). The split is by last-level cache SIZE, not by vendor and not by whether the working set fits — a 512^3 Float64 field is 1 GB and fits nothing.\n\nThe reading that covers all four platforms: the walk serialises the stencil's address chain, and what that gives up is worth most exactly where the memory system could have served many independent misses at once. It supersedes (aj)'s working-set account, which (al) falsified without replacing, and it is falsifiable — a third large-cache platform should regress, a third small-cache one should not.\n\nNot fixed, and deliberately. Making the walk conditional on scheme, rank, verb or host means a second kernel body, which is what decision 47 records going silently dead. The corner is now documented where it actually is; reversing it needs the route the roadmap records — not a branch, but an implementation that keeps the cheaper arithmetic without serialising the chain.\n\nOn proportion, with the same caution applied to the good news. The cross-boundary ledger reads 233 transfer cells faster against 10 slower, but that count comes from the TABLE and so inherits the harness change above; on this host it also compares a transfer-only re-measurement against a full sweep (finding (y)). The half that is methodology-clean is the 12700K, full sweep against full sweep, and there every transfer cell improved, 056–095times. Decision 78 is strongly net-positive. It has one bad cell, and it is on the grid decision 37 tunes for.\n\n(al) The CUDA arm of the A/B finding (aj) handed over: decision 76 costs on BOTH vendors, and decision 78's device gather regression is AMD-only (2026-08-31 on Enteropia; old arm ef70e913, both arms run back-to-back on an idle RTX 5070 Ti with matched CHAR_SIZES/CHAR_TYPES/CHAR_SKIP, so process history matches, and with the two dependency stacks pinned identical).\n\nThe old arm the queue named was the wrong commit, and its own caveat is what caught it. 93d9d8da predates d5f6814a, which repaired the gcsample probe on CUDA (§4(w): CUDA.available_memory is not a binding there, so every row fell back to the per-sample collector). Measured from that commit the transfer rows run at three samples with gcsample = true against 668 samples GC-free in the new arm — two measurement disciplines rather than two versions of a kernel, and an arm that would have reported the new code faster for the wrong reason.\n\nComparing per-row evals, samples and gcsample is what surfaced it, which is the check the handover asked for. The arm was rebuilt at ef70e913, the last commit before decision 76 and after the probe repair, with GPUCompiler pinned to 2.4.1 in both.\n\nDecision 76 regresses on CUDA, with the signature ROCm gave. At 64^3:\n\nvector, 64^3 Float32 old new ratio\nto_spectral 22.0 us 29.7 us 135times\nto_physical! 32.7 us 40.1 us 123times\nto_physical 37.7 us 45.0 us 120times\nto_physical! scratch 35.4 us 42.2 us 119times\n\nvector, 64^3 Float64 old new ratio\nto_spectral 111.0 us 126.1 us 114times\nto_physical! 122.1 us 137.6 us 113times\nto_physical! scratch 127.0 us 142.0 us 112times\nto_physical 130.7 us 145.7 us 111times\n\nThe eight scalar controls — the same four verbs at each element type — span 099–101times.\n\nVector 111–135times against a flat scalar control is what ROCm showed at 115–161times, so the per-component loop costs on both vendors. The roadmap item's own precondition — both hosts agreeing before a route is chosen — is therefore met.\n\nBut the cost is confined to 64^3, and the first version of this finding did not say so because it had only measured there. Extending the same A/B to 128^3 and 256^3 — same arms, same flags — the vector rows run flat or FASTER against scalar controls at 098–100:\n\nvector, new/old 128^3 Float32 128^3 Float64 256^3 Float32 256^3 Float64\nto_spectral 103 103 094 100\nto_physical 093 096 090 100\nto_physical! 087 096 089 100\n\nRead the in-place row first: it carries hundreds of samples GC-free, where the two allocating verbs run at three samples with a per-sample collector in BOTH arms — comparable, but coarse.\n\nThat grid dependence is what the mechanism predicts. Three plan executions where there was one is a FIXED cost per call, decisive when the transform itself takes approx 20 us and invisible when it takes milliseconds. ROCm agrees, its intermediate grids washing out at 084–116times. So the per-component loop costs on both vendors at 64^3 and is neutral-to-positive on both across the 256^3–512^3 production band decision 37 names, which makes the roadmap item a small-grid inefficiency rather than a regression against the grids that matter.\n\nDecision 78's walk does not regress on this card; it wins in every cell. All 24 gather and scatter cells over two grids, two element types and three stencil widths land at or below 1002times, the scatter rows moving less (077–100) than the gather:\n\ngather, new/old 128^3 Float32 128^3 Float64 256^3 Float32 256^3 Float64\ntrilinear 072 078 093 085\nCatmullRom 095 093 090 089\nLalescu(3,3) 056 046 062 086\n\nThe falsifiable prediction fails, and the mechanism behind it does not generalise either. Finding (aj) predicted that 256^3 Float32 (67 MB) would regress WORSE on this card's approx 48 MB L2 than the approx 122times it cost on the 7900 XTX's 96 MB Infinity Cache, the field exceeding the smaller cache. It measures 090times, a win.\n\nThe weaker claim fails too: the sign is said to track the working set monotonically, and here CatmullRom's win GROWS with it (095times at 8.4 MB to 089times at 134 MB) while Lalescu(3,3)'s SHRINKS (046times to 086times), so two schemes move in opposite directions across the same boundary. The cache-boundary account describes the ROCm measurements and is not a property of the walk; (aj)'s statement of it is corrected in place.\n\nWhat that leaves open. The AMD gather regression is real and was reproduced three times there, and it is now known to be vendor-specific rather than a consequence of the walk. Naming its mechanism needs the card, so it is Aiporetne's; the candidates are the AMDGPU backend's handling of the serialised address chain and the occupancy that chain costs, and neither is testable from this host. Decision 78 stands unqualified on CUDA.\n\n(ak) The benchmark's ordered transfer rows had been timing a STALE traversal order since decision 78 shipped, so RESULTS.md reported that feature as a LOSS while this file reported it as a 34times win (found 2026-08-31 on Enteropia while reading the transfer class for finding (al)'s A/B; it is a harness defect, and the package is not implicated).\n\nThe class built visit_order = point_traversal_order(grid, points) and then handed the SAME PointSet to its displace! and wrap! rows, which move points in place. Every row in a group is executed many times before its own trial — by tune!, by the device gcsample probe, and by run — so the points had moved long before the ordered rows were timed.\n\nThe displace! verb applies a FIXED per-point direction field, so this is a drift rather than a random walk: at c = 001 over 10^4 calls each point translates by approx 100, some sixteen domain lengths, and by a different amount per point. The order stayed a valid permutation throughout, so nothing errored and no check fired; it simply stopped describing where the points were.\n\nThe unordered rows were never affected, which is why this survived. Drifted-then-wrapped positions are as uniformly distributed as fresh ones, so the baseline every ordered row is compared against measured correctly the whole time.\n\nWitness on the RTX 5070 Ti, 256^3 Float64, 621378 points, CatmullRom gather:\n\n ms\nunordered, fresh points 1.486\nordered, fresh order 0.489\nunordered, after the drift 1.487\nordered, STALE order 1.749\nordered, order rebuilt for the moved points 0.487\n\nThe archived row reads 1.756 ms against that stale 1.749, and the archived unordered row 1.497 against 1.486 — the archive is reproduced by the DEFECT, not by the fix.\n\nThe repair moves only what it should. Re-measuring the class with the motion rows on their own copy of the positions, medians of fresh over archived:\n\nlane control (88 rows) motion (32) ordered (32)\nCUDA 0997 0997 0589\nCPU t1 1000 0999 0687\nCPU t2 1004 0999 0680\nCPU t4 1002 1002 0668\nCPU t8 0998 1001 0615\n\nA second device confirmation came free: two transfer-only processes, one on the old harness and one on the new, reproduce every UNORDERED row to 099–1001 while the ordered rows move 0099–0319. The point_traversal_order build row also improves 078–080times on device, the same root cause — drifted coordinates saturate the tile index, so the counting histogram collides on a few boundary counters.\n\nThe sign flips, and the repaired columns now agree with finding (ad)'s bespoke script, whose figures were right all along (306times here against its 340, and 403 against 452 for the vector gather, the residual being density — 621 thousand points against its 10^6):\n\ninterpolate! CatmullRom, 256^3 Float64 unordered ordered speedup\n9800X3D t1 (repaired) 105.58 ms 41.48 ms 255times\n9800X3D t8 (repaired) 15.23 ms 7.42 ms 205times\nRTX 5070 Ti (repaired) 1.49 ms 0.49 ms 306times\n12700K t8 (still stale) 35.65 ms 45.66 ms 078times\nRX 7900 XTX (still stale) 1.54 ms 1.81 ms 085times\n\nThe lesson is the contradiction, not the bug. Two committed artefacts described one operation and disagreed by a factor of four for a day — RESULTS.md had the traversal order costing 10–25%, finding (ad) had it saving 34times — and neither was read against the other, because a bespoke study and the standing harness are consulted at different moments and for different questions. Where both exist for one operation, the disagreement is itself a measurement: reconcile them, or one of them is wrong.\n\nNo existing instrument could have caught it, which is the second lesson. The canary measures the package and builds its own state per mechanism, so it has no shared-mutation hazard — and no ordered mechanism either. Pkg.test() does not execute the benchmark suite. The tiny-grid gate runs every row once, which catches a row that no longer dispatches (the decision-60 class) but cannot see a difference that takes thousands of samples to develop.\n\nWhat catches this class is an assertion about the harness's OWN invariant — a row must not mutate state another row's setup captured — and benchmark/gate.jl now makes it one: it snapshots each transfer point set, runs every row once, and requires the coordinates unchanged. One displace! call already breaks it, so a single pass suffices. It was verified in BOTH directions rather than assumed: exit 0 with the fix (190 rows on CPU, 204 on CUDA), exit 1 with the fix reverted, naming both tags.\n\nOnly half the archives could be repaired here. This host's five were re-measured and spliced with benchmark/replace_class.jl, which verified all 2930 untouched rows bit-identical; Aiporetne's four 12700K lanes and the RX 7900 XTX still carry the defect and are marked in RESULTS.md until that host repeats it. Splicing rather than re-sweeping is honest here because src/ is identical between the archives' commit and HEAD, so the untouched rows measure the same package and the archive's commit= vintage tag stays truthful; had the package moved, the lane would have needed a full sweep.\n\nA jitter result fell out of checking that splice, and it contradicts standing guidance. Four independent t1 measurements of identical code put trilinear at 256^3 Float32 at 10.5, 12.8, 17.1 and 12.3 ms — a 162times spread — with CatmullRom at 149times and Lalescu(3,3) at 134times, while their Float64 twins in the very same processes span 104–106.\n\nThe three move together within a run and were monotone in run order, so this is per-process state and possibly drift rather than random scatter; the flat Float64 twins rule out a machine-wide cause. The standing advice to compare at 256^3 or above does not cover those three rows, and RESULTS.md now says so.\n\n(aj) The 2026-08-31 Aiporetne re-sweep: the CPU half is intended fixes and nothing else, and the ROCm half found a cross-backend regression no AMD run had staged (this host's first sweep since 2026-08-07, so decisions 63–79 enter its archives at once; the Enteropia twin is finding (ai)). Medians against the previous archives: CPU t1/t2/t4/t8 0997/0994/0996/0995 over 664 rows each, GPU 1002 over 581. The harness reproduces itself, and the movement is confined to the changed operations.\n\nThe movers each match a recorded mechanism. The transfer class median is 092–095 on all four CPU lanes, the decision-78 wrap walk, with interpolate! CatmullRom at 035–049times. Vector exponential_convolve! runs 32–39times against a scalar twin spanning 0998–1012times, which is decision 79's R ge 1 scope measured rather than asserted. The reduction weighted_inner gains 25–73times across every shape (finding (ac)), and the decision-76 vector transforms up to 24times at 512^3 Float32. Canaries are green on all three lanes and refrozen.\n\nDecision 76 is a cross-backend divergence, and this is its first AMD measurement. The rank-free plans gaining up to 24times on host vector transforms COST on ROCm. The mechanism is the per-component loop itself: _forward_components! issues one mul! per component, so a rank-1 transform became three single-component plan executions where it had been one batched execution over three. An A/B against the old commit, both arms measured back-to-back on an idle card, gives 115–161times slower for vector transforms at 64^3, against 100–109times for the scalar controls in the same run.\n\nThe scalar control is what licenses that attribution, and it holds only at 64^3. Decision 76 cannot move a scalar transform — a rank-0 field is its own single component either way — so scalar staying at 100–109times while vector runs 115–161times is the signature. The staging buffer is NOT involved: it is built only when _component_offsets_align reports an odd physical stride, and every measured grid is even. The old code reproduces its own 2026-08-07 archive to within 1%, which is what licenses the comparison at all.\n\nAt 512^3 Float32 that control FAILS, so the shift there is not decision 76. Scalar moved 117–127times and vector 124–128times, together, where the decision can only move one of them. Whatever shifted that grid shifted every transform on it, and decision 76's marginal contribution is within noise of zero. The cause is unidentified, sitting in the wide 93d9d8da..b452fb17 range or in the environment; reading it as the per-component loop was an attribution error. The intermediate grids wash out at 084–116times.\n\nDecision 76's own \"the measured cost is nil or negative\" was a HOST measurement, taken against FFTW at one and eight threads. No device arm was measured then, and its two obstacles — the UNALIGNED flag and the odd-stride buffer — are both about alignment, which is a different question from how many executions a transform costs. The batched-to-per-component change is the part that crosses to the device, and it crossed unmeasured.\n\nDecision 78's wrap walk regresses the device gather where the field EXCEEDS cache, and the recorded \"wins 1.03–1.28x on device throughout\" is wrong. The matched A/B — same sizes, types and class set in both arms, so identical process history — puts interpolate! CatmullRom at 256^3 Float64 at 174–178times SLOWER, reproduced three times against a new-code value stable to 0.6% (1539.3, 1532.0, 1540.4 us against an old-code 866.3, 879.3, 1047.6).\n\nThe mechanism is dependency structure, not instruction count. The old kernel computed every stencil address independently, node_x = mod(base_1 + (i - 1 - neighbourhood), grid_x) + 1, so all the loads of a stencil could be in flight together. The walk makes each address depend on its predecessor, node_x = _wrap_step(node_x + 1, grid_x), which serialises the address chain: it buys cheaper arithmetic and sells memory-level parallelism. That is the same trade §4 records for the walk's CPU corner, which decision 78 judged absent on a device.\n\nThe sign therefore tracks the working set against the RX 7900 XTX's 96 MB Infinity Cache, monotonically: 128^3 Float32 (8.4 MB) 075times, 128^3 Float64 (16.8 MB) 092times, 256^3 Float32 (67 MB) approx 122times, 256^3 Float64 (134 MB, the only measured configuration that exceeds the cache) 174times. In cache there is little latency to hide and the cheaper arithmetic wins; out of cache the concurrency that hid DRAM latency is what was sold. CatmullRom is the scheme it bites, reading 64 nodes per point — the lowest arithmetic intensity of the family — where Lalescu(3,3) reads 512 and measures flat at 098–105times.\n\nThat account is ROCm-only, and the CUDA arm falsified it — see finding (al). It was written as a mechanism (\"the sign tracks the working set against the last-level cache\"), which makes a prediction for any card, and the prediction is wrong on the 5070 Ti: 256^3 Float32 sits above that card's smaller L2 and still WINS at 090times, and across the same boundary two schemes move in opposite directions. The measurements above stand as measurements of this card; the generalisation does not, and the AMD regression has no mechanism yet.\n\nTwo device rows are NOT regressions, though the archive diff called them so. interpolate! NUFFT at 256^3 reads 1474times against the archives and 1005–1013times in the matched A/B; anterpolate! Lalescu(3,3) reads 0661times against the archives and 105times matched, so that WIN was equally an artefact. Only anterpolate! NUFFT survives, at 111times and reproduced, which pairs with the CPU NUFFT rows at 105–115times in all four thread lanes — the same operation slower on both backends, which is the cross-backend test a real regression passes.\n\nA measurement hazard found while establishing this, and it cost two arms. Some old-code transfer runs on the device produce a size-independent approx 20 ms floor across many rows — 20331 us for a 256^3 Float32 gather whose archive says 627.8 — while other runs of the SAME commit reproduce the archive to within 20%. It appeared in the arms that included Float32 and not in the Float64-only ones, but the cause is unidentified. A device transfer A/B is therefore only trustworthy when the old arm is checked against its own archive first; two arms here were discarded on exactly that check.\n\nThe canary cannot see it. That lane's fft mechanism is to_physical! SCALAR at 128^3, exactly the configuration decision 76 leaves alone and one grid away from both regressed extremes, so all three lanes read green through a 16times device regression. A canary is a fixed sample of mechanisms, and this is the cost when the sample misses an axis: the archive diff found the regression, not the guard built to catch regressions.\n\nFinding (y) extends to the device, and is larger there. The 512^3 Float32 to_spectral vector row measures 45.2 ms when the fft class runs after algebra, convolution and operators, and 25.9 ms when it runs alone — 174times from process history on identical code. The archive diff therefore reads 1759times for that row where the controlled fft-only A/B reads 1275times, so most of the apparent movement is artefact rather than regression. Device rows allocating a field-sized result per call join the five CPU rows §4(y) names.\n\nA cross-vintage fft comparison is uninformative, now demonstrated rather than inferred. The 256^3 Float64 SCALAR rows read 114–118times slower than the 2026-08-07 archive, yet the same rows measured from the OLD commit on today's host read 099times against the new code: identical code, +15–18 against its own archive. The package did not touch scalar transforms, so the residual is the measurement environment, the §4(p) class. The current archives carry stamped platform state, so a recurrence is a diff rather than a reconstruction.\n\nThose rows also swing 066–162times across the four thread lanes in both archives, non-monotonically and differently in each, which is FFTW threaded-plan selection at that size. The 512^3 twin scales cleanly and reads old approx new in every lane.\n\nThe IT item-3 restructuring closes as measured-and-declined on the second host. The standalone prototype at one, four and eight threads on the i7-12700K reproduces the 9800X3D verdict. Out of the hand-restructured arm spans 103–142times at t1, 099–114times at t4 and 099–109times at t8, with bitwise=true on every row. The @simd arm is indistinguishable from the plain one in all 36 cells, so option 2 is dead on a second microarchitecture and its bitwise proof burden buys nothing.\n\nThe t4 and t8 spans are NOT flat, and flattening them would misreport the study. One cell survives at production thread counts, Float32 mul! at 128^3, holding 114times at t4 and 109times at t8, while every other t4/t8 cell sits within pm 5. The verdict rule asks for a BROAD win before touching the package's highest-blast-radius engine, and one operation at one element type and one grid is not that, so the port stays declined — against a measured residual rather than against a clean 100.\n\nThe largest win is Float32 mul! at a single thread, 142times at 256^3, and the bandwidth column shows why it decays: the package call sits at 20.3 GB/s there against the hand loop's 28.7, whereas by t4 both arms read 27–28 GB/s. The consumer runs production at 16–20 threads, where only the 128^3 Float32 cell still pays.\n\n(ai) The 2026-08-31 Enteropia re-sweep: five lanes, and every mover is an intended fix (the first sweep since 2026-08-09/10, so decisions 76–79, the weighted_inner fix and the transfer work all enter the archives at once). Medians against the previous archives: CUDA 0.987 over 516 rows, CPU t1/t2/t4/t8 1.000/0.999/1.000/0.998 over 632–760 — the harness reproduces itself, and the movement is confined to the changed operations.\n\nThe movers, each matching its recorded mechanism: weighted_inner 7.7× at t1 rising to 59× at t8 (finding (ac) — the tuple form at 512^3 fell 1.47 s → 40 ms); exponential_convolve! vector 1.5–2.3× on both backends (decision 79); the transfer gathers 1.1–2.9× unordered (the decision-78 wrap walk; the Lalescu(3,3) device gather 0.35×), with the separable convolve! 1.47× on device from the α-association regrouping. Canaries green on all three lanes and refrozen — an intended improvement refreezes like an intended slowdown, since a stale slow baseline hides the next regression inside its tolerance band.\n\nThe born-slow triage after the sweep: section A fell 46 → 23 flags and its only unexplained rows remain the radial shell binning, now confirmed flat on FRESH lanes (times 108 at eight threads), so that discovery survives its first re-measurement; section B fell 91 → 33, all known, all on the stale 12700K archives. One triage entry was corrected in the process: the SCALAR exponential peer pair flagged the freshly-fixed code at ~20×, because that ratio measures the documented lazy-vs-stored tradeoff (one exp per mode, no fiber to amortize over) rather than a defect — the pair is now a recorded non-declaration, the vector pair staying as the defect-shaped guard.\n\nAnd one self-inflicted loss, recorded so the next sweep does not repeat it: the first t8 merge threw (assigning tags on an immutable struct) and an unconditional cleanup deleted the four part archives before the error was read — forty minutes of measurement destroyed by a rm that did not wait for verification. The re-run swept into the scratchpad, and benchmark/merge_parts.jl now exists: it verifies both directions and THROWS rather than half-succeeding, so cleanup has no excuse to run on faith.\n\n(ah) The exponential fiber hoist — decision 79's measurements, kept here for the record (2026-08-30). Before: the broadcast evaluated exp(c k^2 + s hat w) per output element. After: once per mode, reused across the fiber, bitwise identical. CPU 256^3 Float64 vector: -t1 conv 152.7 → 63.4 ms, -t4 35.4 → 21.3, -t8 19.9 → 20.8 (the one residual, +3–6%, the V-Cache transition band; 512^3 -t8 is even at 165.3 vs 165.3); Float32 -t8 13.5 → 9.1 (147times). Device Float64 256^3: conv 164times, with-offset 209times, combination 133times; Float32 wins except the 256^3 combination at −2% stable. The born-slow triage's declared-peer table carries exponential_convolve! against convolve! at a cap of 5times, so the next re-sweep will show these rows closing the flag the old route earned.\n\n(ag) The benchmark instruments had no referee for a defect present from birth — the born-slow triage is that referee (2026-08-30, user-directed, after finding (ac) asked why nothing caught it). Every existing check compares a row against itself: the canary against its own frozen baseline, a re-sweep against the previous archive, decision 72's speedup against a sibling sharing the same code path. The weighted_inner reduction was serial from the day it shipped, sat in RESULTS.md flat across four thread columns at 5times the same-size inner, and no instrument — or reader — ever asked the question those numbers answered. That is the third instance of one pattern: a comparison whose two sides share the defect is blind to it (decision 52 closed it for tests, decision 55 for oracles; benchmarks had no counterpart).\n\nbenchmark/triage.jl asks the two absolute questions over archives that already exist, riding every report.jl regeneration as a section of RESULTS.md — no new measurement. (A) Thread-flat on every host: genuinely serial code reads 10times from t1 to t8 on all hosts, where a bandwidth-saturated streaming op reads 11–13times on one. Both qualifiers were forced by measurement: a 13 threshold flagged 211 rows of honest saturation, and the 9800X3D saturates DRAM from a single core (add! at 512^3 is flat at 44 GB/s there while scaling 15times on the 12700K), so flat-on-one-host is that host's physics and flat-on-every-host is the code's. (B) Declared peers: pairs whose docstrings claim comparable cost, asserted for the first time — a class-median comparison was tried and dropped, a class mixing algorithms whose work legitimately differs 64times. Both scans skip sub-millisecond rows (§4(u)); flags are questions, closed by a fix or a recorded reason, never by deletion; the self-test perturbs a scaling row flat and demands the flag.\n\nFirst run over the ten committed archives: every flag accounted but one pair. 46 thread-flat rows — 43 known (weighted_inner retro-flags at 099times, reality_residual, the materializers), and radial_spectrum / the co-spectrum flat on BOTH hosts at approx 27 GB/s — far off the roof, so not saturation: the shell-binning scatter-add does not scale on CPU, a genuinely new born-slow candidate for the same treatment as finding (ac). 91 peer violations, all 91 known (weighted_inner until the lanes are re-swept; exponential_convolve! = the recorded IT item 2).\n\n(af) The transfer benchmark class had only ever measured SCALAR fields (2026-08-30, found by the technique survey). Every row in the class built scalar_phys, so the vector gather — what a particle-tracking consumer actually calls, and the case where the traversal order pays most, the field traffic being three components against one coordinate triple — had never been benchmarked at all. Four rows were added: the vector gather and scatter, each with and without an order. This is the same class of gap as decision 60's two dead classes, found the same way: by asking what the rows actually build rather than whether they run.\n\n(ae) After (ad), the transfer is memory-bound in every measured cell — so the compute-side optimizations do not pay, and that closes a class (2026-08-30). A static-unroll rewrite was the top-ranked recommendation of a survey of GPU transfer techniques, on the strength of a compiled measurement: the stencil nest emits a stack frame with a local load in its innermost loop, and about one outstanding field load per thread where the memory system wants two or more. The predicted gain was 13–2times.\n\nIt was implemented — the node count moved into the type domain, so the three loops take static trip counts and the weights resolve to registers — verified bitwise identical over the same 48 configurations, and measured at nothing: 2374 against 2370 ms on the device gather and within noise on both CPU element types. It was reverted rather than kept, since it adds a type parameter to two kernels and two launch sites for no gain. The count was evidently already folding.\n\nThe reading that matters is the general one. Ordering and the wrap walk moved this operation off the compute side: what was left after them is fetching bytes, and no rearrangement of the arithmetic around those fetches changes the time. That retires, for this kernel and this regime, the whole family the survey ranked below the unroll — explicit muladd, a restructured contraction, a fiber-innermost loop, Int32 indices — none of which is worth machine time until a measurement shows the kernel compute-bound again. It also explains finding (z)'s muladd result on displace! and finding (r)'s declined hoist as the same phenomenon rather than three separate curiosities.\n\nTwo independent predictions in that survey were confirmed and are worth keeping. Shared-memory privatization would lose here: the published crossover is at a point density of 005, measured with a stencil eight times wider than this one, and this regime sits at 006 with the narrow stencil, so the tile flush cost — which is proportional to the GRID, not the points — would dominate. And the node-centric scatter this package measured at 6–24times slower fails by exactly the predicted law: its waste factor is the reciprocal of the point density, 167times here, which brackets the measurement.","title":"4. What we have found"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#5.-The-benchmark-suite","page":"Performance","text":"A standing suite under benchmark/ (sibling of test/), opt-in — not part of Pkg.test(), exactly like the GPU tests. A call to build_suite(; array_type, types, sizes) builds a BenchmarkTools.BenchmarkGroup parameterised by the device array type (Array on CPU; CuArray/ROCArray on GPU — the same approach test/gpu/suite.jl uses with to_device), so one suite runs everywhere. A bench_sync blocks the backend so BenchmarkTools times GPU work to completion. Every row that writes to a device buffer synchronizes it, including the transfer rows: relying on a driver's internal synchronization is what left NUFFT anterpolation's final assignment outside its own measurement, and a benchmark should assume nothing about where a driver happens to block.\n\nThe resolution class (decision 85) measures what no other class does: an operation whose cost is set by a field the caller does not pass as its destination. Both embed_modes! and extract_modes! walk the COARSE field, so their rows should follow the coarse grid and barely move as the swept grid grows; a row that tracks the sweep instead is the regression the class exists to catch, a pass over the fine field. Two ratios are swept per lane, a fixed small block and a half-sized coarse grid, because an index map can be right at one ratio and wrong at another. The rows enter RESULTS.md at the next sweep, src/ having moved with the decision.\n\nClasses covered: FFT transforms (each CPU row with a measure twin planned via FFTW.MEASURE, so the default-ESTIMATE experience and the tuned production path sit side by side and finding (h) is re-measured per host/size/type on every sweep; GPU rows have no twin, the device FFT libraries taking no planner flag; plus, per scalar/vector, the decision-64 pair — the preserving scratch arity of to_physical! and the destructive three-argument baseline it is priced against, whose delta is the cost of preservation), field algebra (M2 + M3, representative — including the weighted combination combine! and the determinant det!), differential operators (including the fused symmetric_gradient! and the kernel-composed helmholtz_projection!/divergence! forms of decision 32, whose baseline is the sequential operator + convolve! pair they replace, and the reality projector reality_projection! of decision 83, scalar and vector — an O(N^2) row beside the O(N^3) ones, priced in place), convolution (convolve! scalar/vector, the lazy exponential_convolve! — its exponent scale-aware, so every size measures the streaming pass and not the Float32 subnormal band (finding (m)) — and the kernel-composed combine! of decision 33 — their baselines the stored-kernel convolve! and the sequential combine! + convolve! pair — + the builders spectral_cutoff / spectral_band / radial_kernel / tophat_window / assignment_window / translation_kernel — a builder is paid once and its kernel applied many times, so it is swept for regressions, not for hot-path cost, and a builder returning a compressed kernel is timed on one factor rather than a field it never allocates — the spectral_cutoff and spectral_band rows previously wrapped their builder in materialize_kernel and so reported an O(1) lazy build at the cost of an O(N^3) materialization, with neither trackable on its own). Each compressed kernel class is additionally swept against its materialized twin at the point of application — convolve! on a vector field with the kernel axis-factored, radial, and stored — since the classes compute the same coefficients and only their cost differs, so the stored form is the baseline that makes a storage change legible (finding (o)). The class OPERATIONS join it: the general radial_kernel builder against the two named bands, materialize_kernel on both compressed classes (the escape hatch allocates the O(N^3) field the classes exist to avoid, so its cost is what a consumer trades away by materializing), the in-place translation_kernel! against its allocating sibling, and inv — whose row doubled when decision 56 gave it a zero policy, and deliberately so: that guard is three O(N) reductions over the axis factors, measured at 40.6 µs on an RTX 5070 Ti against a 9.5 µs reciprocal, so it dominates the operation. It is accepted rather than optimized away, because a kernel is inverted once and applied many times — this class is swept for regressions, not for hot-path cost — and because the alternative is returning a non-finite coefficient that poisons every later result. Read against an archive from before 2026-07-30, that step is the guard, not drift. Then the interpolation/anterpolation transfer (the spline schemes plus the spectral NUFFT; the exact DirectDFT is its oracle, not a swept regression target), and the L²/measure reductions (integral/mean/inner/l2norm/lpnorm/maximum, the covariance tensor cov — the one reduction whose result is fiber-valued at R = 2, so it streams a vector field and writes nine accumulators — the scalar-only var in both representations, which prices what skipping the transform is worth (decision 49 gave it a Parseval form), the fused multiplier-weighted weighted_inner, the spectral Parseval inner, the spectral zero-mode integral/mean (an O(1) DC-coefficient read, not a streaming reduction), and the radial reductions radial_spectrum / radial_correlation plus the two-field co-spectra radial_spectrum(f, g) / radial_correlation(f, g) — a single scatter-add shell-binning pass and an inverse rfft, diagnostic-cost; the co-spectra cost the same as the single-field forms, sharing their binning), including the mapped forms maximum(norm, u) / mean(norm, u) / mean(det, A) / mean(tr, A), whose maximum(norm, u) row pairs with the lpnorm vec Inf row as a direct A/B — the same quantity, one materializing a magnitude section and one not.\n\nStorage-family moves (adapt, decision 63) are the seventh class, and the only one whose cost is a link rather than a device: a host↔device copy is bounded by the bus, so it does not improve with the card and does not scale like anything else in the table. The class is device-only and empty on a CPU lane — adapt to the family one already occupies is the identity, so a host row would time a no-op and report nanoseconds as though a transfer had happened. It sweeps both directions separately (they are not symmetric on any real link), the identity case (so that its being nearly free is visible rather than merely asserted), the multipliers (six O(N) axis vectors, so latency-bound where the field rows are volume-bound), and adapt on a FieldTransformPlans, which is not a transfer at all but a planning pass on the target and is priced separately for that reason. The practical guidance the class exists to support is unchanged by any of its numbers: a solver should cross the bus during setup and stay resident, because a per-step transfer of a 256^3 Float64 field is tens of milliseconds against the tens of microseconds its operations take.\n\nRun it:\n\njulia --project=benchmark -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'   # one-time\njulia --project=benchmark -t1 benchmark/runbenchmarks.jl       # serial lane\njulia --project=benchmark -t4 benchmark/runbenchmarks.jl       # threaded lane\njulia --project=benchmark/gpu/rocm benchmark/gpu/rocm/runbenchmarks.jl   # GPU (on hardware)\n\nThe tracked SUITE uses modest grids (64³, 128³) for fast, repeatable, regression-sensitive runs; the large characterisation sweep behind §4 (256³/512³) is reachable with build_suite(; sizes = (256,)). Manifests are gitignored (repo convention); each environment reproduces from its Project.toml.\n\nThe coverage contract. Every public operation class is benchmarked (representative members) over {Float64, Float32} × {representative grid sizes} × {CPU -t1, CPU -t4, GPU}, unless a class has an explicit exemption with its reason — the storage class of decision 63 is device-only, because adapt to the family one already occupies is the identity and a CPU row would time a no-op. New code adds its benchmark to the matching per-class file — the performance analogue of the test coverage contract (TESTING.md §4).\n\nAn adjoint pair is two configurations, never one. Where an operation ships as a pair — the transfer's interpolate and anterpolate, the transforms' to_spectral and to_physical — an axis the two verbs share (the scheme, the stencil width, the grid, the element type) counts as swept only once it has been swept on each verb. This is the twentieth axis of TESTING.md §4, recorded in both places because a benchmark sweep is what taught it: finding (ad) varied the scheme on the gather and left the scatter pinned at CatmullRom, so the width-8 scatter was never measured at any width, and it is that cell which regressed 145–148times at 512^3 and shipped for two days before the history scan found it (finding (am)). A one-verb sweep does not license the sentence \"the scheme axis is covered\".","title":"5. The benchmark suite"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#6.-Tracking-regressions,-and-per-platform-results","page":"Performance","text":"Workflow (manual, BenchmarkTools). Save a baseline, then judge a later run against it on the minimum-time estimates:\n\njulia --project=benchmark benchmark/runbenchmarks.jl save results/cpu-baseline.json\njulia --project=benchmark benchmark/runbenchmarks.jl judge results/cpu-baseline.json   # :regression / :improvement / :invariant\n\nBecause CI runners have no GPU, GPU tracking is manual: run the GPU suite on the device and record the numbers here. Adding a platform = mirror benchmark/gpu/rocm/ (a Project.toml + a 2-line runbenchmarks.jl); the suite needs no changes.\n\nTwo scales. The tracked SUITE (runbenchmarks.jl, grids 64³/128³) is the quick, regression-sensitive run used with save/judge above. The exhaustive characterization (benchmark/characterize.sh) runs the full matrix — every op-class (including FFT) over each backend's default grids (CPU 64³–512³, device 64³–256³, since a device cannot hold 512³ in Float64), on the CPU thread sweep 1/2/4/8 (powers of two up to the performance cores) and the GPU — building one (grid size, element type) at a time to bound memory (a 256³ Float64 tensor field is ~1.1 GB; holding all classes for both types at 256³ overflows the 12 GB GPU — gap 6), and assembles it into the committed report benchmark/RESULTS.md (the raw per-run JSON under benchmark/results/ is gitignored). Regenerate it on any platform with that one script; it stamps the date, commit, and host. The characterize.jl sweep additionally stamps each archive JSON with its own measurement vintage (commit=/measured= BenchmarkGroup tags), and report.jl prints the per-column vintages in the header — so a column measured at an older commit is visible as such instead of silently inheriting the report's HEAD (the 2026-07-22 decision-40 reductions skew, one host's archive swept hours before a threading feature landed, is the motivating incident).\n\nA representative slice — 128³, Float64, minimum time (CPU = Ryzen 9800X3D, GPU = RTX 5070 Ti), read from the current RESULTS.md rather than measured for this table. The last two columns are speedups in ×: CPU thread scaling (-t1 → -t4) and the GPU's lead over the 4-thread CPU. RESULTS.md now carries two CPU thread sweeps (i7-12700K and Ryzen 9800X3D) and two GPU columns — the RX 7900 XTX (gfx1100, ROCm, 24 GB) and the RTX 5070 Ti (Blackwell sm_120, CUDA, 16 GB); the two retired cards were dropped on 2026-08-07, having been measured in the broken gcsample regime of §4(s) with no hardware left to re-sweep them on. The 7900 XTX adds a 512³ Float32 point, and both cards cover the full 256³ matrix in both element types. At 256³ the two are near parity (§4(v)): the median over 71 comparable rows is 1.09× in Float64 and 1.28× in Float32, the NVIDIA lead being a small-grid launch-latency effect rather than a throughput one, and the AMD card winning five of the transfer class's eight 256^3 Float64 rows (§4(v), corrected 2026-08-31). Across vendors the reductions class shows the widest gap and the clearest architectural signature: the radial reductions (radial_spectrum/radial_correlation) bin on an Atomix scatter-add histogram, and CUDA's atomic throughput far outruns RDNA3's — radial_spectrum on a vector field at 256³ is ≈48× faster on the 5070 Ti than the 7900 XTX in Float64 (54.973 → 1.144 ms) but only 1.11× in Float32 (0.915 → 0.824 ms). Re-measured 2026-08-09 with both cards in the fixed regime of §4(s)/§4(w): the approx 48times is confirmed to three significant figures, while the Float32 figure — previously recorded as ≈3.5× — is not, and its collapse sharpens the conclusion rather than weakening it. The cross-vendor gap on this operation is the emulated-atomic penalty of §4(d) at work (RDNA has no native global Float64 atomics): in Float32, where both cards have native atomics, they are equal. That names the mechanism; finding (n) prices the exposure — a per-workgroup pre-reduction would mostly hide it, so the gap is hardware-caused but implementation-addressable. The 7900 XTX pays ≈60× going Float32→Float64 on this op, against the 5070 Ti's ≈1.4×. Those are the post-decision-34 figures, the staging planes fused away, so the atomics are the whole measurement. The full operation × type × grid × thread/backend matrix is in RESULTS.md:\n\noperation (128³, Float64) CPU -t1 CPU -t4 GPU threads -t1→-t4 GPU vs -t4\nto_spectral scalar (FFT) 10.27 ms 2.93 ms 0.399 ms 3.5× 7.3×\nmul! tensor·vec (M3 algebra) 7.03 ms 4.43 ms 0.321 ms 1.6× 13.8×\nlaplacian! scalar (operator) 2.21 ms 0.27 ms 0.030 ms 8.3× 9.0×\ninterpolate! Catmull–Rom (gather) 5.63 ms 1.46 ms 0.132 ms 3.9× 11.0×\ninterpolate! Lalescu (3,3) (gather) 41.63 ms 10.50 ms 0.772 ms 4.0× 13.6×\nanterpolate! Catmull–Rom (scatter) 22.47 ms 6.13 ms 0.169 ms 3.7× 36.2×\n\nTwo patterns the speedup columns make plain. The bandwidth-bound classes barely thread at this size — the tensor product gains only 1.6× from 4 threads, being DRAM-bandwidth-bound rather than compute-bound, confirming §2. (Before decision 38 the cheapest of them, add!, regressed under threading; the slab-threaded rewrite made thread scaling monotone, which is why no row here does.) The transfer threads well (≈3.9× on the Catmull–Rom gather) because its stencil gather carries more work per byte. And the GPU leads the 4-thread CPU by ~3.5–27×, the spread itself being the point (and the serial CPU by far more — the Catmull–Rom gather at 256^3 Float64 runs 105.6 ms serial against 1.485 ms on the RTX 5070 Ti, ≈71×, and 1.540 ms on the RX 7900 XTX — parity on that row, §4(v)), widest for the light/parallel ops and narrowing toward the bandwidth ratio for the heavy streaming ones. (Float32 is ~0.6–0.7× of these Float64 times on the GPU; the full operation × type × grid × thread/backend matrix — including the per-grid scaling and the 1/2/4/8 thread sweep — is in benchmark/RESULTS.md.)","title":"6. Tracking regressions, and per-platform results"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#7.-Current-coverage-and-known-gaps","page":"Performance","text":"Covered: the public operation classes (FFT, algebra, operators, convolution, transfer, reductions; the device-only storage class of decision 63 is wired into the suite but has not yet been swept into the committed archive — see gap 7) parameterised by element type and grid size, runnable on CPU (-t1/-t4) and GPU (ROCm or CUDA) through one device-parameterised suite; the manual save→judge regression workflow; and the exhaustive characterization (characterize.sh → committed benchmark/RESULTS.md): every op-class (incl. FFT) at each backend's default grids (CPU 64³–512³, device 64³–256³), across two CPU 1/2/4/8 thread sweeps (i7-12700K, Ryzen 9800X3D) and two GPUs — RX 7900 XTX (gfx1100, ROCm) and RTX 5070 Ti (Blackwell sm_120, CUDA); the 7900 XTX is extended to a 512³ Float32 point for the bandwidth-bound classes, and the 9800X3D sweep covers 64³–512³, both element types, all classes — the first data in the CPU production band of docs/ARCHITECTURE.md decision 37 (a 512³ Float64 CPU sweep needs tens of GB of host RAM, which a 123 GB workstation clears with room to spare; it was these lanes that exposed finding (l)). The decision-27 additions (combine!, det!, symmetric_gradient!, weighted_inner) are wired into their classes and CPU-verified (0 allocations for combine!/det!/symmetric_gradient!; the small bounded guard for weighted_inner); they enter the committed RESULTS.md table on its next GPU-host refresh. Done (2026-07-21): both GPU archives are refreshed — the RX 7900 XTX (2026-07-20) and the RTX 5070 Ti — so every decision-27/32/33/34 row (combine!, det!, symmetric_gradient!, weighted_inner, the kernel-composed helmholtz_projection!/divergence!/combine! arities, exponential_convolve!, and the fused radial diagnostics) now carries both GPU columns at 64³–256³, both element types. The refresh also re-confirmed the §6 atomic-scatter contrast on the fused decision-34 kernels: radial_spectrum vector Float64 256³ is 54.973 ms on the 7900 XTX against 1.144 ms on the 5070 Ti (the figures §6 and §4(v) carry; this paragraph's earlier 65.95/1.371 pair predated the §4(s) per-row GC repair and is superseded) — a ~48× CUDA lead on what is now a pure scatter-add (its staging planes are gone, so the atomics are the whole story).\n\nGaps:\n\nCLOSED — the device rows no longer carry a blanket per-sample GC. This gap used to warn that the GPU reductions/convolution figures were latency rather than throughput, because every device row ran with gcsample = true. Finding (s) removed that: the collector is now decided per row from a measured device-pool probe, and only the documented materializers keep it. The committed archive is in the fixed regime on both cards, and the old warning's own symptom is gone — a 64^3 Float64 convolve! scalar reads 33.1 µs on the RX 7900 XTX and 10.8 µs on the RTX 5070 Ti, against the approx 15 ms floor this gap was written to describe.\nCUDA spans two NVIDIA generations, both on Enteropia (the office box; CPU Ryzen 9800X3D). characterize.jl now takes a cuda backend beside gpu/rocm (CuArray + CUDA.reclaim(), no comgr warm), so the memory-bounded one-class-at-a-time sweep runs on NVIDIA too. The RTX 5070 Ti (Blackwell sm_120, 16 GB) covers the full 64³–256³ matrix (both element types, all six classes; ~10.8 GB peak). 512³ exceeds 16 GB — the mul! tensor field alone is 4.5 GB and the build OOMs — but unlike AMD's HIP (gap 6), CUDA OOMs gracefully (a Julia OutOfGPUMemoryError, no context corruption), so probing the ceiling is safe. Enteropia now also contributes its 9800X3D CPU thread sweep beside the i7-12700K (host Aiporetne).\nAutomated time-regression detection — BUILT (benchmark/regress.jl, 2026-07-22). One canary per execution mechanism (twelve: the broadcast route, each fused-kernel class, the tree reductions, FFT, transfer gather/scatter, the binning) at 128^3 Float64, sim1–2 minutes per backend, judged on minimum times against a committed per-host baseline (benchmark/baselines/<host>-<backend>[-tN].json) at a 25% tolerance with a hard exit code, plus the CPU serial-vs-threaded alarm t_mathrmthreaded le 11 t_mathrmserial on every override-governed canary — the alarm that finding (l) lacked. Refreeze protocol: an intended slowdown reruns with --freeze in the same commit, with the reason. Calibration lesson from its first run, recorded so it is not relearned: 20 samples of a sim250 µs canary let minima jitter sim 2times at eight threads and false-alarm; the frozen calibration is 100 samples × 5 evals (batch-averaged minima), which held 12/12 invariant across repeated judges on both backends. Baselines for both hosts are committed — Enteropia (cpu-t1, cpu-t8, cuda) and Aiporetne (cpu-t1, cpu-t8, rocm; frozen 2026-07-22 with that host's post-rewrite re-sweep, its freeze-run inversion alarm 8/8 ok). Deliberately not push-triggered CI (cost policy; shared runners are noise): the BenchmarkGroup layout still keeps AirspeedVelocity.jl or PkgBenchmark.jl a drop-in if a CI story ever changes. First live catch (2026-07-24): the t1 helmholtz canary flagged at 1.27× baseline, which turned out not to be a slowdown but a dead-code bug — decision 42 had dropped the kernel's squared-wavenumber arguments while leaving the gated ::CPU loop's signature stale, so the loop fell out of dispatch and CPU helmholtz silently reverted to the KA-on-CPU launch decision 40 had escaped. Restoring the loop returned the canary to baseline without a refreeze — the check working exactly as designed, catching an achieved-time regression the allocation and pass audits cannot see (decision 47).\nThe canary is a 128^3 relative instrument, and on an X3D part it is largely cache-resident. Its grid is fixed at 128^3 (benchmark/regress.jl, unchanged since the lane was built) so a lane costs 2–3 minutes and gets run. The consequence, measured on Enteropia 2026-08-05: add! moves 151 MB against a 96 MB L3, so repeated samples keep much of it resident and the single-thread figure reads 63.8 GB/s — well above the ~41 GB/s a Zen 5 core sustains from DRAM (§4(l)). The canary therefore does not probe the DRAM-bound regime, which is the one decision 37 names as the CPU path's tuning target; at 512^3 the same working set is 9.66 GB and cannot cache. A regression that appears only once a field stops fitting would pass this net silently. That regime is covered instead by the characterization sweep (benchmark/characterize.jl, 64^3–512^3), which is the instrument to reach for when a change plausibly alters memory traffic rather than arithmetic.\nRun-to-run variance — absolute numbers carry ~10% jitter, and the atomic-binning canary is the widest: radial_spectrum at -t8 on Enteropia measured 4.871, 4.793, 4.747 and 4.504 ms over four back-to-back repeats against a 4.384 ms baseline (2026-08-05) — an 8% spread with every draw above the frozen value, which sits low in its own distribution. Its scatter-add threads outside the with_threading override and its summation order is nondeterministic, so contention decides the figure. Read a single reading of that row against the baseline as noise unless it clears the 25% tolerance, and confirm any movement against the other backends before believing it. Cross-platform/-commit comparisons are only meaningful within a single session (hence the side-by-side discipline of §3); the tables here are indicative magnitudes, not contract values. The allocating kernel builders (spectral_cutoff / spectral_band / radial_kernel / assignment_window / translation_kernel) are the noisy extreme: their threaded 256^3 cells swung ±50% between two same-day sweeps (GC timing on paid-once allocating passes) — read them as indicative only, never as regressions.\nGPU at 256³ — three traps, none a rocFFT bug (measured on a since-retired 12 GB AMD card; the 256^3 ceiling below is specific to that capacity, while the cold-compile and allocator traps are not capacity-dependent. The wedge-instead-of-error behaviour itself still binds the current cards — it simply moves up a grid, as this gap's own 2026-06-26 update records for a 512³ Float64 sweep on the 24 GB card). Earlier notes blamed \"rocFFT 256³ planning\"; that was wrong. The facts, measured:\n256³ FFT works. Scalar and vector r2c/c2r at 256³ take ~1 s to plan and ~15–20 ms to run; the full fft class passes at 256³.\nCold kernel compilation looks like a hang. The first GPU use after a reboot compiles kernels host-side via AMD comgr → 0% GPU, 100% CPU, VRAM pool reserved, no output, for minutes. Never kill a cold first run — a killed compile never persists to ~/.cache/comgr, so the next run restarts cold. The characterize.jl sweep warms the cache with one trivial kernel up front. A further mitigation for the rocFFT share of the compile: setting the ROCFFT_RTC_CACHE_PATH environment variable to a writable file makes rocFFT persist its runtime-compiled FFT kernels there and reuse them across processes, turning that part of the cold start into a one-time cost (rocFFT runtime compilation).\nThe 256³ working set overflows 12 GB VRAM, and HIP segfaults instead of erroring — the crash is hipMallocFromPoolAsync in libamdhip64.so. Two compounding causes, two fixes: (i) holding all six classes' fields live for one (size, type) exceeds 12 GB at 256³ (a 4-class sweep fit; adding reductions + convolution did not) → characterize.jl builds one (op-class, size, element-type) at a time, bounding the working set to a single class (~few GB); (ii) GC returns freed device memory to the AMDGPU pool, not the OS, so the pool high-water mark accumulates across iterations and 256³ OOMs late (the first pairs fit, the last does not) → AMDGPU.reclaim() between iterations releases blocks back to the OS. Knock-on: a VRAM-OOM segfault can still degrade the HIP context until a reboot / rocm-smi --gpureset, after which even small ops segfault under load.\nA GC-free benchmark loop wedges the allocating reductions — independent of grid size (seen at 64³), and the cause of a 4-hour hang. The lpnorm reduction materializes a pointwise-magnitude field per call; BenchmarkTools' tune!/run does almost no host allocation, so Julia's GC (host-pressure triggered) never fires, the dead GPU temps pile into the AMDGPU pool until it hits 12 GB, and the allocator spins — CPU pegged, GPU idle, VRAM maxed, for hours. It is GC-lag, not a leak (GC.gc() every ~50 calls holds the pool flat; a real solver fires GC every step, so the op is fine in use). Fixed by gcsample = true for GPU runs (characterize.jl and the ROCm runner): a GC before each sample bounds the pool — the reductions class then completes at ~0.5 GB peak. Distinct from the cold-compile hang above — there comgr is active (compiling, minutes); here it is idle (wedged, hours).\nWith the per-(op-class, size, type) build the full 64³–256³ GPU column is in RESULTS.md. The standing limit on that card was the 12 GB ceiling itself: even one class's both-element-type 256³ fields strain it (hence the one-class-at-a-time build), and 512³ won't fit on that card. If a future OOM-segfault degrades the context, reset with rocm-smi --gpureset from a console (it can crash an active display session) or reboot.\nUpdate (2026-06-26, RX 7900 XTX, gfx1100, 24 GB). On RDNA3 256³ is indeed trivial for every class (both element types), and a 512³ Float32 point is now in RESULTS.md for the bandwidth-bound classes (fft, algebra, operators, convolution). But 512³ is not free even at 24 GB: transfer is omitted (its NUFFT oversamples ×2 to a 1024³ work grid) and reductions is omitted (the radial reductions hold a ≈24 GB live set at 512³ — vector fields in both representations + the inverse-rfft plans). A 512³ Float64 sweep of the heavy classes exceeds 24 GB, and a HIP OOM wedges the allocator (GPU 0 %, one host core spinning, VRAM full) rather than erroring — the same trap as the cold-compile / GC-free hangs above, and gcsample does not help when the live set (not GC lag) is what overflows. So the 512³ column is Float32 and skips those two.\nThe storage class is swept on BOTH vendors — CLOSED 2026-08-23. Decision 63's device-only class carries 66 rows in each of the GPU 5070TI and GPU 7900XTX columns, and the four rows decisions 71 and 72 added are filled on all five Aiporetne lanes, so RESULTS.md has seven class sections with no half-filled table left. See finding (x) for the cross-vendor reading, which is the comparison this class exists to support. Method note, kept because it is what makes a partial sweep safe: characterize.jl overwrites the archive it is given, so a class-only sweep must be written to a separate file and merged. Both halves were done that way — the CUDA one verified 426 pre-existing rows bit-identical afterwards, the ROCm one 467 — and the merge is verified in both directions, re-merging the pre-merge backup into the merged archive to confirm it adds nothing and changes nothing, which catches a row silently dropped as well as a row silently altered.\naarch64 is a production host architecture with zero coverage. The recorded production target (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md decision 37) is NAISS Arrhenius, and its GPU nodes are GH200 superchips: the CUDA device is hosted by a 72-core Grace CPU — ARM, aarch64 — while the CPU partition is x86-64 EPYC. The GPU path's host code will therefore eventually build and run on ARM, and nothing in this package has ever been compiled, tested, or benchmarked on that architecture. No blocker is known: the stack is pure Julia plus JLL artifacts published for aarch64-linux (FFTW, HDF5), and CUDA.jl runs on aarch64 hosts, Grace Hopper among them. But \"no known blocker\" is not \"verified\", and none of the x86-measured CPU findings transfer — finding (h)'s ESTIMATE mis-planning turns out to reach both x86 microarchitectures in the fleet rather than being the Zen 5 artifact it was first recorded as — which is a reason to carry MEASURE to ARM as a default expectation rather than a reason to assume it is absent — and NEON changes FFTW's codelet landscape entirely. Standing guard until access lands: never adopt an x86-only dependency or recommendation — the MKL FFTW provider, which has no aarch64 build, is the canonical tempting example — and when it does land, the first step on an Arrhenius node is the test matrix, then this benchmark suite.\nCUDA unified memory is unmeasured on Grace Hopper, the host it matters on. Finding (as) measured one x86 card, where pages migrate over PCIe. A GH200 reaches its Grace memory over NVLink-C2C, a far faster link, and also supports a mechanism the preference does not select: the GPU addressing ordinary system memory directly through its page tables, with no migration. CUDA.jl's \"unified\" is managed memory, which migrates, so which of the two suits a streaming pseudospectral pass is open.\nThree things make that measurement easy to get wrong. The preference is read at compile time, so device and unified memory cannot be compared in one Julia session; the comparison needs two environments run back to back. CUDA.jl prefetches a unified array before every launch only when the process sees exactly one GPU, so a job restricted to its own device and one seeing all four measure different regimes. And the benchmark harness refuses unified memory by design, so the measurement is a bespoke script, not a characterize.jl sweep.\nThe design that separates the two costs: at a size that fits in GPU memory, device against unified, which prices the overhead alone; then a size exceeding GPU memory by a few percent, on unified memory, which adds the cost of oversubscription. Record the visible-device count with both.\n\n(an) The traversal order's rebuild is allocation-free where it was 15 MiB a call (2026-09-03, decision 80). A point_traversal_order build allocates four arrays — the Int32 result, the counting sort's Int64 keys, the histogram with its prefix sum, and the Bool marks of the permutation check — and a consumer whose points move pays that on every rebuild. Measured on the host at 32^3:\n\nallocating point_traversal_order: 18.9 KB at 10^3 points, 135.9 KB at 10^4, 1.31 MB at 10^5, 13.0 MB at 10^6 — linear in the point count, as its four arrays are.\nin-place point_traversal_order!: 1248 bytes at 10^3 and 1312 at 10^6 — constant, a ratio of roughly 10^4 at the larger count.\n\nThe residue is not the package's: three KernelAbstractions launches plus the validation's own leave about 1.2 KiB of host-side launch bookkeeping, the same class as the FFTW callback of §4(t), and it is why test/allocations.jl guards this row with a bound rather than a zero. The bound is far below one point-sized array, so a regression that began allocating per point could not hide under it. What the device pool cares about is the part that scales, and that is now the caller's storage entirely.\n\nA correction to (t), found 2026-09-04. The callback state is per PLAN EXECUTION, and decision 76 changed how many executions a transform makes: a rank-R field was one batched execution and is now one per contiguous component. Measured at 24^3 on four threads, to_physical! allocates exactly 4032 B per component — 4032 scalar, 12096 vector, 36288 tensor — and 0 B at one thread, where the callback is absent. test/allocations.jl's field_threshold carries slack for ONE execution (4096 times the thread count), so the scalar and vector rows kept passing while the nine-component tensor rows failed at four threads and above; the threaded CI lane had been red on those three rows since decision 76, and the bound now scales with the component count too. The size-independence recorded above still holds: what multiplies is the number of executions, not the cost of one.\n\n(ao) The traversal build's running total was the one unthreaded stage, and its share is set by the GRID (2026-09-04, decision 81). Three of the four stages of point_traversal_order are KernelAbstractions kernels and thread by KA's own partitioning; the running total over the block counts was cumsum!, serial on a host array. Because the block count follows the grid and tile_size rather than the point count, the serial stage does not shrink as the rest speeds up:\n\n9800X3D, 10^6 points, default tile_size, 256^3: build 9.24 ms at one thread and 1.87 at eight (4.9×), with the scan flat at 0.08 ms — 1% of the build serially, 4% at eight threads.\nSame at 512^3 (2.1M blocks): build 3.41 ms at four threads and 2.85 at eight, with the scan at 0.69–0.70 ms — 20% and 25% of the build. This is the default tile size at the grid decision 37 names for the CPU, not a corner.\nAt tile_size = 1 on 256^3 (16.8M blocks) it reached 55% of the build at eight threads.\n\nThreaded (_scan_offsets!), the scan runs 26–73times faster and the build gains 117times at four threads and 126times at eight at 512^3. The scan's own speedup peaks around 2.1M blocks and falls off at 16.8M, where the two-pass form's extra read of the counts saturates bandwidth.\n\nTwo other observations from the same sweep. The build is SLOWER at tile_size = 16 than at 4 on eight threads (4.79 against 1.89 ms), which is atomic contention on only 4096 counters — the same mechanism as the radial shell binning of the triage section. So the build has a sweet spot near the default, with contention above it and the scan below it, and that is the opposite of the gather's preference for coarser blocks; the two pull against each other and only the gather's direction was documented.","title":"7. Current coverage and known gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Convolution/#Convolution","page":"Convolution","text":"","title":"Convolution"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Convolution/#Convolution-operations","page":"Convolution","text":"The verbs below are this module's own. Two further operations reach a kernel through a generic another module owns, and are documented there: combine! has a kernel-composed method — a weighted combination convolved with a kernel in one fused pass — on the FieldAlgebra page, and adapt moves a kernel of any class between storage families as it moves a field, on the Fields page.","title":"Convolution operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Convolution/#Kernel-storage-classes","page":"Convolution","text":"The package holds a kernel in whichever of four forms is cheapest for its multiplier: wrapped in a StoredKernel when the multiplier is arbitrary, as per-axis factors when it separates, as the function computing it when it depends on the wavenumber only through lvert krvert^2, or as any two kernels held unmultiplied when a composition cannot be collapsed into one. All four apply identically and agree bitwise on the host; on a device a complex-valued kernel is the one exception, agreeing there to rounding.\n\nThey part company in one place. Inversion is a mathematical partial operation — a kernel that annihilates a mode has no inverse there — and a class that cannot afford to test that precondition reports a violation differently from one that can, so inv is where the choice of class shows. It matters most for a sharp cutoff, since spectral_cutoff returns the class that cannot.\n\nThe supertype every kernel-taking operation dispatches on is SpectralKernel, documented with Fields.","title":"Kernel storage classes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Convolution/#Kernel-builders","page":"Convolution","text":"Each returns a kernel for a multiplier the package names, in whichever storage class that multiplier admits — and each signature says which class, since that is what determines the cost of applying it: radial_kernel, spectral_cutoff and spectral_band give a RadialKernel (a closure); tophat_window, assignment_window and translation_kernel give a SeparableKernel (three O(N) factors). The escape hatch is radial_kernel, taking any other real function of lvert krvert^2.","title":"Kernel builders"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Convolution/#Internal-API","page":"Convolution","text":"These symbols are not part of the public interface; they are documented for contributors — the coefficientwise convolution, combination and exponential-kernel bodies, the axis-factored pair that evaluates a separable kernel per mode, and the exponential kernel pair — a device kernel and its gated CPU twin — \"gated\" meaning a loop that consults the threading setting before splitting — that evaluates the mode factor once per mode rather than once per component.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution","page":"Convolution","text":"Convolution\n\nPeriodic (circular) convolution of a field with a kernel. On the torus the only convolution is the periodic one, and by the convolution theorem it is exactly a coefficientwise product of the transforms, so the operation lives in spectral representation. A scalar kernel multiplies every fiber component, so one operation serves scalar, vector, and tensor fields. The operation is named convolve (allocating infix ⋆) rather than *, which would read as a pointwise multiplication.\n\nA kernel is not a field. The two hold identical coefficients, so nothing but a type can say which role is meant, and the roles differ: the coefficientwise reciprocal of a kernel is the kernel that deconvolves by it (inv), while the same operation on a field is one the package does not define. Every kernel subtypes SpectralKernel, and the four classes below are interchangeable wherever a kernel is applied to a field, differing in cost rather than in meaning. A kernel also serves as a reduction weight in weighted_inner, which is narrower in one respect: it requires a real multiplier.\n\nPhysics stays with the calling code: an LES filter, a viscous integrating factor, or a de-aliasing mask is a multiplier the caller supplies — through radial_kernel when it depends on the mode only through k^2, or as coefficients built from FieldSpectralMultipliers and wrapped in StoredKernel.\n\nThe parts are, in three groups — what the module does, how a kernel is stored, and which kernels it builds:\n\nConvolution operations:\n\nconvolve / convolve! — convolve a field with a kernel; the in-place form adds the alpha/beta weights on result and destination, and convolve(g₁, g₂) composes two kernels into one.\nexponential_convolve! / exponential_convolve — convolve with the exponential radial kernel, computed from the wavenumber arrays on the fly instead of stored as a field; the two-operand form filters a weighted combination in the same pass.\nThe kernel-composed combine! method — the weighted combination of two fields convolved with a kernel, in one fused pass.\n\nKernel storage classes:\n\nStoredKernel — one coefficient per mode, the O(N^3) storage, cubic in the points per axis, every other class exists to avoid. It wraps a ScalarFieldSpectral in the kernel role.\nSeparableKernel — a kernel that factors over the axes, held as its three per-axis factors, so it costs kilobytes rather than a field; the class translation_kernel, tophat_window and assignment_window return.\nRadialKernel — a kernel depending on the mode only through k^2, held as the function sigma computing it (a closure, evaluated per mode), so it stores no coefficients at all; the class spectral_cutoff, spectral_band and radial_kernel return.\nProductKernel — any two kernels held unmultiplied, which is what a composition that cannot be collapsed into a single kernel gives: a cross-family pair, two stored kernels, or two radial kernels whose coordinates cannot be shown to agree. It applies in one pass.\nmaterialize_kernel — expand a compressed kernel into a StoredKernel, for a caller that wants to inspect or modify the coefficients, and materialize_kernel! to expand it into one that already exists.\ninv — the reciprocal kernel, which deconvolves by the original. Each class returns its own kind and states the precondition it can afford to check, an annihilated mode having no inverse.\n\nKernel builders:\n\nradial_kernel — build a RadialKernel from any real function sigma of the squared wavenumber, which is the route for a multiplier this module does not name (a smooth filter, a hyperviscosity, an integrating factor).\nspectral_cutoff / spectral_band — build the isotropic sharp low-pass kernel and its annular band-pass generalization, both radial kernels.\ntophat_window — the box average of a chosen width, a separable kernel.\nassignment_window — the linear (cloud-in-cell) mass-assignment window, a separable kernel, for deconvolving a gridded point density.\ntranslation_kernel / translation_kernel! — the separable kernel that translates a field by a physical displacement, the shift theorem's phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.convolve!","page":"Convolution","text":"convolve!(destination, field, kernel::SpectralKernel)\nconvolve!(destination, field, kernel::SpectralKernel, α, β)\n\nConvolve field with the kernel whose transfer function is kernel, writing the result into destination in place and returning destination; both field and destination are in spectral representation.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating convolve returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\nfield — the field to filter, in spectral representation.\nkernel — the kernel's transfer function hat g, in any storage class of a SpectralKernel: a StoredKernel holding one coefficient per mode, or one of the compressed classes evaluated per mode (radial, separable, or their product), which costs O(N) or a closure rather than O(N^3). One scalar kernel serves scalar, vector, and tensor fields, and every storage class gives bitwise-identical results on the host, a complex kernel on a device agreeing to rounding instead.\ndestination — the field written with the result; it may alias field or kernel, since the product is coefficientwise.\nα, β — real accumulation weights, converted to the field's scalar type; the three-argument form overwrites (alpha = 1, beta = 0), the five-argument form computes alpha (f star g) + beta textttdestination.\n\nPhysically the periodic convolution is the kernel-weighted local average of the field f about each point x — a low-pass filter when the kernel g is a smoothing bump:\n\n(f star g)(x) = int_Omega f(y)  g(x - y)mathrmdy \n\nwith Omega the periodic box and the displacement x - y reduced onto the torus. On the grid the operation computes the circular sum (f star g)n = sum_m fm  gn - m (indices modulo the grid) — the integral's Riemann sum without the cell volume Omegaprod_i N_i, box volume over the product of the point counts N_i along the axes, the dimensionless convention that makes a kernel act as a per-mode transfer function (hat g equiv 1 is the identity; a sharp cutoff is a projection); the continuum integral above is Omegaprod_i N_i times it. By the convolution theorem (the forward transform unnormalized) the circular sum is exactly the coefficientwise product of the transforms, which this operation computes — hat g = kernel, broadcast over every fiber component of hat f, with the alpha/beta accumulate:\n\nwidehattextttdestination(k) = alpha  hat f(k)  hat g(k) + beta  widehattextttdestination(k)\n\nwarning: A real field needs a Hermitian kernel\nThe stored half-spectrum holds only the modes with a non-negative wavenumber along the folded axis (the layout's fft_dims[1], not necessarily the first physical axis); the rest are implied by the reality of the field, hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k). A kernel returns a field that is still real exactly when its own transfer function carries that symmetry, hat g(-k) = overlinehat g(k) — equivalently, when the physical-space kernel g is real. Every kernel the package builds satisfies it: the real, even ones (spectral_cutoff, spectral_band, assignment_window) trivially, and the complex translation_kernel by construction. A radial_kernel — and so the exponential kernel exponential_convolve! applies — satisfies it too, and for the strongest reason: a real symbol of lvert krvert^2 is real, and lvert krvert^2 is even in k, so the multiplier is real and even whatever the caller's closure computes. A caller-built kernel that breaks the symmetry yields coefficients that are those of no real field, and the inverse transform then silently keeps only their Hermitian part.\n\nnote: Two kernels compose through the allocating form, which allocates nothing\nThis operation applies a kernel to a field. Composing two kernels into one is convolve(g₁, g₂), and there is deliberately no in-place spelling, because there is nothing for one to save: composition never builds a field of coefficients in any storage class. Two separable kernels compose into their per-axis products, and two radial ones into a single closure when both are built over canonical multipliers — the grid's own wavenumbers, built by the convenience constructor rather than supplied by the caller — of the same concrete type (RadialKernel gives the condition); every other pair — two stored kernels, a cross-family pair, or radial operands failing that test — becomes a ProductKernel holding both factors unmultiplied and evaluating them in a single pass. Composing two stored kernels allocates zero bytes, against the O(N^3) a materialized product would cost, and applying the composite is bitwise-identical to applying the two in sequence. Use materialize_kernel if the coefficients themselves are wanted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.convolve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.convolve","page":"Convolution","text":"convolve(field, kernel::SpectralKernel)\nconvolve(kernel::SpectralKernel, field)\nconvolve(first_kernel::SpectralKernel, second_kernel::SpectralKernel)\n\nAllocating form of convolve!: allocate the result and return the periodic convolution of its two arguments, in spectral representation. A field and a kernel, either way round, give the filtered field, with its representation and tensor order preserved; two kernels give the single kernel that applies both, which is composition and allocates nothing of field size. See convolve! for the componentwise definition, the storage classes, and why composition has no in-place form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.convolve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.:⋆","page":"Convolution","text":"field ⋆ kernel\nkernel ⋆ field\nfirst_kernel ⋆ second_kernel\n\nInfix alias for convolve, in every arity: a ⋆ b is convolve(a, b). Convolving a field with a kernel, either way round, filters the field in spectral representation; convolving two kernels composes them into one. The distinct glyph keeps the operation apart from *, which would read as a pointwise product.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.:⋆"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.exponential_convolve!","page":"Convolution","text":"exponential_convolve!(destination, field, multipliers, coefficient::Real)\nexponential_convolve!(destination, field, multipliers, coefficient::Real, α, β)\nexponential_convolve!(destination, field, multipliers, coefficient::Real,\n                      offset_scale::Real, offset::ScalarFieldSpectral)\nexponential_convolve!(destination, field, multipliers, coefficient::Real,\n                      offset_scale::Real, offset::ScalarFieldSpectral, α, β)\nexponential_convolve!(destination, α::Real, a, β::Real, b, multipliers,\n                      coefficient::Real)\nexponential_convolve!(destination, α::Real, a, β::Real, b, multipliers,\n                      coefficient::Real, offset_scale::Real,\n                      offset::ScalarFieldSpectral)\n\nSmooth a field with the periodic heat kernel: convolve it with the exponential radial kernel, optionally times a per-mode exponent field — exp(c k^2 + s hat w), radial in the c k^2 part alone — where c is a real coefficient on the squared wavenumber k^2, and the optional term adds a per-mode real field hat w supplied by the caller under a real scale s. The kernel is evaluated on the fly, never stored as a field, and the result is written into destination in place, which is returned.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating exponential_convolve returns a fresh result instead.\n\nIt acts in spectral representation only.\n\nFor a coefficient c le 0 this is, physically, convolution with the periodic Gaussian (heat) kernel: the operator e^-cDelta, which smooths the field at the length scale sqrt-2c. In spectral representation it is the coefficientwise product with exp(c k^2), the true squared wavenumber kept at the Nyquist frequency (the Laplacian convention), so the kernel is real, even, and positive — a valid kernel for every real field. The optional term s hat w generalizes the exponent by whatever per-mode field the caller supplies.\n\nChoose between this and a stored kernel by reuse and by rank. When the exponent changes between applications — so a stored kernel would be rebuilt each time — the lazy form wins outright: a rebuild writes a whole field of coefficients that this form never writes, let alone reads, and it reads three one-dimensional wavenumber arrays instead.\n\nFor one fixed kernel reused across many calls the two are close, and rank decides, because this form's cost is the exponential it evaluates per mode while a stored kernel's is the coefficient field it streams. On a vector field one exponential serves all three components, so the lazy form runs at 088–103 of a stored kernel's time at 256^3–512^3, on a GPU and on a threaded CPU alike. On a scalar field it pays one exponential per element and the stored kernel leads — 073–309 over those sizes, and on a CPU by as much as 20times at 64^3–128^3, where there is too little memory traffic to hide the exponential. Serially the stored kernel leads throughout, and Float32 shifts the balance further toward it, halving its traffic while leaving the exponential unchanged. The two-operand forms fuse the combination into this same pass, removing the combination's destination write and re-read.\n\nOn a CPU a deeply damped kernel has one further Float32 cost cliff: modes whose factor lands in the subnormal range — exponent arguments c lvert krvert^2 + s hat w between about -103 and -87 — are produced through a hardware assist at roughly 16times the usual per-element cost, so a kernel deep enough to cross that band pays a multiple of its whole pass. The Float64 band (arguments below about -708) is beyond any physical kernel, and the GPU backends flush subnormals to zero, so both are unaffected; where the band matters, use Float64 or a milder exponent.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field written with the result; it may alias field (or a, b, offset), since the product is coefficientwise.\nfield — the field to filter, in spectral representation.\nα, β — in the single-operand forms, where they trail the argument list, the usual accumulation weights: the result is written as alpha (textfiltered  f) + beta textttdestination.\nα, a, β, b — in the two-operand forms, where they lead it, the real weights and fields of the combination alpha a + beta b the kernel is applied to, exactly as combine! takes them; with b === destination the filtered accumulate runs in place. The two roles never share a signature, which is why one pair of glyphs can serve both.\nmultipliers — the wavenumber arrays for the field's grid; the kernel is evaluated from their squared-wavenumber family.\ncoefficient — the real factor c on k^2 in the exponent, dimensionless as received (the caller folds its own physical factors before the call).\noffset_scale, offset — the real scale s and the per-mode field hat w of the optional exponent term s hat w; offset is a scalar field in spectral representation holding real values (as a materialized radial kernel would). The pair enters as scale times field so a changed scale costs nothing — the resident hat w is built once.\n\nThe single-operand accumulate form computes\n\nbeginaligned\nwidehattextttdestination(k) = alpha  expbigl(c k^2 + s hat w(k)bigr)  hat f(k) \nquad + beta  widehattextttdestination(k)\nendaligned\n\nThe two-operand form\n\nPassing α, a, β, b ahead of the multipliers applies the same kernel to a weighted combination, exp(c k^2 + s hat w) cdot (alpha hat a + beta hat b), in one pass — the lazy-kernel twin of the kernel-composed combine! method, and the fusion divergence! and helmholtz_projection! perform when given a trailing kernel. With b === destination it is the filtered accumulate, computed in place.\n\nwarning: The offset must be real-valued and even\nThe exponential of a real, even multiplier keeps a real field real, and both halves of that hypothesis are the caller's to meet. A complex-valued offset breaks the Hermitian symmetry — and so does a real-valued one that is not even in k on the self-conjugate planes, where both members of a pm k pair are stored and nothing forces them equal. Either way the inverse transform then silently keeps only the Hermitian part, exactly as a caller-built kernel can under convolve!. There is deliberately no scalar exponent offset: a constant s_0 in the exponent is e^s_0 times the kernel and belongs in α.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.exponential_convolve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.exponential_convolve","page":"Convolution","text":"exponential_convolve(field, multipliers, coefficient::Real)\nexponential_convolve(field, multipliers, coefficient::Real,\n                     offset_scale::Real, offset::ScalarFieldSpectral)\n\nAllocating form of exponential_convolve!: allocate the result and return the field smoothed by the exponential radial kernel, which for a non-positive coefficient is a periodic Gaussian blur. See exponential_convolve! for the kernel, the argument meanings, and the two-operand forms; the representation and tensor order are preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.exponential_convolve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.StoredKernel","page":"Convolution","text":"StoredKernel(field::ScalarFieldSpectral)\n\nWrap a scalar field in spectral representation so that it plays the kernel role — one coefficient per mode, the O(N^3) storage the compressed classes exist to avoid.\n\nThe wrapper is required rather than a convenience. A field and a kernel hold identical data, so nothing but a type can say which role is meant, and the two admit different operations: the coefficientwise reciprocal of a kernel is the kernel that deconvolves by it (inv), while the same operation on a field is nonlinear in that field's spectral representation and the package does not define it. So a bare ScalarFieldSpectral passed where a kernel is expected does not dispatch — wrap it here, or build the kernel with one of the builders, and the role is then carried by the type rather than inferred from the call site.\n\nWrapping costs nothing per application: the struct holds a reference, and the fused passes reach the coefficients through it without an extra read. Wrap once, where the kernel is built.\n\nThe expansion materialize_kernel returns is this class, so a compressed kernel expanded into its coefficients arrives already in the kernel role.\n\nFields\n\nfield — the ScalarFieldSpectral holding the coefficients, referenced rather than copied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.StoredKernel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.SeparableKernel","page":"Convolution","text":"SeparableKernel\n\nA spectral kernel that factors over the axes, hat g(k) = prod_a g_a(k_a), stored as its three one-dimensional per-axis factors rather than as a full field. The factors are evaluated into the product one mode at a time, so the kernel never occupies a field's worth of memory: three arrays of O(N) entries in place of O(N^3) coefficients — kilobytes rather than the 129 MiB a 256^3 kernel would take — and applying it reads no field-sized kernel stream: the three O(N) factors are read per mode, which is the saving, not zero kernel traffic.\n\nThere is no public constructor, which is why no signature is shown above: a kernel of this class is always obtained from a builder, so that its per-axis factors match the grid they will be applied on. Build one with translation_kernel (the displacement phase, complex factors) or assignment_window (the mass-assignment window, real factors), and apply it exactly like a kernel held as a field: pass it to convolve! or as the trailing kernel of divergence! or helmholtz_projection!.\n\nWhether that is also faster depends on the grid. Applying it moves two field-sized streams where a stored kernel moves three, but evaluates the product per mode, so it trails a stored kernel on a grid small enough to sit in cache and overtakes it once the pass is bound by memory. On a small grid where the difference matters, materialize_kernel trades the memory back, and its docstring carries the measured crossover. Use inv for the reciprocal kernel 1hat g, which is separable too since 1prod_a g_a = prod_a (1g_a) — the deconvolution an assignment window is built for.\n\nnote: Separable, not radial\nA kernel that depends only on the wavenumber magnitude is usually radial, not separable — a general function of k_1^2 + k_2^2 + k_3^2 does not factor into per-axis terms, the exponential being the notable exception (exp(ck^2) factors, and has its own operation). This type is for kernels that genuinely do factor, the typical case being a per-axis phase.\n\nFields\n\nfactor_1, factor_2, factor_3 — the per-axis factors, each shaped to broadcast along its own axis, of the stored spectral length of that axis (the folded axis is the layout's fft_dims[1]).\ngrid, layout — the base and Fourier layout the factors were built for, which a call checks the field against.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.SeparableKernel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.RadialKernel","page":"Convolution","text":"RadialKernel\n\nA spectral kernel that depends only on the wavenumber magnitude, hat g(k) = sigma(k^2), stored as the function sigma rather than as its values — its symbol, in the sense the differential operators use for the multiplier a coefficientwise operator applies. The function is evaluated one mode at a time from the wavenumber arrays the multipliers already hold, so the kernel occupies no field: a closure and three references, against the O(N^3) coefficients a stored kernel needs — bytes rather than the 129 MiB a 256^3 kernel would take.\n\nThere is no public constructor, which is why no signature is shown above: a kernel of this class is always obtained from a builder, so that the multipliers it evaluates against are the ones its grid owns. Build one with spectral_cutoff or spectral_band for the sharp masks, or with radial_kernel for any other such function — a smooth envelope, a hyperviscous exp(-nu (k^2)^p tau) of viscosity, power and time step. Apply it like any kernel: pass it to convolve!, as the trailing kernel of divergence! or helmholtz_projection!, or to weighted_inner as a reduction weight.\n\nWhy this family is closed by construction: k^2 is real and even in every component, so any real sigma of it is real and even too, hence Hermitian-symmetric — the coefficients it produces are always some real field's. It also carries the true squared wavenumber at an even axis's Nyquist — the value a magnitude-dependent multiplier wants there, unlike the derivative wavenumbers, which are zeroed at that mode — so no case analysis is needed anywhere in the family.\n\nnote: Storing no coefficients is not the same as costing nothing\nThe symbol is evaluated per mode, and against a field of tensor order R ge 1 it is broadcast over the field's components, so it is evaluated once per component rather than once per mode; at R = 0 there is no fiber to extrude over and nothing extra to pay. Whether that recompute or the kernel-array traffic it saves dominates depends on the base and on the element type.At 64^3 and R = 1 the radial route costs 11–21times a materialized StoredKernel across the CPU thread counts measured; by 256^3–512^3 the saved traffic has overtaken the recompute on most of them, at 070–097. A device is not exempt: in Float64 it runs at 085–110, but in Float32, where halving the stored kernel's traffic leaves the symbol's cost untouched, the radial route is slower at every size on one of the two GPUs, by as much as 26times at 128^3. Hoisting the evaluation was prototyped and measured, and was declined because it lost at the sizes that matter; materialize_kernel is the escape wherever the recompute wins.\n\nnote: Radial, not separable\nk^2 = k_1^2 + k_2^2 + k_3^2 is a function of the sum, so a general real symbol of it does not factor into per-axis terms and cannot be stored per axis the way a SeparableKernel is. The two families overlap rather than partition: the exponential is in both, since exp(ck^2) = prod_a exp(ck_a^2), which is why exponential_convolve! exists as its own fused operation. What this class buys is the general case, where no such factorization is available.\n\nFields\n\nsigma — the function sigma, called with the real k^2 and returning a real value.\nmultipliers — the FieldSpectralMultipliers supplying k^2, referenced, not copied. Whether they are the grid's own wavenumbers (canonical, built by the convenience constructor from the grid) or a caller's own (supplied through the explicit constructor, which validates lengths and not values) is part of their type, and it decides whether composing two of these kernels collapses into one: two kernels over canonical multipliers of the same type hold the same coordinates by construction and merge, while a kernel over a caller's own coordinates keeps both operands as a ProductKernel, since one composite can carry only one set of coordinates.\ngrid, layout — the base and Fourier layout the kernel was built for, which a call checks the field against.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.RadialKernel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.ProductKernel","page":"Convolution","text":"ProductKernel\n\nThe coefficientwise product of two spectral kernels, held as the pair rather than evaluated — what convolve returns whenever composing cannot produce a single kernel more cheaply. There is no public constructor, which is why no signature is shown above: one arises only from that composition. That covers every cross-family pair, two stored kernels (merging those would mean materializing an O(N^3) product), and two radial kernels whose coordinates cannot be shown to agree.\n\nComposition inside a family usually stays in that family: two axis-factored kernels always multiply into one axis-factored kernel, and two radial symbols multiply into one radial symbol when both are evaluated against the same coordinates, which RadialKernel explains and decides by type. Radial operands that fail that test land here instead. Across families there is no collapse at all: a radial symbol times an axis-factored phase is neither radial nor separable, so the composite keeps both operands and multiplies their per-mode values. Either order composes and gives the same operator, the product being coefficientwise, and a composite composes further in either position. It still stores nothing of field size, and it applies exactly like either operand.\n\nThis is what lets a mask and a displacement — or any radial symbol and any per-axis factor — be applied in a single pass instead of two.\n\nFields\n\nfirst_kernel, second_kernel — the two operands, held unmultiplied and evaluated per mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.ProductKernel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#Base.inv","page":"Convolution","text":"inv(kernel::ProductKernel)\n\nThe reciprocal kernel 1hat g, formed by inverting each factor — the product of the reciprocals is the reciprocal of the product — so it is another ProductKernel and materializes nothing.\n\nwarning: Each factor must have no zeros on any stored mode\nThe composite vanishes wherever either factor does, so both must be invertible; each factor's own inv states the condition it needs.\n\n\n\n\n\ninv(kernel::RadialKernel)\n\nThe reciprocal kernel 1sigma(k^2), formed by composing the reciprocal with sigma, so it is another RadialKernel and still costs no storage.\n\nwarning: The function must have no zeros on any stored mode\nA sigma that vanishes at any stored mode gives a non-finite reciprocal — a sharp mask is exactly such a function, so inverting a spectral_cutoff is not meaningful.\n\n\n\n\n\ninv(kernel::SeparableKernel)\n\nThe reciprocal kernel 1hat g, itself separable because 1prod_a g_a = prod_a (1g_a) — so it is formed per axis, at O(N) cost, with no field materialized. Applying it deconvolves by kernel, which is what a mass-assignment window is built for (assignment_window).\n\nwarning: The factors must have no zeros, and this method checks\nEvery per-axis factor must be non-zero at every stored mode. Unlike the radial method, this one can afford to verify it — the factors are O(N) — so a zero throws rather than returning a non-finite reciprocal. An assignment_window satisfies the condition on every stored mode, whereas a phase kernel does not in general (a displacement can drive an even axis's Nyquist factor to zero).\n\n\n\n\n\ninv(kernel::StoredKernel)\n\nThe coefficientwise reciprocal of a kernel held as a field, which is the kernel that deconvolves by it.\n\nA kernel with a zero coefficient annihilates that mode, so no kernel undoes it and this throws rather than returning a non-finite coefficient. The check is a full pass over the coefficients, which an explicit inversion can afford; the axis-factored SeparableKernel method checks its O(N) factors instead, and the radial method documents the precondition it cannot test cheaply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.inv"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.materialize_kernel!","page":"Convolution","text":"materialize_kernel!(destination::StoredKernel,\n                    kernel::SpectralKernel) -> destination\n\nEvaluate a kernel into storage that already exists: every coefficient of destination is overwritten with the kernel's value hat g(k) at that mode, and destination is returned.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating materialize_kernel returns a fresh result instead. The destination is a StoredKernel sharing the grid, Fourier layout, element type and storage family of kernel, which may be of any of the four storage classes of a SpectralKernel; a mismatch is refused. The evaluation allocates nothing, so a field-sized kernel is written without a second field-sized temporary. A caller holding a field it means to fill wraps it once as StoredKernel(field), which references the field rather than copying it.\n\nThe cost is one pass over the modes, the same as the allocating form's without the allocation, and the warning on materialize_kernel about giving up a compressed kernel's memory saving applies to whatever the destination is then used for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.materialize_kernel!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.materialize_kernel","page":"Convolution","text":"materialize_kernel(kernel::ProductKernel)\n\nEvaluate a product kernel into a StoredKernel, which costs the full O(N^3) the compressed forms exist to avoid. A composite evaluates both factors per mode, so its cost against a stored kernel follows from theirs — and the classes differ, so read the crossover for whichever they are. The warning below applies here too.\n\n\n\n\n\nmaterialize_kernel(kernel::StoredKernel)\n\nReturn a copy of a coefficientwise kernel, a StoredKernel owning storage no other shares.\n\n\n\n\n\nmaterialize_kernel(kernel::RadialKernel)\n\nEvaluate a radial kernel into a StoredKernel, which costs the full O(N^3) the closure form exists to avoid. When that trade is worth making is specific to this class: the compressed classes are measured separately and disagree, one card having the radial form overtake a stored kernel by 128^3 where the axis-factored form never overtakes. RadialKernel carries the measured crossover, and the warning below applies here too.\n\n\n\n\n\nmaterialize_kernel(kernel::SeparableKernel)\n\nEvaluate an axis-factored kernel into a kernel held as a field, returning the StoredKernel whose coefficients are the product of the per-axis factors. The result applies identically and, for real factors, gives bit-identical results — the two differ only in what they cost. Complex factors agree bitwise on the host and to rounding on a device.\n\nUse this to trade memory back for speed on a small grid. Applying an axis-factored kernel moves two field-sized streams where a stored kernel moves three, but evaluates the product per mode; which wins depends on the grid. Measured against a stored kernel on a vector field: threaded on a CPU the axis-factored form costs 175–245times a stored kernel at 64^3, is mixed at 128^3 (083–211, Float32 still behind), and overtakes by 256^3–512^3 at 072–095. Single-threaded it never overtakes in this range. On a device the answer depends on the card: of the two measured, one crosses (137 at 64^3 falling to 088 at 256^3 in Float64) while the other does not, staying 120–253times at every size and element type measured. So a run whose grids are small, or which applies one fixed kernel very many times with memory to spare, can materialize once and keep the field; a threaded run at production resolution should not, and pays a field of residency for nothing if it does (129 MiB at 256^3, against about 10 KiB for the factors).\n\nwarning: This gives up the memory saving entirely\nThe returned field costs the full O(N^3) the axis-factored form exists to avoid. Materialize deliberately, for a measured reason on a specific grid — not by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.materialize_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.radial_kernel","page":"Convolution","text":"radial_kernel(sigma, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers) -> RadialKernel\n\nBuild the RadialKernel for an arbitrary real function of the squared wavenumber — its symbol, in the sense the differential operators use — the general constructor behind spectral_cutoff and spectral_band, for the multipliers the package does not name.\n\nHere sigma is a function of one real argument — the squared wavenumber k^2 — returning a real value, and it must be pure and GPU-safe: it may capture only values, never a type, since a closure holding a Type is not a bitstype and a device rejects it at launch, and it may hold no mutable state and do no scalar indexing of device arrays. Anything it does capture by value, such as a viscosity or a substep, is free to vary per call, because building a kernel allocates nothing of field size. The multipliers are the FieldSpectralMultipliers supplying k^2, and whose grid, Fourier layout, element type and storage family the kernel takes as its own.\n\nThe physics stays with the caller: an integrating factor is radial_kernel(k2 -> exp(-ν * k2 * Δt), …), a smooth filter its own envelope. Because the function is evaluated per mode rather than stored, a kernel whose parameters change every call costs nothing to rebuild, where a stored kernel pays a full field write. That evaluation is per mode, so on a CPU at a small grid a field of order R ge 1 pays it once per fiber component; RadialKernel carries the measured trade-off against a materialized kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.radial_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.spectral_cutoff","page":"Convolution","text":"spectral_cutoff(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n                wavenumber_cutoff::Real) -> RadialKernel\n\nBuild an isotropic sharp low-pass kernel, returned as a RadialKernel — the function itself rather than its coefficients, so it costs no field of storage. The evaluation is per mode, so on a CPU at a small grid an R ge 1 field pays it once per fiber component — see RadialKernel for the measured trade-off against a materialized kernel. Use materialize_kernel to expand it into a StoredKernel. It is built on the multipliers' grid and storage family: the transfer function is 1 for k le k_c and 0 above it.\n\nConvolve a field with it for the ideal low-pass projection, the basis of spectral truncation and de-aliasing. It is idempotent (hat g^2 = hat g), hence a projection, and it is the low-pass special case of spectral_band with k_mathrmlo = 0.\n\nHere multipliers is the FieldSpectralMultipliers whose grid, Fourier layout, element type and storage family the kernel takes as its own, and wavenumber_cutoff is the cutoff wavenumber k_c — a mode is kept when k le k_c.\n\nhat g(k) = mathbf1bigl k le k_c bigr\n\nThe choice of cutoff, such as a 23 de-aliasing rule or an LES filter scale, is the caller's. The squared wavenumber k^2 keeps the true value at the Nyquist frequency (the Laplacian convention).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.spectral_cutoff"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.spectral_band","page":"Convolution","text":"spectral_band(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n              wavenumber_low::Real, wavenumber_high::Real) -> RadialKernel\n\nBuild an isotropic sharp spectral band-pass kernel, returned as a RadialKernel — the function itself rather than its coefficients, so it costs no field of storage. The evaluation is per mode, so on a CPU at a small grid an R ge 1 field pays it once per fiber component — see RadialKernel for the measured trade-off against a materialized kernel. Use materialize_kernel to expand it into a StoredKernel. It is built on the multipliers' grid and storage family: the transfer function is 1 on the wavenumber shell and 0 outside it.\n\nConvolve a field with it to keep an isotropic shell of wavenumbers — a de-aliasing band, a forcing band, or any shell selector. It is idempotent (hat g^2 = hat g), hence a projection, and it is the annular generalization of spectral_cutoff, which is the low-pass case k_mathrmlo = 0.\n\nArguments\n\nmultipliers — the FieldSpectralMultipliers whose grid, Fourier layout, element type and storage family the kernel takes as its own.\nwavenumber_low, wavenumber_high — the inner and outer edges k_mathrmlo, k_mathrmhi of the retained shell.\n\nhat g(k) = begincases 1  k_mathrmlo le k le k_mathrmhi  0  textotherwise endcases\n\nwith k^2 keeping the true value at the Nyquist frequency (the Laplacian convention). The band edges are the caller's choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.spectral_band"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.tophat_window","page":"Convolution","text":"tophat_window(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers, width::Real) -> SeparableKernel\ntophat_window(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n              width::NTuple{3, Real}) -> SeparableKernel\n\nReturn the top-hat (box-average) window of a given width as a SeparableKernel, its three per-axis factors on the multipliers' grid and storage family: convolving a field with it replaces the field at each point by its mean over the box of that width centred there.\n\nUse it to average a field over sub-boxes smaller than the domain — the scale-resolved statistics that remove a sub-box mean, such as the kinetic energy held in scales below a given size, or a box filter of chosen width. The returned w is the window's transfer function hat W; the weights those statistics need are its square, convolve(w, w), which composes per axis at no extra cost.\n\nHere multipliers is the FieldSpectralMultipliers whose grid, Fourier layout, element type and storage family the window takes as its own, and width is the edge of the box in the units of the domain: one number for a cube, or one per axis for a rectangular box. Each edge must be finite and non-negative, and a zero edge leaves that axis unfiltered.\n\nAs a real physical object the window is the normalized indicator of the box, the tensor product of per-axis top-hats, and the returned transfer function is its product of per-axis sincs:\n\nbegingathered\nW(x) = prod_a=1^3 frac1l_a mathbf1biglx_a le tfracl_a2bigr \nhat W(k) = prod_a=1^3 operatornamesincBigl(tfrack_a l_a2Bigr) \noperatornamesinc(y) = fracsin yy\nendgathered\n\nwith l_a the box edge along axis a, x_a the displacement along it reduced onto the periodic domain, and k_a the true wavenumber component, kept at the Nyquist frequency so a real field stays real. On a periodic domain the width may be any non-negative number: at l_a equal to the domain's extent every nonzero wavenumber along that axis is cancelled to rounding, so a box the size of the domain keeps the mean alone, and as l_a to 0 that axis's factor tends to one. The assignment_window is this window at the grid spacing, squared.\n\nnote: It averages the band-limited field, not the grid values\nThe sinc is the transform of the continuum box, so the result is the exact box mean of the band-limited field the grid values represent, even for an edge of a whole number of cells: it is not the mean of the nodes inside the box. That field can itself exceed the range of its grid values, and so can its box mean, where a mean of grid values could not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.tophat_window"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.assignment_window","page":"Convolution","text":"assignment_window(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers) -> SeparableKernel\n\nReturn the linear (cloud-in-cell) mass-assignment window — assignment in the particle-mesh sense of depositing a point's mass onto the grid cells around it, not the sense of writing a value into an array — as a SeparableKernel — its three per-axis factors rather than a field of coefficients — on the multipliers' grid and storage family, for deconvolving a gridded point density.\n\nUse it when a pair correlation is estimated from points deposited on a grid rather than counted directly. Depositing M points on the grid with the positive linear assignment (LalescuSpline(0, 0) — the two-point linear \"tent\" transfer kernel, the smoothness m = 0 member of the Interpolation spline family) spreads each point's mass over a tent, so the gridded density is the true density rho convolved with this window, rho_g = rho star W. Dividing the transform by hat W — a convolve by 1hat W — undoes that smearing before you estimate the radial distribution function g(r) (the pair density at separation r relative to an uncorrelated uniform gas).\n\nHere multipliers is the FieldSpectralMultipliers whose grid, Fourier layout, element type and storage family the window takes as its own.\n\nAs a real physical object W is the linear B-spline (\"tent\") cloud, the tensor product of per-axis tents:\n\nW(x) = prod_a=1^3 frac1Delta x_a \n       maxBigl(0 1 - fracx_aDelta x_aBigr)\n\neach a tent of half-width one cell Delta x_a along axis a, of unit integral — the tophat_window at the grid spacing convolved with itself, hence the squared sinc. It returns its transfer function hat W(k), the squared sinc\n\nbegingathered\nhat W(k) = prod_a=1^3 operatornamesinc^2Bigl(tfrack_a Delta x_a2Bigr) \noperatornamesinc(y) = fracsin yy\nendgathered\n\ncomputed from the per-axis wavenumber k_a (kept at the Nyquist frequency) and the grid spacing Delta x_a, so hat W(0) = 1 and it decays toward the grid scale. Because the squared sinc does not vanish at any wavenumber the grid resolves, dividing by it is stable. Stable is not exact: hat W is the transform of the continuum tent, while a grid deposit applies that transform plus the aliasing sum over the periodic images of k, so the division undoes the smearing only up to that aliasing — trust the band well below the Nyquist frequency. The package supplies the window; the density estimate and the correlation normalization stay with the caller.\n\nReferences\n\nR. W. Hockney, J. W. Eastwood, Computer Simulation Using Particles, Adam Hilger, Bristol, 1988 (deposition / charge assignment; the NGP and CIC windows).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.assignment_window"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.translation_kernel","page":"Convolution","text":"translation_kernel(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n                   offset::NTuple{3, Real}) -> SeparableKernel\n\nBuild the kernel that translates a field by the physical displacement h = offset, returned as a SeparableKernel on the multipliers' grid and storage family: convolving a field f with it gives the displaced field f(x - h), the position x reduced onto the periodic box.\n\nUse it to move a field onto a displaced grid without re-gridding — a shift-and-average filter, the relocation of a probe, or the displaced evaluation of a phase-shift de-aliasing scheme. Apply it with convolve / convolve!, which is allocation-free in place; because a scalar kernel multiplies every fiber component, one call translates a whole vector or tensor field, and because the kernel depends on nothing but the displacement, one kernel serves every field it is applied to (and it is separable, so building it costs three O(N) axis passes, not a field). To sample the field at the displaced positions x + h instead, pass the negated offset — the two are inverse translations. This is the package's one complex kernel: on every sub-Nyquist mode it is a pure phase of unit modulus, so it moves the field without touching any amplitude, unlike the real and even spectral_cutoff or assignment_window. At the Nyquist frequency — the highest wavenumber a grid stores, piDelta x_a, which an even-length axis stores as a single self-conjugate mode — the factor is instead the real number cos(pi h_a  Delta x_a), which attenuates rather than translates and vanishes at a half-cell shift — the reality condition demands it, and the warning below gives the detail.\n\nHere multipliers is the FieldSpectralMultipliers whose grid, Fourier layout, element type and storage family the kernel takes as its own, and offset is the displacement h = (h_1 h_2 h_3) — a physical length per axis in the units of the box rather than a count of cells, so a half-cell displacement is 0.5 .* grid.grid_spacing on the Grid3D the field lives on.\n\nAs a real physical object the translation is the convolution with a Dirac delta displaced to h and periodized on the box, g = delta(x - h), whose sifting property (integrating against a delta evaluates the integrand at the delta's location) gives (f star g)(x) = f(x - h) exactly. On the grid that g is the band-limited (periodic-sinc) interpolation stencil centred at h, so the convolution re-samples the trigonometric interpolant of f at the displaced nodes x - h, and collapses to a plain index shift when h is a whole number of cells. Its transfer function hat g at wavenumber k is the shift theorem's phase, assembled per axis from that wavenumber's component k_a and the displacement h_a along the axis:\n\nhat g(k) = prod_a=1^3 bigl cos(k_a h_a) - mathrmi sin(a_a h_a) bigr\n\nwhere k_a is the true wavenumber and a_a the Nyquist-zeroed derivative one — the package's two families, and the reason this is written with two symbols rather than as the bare phase e^-mathrmi k cdot h. The two coincide on every resolved mode — so the factor there is that pure phase — and part only at an even axis's Nyquist frequency, where the warning below applies.\n\nwarning: At the Nyquist frequency the phase becomes a real attenuation\nOn an even-length axis the Nyquist mode of a real field carries a single real amplitude that stands for both of the wavenumbers pmpiDelta x_a (with Delta x_a the grid spacing along axis a), so its band-limited form is the cosine cos(pi x_aDelta x_a), and displacing that cosine re-samples on the grid to cos(pi h_aDelta x_a) times itself. At that one frequency the kernel therefore carries this real factor rather than a complex phase — and it has to be real: the Nyquist mode is its own conjugate partner, so a complex factor there would leave coefficients that are those of no real field at all. A displaced Nyquist mode is thus attenuated rather than moved: it vanishes for a half-cell displacement and changes sign for an odd whole-cell one. Two consequences follow, both confined to that frequency: the translation is not invertible there (displacing by h and back scales the mode by cos^2), and two translations compose exactly on every resolved mode but not at Nyquist. Whole-cell displacements are exact everywhere, and a field with no Nyquist content — one truncated by a de-aliasing cutoff — never meets the caveat at all.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.translation_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.translation_kernel!","page":"Convolution","text":"translation_kernel!(kernel::SeparableKernel, multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers,\n                    offset::NTuple{3, Real})\n\nRebuild and return kernel for a new displacement offset, overwriting its per-axis factors. Use this in-place form when a displacement changes and the kernel is already allocated; the allocating translation_kernel returns a fresh kernel instead. The cost is three O(N) axis passes, not a field, so rebuilding per call is cheap.\n\nArguments\n\nkernel — the SeparableKernel whose per-axis factors are overwritten.\nmultipliers — the wavenumber arrays, supplying both families (the derivative wavenumbers and the true squared ones), checked against kernel's grid and layout.\noffset — the physical displacement h as a three-tuple.\n\nThe displacement convention and the Nyquist treatment are translation_kernel's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution.translation_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_convolution!-Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Convolution","text":"_apply_convolution!(destination_data, source_data, kernel_data, α, β)\n\nApply the coefficientwise spectral product that realizes the periodic convolution in spectral representation, writing textttdst mathrel= alpha hat g cdot hat f + beta textttdst (textttdst the destination_data, hat f the source_data, hat g = kernel_data). The scalar kernel_data, of the field's spectral spatial shape (the folded axis halved), broadcasts over the fiber components of source_data exactly as the Laplacian's k^2 does, so one body serves every tensor order. The call branches on iszero(β) to overwrite or accumulate. The product is coefficientwise, so it is alias-safe: destination_data may be source_data or the kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_convolution!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_convolution_combination!-Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}, Vararg{Any, 5}}","page":"Convolution","text":"_apply_convolution_combination!(destination_data, a_data, b_data, kernel_data,\n                                α, β)\n\nApply the fused kernel-times-combination product, computing textttdst mathrel= hat g cdot (alpha hat a + beta hat b) in one coefficientwise pass (hat g = kernel_data). The scalar kernel_data broadcasts over the fiber components exactly as in _apply_convolution!, and the product is coefficientwise, so the destination may alias either operand or the kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_convolution_combination!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_exponential_combination!-NTuple{7, Any}","page":"Convolution","text":"_apply_exponential_combination!(destination_data, a_data, b_data,\n                                squared_wavenumber, coefficient, α, β)\n\n_apply_exponential_combination!(destination_data, a_data, b_data,\n                                squared_wavenumber, coefficient,\n                                offset_scale, offset_data, α, β)\n\nApply the exponential radial kernel to a weighted combination, computing textttdst mathrel= exp(c k^2) cdot (alpha hat a + beta hat b) (the eight-argument form adds + s hat w inside the exponential) — the exponential twin of _apply_convolution_combination!, with the kernel evaluated lazily per mode instead of read from a stored field. Coefficientwise, hence alias-safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_exponential_combination!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_exponential_convolution!-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"Convolution","text":"_apply_exponential_convolution!(destination_data, source_data, squared_wavenumber,\n                                coefficient, α, β)\n\n_apply_exponential_convolution!(destination_data, source_data, squared_wavenumber,\n                                coefficient, offset_scale, offset_data, α, β)\n\nApply the coefficientwise product with the exponential radial kernel, computing textttdst mathrel= alpha exp(c k^2) cdot hat f + beta textttdst (the eight-argument form adds + s hat w inside the exponential, with hat w = offset_data and s = offset_scale). The squared_wavenumber is the lazy _squared_wavenumber_broadcast, so the kernel is evaluated per mode inside the fused assignment and never materialized. The call branches on iszero(β) to overwrite or accumulate; the product is coefficientwise, hence alias-safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_exponential_convolution!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_separable_combination!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, SeparableKernel, Any, Any}","page":"Convolution","text":"_apply_separable_combination!(destination_data, a_data, b_data, kernel, α, β)\n\nConvolve a weighted combination with a SeparableKernel in one pass, writing textttdst mathrel= hat g(alpha hat a + beta hat b). It takes the same cartesian routes as _apply_separable_convolution!, and for the same measured reason.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_separable_combination!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_separable_convolution!-Tuple{Any, Any, SeparableKernel, Any, Any}","page":"Convolution","text":"_apply_separable_convolution!(destination_data, source_data, kernel, α, β)\n\nApply a SeparableKernel as one broadcast over the whole section, writing textttdst mathrel= alpha hat g cdot hat f + beta textttdst. The three per-axis factors carry singleton axes, so they broadcast along the remaining mode axes and along the fiber, and one expression serves scalar, vector, and tensor fields exactly as the stored-kernel path does.\n\nThe shape is chosen for two measured reasons. Broadcasting indexes the factors cartesian, so no element pays a linear-to-cartesian conversion — an integer division, emulated on a GPU. And the launch stays flat, which keeps it coalesced — neighbouring threads reading neighbouring addresses, which a GPU serves as one wide transaction. The folded axis is N2 + 1 modes long, half its point count plus one (129 at 256^3), so a three-dimensional launch would cut each row against a length that is not a multiple of the warp — the group of threads a GPU issues in lockstep, 32 on NVIDIA and 64 on AMD — leaving every row's last transaction partly idle.\n\nThe kernel value, the axis factors in order, is associated as the stored path does, alpha((sigma_1sigma_2)sigma_3)hat f, so a real-factored kernel and its materialized product agree bitwise. A complex-factored one can differ by an ulp on a device: the compiler contracts the ac - bd of a complex multiply into a fused multiply-add, differently when the product is inlined here than when a stored kernel rounds it to memory first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._apply_separable_convolution!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._exponential_combination_modes!-NTuple{12, Any}","page":"Convolution","text":"_exponential_combination_modes!(backend, destination, a_data, b_data, multipliers,\n                                coefficient, offset_scale, offset_data,\n                                α, β, fibres, mode_size)\n\nThe combination twin of _exponential_convolution_modes!: exp(c k^2 + s hat w) cdot (alpha hat a + beta hat b) with the factor computed once per mode. Both operands are always read (combine!'s α/β exception class — β scales an operand, never the destination), so there is no overwrite/accumulate split.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._exponential_combination_modes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._exponential_convolution_modes!-NTuple{11, Any}","page":"Convolution","text":"_exponential_convolution_modes!(backend, destination, source, multipliers,\n                                coefficient, offset_scale, offset_data,\n                                α, β, fibres, mode_size)\n\nRun the fiber-hoisted exponential pass over every mode: the factor exp(c k^2 + s hat w) computed once per mode and applied to all fibres components. The generic method launches _exponential_convolution_kernel! on the backend; the CPU method runs the slab-threaded loop, bitwise identical at every thread count (each element is written exactly once).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._exponential_convolution_modes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._kernel_storage-Tuple{StoredKernel}","page":"Convolution","text":"_kernel_storage(kernel)\n\nRepresent the kernel's storage family as an array, one method per storage class.\n\nA kernel is not a section and has no field_data, and the four classes hold their memory in four different shapes: a stored kernel wraps a field, a separable one holds three per-axis factors, a radial one holds no coefficients at all and borrows its family from the multipliers it evaluates against, and a product borrows from its first factor, the two being required to agree. Used only for display, to name the family a kernel would combine within.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._kernel_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Convolution/#GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._show_kernel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{IO, SpectralKernel{T}}} where T","page":"Convolution","text":"_show_kernel(io, kernel)\n\nPrint a kernel as its class, element type, base and storage family.\n\nThe base and the family are what _check_kernel_compatibility refuses on; the class and the element type are what separates two kernels it would accept. Shared by the three classes whose display is this and nothing more — ProductKernel has its own, because it nests its factors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Convolution._show_kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"decisions/","page":"Decision log","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Decision log"},{"category":"section","location":"decisions/#Decision-log-—-GBGPeriodicFields","page":"Decision log","text":"Chronological and append-only. Each entry records a decision, what it replaced, and the evidence that settled it; a later entry supersedes an earlier one's \"remaining\" or \"deferred\" note rather than contradicting it. An entry may use notation the architecture or an earlier entry introduced.\n\nThis log lived as §13 of the architecture until 2026-09-19, where it was three quarters of that file and pushed the design it documents into the last quarter. It is cited throughout the package as \"decision N\", and those citations are unchanged.\n\nChronological and append-only — later entries supersede earlier \"remaining\"/\"deferred\" notes (e.g. the CPU threading and the interpolation layer) rather than contradicting them.\n\nFields refactor: atomic compatibility predicates; shared _check_same_domain; _check_field_matches_context for transforms.\nFieldSpectralMultipliers moved DifferentialOperators → Fields; explicit six-array constructor (validated lengths) plus convenience constructor; host-built wavenumbers copied to the backend; Nyquist split between derivative and squared multipliers (correctness, see §4).\nFieldAlgebra rewritten around code generation with the invariant that the public method set is reproduced exactly; M2 + M3 delivered and parity-verified at runtime (198 methods, the full surface). Validation rewired to direct _check_field_domain_compatibility calls — alias wrappers explicitly rejected.\nDocstring policy: one docstring per operation on the bare binding, curated signatures + validity prose; generators never emit docstrings.\nOperators defined in function op(...) ... end form (parse-proven house style); short form only for unary +.\nGPU-first; CPU threading designed (_assign!, §9) and deliberately deferred.\nRefactor complete: FieldAlgebra loads and is parity-verified (M2 + M3, 198 methods); DifferentialOperators rewired to import FieldSpectralMultipliers and its helpers from Fields, with the umbrella re-export corrected (the old five-argument constructor removed by design); reference test suite in place (test/, Pkg.test() green) including the method-parity baseline. Remaining: deferred CPU threading (§9) and the planned interpolation layer.\nStaticArrays promoted from StaticArraysCore to a direct dependency — the package indexes fibers and uses their algebra in the generated kernels, so the type-only core package left field ± fiber, scalarfield * fiber, and the M3 fiber contractions undefined at runtime (see §3).\nTransform input-preservation contract: in-place to_physical!/to_spectral! may overwrite their source (FFTW c2r destroys input; backend-divergent), so the allocating to_physical/to_spectral copy the input to keep non-! calls non-mutating (see §4).\nGPU testing is opt-in and dependency-free for the package: the data layer is  generic over AbstractArray, FFT plans are built through AbstractFFTs  (backend-dispatched on the array type) and allocations inherit their backend via  similar, so a GPU backend is only ever a test dependency. It lives in  per-backend environments under test/gpu/ (rocm/ and cuda/), each  instantiated only on matching hardware (AMD at home, NVIDIA at the office); a  shared, backend-agnostic suite.jl runs the public surface on device arrays with  allowscalar(false) — turning invariant 6 into a checked guarantee — and compares  against the trusted CPU result. The default Pkg.test() stays backend-free, so it  is portable across CI and every machine.\nTest-suite buildout (beyond the GPU work in item 10): element-type genericity  (Float32 alongside Float64), anisotropic-domain operators (the 2piL scaling),  in-place and alias-guard coverage, a Nyquist end-to-end test, and the operator  adjointness / structural identities on randomized band-limited fields  (nablacdot = -nabla^top, self-adjoint curl, negative-semidefinite Laplacian — negative-definite  on the zero-mean subspace, its kernel being the constant mode — exactly idempotent Helmholtz  projection (decision 42) — the discrete energy/entropy structure). Intentional-absence tests are  symmetric across representations: nonlinear products absent in spectral; the  differential operators absent in physical (spectral-only — no finite-difference  operators yet). Aqua.test_all was added, which required [compat] bounds for every  dependency. Two deliberate deferrals: a global L²/integration reduction is reserved  for separate code (so adjointness tests use an inline L² inner product), and  doctests wait until the package stabilizes and tutorials are written. The doctest deferral  is closed: docs/make.jl sets doctest = true and docs/src/tutorial.md carries jldoctest  blocks, so the tutorial's examples are executed by the docs build. Coverage detail  lives in docs/TESTING.md.\nCPU threading implemented (§9), realizing the design formerly deferred in items 6/9. A  Fields._assign! primitive threads elementwise/fiberwise assignments on CPU  (Base.Threads, contiguous chunks, bitwise-identical to serial), routed in via the  field broadcast copyto! and the kernel assignments (codegen _to_broadcasted plus the  @_assign! macro for hand-written kernels); FFTW threads are matched to Julia's at load.  Hybrid policy: on when nthreads() > 1 and above a 2^14 serial cutoff, with a  task-local with_threading ScopedValue override; the GPU path stays on the  materialize! fallback. [compat] julia was raised to 1.11 here (for ScopedValue); the floor has  since moved again, to 1.12 (decision 31).  Reductions remain deferred (separate FP-ordering design).\nInterpolation-layer design fixed (§10):  grid→point interpolation as the callable f(x) / f.(xs) plus the named  interpolate / interpolate!; point→grid anterpolation as anterpolate /  anterpolate! — the symmetric named pair, each with allocating and in-place forms, and the  α/β-accumulate form on the scatter only (the mul! convention, since they are the linear maps  W / W^top);  only interpolation also has the pointwise callable f(x) (the adjoint of interpolation;  synonyms — spreading,  deposition, scatter, projection/PSI-Cell, gridding, two-way-coupling feedback — listed  on the binding; \"extrapolation\" rejected as a misnomer). Adjointness is explained in  the module docstring via the function–measure duality (Dirac evaluation vs. pushforward  of point masses). Method:  the Lalescu local splines (Lalescu, Teaca & Carati 2010) as in TurTLE  (Lalescu et al. 2022) — local Hermite-with-finite-difference  derivatives, tunable (neighbours n, smoothness m), single (physical)  representation, GPU-friendly; van Hinsberg B-spline (2012) and NUFFT exact-spectral  are the higher-accuracy / reference alternatives held in reserve. Interpolation and  anterpolation share the same partition-of-unity weights, so anterpolation is the  conservative transpose (Sundaram & Collins 1996) — required to conserve total water  and latent heat in the two-way-coupled parcel DNS. Implemented (item 17).\nPoint-data types fixed (§10) and the math-backend policy recorded. The unstructured base  reuses the base/fiber split: Point{T} = SVector{3,T} (a position; an alias of  FiberVector, not a wrapper); PointSet (M positions, SoA (M,3), grid-free);  and PointValues as three concrete types mirroring the field zoo —  ScalarPointValues / VectorPointValues / TensorPointValues{R} under  AbstractPointValues, SoA, representation-free. Naming is domain-neutral by policy:  the package is a math backend, so no particle / tracer type and no concept of time  (no advection / integration / temporal state) — those are the consumer's. The types live in  Interpolation, importing Fields helpers; they are implemented and tested (only the  transfer kernels of item 13 remain future work — see item 15 for the algebra).\nPoint-value algebra implemented (§10): FieldAlgebra's code generators are reused  verbatim over an extended point-value section interface, mirroring the full physical  field surface (M2 + M3, storage-wise broadcasting) with no duplicated formula; locked by a  separate 118-method baseline (the 198-method field surface byte-unchanged), zero  ambiguities, tested on CPU (test/pointvalues_algebra.jl) and GPU (ROCm). The shared  AbstractFiberSection{T, R} supertype the fiber-bundle model (§2) invites — and the lift of  the inlined norm/sym/skew/dev formulas into a builder — is the planned next step, not yet  undertaken.\nPhase A consolidation done (§2, §10). The foundation module FiberSections holds the  base-agnostic supertype AbstractFiberSection{T,R} (the field and point-value types subtype  it), the _assign! threading primitive, and the vertical-algebra code generators — the  single home for every fiberwise formula (norm/sym/skew/dev included, via _section_scalar_type),  reused verbatim by FieldAlgebra and Points. The former Interpolation module is split  into Points (unstructured-base section objects + their generated algebra — a noun peer of  Fields) and Interpolation (the transfer maps — an operation peer of  DifferentialOperators). The field/point asymmetry is math-mandated (no point differential  operators, no second representation; the point algebra is a small generated emission folded into  Points). Both parity baselines (198 + 118) held byte-for-byte through every step, zero  ambiguities. (The transfer kernels themselves followed — item 17.)\nTransfer kernels implemented (§10): interpolation (gather W, also the callable f(x)) and  anterpolation (scatter W^top, with the α/β mul! convention) as the Lalescu local splines.  Both hierarchies are provided: the smooth LalescuSpline(n, m) — a two-point Hermite of  degree 2m+1 matching the value and the order-1 dots m derivatives (centred Fornberg  finite differences) at the two bracketing nodes, C^m with 0 le m le 2n (CatmullRom, the  cubic n=1 m=1, is the default; m=0 is linear) — and the C^0 LagrangeSpline(n), the  degree-2n+1 interpolant of all 2n+2 nodes. The smooth weights are pinned to a tolerance  against TurTLE's beta_n{n}_m{m} (n ↔ interp_neighbours, m ↔ smoothness; the pin asserts  le 1e-12, test/interpolation_transfer.jl) — an earlier \"interpolate the central 2(n-m)+2 nodes\" draft was corrected  after reading the TurTLE source, Lalescu's smooth spline being a two-point Hermite, not a  central-node hybrid. Reproduction is min(2m+1 2n) for n ge 1 (smooth) / 2n+1 (Lagrange); the  closed-form Catmull–Rom and the Lagrange cardinal  basis are independent anchors, with partition of unity (⇒ conservation). Kernels are portable  across CPU / CUDA / AMDGPU via KernelAbstractions (new dep) with the atomic scatter via  Atomix (new dep); both index the native arrays linearly, so one kernel serves  scalar/vector/tensor orders. Adjointness in the counting pairing — sum_p (Wf)_p cdot q_p = sum_n f_n cdot (W^top q)_n, the unnormalized sums over the M points and the N grid nodes — and conservation hold to roundoff;  tested on CPU (test/interpolation_transfer.jl, Float64/Float32, incl. the TurTLE pin) and GPU  (ROCm). Physical-space only. The kernels loop with the contiguous (x) axis innermost  (stride-1 stencil access) — a measured GPU-first choice (≈1.6× at 256³, ≈3.9× for the 512³  Lalescu on a since-retired card), re-affirmed on CPU in the decision-37 production band: the 512³  Zen 5 A/B found z-inner ~10% ahead only on the narrow Catmull–Rom stencil and 23% behind on  the wide Lalescu one, so x-inner stands on both platforms and the reopened tiebreak is closed  (PERFORMANCE.md §4b).  The measure-consistent adjoint against the two normalized pairings is (NM) W^top (N grid  points, M sample points; the factor is the ratio of the 1N and 1M normalizations, not of  a cell volume) — both base measures are uniform,  so it is a scalar rescaling of the counting-transpose anterpolation, not a new operation — and is  verified via inner as M textttinner(Wf q) = N textttinner(f W^top q) (test/interpolation_transfer.jl, decision 19).\nThe L² / measure structure on sections — the third operation family (done). Advances  the reduction deferral of items 6/11/§9. The package's operations split into vertical  (fiberwise, pointwise — dot, norm, cross; return sections), horizontal (differential —  gradient, …; return sections), and now base reductions — integrate the base away against a  measure it carries; return a fiber value or a scalar. Math grounding (§2): a section lives in  L^2(mathrmbase mu) otimes V — the fiber V is a tensor factor, not flattened away — and  the inner product pairs the two factors — the fiber contraction is the metric pairing on V  (cf. Manifolds.jl inner(M,p,X,Y), a fiber-level pairing) and the base integral is the  L^2(mathrmbase mu) factor —  the measure is an explicit object (cf. Gridap.jl Measure, MeasureTheory.jl), and the  moments are the canonical reductions (cf. Statistics mean/std/var). All dispatch on  AbstractFiberSection, so fields and point-values share one set of formulas (FiberSections  \"write it once\").\nThe measure is a property of the base. Grid3D → uniform Lebesgue quadrature (cell volume  prod_i Delta x_i); PointSet → counting measure (unit weight per point) — and this is  forced, not a choice: a PointSet is grid-free (§10, carries no domain), so the only well-defined  measure is counting. Consequences:\nintegral(f) is the extensive sum against the measure, mean(f) the intensive average  textttmean(f) = tfrac1mathrmcountsum f — per base:\nGrid3D (N cells): textttintegral(f) = int_Omega fmathrmdx = (lvertOmegarvertN)sum f, textttmean(f) = tfrac1Nsum f.\nPointSet (M points): textttintegral(f) = sum f, textttmean(f) = tfrac1Msum f.\nSo mean is the arithmetic average over base elements in both cases — tfrac1Msum over points  (a plain sample mean), tfrac1Nsum over the grid (which, the grid being uniform, equals the  domain average langlecdotrangle). Names and conventions:\nintegral(f) — the extensive int fmathrmdmu (the one quantity that scales with the base measure).\nmean(f), std(f), var(f) — extend Statistics (we own the types ⇒ not piracy); intensive, fiber-preserving (mean(VectorField) → FiberVector, base reduced).\ninner(f, g) — the normalized L² inner product langle f grangle = textttmean(textttdot(f g)) → scalar (fiber-contracting). Normalized (intensive) by deliberate choice: it matches the mean convention above and the intensive quantities of homogeneous turbulence (kinetic energy tfrac12 textttinner(uu) = tfrac12langle u^2rangle); for points it is tfrac1Msum textttdot, consistent with mean. Not the textbook extensive int fcdot gmathrmdx — that is integral(dot(f,g)) if ever needed.\ntextttl2norm(f) = sqrttextttinner(ff), generalized as lpnorm(f, p) (p=2 default; p=infty ⇒ sup of the fiber magnitude — the CFL case). Chosen over norm_L2; does not touch the vertical norm.\nmaximum/minimum/extrema — extend Base for scalar sections; vector/tensor sup of magnitude via lpnorm at p = infty.\nNo LinearAlgebra overloading: dot/norm stay the vertical pointwise ops (return sections, scalar fields); the global inner/l2norm are package generics returning numbers.\ninner/l2norm are representation-invariant (Parseval): the physical reductions came first, and the  spectral inner/l2norm are now implemented — for two fields in spectral representation  textttinner(hat f hat g) equals the physical inner via the rfft half-spectrum Parseval weighting (each mode  counts twice except those on the k_x=0 plane and, for even N_x, the Nyquist plane — the planes the  rfft maps to themselves under conjugation, whose partners are already stored, so they count once), computed  as a dot over the array plus a plane correction per self-conjugate plane — allocation-free in the  inner(f, f)/l2norm case, while the mixed inner(f, g) materializes those planes: the folded  axis's zero plane always, and its Nyquist plane as well when that axis has even length, so an odd  folded axis pays for one and an even one for two (decision 26). The integral and mean reductions are  representation-invariant too, by a different route — the zero (DC) Fourier mode: under the  unnormalized-forward convention the DC coefficient is hat f(0) = sum_x f(x) (real for a real  field), so the mean is hat f(0)N and the integral (OmegaN) hat f(0) with no transform,  read from the same zero-mode slot the constant-add writes (invariant 4). The lpnorm reduction and  maximum/minimum/extrema have no single-coefficient form and remain physical-only — lpnorm at  every exponent, p = 2 included, that case being served in spectral representation by l2norm  rather than by an lpnorm method. (This entry's original wording also grouped std/var with them;  decision 49 later found those two physical-only by scoping rather than by validity, and gave them  their spectral form.)  Physics stays consumer-side: energy tfrac12 textttinner(uu), enstrophy  tfrac12 textttinner(omegaomega), dissipation nu textttinner(omegaomega), rms sqrttextttinner, intensities from std. The FP-ordering  concern that deferred reductions (item 11) is real — so they are implemented with a defined summation  order and a tolerance-based test (not the bitwise one used for the threaded assignments).\nThe measure taxonomy — one L²/integration structure, three roles for \"measure\"; the recorded  foundation for the spectral-multiplier family later realized as Convolution (decision 20). The word measure recurs across the package  (reductions, interpolation, and convolution) not by coincidence but  because all three are facets of the same L²/integration structure on sections (§2). This entry  fixes the taxonomy so the three never get conflated and so the multiplier family starts from a  recorded design. The math anchor (Grafakos, Classical Fourier Analysis,  3rd ed., Graduate Texts in Mathematics 249, Springer, 2014, Thm 2.5.10): on a uniform  periodic box every homogeneous (translation-invariant) linear operator is a Fourier multiplier — a  coefficientwise m(k) — and the bounded ones are exactly those with symbol m(k) in L^infty, where  lVert TrVert_L^2 to L^2 = lVert mrVert_infty. So \"apply m(k)\" is not a convenience — it is the complete representation of  the translation commutant. The existing operators are all such multipliers: helmholtz_projection (§4),  the consumer's 2/3 de-alias mask, and the integrating factor exp(-nu k^2 Delta t) are the bounded ones  (m in L^infty); gradient/curl/laplacian are the unbounded multipliers (symbols mathrmia, -k^2 notin L^infty),  diagonalized identically but outside the norm identity above.\nThe roles — each as what it is; operation(s); the map it performs; status:\nbase-quadrature: Grid3D cell volume prod_i Delta x_i / PointSet counting; ops integral, inner, mean, std, lpnorm, radial_spectrum (the k-resolved inner); section → scalar / fiber / radial profile; done (decisions 18, 21).\nkernel — multiplier: translation-invariant convolution, symbol m(k) in L^infty; convolve / convolve! plus the builders spectral_cutoff / spectral_band / radial_kernel (any real symbol of k^2); grid → grid (a Fourier multiplier); done (Convolution, decisions 20, 21).\nkernel — transfer: off-grid partition-of-unity convolution (unit mass; signed or positive); interpolate (W) / anterpolate (W^top); grid ↔ point (base-change, not a multiplier); done (decisions 13/17).\nsample-space (FDF): spatially-filtered fine-grained Dirac density, over the velocity/composition sample space; no package operation; out (physics; invariant 12).\nInterpolation is the base-changing sibling of the multiplier, not a multiplier. The gather weights  {W(x_p, x_g)}_g are a discrete grid measure of total mass 1 (partition of unity), so  u(x_p) = int umathrmdmu_x_p is integration of the field against a unit-mass kernel measure centered at  the point — the same species as a homogeneous filter. Two differences make it its own operation  (§10, in Interpolation, not under the multiplier family): (i) its codomain is the unstructured  point base, which has no wavenumbers, so it is a base-change (grid↔point), not a Fourier  multiplier (it degenerates to one only when points coincide with nodes); (ii) the default  CatmullRom/Lagrange kernels have negative side-lobes — a signed unit-mass measure (higher  reproduction order, but overshoot), while only the linear m=0 / positive-B-spline end is a genuine  probability measure (monotone). Reproduction order = number of matched moments of the kernel  measure (0th moment =1 = partition of unity = constants reproduced). This signed/positive split is  the same dichotomy the LES research surfaces below — evidence the kernel measure is one concept.\nThe transfer adjoint is a row-1 (base-measure) statement, and that is why reductions came first.  Anterpolation W^top is the adjoint of W; \"adjoint w.r.t. which pairing?\" is answered by the base  measures. As shipped (decision 17) the adjointness sum_p (Wf)_p cdot q_p = sum_n f_n cdot (W^top q)_n (the unnormalized counting pairing) and conservation  sum_g (W^top q)_g = sum_p q_p hold in the counting/transpose pairing (the extensive facet of row 1;  conservation = partition of unity). The measure-weighted L² adjoint — W, W^top adjoint with both bases carrying their  normalized (total-mass-one) measures, tfrac1Nsum_n on the grid and tfrac1Msum_p on the  points, which is what inner computes — is the bridge from row 1 to the transfer kernel, and is  now done: both measures being uniform, it is (NM) W^top (a scalar rescaling, no new  operation). The NM is exactly the ratio of those two normalizations; pairing an unnormalized  cell-volume grid measure against counting points would instead give (NOmega) W^top,  verified via inner as M textttinner(Wf q) = N textttinner(f W^top q) (test/interpolation_transfer.jl). That the  reductions had to precede it is recorded here.\nThe multiplier family (designed here; realized as Convolution, decision 20) carries three research-grounded guardrails.  The deep-research findings on LES filtering do not change the design — they justify it and bound  it:\nThe symbol is data, never parameters — this is how the family stays inside invariant 12 (no time, no physics). The applicator multiplies a field-in-spectral by a precomputed k-array; nu, Delta t, filter width, and positivity all live in how the consumer builds that array from the FieldSpectralMultipliers wavenumbers (§4). The viscous factor exp(-nu k^2 Delta t) is literally the heat-kernel probability measure, yet the package never sees nu or Delta t.\nDo not restrict to positive (\"measure\") symbols. L^infty-symbol is the right generality; convolution-with-a-finite-measure is a strict subclass whose symbol must be continuous, and the 2/3 mask and sharp spectral cutoff have discontinuous symbols (kernels not in L^1) — so they are not such convolutions yet are valid multipliers we rely on. Positivity of the kernel ⟺ positive-definite symbol (Bochner) ⟹ probability measure ⟹ realizable SGS stress (Vreman, Geurts & Kuerten 1994, realizability iff positive filter) ⟹ bona-fide filtered PDF (Colucci et al. 1998, the FDF); all of that is consumer semantics. No PositiveFilter type, no realizability checks. (Interpolation already ships signed kernels by default — consistent.)\nHomogeneous-only scope. Variable-width filters are not multipliers (commutation error; Ghosal & Moin 1995, Vasilyev, Lund & Moin 1998) — and the uniform torus admits only homogeneous operators anyway, so this is a clarifying boundary, not a gap. Projections are the idempotent multipliers (the sharp cutoff; helmholtz_projection is the matrix-valued one we already ship); smoothings are the non-idempotent ones — same family, no special case.\nOpen at the time (untouched by the research; all resolved in decision 20 — the Convolution  module, the convolve op, the spectral_cutoff builder): a name distinct from the existing  FieldSpectralMultipliers data (e.g. a FourierSymbol operator or a generic apply_multiplier!);  placement (extend DifferentialOperators, which owns the fixed diagonal symbols, vs. a new module);  and how many neutral builders to ship (bare applicator vs. a spectral_cutoff helper — never a  physics-named gaussian_les_filter). v1 is scalar symbols; matrix-valued symbols are deferred  (helmholtz_projection covers the only case currently needed). The Germano differential filter  1(1 + Delta^2 k^2) is architecturally inert — whatever its symbol, it is one more scalar multiplier the  consumer supplies.\nConvolution — the kernel-multiplier family realized (§2; the new Convolution module).  Implements the multiplier designed in decision 19 as periodic (circular) convolution of a field  with a kernel, in spectral representation: by the convolution theorem on the torus, f ⋆ g is the  coefficientwise spectral product hat f cdot hat g. The kernel g is supplied by its transfer function  hat g as a ScalarFieldSpectral (decision 19's open \"kernel as a field\" call, chosen for  elegance); a scalar kernel broadcasts over the fiber, so one method serves scalar/vector/tensor  fields, and because a kernel is itself a scalar field in spectral representation, convolve(g₁,  g₂) composes two kernels for free (the R = 0 case). (Superseded by decision 60: a kernel is now  its own type, so a ScalarFieldSpectral is wrapped in a StoredKernel to declare that role and  composition is unambiguous by type rather than by argument position.) convolve / convolve! mirror laplacian  / laplacian!: coefficientwise ⇒ alias-safe (no _guard_noalias!; destination may alias the  field), @_assign!-routed (threads on CPU), with the five-argument α/β accumulate form  (invariant 10). Spectral representation only — there is no physical-space convolve (the  differential-operator precedent).\nThis completes invariant 1 rather than weakening it. The coefficientwise spectral product is the  operation invariant 1 names but bans as * (because * would read as the spectral pointwise  product, which it is not). It is offered here under its true name — convolve, never * — and is  linear in the field for a fixed kernel; the spectral pointwise product, division, and norms remain  absent. The convolve / convolve! / spectral_cutoff family are separate generics, so the FieldAlgebra  198-method and point-value 118-method parity baselines are unchanged.\nOne neutral builder ships: spectral_cutoff(multipliers, k) — the isotropic sharp  low-pass (idempotent projection), the basis of de-aliasing / spectral truncation; physics (an LES  filter, an integrating factor exp(-nu k^2 Delta t)) stays a set of coefficients the consumer builds  from the wavenumbers, wrapped as a StoredKernel since decision 60 (invariant 12). Naming follows the §2 convention (the verb convolve for a  generic transformation; the module noun Convolution, parallel to Interpolation / interpolate).  Tested CPU (test/convolution.jl, Float64/Float32 — the translation kernel  exp(-mathrmi k_x m Delta x) Leftrightarrow circshift is the independent physical oracle, plus identity/scaling/  cutoff-idempotence/composition/α-β/alias) and GPU (ROCm device-vs-CPU parity in test/gpu/suite.jl);  benchmarked (the convolution class). The L²-weighted and matrix-valued (anisotropic) kernels  remain future.\nShell-space spectral primitives — the radial coordinate, the band-pass, and the shell-resolved  inner (extending Convolution + Reductions). Three neutral primitives surfaced by the  examples/HIST_* demos, each the code image of an established object and each slotting into an  existing family — no new module, no parity-baseline change (separate generics), physics names  stay consumer-side (invariant 12). They close the largest hand-rolled pieces of a real  pseudospectral consumer (the energy spectrum, the forcing band, the viscous integrating factor):\nsquared_wavenumber_field(reference, multipliers) (Convolution; REMOVED by decision 60 — radial_kernel(identity, …) is the same symbol as a closure) — the radial coordinate k^2 of spectral space as a ScalarFieldSpectral (real, stored complex, like a cutoff mask). Decision 19 fixed that \"the symbol is data\": the consumer builds exp(-nu k^2 Delta t), a smooth envelope, or a Germano filter 1(1 + Delta^2 k^2) by broadcasting over its field_data, then applies it with convolve. The package hands over the coordinate; nu, Delta t, the width stay consumer-side. It wraps the existing _squared_wavenumber_broadcast (the Laplacian's k^2, true value at Nyquist — invariant 5).\nspectral_band(multipliers, wavenumber_low, wavenumber_high) (Convolution) — the isotropic annular band-pass, the generalization of spectral_cutoff (now its wavenumber_low = 0 special case, refactored to delegate). Idempotent ⇒ a projection; it selects a wavenumber shell — a de-alias band, a forcing band, a filter ring. With it the demos' low-shell forcing becomes pure package ops: band energy = inner(u_band, u_band) with u_band = convolve(u, band), then a rescaling convolve — no scalar indexing, and the inner(f, f) form is allocation-free (its fused-abs2 fast path).\nradial_spectrum(f, multipliers) (Reductions; introduced as shell_spectrum, renamed in decision 22) — the radial (shell-binned) power spectrum: inner decomposed into isotropic k-shells. Returns (shells, spectrum) with the exact refinement sum(spectrum) == inner(f, f) (same rfft half-spectrum Parseval weighting, now resolved per mode). This is the genuinely new reduction — spectral-representation only, like the Parseval inner — and the architectural framing is precisely \"the energy spectrum is the radially-resolved inner\" (decision 19's L²/integration structure, refined by the spectral radial coordinate). The consumer's energy spectrum over the shell wavenumber kappa (= k) is E(kappa) = tfrac12 textttspectrum; the neutral noun keeps physics out (invariant 12). Shells run to the corner wavenumber so the refinement is exact; on a de-aliased field the high-k shells are empty.\nDevice-clean by construction. radial_kernel / spectral_band are broadcast kernel  builders (the spectral_cutoff pattern); radial_spectrum bins via a single scatter-add pass — one  portable atomic-histogram kernel over the modes (no scalar indexing), an occasional diagnostic.  (Originally a sequence of masked reductions, one per shell, with the histogram scatter deferred as  \"unneeded\"; decision 25 lifted the deferral — the per-shell masks allocated O(N) full-grid  temporaries per call (N the points per axis), which on the GPU the garbage collector cannot reclaim  promptly, so a single 256^3 diagnostic wedged a 12 GB card.) Tested CPU (test/convolution.jl band/coordinate vs the k^2 oracle + cutoff equivalence;  test/reductions.jl sum(radial_spectrum) == inner, the single-mode shell placement) and GPU (ROCm  device-vs-CPU parity in test/gpu/suite.jl); benchmarked (the reductions / convolution classes).  The examples/HIST_CPU.jl + examples/gpu/rocm/HIST_ROCm.jl demos are refactored onto them, making  the GPU forcing + spectrum fully on-device. Matrix-valued (anisotropic) shells remain future; the  co-spectrum radial_spectrum(f, g) is now done (decision 22, the two-field cross-power). Renamed shell_spectrum → radial_spectrum in  decision 22 (done); the physical-space dual radial_correlation is decision-22 Phase 1, also done.\nRadial statistics — the spectrum↔correlation duality (Wiener–Khinchin), and the FFT route to  point pair-statistics (RDF). [Phase 1 (radial_correlation), Phase 2 (assignment_window + the  pair-correlation recipe), and the two-field co-spectra (radial_spectrum(f, g) / radial_correlation(f, g)) all DONE.]\nThe object. A field's two-point correlation function and its power spectrum are one object  in two representations, linked by the Wiener–Khinchin theorem: on the periodic grid the circular  autocorrelation C(r) = langle f(x) cdot f(x+r)rangle equals mathcal F^-1(hat f^2)N exactly — the inverse  transform of the power spectrum, normalized by the grid-point count N (the cross-correlation theorem).  Radially averaging gives the isotropic radial statistics, a Fourier-dual pair — average hat f^2  over k-shells (the spectrum), or mathcal F^-1(hat f^2) over r-shells (the correlation). This is  invariant 2 — physical and spectral are representations of one object — raised to second-order  statistics: the spectrum and the correlation function are not two diagnostics but one, viewed in  the two representations. The radial_spectrum reduction (decision 21, introduced as shell_spectrum) is the  spectral half; this entry designs the physical half and the point application, and fixes the  terminology so the family is coherent.\nTerminology (decided; the rename is done). shell_spectrum is renamed radial_spectrum,  and the physical dual radial_correlation is added (Phase 1, done). \"Radial\" is the  isotropic-average qualifier common to both spaces — and to the radial distribution function of point  statistics; \"shell\" named the binning implementation and does not pair with the physical-space  correlation. The two are the radial reductions: a Fourier-dual pair in Reductions, each mapping  a field to a 1-D radial profile, named by the quantity's noun per §2 (spectrum / correlation)  under the radial qualifier. The radial_spectrum math is unchanged (sum == inner). Physics names  stay consumer-side: the energy spectrum E(kappa) = tfrac12 textttradial_spectrum, the integral/Taylor scales from  radial_correlation, the RDF g(r) from radial_correlation of a density field. (The rename was  safe — the API is not frozen and shell_spectrum had shipped the same day with no external users.)\nThe physical dual — radial_correlation (done). C(r) = radially-averaged langle f(x) cdot f(x+r)rangle  (full fiber contraction — the trace of the correlation tensor, mirroring inner) = mathcal F^-1(hat f^2)N  binned over minimum-image r shells. The zero-separation value is C(0) = langle f^2rangle = textttinner(f f) (the  second moment — the variance only when f is mean-free); and for a zero-mean field C(r) to 0  at large r. The open design point was resolved  as the user chose: radial_correlation(f, plans) takes the field in spectral representation plus  its transform plans (symmetric with how the differential operators take multipliers) — Reductions  imports to_physical from Fields to do the one inverse rfft, then bins with the same scatter-add  machinery as radial_spectrum (the single-pass shell histogram; decision 25), in r instead of k. Verified against an independent  binned-circshift autocorrelation (CPU test/reductions.jl, GPU test/gpu/suite.jl) and C(0) = inner.\nThe two-field co-spectra — radial_spectrum(f, g) / radial_correlation(f, g) (done). The mixed  second-order statistics of two compatible, equal-order fields, refining the mixed inner(f, g) exactly as  the single-field forms refine inner(f, f). The co-spectrum bins the Parseval-weighted cross-power  density sum_c mathrmRe(hat f_c overlinehat g_c) (c indexing the fiber components) over k-shells (sum_kappa P_fg = textttinner(f g));  the cross-correlation C_fg(r) = langle f(x)cdot g(x+r)rangle = mathcal F^-1(overlinehat f hat g)N  binned over r-shells (C_fg(0) = textttinner(f g)). Two properties fall out of the math and are  pinned by tests: each is symmetric in f g — mathrmRe is conjugation-symmetric for the spectrum,  and the pm r folding of the min-image shells symmetrizes the correlation (C_fg(-r) = C_gf(r), so the  radial average is tfrac12(C_fg+C_gf)) — and the single-field forms are exactly the g = f case  (mathrmRe(hat f overlinehat f) = hat f^2; asserted bit-identical). Unlike the power spectrum the  co-spectrum may be negative in a shell (anticorrelated modes — the canonical use is a momentum/scalar flux  co-spectrum, where sign is physical). Implementation reuses the single-field code: the binning helpers  (_radial_spectrum_bin, _correlation_field_from + _radial_average) are shared, the two-field methods only swap  the per-mode density (hat f^2 to mathrmRe(hat f overlinehat g)) / the per-component spectrum  (hat f^2 to overlinehat f hat g, Hermitian for real f g ⇒ the c2r reconstruction is exactly real).  (The helper set has since been consolidated: the staging fused into the binning kernels themselves and  _correlation_field_from folded into the correlation methods — decision 34; the sharing structure is unchanged.)  These are new methods on the existing Reductions generics — no export change, the 198/118 parity baselines  are unchanged. Verified CPU (test/reductions.jl: analytical oracles — an orthogonal pair cos x perp sin x  giving zero, and cos x pm cos 2x giving pmtfrac12 in adjacent shells that cancel to textttinner = 0;  the g = f consistency; the f leftrightarrow g symmetry; and an independent binned-circshift cross-correlation  on a random pair) + GPU (ROCm device-vs-CPU parity, test/gpu/suite.jl); benchmarked (reductions class).\nPoints → RDF (Phase 2, done). The pair correlation g(r) of M points is computable  in O(N log N + M) (vs O(M^2) direct counting) via the FFT structure-factor estimator — exactly  the implemented recipe: anterpolate the points to a grid density (positive/linear assignment), form the  overdensity delta = rhobarrho - 1, FFT, deconvolve hatdelta by the assignment window  hat W, then radial_correlation of the deconvolved field gives xi(r) = langledelta(x)delta(x+r)rangle  and g(r) = 1 + xi(r) (the i=j self term is the shot noise, isolated in the r=0 shell, so  g at r0 is clean). With hatrho(k) = sum_i e^-mathrmi k cdot x_i,  S(k) = hatrho^2M = 1 + bar n hat h(k) (Ornstein–Zernike) and g = 1 + h. We already owned  anterpolate + the transforms + both radial reductions; the one new neutral primitive is the  assignment-window deconvolution, the rest is the consumer recipe (invariant 12). Three corrections, all  handled:\nAssignment-window deconvolution (assignment_window, Convolution). Scattering with kernel W gives hatrho_g = hat W(k) hatrho, so the measured spectrum is suppressed by hat W(k)^2 — divide it out (a convolve by 1hat W). Shipped for the linear (CIC) positive assignment: hat W(k) = prod_a operatornamesinc^2(k_aDelta x_a2), no in-band zeros. (Higher positive B-splines / the signed-kernel closed forms remain future.)\nShot noise. The i=j self term is a constant +1 floor in S(k) (a delta(r) at the origin): in the binned g(r) it lands entirely in the r=0 shell, so evaluating g at r0 is the subtraction (no extra step). Consumer-side by convention.\nAliasing / resolution. The grid folds high-k at Nyquist — trust k lesssim tfrac23 k_mathrmNyq, and g(r) below a few grid cells is unreliable regardless of corrections. FFT-RDF resolves intermediate / large-scale clustering (e.g. inertial-range preferential concentration), not a sharp contact peak (interlacing — Sefusatti et al. 2016 — is a future refinement). This matches the intended consumer use; it is a documented boundary, not a defect.\nVerified (test/rdf.jl): assignment_window against its prod operatornamesinc^2 formula  (even/odd grids, both element types), and the full recipe against an independent direct O(M^2)  physical-space pair count on a clustered point set — the recipe recovers the clustering peak (within  ~10% of direct counting) and the g to 1 baseline (the normalization).\nDecision-19 tie-in. A density estimate wants the positive (probability-measure) end of the  kernel taxonomy (the linear m = 0 / a B-spline), not the signed default CatmullRom, so g(r) ge 0  and hat W(k) has no in-band zeros (no division blow-up in the deconvolution). Decision 19's recorded  signed/positive split becomes a concrete, motivated choice here.\nInvariant 12. radial_spectrum / radial_correlation and assignment_window are neutral  (harmonic analysis / spatial statistics). The names RDF, structure factor, pair correlation, and the  shot-noise / normalization conventions (which encode \"what a particle is\") stay consumer-side — the  package ships the primitives and a documented recipe (test/rdf.jl, validated against direct pair  counting), not a radial_distribution_function function: that scoping held.\nPhasing — all done. Phase 1: radial_correlation(f, plans) (the field duality; neutral, no new  math), Delta r = the finest grid spacing, minimum-image r binning, C(0) = textttinner  exact. Phase 2: assignment_window (the linear/CIC prod operatornamesinc^2 deconvolution  window, Convolution) + the documented point-RDF recipe, validated against direct O(M^2) counting.  Co-spectra (done): the two-field radial_spectrum(f, g) / radial_correlation(f, g) (the  cross-power / cross-correlation pair — momentum/scalar fluxes; new methods on the existing generics that  reuse the single-field binning, refining the mixed inner(f, g); symmetric, may be negative — see above).  Future (deferred until a consumer makes them concrete): higher positive-B-spline / signed-kernel  window transforms and interlacing (sharper small-r g); and — the \"meaningful fibers over the  radial coordinate\" — the anisotropic / tensor-valued radial spectra E_ij(kappa) and the  Craya–Herring longitudinal/transverse split, which only earn their structure under anisotropy  (e.g. shear flow), per the §2 rule against speculative structure.\nSpectral-domain transfer — DirectDFT (exact) and the native NUFFT (fast). [Both DONE.]\nThe object. The transfer layer (§10) had one scheme family — the physical-domain local splines.  This adds the spectral-domain transfer: evaluation/deposition between Fourier coefficients and  scattered points, the exact off-grid form of the field. Both families are unified under a new  supertype AbstractTransferScheme, sharing the interpolate/anterpolate verbs and the adjoint  contract (the counting-transpose pairing, which the NM rescaling above turns into the exact  L² adjoint); a scheme is a choice of (kernel, representation). The spectral schemes dispatch on a  field in spectral representation (invariant 2: hand W whichever representation it acts on — a  spectral solver hands hat f for free); anterpolate returns a section, here in spectral  representation (the Fourier coefficients of sum_p q_pdelta_x_p).\nDirectDFT (done). The literal O(NM) sum (N the grid-point count, o the grid origin):  type-2 f(x_p) = tfrac1Nsum_textstored k  alpha(k) mathrmRe(hat f(k) e^mathrmi kcdot(x_p-o)) (the rfft half-spectrum reconstruction, with  the Parseval weight alpha of decision 18 — 1 on the k_x=0/even-Nyquist planes, 2 else),  type-1 (W^top q)(k) = sum_p q_p e^-mathrmi kcdot(x_p-o). It is the definition of the  spectral-domain pair, so it is at once a transparent exact baseline, the divergence-free  reference (it reproduces the band-limited field exactly — verified sim 10^-16 off-grid —  where the splines break nablacdot u=0), and the oracle that will validate NUFFT. It  matches to_physical at the grid nodes and to_spectral from node samples (both exact to round-off),  and satisfies M langle What f qrangle_mathrmpts = N langle hat f W^top qrangle_mathrmgrid (the  measure factor, as for the splines — decision 17). Two simple KernelAbstractions kernels (type-2  over points, type-1 over modes — no atomics), the per-axis wavenumbers + alpha built host→device  like the multipliers; no FFT, window, or oversampling. Stateless DirectDFT(); CPU + GPU-portable.  Standing principle (beyond this pair): when a fast or approximate method is added, its exact  reference ships as a first-class sibling scheme, built first — at once the definition a user can  reach for at small sizes and the oracle the fast form is validated against — never a test/-only helper.\nNative NUFFT (decided: own it, no dependency — done). The fast O(Nlog N + M) windowed  gridding form of DirectDFT: spread → FFT → deconvolve, all reusing existing machinery — the  transfer kernels (spread/gather with an exponential-of-semicircle window in place of the spline  coefficients), a FieldTransformPlans on an oversampled (sigma=2) grid, and a 1hatpsi  deconvolution ScalarFieldSpectral — psi the smooth compact spreading window, hatpsi its  Fourier transform — built like assignment_window (which is the hatpsi of the linear/CIC  window — so NUFFT generalizes the decision-22 RDF deconvolution). The one new ingredient is that  window psi (exp-of-semicircle, GPU-trivial) and its transform hatpsi  (host quadrature, the z=sintheta substitution regularizing the endpoint); the stencil width  and the shape parameter beta (the ES-kernel constant of the NUFFT literature — unrelated to  the package's alpha/beta accumulation weights) follow the Barnett–Magland–af Klinteberg  ES rule for the tolerance.  Verified against DirectDFT (and to_physical) to the requested tolerance varepsilon  (the default requests tol = 1e-10; the exponential-of-semicircle window over-delivers, sim 10^-12 measured there) for scalar and  vector fields, with the NUFFT pair an exact adjoint (sim 10^-15).\nThe Nyquist edge. The one subtlety: the rfft half-spectrum Nyquist mode of an even axis cannot be  split losslessly into the finer oversampled grid (zero-padding a real spectrum). It is projected  out in the deconvolution, so NUFFT's type-1/type-2 remain exact adjoints on the resolved  band — exactly the band a spectral solver uses (de-aliasing, or any natural spectral  decay, zeros the Nyquist), where the transfer is exact to varepsilon. DirectDFT (no  oversampling) carries the Nyquist exactly; the two agree on the sub-Nyquist band.\nNonuniformFFTs.jl was evaluated (it fits — real-data rfft, AMDGPU/KA, batched) but not adopted:  owning it keeps the package self-contained and makes the spline transfer and the NUFFT share one  spread machinery, with DirectDFT and a direct O(NM) DFT as independent ground truth. Tested  CPU (test/transfer_spectral.jl) + GPU parity; method parity 198/118 unchanged (the transfer verbs  are not in those surfaces).\nAllocation-free in-place spectral operators (the GPU hot-loop discipline). A solver runs the  operators thousands of times in its time loop. On the GPU, any scratch an operator allocates  internally is invisible to Julia's garbage collector — the collector triggers on host memory  pressure, but the scratch lives in device memory — so the device memory pool grows every step  until it fills VRAM, and the run then wedges (the allocator spins, GPU idle) or hipMalloc aborts.  The discipline this forces: the in-place  operators must allocate nothing, writing only  into their preallocated destination. Two changes brought the spectral operators into line. (a)  _squared_wavenumber_broadcast — the radial coordinate k^2 = k_1^2 + k_2^2 + k_3^2 — now  returns a lazy Broadcasted over the three per-axis squared-wavenumber vectors instead of a  materialized O(N^3) array (N the points per axis), so the Laplacian (-k^2hat f),  inverse Laplacian (-hat fk^2) and Helmholtz projection  (hat f_i - k_i (k_jhat f_j)k^2) fuse it into their own broadcasts without ever allocating  it. (b) The laplacian!, inverse_laplacian! and helmholtz_projection! kernels were rewritten  so their former per-call temporaries (the longitudinal coefficient k_jhat f_jk^2, the safe  1k^2 that is 1 at the zero mode) are fused into the component assignments rather than  materialized; the projection recomputes the longitudinal part inline for each component, which is  safe because the kernel always receives a destination distinct from the source — the aliased  helmholtz_projection!(x, x) allocates its guard temporary upstream (§7).  (The projection has since moved on from both details: its one-pass kernel computes the longitudinal  part once per mode and serves the aliased call directly, guard retired — decision 32.)  Net: each of the three now allocates sim 0 bytes in place (measured), with identical arithmetic  and results (the full suite is unchanged) — so a preallocated, in-place pseudospectral step allocates  nothing per step and runs on a 12 GB consumer GPU with no manual GC.gc() / reclaim, at flat  VRAM (the examples/ HIST solvers demonstrate this — 192^3 Float32 at sim34 ms/step on  a since-retired card). Guarded by test/allocations.jl (every in-place spectral operator, transform,  convolve!, and fiber product/shuffle, both element types). The fiber norm, lpnorm and dev!  deliberately materialize one field (a pointwise magnitude, or the trace they subtract) and are the  documented exceptions — a consumer's CFL speed uses maximum(abs, …) rather than lpnorm.\nAllocation-bounded radial diagnostics — the shell binning as a single scatter-add (the GPU-GC  corollary of decision 24). Decision 24 made the time loop allocation-free; this brings the  diagnostics into the same discipline, for the same reason. The radial statistics — radial_spectrum  (the k-shell power spectrum) and radial_correlation (the r-shell two-point correlation) —  binned by looping over the shells and, for each shell s, reducing a masked broadcast (sum of the  per-mode value over the modes whose rounded shell index equals s). The number of shells scales with  the grid — lceil sqrt3 N2rceil (N the points per axis), approx 220 at N = 256 —  and each iteration allocates a full-grid temporary: the Boolean shell mask, and (because on the GPU  stack a mapreduce over a Broadcasted materializes its argument rather than fusing) the masked  product too. That is O(N) full-grid temporaries per single diagnostic call. On the GPU these are  dead immediately but not reclaimed: Julia's garbage collector triggers on host memory pressure, and a  dead device array is a tiny host wrapper owning a large device buffer (the mechanism of decision 24),  so the pool climbs. One 256^3 correlation snapshot churned several GB; with the live working set  already sim6 GB, the first snapshot wedged a 12 GB card (the allocator spinning, GPU idle).\nThe fix replaces the per-shell loop with a single scatter-add pass — one portable  KernelAbstractions + Atomix histogram kernel (_shell_bin_kernel!, driven by _bin_by_shell) that  sweeps the array once and, per element, atomically adds the value (and a unit population count) into the  accumulator of its shell, keyed by a precomputed integer shell index. (Decision 34 later fused even  that staging away: the shell index and the binned quantity are now derived per element inside the  kernels, and the precomputed-index pair retired.) Per-call full-grid temporaries  drop from O(N) to O(1) (the shell-index array, the binned quantity, and the tiny  length-(textnumber of shells) accumulators): the spectrum keeps the value sum (normalized by  N^2), the correlation divides it by the per-shell population. This is the same atomic scatter the  point→grid anterpolate already uses (§10),  now depositing mode/point energies into radial shells instead of point quantities onto the grid; it  lifts the \"histogram scatter deferred as unneeded\" sub-decision of decision 21. The trade-off —  contention on the small accumulator set — is acceptable for an occasional diagnostic (every  snap_stride steps in a solver), and the old form was itself O(mathrmshellstimes N^3) work; a  privatized/shared-memory histogram is a possible future optimization, unneeded now.\nCorrectness is unchanged (the scatter sums the same per-shell quantities as the masked reductions): CPU  test/reductions.jl (the analytic langlesin^2rangle and independent binned-circshift oracles,  both element types) and GPU parity test/gpu/suite.jl (allowscalar(false) — the scatter is  device-clean, no scalar indexing) both hold. End to end, the 256^3 Float32 forced-HIT example  on a 12 GB card (approx 87 ms/step) now runs to completion at a flat sim6 GB  (approx 8 GB peak) through its radial_correlation snapshots, where the per-shell form wedged at  the first one.\nAllocation-free in-place operators are a standing, exhaustively-enforced contract (generalizing 24  and 25). Decisions 24 (the spectral operators) and 25 (the radial binning) each fixed one family  that allocated a per-call temporary the GPU could not afford. This decision promotes the property from  a sequence of point fixes to a package-wide invariant: every in-place ! operator allocates no  field-sized scratch when called on preallocated buffers with a destination distinct from its sources  — kernels fuse their temporaries into the assignment, never materialize them. The motivation is the one  from decision 24: a solver applies these operators thousands of times per step, and on the GPU a  per-call temporary escapes Julia's garbage collector (which triggers on host memory pressure, while  the temporary is a device buffer behind a tiny host wrapper), so the device pool grows every step  until the run wedges or hipMalloc aborts.\nThe enforcement is an exhaustive test, test/allocations.jl. It warms one in-place call per signature  and asserts a second @allocated call stays below a small fraction of one field — across both element  types, both representations, tensor orders R = 0 1 2, the field/constant/reversed and α/β argument  branches, the point-value algebra, and the spline transfer (the whole in-place surface, not a sample).  It runs on the CPU on purpose: there a materialized field is a host Array and @allocated counts  its full bytes, so a stray temporary trips the threshold; on the GPU the same similar/broadcast  allocates a device buffer whose host wrapper is only a few hundred bytes, so GPU @allocated would  report sim 0 and miss the leak. The operator code is generic over the array type, so a  field-sized materialization — a similar or a broadcast landing in a temporary — is  backend-symmetric: seeing it on the CPU is seeing it on the GPU, and the CPU is simply where it is  visible. That symmetry covers the materialization class, not every byte a device run allocates: a  KernelAbstractions launch also takes a small, size-independent host-side bookkeeping allocation  (finding (j), ~1.5–7 KB, thread-count-dependent) with no CPU-serial counterpart, which the audit's  fraction-of-a-field threshold absorbs rather than measures — sound, because the leak this contract  exists to stop scales with the grid and bookkeeping does not.  The contract binds new code: a  new in-place operator (or any function documented allocation-free) must be added to the audit  (invariant 13; the §4 coverage contract in docs/TESTING.md).\nDocumented exceptions — bounded, not zero. A few operations materialize one field by design and are  excluded from the zero assertion, getting a bounded guard instead (catching a regression to O(N) —  or O(mathrmshells) — extra while allowing the legitimate one-field temporary): the fiber  norm!/lpnorm (a pointwise magnitude) and  the radial diagnostics (O(1)-bounded); dev! left this list when decision 39 fused its trace  into a register, and std/var when decision 49 rewrote them as two fused passes — measured at 16  bytes rather than a field. The spectral inner is the subtle case (N  the points per axis): inner(f, f) — an energy or l2norm — is allocation-free, since  overlinehat f hat f = hat f^2 is real and the whole Parseval sum, correction planes  included, is a fused sum(abs2, …); but the mixed inner(f, g) of two distinct fields allocates an  O(N^2) kₓ-plane correction, because a dot over the strided plane view (which would fuse, no  temporary) has no method on the AMD path (rocBLAS dotc takes no strided argument — verified on the  device), and one route serving every backend is preferred to a per-vendor split.  Consumers form a hot-loop energy as inner(f, f) or the physical inner (a contiguous dot); the  examples/ forcing computes its band energy as inner(u_band, u_band) for exactly this reason. The  audit found the whole in-place surface already clean; the only allocation it surfaced was the mixed  spectral inner, resolved by the inner(f, f) fast path above.\nConsumer-driven fused and missing field-math operations — combine!, det, symmetric_gradient,  weighted_inner (done); a quadratic-form reduction deferred. The sibling consumer  GBGHomogeneousTurbulence (a thin  physics solver built on this field layer) asked the package to absorb  the field-math it hand-rolls hottest, on the principle that a field library should serve its consumer the  way numpy/BLAS serve scientific Python — not only composable primitives, but fused kernels for the  recurring composites. The reason is concrete: Julia fuses within a single broadcast or mapreduce but  not across function-call boundaries, so a chain like gradient! → sym! → inner is three separate  passes that each materialize a tensor-sized intermediate — and on a memory-bound GPU consumer that  intermediate's peak footprint, not the bandwidth, is the binding constraint. The rule for what to absorb:  fuse away the intermediate the consumer never needs, or supply a genuinely missing primitive — never  add an operation \"for completeness\" (invariant 1). An adversarial triage kept four and deferred one; each  kept operation is general field math with no time, particles, or physics (invariant 12 — the physics  names stay consumer-side).\nThe four, by family.\ncombine!(dest, α, a, β, b) (FieldAlgebra) writes the real-weighted combination alpha a + beta b of two same-representation fields a b with real weights alpha beta, in one fused pass. It was a genuine gap: add! is unit-weight (a+b) and multiply! scales a single field, so the weighted combination (an axpby) had no form and the consumer reached into field storage with hand-rolled broadcasts at nine call sites. It completes the package's own alphabeta accumulation convention (already on mul!/convolve!); being linear in the fields it is defined in both representations, and being coefficientwise it is alias-safe with no guard (a or b may be dest). It is a new verb rather than a five-argument add!, to keep add!'s constant-variant dispatch clean. It stays field-only — the consumers are field updates, and the package does not add a point-value method for symmetry alone.\ndet/det! (FieldAlgebra) is the fiberwise determinant, the third principal invariant I_3 (the signed factor by which the tensor scales volumes as a linear map) — the sibling of the trace operatornametr (the first invariant I_1) the package already carried; the gap was the missing invariant, not a completeness fill. The second invariant I_2 stays absent, having no consumer — and a consumer that wants it can build it from the two that ship, as I_2 = tfrac12(I_1^2 - operatornametr(A^2)). Being cubic in the components it is nonlinear, so it is physical-space only (there is no spectral determinant), exactly like norm. It reuses the shared per-component formula builder in FiberSections, so it lands on both fields and point-values (mirroring operatornametr, which already lived on both) with the formula written once.\nsymmetric_gradient/! (DifferentialOperators) forms the symmetric part of a vector field's gradient, S_ij = tfrac12 (partial_x_j u_i + partial_x_i u_j) for a vector field u, directly — spectrally hat S_ij = tfracmathrmi2 (k_j hat u_i + k_i hat u_j) with the same Nyquist-correct derivative wavenumbers k as gradient — skipping the full nine-component gradient tensor nabla u that sym(gradient(u)) builds only to symmetrize. It is the fused symmetric peer of curl, which already extracts the antisymmetric part of nabla u (as a vector). The name is the charter-neutral mathematical one; the fluids name \"strain rate\" stays in the consumer, exactly as the package says curl where the consumer says \"vorticity\".\nweighted_inner(f, w) / weighted_inner(f, g, w) (Reductions) is the multiplier-weighted L² inner product of two fields f g in spectral representation against a real Fourier multiplier w(k), langle f w grangle = tfrac1N^2sum_textstored k alpha(k) w(k)sum_c mathrmRe(hat f_c  overlinehat g_c) (with alpha(k) the rfft half-spectrum Parseval weight — a fixed per-mode function, distinct from the scalar accumulation weight alpha of combine! above — N the grid-point count, and c the fiber index) — the field g filtered by w and paired with f in one fused pass with no filtered field materialized. It is the reduction-dual of convolve (apply a spectral symbol, then reduce) and generalizes inner (the w equiv 1 case); w = k^2 gives the H^1/Dirichlet energy langle f -Delta grangle. Spectral representation only, like the Parseval inner. The consumer formed in-band energy every step as convolve! into scratch then inner; the fused form removes that scratch field.\nThe deferral. A fifth request, a quadratic-form reduction langle v A vrangle =  textttmean(v_i A_ij v_j) (a vector field v contracted through a second-order tensor field  A), was declined for now: its own request flagged it \"for completeness of the fused-reduction  set\", its saving is a single vector intermediate at diagnostic cadence, and its consumer (the enstrophy  production langle omega_i S_ij omega_jrangle, the vorticity omega through the strain S)  is not yet live — so by the §2 rule against speculative structure it waits until that consumer  lands, when it is a small addition on the existing inner machinery.\nScope and verification. The public method set grows to 206 field methods (from the 202 the  earlier trace addition left, combine! adding 2 and det/det! 1 each) and 122 point-value  methods (from 120 — the same earlier trace addition that took the field surface 198 → 202 carried  tr/tr! onto point-values, 118 → 120 — det/det! 1 each); symmetric_gradient and  weighted_inner live in other modules and leave those baselines untouched. Each operation carries the  full definition-of-done: a CPU behaviour test against an independent oracle over both element types (and  both grids where they apply), GPU device-parity, the test/allocations.jl guard  (combine!/det!/symmetric_gradient! allocation-free; weighted_inner allocation-light — only a  small per-axis weight vector, far cheaper than the radial reductions  radial_spectrum/radial_correlation, which build an O(N) shell-index pass), and a benchmark.  That combine! stays field-only and the quadratic form waits both follow the same rule: the consumer,  not symmetry, decides where an operation lives.\nSpectral translation — translation_kernel, and the reality condition a complex kernel must meet.  The sibling consumer GBGHomogeneousTurbulence asked for a fifth kernel builder: the multiplier that  translates a field by a physical displacement h = (h_1 h_2 h_3) (a length  per axis, in the box's units), carrying a field f at position x to the displaced field  f(x - h). Its consumer is phase-shift de-aliasing, where the nonlinear product is evaluated a  second time on a half-cell-displaced grid and averaged with the unshifted evaluation — the memory-lean  alternative to padding the grid; it also serves shift-and-average filters and the relocation of a probe.\nWhy the package owns it. Translation is a coordinate-only operation on the field, with no physics  in it — the exact sibling of the derivative and cutoff multipliers the package already owns — and by the  shift theorem it is a convolution: with the Dirac delta displaced to h, whose transfer  function is the pure phase hat g(k) = e^-mathrmi  kcdot h at wavenumber k. So it needs  no new operation, only a new kernel (decision 20): convolve! already applies a scalar kernel to any  tensor order, with its accumulation weights, alias-safely. Only the builder was missing —  the package exposes no function-of-wavenumbers constructor, and the allocation helper similar_field is  internal, so a consumer cannot build the kernel without reaching into the package's internals.\nThe correctness trap — the real reason it belongs here. A kernel must leave a real field real. The  rfft half-spectrum stores only the modes whose first wavenumber component is non-negative; the rest are  implied by the reality of the field, hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k). Multiplying by a kernel  preserves that symmetry — and so returns a field rather than a complex impostor — exactly when the kernel  is itself Hermitian, hat g(-k) = overlinehat g(k), equivalently when its physical-space form  g is real. The four kernels shipped before this one are real and even in k, so they satisfy the  condition without anyone having to think about it. The translation phase does not, at one frequency per  axis: the Nyquist frequency of an even-length axis (with N_a points and spacing Delta x_a  along axis a), the mode that is its own conjugate partner and where the kernel is therefore forced to  be real. The two obvious candidates each fail, and they fail differently:\nThe true phase e^-mathrmi  kcdot h built from the signed wavenumbers is complex at Nyquist for a fractional displacement, so it returns coefficients that are no real field's — the inverse transform then silently keeps only their Hermitian part. (Whole-cell displacements are safe: the factor there is (-1)^m for a displacement of m cells, which is real.)\nThe Nyquist-zeroed derivative wavenumbers — the ones gradient uses, and the fix the request itself proposed — do keep the kernel real (the factor becomes 1), but the value is wrong: they leave the Nyquist mode unmoved, where a one-cell shift must flip its sign.\nThe correct multiplier follows from what the Nyquist mode is. It carries a single real amplitude  standing for both of the wavenumbers pmpiDelta x_a, so its band-limited form is the cosine  cos(pi x_aDelta x_a), and displacing that cosine and re-sampling it on the grid multiplies it by  the real factor cos(pi h_aDelta x_a) — equal to (-1)^m for a whole-cell displacement of  m cells, and to 0 for a half-cell one, a displaced Nyquist mode being genuinely not representable  on the same grid. Splitting the phase into its even and odd parts,  e^-mathrmi k_a h_a = cos(k_a h_a) - mathrmi sin(k_a h_a), states the rule in  one line: the even (cosine) part takes the true wavenumber, the odd (sine) part takes the derivative  wavenumber. Those are exactly the package's two multiplier families (invariant 5: squared wavenumbers  keep the true value at Nyquist, derivative wavenumbers are zeroed there), so the builder is a  three-factor broadcast and the Nyquist correction falls out of a convention already in place for the very  same reason — an odd function of k must vanish at Nyquist to keep a real field real, which is why the  derivative multiplier is zeroed there. The translation_kernel builder is the first operation to use both  families, and it confirms that split rather than excepting it.\nConsequences, documented on the operation. The kernel is a pure phase of unit modulus on every resolved  mode, so it moves the field without touching any amplitude; at Nyquist it is a real attenuation instead.  Translations therefore compose and invert exactly on the resolved band but not at Nyquist (displacing by  h and back scales that one mode by cos^2). None of this reaches a field already truncated  by a de-aliasing cutoff, which is the consumer's case.\nScope: the builder only. No fused translate! — a convolve! that builds its own kernel — although  the request offered one. The kernel depends on nothing but the displacement, so building it per call  would allocate a field-sized array on every invocation, exactly the allocation the package forbids in  place (decisions 24, 26). The family's build-once-apply-many pattern is already the right one: a  displaced evaluation builds the two kernels (for +h and -h) once and applies them with  convolve! at zero allocation.\nVerification. Two independent oracles pin the operator, and each rejects one of the wrong kernels: a  whole-cell displacement equals circshift on a random field, which carries content at every mode  including Nyquist; and a sub-cell displacement equals the exact DirectDFT evaluation of the field at  the displaced nodes x_n - h (decision 23). The random field matters — the band-limited fields the  older convolution tests use pass with any of the three candidate kernels, which is precisely how a  hand-rolled kernel gets away with being wrong. Reality is checked directly (transforming the displaced  field out and back is the identity, the inverse transform being the Hermitian projector, so a  non-Hermitian result would move), alongside the analytic displaced cosine, the Nyquist attenuation, and  unit modulus on the resolved band — over both element types, a mixed-parity grid, GPU device-parity, and  a benchmark. The method-set baselines (206 field / 122 point-value methods) are untouched: like  convolve itself, this is a new generic in Convolution.\nHDF5 field persistence — write_field / read_field, a weak-dependency extension. The sibling  consumer GBGStreamingStatistics ships write_histogram / read_histogram to persist its durable  summaries to HDF5; the request was the field analogue, so a solver can checkpoint a field for  restart, dump a full-resolution snapshot for later analysis, or hand a field to another tool as  easily as it dumps statistics. The demos already hand-rolled HDF5 for their plotted curves; this makes  the fields themselves first-class.\nPersistence, not a mathematical operation — so it does not touch the math API. Serializing a field is  I/O, not an operation on sections, so the rule that design changes be justified by established  mathematics (§2),  which governs the operation set, does not bar it — and the mechanism keeps it out of the way:  write_field / read_field are a weak-dependency package extension ([weakdeps] HDF5 +  [extensions], methods in ext/GBGPeriodicFieldsHDF5Ext.jl), so the HDF5 binary never enters the core  and a user who does not persist pays nothing. Bare function write_field end stubs and their docstrings  live in Fields, so ?write_field and the manual work without HDF5 loaded; the methods appear only when  it is. This mirrors the sibling exactly.\nThe line held (invariant 12 — no time). A field is a self-describing object — its numeric array plus  its Grid3D geometry, Fourier layout, element type, and tensor order — so it maps onto one HDF5  group: the array is the dataset data, and everything else is attributes (a type tag, a schema  version, the grid geometry, the fft_dims, the element type, the tensor order, the representation).  The primitive is one field at a time, and — the load-bearing choice — it takes an open File or  Group, never a path. So the consumer bundles a whole iteration snapshot (velocity, vorticity, …)  into one file under separate names and attaches its own time / iteration attributes: the package  serializes the fields, the consumer owns the bundling and every notion of time. There is no Snapshot  type and no clock in the field layer. A field group and a GBGStreamingStatistics histogram group share  the same self-describing-group convention, so a run's fields and its statistics can live in one file.\nBoth representations, host and device. A field in physical representation stores a real array; a field in spectral  representation stores its complex half-spectrum (HDF5 round-trips complex arrays). Device (GPU) arrays  are copied to the host on write via Array(…) — backend-agnostic, a no-op on the CPU — and the reload is  a host field the consumer moves to a device if wanted. On read, read_field dispatches on the stored  type tag and reconstructs through the public constructor: the element type follows the stored array (real or  complex), the tensor order follows its dimensionality, and the grid spacing is recomputed bit-for-bit, so  the reload is exact. The sugar parent[name] = field is setindex! for write_field, made strictly  more specific than HDF5's own array-writing setindex! so it never clashes.\nScope. Every field type (the six: scalar / vector / tensor × physical / spectral); no compression or  chunking options until a consumer needs them; no snapshot / time type (invariant 12).\nVerification. The round-trip test test/hdf5.jl covers every field type over both element types,  asserting the data (real and complex), the full Grid3D geometry, the element type, the tensor order,  and the layout are recovered bit-for-bit; it exercises the setindex! sugar, a snapshot bundle carrying consumer-owned  time / iteration attributes, the self-describing attributes read back as plain HDF5, and the error  paths (a dataset instead of a group, an absent name, a group with no type tag). The method-set baselines  (206 field / 122 point-value methods) are untouched — write_field / read_field are new generics, and  their methods exist only under the extension.\nMapped reductions — a leading function on five of the measure reductions. The consumer  GBGHomogeneousTurbulence audited its memory and found a recurring shape: to average a nonlinear  invariant of a tensor field — the determinant of a strain tensor, the magnitude of a velocity — it had  to build that invariant as a whole section first and then reduce it, paying one field of scratch  for a single number. Here a section is a field or a set of point-values, and its fiber value at a  base point is the scalar, vector, or 3 times 3 tensor sitting over that point. So five reductions  gained a form taking a leading function, applied to the fiber value at every base point:  mean(det, A) averages a determinant, maximum(norm, u) takes a supremum of magnitude, and the  mapped quantity is consumed as it is formed rather than stored.\nThe standard library sets the surface, including where it stops. The mapped spellings mirror  Statistics' mean(f, itr) and Base's sum/maximum/minimum/extrema with a leading function,  so mean, maximum, minimum, extrema, and the package's own integral (the extensive peer of  mean) take one. Statistics gives var and std no such method, so neither does this package:  inventing a spelling the standard library declines to have would be exactly the \"for completeness\"  addition invariant 1 exists to refuse. Being a nonlinear map of the fiber, the mapped forms are  physical-only, like lpnorm and std before them.\nIt reconciles an older deliberate absence rather than overturning it. The bare  maximum/minimum/extrema are scalar-section-only on purpose: the supremum of a vector or tensor  section is ambiguous between the components and the magnitude, and the package refuses to guess  (§12). The mapped form does not guess — the caller names the map, so  maximum(norm, u) is well posed where maximum(u) is not. The absence was of an ambiguous operation,  and naming the map is precisely the condition that removes the ambiguity, so the mapped forms carry no  ambiguity restriction. That is the restriction this entry lifts, and it is not the same as carrying  no ceiling at all: decision 56 later fixed the supported surface at R  3, and the  fiber-preserving and mapped reductions throw above it rather than return a wrong answer. They also give a fused peer to lpnorm(u, Inf), which computes the same  supremum of magnitude by materializing a magnitude section first.\nFiber-last layout dictates the implementation, and hides two allocation traps. Because components are  stored last (invariant 3), a base point's components sit a whole base apart, so a fiber cannot be  reinterpreted into a StaticArray in place; the fiber is instead gathered through a lazy Broadcasted  over the component arrays, which reduces in one pass on both backends. Two spellings that look  equivalent are not, and each silently allocates:\nthe multi-argument mapreduce(fiber_map, combine, components...) is fused by GPUArrays but falls back on the CPU to a route that materializes a full section-sized array;\nan anonymous (values...) -> fiber_map(V(values)) leaves the component count uninferrable, so the fiber cannot be built in registers and the reduction allocates per base point — measured at about four and a half times the tensor section's own size. A callable struct carrying the fiber type restores the inference.\nA third trap sits in the public signatures: Julia declines to specialize on an argument of Function  type that a method only forwards instead of calling, so each mapped form names its map's type as a  parameter. Without that, the map arrives boxed and every call allocates. All three are guarded by the  @allocated audit, which pins the whole family at zero bytes.\nThe reduction is split by backend, so the fused route wins on both. How a reduction consumes its  input is where the backends disagree, and a single formulation cannot serve both. Reducing a  contiguous array uses a vectorized blocked loop; reducing a lazy Broadcasted forgoes that loop, at  a measured 56times on the reduction alone — more than materializing an extra section costs in  traffic. A GPU has no such loop to forfeit: its reduction is thread-parallel and bandwidth-bound, so  index arithmetic hides behind memory latency and the section write and read-back that fusing skips  is pure profit. The implementation therefore reduces through a vectorized blocked loop over the  component arrays on a CPU and one fused pass over a lazy Broadcasted on a GPU. Measured with  the same map on both sides (mean(det, A) against mean(det(A)), N = 128, Float64), the fused  route then costs 5.1 ms against 13.0 ms on a CPU and 0.55 ms against 0.69 ms on a GPU — cheaper on  both, allocating nothing against one full section. The CPU loop blocks pairwise like Base's own  reduction, which also makes it the most accurate of the routes (relative error 12times10^-16  against the lazy form's 37times10^-15). The strided gather that fiber-last layout forces  costs only about 2times, and building the StaticArray per base point is free.\nA caveat that outranks the route, recorded because it misled this analysis twice. The fiber map's  own cost can outweigh the choice of reduction entirely. LinearAlgebra.norm applied to a fiber uses  the overflow-safe scaled 2-norm, measured at 43times the plain sqrtsum_i u_i^2 that the  fieldwise norm evaluates. Comparing maximum(norm, u) against lpnorm(u, Inf) therefore varies the  algorithm and the route at once, and reads as a fusion penalty that is not the fusion at all —  given the same formula, the fused form is 1.8 ms against 4.2 ms. Vary one thing at a time when  measuring this family. The integral docstring carries the practical form of both points for the  reader who never opens this file.\nThe Julia compat floor moved 1.11 → 1.12, on a measurement rather than a preference. The package  declared julia = \"1.11\", and CI ran that as an explicit compat-floor lane beside current stable. The  floor is now 1.12, so the matrix differs only in thread count (-t1 against -t4) and the local  pre-commit run is a single lane.\nWhat the measurement showed, which is the opposite of the obvious guess. On 1.11 the package runs  faster — every operation measured, on a 64^3 grid in Float64, in the threaded lane (so these absolute  times are not comparable with §4(l)'s single-thread column, and none of them needs to be — the  comparison here is one Julia version against another on one fixed configuration): mean(det, A) 0.241 ms against  0.251, the threaded assignment add! 0.296 against 0.338, radial_spectrum 1.743 against 2.288,  inner 0.012 against 0.017. What is slower is compilation, by a wide margin and concentrated in  exactly the generated and broadcast-heavy surfaces this package is built from: first-call cost for  add! (the _assign! primitive) is 45times higher on 1.11, and the KernelAbstractions binning  path 14times. That is why the two slowest testsets were threading.jl and the radial-diagnostic  allocation audit, and why a full Pkg.test() took 31 minutes on 1.11 against 5.4 on 1.12.\nWhy \"make it as fast\" was not on the table. The gap is Julia 1.11's compiler working on our  @eval-generated method families and fused broadcasts, not a pathology in the package — there is  nothing here to fix. Baking the workload into the precompile image with PrecompileTools would  relocate the cost to precompile time rather than remove it, for a standing maintenance surface. So the  choice was to pay 31 minutes per pre-commit check or to drop the lane; since the local run is  mandatory (CI is manual-only, TESTING.md), the tax fell on every commit.\nWhat it buys, and what it costs. Dropped with it: the per-version manifest dance (the gitignored  top-level Manifest.toml is Julia-version-specific, so running the second lane meant moving it aside  and restoring it — a footgun that bit twice while gathering the numbers above), and the wide lower  bound on the LoweredCodeUtils test pin, which existed only to admit the older JET that 1.11  resolved to. The cost is that the floor now tracks current stable closely, and that sibling packages  declaring julia = \"1.11\" — GBGHomogeneousTurbulence does — must be bumped in step, or they claim  support for a Julia their dependency no longer builds on.\nThe one-pass Helmholtz kernel, and a convolution fused into the projection and the divergence  (the operator ∘ kernel family). Two requests from the consumer package GBGHomogeneousTurbulence  (\"IT\") — items 1 and 6 of its 2026-07 memory and compute audits — triaged adversarially and landed  as one kernel rewrite, because they touch the same code.\nThe one-pass projection. helmholtz_projection! — the Leray/Helmholtz projection mathbb P,  which removes a vector field's curl-free part — used to run as three fused broadcasts, one per  output component, each recomputing the longitudinal factor k_j hat u_j  k^2 (the  wavenumber-parallel part of the field, k the wavenumber vector and hat u the field's  spectral coefficients) from all three source components: nine component reads per mode for three  writes, and an in-place call (destination aliasing the source) was detected and routed through a  guard temporary costing one full vector field per call — the single largest transient in IT's  measured budget, and the one op whose natural call is in place. It is now a single portable  KernelAbstractions kernel (the package's third KA site, after the transfer kernels and the shell  binning): per mode, the three source values are read into registers, the longitudinal factor is  computed once, and the three destinations are written. Three reads for three writes, and  alias-safe by construction — all of a mode's reads precede its writes, and modes are disjoint —  so the guard and its temporary are retired (§7) and the aliased call is  bitwise-identical to the non-aliased one. Two implementation notes worth recording: the sources are  deliberately not marked @Const, whose no-other-writes promise the aliased call would break;  and the KA launch keeps a small size-independent host-side bookkeeping allocation (measured  sim1.5–7 KB, thread-count-dependent), which the allocation audit's fraction-of-a-field  threshold absorbs — the field-sized temporary is what is gone. Measured on the 12700K, the rewrite  is 2.7–6.5× faster than the three-broadcast form (64^3–128^3 Float64, one and four  threads) — the memory fix paid compute, as predicted: the projection is bandwidth-bound, and nine  reads became three.\nThe kernel-composed forms. helmholtz_projection! and divergence! gain trailing  kernel[, α, β] arities: op!(dest, f, multipliers, kernel) and  op!(dest, f, multipliers, kernel, α, β), with the four-argument form delegating with one(T),  zero(T) (invariant 10) and allocating op(f, multipliers, kernel) wrappers. The mathematics is  the §4 multiplier picture closing under composition: a  coefficientwise kernel hat g (a Fourier symbol, as convolve! takes it) commutes through both  operators — scalar times matrix symbol for mathbb P, scalar times the contraction  mathrmi a cdot for the divergence — so \"operator then convolve\" is itself one Fourier  multiplier, and the fused form computes it in the operator's own pass. The consumer pattern it  serves is mask-de-aliasing: every de-aliased right-hand side in a pseudospectral solver is the  operator followed immediately by convolve! with the de-alias kernel, so fusing saves that pass's  full read and write of the destination (two passes of the field's class per evaluation). The  α/β pair is load-bearing, not completeness: the fused form replaces a convolve! that  carries α/β — IT already rides the -nablacdot sign of its scalar advection on  convolve!'s α — so shipping less would strand the very chains being fused; and β collapses  IT's phase-shift de-aliasing tail entirely, as two projection calls — the primary with the de-alias  kernel at alpha = 12, the displaced evaluation with the composed kernel (de-alias ∘  shift-back, built once at setup by the kernel composition convolve(g₁, g₂)) at  alpha = 12, beta = 1 — eliminating  the standalone shift-back, average, and truncation passes (four passes per evaluation at wiring  time; two more were already banked consumer-side by rewriting the average as convolve!'s  five-argument form). The curl! operator deliberately does not get the kernel arity: IT's curls feed  transforms directly, so there is no consumer — the absence is the §2 no-speculative-structure  rule, not invariant 1. The distinction matters and the two must not be blurred: invariant 1's  absences are mathematical statements a user can rely on (a spectral * is invalid and always will  be), while this one is merely unbuilt — curl! is exactly as composable with a kernel as  divergence!, and the arity would be added the day a consumer wants it. Recorded  here. Validity is unchanged: spectral-only, the kernel under convolve!'s Hermitian reality  condition, and the Nyquist split untouched (per-mode scalars multiply regardless of their Nyquist  values). Guards: the fused-equals-sequential equalities, the α/β references, the bitwise aliased  projection, and the kernel-aliasing case in test/differentialoperators.jl; zero-allocation pins  for every new arity and the aliased call in test/allocations.jl; device-vs-host parity in  test/gpu/suite.jl; the fused-vs-sequential benchmark rows in the operators class.\nThe exponential radial kernel and the kernel-composed combination (the lazy-kernel pair). Two  requests from GBGHomogeneousTurbulence's audits — items 2 and 7 — landed as one broadcast-builder  family in Convolution, because the second is the first with a two-field prologue.\nThe exponential family. exponential_convolve! (allocating exponential_convolve) applies the  exponential radial kernel exp(c k^2 + s hat w) — c a real coefficient on the squared  wavenumber k^2, hat w an optional real per-mode exponent field with real scale s —  evaluated lazily from the three one-dimensional wavenumber arrays inside the fused assignment.  The kernel is never built, so against the stored-kernel route (radial_kernel →  consumer broadcast → convolve!) it removes the kernel's residency, its build pass, and its full  field read per application, reading O(N) wavenumber rows instead (N the points per axis).  The measured trade is a reuse question (PERFORMANCE.md finding (k)): with a  varying exponent — the consumer's case, whose stage substeps differ — the lazy form beats  rebuild-then-apply by approx 14times on the GPU; with one fixed kernel applied many times  the stored route keeps a approx 11times (GPU) to approx 17times (CPU) edge, the  per-mode exponential costing more than the field read it saves. The two-operand fused forms below  win on both backends regardless (approx 11–15times, the CPU two-operand case the low  end — the finding-(k) table of PERFORMANCE.md is the source of record).  For c le 0 the kernel is, as a real physical object, the periodic Gaussian: the operator  e^-cDelta, smoothing at length sqrt-2c — and the identification is exact on the  grid because the exponent uses the same true-Nyquist squared-wavenumber family as the Laplacian's  own symbol (invariant 5).\nThe boundary, and two signature decisions made against the request's framing. The consumer  framed the exponent as built from a diffusivity and a substep; neither crosses PF's boundary  (invariant 12) — the consumer folds them into the dimensionless c, and the name \"rate\" was  declined (a rate is per-unit-time, and the array that would cross is the dimensionless product,  not the rate). Working the consumer's stage structure sharpened the offset into its final shape:  a scalar exponent offset does not exist, because exp(ck^2 + s_0) = e^s_0 exp(ck^2)  folds into the accumulate weight alpha — a deliberate absence; and the per-mode offset  enters as scale times field (s hat w, not a pre-scaled array), because the consumer's  substeps vary per stage — with a bare offset array every stage would pay a full array rebuild,  while the scalar s is free and the resident hat w is built once.\nThe kernel-composed combination. combine! gains the method  combine!(dest, α, a, β, b, kernel) — the weighted combination convolved with a stored kernel,  hat g cdot (alpha hat a + beta hat b), in one coefficientwise pass — and  exponential_convolve! carries the same two-operand form with its lazy kernel. The consumer  evidence is the integrating-factor state update (a combine! followed immediately by a  convolve! with the factor, per stage per field — two full passes of the destination saved per  site, about six vector plus six scalar passes per three-stage step); the lazy twin additionally  retires the factor's per-stage build. A third proposed consumer — the phase-shift average — was  found not to be an instance of this form at all (its kernel lands on one operand), and is served  instead by decision 32's beta accumulate; the correction is recorded there. With  b === textttdest the method computes the kernel-filtered accumulate in place  (coefficientwise, hence alias-safe with no guard), which is why it carries no second trailing  accumulate pair — two alpha/beta roles in one signature is the glyph-collision failure  mode, and the aliasing provides the accumulate for free.\nNaming and the method-set bookkeeping. The kerneled combination is a method of combine!  with a trailing kernel argument — the decision-32 composition idiom (divergence! and  helmholtz_projection! compose the same way) — not a new verb; the earlier weighted_inner  naming (decision 27) is the reductions family's convention and stands there. The method is  defined in Convolution, and the honest reason is narrower than \"include order\": DifferentialOperators  precedes Convolution too and kept its own kernel-composed divergence!/helmholtz_projection!  arities (decision 32), so precedence alone does not decide it. What differs is that those two are  operators whose symbol the kernel multiplies, defined where the operator lives, whereas  combine!'s kernel form is a convolution applied to a combination — a Convolution operation  taking a FieldAlgebra verb's arguments. Placing it here happens to leave the FieldAlgebra  method-parity baseline untouched, the parity count filtering on the defining module. That is an  observation about a consequence and never a reason: a baseline exists to notice movement, so  avoiding movement is not a design goal, and placement follows the operation's mathematics. A change  that moves a count refreezes it with the reason (decision 53's protocol) rather than being reshaped  to avoid it. Validity:  spectral-only (a kernel is a spectral object; the physical absence is a validity absence), the  exponential kernel real, even, and positive — Hermitian-safe by construction — and a  complex-valued hat w breaks reality exactly as any consumer-built kernel can under  convolve! (the docstring warns; the trust model is unchanged). Guards: stored-kernel equality  oracles, the single-mode eigenvalue oracle e^ccos x, fused-equals-sequential for both  two-operand forms, the in-place filtered accumulate, and the absences in test/convolution.jl;  zero-allocation pins in test/allocations.jl; device parity in test/gpu/suite.jl; and the  convolution benchmark rows whose baseline is the sequential combine! + convolve! pair.\nThe radial diagnostics fuse their staging into the binning kernels. The audits' item 5: each  radial_spectrum call staged three full spectral planes — a fiber-contracted magnitude array, an  integer shell-index array, and a Parseval-weighted array — before the decision-25 scatter-add pass  consumed them (measured 1.6 of the consuming solver GBGHomogeneousTurbulence's units at 256^3, reproduced here; the cross-power form  staged a fourth, fiber-shaped, plane), and radial_correlation staged a separation-magnitude and  a shell-index array of its own. All of it is now derived per element inside the kernels: the  spectral kernel computes a mode's k from the three one-dimensional squared-wavenumber arrays  (the same left-associated sum the lazy k^2 broadcast evaluates, so shell assignment is  bitwise-unchanged), contracts the fiber inline (sum_c hat f_c^2, or  sum_c mathrmRe(hat f_c overlinehat g_c) for the cross form — one kernel, the second  field nothing-dispatched away), and reads the rfft Parseval weight off the plane index; the  physical kernel derives the minimum-image separation from the grid index and folds the 1N  correlation normalization into the tiny per-shell average. The shell count is computed  analytically from the corner: the separable k^2 (and the min-image r) is maximized  componentwise, so the corner value is the same floating-point sum the kernel forms for that  element — three O(N) reductions over one-dimensional arrays (spectrum) or pure host  arithmetic (correlation), and no bin can overflow by construction. Each kernel allocates only the  accumulators it uses (the spectrum drops the population counter the correlation needs).\nMeasured at the audit size: radial_spectrum fell from three staged planes to sim 14 KB —  the shell accumulator plus launch bookkeeping — and moved from the bounded-diagnostic guard to a  tight fraction-of-a-field pin in test/allocations.jl (scalar, vector, tensor, and cross forms).  radial_correlation keeps exactly its substance: the per-component power field it builds and the  allocating inverse transform (whose input copy is invariant-11 semantics — a recorded possible  trim is to_physical! with a package-built physical destination, destroying the scratch it no  longer needs; not taken, since the wedge-class problem was the O(N)-temporary staging, which  is gone). The decision-25 helpers _shell_bin_kernel!/_bin_by_shell — the precomputed-index  histogram this fusion supersedes — are retired (that entry carries the pointer); the identities  (sum_kappa P = langle ffrangle, C(0) = langle ffrangle, the g = f reductions  bit-identical to the single-field forms) held through the rewrite unchanged.\nThe audit-response declines, recorded: no general fusion API, two memory items deferred, and  the counterparty's standing reservation. The 2026-07 audit exchange with GBGHomogeneousTurbulence  (\"IT\") shipped five items (decisions 32–34). This entry records what was declined or deferred,  because a decline is a decision, not a gap.\nNo general fusion API. After two rounds of fusion requests the natural follow-up was \"expose a  general fusion mechanism and stop shipping one-offs.\" Declined, on five grounds. (a) The  general mechanism already exists de facto — Julia's dot-broadcast fuses over fields — and it is  precisely the problem: it is unguarded, able to express what the method set deliberately withholds  (a .* of two fields in spectral representation is the banned coefficientwise product), and no  type system can help, since a legitimate kernel is a ScalarFieldSpectral — kernel application  and the banned field product are indistinguishable at the type level. (Decision 60 later made a  kernel its own type, so that particular pair is now distinguishable; the ground stands anyway,  since a .* of two fields remains expressible and unguarded.) The broadcast layer is  therefore internal machinery, not a public contract (§8). (b) The  public method set is the spec (invariant 1); a fusion surface erases it. (c) Verification does  not fuse: each named operation carries alias analysis, the α/β branch, Nyquist reasoning  (decision 28 needed both wavenumber families to keep a displaced field real), the Hermitian  reality condition, allocation pins, device parity, and bitwise threading identity — a  client-composed kernel carries none of them, and the consumer's own architecture (\"raise, don't  reimplement\") exists because it does not want to own kernel correctness. (d) The triage  protocol is where correctness happens: across two rounds it caught a wrong signature, a missing  β, an invariant-12 boundary violation, and produced two fusions larger than requested — a  compiler would have delivered none of that. (e) Demand is measurably exhausted: everything  that survived triage instantiates one structure — sections in spectral representation as a module  over the ring of Fourier symbols — whose operations the named set now covers (ring multiplication  is kernel composition, the ring-weighted combination is decision 33, operator ∘ symbol is  decision 32), and with the five items landed IT's frozen pass ledger sits at its fused ideal.  Two validity-closed escalation paths are recorded for when demand materializes, not built:  radial real symbols sigma(k^2) as closure kernels (real and even by construction, hence  Hermitian-safe and Nyquist-unambiguous; exponential_convolve! is the family's first instance,  and a third functional form — a Gaussian filter, hyperviscosity exp(-nu (k^2)^p tau) —  is the trigger to generalize); and a stdlib-shaped physical map!(φ, dest, sections...) (since  built — decision 48), the  section-valued sibling of the mapped reductions (every pointwise fiber map is valid in physical  representation), waiting for a consumer needing a fiber map outside the shipped algebra.\nDeferred: symmetric second-order tensor storage (IT item 3). Mathematically legitimate — the  symmetric tensors mathrmSym^2(V) are a genuine subbundle, so a six-component storage type  clears the §2  bar — but it is the largest surface on the table (two field types, a symmetric fiber type,  six-component transform plans, and a combinatorial decision over the 206-method algebra), and a  symmetric type wired into only a few operations is exactly the partial surface invariant 1 exists  to prevent. It is also dominated on its own motivating episode by item 4 below, which composes  with it. Revisit when a consumer needs the symmetric type as a first-class algebra citizen, not as  storage for one diagnostic.\nDeferred: an in-place real-to-complex transform mode (IT item 4). Requested \"when convenient\"  with its consumer archived, so the no-speculative-structure rule settles it — and the design cost  is real: in-place r2c needs the padded first dimension 2(N_12+1) (N_1 the first-axis  points), making the physical data a non-contiguous view, which collides with invariant 3's  contiguity guarantee (relied on by FFT batching and the threading chunking) and needs a  rocFFT/cuFFT in-place story. It is, though, the largest-reach memory lever on the table; if  memory genuinely binds, un-archiving the consumer to make this concrete beats pursuing the  symmetric storage. The wider byte-level buffer-sharing framing is a scratch-arena design, and the  package's stance is that the consumer owns buffers.\nThe counterparty's standing reservation, quoted so ground (e) stays honest. IT pre-registered —  deliberately not raising it now — that its post-wiring ledger sits near but not at the fused  ideal: the residual is the two-output stage update  (hat h hat u) leftarrow (hat N + a hat h mathcal E odot (hat u + b hat h)) — a  module map with two outputs sharing an intermediate, outside the ring structure the shipped  set closes (not ring multiplication, not the ring-weighted combination, not operator ∘ symbol) —  of order 13 % of its pass traffic under the mask strategies. If the regenerated ledger confirms it  once the five items are wired, it returns as a round-3 request with consumer evidence, per the  demand protocol. It would not fail the §2 bar (a map into a direct sum of section spaces with  ring-weighted entries is honest structure); the questions it will face are surface shape — the  package has no two-destination operation — and whether the measured share justifies one.\nThe pass audit: element traffic measured per operation against a frozen fused ideal. The  audit exchange proved a hole in the verification stool: an unfused operation passes every  existing layer — the correctness oracles (right values), the allocation audit (recomputation  allocates nothing), static analysis — and only a benchmark number drifts, with no ideal beside it  to notice. The three-broadcast Helmholtz projection read every source component three times per  mode from decision 24 until decision 32, and no test failed. Since the pointwise operations are  bandwidth-bound streaming (PERFORMANCE.md §2), their element traffic is  their cost — so test/pass_audit.jl now measures it and pins it exactly.\nMethod — measured, not transcribed. the consuming solver GBGHomogeneousTurbulence's compute audit (its decision 34) freezes an  analytically transcribed per-step pass ledger with a fused-ideal bound (live-ins read once,  live-outs written once, intermediates free) and a named-holder gap register; that is the honest  form for a schedule its author owns. The package has no schedule (invariant 12), so its unit is  the operation — and, per its own testing philosophy (verify against ground truth, never the  code's own account), it measures rather than transcribes: a test-local CountingArray  (getindex/setindex! under atomic counters) slides beneath the field types through the generic  storage of invariant 6, flows through the field broadcast, _assign!, and the  KernelAbstractions CPU kernels alike, and every audited call asserts each participating array's  exact absolute element counts — absolute, not per-array multiples, because legitimate  patterns break multiples (sym! reads an off-diagonal twice and a diagonal once). Beside each  frozen count sits the fused ideal, and a count above its ideal must carry a named holder or  the suite fails — the gap register transplanted intact. CPU-only, like the allocation audit and  for the same reason: the code is generic, so the pass structure is backend-independent, while  launch counts and achieved bandwidth stay benchmark-side. Excluded with reasons — each exclusion cut to its  true width: the transform interiors (FFTW's internal passes are FFTW's; plans reject wrapped  storage — but an operation containing a transform still audits its countable stages, and  radial_correlation's input read and its decision-34 physical binning kernel are audited, the  wrapper never reaching the FFT because similar on it yields plain storage); the anterpolation  scatter (its Atomix accumulate needs pointer atomics a wrapper cannot provide — the  gather is audited at its exact per-point stencil arithmetic, M (2n+2)^3 field reads per  component, and extending the atomics onto the wrapper is the recorded route to the scatter); and  O(N) configuration arrays and point positions (only field- and point-value-shaped storage is  wrapped, its own scope line).\nWhat the first measurement found. The decision-32/33/34 kernels sit at their ideals — the  one-pass projection reads 3 and writes 3 (the audit would have failed the old 9), its beta = 0 forms provably never read the destination, the KA kernel reads a composed ĝ once per mode  where the broadcast family re-reads a scalar kernel once per fiber component, and the fused  radial binning reads the field once. The standing gaps are all one family — per-component  formula granularity: gradient! re-reads its scalar source per output (3×), curl! and  cross! read each source component per formula containing it (2×), symmetric_gradient! 5×,  mul!'s vector operand 3×, and the broadcast kernels re-read a scalar kernel per fiber  component — each now frozen with its holder named, so any of them is a known, priced candidate  for a decision-32-style one-pass rewrite the day a consumer's ledger makes one worth it. (Since  retired: decisions 38/39 took the whole family to its fused ideals; the one holder standing is  the exponential ŵ fiber extrusion.) Two  curiosities the exact counters caught: LinearAlgebra's generic dot (and the component  reduction under mean) peeks one element to type its accumulator (+1 read, frozen with its  holder), while the fused mapped reductions peek nothing.\nRegeneration. PASS_AUDIT_PRINT=1 julia --project=. test/pass_audit.jl prints every measured  cell; a raised count is a fusion regression unless a new holder justifies it, and a deliberate  change freezes its new values in the same commit, citing the decision — IT's regeneration  protocol, verbatim.\nThe CPU path's performance target is one cluster node, not small problems — and that reopens the  GPU-first tiebreaks. The performance model (§9,  PERFORMANCE.md §1) recorded the CPU path as serving \"development, testing, and small problems\",  and every platform tiebreak below was licensed by that sentence. It understated the role. The CPU  path does serve development and small tests — but at those grids performance seldom decides  anything, so that role sets no tuning target. The role that does is the opposite of small: a  single large CPU node, running the resolutions that exceed one GPU's memory. Since distributed  and multi-GPU execution are out of scope (§1), the memory ceiling of  a run is whatever one node holds, and a CPU node holds far more than a GPU. On NAISS Arrhenius —  the concrete target — a thin CPU node pairs two 128-core AMD EPYC 9755 processors with 768 GB of  DDR5 and a fat node carries 3 TB, while the GPU partition's GH200 offers 96 GB of HBM. Taking one  scalar field in either representation as 8 bytes per grid point in Float64 (the physical array and  the rfft half-spectrum agree to within a part in the per-axis size) and a solver's live set at order 25 fields, one  GH200 stops near 768^3 where a thin CPU node reaches about 1536^3. The two partitions are  also distinct machines, so the split is clean: GPU work runs on GPU nodes, CPU work on CPU nodes.\nWhat this reopens. Three recorded results rest on the understated premise, and each was measured  outside the regime that now matters. The loop order (PERFORMANCE.md §4b)  kept the x-inner stencil traversal for the transfer kernels at a measured 075times CPU  regression, justified on two grounds — that \"the CPU's large caches absorb the strided access\", and  that the GPU is the production platform. At 1536^3 a single Float64 vector field is 87 GB  against 512 MB of L3 per socket, so the first ground fails outright, and the second no longer holds in this  band; the regression there is plausibly worse than the measured figure, not better. The pass  audit's standing gaps (decision 36; since retired by decisions 38/39) — gradient! re-reading its source 3times,  symmetric_gradient! 5times — are close to free while a field is cache-resident and become  pure DRAM traffic once it is not, so they price very differently here. And NUMA first-touch,  which §9 sets aside as a consumer concern, is a  two-socket node's dominant risk: similar reserves address space without touching pages, so the  placement is decided by whichever code writes first, and a threaded _assign! write distributes  while a serial fill concentrates. Whether that lands well is a measurement nobody has taken.\nThe measurement gap this exposes. benchmark/characterize.jl sweeps 64^3–256^3 by  default, and no CPU column above 256^3 has ever been recorded — so the entire CPU production  band is unmeasured, and the tiebreaks above were settled two to four octaves below it. Closing  that is a prerequisite for revisiting them, and it is only partly possible off-cluster: a  single-socket workstation can extend the grid sweep but cannot exhibit a NUMA effect at all.\nThe same target fixes a portability bar: aarch64. Arrhenius's GPU nodes are GH200  superchips, so the CUDA device is hosted by a Grace CPU — an ARM (aarch64) host — and  production will eventually run on both partitions. The package must therefore stay buildable on  ARM, and no design decision or dependency may be adopted that is x86-only — the canonical  tempting example is swapping the FFT provider to MKL, which has no aarch64 build; any  x86-intrinsic tuning falls under the same bar. Nothing in the current dependency tree is known  to be x86-only (pure Julia plus JLL artifacts published for aarch64-linux), but nothing has  ever been built or tested on ARM either — a coverage gap recorded in  PERFORMANCE.md §7.\nThe threaded assignment runs the real engine per slab, and the fused operators leave the  broadcast route. Finding (l) (PERFORMANCE.md §4) showed the threaded _assign! path  indexing the fused Broadcasted element by element — a loop that does not vectorize (~8.6 GB/s  per thread against materialize!'s ~41 on one Zen 5 core), so two and four threads ran slower  than serial on every streaming operation, on both archived CPUs, at every size, unnoticed since  the feature landed. Two rewrites follow. The broadcast route: _assign_threaded! now splits  the destination's slab axis (the last among the largest, so a whole-vector-field write splits a  grid axis and never the length-3 fiber) and runs the standard fused materialize! per slab over  view-sliced operands — each slab executes the same SIMD kernel as serial, each element is  written once from the same expression, bitwise identity preserved; a Val function barrier  keeps the slicing inferred (a runtime axis boxed kilobytes per slab), and the allocation audit  absorbs the remaining size-independent per-task bookkeeping with an explicit field-destination  floor. Measured: 512³ Float64 add! at two threads fell 187.7 → 73.3 ms — the DRAM ceiling,  which previously took all eight. The operator kernels: gradient! (both arities), curl!,  and symmetric_gradient! are one-pass kernels in the decision-32 shape — a KernelAbstractions  kernel on a device, a slab-threaded stride-1 @simd loop on the CPU (the KA CPU backend was  measured at 17.7 GB/s single-thread, hence the loop) — every source coefficient read once per  mode, the audit rows at their fused ideals, curl!'s alias guard retired (its fused pass reads  a mode before writing it), and each verified bitwise against captured oracles of the  replaced broadcasts. The serial trade is recorded honestly: symmetric_gradient!'s nine-output  loop spills registers and gives back ~5% single-threaded at 512³ while gaining 1.54× at eight  threads; a two-pass split is the fallback if a serial consumer ever needs it.\nOne generic fused executor runs the fiberwise algebra; the alias-guard family is retired.  The table-stamped algebra ran one broadcast per output component, re-reading shared sources  once per formula (the audit's holders: cross! 2×, mul!'s vector operand 3×, sym!'s  off-diagonals 2×, det!'s cofactor rows 2×, the scalar-field action and divisor once per  output component, convolve!'s scalar kernel once per fiber component) and wrapped the index  shuffles in _guard_noalias! — a field-sized temporary on every aliased call. Now a pure  scalar values function per operation maps one element's source values (plus fiber  constants) to the tuple of outputs, and one portable executor (_fiberwise_pass!) streams it:  sources read once into registers, every output written from them, the α/β convention owned  centrally, alias-safe by construction. The backend split follows decision 30 — one  KernelAbstractions kernel on a device, a slab-threaded @simd loop over the linear index  range on the CPU — and linear indexing is what makes the executor base-agnostic: contiguous  component views index identically for a 3-D grid and a 1-D point set, so fields and  point-values share it, and the values functions are stamped from the same per-component  formula builders the broadcasts used (a :scalar accessor kind plus _undot), keeping every  formula written once. Routed through it: cross!, the mul! family, transpose!, sym!,  skew!, dev!, det!, the scalar-field action/division, and the kernel-composed  convolve!/combine! fiber paths (α folded into the values so the broadcast's own association  is preserved); dot!/tr!/the norms stay broadcasts — their traffic was already ideal. The  exponential family's fiber extrusion stays a named holder: its per-mode coefficient is the  lazy cartesian k^2, which the linear executor cannot see — the priced fix is the  finding-(k) mode-loop hoist. The dev! operation stopped materializing its trace (now a per-element  register) and left the documented-allocators list. Every routed operation verified bitwise  against captured oracles (54 cases: both element types, physical and spectral, field and  fiber-constant patterns, aliased shuffles through the former guard path, α/β accumulates, the  point-value mirrors), and both parity baselines (206 + 122) are byte-identical — the public  surface never moved.\nThe reductions thread on a fixed tree — bitwise thread-count-invariant — and  helmholtz_projection! joins the gated loops. The last §9 deferral closes. Every CPU  reduction — inner (physical and the spectral Parseval forms), l2norm, integral, mean,  std/var, maximum/minimum/extrema, and the whole mapped-form family — now reduces  through one fixed midpoint tree: a binary recursion whose splits are a function of the  element count alone, its top lceil log_2 textttnthreads rceil + 1 levels evaluated as  spawned tasks (through the same _use_threads gate as every other route) and its leaves as the  existing pairwise-blocked @simd loops. Because the tree never depends on the schedule, the  result is bitwise-identical at every thread count — serial equals two threads equals  sixty-four — which the threading suite asserts both by toggling the override and across a  subprocess launched at a different -t. This is deliberately stronger than the tolerance  framing the reductions previously carried: that framing remains for cross-backend  comparisons (a GPU reduction tree is the device's own), while same-backend CPU values now carry  an exact contract. The chunk-indexed, fixed-combine design is also the post-task-migration  idiom (no threadid() state anywhere), and accuracy inherits the pairwise story — the tree  only deepens it. Two ledger holders retired on the way: the fixed-tree kernels seed their  accumulator from the first element, so the \"+1 typing peek\" rows of inner and mean froze at  ideal (and the audit immediately caught — and priced away — a first-component double pass in  the new dims reducer, doing exactly its job). The switch changes reduction values once, in the  last bits (a different, deeper summation tree), absorbed by the analytic oracles.  weighted_inner stayed serial at the time of this decision — its coefficient being the lazy  cartesian k^2, the finding-(k) class — and was moved onto this same fixed tree on  2026-08-30 (PERFORMANCE.md finding (ac)), so the deferral recorded here is discharged. Measured honestly on the  2-channel desktop the gains are 10–25% at eight threads (serial streaming already sits near the  read ceiling there); the beneficiary is the decision-37 node, where serial is a twelfth of the  socket. Finally, helmholtz_projection! gains the decision-38-shape gated ::CPU loop twin —  bitwise-equal to its KernelAbstractions engine, verified — so the policy-exempt set shrinks to  the operations whose kernels are structurally atomic/irregular: the grid↔point transfer and the  radial binning. The exemption is now a principle, not a history. (Decision 42 then accidentally  severed this loop from dispatch for a day; decision 47 restored it and added the guard that pins  it live.)\nThe spectral reductions follow the layout's folded axis (decided 2026-07-23; a Phase-B  formula-sweep finding). _parseval_weight, the spectral inner's correction planes, and the  shell-binning kernel all folded array dimension 1 unconditionally, while RealFourierLayout  admits any fft_dims permutation and the transforms and DirectDFT were already general — so a  legal permuted layout silently corrupted the spectral inner, weighted_inner, and the radial  family. Generalized: the folded dimension is read from fft_dims[1] — the Parseval weight is  reshaped along it, the correction planes select it, and the shell kernel takes it as a Val and  picks the folded mode index — with the default layout bitwise unchanged. Why the tests never  caught it: no test ever constructed a non-default layout, so the hardcoded dimension always  coincided with the fold on every tested path. The suite's test/reductions.jl now runs the Parseval, shell,  and weighted identities under permuted layouts ((2, 1, 3), (3, 2, 1), a mixed-parity grid),  and test/gpu/suite.jl carries a device-parity case. The follow-up layout sweep (same  session) extends the lane across every layer whose correctness rests on fft_dims:  test/fields.jl (a permuted transform round-trip + both multiplier families against  rfftfreq/fftfreq on every axis, odd and even folds), test/differentialoperators.jl  (analytic gradient/Laplacian under a permuted fold), test/convolution.jl (the translation  kernel's circshift oracle with a live Nyquist factor on the folded axis),  test/transfer_spectral.jl (DirectDFT at the nodes + NUFFT against its oracle),  test/hdf5.jl (fft_dims persistence), and the reductions set gains radial_correlation.  The sweep immediately caught a third site: the NUFFT constructor allocated its oversampled  work fields with the layout-correct shapes but wrapped them through the layout-dropping  _wrap_field/_wrap_field_spectral helpers, so a permuted reference threw a shape mismatch at  construction (the embed maps and the deconvolution were already general). The wrappers gained  layout-taking forms and the constructor threads the reference's layout through both templates;  the bare-grid spline anterpolate keeps the default-layout form, having no reference to  inherit from.\nThe Helmholtz projector joins the derivative family whole — exact idempotency and exact  solenoidality (decided 2026-07-23; a Phase-B formula-sweep finding). The one-pass kernel  divided the derivative-wavenumber numerator a by the true k^2, so at a  mixed-Nyquist mode (a cdot a  k^2) the projector was neither exactly idempotent nor  exactly divergence-annihilating — the identities held sub-Nyquist only, and  test/adjointness.jl deliberately scoped its fixtures to dodge it (its own header conceded the  restriction). The denominator is now a cdot a, squared in-register from the same loaded  wavenumber values (both the KA kernel and the gated ::CPU loop drop their three  squared-wavenumber array arguments — the loop was missed on the first pass; decision 47), making  mathbb P = mathrmid - (a a^top)(a cdot a) the exact orthogonal projector onto the kernel  of the discrete divergence: mathbb P^2 = mathbb P and nablacdot(mathbb P u) = 0 at  every mode, the zero and pure-Nyquist modes passing through as the identity. Sub-Nyquist the  two families coincide value for value, so resolved-band results are bitwise unchanged and every  existing analytic and parity test holds as-is. The composite  mathbb P u = u - nabla Delta^-1(nablacdot u) becomes the resolved-band description  (Delta^-1 keeps the true k^2 per invariant 5); the projector definition is primary.  Full-spectrum Nyquist-content identities are pinned in test/differentialoperators.jl.\nThe coverage-axes audit — the explicit denominator, its closure lanes, and the one bug it  found (2026-07-23; the follow-up the decision-41/42 defects demanded). The three  layout/Nyquist defects shared one cause: the suite was exhaustive only over the axes it  named, and no written list of axes existed — so an axis living solely in the type system  (fft_dims with one value ever constructed) was invisible to every sweep. The denominator  is now written down (docs/TESTING.md §4, fourteen axes at the time — decisions 60, 61 and 63 have  since taken the list to seventeen: representation, tensor order,  element type on the CPU lane, layout fold, axis parity, grid-size cubicity, domain-length  anisotropy, origin, degenerate bases, the alpha = 0 branch, aliasing patterns, Nyquist  content, backend, thread count), with the rule that a new type parameter or admissible  input region adds its axis row and its suite lane in the same change. Five sequential audit  lanes filled the operation × axis matrix and every finding was closed as fixed or  dismissed-with-reason. The closure testsets (~300 assertions across ten test files) include  the full operator set against closed forms on a non-cubic anisotropic permuted box in both  element types with mixed-Nyquist oracles; the kernel-composed arities on full-spectrum  fixtures; adjointness.jl's new full-spectrum lane (the decision-42-exact identities; the  dissipation identity alone stays band-limited — invariant 5's design); the  alpha = 0 beta ne 0 branch of every α/β-capable operation, previously asserted  nowhere in the package; destination-aliasing in both directions, including the branchy  field ± constant path and cross! with the destination aliasing the second operand; the  four never-exercised tensor-constant mul! methods; the reversed and subtract directions  of the spectral constant family; every kernel builder and the lazy exponential kernel off  the default box; the anisotropic translation_kernel; the spectral transfer on an odd fold  with origin, out-of-box points, tensor order two, and a non-default oversampling — with  independent oracles for both verbs; the point-algebra aliasing block; guard wiring through  the public entries; the HDF5 corruption paths (plus two new ArgumentError guards in the  extension for a missing data dataset or geometry attribute); and the anisotropic radial  family with single-mode placement oracles. The audit's one genuine code bug, found by the  degenerate-input probe lane: the CPU fixed-tree reductions seeded their accumulator from  the first element before checking emptiness — an @inbounds out-of-bounds read on an  M = 0 point set — whose user-visible symptom was maximum/minimum/extrema fabricating  0.0 on an empty section instead of erroring, a silent no-data-versus-zero trap for the  Lagrangian consumers whose point sets legitimately empty. The four tree entries now guard  emptiness with deliberate per-operation semantics mirroring the stdlib counterpart and the  device branch: an order statistic throws the Base empty-collection ArgumentError, the  mean family (mean, var, std, l2norm, the normalized inner) is NaN, and the raw  sum is zero, so integral over no points is exactly zero; the fiber (mapped) path already  had its guard. Every other probed degenerate — axes of length one and two, the Nyquist  split there, zero-work kernel launches across all three transfer schemes, out-of-box  periodic wrap, the alpha = beta = 0 NaN-prefill — behaved exactly as the documented  invariants promise and is now pinned. The audit's meta-result: beyond this one bug, every  previously unexercised path proved correct once tested — decision 39's bitwise  alias-safety, assignment_window's true-k Nyquist convention, the NUFFT tensor path,  the radial shell bound at any aspect ratio (structural: the corner bound is the sum of the  same per-axis maxima the kernel forms, and rounding is monotone) — and the one docstring  defect (radial_spectrum's non-cubic caveat naming min(N_i)2, wrong once lengths and  sizes decorrelate) was corrected to the per-axis inscribed-sphere form.\nThe section allocators become public: similar_field and similar_point_values (decided  2026-07-24; a /check-consistency panel finding). Both build a section carrying a reference's  representation, tensor order, base grid and storage backend — the allocation a consumer needs for  the scratch an in-place loop writes into — yet both were documented as Internal API, under a  heading stating they are not public. The evidence that this was a gap rather than a boundary is  the flagship example: examples/HIST_CPU.jl, which exists to show how a real consumer writes  against the package, hand-rolled the equivalent thirteen times in make_workspace, spelling out  field_spectral_shape, zeros, an element type and a concrete field constructor for each scratch  field. Two facts settle the direction. A consumer cannot reach the idiom without them, since the  concrete constructors demand exactly the shape and element-type knowledge the allocator exists to  encapsulate; and the GPU twin needed a parallel hand-rolled copy differing only in a dev(...)  wrapper, where the allocator carries the backend for free. Both are now exported and documented  in their own API-page sections, and both examples build their whole workspace through them. The  allocated storage is uninitialized (similar, not zeros), which the docstrings state and  the examples handle by zeroing what they allocate. The method-parity baselines (206 field / 122  point-value methods) are untouched: those lock the algebra generics, and these are separate ones.\nGrid3D enforces its own uniformity (decided 2026-07-24; a /check-consistency panel  finding). The type is documented as a uniform grid, so its spacing is not independent data — it  is domain_size ./ grid_size along every axis. But grid_spacing is stored (the transfer  kernels read it directly, and recomputing it per launch buys nothing), and Julia's generated  four-argument constructor is public, so a caller could hand in a spacing bearing no relation to  the domain and get a silently self-contradictory grid. Nothing in the tree did: the only  four-argument call site was the two-argument convenience constructor itself, with the HDF5  reader, the NUFFT oversampled grid and the tests all going through the two-argument form — and  the reader in fact re-derives the spacing on load, so a persisted inconsistent grid would not  have survived its own round trip. The failure it invites is the quiet kind: the spline transfer  kernels turn a position into a cell index with the stored spacing, so a wrong value mislocates  every interpolated point instead of raising anything. An inner constructor now rejects a spacing  that contradicts the domain, and a non-positive grid size with it. The comparison is a relative  floating-point tolerance rather than equality, so a spacing reached by an equivalent but  different arithmetic route is still accepted; that case is pinned in test/fields.jl, along with  both rejections and the Float32 tolerance.\nHDF5 persistence extends to the point layer — write_points / read_points (decided  2026-07-24). Decision 29 gave fields a serializer and stopped there, which left a plain asymmetry:  a solver could checkpoint the velocity field it was integrating but not the point set it was  advecting through that field, so a restart could not reconstruct the state it had. Since the point  layer exists for consumer-side Lagrangian methods (§10), the half that cannot be persisted is the  half a restart most needs. The pair mirrors the field pair exactly, in the same weak-dependency  extension, with the same bare generics and docstrings in the core so ?write_points works without  HDF5 loaded.\nWhy decision 29's reasoning carries over unchanged. Serializing a section is I/O, not an operation  on sections, so the rule that design changes be justified by established mathematics (§2) —  which governs the operation set — does not bar it, exactly as for fields. Invariant 12 is  untouched as well: a PointSet is a set of positions, not a particle with a history, and the  package still attaches no notion of time. The consumer owns the file, bundles a snapshot, and writes  its own time / iteration attributes.\nThe group is simpler than a field's, because a point object carries less. A field group must  describe its Grid3D geometry, its Fourier layout and its representation; a point object has none  of those. So the group holds the array as the dataset data plus four attributes — a type tag, a  schema version, the element type, and the point count — with a fifth, tensor_order, on a  point-value section but not on a PointSet, which is a base and has no fiber. The tensor order is  recovered on read from the array's dimensionality, as a field's is.\nPositions and values are separate groups, deliberately. They are separate objects — the base, and  sections over it — and one stored set of positions is often shared by several value sets, so  embedding the coordinates in every value group would duplicate the largest array in the file. A  consumer that wants them bundled writes both under one parent group, which costs nothing and keeps  the choice with the consumer.\nVerification. test/hdf5.jl covers both element types across all four point types (including a  third-order TensorPointValues, whose order must survive as the array's dimensionality), the  parent[name] = points sugar, the self-description attributes — asserting that a PointSet group  carries no tensor_order and no grid geometry — that the two families coexist in one file with  neither reader accepting the other's groups, and the five corrupted-group error paths. The testset  grew from 143 to 207 assertions.\nThe gated helmholtz_projection! CPU loop, dead since decision 42, is restored — and the  canary lane caught it (2026-07-24). Decision 42 rewrote the projector's denominator to the  derivative-family a cdot a, computed in-register, and dropped the three squared-wavenumber  array arguments. It updated the KA kernel, the generic launch method, and the dispatcher — but  left the gated ::CPU loop (decision 40) with its old six-array signature. With the dispatcher  now passing three wavenumber arrays where the loop demanded six, the loop's arity no longer  matched, so it silently stopped being a dispatch candidate and every CPU call fell through to the  generic KernelAbstractions-on-CPU() launch — the ~17 GB/s single-thread path decision 40  existed to escape. The gated loop had become dead code, and its stale body still read the  true-k^2 array, so had the arity ever been fixed without the body it would have computed  the wrong Nyquist denominator.\nNo numerical harm occurred, because CPU and GPU both ran the one correct KA kernel: parity and  the full-spectrum Nyquist tests passed throughout. Three things did break. Decision 40's stated  reach — cutoff and with_threading governing helmholtz — was false for a day. The  with_threading bitwise assertion added for a 2026-07-24 consistency finding was vacuous: it  toggled an override the live path ignores, comparing a path against itself. And the operation ran  slower, which is what surfaced it — the canary lane (benchmark/regress.jl), on its first live  exercise, flagged the t1 helmholtz canary at 1.27× its frozen baseline, the exact  achieved-time regression the allocation and pass audits cannot see.\nThe fix drops the three squared arguments from the loop and squares a in-register, mirroring  the kernel; the loop is verified bitwise-equal to the KA kernel on a Nyquist-bearing field,  and divergence-annihilating at every mode. The canary returned to baseline without a refreeze  — proof the baseline was right and the code had drifted, not the reverse. The gap that let a  dispatch fall through unseen is closed by a dispatch-target guard in test/threading.jl: a  CPU helmholtz call must resolve to the KernelAbstractions.CPU specialization, not the  untyped-backend fallback. The lesson generalizes — an operation with a device kernel and a  gated CPU twin has two bodies to keep in sync, and only the guard makes \"they diverged\"  visible at test time rather than in a benchmark weeks later.\nmap! / map — the arbitrary-fiber-map escape hatch, physical-only by construction  (2026-07-28). The section-valued sibling of the mapped reductions (decision 30) and decision-35's  first map! instance: map!(φ, destination, sources...) applies an arbitrary fiber map φ to every  base point and writes a section, where a mapped mean reduces it. It reuses the _fiberwise_pass!  executor unchanged — that executor already takes a runtime values function and keeps its targets  and arrays tuples separate, so an order-changing map (exp ∘ det : tensor → scalar) works with  no new machinery — so map! inherits the fused pass's parallelism (a device kernel on a GPU, a  slab-threaded loop on a CPU) and its allocation-free contract, and is embarrassingly parallel  (disjoint writes, no atomics), bitwise thread-count-invariant on the CPU.\nThe only φ-specific piece is a small adapter in FiberSections (_MapValues): it groups the flat  component scalars a fused pass hands it back into fibers (column-major, so assembly is a bare  SMatrix constructor and the flatten a bare Tuple), applies φ, and flattens the result — a struct,  not a closure, so the input count stays type-level, exactly _FiberGather's reasoning for the  reductions. The public methods live where the vertical algebra lives: FieldAlgebra for physical  fields, Points for point-values (both over the shared _map_pass! driver), extending  Base.map! / Base.map (not piracy — the types are ours). It is physical-only for fields: an  arbitrary φ is nonlinear, so by invariant 1 there is no spectral method, and the result is aliased in  spectral representation (the FieldAlgebra module warning). Point-values are representation-free and  carry no such restriction. The allocating map infers the output tensor order from φ's return type  (Base.promote_op); when it cannot, it errors and steers to map! with a preallocated destination.\nWhy an escape hatch is allowed at all, against invariant 1's \"the method set is the spec\": map! is  validity-closed — physical-only-by-construction is its type signature, so it can only ever produce  a valid operation, never forge an invalid spectral one. It does not subsume the named algebra:  the linear fiber operations (tr, sym, skew, dev, transpose) exist in both representations  and so cannot route through a physical-only map, and the named nonlinear ones (det, norm) already  share the same executor a level down — map! serves the un-named maps, and the named operations  stay named to keep the representation-validity encoding intact.\nvar/std are scalar-only, gain a spectral form, and the vector second central moment is the new  covariance cov (2026-07-28). The fiber-preserving var/std — a per-component variance /  standard deviation for a vector or tensor section — were two mistakes wearing one API. First, they  were physical-only by scoping, not validity: operatornamevar(f) = langle f frangle -  langle frangle^2 = N^-2sum_textstored k ne 0 alpha(k) hat f_k^2 (the fluctuation energy, with  alpha(k) the rfft half-spectrum multiplicity and the N^-2 the normalization the inner  product itself carries; that displayed form is the population moment — corrected = false —  and var's default corrected = true returns it scaled by N(N-1)), both terms already  Parseval-available, so they were wrongly grouped with the genuinely physical-only lpnorm/maximum.  Second, the per-component value is only the basis-dependent diagonal of the covariance tensor  R_ij = langle u_i u_jrangle in operatornameSym^2(V) — the coordinate-free second central moment, the primes marking fluctuations about the mean —  and operatornamestd = sqrtoperatornamevar has no canonical vector form at all: the  matrix square root of the covariance is not a standard deviation, the per-component root is  basis-dependent, and sqrtoperatornametr R is a scalar RMS — three different objects. And  var-as-covariance could not even be uniform (a vector's covariance has tensor order 2, a tensor's order 4;  the result would be an order-4 fiber value, not a section, so the R  3 section ceiling is  not what stands in the way — FiberTensor4 exists as a type. The actual limit is narrower: the  fiber assembly in FiberSections (_fiber_type/_assemble_fiber) is enumerated through order 2,  so an order-4 result has nowhere to be built. The cov reduction is therefore defined on vector sections  alone, which is where its consumer is).\nSo var/std are now scalar-only — the one representation where each is unambiguous — physical  and spectral (the Parseval form, equal to the physical value; the spectral one-pass loses precision  to cancellation when the mean dominates its fluctuations, which the docstring notes). A vector or  tensor section raises a clear error pointing to cov. The new cov (extending Statistics.cov; not  re-exported at the time, like mean/var/std — all four have been re-exported by the umbrella  since 2026-08-13, per the naming rule's export policy in §2) returns the symmetric covariance FiberTensor2 of a  vector section — its diagonal the per-component variances (the normal stresses, in a turbulence  reading), its off-diagonal the covariances (the shear stresses) a per-component var discarded —  computed in one fused mapped pass as textttmean(u otimes u) - bar u otimes bar u  (reusing decision 48's map! machinery through the mapped mean), Bessel-scaled. It is physical-only  (the outer product is nonlinear). The 206/122 parity baselines are unchanged — these are Statistics  extensions, outside the M2 + M3 surface.\nThe separable kernels are stored as their per-axis factors, not as fields (2026-07-28).  translation_kernel and assignment_window build a kernel that factors over the axes,  hat g(k) = prod_a g_a(k_a), and both already computed the three one-dimensional factors  internally before materializing their product into a full field on the last line. That last line is  now gone: both return a SeparableKernel holding the three factors, and the product is evaluated one  mode at a time inside the pass that consumes it.\nThe saving is a representation change with no numerical content — every result is bitwise  identical to the materialized kernel, at every tensor order and on both the overwrite and the  alpha/beta branch, which is what the tests assert against the stored product as oracle. What  changes is the cost: three O(N) arrays in place of O(N^3) coefficients (5 KiB against 16.2 MiB  at 128^3, ~10 KiB against 129 MiB at 256^3), and a pass that reads no field-sized kernel  stream — the three O(N) factors are read per mode, so a kernel application drops from three  field-sized streams to two.\nFewer streams did not hand back a free speed-up, and finding out why fixed the implementation.  Applying the kernel is bandwidth-bound, so the saved stream is worth having only if evaluating the  product costs less than the read it replaces, and how the product is indexed decides that. Handing  the lazy product to the linear-indexed fiberwise executor makes every element convert a linear index  to a cartesian one — an integer division, which a GPU emulates — and that measured 0.29× a stored  kernel at 256^3. A hand-written three-dimensional kernel removed the conversion but lost as much  again to warp fragmentation, because the folded axis is N2 + 1 — 129 at 256^3 — so its rows  do not align to a warp (0.55× on a scalar field, worse than doing nothing). What works is the  plainest form: one flat broadcast over the whole section, the per-axis factors carrying singleton  axes so they extend along the remaining mode axes and along the fiber. Broadcasting indexes them  cartesian and keeps the launch flat and coalesced. Measured against the stored kernel at 256^3  on an RTX 5070 Ti: 1.37× (scalar) and 1.00× (vector) in Float32, and 0.96× / 0.77× in  Float64 — the Float64 shortfall being the extra complex multiplies against a consumer card's  1/64-rate FP64, which full-rate production hardware does not pay. The lesson is the general one for  this package: reach for the broadcast route before a hand-written kernel, and measure the launch  shape, not only the arithmetic. Decision 28's Nyquist  treatment survives unchanged and unexamined, because the per-axis factors are produced by exactly the  code that was already correct.\nThe mechanism is that a kernel's contribution to a fused pass is fetched through _kernel_data,  which yields a stored array for a kernel held as a field and an instantiated Broadcasted for a  separable one. Both the broadcast route and the linear-indexed fiberwise executor consume that lazy  product exactly as they consume an array — verified including inside the KernelAbstractions device  kernel — so no operator body changed to accept one; the kernel arguments merely widened to a union of  the two storage classes. That widening initially reached only within Convolution — every operator  arity outside it stayed hard-typed to a stored kernel, so a SeparableKernel threw a MethodError  at helmholtz_projection!, divergence! and weighted_inner. The module-placement panel of  2026-07-28 caught the overclaim and the gap: the kernel protocol (AbstractSpectralKernel, the  _kernel_data and _check_kernel_compatibility generics, the SpectralKernel union) now lives in  Fields, the one module every kernel-applying module can see, and the arities are genuinely widened  package-wide — pinned by a test that walks every kernel-taking entry point and requires the two  storage classes to agree bitwise.\nTwo operations come with the type. Composition stays inside the family (the factors multiply axis by  axis) — but composing two translation kernels is deliberately not the same operator as one  kernel for the summed displacement, because at an even axis's Nyquist the real factor  cos(pi h_aDelta x_a) obeys cos acos b ne cos(a+b); displacements compose by addition,  which is the more correct route as well as the cheaper one. And inv gives the reciprocal kernel,  separable because 1prod_a g_a = prod_a (1g_a) — the deconvolution an assignment  window exists for, which retires the one place the point-density recipe reached into kernel storage.\nDeliberately not done here: a general SeparableSymbol(σ₁, σ₂, σ₃) closure. Per-axis factors  supplied as functions of k_a would be silently ambiguous about which wavenumber family they are  handed — the true k_a that keeps its Nyquist value, or the derivative k_a that is zeroed  there (invariant 5) — which is precisely the trap decision 28 documents. Storing the factors, built by  the package, keeps that choice where it was already made correctly. The radial fragment  sigma(k^2) is a separate closure-shaped escalation and is not this decision.\nThe radial kernel: a symbol of k^2, evaluated per mode (2026-07-29). The escalation  decision 35 recorded and the consuming solver GBGHomogeneousTurbulence's lazy-per-mode-symbols.md requested. The builders spectral_cutoff and  spectral_band no longer build a field; they return a RadialKernel holding the symbol  sigma itself, and radial_kernel(σ, multipliers) builds one for any other symbol  — a smooth filter envelope, a hyperviscous exp(-nu(k^2)^ptau), an integrating factor.  The kernel costs a closure and three references against O(N^3) coefficients, and the builders'  signatures are unchanged, so no call site moves.\nWhy the family is closed with no case analysis: k^2 is real and even in every component,  so any real sigma of it is real and even too, hence Hermitian-symmetric — the coefficients are  always some real field's. It also carries the true squared wavenumber at an even axis's Nyquist  (invariant 5), which is the value a magnitude symbol wants there. This is exactly the argument  decision 50 found unavailable for a separable closure, where per-axis factors are ambiguous  between the two wavenumber families; the radial case has one family, so the argument that killed  that closure does not carry over.\nStorage is a symbol, not a table, because a radial kernel has no cheap middle. An axis-factored  kernel wins by evaluating its transcendentals 3N times and multiplying per mode; k^2 is a  function of the sum of the squared axis wavenumbers, so a general sigma of it does not factor  and there is no per-axis table to precompute (the exponential does factor, and is why  exponential_convolve! is its own operation rather than a radial_kernel call) — only the closure (sigma evaluated per mode, on a k^2 that  is itself already lazy over three stored O(N) axis arrays) or a full field. Which wins follows  the crossover of PERFORMANCE.md finding (o) plus the symbol's own cost: a cheap sigma (a  mask is one comparison) or one whose parameters change per call — the integrating factor, whose  substep varies every stage — favours the closure decisively, since a stored kernel must be rebuilt  and re-read; a fixed expensive sigma applied many times can favour materializing, which  materialize_kernel provides.\nComposition follows the families. Two radial symbols multiply into one radial symbol (refined by  decision 61: unconditionally so only when both operands carry canonical multipliers of the same  concrete type; any other radial pairing keeps both operands); a radial and  an axis-factored kernel do not collapse, so convolve returns a ProductKernel holding both and  multiplying their per-mode values — the cross-family composition that lets a mask and a  displacement apply in one pass. Only a compressed kernel may lead: convolve(stored, compressed)  stays \"apply a kernel to this scalar field\", since a ScalarFieldSpectral is also a field.  (Superseded by decision 60: a field is no longer a kernel, so the lead-argument rule is gone —  both orders apply and agree bitwise, and composing two kernels is unambiguous by type.)  weighted_inner accepted a stored or radial kernel as a weight and deliberately not a separable  one — superseded by decision 62, which found that restriction to be no constraint at all and  widened the weight to every class. The reasoning recorded here was already the third attempt at the  question and still short of the mark: it correctly called the exclusion scoping, not validity  (a real separable w gives a well-defined langle f w grangle, so invariant 1 never reached  it) but still supposed each admitted class would cost its own fused reduction path, which measurement  disproved — every class already agreed bitwise through the one pass. The two rationales it was  correcting were wrong outright: that separable factors are not real in general, contradicted by  assignment_window's operatornamesinc^2; and that a magnitude-dependent weight does not factor  per axis, which argues about the radial class that was accepted rather than the separable one that  was not.\nOne implementation trap, found on the device and worth the sentence: a symbol closure must capture  values, never a type. The spectral_band builder first captured T to write one(T)/zero(T) in the  body, which makes the closure hold a Type{Float64} — not a bitstype, so a GPU launch rejects it  outright. Capturing the two values instead fixes it, and the radial_kernel docstring states the  rule.\nAn adversarial mutation audit of the test suite, and the two live bugs it found (2026-07-29).  The suite was audited by injecting defects rather than by reading coverage: six defect classes —  each drawn from a real escape — proposed small, silent, plausible edits, and every one was applied  in an isolated worktree with the suite actually run, so \"uncaught\" is a measured fact rather than a  prediction. The framing was chosen because every defect found in the preceding review had been  invisible to a coverage read-through: an axis consulted and waved off with wrong reasoning, a  documented claim no test asserted, a property held by one function and silently absent in its  neighbour, a test that passed while comparing a path against itself, one direction of a symmetric  operation.\nTwo live bugs came out of it, both confirmed by direct execution before any fix. A NUFFT plan  used at a higher tensor order than the reference it was built from returned NaN and  uninitialized memory — the spread wrote fewer fibre components than the extract read — where  DirectDFT handled the identical call correctly; the plan's own order was never checked, only the  field's against the values'. And extrema leaked the scalar-only restriction that maximum  and minimum enforce: those are absent above R = 0 by not being defined there, which suffices  because Base has no fallback that accepts a section — but extrema has one, so it iterated and  returned the per-component pair the restriction exists to refuse. An absence that relies on the  absence of a fallback is not an absence; it has to be stated.\nThe audit's sharpest finding was fixture blindness, not missing tests. The vector gradient's  testset is named for the index convention (nabla u)_ij = partial_x_j u_i, but its fixture  u = (sin x sin y sin z) has a diagonal gradient, so transposing the whole operator  passed silently — the name asserted a convention the fixture could not see. The tensor divergence  was worse: it had no analytic oracle at all, its only value fixture being a tensor of nine  identical components (hence A equiv A^top, every index permutation invisible), with every  other assertion self-referential — threaded against serial, device against host, compressed kernel  against stored — each comparing one contraction against itself. Both now carry closed-form oracles  over deliberately asymmetric fixtures, and both were verified by re-applying the mutation and  confirming the new tests fail, which is the check that distinguishes a real test from a vacuous  one.\nThe general lesson is about fixtures rather than counts: a symmetric fixture cannot see an  asymmetric defect, and a self-referential assertion cannot see a defect shared by both sides. When  an operation's convention is stated in a name or a docstring, the fixture must be able to falsify  it.\nThe parity baseline is derived, not enumerated (2026-07-29). The mutation audit's structural  half asked what would fail automatically if a new operation shipped with no tests, and the answer  was: almost nothing. Of eight enforcement layers only the documentation gate  (checkdocs = :exports) reacts to a new export at all; every other layer is a hand-maintained  list. The parity baseline is the sharpest case, because it is the one that claims to enforce  invariant 1: it counts 28 enumerated generic names filtered to two modules, so decisions 48, 49  and 51 each added public surface — map!/map, cov, the kernel builders — and neither the 206  nor the 122 moved.\nBeside it now sits a derived baseline: one frozen count per module, over every function that  module owns, counted across the methods defined in that module. Any new method anywhere moves a  number, which must then be refrozen deliberately — the pass-audit protocol applied to the method  table. Verified by adding an untested generic to Reductions and confirming the derived baseline  fails while both enumerated ones stay silent. The enumerated baseline stays, because it localizes a  change to the generic that moved; the derived one exists to make sure something moves.\nThe distinction generalizes past this one file. A guard that enumerates what it checks can only be  as complete as the last person's memory, and this package's guards are otherwise good enough that  the gap is invisible: the same audit found the allocation ledger, the traffic ledger, the JET call  list and the canary suite all hand-enumerated, and the dispatch-pair scan covering three of eight  modules. Prefer deriving the set to be checked from the code — methods, names, the export  list — over writing it down, wherever the derivation is possible.\nFour mechanical guards, each proven by mutation (2026-07-29). Decision 53 answered what  forces a future addition to be tested — almost nothing — and this is the response. Every guard  replaces a convention with something that fails on its own, and none was believed until a  deliberate defect was injected and the guard was watched to fail.\nDerived parity. The enumerated baseline names 28 generics in two modules, so decisions 48, 49  and 51 each added public surface invisibly. A derived count per module, over every function that  module owns, now moves whenever any method does. Proof: an untested generic in Reductions  fails it while both enumerated baselines stay green.\nLedger completeness. allocations.jl and pass_audit.jl were hand-written tables; each now  derives its requirement from the export list, and an exemption must carry a reason. Proof: on  its first run it found four operations audited nowhere while their own siblings were —  subtract! beside add!, skew!/dev! beside sym!, norm! beside det!.\nReachability. which(...) answers \"what would run for these types\", not \"does the public  entry point get there\" — the gap decision 47's dead loop lived in. A subprocess now gives each  gated CPU twin a throwing override and calls the public operation. Proof: hoisting the  generic kernel branch above the family branches makes two twins dead code; the probe fails on  exactly those two while every which(...) assertion stays green.\nGenerated sweeps. Metadata propagation is checked over four folds for every field-producing  operation, with order-carrying argument slots varied independently — the sampling the rest of  the suite lacks, since every other fixture builds field, point-values and plan from one R and  so runs down the diagonal of a cube. And the analytic oracles are generated over a fixture family  that is asymmetric by construction. Proof: dropping the layout in materialize_kernel or in  similar_field's vector arity fails the first; transposing gradient's packing or contracting  divergence's wrong index fails the second. All four survived the entire suite beforehand.\nTwo lessons generalize past the guards themselves. A guard needs a guard: the reachability  probe first reported a live twin as dead, because the override was less specific than the method  it meant to replace — so it now asserts each override actually owns a method, separating \"stale  probe\" from \"dead twin\". And generate where the oracle is free. Metadata and asymmetry cost  nothing per case, which is why those two sweeps are generated; an analytic oracle costs one rule  per operator, not per case, which is why that one is generated too. What genuinely resists  generation is small: a claim with no value oracle at all, and the ambient method table (whether  Base supplies a fallback that swallows an intended absence — the extrema leak of decision 52).\nOracle provenance is an enumerated axis (2026-07-29). §1 of docs/TESTING.md has always  opened with the bar — verify against independent ground truth, never against the code's own  other path — and nothing enforced it. So weighted_inner shipped and lived its whole life  without a single direct value: every assertion compared it to inner, to laplacian, to  gradient, or to a sharp band applied by convolve. Each of those is a real test and each  catches real defects; together they establish that weighted_inner is correct exactly insofar as  four other operators are.\nThe mutation audit of decision 52 could not see this, and the reason is worth recording, because  it is the same shape as the coverage-axes gap decision 43 closed. That audit asked would a  defect be caught? — and for weighted_inner the answer is yes, because a relational chain  terminating in an anchored reference does catch defects. The question it never asked is does  this operation have an oracle that is independent of other package operations? An axis nobody  enumerates is invisible to a review, and a review is where this one was supposed to be caught.\nEvery public value-returning operation now carries a recorded provenance in  test/oracle_registry.jl — analytic, independent, property, relational, or structural — with the  file its strongest oracle lives in. Two assertions do the work: an operation with no entry fails  the suite, and an operation classified :relational fails it too. The second is the standing  bar rather than a snapshot; the set is empty today and the assertion is what keeps it empty.  Proof: a new exported operation in Reductions, tested nowhere, fails the registry.\nThe registry's own domain was the next instance, found within the hour. It derived the surface  from names(GBGPeriodicFields), and the package at the time deliberately re-exported none of the generics it  extends rather than declares (the Statistics and LinearAlgebra ones have since been — the  export policy in §2) — so map/map! (decision 48), mean/std/var/cov (49), the  order statistics, and the field arithmetic + - * / were all invisible to it, as they had been to  both ledgers. Scanning module namespaces instead reached those, and still missed inv (decision  50), whose method is defined under the qualified name Base.inv and therefore creates no binding  to scan. The derivation that holds walks the loaded modules and asks each function whether a  package module owns one of its methods — the one question whose answer cannot depend on how the  definition was spelled. Domain 58 → 103 → 113.\nWidening it immediately paid: the traffic ledger parsed its row labels by splitting on whitespace,  so \"map!(det) tensor→scalar\" recorded Symbol(\"map!(det)\") and four more rows recorded phantoms  naming nothing. The entry for map! read as unaudited while two rows for it sat in the file. Both ledgers now  assert that every captured name resolves to a real package function — the decision-54  a guard needs a guard lesson, applied a second time to a second guard.\nThis is the fourth instance of one pattern, and the pattern is now the point. Decision 43 found  a missing coverage axis, decision 48 shipped a bug through an axis waved off as not applicable,  decision 53 found the guards enumerating what they check, and this one finds a review enumerating  what it asks. The defect is never the individual gap — it is that the list of things to check was  written by hand and believed. Derive the list from the code, and make the missing entry fail.\nAn external static audit, triaged and answered (2026-07-30). A manual static review of src/,  ext/, test/, docs/ and benchmark/ at commit 2c6ff752 filed 57 findings. It was read as  evidence rather than as a work list: a sample was verified against the code first, and that sample  already showed three failure modes — stale (its benchmark part predates the same day's  benchmark commits), severity-inflated, and understated. The last mattered most, so it is  worth naming: what the report called a \"partial, meaningless contraction\" in inner was an  out-of-bounds read under @inbounds that returned a plausible number because the memory beyond  the array happened to be zero.\nFour findings were rejected as recorded trade-offs rather than defects: manual-only CI for the  package tests and for the canary lane (a deliberate cost decision after the free tier was  exhausted), and the absence of aarch64 and GH200 measurements (already PERFORMANCE.md §7 gap  7). Those are real limitations; none is news, and re-enabling push CI would reverse an explicit  decision.\nThe user's rule governed the rest: no silent failures and no silent corruptions. That decided  every open question. Where an operation returned a plausible wrong value it was made to throw;  where it already failed loudly but in the wrong place, the check moved to the entry point; and  where the honest answer was \"this is not supported\", the boundary was documented and pinned by a  test rather than enforced by a constructor.\nCompatibility became one protocol, not seven checks. _context_grid / _context_layout are  generics that every participant in a spectral operation answers — fields and multiplier sets in  Fields, all three kernel classes in Convolution, the NUFFT plan in Interpolation — so a  single predicate compares any two of them without either module knowing the other's field names.  Storage family joined the field domain check, expressed as the array type's name so it costs  nothing at run time. The audit proposed a context descriptor struct; dispatch does the same work  without a new type.\nThe tensor-order ceiling is R < 3 (writing R for a section's tensor order), documented rather than enforced (user-decided). The  reductions, map and the decompositions serve R le 2; storage, HDF5 persistence, the vector-space  operations, inner/l2norm and the spline transfer serve higher orders, and general R was  deferred to ship the second-order surface a pseudospectral solver needs. Enforcement at the  constructor was considered and rejected: it would break the R=3 paths that legitimately work, and  it buys nothing, because above the ceiling every entry point either works or throws — verified,  not assumed. A contract lane pins exactly that property, so the boundary is visible and an  operation that ever starts returning a plausible wrong value there fails a test.\nOne finding is confirmed and deliberately not fixed. A fused pass reads element n and  writes element n, which makes sym!(A, A) safe and makes two different views overlapping at  different base indices unsafe — cross! on views offset by one along an axis gives the wrong  answer, serially and threaded. A mightalias guard would reject sym!(A, A) itself, whose target  and source component views already have different footprints on one parent, and decision 39 made  that case guard-free after measurement. (FALSE, per decision 59: the target (ij) and source  (ji) views select disjoint memory, so mightalias is false and would not reject the call.  No guard is still the right answer, for decision 59's different reason. Marked here because this  belief deferred the defect for a day.) Rather than ship a check that might break a measured,  load-bearing property, the contract is now stated precisely in the docstring and the failing case  is recorded here.\nA crash the audit did not find turned up while testing its alias claim. eachindex of a  non-contiguous view is CartesianIndices, not a range, and the fused executor's partitioner had a  method only for ranges — so an operation on a view-backed destination threw from inside the  scheduler at eight threads and succeeded at one. Storage is a generic AbstractArray by invariant  6, so that is a legitimate operand, and thread-count dependence is precisely what  test/threading.jl exists to catch. It was invisible because every fixture in that file is backed  by a plain Array — the fixture-blindness of decision 52, in a new place.\nThe generalizable lesson is about the denominator again. The operation materialize_kernel was missing for  an exported kernel class while every completeness ledger read it as covered, because the ledgers  count operation NAMES and the generic had methods. The fix that matters is not the method; it is  that the sweep now takes the subtype set, derived from the module namespace, so a new class  fails until it is given a specimen.\nStoredKernel — the kernel role, made explicit where it matters (2026-07-30, user-decided).  A ScalarFieldSpectral serves two roles: a field in spectral representation, and the storage class  for a kernel held as one coefficient per mode. That doubling is what the SpectralKernel union  exists to express, and for applying a kernel it is exactly right — the coefficients are the  coefficients, whatever the object means.\nIt stops being right for operations whose meaning depends on the role. Decision 56 added inv on  a stored kernel so a mixed compressed/stored product could be inverted, and in doing so defined the  coefficientwise reciprocal of a field in spectral representation — which is a nonlinear operation  on a field, exactly the class invariant 1 excludes there. The value returned is not wrong; the  method's existence is, because a user writing inv(f) for a field gets an operation the spec says  the package does not have.\nThe fix is a marker rather than a restriction, because the ambiguity has a natural resolution:  passing a field as a kernel argument already declares the role. So StoredKernel(f) states it  where a caller means it, inv is defined on the marker instead of on the field type, and  composition wraps automatically — convolve(compressed, f) yields a product whose stored factor is  a StoredKernel, so a mixed product still inverts with nothing asked of the caller. Application is  untouched: the union still accepts a bare field everywhere a kernel is taken, so nothing that  worked before needs a wrapper. (Superseded by decision 60, which is precisely where that  stopped being true: a bare ScalarFieldSpectral no longer dispatches as a kernel, so code that  passed one now does need StoredKernel — the compatibility this entry preserved was given up  deliberately, for the type to carry the role.)\nTwo options were weighed and rejected. Restricting inv to the compressed classes was cheapest —  the only consumer of the stored method is the mixed-product path — but it leaves a kernel that  exists only as coefficients, read from HDF5 or measured, with no inverse at all. Documenting the  role and keeping the method was cheaper still, and the reason it loses is worth stating: the risk  there is not a user harmed but the invariant eroded, and \"the public method set is the spec\" is  only true while the method set is kept honest.\nThe marker is also the shape the kernel protocol wants long-term. With a stored class that is a  real type, the SpectralKernel union could collapse to the abstract supertype and every  kernel-taking entry point would take one type. That is a breaking change and is not made here; it  is now a one-step move rather than a redesign. (Superseded by decision 60, which made exactly  that move: SpectralKernel is now an abstract supertype and every entry point names the one type,  so the union this entry reasons about no longer exists.)\nThe subtype sweep of decision 56 earned itself immediately: adding the class failed that testset  until it was given a specimen, which is precisely the moment to notice a new class needs the  class-wide methods.\nThe context-mismatch matrix — a derived denominator for the negative surface (2026-07-30,  user-directed). The suite already held ~94 negative assertions before the audit, so the gap was  never \"no negative tests\". Every one of them was written by hand, which makes a negative surface  complete only until someone adds an argument slot — and the audit's source findings were almost  all SEMANTIC mismatches, structurally indistinguishable from valid input, which is exactly the  class nobody thinks to write a case for.\nDecision 56 closed those findings but closed them the same way: contracts.jl is 72 assertions  and two loops, a hand-picked table. The instruction here was to generate all the combinations  rather than choose representatives, and the design that achieves it is two tiers, each with a  denominator read out of the code.\nTier 1, the protocol product. The participant set is methods(_context_grid), and every  ordered pair is checked against every mismatch axis in both argument orders. Adding a participant  adds a row and a column; adding one without a builder fails a completeness assertion rather than  silently sitting out. Tier 2, the entry-point product. Every method the package owns is  scanned for a context-carrying parameter, giving (operation, carrier-kind) pairs — the right unit,  since α/β variants of one call reach one check. Each pair must be invoked or exempted with a  reason.\nThe two are not redundant, and the mutations prove which is which. Neutering the shared  predicate fails Tier 1. Removing one entry point's check while the predicate stands leaves Tier 1  entirely green and fails Tier 2. A predicate nobody calls and a caller with no predicate are  different defects, and one tier sees each.\nThe derivation caught seven combinations the hand-written table had missed, which is the whole  argument for deriving it. Five were kernel arities — convolve!, divergence!,  helmholtz_projection!, combine!, weighted_inner — invisible because a slot declared as the  SpectralKernel UNION is a subtype of neither of its halves, so a plain <: test walks past it.  Two more were the allocating kernel forms, invisible one level further up: SpectralKernel{T} is a  UnionAll wrapping that union, so it is not even isa Union. (Superseded by decision 60: the  union became an abstract supertype, so a plain <: test now finds those slots — the derived  denominator this entry builds is unchanged, but the invisibility it works around is gone.) Both misses were in the detector,  found only because the completeness assertion compares what it derived against what was written by  hand and complains in both directions. A one-directional check would have reported success.\nThe axis worth naming is domain: two grids of identical shape and different extent. Every  array is the same size, every structural check passes, and only a context comparison can tell them  apart — which is why the operations that lacked one returned plausible wrong answers for months  rather than failing.\nA section owns its storage, which is what makes invariant 9 true (2026-07-30, user-decided).  The exhaustive suite's first lane crossed a shared storage pool over every in-place operation and  found fourteen that returned a wrong answer, silently, across three execution routes. The cause is  one sentence: a fused pass reads element n and writes element n, which is safe when the  destination IS an input and unsafe when one section's element n is another's element  n + m. Two overlapping views at different base offsets are exactly that.\nThree fixes were available and two were wrong. A mightalias guard on every pass would work but  puts a per-call test on paths decision 39 measured as guard-free, and leaves invariant 9 needing a  caveat forever. A contiguity requirement looks right and is not: reshape(view(flat, 2:end), …)  is densely strided, overlaps another dense view at an offset, and still corrupts — verified, not  reasoned. Contiguity is the wrong property; ownership is the right one.\nSo the constructors reject storage with a SubArray anywhere in its parent chain. Two sections can  then share memory only by being backed by the same array, which is the case the passes already  handle, so the class is not guarded against — it is unconstructible. The check is on ownership  rather than layout, so Array, CuArray, ROCArray and a reshape of any of them all pass and  invariant 6 is untouched. Note that isa DenseArray would NOT have served: it is false for a  contiguous host view and true for the identical CUDA construction, so it answers differently per  backend.\nThe cost is one foreclosed pattern: a consumer keeping solver stages in one buffer and slicing  view(stages, :, :, :, :, k). Those slices are contiguous and disjoint, hence safe, and they are  now refused. Accepted deliberately, on the principle that relaxing a restriction later is safe  and tightening one is breaking — if a consumer needs it, permitting views that are full in the  three base dimensions is a small non-breaking follow-up, and that class provably cannot produce a  base-index offset.\nTwo claims made while investigating this were wrong and are recorded so they are not repeated. \"A  view-backed field cannot be transformed\" — false; only the allocating to_spectral rejects one,  while to_spectral! accepts it, which is why views sat in the worst possible state: working for  the algebra, working for one transform, failing for the other, and corrupting under overlap. And  \"a mightalias guard would reject sym!(A, A)\" — false; the component views for target (ij)  and source (ji) select disjoint memory, so mightalias is false on them. That mistaken  belief is why the defect was deferred for a day.\nThe invariant now says what is true rather than what was hoped: aliasing is safe by construction  because a section owns its storage. The survey in test/exhaustive/ checks both halves — that no assignment  changes an answer, and that the refusal has not reopened — and the second is mutation-verified:  removing one constructor's check makes the lane name every reopened case and turn its exit from  0 to 2.\nA field in spectral representation is not a kernel (2026-08-03, user-decided). SpectralKernel  was a union of ScalarFieldSpectral — a scalar field carrying one coefficient per mode — and an  abstract AbstractSpectralKernel covering the compressed classes. Three things were wrong with  that. The abstract type read broader than the union while being strictly narrower, so its name  misled at every signature. A StoredKernel marker had to exist purely so inv could tell a kernel  from a field, since the two hold identical data. And convolve became asymmetric in a way  commutativity forbids: convolve(field, kernel) applied the kernel while  convolve(kernel, field) composed, because argument position was the only thing left carrying  the role.\nThe union is gone. SpectralKernel{T} is now the abstract supertype of four concrete classes —  StoredKernel (one coefficient per mode), SeparableKernel (per-axis factors), RadialKernel (the  real function sigma of lvert krvert^2, held as a closure), and ProductKernel (two  factors held unmultiplied). Every signature naming kernel::SpectralKernel{T} is unchanged,  materialize_kernel returns a StoredKernel, and both argument orders of convolve apply and  agree bitwise at every tensor order. Composing two convolution operators is convolving their  kernels, so composition keeps the same verb and is now unambiguous by type rather than by position.\nA field of coefficients is wrapped once, where the kernel is built. Measured at 64^3, the  wrapper costs 144 bytes at construction and zero per call, with _kernel_data inlining to a  getfield — so the cost objection to requiring the wrapper does not exist. Two package methods  went (Fields 105 → 103, refrozen with the reason), and the materializing  squared_wavenumber_field was removed: lvert krvert^2 is a radial function, so  radial_kernel(identity, multipliers) is the same object at a closure's cost rather  than O(N^3) coefficients.\nThe rename's one hazard was caught by the pass audit and by no other layer. A guard reading  kernel isa AbstractSpectralKernel MEANT \"compressed\"; renaming made it true for the stored class  too, routing it off the fused pass — vector traffic 3M reads against a frozen ideal of M. Every  correctness oracle passed throughout, because the answer stayed right and only the traffic changed.  That is exactly the hole decision 36 built the element-traffic ledger to cover.\nA radial kernel's coordinates are part of its type (2026-08-04, user-directed). The product of  two radial symbols is a radial symbol — always, mathematically. Yet  convolve(::RadialKernel, ::RadialKernel) returned RadialKernel or ProductKernel depending on  an === test of the two operands' multiplier objects, so inference saw a union and the result type  turned on object identity, which is invisible at a call site. The exhaustive survey reported it as a  type-stability finding in every configuration it built.\nThe conservatism was not wrong. A composite keeps ONE multiplier set, so collapsing two kernels that  evaluate their symbols against different wavenumbers would silently apply the second symbol at the  first's lvert krvert^2. Equal grid and layout do not rule that out, because the six-array  FieldSpectralMultipliers constructor validates the arrays' LENGTHS and not their values — the  escape hatch a sheared, time-dependent (Rogallo) coordinate set would use, which §12 keeps  consumer-side. So the type RadialKernel did not carry the guarantee its name implies, and the  runtime check was compensating for that at every composition.\nThe fix puts the guarantee in the type. FieldSpectralMultipliers gains a final Canonical type  parameter recording whether its arrays were derived from the grid (the convenience constructor)  or supplied by a caller (the six-array form). Two canonical sets of the same concrete type, on a  matching grid and layout, hold identical values by construction — same derivation, same inputs — so  the collapse is unconditional there and is selected by dispatch. Every other pairing, including a  custom set on either side and two canonical sets of different array types (different backends), keeps  both operands. All four cases now infer a concrete type, and the parameter goes last so that  every bare ::FieldSpectralMultipliers annotation and the one {T} accessor are untouched.\nOne behaviour change, and it is the one the mathematics asks for: two separately-built canonical  multiplier sets used to give a ProductKernel and now collapse to a RadialKernel. They hold equal  values, so the collapsed form is correct; the old answer was merely conservative. The lane in test/contracts.jl  asserted the conservative behaviour and now asserts the collapse, alongside a custom set reaching the  retained form — the canonical-versus-custom axis, added to docs/TESTING.md §4 in the same change.\nThe type stability is the smaller half of this. A composition happens once, outside any loop, and a  two-wide union costs nothing measurable there. What the change buys is that RadialKernel now means  what it says.\nAny kernel class may weight a reduction, and a complex weight is refused (2026-08-05,  user-directed). The reduction weighted_inner(f, g, w) — the multiplier-weighted inner product  langle f w grangle of decision 27 — took its weight as Union{StoredKernel, RadialKernel},  two of the four storage classes a kernel comes in (decision 60). It now takes any SpectralKernel.\nThe restriction was never a constraint. Every class implements the _kernel_data protocol as an  instantiated broadcastable — coefficients for the stored class, a lazy per-axis product for the  separable one, a lazy symbol evaluation for the radial one, a lazy product of two for the composite —  and the fused reduction never asks which class it holds. Running its body unchanged against the two  excluded classes gave results bitwise identical to the accepted route, before any code was  changed. The Union was a signature written narrowly and never revisited.\nTwo rationales for it were recorded over time, and both were wrong. The first said a separable  kernel's factors are not real in general — contradicted by assignment_window, whose  operatornamesinc^2 factors are real. The second said a magnitude-dependent weight does not  factor per axis, which is a true statement about the radial class that was accepted and says  nothing about the separable one that was not. Both are now recorded beside the code as dead, because  the failure mode here is inventing a third: when a restriction has no reason, each attempt to state  one produces a sentence that is locally plausible and describes something else.\nA complex weight now throws. langle f w grangle is an inner product only for a real w,  and the previous code took real(w) elementwise — so translation_kernel, the one complex builder,  would have been silently accepted with its phase read as a cosine attenuation. The rejection is made  where the type decides it, through Base.Broadcast.combine_eltypes on the kernel's data (plain  eltype of an unmaterialized Broadcasted is Any and does not infer). The branch is on a  compile-time-known element type and folds away: measured at 128^3, old-versus-new ratios are  1.001 (radial), 0.997 (separable) and 1.000 (stored) — free, which was the condition set for adding  it at all.\nStoredKernel is the one exemption, and it is a genuine one. Its storage is Complex{T} by  construction, a spectral field's being complex, so its type cannot distinguish a real multiplier from  a complex one — a materialized spectral_cutoff and a materialized translation_kernel have the  same type. Separating them needs a full extra pass over the coefficients, which this reduction's  single-pass contract does not permit, so that class keeps the documented real-part behaviour. The  rule is therefore the check is made wherever the type can decide it, which is a statement about  what is knowable for free rather than an inconsistency to tidy away later. Settled by the user  (2026-08-05): the exemption stands and the O(N) scan is declined. Reaching that path takes a  deliberate act — hand-building a stored kernel's coefficients, or explicitly materializing a complex  one — and a consumer who does either knows what they are doing; paying a full coefficient scan on  every call to protect a deliberate act is the wrong trade.\nCovered in test/reductions.jl (each class bitwise-equal to its own materialized form over  R = 0 1 2 in the tensor order R; the complex refusal on the separable class, on a composition carrying it, and the  stored exemption), pinned allocation-free for all three newly admitted classes in  test/allocations.jl — the assertion that would catch a widening which quietly reached for  materialize_kernel — and given device parity in test/gpu/suite.jl, where the composite class  nests two lazy Broadcasteds and is the case that would resurface the instantiate this reduction  already needed for AMDGPU's mapreducedim!. Both tests were verified by mutation: disabling the  realness check, and dropping a factor from the separable kernel's data, each fail the suite. The  Reductions module baseline moves 119 → 123 — the widening itself is a signature change rather than  new methods, and the four are the two internal helpers it needed, at two methods each.\nStorage families are crossed by adapt, the ecosystem's verb, and precision is not crossed at  all (2026-08-10, user-directed). Invariant 14 makes every operation refuse a pair of sections  that disagree in storage family, rather than promoting or transferring one to meet the other. That  refusal was always half a design: it names what the package will not do implicitly without offering  the explicit operation that does it. The complement has now shipped, and it is not a new verb.\nThe interface is Adapt.jl's adapt. Adapt exists for exactly this — take a struct holding  arrays, return the same struct holding another array type — and CUDA, AMDGPU, oneAPI, Metal,  GPUArrays and KernelAbstractions all speak it. The package defines adapt_structure for the six  field types, the three point-value types, PointSet, FieldSpectralMultipliers, the four spectral  kernel classes and FieldTransformPlans, and re-exports adapt so using GBGPeriodicFields  supplies it. Every method rebuilds through the same allocator the rest of the package uses, so the  grid, the Fourier layout, the origin and the tensor order propagate by construction rather than  by being remembered — the defect that made map drop the layout on every permuted fold  (decision 48).\nA package-owned verb was considered and declined. It would have been a pure synonym: with  precision excluded, adapt(CuArray, u) takes exactly one parameter, and a second spelling is a  second thing to keep consistent. The decisive argument is a dependency one. Because  adapt_structure dispatches on a generic target, CUDA supplies the CuArray storage rule and  AMDGPU the ROCArray one, so the package gains both vendors without depending on either —  preserving the rule that vendor stacks stay test-only. A hand-rolled convert_storage would need  that same generic dispatch and would be adapt under another name. Adapt itself is a  dependency-free package already reachable through KernelAbstractions, and pure Julia, so it is  safe for the aarch64 target of decision 37.\nPrecision is deliberately excluded, and the reason is structural as well as prudential. The  prudential half is cu, which silently demotes Float64 to Float32: a transfer that changes what  a solver computes is the anti-pattern invariant 14 was written against. The structural half is that  precision is not the same kind of operation at all — Grid3D{T} stores the domain size, spacing and  origin as NTuple{3, T} and a field demands its grid share its element type, so changing  precision must rebuild the grid, where changing storage touches only the array. It remains recorded  future work.\nTransform plans are rebuilt, and they need nothing from the caller. An FFT plan is bound to its  backend and cannot be moved. It was first thought that adapting one would have to refuse and send  the user to the constructor — wrong, and the question that exposed it was whether a plan requires  both representations. It does not: the constructor reads no data, only shape, element type and  family, and a plan outlives the arrays it was planned against (verified on FFTW and cuFFT). A  FieldTransformPlans stores its own grid and layout and carries T (its element type) as a type  parameter, so it can allocate its own witnesses, plan, and release them. (It also carried R, its  tensor order, when this was written; decision 76 removed it — a transform is a property of the  base, so one plan pair now serves every rank and the rebuild uses order-0 witnesses.) The struct now also records the planner  request it was built with, because a rebuild would otherwise fall back to ESTIMATE — which  finding (h) of docs/PERFORMANCE.md records as capable of badly mis-planning a single-precision  transform. Planner flags are an FFTW concept and a device rebuild ignores them (cuFFT rejects  them with an unrelated-looking pointer error) while retaining them, so a later move back to the  host restores the original intent.\nAdapting to the family one is already in is the identity, following Adapt and convert: it  returns the same object, sharing storage. That is why the gate drives the metadata assertions with a  custom adaptor mapping a host array to a distinct host array — adapting to Array would exercise  nothing. The GPU suite's three hand-written device-twin helpers were deleted in favour of the  shipped interface, so the suite can no longer pass by testing a private imitation of it.\nThe derived per-module baselines move (decision 53's protocol, refrozen with the reason in the  same commit): Fields 103 → 115 — four adapt_structure methods and the eight internals they need  (_storage_prototype, the two three-method order-dispatch families, and _is_canonical) —  Convolution 79 → 83 for one method per kernel class, and Points 207 → 209 for the point-value  and PointSet methods.\nA pre-existing limitation surfaced on the way, unrelated to this change: cuFFT rejects any  non-strictly-increasing FFT region, so a permuted RealFourierLayout (decision 41) cannot be  planned on CUDA at all — only the default (1 2 3) fold works there. It is recorded in  docs/TESTING.md §4 rather than fixed, since it is a vendor constraint and the permuted folds are a  CPU-side generality.\nThe preserving scratch arity of to_physical! (2026-08-13, requested by the consuming solver  GBGHomogeneousTurbulence (\"IT\") in its third request round). The  inverse real transform may consume its input as scratch (the transform input contract above:  FFTW destroys it, some device backends happen not to), so the package offered two contracts —  the destructive in-place to_physical!(physical, spectral, plans) and the preserving-but-  allocating to_physical(spectral, plans) — and the preserving allocation-free member was  missing. Every consumer whose spectral coefficients are a state it keeps (a spectral solver's  resolved fields, or any field recovered to physical space more than once) was hand-composing  copyto! plus the destructive form; the requesting solver did exactly that in two wrappers and a  phase-shift chain that recovers twice from one source.\nThe new member is a fourth-argument arity of the existing generic, not a new verb:  to_physical!(physical, spectral, plans, spectral_scratch) copies the coefficients into the  caller-supplied scratch, transforms from there, and returns with spectral bitwise-unchanged and  nothing allocated; the scratch's coefficients are undefined on return, exactly as the  three-argument form leaves its source — stated that way deliberately, because the destruction  itself is backend-divergent and the contract must not be. The arity form follows the house  convention for \"same operation, more contract\" (the kernel-composed operator arities of  decision 32, the α/β accumulation forms): the operation is still the transform, and the trailing  argument buys the preservation contract, checked by dispatch — the scratch must be a spectral  field of the same element type and tensor order, which a keyword could only check at runtime.\nAliasing is refused, not defined: spectral_scratch sharing storage with spectral would  make the copy a no-op and the transform would then consume the very source the argument exists to  preserve — a silent contract violation, the worst kind. Same-array is the only sharing the  ownership rule of decision 59 leaves constructible, so the one cheap === check on the storage is  exhaustive. The copy is a plain copyto! over the storage, deliberately: the price of  preservation is exactly one extra read and write of the coefficients, and nothing else — a  benchmark row prices it against the destructive form directly.\nThe to_spectral! twin is deliberately absent. Not because the forward transform is safe —  the input contract records it as backend-divergent too — but because the requesting consumer  established that every forward transform's source in its loops is consumable by design, so the  twin would be speculative structure — the §2 rule, not invariant 1: as decision 32 says of the  identical shape, an invariant-1 absence is a mathematical statement a user can rely on, while this  one is merely unbuilt. It becomes a one-method addition the day a consumer needs it. Tested by a bitwise preservation check against the  three-argument form's contract, bitwise equality with the allocating form (whose own oracle is  the independent DFT sum, terminating the chain independently), the aliasing and context  refusals, a zero-allocation pin, and GPU parity; the Fields baseline moves 115 → 116.\nSpectralKernel is exported (2026-08-15, from the round-6 documentation panel). The four  concrete kernel storage classes — StoredKernel, SeparableKernel, RadialKernel and  ProductKernel — were exported while the supertype they share was not, so a user who wanted to  write a method or a type annotation against the family had to name  GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.SpectralKernel explicitly. That is the type appearing in the public  signature of convolve!, of the kernel-composed divergence! and helmholtz_projection!, and of  weighted_inner, so the export makes the type public exactly where its methods already are.\nThe panel's larger finding was declined, and the reason is worth recording. A hat proposed  moving the protocol — the abstract type and the two generics _kernel_data and  _check_kernel_compatibility — out of Fields, on the argument that the package had met the same  structural problem before and solved it by extracting FiberSections. Three placements were  considered against the alternative of leaving it. FiberSections is refused on the architecture's  own mathematics: its defining property is base-agnosticism, and a spectral kernel is one  multiplier per Fourier mode of a specific grid under a specific layout — base-bound and  representation-specific, the horizontal side of the §2 split rather than the vertical one. A  dedicated module is refused as disproportionate: the protocol is three declarations with no  dependencies and no bodies, where FiberSections was extracted for shared content — the fiber  types, _assign!, the whole vertical-algebra codegen. And moving the protocol down into  Convolution — which the include order permits, since Convolution and DifferentialOperators  have no dependency edge in either direction — is refused on dependency direction: it would make  DifferentialOperators and Reductions depend on a heavyweight peer to obtain an abstract  type, where today all three depend only on the foundation. A protocol in the foundation is what  keeps the consumers independent of one another.\nThe placement is also not an accident but the second instance of a recorded convention:  FieldSpectralMultipliers was moved into Fields from DifferentialOperators for the  identical reason. The shared context object and its compatibility protocol live in Fields; the  module that owns the mathematics owns the concrete types and the builders. No method is added, so  every parity baseline is unchanged.\nThe kernel builders drop their reference field (2026-08-15, from the same panel round). The  five builders took a reference field in spectral representation and a  FieldSpectralMultipliers, where the multipliers already carry everything the reference supplied.  Reading the bodies settles it: each used the reference for exactly field_scalar_type,  field_grid, field_layout and the compatibility check, and a FieldSpectralMultipliers stores  grid::Grid3D{T} and fourier_layout — so the grid, the layout and the element type are all in  it. Even the array backend came from the multipliers rather than the reference: the per-axis  factors are built by broadcasting over multipliers.squared_wavenumber_*, so the result already  lived in the multipliers' storage family.\nThe build-time check was itself the proof. _check_multipliers_compatibility compares the  scalar type, the grid and layout, and the storage family — which is to say it refused every pair  in which the reference could have told us anything the multipliers did not. An argument required  to agree with another argument in every observable respect carries no information.\nIt was a fossil, not a choice. Before decisions 50 and 51 these builders returned a  materialized field of coefficients, and allocating one needs a storage prototype of the right  shape, type and backend — which the reference was. Those decisions replaced the materialization  with per-axis factors and a closure, and shipped \"signatures unchanged\" to avoid breaking  consumers; the argument's only job went with the materialization and nobody noticed, because it  still type-checked and was still validated. The in-place translation_kernel! had already been  written in the target shape, taking the multipliers alone.\nThe audit covered every other reference argument and found no second instance. The  similar_* allocators are defined by their reference; _axis_wavenumber_arrays and  _nufft_index_map take one as a similar prototype, which nothing else supplies;  _nufft_deconvolution and the NUFFT constructor take the only field they are given. The  spectral anterpolate(reference, points, quantities, scheme) is the one arguable case — a  NUFFT scheme stores its own reference_grid and reference_layout — but its DirectDFT  sibling is a stateless singleton with no other source of grid, layout, backend or element type,  and both are methods of one verb under one scheme-agnostic contract, so the argument stays and  the mismatch is caught: the allocating form delegates to anterpolate!, which runs  _check_nufft_compatibility.\nConsequence for the negative surface. Each builder now takes exactly one context-carrying  argument, so there is no second context to disagree with at build time and the five move from  invocations to exemptions in test/context_entrypoints.jl. Nothing is lost — a kernel carrying a  foreign context is caught where it meets a field, which the nine (operation, kernel) rows  already cover — and translation_kernel!, which takes a kernel and multipliers, keeps its  two-carrier check. The gate moves 6541 → 6523: twenty assertions retire with the five pairs (a  matching case and three foreign ones each) and three replace one, the retired call now being  asserted to fail loudly rather than resolve. No method count changes; every parity baseline  holds.\nBase.similar is declined for the section allocators (2026-08-15, user-decided, from the same  panel round). A hat observed that similar_field and similar_point_values do what Base.similar  exists to do for an array-like wrapper — build compatible uninitialized storage from a reference —  and that no rationale for declining it was recorded anywhere, unlike the explicit \"we own the  types, so extending is not piracy\" note that justifies extending dot, cross and mean. The  observation is fair; the conclusion does not follow, and the names stay.\nThe second argument is the problem. Base.similar's four canonical forms are similar(A),  similar(A, ::Type{T}), similar(A, dims) and similar(A, ::Type{T}, dims): that positional  slot means an element type or a set of dimensions. Here it means the tensor order, carried as  a Val{R}, which is neither. Borrowing a name while breaking the convention that gives the name  its meaning is worse than a distinct name, which promises nothing.\nAnd its most idiomatic spelling would be a trap. similar(A, T) is the way a Julia  programmer changes element type, while changing element type is exactly what this package must  never do implicitly — invariant 14 refuses a mixed-precision pair rather than promoting it, and  decision 63 excluded precision from adapt for the same reason. So similar(u, Float32) would  read as a request for single precision, which is the one operation the design refuses to perform  quietly, and the better the reader knows Julia the more likely they are to write it. It is not  even a storage-level operation: Grid3D{T} holds the domain size, spacing and origin at the  element type, so a precision change rebuilds the grid.\nTwo smaller reasons complete it. The data form wraps storage the caller supplies, and  Base.similar never takes data, so half the family could not carry the name in any case and the  package would end up with both spellings. And a section is not an AbstractArray — these types  subtype AbstractFiberSection{T, R} — so similar's eltype-and-dims contract cannot express the  grid, layout, representation and tensor order that propagating is this operation's whole purpose.\nA narrow additive alternative was considered and declined with it: defining only  Base.similar(::AbstractFiberSection), the no-extra-argument form, which carries no semantic  collision and would let a newcomer's reflex work while similar(u, Float32) failed loudly. It  would still be a second spelling of one operation, which invariant 1 declines to add for  convenience. The reasoning now lives on both allocators' docstrings, where a reader meets the  name and asks the question.\nadapt keeps the canonical flag in the TYPE, not just in the value (2026-08-17). Moving a  FieldSpectralMultipliers between storage families returned an object whose last type parameter —  decision 61's Canonical — inference could not resolve, and RadialKernel and ProductKernel  inherited the imprecision through the multipliers type they carry. The rebuilt value was always  right, so nothing computed a wrong answer; what was lost is the point of putting the flag in the  type at all. Decision 61 did that so convolve can collapse two radial kernels over canonical  multipliers statically, and a non-concrete parameter turns that back into a run-time decision  for every kernel that has crossed the bus.\nThe cause is one line: adapt_structure read the flag with _is_canonical, an ordinary Bool,  and handed it to a constructor that lifts its value into new{…, canonical}. Every other  construction passes a literal, which inference folds, so the defect existed only on the one path  that forwards the flag from an existing object — which is exactly the path no unit test typed.  Binding Canonical in adapt_structure's own signature was tried first and measured  insufficient: constant propagation does not reach through the constructor's length validation.  The flag is therefore taken as Val{Canonical}, so it is in the type domain at the call rather  than lifted out of a value inside new. Four call sites, all in Fields, and no method is added,  so every parity baseline holds.\nVerification. test/adapt.jl asserts both halves for canonical and caller-supplied multipliers  and for all three kernel classes that can carry them: that the moved object's type is unchanged,  and that Base.return_types(adapt, …) is concrete. The value assertion alone passed  throughout the period the type assertion would have failed, which is why the test now makes both.  The gate moves 6523 → 6533.\nHow it was found. Not by a test — by the exhaustive survey, and only after its adaptor-slot  noise was cleared the same day. The finding sat under 42,336 cells reporting non-defects, which is  the argument for repairing an instrument rather than reading around it.\nadapt! — the storage move into a destination that already exists (2026-08-18). adapt  allocates its result, and for the return trip that allocation is most of the cost: finding (x)  measured a device-to-host move at 51 GiB/s against the 190 GiB/s the same transfer  reaches into a destination that already exists, on the same card and the same 256^3 Float64  field. The gap is not the bus — the outbound direction runs at approx 22 GB/s — it is that  the return trip allocates a fresh pageable host array and pays for the pages. A consumer pulling a  field back on any regular basis had no way to hoist that.\nMeasured into the table 2026-08-23, which sharpened the estimate. On the return trip adapt! is  3.48–3.66× the allocating form at 256^3 (both element types, scalar and vector); the first  estimate said \"about 4times\", from a bare-array probe. Outbound it is a wash  (10times), which is the diagnosis confirming itself — the device allocation is pooled and  cheap, so there was never a page cost to avoid in that direction. And the gain has a size  threshold: a 128^3 Float64 scalar (16.8 MB) shows 101times while the vector on  the same grid (50 MB) shows 348times, consistent with the cause being page faults on fresh  host pages, which bite only once an allocation is too large for the allocator's reused blocks.\nThe name is ours, and copyto! was rejected for a reason worth recording. Adapt.jl has no  in-place form — it exports adapt, WrappedArray, parent_type, unwrap_type and the  adapt_structure/adapt_storage hooks — so adapt! is a package-owned verb paired with a  re-exported one. Julia's standard verb for \"into a preallocated destination\" is copyto!, and it  is exactly what the implementation calls on the underlying arrays; it is the wrong public  spelling here because the package's only copyto! methods are the broadcast entry point. A  section-to-section copyto! that crossed storage families would cross where  destination .= source refuses under invariant 14 — the same two objects giving opposite  answers under two spellings, which is the ambiguity decision 60 removed rather than one to add.\nThe signature takes no target. adapt(to, x) needs one; adapt!(destination, source) does  not, because the destination already is the target family, so the two cannot disagree.  Everything except the family must match — element type, tensor order, grid, Fourier layout — and  the check is _check_adapt_target, which is _check_same_domain minus its backend clause;  reusing that check would refuse precisely the pair this verb is for. A representation mismatch has  no method rather than an error, since a physical field and a spectral one are not the same  section, and precision is not converted here for the same reason adapt does not convert it. A  same-family pair is an ordinary copy rather than an error, so generic code need not ask which side  of the bus it is on, and an aliased call is a guarded no-op.\nSections only, deliberately. adapt also moves a FieldSpectralMultipliers, a spectral  kernel and a FieldTransformPlans; those have no in-place spelling because they are built  rather than filled — moving plans replans the transforms and a RadialKernel carries a closure —  so there is no preallocated storage a destination could offer. That is an absence in invariant 1's  sense: stated, and not to be filled in for symmetry.\nVerification. The gate proves everything except the crossing, which on a host-only run cannot  exist: destination returned and filled, metadata untouched, every mismatch refused, the aliased  no-op, and — the assertion the feature stands on — zero allocation, with the destinations built  outside the measured call, since building one inside it measures the very allocation the operation  exists to avoid. The crossing itself is the CUDA suite's, both directions and the point layer with  it (373 → 382). A test/pass_audit.jl row pins the traffic at its ideal, one read per source  element and one write per destination element. Benchmark rows sit beside the allocating ones in  the storage class, because the comparison is the justification. Gate 6535 → 6574; Fields  116 → 119 (adapt!'s two field methods and _check_adapt_target) and Points 209 → 211 (the two  point methods), both refrozen with their reasons.\nThe radial-symbol operators allocate nothing, not merely less than a field (2026-08-23,  raised by the consuming solver with a reproduction). The operators laplacian!, inverse_laplacian!,  divergence! and exponential_convolve! each allocated 144 bytes per call, constant in the  grid. The cause is one line repeated: _squared_wavenumber_broadcast and  _wavenumber_broadcast_view built their reshape wrappers per invocation, and a reshape  allocates a 48-byte array header — three of them, every call. The consumer measured 1152 B per  RK3 step and 720 B per RK2 step, byte-exact multiples of 144, which is what a per-step constant  looks like from the outside.\nWhy the audit did not see it. test/allocations.jl pinned these under field_threshold,  which is deliberately generous because its job is to catch a materialized field — and 144 B  passes that trivially. So the guard said \"no field\" while CLAUDE.md said \"allocates nothing per  step\", and nothing in the tree could tell the two claims apart. A consumer counting bytes per step  could. The pin is now split: serial asserts zero, which is the claim; the threaded lane keeps  a bound, because Base.Threads allocates task state per _assign! that is kilobytes and  grid-independent. Both lanes assert something, since a testset that silently skips is how this  reappears.\nThe fix stores the orientation instead of rebuilding it. FieldSpectralMultipliers gains six  fields — each of its six vectors reshaped to (n,1,1), (1,n,1) or (1,1,n) — built once in the  constructor, aliasing the vectors rather than copying them (verified: same pointer, and a mutation  of the vector is visible through the view). The two helpers return the stored views, and every  caller reaches zero. The 1-D fields stay exactly as they were, because 87 sites read them — the KA  kernels take raw vectors, radial_spectrum takes their maximum, the separable builders  broadcast per axis — so this is additive, not a change of shape.\nIt cost six type parameters, and three sites match them positionally. _is_canonical,  adapt_structure (decision 68) and Convolution's static collapse of two radial kernels  (decision 61, which spells the canonical case as a trailing true) all broke at once when  Canonical moved six places — loudly, at load. A comment at the struct now names the three, and  both properties are re-verified: the collapse is still static and adapt's inference still  concrete.\nOne test was measuring the wrong thing and is now measuring the right one. Decision 51's storage  claim was asserted as summarysize(cutoff) < 1024, which broke at 1136. But that bound counted  the kernel plus the multipliers it references, whose per-axis vectors are O(N) — a  small-grid constant wearing the name of a property. The claim is \"a closure and three references\",  so it is now asserted as exactly that: the kernel references the caller's multipliers rather  than copying them, and what it adds on top of them is 152 bytes at every grid, compared across  two that differ by 512× in mode count.\nreality_residual — the contract the half-spectrum cannot enforce (2026-08-23, requested by  the consuming solver at low priority). A real field's spectrum satisfies  hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k), and the real-to-complex layout exploits that by storing  only half the modes — so for most modes the redundant partner is not stored and cannot disagree.  Two planes are the exception: the folded axis's zero plane, and its Nyquist plane when that axis  has even length, are each their own mirror, so within them both partners are stored and the  constraint becomes a relation a caller can break. The reality_residual diagnostic returns the largest  discrepancy lvert hat f(k) - overlinehat f(-k) rvert over those planes, zero exactly  when the coefficients are a real field's.\nThe gap it closes is a promise the type makes but does not guarantee. By invariant 2 a field  is a real physical object and the complex array is storage detail, so ScalarFieldSpectral means  \"the coefficients of a real field\" — and nothing enforced the symmetry that makes it true. Every  operation downstream is entitled to assume it and all of them keep working when it is false, which  is why the failure shows up as spectral diagnostics quietly disagreeing with physical images. The  consuming solver had documented the contract as far as it could: \"Coefficients written by hand  must respect the real-field layout's conjugate symmetry — it is not checked.\" This is what lets  that sentence change.\nIt reports rather than repairs, and the repair already exists. A physical round trip is the  projection onto a real field's spectrum, since the inverse real transform is exactly that  projector — measured: a residual of 0.728 returns to 0.0. So a repairing verb would be an  optimization of something reachable today, not a capability, and it would silently rewrite a  caller's data at the one boundary where the responsibility is explicitly theirs. The check is  O(N^2) — one or two planes — against the round trip's O(N^3 log N). The table shows that  shape rather than asserting it: on the CPU it runs 92 µs → 367 µs → 1.478 ms across  64^3/128^3/256^3, about fourfold per doubling, which is a plane's area; on the GPU it  is flat at 99–104 µs over the same range, a plane being too small to be anything but launch-bound.  The O(N^3) inner beside it goes 85 µs → 204 µs → 1.115 ms.\nA second vendor and a second CPU confirm both halves of that shape (measured 2026-08-23). The  RX 7900 XTX is flat at 238–244 µs across 64^3/128^3/256^3, the same launch-bound  plateau at a different height, and the i7-12700K grows 80 µs → 362 µs → 1.586 ms → 13.258 ms out to  512^3, fourfold per doubling as the area argument requires. The two profiles cross: at  64^3 the CPU is about three times faster than the AMD card, and by 256^3 the card is about  six times faster than the CPU, which is what a launch-bound cost meeting an area-bound one looks  like. A caller choosing where to run this should read the crossover rather than the peak.\nIt does not thread, and threading is not what it would need. The i7-12700K reads 80.2 µs at one  thread and 80.6 at eight, 1.586 ms against 1.588 at 256^3, and at 512^3 eight threads is  9.7% slower; the 9800X3D is likewise flat — zero scaling on both CPUs at every grid. The pass is  written as whole-array reverse, circshift and maximum over one or two planes, so it runs  through Base rather than through the package's threaded assignment primitive, and it structurally  cannot reach the fixed reduction tree, which lives in a module included after this one. But the  binding constraint is that the pass is allocation-bound: it allocates approx 6times the  plane bytes it reads — three temporaries over each stored plane, from the reversal, the shift, and  materializing the difference as the reduction's argument — so a threaded reduce alone would leave  the serial allocation in place, which is the likeliest reading of the 512^3 inversion. The fix  that would work is to fuse the mirrored index into one pass, after which threading follows for  free. That is worth doing only if the fused form is at least as fast serially, since this is an  O(N^2) diagnostic beside the O(N^3 log N) round trip it exists to let a caller avoid and  the GPU, being launch-bound, gains nothing either way. Recorded as a roadmap item rather than  settled here.\nTwo naming decisions. It is not called symmetrize!, though that is the requester's word and  TurTLE's precedent: the package already exports sym, sym!, symmetric_gradient and  symmetric_gradient! for the symmetric part of a tensor's fiber, and reusing the word for a  relation among spectral coefficients would put one name on two operations acting on different  structures. It is named for the reality condition instead, which is the vocabulary  convolve!'s docstring and decision 28 already use. And it does not zero the Nyquist planes as  TurTLE's symmetrize() does: invariant 5 keeps Nyquist content deliberately, so a violation there  is named like any other.\nVerification. An elementwise oracle written from the definition, sharing no code with the  implementation — which walks whole planes with reverse/circshift so it never indexes a device  array one element at a time, and is checked under allowscalar(false) in the CUDA suite. Crossed  over both element types, tensor orders 0/1/2, permuted folds, an odd folded axis (one  self-conjugate plane rather than two), the pair-consistency property (fixing the partner restores  zero), the self-paired corners (whose residual is twice the imaginary part), and the round-trip  repair. Fields 119 → 120, refrozen with its reason; the gate gains 170 assertions.\nSeveral weights, one traversal (2026-08-23, the consuming solver's highest-value request).  weighted_inner(f, (w₁, …, wₙ)) returns one scalar per weight from a single pass over the  coefficients. The requester's variance controller reduces the same field three times at every RK  stage — against unity, a forced band, and lvertkapparvert^2 — and it is not the  arithmetic it pays for but the return to the host: each scalar is a blocking readback, nine of  the ten synchronizations in a default run. One call is one readback.\nThe tuple is the whole design, and its spelling was not free. A tuple carries its length in  its type, so the return is a concrete NTuple{n, T} and a caller destructures it. Fusing needs an  accumulator per weight, and the obvious spelling — zip the operands — falls back to scalar  indexing and dies under allowscalar(false); broadcasting into an NTuple and reducing with a  tuple + stays on the device. That is measured, not assumed. In the committed table it beats the  three calls it replaces on every one of the ten lanes — 1.16–2.97× on the two CPUs (median  133) and 1.51–2.81× on the two GPUs — before counting the two synchronizations that stop  happening.\nThe CPU band was re-derived 2026-08-31; this entry's original 164–197times  \"uniformly\" is retired. Only 4 of 64 CPU cells now fall in that band, and one row (12700K,  256^3 Float64) spans 156 to 289times across its thread lanes where it read  171–172 flat. Nothing about the tuple form changed: finding (ac) moved the CPU leg onto  the threaded fixed tree, the path both compared rows traverse, so the ratio moved without  either arm being touched. The decision stands — the tuple form wins every cell — but a ratio  between two rows is not stable under a change to the path they share.\nThree things the spread says. The CPU figure sits near two rather than three because  only the coefficient reads are shared: each weight is still evaluated per mode, so a third of the  traffic was never the target. The NVIDIA card's Float64 rows are its weakest (1.36× at 256^3  against 1.96× for Float32), which is what a consumer card doing double precision at 1/64 rate  looks like — compute-bound work has less to gain from reading less. And the AMD card does not  show that split (1.86× against 2.48× at 256^3, and its best figures anywhere), which is the  same explanation seen from the other side rather than a second one.\nThe AMD figures were measured 2026-08-23, and the vendor risk they were checking did not  materialize. A tuple accumulator reduced with a custom non-+ operator is a different AMDGPU  code path from sum or mapreduce, and decision 62 had already found one place where that  vendor's mapreducedim! throws on a Broadcasted the CPU accepts — so no CUDA run could speak  for this one. It neither threw nor fell back: the ROCm suite reads 395 of 395, the same count as  CUDA, and the operation turns in its largest speedups of any lane. The Base.Broadcast.instantiate  that decision 62 made load-bearing is still what makes that true, and remains required.\nIt is one method on the existing generic, not a new operation. langle f w frangle at  several weights is the same mathematics at several w, so invariant 1 is satisfied by extending  weighted_inner rather than naming something new; a variadic form was declined because  weighted_inner(f, w₁, w₂) sits too close to the two-field weighted_inner(f, g, w) for a reader  to separate at a glance, where a tuple says these are weights structurally. The two-field  generalization langle f w grangle at several weights is deliberately absent: no consumer  asked, and invariant 1 does not add combinations for completeness. Every refusal the single-weight  form makes is made here in any position — a complex multiplier, a foreign context — and an empty  tuple returns () without touching the field.\nFusion is exact, and one thing a consumer will notice is not. The fused result is bitwise  identical to the separate calls, same arithmetic in the same order with only the pass shared.  But inner(f, f) and weighted_inner(f, unit) differ in the last ulps — 11times10^-14  relative, measured — because inner reduces on the threaded fixed tree and this pass does not.  That difference is pre-existing and belongs to the two routes, not to fusing; it is recorded  because a consumer replacing inner(f, f) with the unit-weight entry will see it and should know  which of the two it came from.\nThe stronger form the request floated — a device-resident scalar pack that convolve! accepts  as a coefficient source — is declined, on a concrete cost rather than a principle. The  requester's own arithmetic between the reduction and the use is  A = (varepsilon - R)(2k_f) guarded by an emptiness test: a division and a branch.  Keeping the scalars on the device would require alpha to accept a device handle across every  α/β signature in the package (invariant 10's contract, dozens of methods) and the consumer to  run a device-side select — the package dictating where a consumer's formula executes, which is  invariant 12's line. It is also diminishing: the tuple form already takes that run from ten  crossings to four, and the remaining three are one per stage.\nVerification. 344 gate assertions: fused bitwise-equal to the separate calls for every n from  1 to 5 over a heterogeneous tuple holding one weight of each storage class, both element types,  tensor orders 0/1/2, permuted folds; the order property (reversing the weights reverses the results  and nothing else, which is the guard against accumulating into the wrong slot); @inferred for the  concrete NTuple; both refusals in any position; the empty tuple; and the inner agreement at its  honest tolerance. On device, values match both the separate device calls and the host, under  allowscalar(false). Pinned allocation-light beside the single-weight row, with a benchmark pair —  fused against three separate — because that comparison is the claim. Reductions 123 → 124.\nBase-space motion: displace! and wrap! — the exponential map of the flat torus, and the  canonical representative (2026-08-24, requested by the consuming solver). The solver's  Lagrangian layer routed every field sampling through the point layer but performed every  trajectory update by unwrapping storage and broadcasting on raw arrays — the only raw-array  arithmetic on point-typed data in its tree — because moving the base is not expressible in the  fiberwise algebra, which by design maps sections to sections and never moves a point. The gap is  real and the operation is canonical: on a flat periodic base whose fiber carries one global  orthonormal frame, the tangent space at every base point is canonically the Cartesian  mathbb R^3 of the fiber, so displacing a point x_p along vector values v_j(p) held  over it, each section scaled by one real number c_j, is exactly x_p leftarrow x_p + sum_j c_j  v_j(p) — the exponential map of the flat metric, which carries a point along a vector and on  a flat torus is plain addition, needing no connection choices. The design  facts, each argued from that geometry:\nA named verb, never +. Positions are points of the domain manifold, not vectors; an algebra on PointSet would assert a linear structure the base does not have. The verb displace! is the one operation that legitimately couples base and fiber, and it crosses the position-versus-value role split by name rather than dissolving it.\nThe grid argument selects cover or torus, and the type structure forces that split. A PointSet carries no grid (a deliberate absence recorded on its docstring), so the grid-free arity cannot wrap: it is displacement in the universal cover — the unwrapped torus, where a coordinate keeps growing instead of folding back — which preserves winding — a mean-square-displacement diagnostic needs exactly that. The arity taking a Grid3D is displacement on the torus proper: the reduction to the fundamental domain is fused into the same pass, not a second traversal. Only domain_size and origin enter; the mesh plays no role.\nwrap! owns the periodic-box subtleties once. The canonical representative lands in the half-open o_a o_a + L_a) per axis — origin-aware, which the consumer's own four-line wrap was not (mod(X, L) assumes a zero origin) — with the excluded endpoint folded explicitly (floating-point mod can return the period itself: mod(-1e-20, 2π) === 2π, a pinned test), and the fold testing equality rather than order so a non-finite coordinate passes through non-finite — an x < L test is false for NaN and would silently rewrite a diverged trajectory into a tidy in-box position. The mod function maps an infinity to NaN, so divergence survives as non-finiteness, though not as the same non-finite value.\nSampling needs no wrap, and that is now a documented contract with an honest limit. The transfer kernels wrap their stencil indices, so interpolation is invariant under shifting a coordinate by whole periods (tested, at tolerance — the invariance is exact in exact arithmetic and floating-close in practice). It is not \"any finite coordinate\": at extreme magnitudes the cell-index arithmetic leaves its exact range, and absolute floating-point resolution degrades as coordinates grow — single precision especially — which is why regular canonicalization is load-bearing on a long run rather than presentation-layer hygiene.\nThe coefficients are the operation's definition, not α/β. As with combine! (the recorded invariant-10 exception class), the c_j scale the operands of what the operation is; no trailing α/β pair exists or may be added (the glyph-collision rule).\nIn-place only, one fused traversal. There is no allocating sibling — displacement is motion of an existing base, and a fresh-PointSet variant has no consumer (invariant 1). The n-term form reads the positions once, each section once, and writes once, collapsing a K-term Adams–Bashforth combination from K broadcast passes to one; the destination may be the source (the fused pass reads an element's inputs before writing its outputs, invariant 9), and a separate destination form serves the predictor step that writes X^ast beside X.\nImplementation shape. The pass is _assign!-routed (threaded on CPU, materialize! on device, bitwise thread-count-invariant), built by recursion over the term tuple rather than by closures — a closure capturing the element type heap-allocates itself, and an unbounded forwarded Vararg is not specialized, both measured before the recursive spelling brought every arity to zero bytes. The torus arity runs column-wise so each axis sees its extent and origin as plain scalars; the columns are contiguous (SoA), so traffic is still one traversal.\nRefusals are the package's decision (invariant 14): point count, scalar type and storage family are checked explicitly against the destination — the torus path bypasses the broadcast machinery's own enforcement, and a Grid3D is checked as the non-section partner it is.\nVerification. An independent raw-array oracle over both element types; the fused torus arity bitwise-equal to cover-then-wrap!; the endpoint, non-finite and origin contracts each pinned by name; GPU parity exact on both arities plus the mixed-family refusals; zero-byte allocation rows for every arity; exact traffic rows (the torus row's counts identical to the cover row's — the fused wrap measured free); four benchmark rows in the transfer class. Points 211 → 226 (five public methods, ten internal), the field surface untouched.\nAllocation from context: the constructors accept a storage family (2026-08-24, requested by  the consuming solver). Every route to field storage required an existing value in hand: the six  concrete constructors take a storage array the caller must already have produced on the right  backend, and similar_field (decision 44) copies its context from a reference field. A consumer  whose domain frame deliberately owns no field-sized arrays — the design this package's own  lazy kernels enable, and which the solver's memory ledger now asserts — therefore had no  sanctioned route to its first field, and was reading a multipliers axis vector as a  backend-and-element-type witness for a raw Base.similar, reproducing the package's  shape-and-backend logic outside the package.\nThe surface is the constructors, not a new verb. The request framed the need as \"the reference-free completion of the similar_field family\", but similar_field's identity is allocation from a reference — \"similar\" with nothing to be similar to is a contradiction in the name (and Base.similar itself was declined for these allocators by decision 67, for convention reasons that would apply doubly here). What was missing is construction from context, and construction already has a home: the same six constructors now accept a storage family — Array, CuArray, ROCArray — in place of the array, and produce the storage themselves: VectorFieldSpectral(ROCArray, grid, layout). No new export, no new name; the representation and tensor order are carried by the type name, as they always were.\nThe element type comes from the grid. Grid3D{T} already stores the domain at the field's element type, so the metadata list the request enumerates — grid, layout, order, representation, element type, backend — has one redundant entry, and the surface drops it: a bare family takes T from the grid, a parameterized Array{Float32} must agree with the grid or is refused (invariant 14, refusal over conversion), and a complex parameter is refused always — the field's element type is its real scalar type, spectral storage being a representation detail allocated as Complex{T} internally (invariant 2).\nThe backend is a generic array type, the decision-63 argument verbatim: dispatching on a generic target gains both vendors without depending on either, and the family is reduced to its unparameterized wrapper (the _storage_family comparison in reverse) before the storage is produced.\nThe storage is uninitialized, exactly as similar_field's is (decision 44 records the same choice): zero-filling is a cost the caller may not want, and the constructor's contract is storage, not values.\nDispatch note. The physical types also carry a sampling constructor whose function argument is untyped, so ScalarFieldPhysical(Array, grid) would have reached it (and failed at call time); the family methods are typed Type{<:AbstractArray} and win by specificity. The ambiguity check guards the pair.\nNo benchmark row, deliberately. An allocator's cost is the backend allocator's, not the package's, and finding (y) records exactly what a per-call field-sized allocation does to a benchmark row: it lands in a process-history-selected regime and stops being comparable across process shapes. A row would measure the harness's memory history, not the operation.\nVerification. Every allocated field is type-identical to its data-built twin and to similar_field of that twin, over both element types, two layout folds (the decision-48 metadata trap — layout, grid and origin must survive any operation that allocates), the default-layout arity, orders through R = 4, and a physical round trip through freshly allocated scratch; both refusals; on device, allocation directly on the vendor family with a reduction and both refusals exercised (test/gpu/suite.jl). Fields 120 → 122 (the two helpers; the sixteen constructor methods are Type methods the per-function count does not see). The consumer deletes its _reference/_blank pair, and no code of its reads a multipliers field for any purpose but wavenumbers.\nTransfer schemes persist, because a scheme is its parameters (2026-08-24, requested by the  consuming solver). The HDF5 extension persisted the ten section classes and nothing else, so a  consumer restoring a tracer set — positions, values, and the scheme that samples with them —  had one leaf it could not reach through the package. Writing the tags itself would have put this  package's type names, outside this package's schema version, in a second package: the drift  argument that carried decision 74, one layer up.\nThe line is drawn by what an object is, not by which types were asked for. A LalescuSpline stores neighbours, smoothness and a weight SMatrix — but the matrix is derived, recomputed by the constructor from (n m). So the faithful record is the parameters, and storing the weights would duplicate derived data, the very thing the consumer's checkpoint design avoids everywhere else. LagrangeSpline is the same with one parameter, and DirectDFT is the degenerate case: a singleton whose tag is its whole record. It is covered although nobody asked, because that makes the HDF5-persisted class \"a scheme that is its parameters\" rather than \"the two the consumer named\".\nNUFFT is refused, and the refusal is a statement about the object. It owns oversampled FFT plans and a deconvolution window built against a particular reference field. That is a compiled schedule, the same kind of object as FieldTransformPlans, which decision 63 likewise rebuilds rather than moves when storage families change. A stored NUFFT would be either a large duplicate of derived data or a parameter set pretending to be the object; the consumer rebuilds it from the reference field it restores anyway. The error says so and names the rebuild.\nThe group has attributes and no data. Every other object written to HDF5 is a section and its group is built around an array; a scheme's is not, which is the shape difference worth naming rather than smoothing over.\nThe element type is resolved through a closed table, never eval. Reconstruction needs the weight element type back from the string \"Float64\", and the obvious spelling — parse and evaluate — would execute an arbitrary expression out of a file this package did not write. The mapping is a two-entry Dict and anything else is refused.\nReading revalidates rather than trusts. The parameters go back through the ordinary constructor, so a group asserting an impossible combination (m  2n) throws there instead of yielding a malformed scheme, and the group's schema version, type tag, and required attributes are each checked.\nAn adjacent defect, found and fixed in the same change. The extension's header has always claimed its self-describing attributes are \"CHECKED, not merely written\", and for read_field they were — but read_points never checked its schema version, so a point group written by a newer version was silently accepted. The version gate is now one shared helper applied by all three read verbs, and the header's claim is true for the first time.\nDefinition of done, with two reasoned exemptions. No GPU parity lane: a scheme is a host-side parameter record, and the persistence layer returns host objects by construction, so there is nothing for a device to disagree about. No benchmark row: the operation writes four attributes, and there is no HDF5 benchmark class to put it in — inventing one to time a constant would measure the library, not the package.\nVerification. Round trips over nine schemes — both spline families across the parameter region including the m = 2n boundary and the n = m = 0 corner, both element types, the CatmullRom alias (which needs no identity of its own: it is LalescuSpline(1, 1) and returns egal to it), DirectDFT, and the parent[name] = scheme sugar — asserted with ===, the strongest available equality, and reinforced by a behavioural check that a read scheme interpolates bitwise identically, so the test still bites if a scheme ever gains state that egality sees and the stored parameters do not determine. Eight malformed-group paths each refused for their own reason, the NUFFT refusal on both the verb and the sugar, and a completeness guard walking every concrete AbstractTransferScheme subtype and requiring it to round-trip or be a recorded refusal — verified by perturbation (dropping one scheme fails it), so a future scheme family cannot ship silently unpersistable.\nThe transform plans carry no tensor order (2026-08-26, prompted by a residency request from  the consuming solver GBGHomogeneousTurbulence (\"IT\")). FieldTransformPlans was parameterized on  the tensor order R, and built one plan pair per rank: a scalar field, a vector field and a  second-order tensor field each needed their own. That was never a recorded decision — it was the  shape the original construction happened to take — and it is a category error in the terms  §2 already sets out. A real Fourier transform on a periodic box is defined on the box; a  rank-R field is that transform applied along a fiber. The transform is therefore horizontal,  a property of the base, and R is vertical, a property of the fiber. Encoding the second  inside the first is the same defect the documentation rules name elsewhere as \"a vector space  equated with an integer\".\nWhat it cost, in a currency the package does not itself pay. On a GPU backend the wrapper  behind plan_rfft allocates a device array per plan, sized to the coefficients that plan  transforms. Per-rank plans therefore multiply that array by the rank: IT measured 516 MiB of plan  residency against 1158 MiB of working arrays at 256^3 in single precision, with a further  1161 MiB once a nine-component diagnostic is enabled. The package holds no plans of its own, so  the saving accrues entirely to consumers — but the arrangement that causes it is the  package's, and so is the fix.\nOne pair now serves every rank. The plans are built for a single component (the tensor-order  zero shapes), and each transform applies them once per component over contiguous component views,  which the fiber-last layout of invariant 3 makes possible: a component of a rank-R field is a  contiguous block, exposed by one reshape. FieldTransformPlans{T, PF, PI, G, L, S} no longer  names R, the transform generics no longer match on it, and the compatibility check was  already rank-free — it compares grid and layout, which is exactly what a base-level object should  match on (decision 56's context protocol).\nThe measured cost is nil or negative ON THE HOST, which is why this could be taken at all —  and the device was not measured then. PERFORMANCE.md findings (aj) and (al) later measured  it on both vendors: the per-component loop costs 111–161times on a device vector  transform at 64^3 and is flat or faster at every larger grid, so the host verdict below  stands and is not a claim about a GPU. Holding  planner effort fixed — and that qualifier is load-bearing, see finding (z) and below — a  single-component plan runs at 098–104 of the batched plan at one thread and  072–113 at eight, i.e. free at t1 and a 10–28% win at 256^3 on eight  threads, which is the regime decision 37 names as the CPU tuning target. IT reports the same  direction on its own harness.\nThe obstacle, and why it does not become a restriction. A component of a rank-R field  begins at element offset (c-1) prod N_a, and a transform library may refuse a pointer whose  alignment differs from the one it planned for. The two backends ask different questions, so the  package answers them differently and _component_offsets_align reports both. FFTW records an  alignment class for the real and the complex array alike; the answer there is to plan with  FFTW.UNALIGNED, which costs no memory and, measured, 2–65% of throughput — paid only by grids  that need it, and no production grid does. The cuFFT library needs only the real pointer aligned to its  complex element, which reduces to the real component stride being even, i.e. to the grid not  being odd on all three axes; a device has no UNALIGNED flag, so such a grid transforms  through a component-sized buffer instead. Both answers are chosen once, at construction, from the  grid alone. No grid is refused, which was the alternative considered and declined: every  other absence in the package encodes mathematics (invariant 1), whereas \"no grid with three odd  axes\" would have encoded a pointer-alignment rule, and the mathrmodd axes are what  invariant 5's even/odd Nyquist split is tested against.\nA measurement trap that nearly reversed the conclusion, recorded because it is cheap to repeat.  FFTW_MEASURE is 0, so passing flags = FFTW.UNALIGNED replaces the default rather than  adding to it and silently selects MEASURE. A first pass read UNALIGNED as up to 43times  faster; it was comparing planner efforts, not plan shapes. The constructor therefore composes  the flag onto the caller's request (requested | FFTW.UNALIGNED) rather than replacing it. A  second pass had one plan of six left on ESTIMATE by an incomplete edit, which read as a  46times penalty for the very variant under test. Vary one thing at a time; this is the  third and fourth instance the performance notes record.\nA finding that fell out of it and is worth more than this decision. Under ESTIMATE, which  is what the package plans with by default, a 256^3 Float32 forward transform measures  513 ms against Float64's 496 ms — single precision should be roughly twice as fast, not  equal. Under MEASURE the same two cells read 108 ms and 180 ms. That is a factor of  47 left on the floor by the default planner effort, it is independent of everything above,  and it belongs to PERFORMANCE.md finding (h)'s territory rather than to this entry.\nThe inverse curl — curl inverted on the solenoidal subspace (2026-08-28, user-requested).  inverse_curl!/inverse_curl recover the divergence-free vector field u whose curl is a  given w, as one coefficientwise pass hat u = mathrmi atimeshat wlvert arvert^2  with a the derivative wavenumbers of invariant 5 and the k = 0 mode written zero.\nWhy it is well posed, and what the choice costs. nablatimes u = w fixes u only up to a gradient, since nablatimesnablaphi = 0; this returns the representative that is itself divergence-free, which is the gauge the operator picks and the one a velocity field satisfies. It also needs a solvable right-hand side — a curl is divergence-free, so nablacdot w = 0 is a condition on the input, and a violating w has its longitudinal part annihilated rather than reported. That mirrors inverse_laplacian, which likewise projects a non-mean-free input onto the compatible part instead of refusing it.\nBoth wavenumber families would give the same answer sub-Nyquist; only one is exact. The numerator and the denominator are built from the SAME derivative family, so (nablatimes)^-1(nablatimes) reproduces a solenoidal field exactly at every mode rather than only on the resolved band — decision 42's argument, one operator over.\nRoute. A one-pass KernelAbstractions kernel with a gated CPU twin, which is what §9's execution-route policy prescribes for a cartesian-coefficient operator reading three components of a mode and writing three. It is therefore alias-safe by construction (all of a mode's reads precede its writes) and needs no guard temporary. The decision-47 dispatch-pair guard counted the new pair and the reachability probe covers it, so a stale CPU-twin arity fails at test time.\nDeliberate absences. No α/β arity and no kernel-composed arity, matching curl! rather than helmholtz_projection!: there is no consumer for either, and invariant 1 says a combination is added when something needs it, not for symmetry with a neighbour.\nThe transfer traversal order — locality as an option, and the wrap as a walk (2026-08-30,  user-directed). The grid↔point transfer is dominated by where its points read and write, not by  the arithmetic on them, and two separate things were costing it.\nThe problem, measured before anything was built. A consumer holds points in the order it seeded them, which bears no relation to the grid, so consecutive work-items touch unrelated parts of the field and every stencil is a fresh set of cache misses. At a 256^3 grid with 10^6 points — the regime the consumer runs — visiting the same points in grid order instead was worth 31times on the device gather and 38times on the scatter. The signature was visible in the baseline before the cause was: the Float64 gather cost 20times its Float32 twin at 256^3 but only 126times at 128^3, which is a field outgrowing cache rather than arithmetic getting harder.\nAn order is a TRAVERSAL ORDER, not a reordering, and that is the whole design. order[i] names the point work-item i handles. Nothing is moved, and each point's result still lands in its own slot. The alternative — permuting the PointSet — was rejected because a consumer holds PointValues indexed alongside its points, and a permutation behind its back desynchronises them silently. It also makes the option purely additive: a keyword on the four point-indexed verbs, no signature changed, and a consumer that ignores it sees nothing. GBGHomogeneousTurbulence is in final development, which made that a requirement rather than a preference.\nThe gather is bitwise invariant under any order, because each output element is an independent sum; the tests assert ==, not isapprox, and a reversed order beside the built one, so the invariance is of the order and not of one order. The scatter's atomic summation order changes, which it already does between runs (§9), so it is compared to tolerance with conservation asserted beside it.\nValidity is carried by a TYPE, after an adversarial review showed it was only asserted. The first version took a bare index vector and checked its length and storage family. A vector of the right length that was not a permutation was accepted, and every failure it caused was silent: a gather left slots unwritten, so the allocating form returned uninitialised memory as point values; a scatter lost part of the deposited total; an out-of-range entry read and wrote past the end of an array inside @inbounds, segfaulting on CPU and corrupting quietly on device. The check's own error message already claimed the bijection was enforced. PointTraversalOrder now validates once, where the order is built — two reductions and a marking pass, the same work a per-transfer check would do but paid once — so the transfers read a field they can trust. Same shape as decision 59: make the property structural rather than guarded.\nThe order is built by a counting pass, not a sort. Three portable KernelAbstractions/Atomix kernels — key, histogram, place — over blocks of cells, adding no dependency and needing no vendor sort. It costs 0.24–0.27 ms on device at 256^3 — the figure for the code that ships, the build itself being 0.098–0.13 ms before the permutation validation the adversarial review added — so it repays inside a single transfer; the recorded estimate it replaces guessed 12–24 transfers, having assumed a vendor sort, and the vendor sortperm over the same keys does cost 25times more.\nMorton was the recorded plan and it LOSES. Plain lexicographic block order beat Z-order on every measured cell, and the literature agrees for this access pattern: Domínguez et al. (2011) report the same result in SPH and name the discriminator — Morton wins where cell access is random, lexicographic where it is a sequential sweep, which a sorted stencil transfer is. Two further candidates were measured and dropped: stability within a bucket changes nothing, so the atomic placement stands, and one bucket per cell is no better while costing 14times more to build.\nThe periodic wrap became a walk. The stencil visits consecutive nodes, so each index is its predecessor plus one — yet every node index came from mod, which LLVM shared down to 32 integer divisions per point per fibre. _wrap_step replaces the division with a conditional zero, keeping three mods per point for the starts. It is bitwise identical to the previous kernel, verified over 48 configurations including a stencil wider than the grid, where the walk wraps repeatedly; only the index arithmetic changed.\nThe walk has one bad corner, and this entry has now described it wrongly twice. It is worth 13–19times on the CPU gather up to six nodes per axis, and on the device it wins on CUDA (24 of 24 cells at or below 1002times) while LOSING 174times on the RX 7900 XTX CatmullRom gather at 256^3 Float64 — the \"1.03–1.28x on the device throughout\" this entry used to carry is RETRACTED by PERFORMANCE.md findings (aj) and (al). At eight nodes per axis it can also lose on CPU: 4–14% depending on the scheme, only from about four threads, and at R = 1 rather than at every rank — R = 2 is less affected, and at one thread the walk is 17–22% FASTER. The mechanism recorded here was backwards: mod is the form that VECTORIZES (983–1588 vector instructions against the walk's 133–138) and neither variant spills on the host, so the CPU loss is the walk forfeiting the memory-level parallelism that independent indices keep. On gfx1100 the walk DOES spill for CatmullRom (192 VGPRs, occupancy halved 16 → 8), the device regression's mechanism — finding (aq). The corner is stated rather than fixed, decision 47 forbidding the conditional second body; finding (ad) carries the tables.\nWhat the two wrong versions had in common is worth more than either correction: each was measured at one point of a space and written as though it held across it — first at one scheme, then at one thread count. The rule the package already applies to tests, that a fixture must be able to falsify the claim its name makes, applies to benchmarks in exactly the same way.\nWhere it does not pay, which is why it is an option and not a default. At 64^3 with 10^5 points everything is launch-bound and building an order for a single transfer loses. On a CPU whose cache holds the whole field there is little locality to win, and the order's own scattered reads of the coordinates cost more than they save. The win grows with tensor order, because a vector field reads three components per point against one coordinate triple.\nIt also grows with the STENCIL, which is the axis the first sweep missed. Across schemes of 2, 4, 6 and 8 nodes per axis the device gather reads 186, 339, 384, 369times and the CPU rises to 695times at the widest — a wider stencil reads more nodes per point, so there is more traffic for locality to save. Past 256^3 the schemes separate sharply: at 1024^3 the default gets 121times where an eight-node stencil gets 429times. The option therefore matters most exactly where a run is most expensive. The measured tables are docs/PERFORMANCE.md finding (ad).\nThe speedups are a property of the INPUT order. Measured across seedings at 256^3 with 10^6 points, the same operation reads 12times for uniformly random positions and 093times for a lattice in sweep order, with the ordered arm nearly constant throughout — it normalises the access pattern, and what varies is how bad the baseline was. The option repairs a bad order rather than improving a good one, which is the honest statement of when a consumer should reach for it.\nThe signature leads with the grid (point_traversal_order(grid, points)), corrected 2026-08-30 from a points-first first version: the function is TRANSFER infrastructure, so it takes the transfer verbs' argument order (anterpolate(grid, points, …)), not the motion verbs' trailing-grid one (wrap!(points, grid)) it briefly borrowed. The tile_size option was questioned the same day and KEPT on a measurement: the optimal tile tracks the backend, not the interpolation scheme (flat in tile on the device at stencil widths 4 and 8 alike; the CPU prefers coarser blocks for both), and one order serves any scheme and both directions, so a scheme argument would misstate both what the option controls and what the order is valid for.\nNo cadence, per invariant 12. An order describes where the points were when it was built. A consumer whose points move decides when to rebuild it; the package holds no notion of time, and the measured build cost is small enough that \"every step\" is a defensible default for a consumer to choose.\nThe exponential fiber hoist — one exp per mode, and the routes it forced (2026-08-30,  user-directed; IT item 2). The exponential kernel exp(c k^2 + s hat w) depends on the  mode alone, yet the broadcast route evaluated it per output element — three times per mode for a  vector field, nine for a tensor, identical arguments each time. The consumer's codegen report  measured that at approx 35times the shape-identical combine! single-threaded, and it was  the pass audit's standing ŵ-extrusion holder.\nThe change is BITWISE INVISIBLE, and that is the contract it was built under. The user's condition was no regression of any kind, so every variant was verified bit-identical to the committed broadcast over 126 cells — seven arities including the aliased filtered accumulate, three tensor orders, both element types, odd folds — before any performance was even measured. Equal arguments give equal bits; only the number of exp EVALUATIONS changes. The scalar (R = 0) forms keep the broadcast route untouched: one component, nothing to hoist.\nThree CPU bodies, each forced by a measurement. The first attempt looped the fiber innermost and lost 24% at 256^3 Float64 -t8 — the visit-order lesson in reverse, 3^R strided streams where the broadcast had one. The second buffered one x-row of factors and still trailed by 3–6% there, the factors being computed BEFORE the row streams rather than under them. The shipped body fuses the factor computation into the FIRST component's pass — exp hides under that stream exactly as it hides under the broadcast — and the remaining components reuse the buffer as pure streams: strictly less work than the broadcast in every regime. SERIALLY the bufferless body runs instead, because decision 70's lane pins this operator at literally zero bytes on the serial lane and a row buffer is an allocation; the two bodies are bitwise identical, so the split costs a comment.\nWhat it bought, measured. CPU -t1 (the consumer's stated pain): 24–39times; -t4 at 256^3: 165times; device: 13–21times across every Float64 cell. What it did not: two residual cells, disclosed rather than absorbed. Float64 vector at 256^3 and EXACTLY eight threads on the 9800X3D reads +3–6% (the transition band where the field just exceeds the V-Cache and eight threads just saturate the bus while exp still fills a third of the roof — at 512^3 -t8 the two routes are even to 0.7%); and the device Float32 combination at 256^3 reads −2%, stable. Both are the price of the constraint being measured honestly instead of asserted.\nA complex ŵ found the fixture blindness. The factor buffer was first typed real, and 126 bitwise cells passed — every fixture held real values in complex storage, so the imaginary part was zero everywhere (decision 52's class, again). A true spectrum as ŵ threw InexactError where the broadcast had always computed the complex exponential; the buffer now takes the offset storage's element type, so decision 79 tightens no contract the broadcast honoured. The threading toggle keeps that fixture, at an offset scale small enough that the exponent stays finite — its first version overflowed exp and read three identical NaN payloads as a route mismatch.\nThe decision-47 guard had a module-shaped blind spot, and this change found it. The dispatch-pair scan named three modules, and Convolution was not one — so the two new _*_modes! pairs did not announce themselves, and neither had the THREE pre-existing pairs (_separable_*_pass! ×2, _radial_convolution_pass!) that had carried gated CPU loops since the decision-50/51 era with nothing checking their arities stayed dispatch candidates. The scan now includes Convolution and the frozen count is 17, with the lesson recorded where the count is: a frozen count only defends the modules the scan names.\nThe ŵ-extrusion holder is retired at the fused ideal. The pass audit measured the offset field's traffic drop 3M → M on the vector fixture, which is the entire fix the holder had priced; test/pass_audit.jl freezes the new ideal with the history in place.\nA traversal order is rebuilt in place, so a moving point set stops growing the device pool  (2026-09-03, user-directed). Decision 78 shipped point_traversal_order as an allocating builder, and  its own docstring closed with the sentence \"There is no in-place form yet\". Nothing tracked that  \"yet\": a consumer whose points move rebuilds, and each rebuild allocated four arrays — the  Int32 result, the counting sort's Int64 bucket keys, the histogram with its exclusive prefix  sum, and the Bool marks the permutation check writes — about 15 MiB at 256^3 with 10^6  points. On a device those are freed by a collector that runs on host pressure and cannot see  them, which is the failure mode invariant 13 exists to prevent, reached through a builder rather  than through an operator.\nThe scratch is the caller's, not the order's. point_traversal_order!(order, grid, points, scratch) takes a PointTraversalOrderScratch, built once for a grid, a point count and a tile_size. Two other shapes were considered and rejected. Letting the allocating builder RETAIN the arrays it already allocates would have given the simpler call point_traversal_order!(order, grid, points) at no extra peak memory — but it triples what a build-once consumer holds, and it makes an operation's allocation depend on how its argument was constructed, since PointTraversalOrder(indices) accepts a user-supplied permutation and has no scratch to carry. Sizing the scratch inside the call would have defeated the purpose. The explicit argument follows decision 64's four-argument to_physical!, which is this package's recorded answer to the same question: when an in-place form needs working storage, the caller supplies it and the cost stays visible at the call site.\nThe block layout travels with the scratch, not as a keyword. point_traversal_order! takes no tile_size; it reads the block counts from the scratch it was given. A keyword would have let an order and its histogram disagree about how many buckets exist, which is an out-of-range atomic write rather than a wrong answer — the same class as the Int32 key overflow decision 78 records fixing.\nThe validation survived the move. The allocating form validates the permutation rather than trusting it, and the Bool marks it writes are one of the four arrays that accumulated. The check is not traded away for the allocation contract: _validate_traversal gained a method taking the caller's marks, so the in-place path runs the identical check against scratch storage.\nIt is not zero, and the docstring does not claim zero. Three KernelAbstractions launches and the validation's own leave about 1.2 KiB of host-side launch bookkeeping — the same class as the FFTW callback of PERFORMANCE.md §4(t). What matters is that it is constant in the point count: measured 1248 bytes at 10^3 points and 1312 at 10^6, where the allocating form grows from 18.9 KB to 13.0 MB. Nothing per-rebuild scales, and nothing per-rebuild is on a device.\nRenamed to PointTraversalOrder in the same round (user-directed). TransferOrder was the minority spelling of its own concept: the prose said \"visit order\" 28 times and \"transfer order\" 7, and the API page section was already titled for the former, so the code name and the documentation disagreed 4:1. Worse, order means tensor order 249 times in this package, and the same source tree held both order = field_tensor_order(...) and order = transfer_order(...) — the keyword read, at the call site, as if it took a rank. And in plain English \"transfer order\" parses as an instruction to transfer, which is the wrong first reading for this package's audience. Point matches the layer's own prefix family (PointSet, point_positions, point_value_tensor_order), and \"traversal\" already carries the sense \"one pass over the points\" here — displace! documents \"one fused traversal\" — and is the word the cited reference uses, Domínguez et al. (2011) being described in §References as a cell traversal order for neighbour search. The keyword moved with the type, order to traversal, since leaving it would have preserved the exact ambiguity the rename removes. The prose moved too: \"visit order\" is retired so the concept keeps one name.\nAn adversarial review (2026-09-03) found two claims the code did not keep, and both are now enforced rather than reworded. _check_point_traversal_order refused a foreign-family order and told the caller to \"move it with adapt\", while no adapt_structure method existed — so Adapt's identity default handed the same order back and the caller met the identical refusal. And the refill's grid check tested that the scratch's block layout FITS inside the grid rather than agreeing with it, so a scratch built on a 16^3 grid was accepted for a rebuild on a 32^3 one, silently doubling the block edge while the docstring claimed the layout was checked. The scratch now stores its grid and the refill compares it, which also catches an extent or origin change that a size test never could. Both are pinned in test/point_traversal_order.jl, the adapt one through a probe adaptor, since adapting to the family an object is already in is the identity by contract and cannot distinguish a working method from a missing one.\nThe memory figures moved when this decision landed, and the docstring had kept the old ones. cumsum on an Int32 count array widens to Int64, so the pre-decision path held the running total at twice the counts' width; the scratch allocates it with similar, at the same width. The grid-scaled storage is therefore 2 MiB rather than 3 at the default tile_size on a 256^3 grid, and 128 MiB rather than 192 at tile_size = 1. The narrowing is safe for the same reason the order itself is Int32: the running total's largest value is the point count, which must already fit an Int32 index.\nBoth forms share one build. _fill_traversal! and _traversal_tiles are called by the allocating builder and the in-place one alike, so the two cannot drift into computing different orders — the failure decision 47 records for a device kernel and its gated CPU twin, in a different place. test/point_traversal_order.jl asserts the agreement block-wise rather than index-wise, because the scatter's atomics leave the order within a block unspecified in both forms; an index-wise comparison would be testing the atomics, not the operation.\nThe traversal order's running total is threaded on the host (2026-09-04, user-directed).  Building a traversal order runs three KernelAbstractions kernels, which thread by KA's own  partitioning, and one running total over the block counts, which did not thread at all: it was  cumsum!, a serial loop on a host array. Its cost is set by the number of BLOCKS — that is, by  the grid and tile_size — and not by the point count, so it does not shrink as the rest of the  build is parallelised. Measured on a 9800X3D at 10^6 points with the default tile_size: at  256^3 it was 4% of the build at eight threads, and at 512^3, the grid decision 37 names as  the CPU tuning target, 25%.\n_scan_offsets! replaces the call, and only the host method threads. The generic method is cumsum!, which is already a parallel scan on a device; the host method is a two-pass form — each chunk sums its own slice, the chunk totals are scanned serially, each chunk then writes its slice offset by the total before it. Below a chunk of 4096 blocks, or with threading turned off, it falls back to cumsum!. Measured end-to-end at 512^3: the build goes 3.41 → 2.91 ms at four threads and 2.85 → 2.26 ms at eight, so 117–126times; the scan alone is 26–73times.\nBitwise equality is a property here, not a tolerance. Integer addition is exact and associative, so regrouping the sums cannot change them and the threaded result equals cumsum! bit for bit — no fixed reduction tree of the kind decision 40 needed for floats. Pinned over sizes bracketing the cutoff and four distributions.\nThe overflow contract had to be carried across deliberately. cumsum! into a narrow integer RAISES on overflow; a scan accumulating in that same width wraps silently, and a wrapped total is a negative offset that the placement kernel would use as an index inside @inbounds — the class decision 78 records fixing. The chunk totals are therefore accumulated wide and the grand total is checked against typemax before the second pass. Both forms are asserted to throw.\nA data race in the first draft is worth recording, because reading did not catch it. Pass two wrote acc = carry[c] where acc was already bound in the enclosing function scope, so the assignment targeted the OUTER local and every task shared one accumulator. It produced wrong answers and ran 50× slower than the serial loop. It surfaced only because the correctness harness crashed on its first mismatch; the fix is local run = …, and the hazard is that a name reused from an enclosing scope inside Threads.@threads is shared rather than shadowed.\nA section owns a field-sized array, so it needs a display (2026-09-04, user-directed). Julia's  default struct display prints every field, and a section's largest field is the data. A PointSet  of 10^5 points therefore rendered as 5.6 million characters, a NUFFT as 2.5 million, and a  SeparableKernel on a 512^3 grid as 56742. The REPL flood is the visible half. The half that  bites harder is that this is what an exception carries: every error message in the package reads  well today only because each author remembered to interpolate size(field_data(f)) rather than  f, and nothing enforced that.\nTwo forms, because Julia uses them in different places. The two-argument show is what appears inside a container, inside $(...) interpolation and inside an exception, so it is one line and self-delimiting — Type{T}(body), parenthesised the way Diagonal([1.0, 2.0]) and CartesianIndex(1, 2, 3) are. A bare Type: a, b cannot be told from its neighbours once a container puts commas between elements, and a display that is ambiguous in a container is ambiguous in the error message that motivated it. The three-argument MIME\"text/plain\" form is the REPL's and may spend lines; it carries the stored shape and element type, which for a field in spectral representation are the rfft half spectrum in a complex type rather than the grid.\nWhat the body carries is decided by what the package REFUSES on, not by what is interesting. _check_field_domain_compatibility rejects a pair differing in scalar type, grid size, extent, origin, layout or storage family. A display omitting those printed two byte-identical strings for the two operands of the very error it existed to explain — measured on four mutually incompatible fields, which rendered as one string. Extent, origin and the fold are therefore shown whenever they differ from the ordinary isotropic 2pi box at the origin in axis order, which keeps the common line near fifty characters while making that collision impossible. The property is asserted directly, over every pair of a family of refused fields.\nThe rule binds every base-bound object, not only the fields: an object shows what the check that refuses it compares. A NUFFT keeps its reference grid and layout precisely because a plan handed a same-order field on another domain returns a silently wrong answer; a traversal scratch is refused a rebuild against another grid; a kernel reaches the same domain check through _check_kernel_compatibility.\nAll three showed their grid size and nothing else, so all three collided on exactly the pairs they are refused on — found by re-reading rather than by the testset, which covered fields alone when it was written.\nCoordinates print at full precision, and the predicate that suppresses them is EXACT. Both halves were wrong in a first draft and the second is the instructive one. Rounding to four significant digits reintroduced the collision one axis-scale down, since the grid check is exact equality and boxes differing in the sixth digit are refused. Fixing that alone was not enough: the test asking whether the box was ordinary was ≈(2π), so a box of 2pi + 10^-9 fell inside the default tolerance and its extent was suppressed before precision could matter. A loose predicate deciding whether to print defeats any precision in the printing. The regression test carries that case.\nNothing may read an element. Every quantity is host-side metadata, so a display is safe under allowscalar(false) and forces no synchronisation. A future addition printing a norm, a range or a sample value would break that on the backend where it matters most, so it is prohibited rather than discouraged.\nA derived quantity is not shown in place of what it derives from. A spline scheme's weight matrix comes from (n m) — the property decision 75 persists it by — and the default printed 747 characters of matrix and neither number. The constructor call is the honest form and it round-trips, with Julia's parser as an independent oracle. Grid3D was the same case, printing the grid_spacing that decision 45 requires to equal domain_size ./ grid_size.\nThe element type appears as the keyword the constructor takes, and only when it is not the default, so both forms round-trip while a Float32 and a Float64 scheme — which invariant 14 refuses against each other — cannot print alike.\nThe gate is DERIVED, in the allocation-audit shape. test/display.jl enumerates every concrete struct type the package defines and requires each to carry a package-owned show or a cited exemption, with completeness checked both ways so a stale exemption fails too. A listed set of types would go stale exactly when a type is added, which is when the check has something to say. Falsified by perturbation before being kept: a planted struct fails it, and so does an exemption naming a type that does not exist.\nSix types are exempt — RealFourierLayout, whose default is already short, unambiguous and round-tripping, and five pass-local callable adapters that are never returned, never stored on a user-visible object, and never interpolated into an error.\nThe work surfaced a defect it was not looking for: three spellings of the storage family. Fields._storage_family took a section and called parent once, Points._motion_storage_family duplicated it for displace!'s refusal, and the display added a third that recursed.\nTwo of the three were wrong. The constructors reject only a SubArray, so a reshape of a PermutedDimsArray of a device array is constructible, and one parent call answers ReshapedArray for it — not a storage family at all. So _same_backend could refuse two differently-wrapped views of the same device memory. That is invariant 14's predicate, so unifying them on the recursing form makes a check stricter as well as removing a divergence between a refusal and the display beside it.\nreality_projection! — the projector onto real-field spectra, because decision 71's premise  holds only to the ulp (2026-09-05, requested by the consuming solver with a measured failure;  owner-decided). Decision 71 declined a repairing verb on three grounds: everything the package  produces satisfies the reality condition by construction; the repair already exists as a physical  round trip; and a repairing verb would silently rewrite a caller's data. The first two are now  measured false, and the third is honoured by the design rather than overturned.\nThe premise, measured on this fleet's AMD host. The reality_residual of a fresh to_spectral  of a random real field is exactly zero for FFTW at 32^3, 64^3 and 128^3 and nonzero at  96^3 and 256^3 (13times10^-4 and 74times10^-4 in Float32 coefficient  units), and nonzero for rocFFT at every size (2times10^-5 to 8times10^-4); cuFFT  behaves like rocFFT on the consumer's host. So \"by construction\" is true only for FFTW at some  power-of-two sizes, and elsewhere a spectrum leaves the transform carrying an anti-Hermitian part  at the round-off floor.\nWhy a floor becomes a field. On the folded axis's zero plane both members of a conjugate  pair are stored, and their anti-Hermitian combination is invisible to the inverse real transform  — so the nonlinear term, formed from physical fields, neither sees it nor feeds it — and is  damped only by nukappa^2 at kappa approx 1. Any forcing proportional to the field,  hat N mathrel+= A mathbf1_textband hat u, amplifies it with the same  coefficient A as the physical modes, which A must hold positive on average to balance  the dissipation.\nThe consumer measured the net growth at A - nukappa^2 approx 026 per time unit: from the  Float32 floor the parasitic mode reaches order one at t approx 60, and six seeds died at  t = 66–71 for RK3 and RK4 alike and for both dealiasing rules, Float64 at t = 142  — ln(10^9)026 approx 80 time units later, as the rate predicts. Spectral energy held its  target throughout while the physical field's energy, dissipation and maximum speed collapsed;  the residual went 4times10^-2 to 13times10^6. Same shape as Wang & Rosa (2009) on  the divergence-free constraint: a linear constraint the discretisation does not enforce, a  round-off seed, a linear amplifier, and re-projection per step as the remedy.\nWhat ships. reality_projection!(destination, source) and the in-place  reality_projection!(field): on the stored self-conjugate planes — the folded axis's zero  plane, and its Nyquist plane when the full axis is even — each pair is replaced by  tfrac12(hat f(k) + overlinehat f(-k)) and its conjugate, a self-paired corner by its  real part from the same formula, and nothing else is touched. This is the orthogonal projector  onto real-field spectra in the Parseval inner product: mathbb R-linear, idempotent — a  projected pair is bit-identical under a second application — and commuting with every per-mode  multiplier m with m(-k) = overlinem(k). FORMALIZATION carries the typed statement.\nSemantics decided with the request: projection only — no Nyquist zeroing, which is a dealiasing  policy and the consumer's truncation keeps those planes empty anyway; a mutator returning its  argument, no residual returned, so reality_residual stays the checker and the report/repair  split of decision 71 stands; and nothing folded into to_spectral!, since a fresh transform of  real data satisfies the condition to round-off and the defect lives in state accumulated in  place. The two-argument form exists so the twins read alike and costs a field copy before the  O(N^2) projection; the in-place form is the cheap one and the one the consumer calls, once  per step beside helmholtz_projection! at the step's closing deposit.\nWhy the round trip is not the same repair. It costs O(N^3 log N) — six transforms per  step on a vector field — and, measured by the consumer, it resets the residual only to the  transform's floor, since the forward transform reseeds it; the projection zeroes it exactly and  perturbs no other mode, where the round trip perturbs every mode by round-off. Decision 71's  \"the repair already exists\" named a worse operation for this purpose, not the same one.\nNaming. reality_projection!, pairing with reality_residual: the two verbs act on one  condition and share its stem, and \"the reality condition\" is the term decision 28 and convolve!  already use. Declined: real_projection! — the request's word — because it is accurate only at  the four self-paired corners, where the result is literally operatornameRehat f, and reads  as real(z) everywhere else; hermitian_projection!, because LinearAlgebra.hermitianpart is  the same projection on MATRICES and the package's fibers are matrices, so the name would read as a  fiber operation — the collision decision 71 recorded for symmetrize!, TurTLE's word; and  real_field_projection!, precise but a rename of the shipped verb for precision it already has.\nImplementation. A decision-38 kernel pair in Fields, beside the residual (one concept, one  home): a KernelAbstractions kernel on a device and a gated slab-threaded loop on the CPU, both  calling one inlined _project_pair! so they agree bitwise. Each pair is owned by its  lexicographically smaller member, so it is touched by exactly one work item and no two items  write one element — the pass audit's ideal, every stored plane element read once and written  once, holds exactly.\nIndices are mirrored modularly and placed by the layout's folded axis through a Val dispatch,  so a permuted fold projects whichever axis is folded and there are no reverse/circshift  temporaries — the traversal the roadmap prescribes for fusing reality_residual, now written.  Fields joined the decision-47 dispatch guard's scanned modules with its first kernel pair (17  → 18), and its method count refroze 141 → 157 with all sixteen named (nine hand-written, seven  generated by @kernel).\nVerification. test/fields.jl: 196 cells per element type — the identity on an exact  spectrum, an elementwise pair-mean oracle over the ORIGINAL coefficients, off-plane coefficients  bitwise untouched, exact idempotence, agreement with the round trip to 64varepsilon,  linearity, commutation with the Laplacian symbol, the two-argument form, the domain refusal and  the physical absence, over tensor orders 0/1/2, permuted folds and an odd folded axis. GPU  parity on ROCm: 491 → 494, the device result exactly Hermitian and bit-equal to the host's. The  allocation audit pins both arities, the pass audit both rows at the ideal, and the benchmark  gates run the four new rows (192 → 196 CPU, 206 → 210 ROCm).\nCUDA parity is the office host's to run; its count moves too.\nMeasured (2026-09-05, idle box; the other 80 operators rows reproduced the day-old archives  at medians 0998–1001). Scalar Float64 at one thread: 20, 102, 449 and 3140 us  across 64^3–512^3, against reality_residual's 80, 362, 1586 and 13258 on the same host  — 42times faster at 512^3 while also writing, the fused traversal having no  reverse/circshift temporaries. Eight threads bring 512^3 to 331 us, 95times. The  vector rows run three times the scalar ones at small grids and 14.7 ms at 512^3 Float64  one-threaded, 4.5 ms at eight. On the RX 7900 XTX every 64^3–256^3 cell sits at 27–48 us,  launch-bound, where the residual sits at 238–244; 512^3 Float32 vector reads 302 us.\nThe pass is strided by the layout's nature. The folded axis is the contiguous one, so its  zero plane is one element per cache line: 512^3 scalar moves 16.8 MB in 3.1 ms at one thread,  about 5 GB/s, and threading is what recovers it. One observation recorded without a cause: at  512^3 the Float32 scalar row is 104–129times SLOWER than Float64 on every CPU  lane. The archives were not spliced — src/ moved with this decision, which is the case the  splice tool refuses — so the rows enter RESULTS.md at the next sweep.\nWhat decision 71 keeps. The check reports; the projection is an explicit verb the caller  invokes; no transform enforces the symmetry — per-operation enforcement would tax every transform  for a problem only a multiplicative forcing creates, and a decaying run never needs it. What 71  loses is one sentence, \"everything the package produces satisfies the constraint by  construction\", which was a claim about FFTW at power-of-two sizes and is now stated as such  wherever it appeared.\n\nCUDA unified memory is a supported configuration, chosen by CUDA.jl's preference and not by the package (2026-09-17, owner-decided). A consumer runs out of memory on a 96 GiB GH200 at 768^3 by a few GiB, and CUDA.jl can place every array in unified memory, which the driver pages between the GPU and host RAM. The package needs no code for it: it never names a memory kind, and a CuArray allocated without one takes CUDA.jl's default_memory preference.\nSo every allocating route — the family constructors, adapt, similar_field, the multipliers, the kernel builders, the traversal order, the allocating operations — follows the setting, while cuFFT's own workspace, which it allocates itself, stays in device memory. The how-to is one page, Running on a GPU, which covers ROCm too, where AMDGPU has no unified memory at all.\nInvariant 14 is unchanged. A unified CuArray and a device CuArray are one storage family, because the family is the kind of array and not the memory holding it, and they combine rather than being refused. The definitions that said \"host memory, or one vendor's device memory\" were wrong under this configuration and now say \"the kind of array\".\nThe whole process, not per field. Unified memory for some fields and device memory for others is not offered. The family constructors read only a family's element type, so CuArray{Float64,3,UnifiedMemory} yields device memory, and their docstrings now say so; and in a mixed pair the field broadcast takes the result's memory from its first operand, where CUDA.jl's own array broadcast promotes to unified either way. Per-field placement would need both settled, and it is deferred until a measurement on GH200 shows all-unified is too slow to use.\nWhat the configuration cannot check. CUDA.jl serves element-by-element access to a unified array through the host and does not honour allowscalar(false) for it, so a unified run is blind to invariant 6. The unified test lane, test/gpu/cuda-unified, therefore adds to the device lane rather than replacing it; it refuses to run when the preference has not taken effect, asserts that every allocating route is unified, and runs a working set larger than the card.\nWhat it cannot measure. The benchmark harness decides its per-row GC from CUDA.used_memory(), which does not count unified allocations, so it refuses the configuration outright rather than report timings of the wrong thing. The one measurement taken is a bespoke script on an x86 card (Performance finding (as)); GH200 is unmeasured (Performance §7 gap 9).\nThe preference itself is the fragile part. CUDA.jl 6 reads it inside its CUDACore subpackage, which the running project must name in [extras], and CUDA.jl's own template still shows the section as [CUDA] — silently ignored. The documentation build rejects that spelling, and the test lane's first act is a residency check, because the failure mode is a green run that proves nothing.\nTwo grids over one box share their low modes, and two verbs move them (2026-09-18, user-directed, after an adversarial naming panel). A consumer forcing a turbulent flow needs content computed on a small grid — a stochastic process on a few dozen modes, or the nonlinear term of a low-wavenumber block obeying its own equations — to reach a large field every stage, and the reverse for the block's input. Nothing in the method set did that: the algebra needs operands of one shape, a kernel multiplies rather than adds, and the point transfer would sum over points at every mode.\nA grid of N points over a domain of length L resolves the wavenumbers 2piL times the integers it stores, so two grids over the SAME box name the same physical modes where they overlap, whatever their counts. The verb embed_modes! writes a coarser field's modes into a finer one and extract_modes! reads them back, each leaving every other mode of its destination untouched, at a cost set by the coarse field.\nWhy two verbs for one index map. The directions are different operations: embedding is injective and lossless, extraction discards everything the coarse grid cannot hold, and their scale factors are reciprocal. The deciding argument is a failure mode rather than taxonomy. The motivating consumer calls both directions on the same pair of fields a few lines apart, so under a single verb taking its direction from which argument is coarser, a transposed call would be legal by definition — no assertion could be written against it — and would leave a run silently unforced. With two verbs each wrong argument order is refused from grid metadata before a coefficient is read.\nAmplitudes are physical. The stored coefficient of a mode is prod(grid_size) times its amplitude (invariant 4), so a transfer between resolutions carries the ratio of the two counts, and the operations apply it. That is what makes the round trip an identity on the shared modes, keeps a grid-size ratio out of the middle of a consumer's equation of motion, and makes the two verbs exact adjoints under inner, with no measure factor — unlike the point transfer's W/Wᵀ, which carries N/M. Raw coefficient copying would be adjoint only up to (N_cN_f)^2.\nThe coarse grid's Nyquist shell is excluded, and that exclusion is what makes the shared set a subspace. A coarse mode at k = N_c2 on an even axis describes one sampled wave that the fine grid splits into two modes, so the single coarse coefficient does not determine them. Transferring it would have to choose, and either choice breaks the reality condition or the adjointness. Excluding it costs nothing a de-aliased solver relies on.\nRefusals are structural, never data-dependent: same domain size, origin, Fourier layout, scalar type, tensor order and storage family, and a coarse grid no larger on any axis and smaller on at least one. Equal grids are refused, because the Nyquist exclusion would make such a call a copy that silently leaves those planes stale, and two fields on one grid combine with add! or combine!.\nNeither verb has an allocating sibling. The result depends on the destination's prior contents — the modes outside the shared set survive — so an allocating form would have to invent them. The same reasoning as combine! and displace!.\nThe NUFFT was refactored onto these verbs, as the strongest available test of them. Its oversampled work grid covers the same box as the reference with more points, so the reference's modes are a subset of it and the scheme's own _nufft_embed_kernel!/_nufft_extract_kernel! were this operation in private form. They are gone, with _nufft_index_map and the three embed_map_* fields and their type parameter: 57 lines, three device arrays per plan, and a piece of index arithmetic that had to be kept in step with the layout by hand. The scheme is verified exact against DirectDFT, and that equality still holds — which is a test no fixture of this operation's own could give it.\nTwo differences had to be paid rather than assumed. The old map carried the reference's Nyquist shell to -N/2 of the oversampled grid where the shared set excludes it, which is consistent with the scheme's documented band but means its scratch must now be ZEROED, since the operation writes only shared modes and similar returns whatever was in memory — the first run after the refactor produced NaN for exactly that reason. And the scale factor moved: the embed site dropped its hand-applied total_oversampling because the operation carries that ratio, while the extract site multiplies by it, the operation dividing where the old kernel did nothing. The NUFFT had been applying the same physical-amplitude convention all along.\nThe names were chosen adversarially, against the alternatives truncate, project, inject, restrict, prolong, pad and a single copy_shared_modes!. truncate and chop are exported by Base and truncate already means de-aliasing in the consuming solver; project is spent here on two genuine idempotent projections; inject reverses direction between audiences and means energy injection in the calling module; restrict reads as this package's word for an invariant-1 absence; prolong/restrict are multigrid's PHYSICAL-space transfer operators and would become a lie the day a physical analogue arrives. The _modes suffix is earned by the same rule as the package's other verb-plus-object names: the bare verb is ambiguous, this package embedding points into cells and coarse spectra into oversampled ones elsewhere.\nThe Lamb vector is omega times u (2026-09-24, owner-decided). For a velocity field u with vorticity omega = nabla times u, the rotational form of the Navier–Stokes nonlinearity is u cdot nabla u = omega times u + nabla(tfrac12lvert urvert^2), and the package's documentation calls omega times u the Lamb vector. That is the literature's convention: Sagaut & Cambon (2018) name \"the Lamb vector omega times u\" (their Eq. 12.2 and the text after it), and Pope (2000) writes the opposite product U times omega in the same identity but never uses the name. The opposite sign, u times omega, is in circulation too, as Wikipedia's article shows, so the name without its sign is ambiguous.\nWhat it changed. The CPU example advances the Leray projection mathbbP of the transform of u times omega, and called u times omega the Lamb vector. The equation was right and the name was off by a sign; the prose now says the term is minus the Lamb vector. The code is unchanged, since it computes u times omega, which enters with the sign the equation needs.\nHow it surfaced. The first documentation review's differential-geometry reader flagged it (finding 004 of 2026-09-22), and the wording changed only after the sign was checked in Pope (2000) and Sagaut & Cambon (2018).\nA top-hat window of any width, and the cloud-in-cell window as its square (2026-09-26, owner-approved; requested by GBGHomogeneousTurbulence on 2026-09-22). The builder tophat_window(multipliers, width) returns the box average of edges l_a as a SeparableKernel whose per-axis factors are operatornamesinc(k_a l_a  2), taken from the true wavenumber component k_a so the window is real and even. Physically it is the normalized indicator of the box: convolving a field with it replaces the field at each point by its mean over the box centred there.\n\n- **Why the package builds it.** The consumer needed the kinetic energy held in scales below a size\n  ``l`` and the matching dissipation, both of which reduce exactly to spectral reductions weighted by\n  ``1 - \\hat W_l^2``, with ``\\hat W_l`` this window. The per-axis sinc is physics-free field\n  mathematics, and `SeparableKernel` has no public constructor, so the caller could not build it;\n  the diagnostic itself stays with the caller (invariant 12), as the pair-correlation recipe does\n  beside `assignment_window`.\n\n- **The window, not its square.** ``\\hat W`` is what acts on a field. Its square is the window\n  convolved with itself, which separable kernels already compose per axis at no cost (decision 50),\n  so shipping both would be adding an operation for completeness (invariant 1). The caller squares\n  it with `convolve(w, w)`.\n\n- **`assignment_window` is now that square at the grid spacing.** The cloud-in-cell tent is a\n  cell-wide top-hat convolved with itself, so the older builder became the composition of two\n  cell-wide windows. The result is bitwise the former closed form, because squaring a\n  floating-point factor is the same product either way; this was checked in both precisions on a\n  cubic grid and on an anisotropic grid with an odd axis. The two builders now share one formula.\n\n- **The name and the argument.** It is not `box_window`, because \"the box\" means the domain\n  throughout this package. The width is positional, since it changes the result: one number is a\n  cube, which is the consumer's case, and a triple a rectangular box, as `assignment_window` uses\n  one spacing per axis. A negative or non-finite edge is refused, a zero edge leaves its axis\n  unfiltered, and an edge as long as the domain is allowed: on a periodic domain it cancels every\n  nonzero wavenumber along that axis, so a cube the size of the domain keeps the mean alone.\n\n- **A continuum average.** The sinc is the transform of the continuum box, so the result is the\n  exact box mean of the band-limited field, not the mean of the grid values inside the box, even\n  for an edge of a whole number of cells, and it can exceed the range of those values.\n\n- **A caution passed to the consumer.** Batching many widths into one tuple reduction nests one\n  product kernel per width. Finding (aq) measured register pressure halving occupancy on the AMD\n  card in another kernel, a wide tuple accumulator is the kind of shape that raises it, and nested\n  lazy broadcasts are the fragile spot on that backend, so the batch size is to be swept rather than\n  assumed.\n\nAn operand count the caller chooses is worked through in groups of a fixed size (2026-09-26, owner-approved; reported by GBGHomogeneousTurbulence on 2026-09-26). Four operations take as many operands as their caller passes: add! and subtract! over sections (with the + and - they back), map! over sources, displace! over terms, and weighted_inner over a tuple of weights. Each was written as one pass over all of its operands.\nEach now handles at most a fixed number per pass — eight for the first three, four weights for the reduction — and continues from its own partial result. Within a pass every per-element step that indexes the operands is written out with a literal index per operand, generated per count.\nWhat broke. The consumer's weighted_inner with 32 weights did not compile on a device. The research the report prompted found the same shape in the other three, where the count had simply never been large: an unnamed trailing argument list (fields...), which Julia need not specialize on its length, and tuple maps and splats, which leave Base's specialized path at 32 entries for a generic one that allocates on a CPU and cannot run in a kernel. A device kernel's arguments are also limited in size, which bounds how many lazy operands one launch may carry.\nWhat it cost before. Measured on the CPU before the change, with every operand written out at the call site and a 24^3 vector field: at 33 operands add! allocated 775 MB per call, the 33-weight weighted_inner 139 MB, displace! 0.6 MB, and map! over six tensor fields 57 MB already at six sources. At 9 operands add! allocated 16.7 KB, still more than it does now at 33.\nA fixed size, not a tuned one. The owner raised the danger of tuning the group size to a backend or a card and proposed a fixed safe size instead. The limits above differ between vendors and drivers, a tuned constant would be right only on the machine that measured it, and nothing the package does needs a pass wider than the counts a solver passes, which fit in one group. Eight and four sit well below every ceiling named above. The alternative of recursing on argument lists was declined for the same reason as splatting: it relies on the compiler's heuristics rather than stating the count.\nGrouping changes no result. Each group continues the previous one's left-to-right order, so add! over 33 sections is bitwise the plain left fold, displace! over 20 terms bitwise the term-by-term sum, and the tuple weighted_inner on the CPU bitwise one single-weight call per weight. Two cases needed care. An operand that is also the destination would be overwritten by the first group before a later group reads it, so the later group reads a copy of it; and the torus form of displace! wraps once, after the last group, since wrapping between groups would round differently wherever an intermediate position crossed the boundary.\nWhat it costs. At one thread every grouped call allocates zero bytes at 9 and at 33 operands; threaded, a field add! pays Base.Threads task state once per pass; and weighted_inner allocates one single-weight call's worth per group of four (Performance finding (au)). Above one group, a pass per group re-reads what one pass would have read once: a tuple of 33 weights takes nine traversals. Measured, that is exactly the added traffic — add! past eight operands about 20–24% slower than one pass would be — and 33 grouped weights still beat 33 separate calls (finding (au)).\nThe first version broke a case the CPU suite passed. The per-element read was first an ntuple over a closure, i -> arrays[i][n]. When the operands are of different types, as in a kernel-composed combine! or convolve! where a radial kernel's lazy expression sits beside plain arrays, the compiler did not inline that closure and indexed the tuple at run time. On the CPU that ran, allocating 8.3 MB per call on a 16^3 vector field; on a device it did not compile.\nWhy only the device saw it, and the repair. The CUDA suite caught it, and the CPU allocation audit had not, its kernel-composed rows using only a stored kernel, whose operands are all plain arrays. The read is now a generated function with a literal index per operand, back to the 16 bytes the call cost before, and the audit has rows for every compressed kernel class, which fail against the closure form.\nThe repair itself cost speed until the canary caught it. @inline cannot annotate a @generated function, so the generated read stayed a call inside the executor's @simd loop and blocked vectorization: the mul! canary ran 32times slower on one CPU thread and 135times slower on the device, sym! 21times and 95times. Every test passed throughout, the pass being correct and allocation-free. The generated bodies now carry their own inline marker, and the canary reads flat on the device and at eight threads and faster at one: mul! 7.1 to 5.7 ms, sym! 13.1 to 9.3 ms.\nThe exhaustive survey found the map path past 32 sources. Its first run with sources past the group size reported the allocating map over 33 sources inferring a Union of every tensor order. The cause was three Base.map calls over the source tuple in the shared driver, which leave the specialized path from 32 elements.\nAnd what the survey's type check could not see. Chasing it found that map! over 33 sources allocated about 1.9 KB per point, 38 MB per call on a 24^3 vector field, in a per-point recursion over the sources whose results were splatted into φ. A generated tuple map replaces the three calls and a generated call replaces the recursion; both now read zero bytes and a concrete type, with map! unchanged at ordinary counts.\nHow it is pinned. The file test/caller_counts.jl sweeps each operation past its group size and past 32 operands against raw-array arithmetic, including a destination that aliases a later operand; test/allocations.jl asserts the serial zero with every operand written out at the call site, a splat of more than 32 values allocating in the caller; and the GPU suite runs each class past the old ceiling on the device. The operand count is Testing axis 23. Two falsification runs confirmed the new tests bite: removing the aliased-operand copy fails 28 assertions, and wrapping after every group fails 6.\nThe four audit harnesses are declared to GBGCodingTools (2026-09-27, owner-ruled). The allocation audit, the element-traffic audit, the oracle registry and method parity now call the tool's engines, GBGCodingTools.TestHarness, in place of the copies each test file defined. The tool's engines were extracted from those same files, so every row, frozen count and exemption stays in PF; what moved is the machinery that reads them.\nWhy. The rulebook's four harness rules bind only a package that declares the harness, and a package declares one by calling the tool's engine from its suite. Undeclared, the rules did not bind PF, so CLAUDE.md's own statements of the audits were the only binding text and could not be cut as restatements of the rulebook.\nTwo counts moved, and no method was added. The tool's surface walk descends into Base's submodules, so it sees the Base.Broadcast.broadcastable methods of Fields and Points, which PF's own scan never did: the derived method counts are refrozen at 177 and 235, and broadcastable is classified in the oracle registry as structural, returning the section itself.\nWhat was kept of PF's. The frozen method counts moved to test/method_parity_baseline.toml, their refreeze history with them. The oracle table stays in Julia, being partly generated and heavily annotated, and is handed to the engine in the shape it reads. PASS_AUDIT_PRINT=1 still regenerates the traffic table.\nVerified. Each ported file passes unchanged, and each fails on a planted defect, naming it: a removed allocation row (wrap! uncovered), a removed traffic row (transpose! uncovered), an altered frozen traffic count, an altered method count, and a removed oracle entry.\nRemoving a field's mean, and evaluating a kernel into existing storage, are named operations (2026-09-28, owner-ruled). The consumer GBGHomogeneousTurbulence asked PF to review how it uses PF, and two of its workarounds turned out to be missing operations rather than misuse. mean_free_projection!(f) sets the mean of a field in spectral representation to exactly zero, and materialize_kernel!(destination, kernel) writes a kernel's coefficients into a StoredKernel that already exists.\nWhy a projector, rather than guidance. The consumer zeroed its nonlinear term's mean by writing into the stored coefficient at index (1 1 1), which is correct under every layout but is knowledge PF owns. The first answer drafted for it was a recipe: fold a kernel that vanishes at k = 0 into a pass it already makes, relying on how the projection treats that mode, that composition multiplies every other mode by exactly one, and that the ordinary cutoff must not change because it also truncates initial conditions. A consumer should not need four facts about the implementation to take a mean out, so the operation was named instead.\nWhy subtracting the mean does not serve. Subtracting mean(f) is not a substitute: it reads the value back to the host, leaves round-off at a grid size that is not a power of two, and clears only the real part, mean being real.\nWhat mean_free_projection! is. The L^2-orthogonal projector onto the complement of the constants, f mapsto f - lvertOmegarvert^-1 int_Omega f mathrmdx, the companion of reality_projection! and helmholtz_projection!. In spectral representation it writes zero into the zero coefficient of every fiber component, both parts, and touches nothing else: one write per component, no read, no copy, no readback, so it is exactly idempotent and its result's mean is exactly zero.\nSpectral representation only, by the owner's ruling. Invariant 1 has operations linear in the field exist in both representations. In physical space the mean occupies no single stored value, so the projector would be a full pass leaving round-off, which subtract!(f, f, mean(f)) already is; that form stays the route there, and the absence gate asserts the physical call throws.\nNo \"set the mean to a value\". The consumer asked for zero or a given constant. Setting it to c is the projection followed by add!(f, f, c), which already writes only the zero mode (invariant 4), so only the projection is new.\nWhy materialize_kernel!. The consumer's steady forcing held a band indicator as a raw array for the whole run (1.69 GiB at 768^3 in Float64) and multiplied it into a rate field every step. Once the rate is filled only when the forcing is built, the band can stay a lazy kernel, but filling a field with its coefficients then cost a field-sized temporary per call of materialize_kernel — two calls at construction, 3.38 GiB each at 768^3, on a device the consumer already runs out of.\nWhy a name rather than the existing route. An allocation-free route existed: fill the field with ones, the spectrum of a unit impulse, and convolve it with the kernel. It rests on a fact about Fourier spikes a consumer should not need, so the operation was named. Mathematically it is the in-place twin of materialize_kernel, as translation_kernel! is of translation_kernel.\nThe destination is a StoredKernel, not a field. Decision 60 separates a field from a kernel, and materialize_kernel returns a StoredKernel, so its twin writes one. A caller holding a field wraps it once as StoredKernel(field), which references the field rather than copying it. No scale argument: a multiply! afterwards serves, the evaluation being a construction-time cost.\nThe divergence's zero mean is now stated. The consumer's scalar right-hand side zeroed a mean that the divergence already computes as exactly zero, every derivative wavenumber being zero at that mode. The divergence! docstring had only implied it, so it now says so, with its one qualifier (wavenumbers built from the grid), and test/differentialoperators.jl pins it; the consumer deletes its zeroing on the strength of that sentence. The reality_residual docstring likewise now states its units, the unnormalized stored coefficients.\nDeclined in the same review. A mode count for a band (the consumer's own loop is exact and runs on a tiny grid) and a relative form of reality_residual (the consumer's comparison with the largest stored coefficient is the right one, now documented).\nSections compare by value, and adapt! refuses every mismatch with a reason (2026-09-28, owner-ruled). Two changes from the second round of the consumer's API review.\nWhy equality. u == v on two fields fell back to Base's default, which compares identity: two fields holding identical values compared unequal, and nothing warned the caller. Equality of two sections is well defined, so ==, isequal and hash are now defined for fields, point-value sections and point sets. Two sections are equal when they are the same kind on the same base (representation, tensor order, grid and layout for a field; tensor order and point count for point values) and every stored value is equal.\nWhat is refused rather than answered. A field in physical representation against one in spectral representation may be two representations of one field, so false would be wrong and true would need a transform; the pair throws. Different element types or storage families throw as invariant 14 has every two-section operation do. Everything else that differs gives false.\nThe value semantics are Julia's arrays'. Under ==, -0.0 equals 0.0 and NaN equals nothing; isequal distinguishes the signed zeros and equates NaN with itself, which makes it the bit-identical test. The hash is built from the kind and base alone, not the values, so it is consistent with isequal and reads nothing from device storage.\nWhy adapt! changed. A pair differing in element type or tensor order matched no method, the constraints being in the signatures, so it met Julia's generic MethodError, which names no reason. The consumer wrote its own pre-checks over raw storage to get one, and invariant 14 promises a clear ArgumentError. The checks now run inside the method, each naming the property that differs and both values, the grid message showing both grids. A catch-all method for the mismatched pairs was tried first and discarded: Julia's dispatch preferred it for a pair it should not have reached, reporting a grid mismatch as a tensor-order one.\nA defect found on the way. The point-set method checked only the point count, so a Float64 source written into a Float32 point set was rounded without complaint, the silent conversion invariant 14 forbids. It is refused now, and test/regressions/pointset-adapt-converts-precision.jl pins it.","title":"Decision log — GBGPeriodicFields"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#FieldAlgebra","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"","title":"FieldAlgebra"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Vector-space-and-scalar-field-scaling-operations","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"","title":"Vector-space and scalar-field-scaling operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Contraction-and-product-operations","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"The allocating wrapper of mul! is *, which is documented once — with the scalar-field scaling it also serves — under Vector-space and scalar-field-scaling operations above, rather than repeated here.","title":"Contraction and product operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Transposition-and-decomposition-operations","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"","title":"Transposition and decomposition operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Norm-operations","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"","title":"Norm operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Arbitrary-fiber-map","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"Point-values take the same two verbs, in Points; see map! for the aliasing caveats.","title":"Arbitrary fiber map"},{"category":"section","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Internal-API","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"These symbols are not part of the public interface; they are documented for contributors — two storage helpers the generated operations share: one copies a field's data into a destination for the paths that cannot assume it starts empty, the other reshapes a scalar field so it broadcasts against a fiber.\n\nThe code generators themselves are not here. They live in FiberSections, which is what lets FieldAlgebra and Points stamp the same formulas over their different bases, and the per-operation methods @eval produces from them carry no docstrings of their own — the formula is documented on the builder.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"FieldAlgebra\n\nFiberwise and pointwise algebra for the scalar, vector, and second-order tensor fields defined in Fields. Each operation comes as an allocating form — a Base or LinearAlgebra operator where one exists — and an in-place ! form.\n\nField-field and field-constant operations preserve the representation of their arguments: they act pointwise on fields in physical representation and coefficient-wise on fields in spectral representation. An operation that is not linear in a field argument — multiplying two fields together, or contracting two fields — is defined only in physical representation, since only a linear map commutes with the rfft isomorphism; the exception is a fiber-constant factor, the same at every point, which keeps the operation linear and available in both. The missing combinations are deliberate.\n\nThree words recur below. The base is the set of locations a section attaches a value to — the grid's nodes for a field — so a formula that is \"pointwise over the base\" applies at each of them independently and touches no neighbours. A fiber is the value at one base point, a scalar, a FiberVector or a FiberTensor2. A section is one fiber at every point of a base, which is what a field and a set of point-values each are, and that shared shape is why one set of formulas serves both.\n\nThe parts are:\n\nVector-space and scalar-field-scaling operations — add! and subtract! (+, -), the weighted combination combine! of two fields, real scaling, and scalar-field multiplication and division (multiply!, divide!, *, /). Addition, weighted combination, and real scaling are the vector-space structure; scalar-field multiplication is the action that makes fields a module over the ring of scalar fields — the algebraic sense of the word, not a Julia module: scaling a field by another field rather than by a number.\nContraction and product operations — dot! computes the vector Euclidean and tensor Frobenius inner products (⋅), cross! the cross product (×), mul! the tensor-on-vector action (left and right) and the tensor-on-tensor product — contracted (matrix) products, not the outer tensor product — with a five-argument α/β accumulation form and an allocating *, tr! the trace, the self-contraction of a tensor's own two indices, and det! the determinant, the tensor's third principal invariant.\nTransposition and decomposition operations — transpose! transposes the fiber indices, and sym, skew, dev split off the symmetric, skew-symmetric, and deviatoric parts (each with a ! form).\nNorm operations — norm! computes the pointwise absolute-value, Euclidean, and Frobenius norms.\nArbitrary fiber map — map! and map apply any fiber map φ you supply at every base point — a composition like exp ∘ det, a pointwise equation of state, whatever is not one of the named operations above. This is the section-valued escape hatch for un-named maps (and the sibling of mapped reductions such as mean(det, A)): φ may change the tensor order, and the result's order is φ's (map(det, A) gives a scalar field). A field must be in physical representation, since an arbitrary φ is nonlinear.\n\nwarning: Nonlinear results are spectrally aliased\n(Spectral aliasing here — content folding back onto resolved modes — not the storage aliasing of the admonition below, which is about two arguments sharing memory. The two senses are unrelated.) A nonlinear operation — a field-field product (*, mul!, dot, cross, divide!) or a nonlinear fiber invariant (det, norm) — generates content above the grid's Nyquist wavenumber, so its result is not band-limited — it carries wavenumbers the grid cannot represent. The pointwise values at the grid nodes are exact, but transforming the result to spectral representation folds that out-of-band content back onto the resolved modes as aliasing error. Before feeding such a result into a spectral computation — a solver's nonlinear term — de-alias it with the sharp low-pass spectral_cutoff or the phase-shift translation_kernel from Convolution. This is the same fact that makes these operations physical-representation only: a nonlinearity has no finite-band spectral form.\n\nThe mul! product follows the LinearAlgebra convention that the destination must not share storage with an input, while the index-shuffling operations (cross, transpose, sym, skew, dev) are alias-safe by construction (each pass reads an element's sources before writing any output) and may be called with the destination aliasing an input.\n\nnote: Why an aliased in-place call is safe\ndestination may be an input — sym!(A, A) is computed directly and is bitwise-identical to the non-aliased call — because each pass reads all of an element's sources into registers before writing any of that element's outputs. That argument needs one section's element n to be the other's element n, and it is the ownership rule that supplies it: a section's constructor refuses borrowed storage, so two sections can share memory only by being backed by the same array. Two partially-overlapping views at different base indices would break the argument, and they are not merely undetected — they cannot be constructed. The mul! operation is the one exception, keeping LinearAlgebra's contract that its destination must not alias.\n\nMost of this module is mirrored onto point-values (AbstractPointValues) — the same fiberwise algebra applied to field values sampled at an unstructured point set, generated from the same builders so no formula is written twice. The mirror covers the physical-representation surface, not every binding: the weighted combination combine! has no point-value method, for instance. See the Points module for what the point layer provides.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.add!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"add!(destination, a, b, fields...)\nadd!(destination, field, constant)\nadd!(destination, constant, field)\n\nAdd fields, or a field and a constant, in place, and return destination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating + returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field that receives the sum; it shares the domain and tensor order of the operands.\na, b, fields... — two or more fields to add.\nfield, constant — the field-and-constant variants, in either argument order: a Real with a scalar field, a FiberVector with a vector field, a FiberTensor2 with a second-order tensor field.\n\nField-field addition is elementwise and representation-preserving: it acts pointwise on fields in physical representation and coefficient-wise on fields in spectral representation.\n\nIn physical representation the constant is added at every grid point. In spectral representation only the zero Fourier coefficient changes, scaled by the number of grid points prod(grid_size), so that the physical values shift by exactly the constant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.add!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Base.:+","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"+(a, b, fields...)\n\nAllocating form of add!: add fields or point-value collections — or a section and a compatible constant — and return a new section. The unary + returns its section unchanged. See add! for the componentwise definition and the representation-validity rules.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:+"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.subtract!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"subtract!(destination, a, b, fields...)\nsubtract!(destination, field, constant)\nsubtract!(destination, constant, field)\n\nSubtract fields, or a field and a constant, in place, and return destination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating - returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field that receives the difference.\na, b, fields... — the fields to subtract in order.\nfield, constant — the field-and-constant variants, with the constant typed as in add!; the reversed order computes constant - field.\n\nField-field subtraction subtracts b, then each of fields in turn, from a; it is elementwise and representation-preserving. In physical representation the constant is subtracted at every grid point; in spectral representation it changes only the zero Fourier coefficient. The reversed argument order computes constant - field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.subtract!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Base.:-","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"-(a, b)\n\nAllocating form of subtract!: subtract fields or point-value collections (or negate one) and return a new section. See subtract! for the componentwise definition and the representation-validity rules.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:-"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.combine!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"combine!(destination, α::Real, a, β::Real, b)\ncombine!(destination, α::Real, a, β::Real, b, kernel::SpectralKernel)\n\nOverwrite destination with the real-weighted linear combination alpha a + beta b of two compatible fields, and return destination.\n\nReach for combine! with a preallocated destination when you need a real-weighted combination of two fields in a single allocation-free pass. It fills the gap between add!, which is unit-weight (a + b), and multiply!, which scales a single field; the allocating equivalent is the operator expression alpha a + beta b, which builds scratch fields instead. The combination is linear and representation-preserving, so it is defined for both physical and spectral representation.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field that receives the result. The write is coefficientwise, so a, b, or both may be destination itself, and so may a trailing kernel; the aliased call combine!(u, 1, u, β, b) computes the scaled accumulate u leftarrow u + beta b in place.\nα, a — the real weight and the first field.\nβ, b — the real weight and the second field.\n\ntextttdestination = alpha  a + beta  b\n\nThe kernel-composed form\n\nPassing a trailing kernel — a kernel's transfer function hat g, held either as one coefficient per mode or in a compressed class evaluated per mode, exactly as convolve! takes it — convolves the combination with the kernel in the same pass. As there, hat g must be Hermitian between opposite modes k and -k, hat g(-k) = overlinehat g(k), or the result is the spectrum of no real field. Physically the result is the combination's periodic convolution with the physical kernel, g star (alpha a + beta b); in spectral representation it is one coefficientwise product,\n\nwidehattextttdestination(k) = hat g(k)  bigl(alpha hat a(k) + beta hat b(k)bigr)\n\nreplacing a combine! followed by a convolve! — one pass instead of two, saving a full read and a full write of destination per call. This form exists only in spectral representation (a kernel is a spectral object); α/β keep their combination-weight roles, and with b === destination it computes the kernel-filtered accumulate in place. The scalar kernel broadcasts over the fiber, so one method serves every tensor order, and the write stays coefficientwise.\n\nThe additional argument is:\n\nkernel — the transfer function hat g, in any of its storage classes, exactly as convolve! takes it.\n\nnote: Here α and β weight the two inputs, not the destination\nAlmost every in-place operation in the package uses trailing α, β that accumulate a result onto destination: alpha (mathrmresult) + beta textttdestination. This operation is one of two exceptions — the other is exponential_convolve!'s two-operand forms, which carry this same combination into a fused convolution — and it is the exception the name announces: it is the weighted combination, so its α and β sit between the operands they weight and there is no accumulate form — pass b === destination when you want one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.combine!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.multiply!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"multiply!(destination, scalar::Real, field)\nmultiply!(destination, field, scalar::Real)\nmultiply!(destination, scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical, field)\nmultiply!(destination, scalar_field, constant)\n\nMultiply a field in place by a real number or by a scalar field, and return destination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating * returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field that receives the product.\nscalar — a Real factor, accepted in either argument order.\nscalar_field — a scalar field factor, multiplying the other operand pointwise.\nfield, constant — the operand being scaled: a field of any tensor order, or a constant FiberVector or FiberTensor2 paired with a scalar field.\n\nMultiplication by a real number is representation-preserving and is defined for fields in both physical and spectral representation, in either argument order.\n\nMultiplication by a scalar field is pointwise, so when the other factor is a field it is defined only in physical representation; in spectral representation the product of two fields is a convolution, which this module does not provide. The field-field result is not band-limited, so it aliases if transformed back to spectral representation; de-alias (spectral_cutoff) before spectral reuse. When the other factor is a constant FiberVector or FiberTensor2, the operation is linear in the scalar field and stays coefficient-wise, so it is defined in both representations and yields the corresponding vector or tensor field. Either argument order is accepted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.multiply!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Base.:*","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"*(a, b)\n\nAllocating form of multiply! and, for the tensor contractions, mul!: a real scale, a scalar-field action, or a tensor contraction — field-field (a tensor field acting on a vector or tensor field, physical representation only) as well as fiber-constant — returning a new section (fields and point-value collections alike). See those for the componentwise definitions. There is deliberately no field-times-field method in spectral representation — that coefficientwise product is a convolution and lives under convolve's name, in the Convolution module.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:*"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.divide!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"divide!(destination, field, scalar::Real)\ndivide!(destination, field, scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical)\ndivide!(destination, scalar::Real, scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical)\ndivide!(destination, a::FiberVector, scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical)\ndivide!(destination, A::FiberTensor2, scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical)\n\nDivide a field in place, and return destination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating / returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field that receives the quotient.\nfield — a dividend field of any tensor order.\nscalar — a Real: the divisor after field, or the dividend before scalar_field.\na, A — a constant FiberVector or FiberTensor2 dividend.\nscalar_field — a scalar field divisor, dividing pointwise.\n\nDivision by a real number is representation-preserving and is defined in both representations. Division by a scalar field is pointwise and is defined only in physical representation; the numerator may be a field of any tensor order (the division acts on all of its components), a real number (yielding a scalar field), or a constant FiberVector or FiberTensor2 (yielding the corresponding vector or tensor field). When the divisor is a scalar field the result is not band-limited, so it aliases if transformed back to spectral representation; de-alias (spectral_cutoff) before spectral reuse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.divide!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Base.:/","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"/(a, b)\n\nAllocating form of divide!: divide a field or point-value collection by a real — or, in physical representation only, by a scalar section — and return a new section. See divide! for the componentwise definition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:/"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dot!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"dot!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, u::VectorFieldPhysical,\n     v::VectorFieldPhysical)\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2},\n     B::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, operand::VectorFieldPhysical,\n     constant::FiberVector)\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldSpectral, operand::VectorFieldSpectral,\n     constant::FiberVector)\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, operand::TensorFieldPhysical{2},\n     constant::FiberTensor2)\ndot!(destination::ScalarFieldSpectral, operand::TensorFieldSpectral{2},\n     constant::FiberTensor2)\n\nCompute a fiberwise inner product in place, and return destination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating dot returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the scalar field that receives the contraction.\nu, v — two vector fields, or A, B two second-order tensor fields.\noperand, constant — a field and a constant FiberVector or FiberTensor2 contracted against it, in either argument order.\n\nFor two vector fields this is the Euclidean scalar product; for two second-order tensor fields it is the Frobenius double contraction. Either argument order is accepted.\n\nContraction of two fields is nonlinear and is defined only in physical representation. In spectral representation only contraction with a constant fiber vector or fiber tensor is provided, which keeps the operation coefficient-wise. The field-field result is not band-limited, so it aliases if transformed back to spectral representation; de-alias (spectral_cutoff) before spectral reuse.\n\nEach base point x gives, summing over the repeated fiber indices:\n\nbegingathered\n(u cdot v)(x) = u_i(x)  v_i(x)  (A  B)(x) = A_ij(x)  B_ij(x)\nendgathered\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dot!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.dot","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"dot(u::VectorFieldPhysical, v::VectorFieldPhysical)\ndot(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, B::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\n\nAllocating form of dot!: allocate the scalar-field result and return it. For two vector fields this is their pointwise Euclidean product; for two second-order tensor fields, their pointwise Frobenius product. Two fields contract only in physical representation; with a constant fiber the contraction stays coefficient-wise and is available in both representations. The infix alias cdot (⋅) is also exported. See dot! for the componentwise formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.dot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.:⋅","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"dot(u::VectorFieldPhysical, v::VectorFieldPhysical)\ndot(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, B::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\n\nAllocating form of dot!: allocate the scalar-field result and return it. For two vector fields this is their pointwise Euclidean product; for two second-order tensor fields, their pointwise Frobenius product. Two fields contract only in physical representation; with a constant fiber the contraction stays coefficient-wise and is available in both representations. The infix alias cdot (⋅) is also exported. See dot! for the componentwise formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.:⋅"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.cross!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"cross!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, u::VectorFieldPhysical,\n       v::VectorFieldPhysical)\ncross!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, u::VectorFieldPhysical, a::FiberVector)\ncross!(destination::VectorFieldSpectral, u::VectorFieldSpectral, a::FiberVector)\n\nCompute a fiberwise cross product (cross, times) in place, and return destination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating cross returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the vector field that receives the product.\nu, v — the two vector fields.\na — a constant FiberVector crossed with the field. Both orders have methods, and being a cross product they differ in sign: a times u = -(u times a).\n\nCross products of two fields are nonlinear and are defined only in physical representation. In spectral representation only the cross product with a constant fiber vector (in either argument order) is provided, which keeps the operation coefficient-wise. The field-field result is not band-limited, so it aliases if transformed back to spectral representation; de-alias (spectral_cutoff) before spectral reuse.\n\nPointwise over the base, with the Levi-Civita symbol varepsilon_ijk (summed over the repeated j k), the components are\n\nbegingathered\n(u times v)_i = varepsilon_ijk  u_j v_k \n(u times v)_1 = u_2 v_3 - u_3 v_2 \n(u times v)_2 = u_3 v_1 - u_1 v_3 \n(u times v)_3 = u_1 v_2 - u_2 v_1\nendgathered\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.cross!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.cross","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"cross(u::VectorFieldPhysical, v::VectorFieldPhysical)\n\nAllocating form of cross!: allocate the vector-field result u times v and return it. Two vector fields cross only in physical representation; with a constant fiber vector the operation stays coefficient-wise and is available in both representations. The infix alias times (×) is also exported. See cross! for the componentwise formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.cross"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.:×","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"cross(u::VectorFieldPhysical, v::VectorFieldPhysical)\n\nAllocating form of cross!: allocate the vector-field result u times v and return it. Two vector fields cross only in physical representation; with a constant fiber vector the operation stays coefficient-wise and is available in both representations. The infix alias times (×) is also exported. See cross! for the componentwise formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.:×"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.mul!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"mul!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2},\n     u::VectorFieldPhysical[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2},\n     a::FiberVector[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::VectorFieldSpectral, A::FiberTensor2,\n     u::VectorFieldSpectral[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2},\n     B::TensorFieldPhysical{2}[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2},\n     B::FiberTensor2[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, u::VectorFieldPhysical,\n     A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}[, α, β])\nmul!(destination::VectorFieldPhysical, a::FiberVector,\n     A::TensorFieldPhysical{2}[, α, β])\n\nCompute a fiberwise tensor (matrix) product in place, and return destination. The three-argument forms compute the tensor-on-vector left and right action, or the tensor-on-tensor product; the five-argument form scales and accumulates as textttdestination = alpha (A B) + beta textttdestination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating * returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the field written with the product. It must not share storage with either operand, and that is not checked: mul! keeps LinearAlgebra's contrary contract, where every other in-place operation here is alias-safe by construction.\nA, B — the tensor operands, each a second-order tensor field or a constant FiberTensor2.\nu, a — the vector operand, a vector field u or a constant FiberVector a. The operands come in the order they multiply, so the signatures above cover both the left action (A u)_i = A_ij u_j and the right action (u A)_i = u_j A_ji. At least one operand must be a field, since a product of two constants is not a field operation.\nα, β — real accumulation weights, converted to the field's scalar type. The three-argument forms overwrite (alpha = 1, beta = 0); the five-argument forms compute alpha (A B) + beta textttdestination, so beta = 0 overwrites and any other value accumulates — the LinearAlgebra.mul! convention.\n\nA product of two fields is nonlinear and is defined only in physical representation; a spectral-representation product requires one factor to be a constant fiber tensor or fiber vector. The field-field result is not band-limited, so it aliases if transformed back to spectral representation; de-alias (spectral_cutoff) before spectral reuse.\n\nEach base point gives, summing over the repeated fiber indices:\n\nbegingathered\n(A u)_i = A_ij u_j \n(u A)_i = u_j A_ji \n(A B)_ij = A_ik B_kj\nendgathered\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.mul!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.tr!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"tr!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ntr!(destination::ScalarFieldSpectral, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nCompute the fiberwise trace (tr, operatornametr) of the second-order tensor field A in place, writing it into destination, which is returned. The trace is linear and representation-preserving, so it is defined for both representations.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating tr returns a fresh result instead.\n\nPointwise over the base, it contracts the two fiber indices to a scalar — the first tensor invariant:\n\noperatornametr(A) = A_ii = A_11 + A_22 + A_33\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.tr!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.tr","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"tr(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ntr(A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nAllocating form of tr!: allocate the scalar-field result and return the fiberwise trace — the first principal invariant I_1 — of a second-order tensor field. See tr! for the componentwise definition and the physical/spectral validity. (A single FiberTensor2 value already has LinearAlgebra.tr.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.tr"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.det!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"det!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\n\nCompute the fiberwise determinant (det, det) of a second-order tensor field in place, and return destination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating det returns a fresh result instead.\n\nThe determinant is cubic in the tensor's components — the third principal invariant I_3, the signed factor by which A scales volumes as a linear map. Unlike the linear trace tr!, it is nonlinear, so it is defined in physical representation only; there is no spectral form. Its result is not band-limited, so it aliases if transformed back to spectral representation; de-alias (spectral_cutoff) before spectral reuse.\n\nEach base point gives, with the Levi-Civita symbol varepsilon_ijk (summed over the repeated fiber indices):\n\ndet(A) = varepsilon_ijk  A_1i  A_2j  A_3k\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.det!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.det","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"det(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\n\nAllocating form of det!: allocate the scalar-field result and return the fiberwise determinant — the third principal invariant I_3 — of a second-order tensor field. It exists in physical representation only; det! gives the formula. (A single FiberTensor2 has LinearAlgebra.det.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.det"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.transpose!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"transpose!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ntranspose!(destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nTranspose the fiber indices of the second-order tensor field A in place (transpose equiv (cdot)^top), writing the result into destination, which is returned.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating transpose returns a fresh result instead.\n\nThe operation is linear and representation-preserving, so it is defined for both physical and spectral representations. Unlike Julia's matrix transpose, it writes a materialized tensor field rather than a lazy wrapper.\n\nEach base point gives:\n\ntextttdestination_ij = A_ji\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.transpose!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Base.transpose","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"transpose(A)\n\nAllocating form of transpose!: allocate the result and return the transpose A^top of a second-order tensor field, in either representation. See transpose! for the componentwise definition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.transpose"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.sym!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"sym!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\nsym!(destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nCompute the symmetric part of the second-order tensor field A in place, writing it into destination, which is returned. The operation is linear and representation-preserving, so it is defined for both representations.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating sym returns a fresh result instead.\n\nEach base point gives:\n\noperatornamesym(A) = tfrac12 (A + A^top)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.sym!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.sym","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"sym(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\nsym(A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\nsym(A::FiberTensor2)\n\nAllocating form of sym!: allocate the result and return the symmetric part of a second-order tensor: fiberwise for a tensor field, or directly on a single FiberTensor2 value. See sym! for the defining formula and the physical/spectral validity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.sym"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.skew!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"skew!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\nskew!(destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nCompute the skew-symmetric part of the second-order tensor field A in place, writing it into destination, which is returned. The operation is linear and representation-preserving, so it is defined for both representations.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating skew returns a fresh result instead.\n\nEach base point gives:\n\noperatornameskew(A) = tfrac12 (A - A^top)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.skew!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.skew","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"skew(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\nskew(A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\nskew(A::FiberTensor2)\n\nAllocating form of skew!: allocate the result and return the skew-symmetric part of a second-order tensor: fiberwise for a tensor field, or directly on a single FiberTensor2 value. See skew! for the defining formula and the physical/spectral validity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.skew"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dev!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"dev!(destination::TensorFieldPhysical{2}, A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ndev!(destination::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\n\nCompute the deviatoric (trace-free) part of the second-order tensor field A in place, writing it into destination, which is returned. The operation is linear and representation-preserving, so it is defined for both representations.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating dev returns a fresh result instead.\n\nEach base point gives, with I the identity tensor:\n\noperatornamedev(A) = A - tfrac13 operatornametr(A) I\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dev!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dev","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"dev(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\ndev(A::TensorFieldSpectral{2})\ndev(A::FiberTensor2)\n\nAllocating form of dev!: allocate the result and return the deviatoric part of a second-order tensor: fiberwise for a tensor field, or directly on a single FiberTensor2 value. See dev! for the defining formula and the physical/spectral validity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.dev"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.norm!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"norm!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, operand::ScalarFieldPhysical)\nnorm!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, operand::VectorFieldPhysical)\nnorm!(destination::ScalarFieldPhysical, operand::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\n\nCompute the fiberwise norm of a field in physical representation (norm, cdot) in place, and return destination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating norm returns a fresh result instead.\n\nThe norm is nonlinear, so it is defined only in physical representation. Its result is not band-limited, so it aliases if transformed back to spectral representation; de-alias (spectral_cutoff) before spectral reuse.\n\nEach base point gives the absolute value of a scalar varphi, the Euclidean norm of a vector u, and the Frobenius norm (not the operator norm) of a second-order tensor A:\n\nbegingathered\nvarphi \nu = sqrtu_i u_i \nA = sqrtA_ij A_ij\nendgathered\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra.norm!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#LinearAlgebra.norm","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"norm(scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical)\nnorm(u::VectorFieldPhysical)\nnorm(A::TensorFieldPhysical{2})\n\nAllocating form of norm!: allocate the scalar-field result and return it — the pointwise magnitude of a scalar, vector or tensor field. It exists in physical representation only; norm! gives the formula.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.norm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Base.map!","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"map!(φ, destination, sources...)\nmap!(φ, destination, source, α, β)\n\nApply an arbitrary fiber map φ at every base point, writing into and returning destination — the section-valued companion of the mapped reductions (mean(det, A), maximum(norm, u)): where those reduce φ of the fiber over the base, this writes it as a field. It runs in one pass, building no intermediate section, in parallel (a device kernel on a GPU, a loop threaded over slabs on a CPU); being a per-point transform with disjoint writes, it is bitwise thread-count-invariant.\n\nUse this when the operation is not one of the named fiberwise operations (det, norm, the algebra) — an arbitrary pointwise function of the fiber, a composition such as exp ∘ det included. Prefer the named operations where they exist (they carry their validity and their spectral forms); map! is the escape hatch for the unnamed, physical-only maps.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating map returns a fresh result instead.\n\nAt every base point x, destination's fiber is set to φ applied to the sources' fibers there; map!(φ, dest, f, g) sets it to φ(f(x), g(x)).\n\nArguments\n\nφ — the fiber map, applied to the input fiber value(s) and returning the output fiber value. It must be a pure, GPU-safe function (no captured state, no scalar indexing of device arrays); its output tensor order sets destination's order (det gives a scalar field, an SMatrix-valued φ a tensor field). It must also read only the fiber it is given — see the warning below.\ndestination — the field (or point-value section) receiving the result; its tensor order must match φ's output, and it may alias any of the sources.\nsources — one or more compatible fields (or point-value sections) on destination's base; φ takes their fibers in argument order.\nsource — the one input of the accumulating form, a field (or point-value section) as for sources.\nα, β — the accumulation of the single-source form, textttdestination = alpha varphi(textttsource) + beta textttdestination (β = 0 overwrites, the mul! convention); the variadic form overwrites (α = 1, β = 0).\n\nwarning: A fiber map must not read the destination\nThe pass writes each base point independently and in an unspecified order, so φ may read only the fiber handed to it. A φ that reads elsewhere in the destination sees storage another thread may already have overwritten, and the result depends on the schedule — it will differ between runs, and between a threaded and a serial one. A closure that captures the destination cannot be detected, so that much is the caller's responsibility.\n\nwarning: Physical-space only, and the result is aliased in spectral representation\nAn arbitrary φ is nonlinear, so — like the other nonlinear operations of this module — map! is defined for fields in physical representation only, and its result carries content above the grid's Nyquist: it is aliased once transformed to spectral representation. De-alias before spectral reuse (see the module docstring). Point-value sections are representation-free and carry no such restriction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.map!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#Base.map","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"map(φ, sources...)\n\nAllocating form of map!: apply the fiber map φ at every base point and return a fresh section holding the result. The output's tensor order is inferred from φ (map(det, A) returns a scalar field); when it cannot be inferred, call map! with a preallocated destination. See map! for the arguments and the physical-representation-only / aliasing caveats.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._copy_field_data!-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}, Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_copy_field_data!(destination::AbstractField, field::AbstractField)\n\nCopy field's storage into destination's storage unless they already alias; returns destination. Used by copy-then-kernel paths that must not assume the destination begins empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._copy_field_data!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/FieldAlgebra/#GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._scalar_field_broadcast_data-Tuple{ScalarFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldPhysical}","page":"FieldAlgebra","text":"_scalar_field_broadcast_data(scalar_field::ScalarFieldPhysical,\n                             field::AbstractFieldPhysical)\n\nReshape a scalar field's data with singleton fiber dimensions so it broadcasts against the fiber components of field. Used by pointwise scalar-field multiplication and division so the scalar acts on every component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra._scalar_field_broadcast_data"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Points","page":"Points","text":"","title":"Points"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Point-and-point-set-types","page":"Points","text":"","title":"Point and point-set types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Point-value-types","page":"Points","text":"","title":"Point-value types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Point-accessor-functions","page":"Points","text":"","title":"Point accessor functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Point-value-allocation","page":"Points","text":"","title":"Point-value allocation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Point-value-algebra","page":"Points","text":"The point values carry the same fiberwise algebra as fields in physical representation, through the very same generics extended to accept AbstractPointValues. There is no second set of docstrings: each operation is documented once, on the FieldAlgebra page.\n\nThe weighted combination combine! is the one deliberate absence, and not for a mathematical reason: it is linear and coefficientwise, so it would be perfectly well defined on point values. It exists on fields because a solver's stage updates are the one place the fused axpby saves a whole pass over a field; nothing needs it on points, so it has not been built, and a user should not read its absence as a mathematical restriction.","title":"Point-value algebra"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Base-space-motion","page":"Points","text":"Displacement moves the points themselves, where every operation above maps sections over a fixed base and never moves a point.","title":"Base-space motion"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#HDF5-persistence","page":"Points","text":"","title":"HDF5 persistence"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Internal-API","page":"Points","text":"These symbols are not part of the public interface; they are documented for contributors — the point-value broadcast style, the compatibility checks the algebra routes through, the shape and unwrapping helpers, and the base-space motion internals — the coefficient conversion, the fused displacement expression, the per-axis terms and the single-coordinate wrap. (The section-interface accessors the FieldAlgebra builders reuse — field_data and the component accessors — are extended here but owned by Fields, so they are documented on that page.)","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Types","page":"Points","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Points/#Functions","page":"Points","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points","page":"Points","text":"Points\n\nThe unstructured base of the package's fiber-bundle model: off-grid sample positions and the field values sampled at them. A point set plays the role the grid plays in Fields, and point values are sections over it.\n\nThe parts are:\n\nPositions. Point is a single coordinate triple, and PointSet holds M positions in a structure-of-arrays layout. A point set carries no grid; a periodic domain wraps any coordinate, so the grid comes at use time, from the grid-to-point transfer (interpolation, and its adjoint anterpolation) in the Interpolation module.\nPoint-value sections. ScalarPointValues, VectorPointValues, and TensorPointValues, gathered under AbstractPointValues, mirror the field tensor orders. They have no spectral form, a sampled value being always a physical-space quantity.\nA full fiberwise algebra on the point values. Addition, real scaling, scalar multiplication and division, dot, cross, mul!, transpose, norm, the tr and det invariants, and the sym/skew/dev decompositions form the same physical surface the fields carry, together with map!/map, which apply any fiber function — each documented once, in FieldAlgebra. It reuses the FiberSections code that generates the field algebra, so no per-component formula is written twice.\nBase-space motion. displace! moves the points themselves along coefficient-weighted vector sections — in unwrapped coordinates or, given a Grid3D, back in the periodic box in the same pass — and wrap! reduces positions to their canonical in-box representatives. It is the one family coupling base and fiber, and positions deliberately have no + with point values.\nAllocation. similar_point_values returns uninitialized storage carrying a reference's tensor order and backend — the point-value twin of similar_field, and what the broadcast machinery and the allocating algebra wrappers build their results with.\nPersistence. write_points and read_points map a point set or a point-value section to and from one self-describing HDF5 group, positions and values kept separate (methods load with the HDF5 package, as a weak-dependency extension). They are the point-layer twins of write_field/read_field.\nMoving between storage families. adapt moves a PointSet or any point values between host and device arrays, exactly as it moves a field; the operation is documented once, with its field methods, in Fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.Point","page":"Points","text":"Point{T} = SVector{3, T}\n\nA single position in the domain, stored as a coordinate triple. Point is the base-space analog of FiberVector: it shares the SVector{3,T} representation but plays a different role, naming a location rather than a field value. It is a type alias rather than a wrapper, so arithmetic on positions stays StaticArrays-native and the position-versus-value distinction is one of role, not of dispatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.Point"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.PointSet","page":"Points","text":"PointSet(positions::AbstractArray{T, 2}) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nA collection of M off-grid positions, stored structure-of-arrays so that each coordinate axis is contiguous across points, mirroring the fiber-last field layout. Storage is backend-generic: the array may live on the CPU (Array) or a GPU (CuArray/ROCArray). A point set carries no grid, because a periodic domain wraps any coordinate; a transfer operator takes the grid from the field it acts on.\n\nFields\n\npositions — the coordinate triples, an AbstractArray of shape (M, 3) — point index first, coordinate axis last — whose second axis must have length 3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.PointSet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.AbstractPointValues","page":"Points","text":"AbstractPointValues{T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nAbstract supertype for field values sampled at the M positions of a PointSet. The parameter T is the real scalar type, and R is the tensor order, an integer value parameter. Point values are representation-free: a sampled value is always a physical-space quantity, so there is no physical/spectral distinction here, unlike the field types. The concrete subtypes are ScalarPointValues at tensor order 0, VectorPointValues at order 1, and TensorPointValues at order 2 and above.\n\nnote: A section owns its storage\nEvery constructor here — and PointSet's — rejects borrowed storage, a SubArray anywhere in the parent chain, so wrapping a view into a larger buffer will throw. Ownership is what makes an aliased in-place call safe (FieldAlgebra). Allocate with similar_point_values, or copy the slice. Any owning array is fine — Array, CuArray, ROCArray, or a reshape of one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.AbstractPointValues"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.ScalarPointValues","page":"Points","text":"ScalarPointValues(data::AbstractArray{T, 1}) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nOne scalar value per point, sampled at M points — the point-set counterpart of a ScalarFieldPhysical. Point-values carry no second representation, there being no periodic grid to transform over.\n\nFields\n\ndata — the values, with shape (M,).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.ScalarPointValues"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.VectorPointValues","page":"Points","text":"VectorPointValues(data::AbstractArray{T, 2}) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nOne vector value per point, sampled at M points — the point-set counterpart of a VectorFieldPhysical.\n\nFields\n\ndata — the components, structure-of-arrays with shape (M, 3); the second axis must have length 3, and the constructor checks it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.VectorPointValues"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.TensorPointValues","page":"Points","text":"TensorPointValues{R}(data::AbstractArray{T}) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\nTensorPointValues(data::AbstractArray{T}) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nOne tensor value of order R per point (R ge 2; use ScalarPointValues or VectorPointValues for orders 0 and 1, which the constructor enforces), sampled at M points — the point-set counterpart of a TensorFieldPhysical.\n\nThe constructor written without an explicit TensorPointValues{R} infers the order as ndims(data) - 1.\n\nFields\n\ndata — the components, structure-of-arrays with shape (M, fiber_shape(Val(R))...) — (M, 3, 3) at order 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.TensorPointValues"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.point_positions","page":"Points","text":"point_positions(point_set::PointSet)\n\nReturn the (M, 3) array of point positions held by point_set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.point_positions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.point_values","page":"Points","text":"point_values(sampled_values::AbstractPointValues)\n\nReturn the underlying array of sampled values held by sampled_values, a structure-of-arrays with shape (M, fiber_shape(Val(R))...) — (M,), (M, 3) and (M, 3, 3) at tensor orders 0, 1 and 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.point_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.point_value_tensor_order","page":"Points","text":"point_value_tensor_order(sampled_values::AbstractPointValues{T, R}) where\n    {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nReturn the tensor order R of sampled_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.point_value_tensor_order"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.npoints","page":"Points","text":"npoints(point_set::PointSet)\nnpoints(sampled_values::AbstractPointValues)\n\nReturn the number M of points, the leading-axis length of the coordinate array of a PointSet or of the value array of an AbstractPointValues.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.npoints"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.similar_point_values","page":"Points","text":"similar_point_values(reference::AbstractPointValues, data)\nsimilar_point_values(reference::AbstractPointValues, ::Val{R}) where {R}\n\nAllocate point values shaped like reference. The data form wraps the supplied array in a point-value type of the same tensor order as reference. The Val{R} form allocates a fresh (M, fiber_shape(Val(R))...) array of order R on reference's backend, through similar, and wraps it. The broadcast machinery and the allocating algebra wrappers use it to build their results.\n\nnote: Deliberately not an extension of `Base.similar`\nThe reasoning is similar_field's: Base.similar's second argument means an element type or dimensions rather than a tensor order, similar(q, Float32) would read as a precision change the package never makes implicitly, and the data form wraps supplied storage rather than allocating.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.similar_point_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.displace!","page":"Points","text":"displace!(destination::PointSet, source::PointSet, terms...)\ndisplace!(destination::PointSet, source::PointSet, grid::Grid3D, terms...)\ndisplace!(points::PointSet, [grid::Grid3D,] terms...)\n\nMove each point of a point set along the weighted sum of vector values over it, returning it: writing x_p for the position of point p and v_j(p) for the value the j-th section holds at that point,\n\nx_p leftarrow x_p + sum_j c_j v_j(p) \n\nEach element of terms is a tuple (coefficient, section): the j-th term supplies one host scalar c_j — a single number, applied to every point, not a per-point array — and one VectorPointValues section v_j, which does hold one vector per point. The subscript j counts the terms of the call, so a two-term call has c_1 c_2 and v_1 v_2. Up to eight terms apply in one fused traversal: the positions are read once, each section once, and the positions written once, on CPU and GPU alike. A longer call takes one traversal per group of eight, each continuing from the last in the same left-to-right order, so the result is bitwise the single traversal's; with a grid the wrap runs once, on the finished sum. This is the one operation that crosses the position-versus-value role split, and it is a named operation rather than a + method because positions are points of the periodic domain, not vectors: what is being applied is a displacement, exact because the domain is flat and the fiber carries one global frame.\n\nWithout a grid the sum is taken literally, in the universal cover — the unwrapped picture of the torus, in which a coordinate keeps growing instead of folding back: coordinates may leave the box, and sampling such positions remains correct because interpolation wraps the grid nodes it samples periodically. With a grid the displaced coordinates are additionally reduced, componentwise and in the same fused pass, to the canonical representative in the fundamental domain — the same reduction wrap! applies on its own, whose docstring states the endpoint and non-finite contract. Displacement on the periodic domain proper is the grid form; the grid-free form is its lift to the cover, which preserves winding information (a mean-square-displacement diagnostic needs the unwrapped trajectory).\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the PointSet receiving the displaced positions. It may be the source itself (the two-argument convenience forms are exactly that call): the pass reads each element's inputs before writing its outputs, so the aliased call is computed directly.\nsource — the PointSet holding the starting positions. When omitted, points is both source and destination.\ngrid — optional; a Grid3D supplying the periodic domain. Only its domain_size and origin enter: the wrap needs the box, not the mesh.\nterms... — zero or more (coefficient, section) tuples, each pairing one real number with one section. The coefficients are part of what the operation is — the displacement sum_j c_j v_j — so they are named coefficients rather than the α/β accumulation scalars the in-place operations take, and there is no trailing α/β pair. With no terms the call copies (and, with a grid, wraps) the positions.\n\nThere is deliberately no allocating sibling: displacement is motion of an existing base, and a freshly allocated destination would have no user. Sections and point sets must agree in point count, scalar type and storage family; mixed pairs are refused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.displace!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.wrap!","page":"Points","text":"wrap!(points::PointSet, grid::Grid3D)\n\nReduce every position to its canonical representative in the periodic domain: componentwise,\n\nx_pa leftarrow operatornamemod(x_pa - o_a L_a) + o_a \n\nwhich lands each coordinate in the half-open fundamental domain o_a o_a + L_a), where L_a is the domain extent along axis a and o_a the domain origin — both read from grid, whose mesh plays no role. Wrapping is never required for sampling — interpolation wraps the grid nodes it samples, so it is invariant under shifting any coordinate by whole periods — but it is the operation that keeps coordinates bounded over a long run (absolute floating-point resolution degrades as a coordinate grows, single precision especially) and that presents in-box positions to a caller bucketing by cell index. It discards winding: a mean-square-displacement diagnostic must run on unwrapped positions, which is why displacement and wrapping are separate operations rather than one.\n\nThe reduction runs in place and returns points. The domain's element type must match the point set's; a mixed pair is refused, never converted. To wrap in the same pass as a displacement, use the grid form of displace!.\n\nwarning: The endpoint is folded, and non-finite coordinates pass through non-finite\nFloating-point mod can return exactly L_a for a coordinate a hair below the origin (measured: mod(-1e-20, 2π) === 2π), so the excluded endpoint is folded back to o_a explicitly. The fold tests equality rather than order, because an order test is false for NaN and Inf and would silently rewrite a diverged trajectory into a tidy in-box position — a non-finite coordinate comes out non-finite (an infinity as NaN, mod's own image of it), never boxed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.wrap!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.write_points","page":"Points","text":"write_points(parent::HDF5.H5DataStore, name::AbstractString, points::PointSet)\nwrite_points(parent::HDF5.H5DataStore, name::AbstractString,\n             values::AbstractPointValues)\n\nPersist a point object — a PointSet of positions, or any of the three point-value types — as a new HDF5 group name under parent, returning the group it created. Use it to checkpoint the unstructured base and its samples for restart, or to hand them to another tool; the reload with read_points is exact. It is the point-layer twin of write_field, which persists a field.\n\nThe group is self-describing: the numeric array becomes the dataset data, and the metadata become attributes — a type tag, a schema version, the element type, the point count, and, for point values, the tensor order — so any HDF5 tool can read it. A device (GPU) array is copied to the host on write. The sugar parent[name] = points is equivalent.\n\nPositions and values are written separately, one group each, because they are separate objects: a PointSet is the base, and point values are sections over it. A caller that wants them together writes both under one parent group, which also lets several value sets share one stored set of positions.\n\nBecause parent is a handle you opened and own, this never opens or closes a file, and the package attaches no notion of time — the caller bundles a snapshot and owns any time / iteration attributes.\n\nArguments\n\nparent — an open HDF5 File or Group to create the new subgroup under.\nname — the name of the group to create.\npoints, values — the PointSet or point-value section to write.\n\nProvided by a package extension, so it is available only when HDF5.jl is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.write_points"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.read_points","page":"Points","text":"read_points(parent::HDF5.H5DataStore, name::AbstractString)\n\nReconstruct the point object written by write_points from the HDF5 group name under parent, dispatching on the stored type attribute to return it in the matching type (PointSet, ScalarPointValues, VectorPointValues, TensorPointValues). The reload is exact — the array data, the element type, the point count, and the tensor order are recovered bit-for-bit. It is the point-layer twin of read_field.\n\nThe reconstructed object is a host (CPU) one regardless of where the written one lived; move it to a device with adapt or adapt! afterward if needed.\n\nHere parent is an open HDF5 File or Group holding the group, and name is the name (or path) of that group under parent.\n\nProvided by a package extension, so it is available only when HDF5.jl is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.read_points"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points.PointValuesBroadcastStyle","page":"Points","text":"PointValuesBroadcastStyle{N}\n\nBroadcast style for point values, where N is one plus the tensor order — 1, 2, or 3 for the (M,), (M, 3) and (M, 3, 3) storage layouts at tensor orders 0, 1 and 2, and one more dimension for each order above that. Inside a broadcast a point-value object stands for its storage array, and this style materializes the result back into a point-value object of the same order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points.PointValuesBroadcastStyle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._axis_terms-Tuple{Int64}","page":"Points","text":"_axis_terms(axis, terms...)\n\nTake the axis column view of each converted term's data, keeping the coefficient — the per-axis operands of the fused displace-and-wrap pass. Recursion for the same reason as _convert_terms: a specialized tuple in, a specialized tuple out, nothing boxed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._axis_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_broadcast_point_values_compatibility-Tuple{AbstractPointValues, AbstractPointValues}","page":"Points","text":"_check_broadcast_point_values_compatibility(destination, argument)\n\nCheck, during broadcasting, that argument covers the same number of points as destination, recursing into a Broadcasted argument. Arguments that are not point values — real numbers, fibers, or plain arrays — are accepted without a check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_broadcast_point_values_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_combinable-Tuple{Vararg{AbstractPointValues}}","page":"Points","text":"_check_combinable(values...)\n\nCheck that point-value operands may be added or subtracted: equal point count, tensor order and scalar type, returning nothing or throwing.\n\nA vector-space combination is only defined between sections of the same bundle, and the broadcast that implements it is not fussy enough to say so. Two failures followed, both order-dependent, the tell that no operation was being expressed. Mixed precision NARROWED toward the first operand, so a32 + a64 silently truncated a Float64 operand while a64 + a32 did not. Mixed tensor order was accepted in one order — a scalar broadcast across a vector's fiber — and rejected in the other, so + was not commutative; and adding a scalar section to a vector one is not in the method set for fields either.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_combinable"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_displacement_compatibility-Tuple{PointSet, PointSet, Any}","page":"Points","text":"_check_displacement_compatibility(destination, source, sections)\n\nRequire the point count, scalar type and storage family of source and of every displacement section to match destination's, returning nothing or throwing an ArgumentError rather than promoting or transferring. The refusal is the package's decision, not the launch layer's: the torus path assembles per-axis views and so bypasses the broadcast machinery's own enforcement, and a mixed host/device pair must fail alike on every path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_displacement_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_displacement_domain-Tuple{PointSet, Grid3D}","page":"Points","text":"_check_displacement_domain(points, grid)\n\nRequire the periodic domain's element type to match the point set's, refusing a mixed-precision pair rather than promoting (the rule binds non-section partners too — a Grid3D here plays the role FieldSpectralMultipliers plays for a spectral field).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_displacement_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_npoints_match-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Points","text":"_check_npoints_match(a, b)\n\nThrow a DimensionMismatch unless a and b cover the same number of points M, and otherwise return nothing. Each of a and b is a PointSet or an AbstractPointValues. This is the internal point-count check that the point-value algebra and the transfer operators run before combining two operands.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._check_npoints_match"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._convert_terms-Union{Tuple{Type{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Points","text":"_convert_terms(::Type{T}, terms...)\n\nConvert each (coefficient, section) displacement term to (T(coefficient), data) by recursion over the tuple rather than by a map closure: a closure would capture the type T as a DataType field and heap-allocate itself per call, which is the 32-byte residue the allocation audit would flag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._convert_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._displacement_rhs-Tuple{Any}","page":"Points","text":"_displacement_rhs(base, terms...)\n\nFold the displacement terms into one lazy broadcast expression textttbase + sum_j c_j v_j — the argument base is a coordinate array (or a lazy expression over one) and each element of terms is a (coefficient, data) tuple whose parts are already converted and unwrapped. The recursion keeps the term count in the type domain, so the fused pass specializes per arity and allocates nothing. The public methods name the vararg count N in their signatures because Julia does not specialize an unbounded Vararg that is only forwarded — the same trap as the forwarded-Function spelling pinned in test/allocations.jl — and without it a three-term call boxed per element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._displacement_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._first_point_values-Tuple{AbstractPointValues}","page":"Points","text":"_first_point_values(x)\n\nReturn the first AbstractPointValues found in x — a value, a Broadcasted tree, or an argument tuple — or nothing when there is none. Out-of-place broadcasting uses it to seed the allocated result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._first_point_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._point_value_shape-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{Integer, Val{R}}} where R","page":"Points","text":"_point_value_shape(number_of_points, ::Val{R}) where {R}\n\nReturn the storage shape (M, fiber_shape(Val(R))...) of point values of tensor order R at M points, where M is number_of_points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._point_value_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._show_count-Tuple{IO, Any}","page":"Points","text":"_show_count(io, n)\n\nPrint a point count with its noun, singular at one — \"1 point\", \"500 points\".\n\nBoth point types carry a count and both display it, so the agreement is written once here rather than twice at the call sites.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._show_count"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._unwrap_point_values_argument-Tuple{AbstractPointValues}","page":"Points","text":"_unwrap_point_values_argument(x)\n\nReplace each point-value object in a broadcast expression with its storage array, recursing through a Broadcasted tree, so that a point-value broadcast lowers to a plain array broadcast on the backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._unwrap_point_values_argument"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Points/#GBGPeriodicFields.Points._wrap_coordinate-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, T, T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Points","text":"_wrap_coordinate(coordinate, extent, origin)\n\nReduce one coordinate to its canonical representative in the fundamental domain textttorigin textttorigin + textttextent). Floating-point mod can return exactly extent for a coordinate a hair below origin (measured: mod(-1e-20, 2π) === 2π), so the excluded endpoint is folded back explicitly; the fold tests equality with extent rather than an order comparison, because an order test is false for a non-finite coordinate and would silently rewrite a diverged trajectory into a tidy in-box position. A non-finite coordinate therefore passes through non-finite (mod sends an infinity to NaN).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Points._wrap_coordinate"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/","page":"Architecture","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Architecture-—-GBGPeriodicFields","page":"Architecture","text":"This document records the architecture of the package and, more importantly, why it is the way it is. It exists so that future work (human or AI) extends the design instead of accidentally reverting it. CLAUDE.md carries the operational summary; this file carries the reasoning, the conceptual model (§2) and the writing and docstring conventions (the §2 documentation policy).\n\nThis file is self-contained: every piece of notation — a mathematical symbol (in LaTeX) or a code identifier — is introduced in plain language at its first use, the meaning stated before or in the same sentence as the symbol. So it reads top-to-bottom without opening the source or another document, as each entry of the separate decision log does. (Universally standard math — nabla, Delta, int, mathbb R — may stand unglossed.) Keep it that way when editing.","title":"Architecture — GBGPeriodicFields"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#1.-Purpose-and-scope","page":"Architecture","text":"The package is the field layer for pseudospectral simulation of incompressible fluid dynamics and turbulence on three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains. Its primary consumer is GBGHomogeneousTurbulence.jl, a direct numerical simulation of homogeneous isotropic turbulence built on it; the operation families below were shaped by what that solver needs, which is why a proposed addition without a consumer is deferred rather than built. It provides scalar, vector, and second-order tensor fields with two storage representations — physical and spectral (the spectral one stored non-redundantly as the real-to-complex / rfft half-spectrum) — plus FFT transform plans, spectral multipliers, storage-wise broadcasting, fiberwise algebra (FieldAlgebra), differential operators applied as spectral multipliers (DifferentialOperators), periodic convolution (Convolution), and the L²/measure reductions (Reductions). Interpolation and its adjoint anterpolation — the grid↔point transfer layer that serves consumer-side Lagrangian particle tracking — are in place (§10): the point-value sections, their fiberwise algebra, and the transfer schemes — local splines (Lalescu, Teaca & Carati 2010: the smooth Hermite and C^0 Lagrange hierarchies) and exact spectral-domain transfers (DirectDFT, NUFFT), unified under one AbstractTransferScheme. The same code must run on CPU Arrays and on GPU arrays (CUDA CuArray, AMDGPU ROCArray); development prioritizes GPU correctness and performance first. The target is a single node with shared-memory parallelism only — CPU multithreading (§9) and a single GPU device. Distributed-memory / multi-node execution (a cluster, MPI) and multi-GPU sharding are deliberately out of scope — a consumer's concern if ever needed, not this field layer's (every field is one host or device array, allocated via similar).\n\nThe package is a math backend — a library of field operations that turbulence and cloud solvers consume, not a solver itself. Its vocabulary is therefore domain-neutral (base/fiber, point/value), never particle or tracer (a consumer's interpretation), and it carries no concept of time: no advection, time integration, or temporal state. Trajectory dynamics belong to the consumer (§10).","title":"1. Purpose and scope"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#2.-The-central-principle:-the-architecture-follows-established-mathematics","page":"Architecture","text":"Every abstraction in this package is the code image of a standard mathematical object, and design decisions are settled by that mathematics rather than by implementation convenience. The primary source is differential geometry — the language of fiber bundles and their sections; the working rule is that a proposed abstraction must first be identifiable as a known mathematical object (or a faithful discretization of one) before it earns a place in the design. \"For completeness\" or \"it would be convenient\" is never a justification — indeed the recurring lesson below is that the absence of an operation is itself a precise mathematical statement.","title":"2. The central principle: the architecture follows established mathematics"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#The-fiber-bundle-model","page":"Architecture","text":"A field, and a collection of values sampled at scattered locations, are both sections of a tensor bundle over a base. The domain is a flat periodic box (a 3-torus) with a single global frame, so the bundle is trivial — the product E = B times V of a base B and a fiber V. The word \"base\" carries two related meanings. The continuum base is the torus Omega itself. The bundle is drawn over both: the trivial product exists over Omega and over each discrete base alike, and §10's pullback iota is what relates them. The discrete base is the finite set a section is actually stored on — a Grid3D of nodes or a PointSet of scattered locations, each a finite subset of Omega — and it is the one meant wherever the docs say a section is \"over a base\" or a reduction \"integrates the base away\". Neither reading is dispensable: the continuum carries the derivative and measure, the discrete set carries the data. The fiber is a tensor power of mathbb R^3, fixed by the tensor order R: V = mathbb R (R = 0, scalar), mathbb R^3 (R = 1, vector, FiberVector), mathbb R^3 otimes mathbb R^3 = mathbb R^3times 3 (R = 2, second-order tensor, FiberTensor2), and higher (FiberTensor3/4).\n\nThat single global frame is what lets a derivative index be stored as a fiber index: nabla maps a section of E to a section of E otimes T^*Omega, and the frame identifies TOmega — and, being orthonormal, T^*Omega with it — with the GENERATING space mathbb R^3, the R = 1 fiber. It is not identified with V, which has rank 3^R and matches TOmega only at R = 1. So E otimes T^*Omega has fiber V otimes mathbb R^3 = (mathbb R^3)^otimes (R+1), derivative index LAST: (nabla u)_ij = partial_x_j u_i, the slot the divergence contracts, leaving nabla u one order higher. That single global frame is orthonormal and right-handed, so the fiber carries the Cartesian Euclidean structure: components refer to one fixed rectangular frame, indices are neither raised nor lowered, and there is no metric to carry because it is the identity. This is what makes the bundle trivial and flat rather than merely modelled as such, and it is why the bundle vocabulary here organizes the code without promising machinery on manifolds — the tensors are Cartesian tensors in the classical continuum-mechanics sense, save that cross products and curls flip sign under reflection. Do not introduce curvilinear-coordinate machinery, explicit metric tensors, or a vector/covector distinction unless the package design changes to need them: each would make the frame a variable the whole operation set has to carry, and none has a consumer. A section assigns a fiber value to each base point; the fiber-last data layout (§3) is exactly that section, stored base-indexed.\n\nThe package's structures then organize around that one object, rather than standing as separate worlds. Two of them are parameters of a section — what a section is built from — and three are operation families, maps acting on sections, each reaching only as far as the structure it needs:\n\nThe base is either the structured grid Grid3D — a field is a section sampled on the grid — or the unstructured point set PointSet — point-values are a section sampled at scattered points (§10). Same fibers, different base.\nFiberwise algebra is the vertical (within-fiber) structure: addition, real scaling, contraction with a constant fiber, dot/cross/tensor products, transpose, sym/skew/dev, norms, and an arbitrary fiber map (map!, the escape hatch) all act inside fibers, pointwise in the base, and are therefore base-independent — the very same operation on the very same fiber type, whether the base is a grid or a point set. This is the structural reason one set of generated kernels (§6) serves fields and point-values verbatim, and the mathematical motivation for treating the section as the primary abstraction: a shared AbstractFiberSection{T, R} supertype spanning both, in the FiberSections module (§10).\nDifferential operators are the complementary horizontal structure — gradient, divergence, curl, Laplacian are derivatives along the base, using the periodic box's flat connection — its one fixed frame, which lets vectors over different base points be compared directly (§4). Read that precisely, since a Grid3D is a finite set and has no tangent bundle of its own: the derivative lives on the continuum torus, and a grid section reaches it by standing for the trigonometric polynomial its coefficients define, of which each operator is the exact term-by-term derivative on the resolved band, the modes short of each even axis's Nyquist.\n\nWhat distinguishes the two bases is therefore whether   such an identification exists — a uniform periodic grid determines that polynomial, so   it inherits the derivative; a PointSet of scattered points determines no such   interpolant, so point-value sections carry the vertical algebra and no derivatives.\n\nBase reductions are the third operation family — integrating the base away against a measure the base carries (Grid3D cell volume; PointSet counting): integral (extensive), the normalized inner/l2norm, and the mean/std/var moments. They map a section to a scalar or a fiber — with one sub-family deliberately excepted, since naming it here saves a reader the contradiction: the radial reductions radial_spectrum and radial_correlation return a profile, a pair of vectors over shells, not a single value. They are base-dependent (the measure is the base's), yet — like the vertical algebra — are shared by fields and point-values through AbstractFiberSection (§12, decision 18). The same measure concept recurs as the interpolation kernel (below) and a convolution multiplier (the Convolution module, decision 20): one L²/integration structure in three roles (the measure taxonomy, decision 19).\nRepresentation is a choice of basis for a grid section's data (distinct from the fiber frame above): physical storage uses the nodal basis, spectral storage the Fourier basis, and the rfft is the linear isomorphism between them. \"Basis\" is the right intuition and slightly flatters the codomain: the transform is not an automorphism of one space, because the stored complex half-spectrum has strictly more real dimensions than the section it came from. It is an isomorphism onto the Hermitian-constrained subspace of that array — the constraint being what makes the map a bijection and the round trip exact (FORMALIZATION.md states it typed). A point-value section has no second representation — there is no periodic grid to transform over — so the point types are representation-free (§10).\n\n(The vertical/horizontal language is the bundle-theoretic one — fiber directions versus the connection's base directions — applied here by analogy to operations: the fiberwise algebra is order-zero and base-preserving — it covers the identity on the base, acting inside the fiber over each point and touching no neighbour. It is deliberately not called fiber-preserving, which this document reserves — in a narrower sense than the standard bundle-theoretic one — for a map that leaves the fiber by the identity (the transfer maps, the fiber-preserving reductions); dot, norm, det and map! all change the fiber. The horizontal family, by contrast, is built from the connection's covariant derivative, order one. Built from rather than equal to: gradient, divergence, curl and symmetric_gradient are that derivative, but inverse_laplacian and helmholtz_projection invert or project with it and are nonlocal — no local partial-derivative formula, the value at a point depending on the whole field. Strictly, vertical (ker mathrmdpi, the kernel of the bundle projection pi's differential — canonical) and horizontal (a connection-chosen complement) are subbundles of the total space's tangent bundle.)\n\nTransfer between sections over different bases is the interpolation/anterpolation pair: pulling a grid section back to points (evaluation — pairing with a Dirac mass delta_x — f(x) = delta_x(f)) and the adjoint pushforward of point masses onto the grid. The full function–measure duality is given in §10; the Interpolation module docstring carries its concrete face (the adjoint identity with the point and grid counts M/N); the interpolation weights are themselves a unit-mass kernel measure (signed for higher-order schemes), the base-changing sibling of the convolution multiplier — see the measure taxonomy (decision 19).","title":"The fiber-bundle model"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#The-architecture-map","page":"Architecture","text":"A field — or values sampled at scattered points — is a section f, a right inverse of the bundle projection pi:\n\nB xrightarrow f  E xrightarrow pi  B qquad pi circ f = mathrmid_B\n\nwith base B (the structured Grid3D or the unstructured PointSet), E = B times V the trivial tensor bundle, and f the field (AbstractFiberSection): one fiber value per base point. Everything below operates on sections, plus the support mechanics — each explained in its own section, referenced here rather than repeated:\n\nRepresentations — physical (nodal) and spectral (rfft, written hat f) views of one section, exchanged by the isomorphism mathcal F (to_spectral/to_physical); the spectral machinery and the operator-as-multiplier square are §4.\nVertical — fiberwise algebra (FieldAlgebra on fields, Points on point-values): add/scale, the weighted combination combine!, contract (dot/cross), transpose, sym/skew/dev, the tr/det invariants, pointwise norms — generated once (§6) — plus map!, the arbitrary-φ escape hatch (any unnamed pointwise fiber map; decision 48).\nHorizontal — differential operators (DifferentialOperators): gradient, divergence, curl, symmetric_gradient, laplacian, …, applied as spectral multipliers (§4).\nConvolution (Convolution) applies the kernel multiply hat f cdot hat g in spectral representation (§11).\nMeasure / L² (Reductions) provides integral, inner, weighted_inner, l2norm, mean, the radial spectra (§12).\nThe grid↔point transfer (Interpolation): interpolation W and its adjoint anterpolation W^top (§10).\nMoving between storage families (adapt, decision 63): host and device are two families of the same object, and crossing between them is an explicit operation rather than something arithmetic does implicitly — invariant 14 refuses a mixed pair, and this is its named complement.\nSupport mechanics — types & memory layout (§3), validation design (§5), code generation (§6), aliasing (§7), broadcasting (§8), performance & threading (§9).\n\nReductions, convolution, and the grid↔point transfer are the same L²/integration structure in three roles — the measure taxonomy (decision 19). Which operation is valid in which representation, the naming rules, and the documentation policy follow.","title":"The architecture map"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#The-invariants","page":"Architecture","text":"Fourteen rules constrain every change, and the rest of this document refers to them by number. They are collected here so a reader meeting \"invariant 6\" has somewhere to look; each is stated in one line, with a pointer to the section that develops it.\n\nThe public method set is the spec. Deliberate absences encode representation validity — there is no field-times-field multiplication in spectral representation (the coefficientwise product is a convolution, offered under its true name in §11), no spectral division by a scalar field, no spectral pointwise norms. Operations linear in the field exist in both representations; nonlinear ones are physical-space only. A missing combination is never added for completeness (see the next subsection).\nA field is a real physical object, and physical and spectral are two representations of it. Complex arrays are storage detail, never user-facing — hence \"a field in spectral representation\", not \"a spectral field\".\nThe data layout is fiber-last, (N_x N_y N_z 3 3), so component slices are contiguous; FFT batching and CPU threading both rely on it (§3).\nThe transform normalization is AbstractFFTs': forward unnormalized, inverse normalized. Adding a physical constant in spectral representation therefore updates only the zero Fourier mode, scaled by the grid-point count, rather than every coefficient (§4).\nThe Nyquist split is deliberate. On an even-length axis the derivative wavenumber is zeroed at the Nyquist frequency, so derivatives of real fields stay real, while the squared wavenumber keeps the true k^2, so the Laplacian and Poisson-type solves stay correct. The two families are not unified (§4).\nThe package is GPU-portable by construction: storage is a generic AbstractArray, no package method scalar-indexes a device array, allocation goes through similar/similar_field, and fused broadcast assignments are preferred (§9).\nNo type piracy: a Base or LinearAlgebra function is imported before it is extended.\nNo fiber-fiber methods. The fiber types are StaticArrays aliases and already carry their own algebra, so the package does not redefine it.\nAliasing is safe by construction, and a section owns its storage so that it is. Every fused pass reads an element's sources into registers before writing any of its outputs, so an in-place aliased call is computed directly and is bitwise-identical to the non-aliased one; the former guard family is retired. That argument holds only while one section's element n is the other's element n, which is the ownership half of the invariant and not a detail of it: the constructors reject borrowed storage — a SubArray anywhere in the parent chain — leaving same-array as the only way two sections can share memory (decision 59). Ownership, not layout: an Array, a CuArray, a ROCArray and a reshape of any of them all pass. The mul! contract keeps LinearAlgebra's documented contract that the destination must not alias (§7).\nThe alpha/beta convention. A five-argument form converts both weights to the field's  scalar type and branches on beta = 0 (overwrite) versus beta ne 0 (accumulate); a  three-argument form delegates to it with alpha = 1, beta = 0. Throughout, alpha scales  the computed result and beta the destination accumulated onto. Two deliberate exceptions, and  only these two: combine! is the weighted combination alpha a + beta b, so its two weights  scale its two operands instead — which is what the operation is, and what its name announces —  and exponential_convolve!'s two-operand forms carry the same combination, and so the same reading  of the glyphs, into a fused convolution. Both are operations whose definition is a weighted  combination; no operation may reuse the glyphs for a role other than that.\nThe transform input contract. The allocating transforms preserve their input; the in-place ones  may overwrite the source, and the inverse real transform uses its spectral input as scratch on some  backends but not others. Never rely on a !-transform's source surviving — with one deliberate  exception that guarantees it: the four-argument scratch arity of to_physical!, which consumes a  caller-supplied scratch in the source's place (decision 64; §4).\nThis is a math backend — no time, and no particles. The terminology stays domain-neutral  (base and fiber, point and value), and the package introduces no advection, time integration, or  per-step state; trajectory dynamics belong to the consumer (§10).\nAllocation-free is an enforced contract. Every in-place ! operator allocates no field-sized  scratch when called on preallocated buffers with a destination distinct from its sources. This is  load-bearing on a GPU: a per-call device temporary escapes Julia's garbage collector, which  triggers on host pressure and cannot see the device buffer a dead array owns, so the pool grows  every step until the run wedges (§9).\nSections combine only within one precision and one storage family — never by conversion. An  operation over two or more sections requires them to agree in element type and in storage family  (Array with Array, CuArray with CuArray), and refuses anything else with a clear error  rather than promoting, copying, or transferring. The refusal is a decision of the package, not an  accident of a vendor library declining a mixed-pointer call or a kernel launcher declining mixed  arrays. It binds every argument an operation takes, the destination and the non-section partners  included — a spectral field and its multipliers must match too. Conversion is an explicitly-named  operation, never something an arithmetic operation does implicitly: crossing storage families  is adapt (decision 63), or adapt! into a destination that already exists  (sections only; decision 69), and crossing precisions remains future work.","title":"The invariants"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Representation-validity-lives-in-the-method-set","page":"Architecture","text":"Seeing each representation as a basis for a grid section's data — the nodal basis (physical) and the Fourier basis (spectral), with the rfft the linear isomorphism between them — fixes exactly which operations are valid where: an operation exists in a representation only when it is the same operation on the underlying section there — equivalently, only when it commutes with the rfft isomorphism.\n\nLinear in the field — addition/subtraction of fields, scaling by real numbers, contractions with constant fibers, transpose, sym/skew/dev are valid in both representations, the Fourier transform commuting with them.\nAdding constants — in physical space, every grid value shifts; in spectral space, mathcal F(f + c) = mathcal F(f) + c N delta_k0 under the unnormalized-forward convention, with N the total grid-point count (prod(grid_size)) and k the wavenumber — so only the zero mode (k = 0) changes, scaled by N. This is why _spectral_constant_scale exists and why constant kernels in spectral representation touch a single coefficient (per component).\nNonlinear in the field — products of two fields, division by a scalar field, pointwise norms, contractions of two fields (dot, cross, tensor mul!, and the fiberwise tensor inner product dot! — not the inner reduction, which is Parseval-valid in spectral representation): physical-space only. The coefficientwise spectral product is not the Fourier representation of the pointwise product (that would be a convolution), so offering it as a product would silently compute the wrong physical operation — that coefficientwise product is convolution, exposed under its true name as convolve (the Convolution module, decision 20), never as *.\n\nThe pointwise product is not preserved by mathcal F — the convolution-theorem square (star denoting periodic convolution, the package's convolve):\n\nbeginCD\n(fg)            star   f star g \nVmathcal FVV               VVmathcal FV \n(hat fhat g)  cdot   hat f cdot hat g\nendCD\nqquadqquad mathcal F(f star g) = hat f cdot hat g \n\nThe coefficientwise product downstairs is convolution upstairs (offered as convolve (star), §11); the pointwise product f cdot g transforms to a convolution with no coefficientwise spectral form — exactly why those nonlinear operations are physical-space only.\n\nThe same finiteness makes a nonlinear result aliased. A product doubles the spectral band — the determinant (a triple product) triples it, a non-polynomial map such as the norm is unbounded — so content beyond the grid's Nyquist (highest stored) wavenumber folds back onto the resolved modes when the physical result is transformed to spectral representation. The grid point values themselves stay exact; the aliasing is a spectral-representation artifact, and de-aliasing — the sharp low-pass spectral_cutoff, or phase-shift via translation_kernel (§11) — is the consumer's choice, the package supplying the tools rather than the strategy.\n\nThe consequence is a hard project invariant: a missing method is a statement, not an omission. The 206-method public surface of FieldAlgebra is the machine-checkable encoding of the validity table above. Any refactor must reproduce the method set exactly; it was verified signature-for-signature against the original — M2 (98 methods: add!/subtract!/multiply!/divide!/+/-/*//) and then M3 (the dot/cross/mul!/transpose/norm/sym/skew/dev/trace contraction and decomposition families, plus their * operators), bringing the total to 198; the later trace tr/tr! addition brings it to 202, and the weighted combination combine! (2) with the determinant det/det! (2; decision 27) complete the 206-method surface, all parity-checked at runtime in test/method_parity.jl. The point-value algebra realizes the vertical operations on the unstructured base — it mirrors the physical field surface and is locked by its own separate baseline (§10).","title":"Representation validity lives in the method set"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Naming-conventions","page":"Architecture","text":"Operation names follow what the operation is — the same mathematics-first principle:\n\nReductions (section → scalar/fiber) take the quantity's noun: integral, inner, l2norm, lpnorm, radial_spectrum (the radial profile is still the quantity's noun).\nCanonically named operators (section → section with a standard, often eponymous operator name and no natural verb) take that noun: gradient, divergence, curl, inverse_curl, laplacian, inverse_laplacian, helmholtz_projection.\nGeneric transformations (section → section, named by the action) take the verb: the elementwise algebra (add, subtract, multiply, divide), the representation changes (to_physical, to_spectral), and the kernel-measure maps interpolate / anterpolate and convolve.\nIn-place forms append ! uniformly (gradient!, interpolate!, convolve!).\nExtensions of Base / LinearAlgebra / Statistics keep the source name, overriding the above (dot, cross, mul!, transpose, norm, map, map!, mean, std, var, cov, maximum, minimum, extrema); the continuum-mechanics decomposition abbreviations sym / skew / dev are likewise conventional. An extended generic is re-exported when its source is not already in scope, so that a caller need not know which standard library a verb lives in: the LinearAlgebra and Statistics names are re-exported by the top-level GBGPeriodicFields module, while the Base ones need no such treatment. Re-exporting a binding the package only extends is safe — two packages re-exporting the same binding do not collide, only two exporting different bindings of one name do.\nModules carry the family noun (Fields, DifferentialOperators, Reductions, Interpolation, Convolution); the operations inside follow the rules above — so Convolution / convolve mirrors Interpolation / interpolate.\n\nThe discriminator that places a new operation: a generic transformation with a natural verb is a verb (convolution → convolve); a canonically named operator is its noun (the Laplacian → laplacian). The operators gradient/laplacian/helmholtz_projection are nouns because they are the names of those operators, not by a noun preference.","title":"Naming conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Documentation-policy","page":"Architecture","text":"The @eval code generators (§6) emit methods only, never docstrings. Each operation has one docstring attached to the bare binding (function f end for package-owned generics; the bare imported name for Base/LinearAlgebra functions — never function f end there, since a missing import would silently create a new generic). Docstrings show a few canonical signatures plus prose about representation validity, including which combinations are intentionally absent. Exhaustive signature lists were dropped: they duplicated the tables and went stale instantly.\n\nMathematics is rendered LaTeX, with the symbol bound to the code. All math in docstrings and in these doc pages is written as Documenter LaTeX (KaTeX) in standard notation — inline langle f grangle, display math blocks — never Unicode-glyph approximations (angle brackets, a summation sign or an integral sign typed as characters). Because the package is grounded in mathematics (the principle of this section), each code object is explicitly identified with its mathematical symbol on first mention so the correspondence is never left implicit: gradient (nabla), divergence (nablacdot), curl (nablatimes), laplacian (Delta), inverse_laplacian (Delta^-1), helmholtz_projection (mathbbP), inner (langlecdotcdotrangle), l2norm (lVertcdotrVert_2), integral (intmathrmdmu), convolve (star), interpolate/anterpolate (W/W^top), to_spectral/to_physical (mathcal F/mathcal F^-1), a field in spectral representation written hat f. (In a \"\"\" docstring the LaTeX backslashes are escaped \\\\; in a .md page they are raw. Plain # comments stay Unicode — they are not rendered.) Reductions is the reference implementation.","title":"Documentation policy"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#3.-Types-and-memory-layout","page":"Architecture","text":"Grid3D{T} holds domain geometry; RealFourierLayout records the rfft layout. Field types are parameterized by scalar type T<:AbstractFloat: ScalarFieldPhysical/ScalarFieldSpectral, VectorFieldPhysical/VectorFieldSpectral, TensorFieldPhysical{2}/TensorFieldSpectral{2}. Constant fiber values are StaticArrays aliases — FiberVector{T} = SVector{3,T}, FiberTensor2{T} = SMatrix{3,3,T,9} (plus FiberTensor3/4) — so fiber-fiber algebra comes from StaticArrays and LinearAlgebra. StaticArrays is a direct dependency, not just the type-only StaticArraysCore: the package indexes fibers (a[i], A[i, j]) and uses their algebra inside the generated kernels, so the core-types package alone leaves those operations undefined.\n\nData layout is fiber-last: (Nx, Ny, Nz) for scalars, (Nx, Ny, Nz, 3) for vectors, (Nx, Ny, Nz, 3, 3) for tensors; spectral arrays are Complex{T} with the axis the real transform halves being the first under the default RealFourierLayout, which may assign any of the three. With column-major storage, each component slice data[:, :, :, i] (or data[:, :, :, i, j]) is one contiguous block. That gives batched per-component FFTs, coalesced GPU access, pure streaming writes for elementwise kernels, and trivially chunkable index ranges for the CPU threading (§9). Component access goes through views (_vector_component_data, _tensor2_component_data); views are lazy, so splicing them into generated formulas costs nothing.\n\nStorage is any AbstractArray, and the package names no vendor type and no memory kind. A field's storage family is the kind of array holding its values — Array, CuArray, ROCArray — and on CUDA the same CuArray may sit in device memory or in unified memory paged between the GPU and host RAM. Which of the two is CUDA.jl's configuration rather than the package's, and every allocating route follows it, so a CUDA run can hold fields larger than the card with no change to the code (decision 84, Running on a GPU).","title":"3. Types and memory layout"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.-Spectral-machinery","page":"Architecture","text":"FieldTransformPlans owns the FFT plans (per backend). Forward transforms are unnormalized; inverse transforms carry the normalization (AbstractFFTs convention) — this single convention choice propagates into the zero-mode constant updates described above.\n\nTransform input-preservation contract. The in-place to_physical!/to_spectral! apply their plans through mul!, the no-copy path, so the source field may be overwritten as scratch. This is intrinsic to real↔complex FFTs: FFTW's complex-to-real (irfft) transform destroys its input by default, and the multidimensional case has no input-preserving algorithm at all. It is also backend-divergent, and which backend overwrites which direction is not uniform: FFTW preserves the forward to_spectral! but destroys the inverse to_physical!, while a GPU backend (rocFFT) can differ on either. The package therefore guarantees nothing about a plain !-transform's source on any backend, matching the to_spectral!/to_physical! docstrings — the one guaranteed-preserving ! call is the four-argument to_physical! scratch arity, which pays one explicit copy into a caller-supplied scratch for exactly that guarantee (decision 64); to keep a uniform, portable contract, the allocating to_physical/to_spectral copy their input before transforming, so non-! calls never mutate their argument (the Julia convention) while ! calls stay allocation-light and document that the source may be consumed.\n\nFieldSpectralMultipliers holds six one-dimensional arrays: derivative wavenumbers wavenumber_1–wavenumber_3 and squared_wavenumber_1–squared_wavenumber_3, plus the grid and layout. It lives in Fields, as a sibling of FieldTransformPlans, because it is representation infrastructure (what the spectral axes mean), not a differential operator. Two constructors by design:\n\nAn explicit constructor taking all six arrays (lengths validated), testable in isolation and letting callers control the backend.\nA convenience constructor from a field: wavenumbers 2pi nL, with n the integer mode index and L the box length, are built on the host and copied to the field's backend (no scalar GPU writes).\n\nThe two differ in the type they produce, not only in convenience: a final Canonical type parameter records whether the arrays were derived from the grid (the convenience constructor) or supplied by the caller (the explicit one, which validates lengths and not values). Two canonical sets on a matching grid and layout therefore hold equal values by construction, which is what lets convolve collapse two radial kernels without a runtime test — see decision 61.\n\nThe Nyquist split is deliberate and must not be unified. On even-length axes:\n\nDerivative multipliers are zeroed at the Nyquist frequency. The Nyquist mode of a real signal is a pure cosine; multiplying by mathrmi k_N would produce a component the real signal cannot represent, and the symmetric, real-preserving choice for its derivative is zero. This is standard pseudospectral practice and keeps derivatives of real fields exactly real.\nSquared multipliers keep the true k^2 at Nyquist. The Laplacian of the Nyquist mode is perfectly well defined (-k_N^2 mathrmmode); zeroing it would silently remove grid-scale dissipation and corrupt inverse_laplacian.\n\nReference tests pin this down: multipliers vs fftfreq/rfftfreq, transform round-trips, and a real-field Nyquist end-to-end test (the derivative zeroed at Nyquist vs the squared wavenumber kept) — see docs/TESTING.md.\n\nOperators are multipliers — the intertwiner square. mathcal F (to_spectral, with inverse to_physical) is a linear isomorphism, and every translation-invariant operator L — gradient (nabla), divergence (nablacdot), curl (nablatimes), laplacian (Delta) — is diagonalized by it into a pointwise multiplier hat L = m(k) cdot (mathrmia, mathrmiacdot, mathrmiatimes, -k^2, writing a for the derivative wavenumber and k for the true one — the Nyquist split just above):\n\nbeginCD\nf      mathcal F   hat f \nVLVV                   VVhat LV \nLf     mathcal F   widehatLf\nendCD\nqquadqquad mathcal Fcirc L = hat Lcircmathcal F \n\nThis commuting square is invariant 2 (one object, two representations) and the entire design of DifferentialOperators: L is never formed in physical space — the multiplier hat L is applied in spectral and conjugated by mathcal F^pm1. It is the worked case of §2's representation-validity rule: an operation may be offered in a representation iff its square commutes.","title":"4. Spectral machinery"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#5.-Validation-design","page":"Architecture","text":"Fields owns compatibility checking, built from atomic Boolean predicates (_same_scalar_type, _same_grid, _same_layout, _same_backend) composed into _check_same_domain. The last of those is what realizes invariant 14: it throws when two sections live in different storage families (an Array against a CuArray), and it sits in the domain check rather than below it, so the error does not depend on which operation the user happened to call. Tensor order is deliberately not among them — _same_tensor_order is checked one level up, in _check_field_compatibility only, because the domain check must let order differ for the operators that change it (gradient, divergence). Composed on top are the representation-dispatched _check_field_domain_compatibility (physical-physical and spectral-spectral methods — the dispatch barrier that prevents cross-representation mixing), the unexported _check_field_compatibility (un-prefixed, but documented with the internals), and _check_field_matches_context for transform plans.\n\nHistory: FieldAlgebra used to carry near-duplicate checkers (_check_scalar_field_compatibility, _check_tensor2_vector_field_compatibility). These were removed in favor of direct calls to Fields._check_field_domain_compatibility — an explicit decision against alias names: one concept, one name, imported explicitly.\n\nCheck placement follows one rule: bodies that go through field broadcasting do not pre-check, because the broadcast machinery already enforces compatibility (shape/grid/style promotion); bodies that bypass broadcasting — copy-then-kernel paths, zero-mode updates — check explicitly. This is why, e.g. physical scalar±Real methods are bare broadcasts while their spectral counterparts call _check_field_compatibility before _copy_field_data!.","title":"5. Validation design"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#6.-The-code-generation-architecture-of-FieldAlgebra","page":"Architecture","text":"","title":"6. The code-generation architecture of FieldAlgebra"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Why-it-emerged","page":"Architecture","text":"The original FieldAlgebra was 3033 hand-written lines defining ~198 public methods, with per-component kernels unrolled by hand (three statements per vector operation, nine per tensor). The regularity was overwhelming: one mathematical operation expands into 8–32 near-identical methods (two representations × two argument orders × in-place/allocating × vector/tensor). Hand maintenance meant every new operation or convention change had to be edited consistently in dozens of places — a divergence machine. The refactor regenerates the same method set from small tables, so each formula and each convention is written exactly once, while the public surface (and its deliberate absences) stays bit-identical.","title":"Why it emerged"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Layers","page":"Architecture","text":"M1 — expression builders (the vertical-algebra builders in FiberSections, imported by FieldAlgebra): _vector_component(symbol, kind, i) / _tensor_component(symbol, kind, i, j) produce component-access expressions, with kind = :field (component view) or :fiber (StaticArrays indexing); _sum_terms and _product combine formulas; _vector_kernel_body / _tensor_kernel_body (with alpha_beta::Bool) and _scalar_kernel_body wrap formulas into kernel bodies ending in return destination. With alpha_beta = true the body branches on iszero(β_value) so the pure-overwrite path stays allocation-free.\nM2 — elementwise families (done): flat @eval tables stamp out constant-fiber kernels (an update lambda (target, value) -> :($target .+= $value) applied per component via statement splats), field-field combination and operators, scalar-constant operators, field±fiber (one row per field type carries the kernels and yields eight methods), real scaling, and scalar-field×fiber (rows carry the destination tensor order for similar_field(…, Val(order))). Type parameters enter tables as expressions — :(TensorFieldPhysical{2, T}) with where {T<:AbstractFloat} on the emitted method. Irregular corners stay explicit, each with a comment saying why: the scalar±Real family (physical broadcasts vs zero-mode updates; reversed subtraction is its own operation), the four ambiguity-resolving multiply! signatures, and the physical-only divisions.\nM3 — contraction kernels (done): one formula table per family — dot, cross, tensor-vector and tensor-tensor mul!, transpose, tensor inner products, pointwise norms, sym/skew/dev — fed through the kernel-body builders. Per-family flags: has_beta (α/β accumulation for the mul! families; five-argument forms convert T(α), T(β), three-argument forms delegate one(T), zero(T)) and the routing through the fused executor (cross and the unary index-shuffles — transpose, sym, skew, dev — run alias-safe by construction, an element's sources read before its outputs are written, decision 39; mul! documents the no-alias contract instead, per LinearAlgebra convention). The sym/skew/dev methods on bare FiberTensor2 remain explicit StaticArrays one-liners.\nM4 — public wrappers/operators (done, folded into M3): the allocating forms and the * operators (tensor·vector, tensor·tensor) are generated alongside their mul!/dot/cross/… kernels, completing the 206-method surface.\n\nThe same builders are reused verbatim to generate the point-value algebra over the unstructured base (§10) — the formulas are base-independent (§2). They live in the shared FiberSections module (with the _assign! primitive); both FieldAlgebra and Points source them from there, so each fiberwise formula — including the norm/sym/skew/dev ones, via _section_scalar_type — is written exactly once.","title":"Layers"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Verifying-codegen-changes","page":"Architecture","text":"With Julia available, verification is runtime introspection: load the package, compare per-generic methods(f) counts against the committed baseline (test/method_parity.jl), and run detect_ambiguities (from Test) over the package. The full M2+M3 baseline is 206 public methods (e.g. * 28, mul! 30, dot/dot! 10 each, add! 14, combine! 2, tr 2, det/det! 1 each). (During the no-Julia refactor phase, parity was checked by simulating table expansion and diffing normalized signatures against the original file; runtime introspection supersedes that.)","title":"Verifying codegen changes"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#7.-Aliasing-policy","page":"Architecture","text":"Alias safety is by construction: every fused pass — the one-pass differential operators (decisions 32 and 38's operator kernels) and the generic fiberwise executor behind the algebra (decision 39) — reads all of an element's source values into registers before writing any of that element's destinations, and elements are disjoint, so an in-place call (curl!(u, u, m), transpose!(A, A), sym!(A, A)) is computed directly and is bitwise-identical to the non-aliased call, with no temporary.\n\nThat argument carries one hypothesis, load-bearing rather than incidental: one section's element n has to be the other's element n. Ownership supplies it — a section's constructor rejects borrowed storage, a SubArray anywhere in the parent chain, so same-array is the only way two sections can share memory (decision 59). Before that rule, two partially-overlapping views at different base indices broke the argument silently across fourteen operations; such a pair is now not merely undetected but unconstructible.\n\nThe former guard family (_guard_noalias! and its point-value twin), which ran an aliased call through a fresh field and a copy-back, is retired — decision 39 removed its last callers. The one remaining aliasing rule is contractual: mul! documents that the destination must not alias the inputs, matching LinearAlgebra.mul! semantics (its fused pass happens to be alias-safe too, but the documented contract is not widened).","title":"7. Aliasing policy"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#8.-Broadcasting","page":"Architecture","text":"Fields broadcast storage-wise: a field in a broadcast stands for its data array, and the field broadcast style (owned by Fields) materializes into field storage. Style promotion is one-sided by design — define a single binary rule per style pair, never symmetric mixed-representation rules. The broadcast layer doubles as the compatibility enforcement for broadcast-mediated methods (§5).\n\nThe broadcast layer is internal machinery, not a public contract. A consumer can dot-broadcast fields — Julia's dispatch makes that unpreventable without hostile piracy against Base idiom — and the expression will fuse and return a field; but nothing about that surface is specified, verified, or guarded. In particular it can express what the method set deliberately withholds (invariant 1): a .* between two fields in spectral representation compiles and computes the banned coefficientwise product, with none of the named operations' checks. The public contract is the named method set; the broadcast layer exists to implement it (decision 35).","title":"8. Broadcasting"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#9.-Performance-model-and-CPU-threading","page":"Architecture","text":"Elementwise field operations are memory-bandwidth-bound streaming; FFTs dominate asymptotically. Both are now threaded on CPU (shared memory). On GPU, broadcasting and the device FFT libraries already launch parallel kernels, so the CPU threading is dispatched away (below). The full performance model (per operation class), the externally sourced best practices, the recorded benchmark findings, and the tracked benchmark suite live in the companion PERFORMANCE.md.\n\nFFTs. FFTW threading is baked in at plan time, so the package's __init__ calls FFTW.set_num_threads(Threads.nthreads()) before any FieldTransformPlans is built (set_fft_threads! retunes it). Oversubscription caveat: FFTW's pthreads multiply with Julia's tasks — code that parallelizes across fields and transforms inside each task should build plans under set_fft_threads!(1). The common case (one field, transforms serial in a time loop) wants nthreads() FFTW threads, the default.\n\nElementwise/fiberwise ops. One internal primitive _assign!(destination, bc) in FiberSections. The generic method is Broadcast.materialize!, the safe default: GPU and unknown array wrappers keep the standard fused broadcast, so invariant 6 holds by dispatch, not discipline. The Union{Array, SubArray{<:Any, <:Any, <:Array}} specialization threads a single instantiated Broadcasted over contiguous chunks (one Broadcasted tree, no temporaries, one memory pass), bitwise-identical to serial because each output index is written once. The threaded path splits the destination's slab axis — the last among the largest, so a whole-vector-field write splits a grid axis, never the length-3 fiber — and runs the standard fused materialize! per slab over sliced views of the operands, so each slab executes the same SIMD kernel the serial path uses (decision 38; its per-element predecessor ran ~4.7× slower per core and inverted at low thread counts — PERFORMANCE.md §4 finding (l)). A serial cutoff (_SERIAL_CUTOFF, default 2^14) keeps small ops — including the one-element zero-mode views — serial. Policy is hybrid: threaded when nthreads() > 1 and above the cutoff, with a task-local ScopedValue override (with_threading(enabled = …)) for nested-parallel callers.\n\nTwo plug points route work through _assign!: the field broadcast style's copyto! (all whole-field dotted ops and the field-level algebra), and the kernel assignments — the codegen body builders emit _assign!(target, …) (via _to_broadcasted, which rewrites a dotted formula into a lazy broadcasted tree at codegen time), and the hand-written FieldAlgebra / DifferentialOperators kernels use the @_assign! macro in place of .=. (The decision-39 fused executor is the third route: its values functions, each reading one element's inputs and returning its outputs, run inside _fiberwise_pass!'s kernel and slab loop under the same gate.) No types, signatures, or method tables change — the 206-method surface and the zero-ambiguity property are preserved. Discipline: a raw elementwise write must go through _assign!/@_assign!, never a bare .=, or it silently runs single-threaded.\n\nReductions (global norms, integrals — the L²/measure structure) are now provided in the Reductions module (decision 18), built on sum/dot/maximum rather than the threaded _assign!: those reorder floating-point adds (pairwise on the host, differently on threads/GPU), so they are tolerance-tested, not bitwise like the assignments here. (The only already-threaded part is a prior step in a couple of them: lpnorm for p ne 2 builds the pointwise fiber magnitude lvert frvert via the vertical norm — an _assign!-routed fiberwise op, hence threaded — then reduces that with the plain sum/maximum. The reductions inner/l2norm/integral/mean build no such field, so they are a single, unthreaded dot/sum.) That remaining work landed as decision 40: every CPU reduction now runs a fixed midpoint tree, spawned at its top levels through the same gate, bitwise thread-count-invariant; the speedup saturates at socket bandwidth over single-core (typically 2–6×; more on multi-socket nodes, where thread pinning and NUMA first-touch matter — ThreadPinning.jl). Placement is not settled by similar, which reserves address space without touching pages: the first write decides, so a threaded _assign! fill distributes pages across domains while a serial one concentrates them. On the two-socket cluster node decision 37 names as the CPU path's target, that difference is the dominant bandwidth risk, and no measurement of it exists — a single-socket workstation cannot produce one.\n\nThe execution-route policy — which mechanism a new or rewritten operation takes. The routes below were each adopted by measurement (decisions 30, 38, 39, 40); together they form the standing rule, so a future operation is placed by its structure, not by precedent-mining:\n\nSingle-output elementwise work whose traffic is already at the fused ideal takes the broadcast route (@_assign!): one fused expression, the threading gate and slab materialize! on CPU, the device's native broadcast elsewhere. (add!, combine!, laplacian!, the scalar convolve!.)\nMulti-output fiberwise work with shared source reads and no coordinate-dependent coefficient takes the fused executor (_fiberwise_pass!): a values function stamped from the shared formula tables, one KernelAbstractions kernel on a device, the gated linear @simd loop on CPU. (cross!, the mul! family, the shuffles and decompositions, det!, the scalar-field actions, the fiber cases of convolve!.)\nElementwise work whose coefficient depends on the mode's cartesian index (wavenumbers) takes a decision-38-shape pair: a hand-written KA kernel for devices and a gated cartesian @simd loop for CPU, both from one set of per-mode expressions. (gradient!, curl!, symmetric_gradient!, helmholtz_projection!, and applying a kernel not held as one coefficient per mode — decision 50's axis-factored class and decision 51's radial one, which take it for the same reason: a broadcast device route paired with a gated CPU loop, rather than a hand-written kernel with one, as PERFORMANCE.md finding (o) measures. The exponential family's vector and tensor forms took this shape in decision 79: one exponential of k^2 per mode.)\nReductions take the backend split of decisions 30/40: the fixed midpoint tree on CPU — gated, spawned at its top levels, bitwise thread-count-invariant — and the device's own parallel reduce elsewhere.\nAtomic accumulation or data-dependent irregular access — and only that — takes KernelAbstractions on every backend (the transfer gather/scatter, the shell binning): a gated loop cannot serve atomics or per-point stencils, so these are the only operations outside the threading policy, and the atomic ones are tolerance-tested where reordering perturbs last bits.\n\nWhatever the route, three obligations are constant: a migration is verified bitwise against the implementation it replaces (or against an oracle when the arithmetic legitimately changes), the element-traffic test counts no reads or writes beyond the fused minimum or names why, and alias safety is either by construction (an element's reads precede its writes) or a documented contract (mul!).\n\nAn operand count the caller chooses is worked through in groups of a fixed size (decision 88). Four operations take as many operands as their caller passes: add! and subtract! over sections, map! over sources, displace! over terms, and weighted_inner over a tuple of weights. Whatever route one of them takes, it handles at most a fixed number of operands per pass — eight for the first three, four weights for the reduction — and within a pass every per-element step that indexes the operands is written out with a literal index per operand, generated per count, never a splat of an unbounded list, a map over a tuple, or a closure indexing a tuple of mixed types. The ceilings this keeps clear are the compiler's, not the mathematics': Julia may decline to specialize a function on the length of a trailing argument list unless its signature names the count (Vararg{T, N}); a tuple of 32 or more entries leaves Base's specialized tuple functions for a generic path, which allocates on a CPU and cannot run inside a device kernel; and a device kernel's arguments are limited in size. The group sizes are package constants rather than per-machine tunings, set well below all three. Grouping must not change a result: each group continues the previous one's left-to-right order, an operand that is also the destination is copied before a later group would read it, and the torus form of displace! wraps once, after the last group.\n\nThe kernel sites split into two classes for the threading policy — and two kernels are not bitwise. The fused operations of decisions 38/39 (gradient!, curl!, symmetric_gradient!, the fiberwise executor behind the algebra) are KernelAbstractions kernels on a device only; on a CPU array they run slab-threaded plain loops that pass through the same _use_threads gate as the broadcast route, so the cutoff and the with_threading override govern them.\n\nBitwise is a same-backend contract, and the reason it cannot cross a backend is compiler contraction. A device compiler folds x + c * v into a fused multiply-add, rounding once where the host's two instructions round twice, so the same source expression is not the same arithmetic. The difference is a last bit — for displace! it measures 18 times 10^-15 in Float64 on coordinates of order one — but it is real, it is not noise, and it is why every cross-backend comparison in the suites is tolerance-tested while thread-count invariance is asserted exactly. Writing muladd on the host side to force the two into agreement was measured and declined: it buys no speed on either backend, improves the error bound without improving every element, and would rest a package guarantee on undocumented vendor contraction (PERFORMANCE.md finding (z)).\n\nOne pairing the route list above does not name outright, because it mixes two of its entries, is worth recording since decision 50 added the second instance: an operation may take the broadcast route on a device and a hand-written gated loop on a CPU. The helmholtz_projection! operator pairs a KernelAbstractions kernel with a gated CPU loop (decisions 38, 40); the axis-factored kernel of decision 50 pairs a broadcast with one. The reason is measured rather than aesthetic — a broadcast whose operands carry singleton axes indexes cartesian and launches flat, which is what a device wants, but does not vectorize on a CPU, where it ran at a fixed element rate independent of precision (PERFORMANCE.md finding (o)). Either way the pair is backend-dispatched, and a test checks both halves are reached, lest the CPU half be dead code.\n\nTwo groups of operations, however, are KA kernels on every backend — the gather/scatter transfer kernels (§10), and the radial shell binning (decisions 25, 34) — and on a CPU array these launch on the CPU() backend, which partitions the index range by Threads.nthreads() itself (helmholtz_projection! left this set when decision 40 gave it a gated loop twin, so what remains is exactly the atomic/irregular kernels — the exemption is structural, not historical). Two consequences follow for those two families, and neither is a defect to fix — they are the price of their kernel shapes. First, a KA launch never consults the _assign! policy above: the serial cutoff and the with_threading override do not reach those two groups, which thread by KA's rules at every size (the exclusion is stated on with_threading's own docstring). Second, the two kernels that accumulate through Atomix — the anterpolation scatter and the shell binning — sum in a nondeterministic order, and floating-point addition is not associative, so they are not bitwise-reproducible: not across thread counts, and not even between two runs at one thread count. Measured on a Ryzen 9800X3D over twelve trials at 8 and 16 threads, every trial differed, anterpolate! by 2–3 ulp of the field's scale and radial_spectrum only inside numerically-zero shells (max lvert Delta rvert = 1.4e-47). The remaining kernel with disjoint writes — the gather — is bitwise-reproducible, and was measured to be so across 1, 2, 4 and 8 threads, as is helmholtz_projection! on the gated loop it moved to. The threading suite (test/threading.jl) encodes exactly this split: bitwise equality asserted for the disjoint writers (including against a subprocess launched at a different thread count, the one comparison the in-process with_threading toggle cannot make for a KA kernel), a tolerance for the Atomix pair — the same tolerance-not-bitwise treatment the reductions above receive, and for the same reason.","title":"9. Performance model and CPU threading"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#10.-Interpolation-and-anterpolation-(the-gridpoint-transfer-layer)","page":"Architecture","text":"Lagrangian particle tracking by a consumer (§1) requires two transfer operations between the Eulerian fields and off-grid positions. They are formal adjoints of one another, and the package gives each a fixed name:\n\nInterpolation — grid → point (the gather direction): evaluate a field f at the off-grid positions xs (a PointSet, x_p the p-th position). Allocating interpolate(f, xs[, scheme]) / in-place interpolate!(out, f, xs[, scheme]), plus the callable f(x) (and its broadcast f.(xs)) as the ergonomic face of interpolate.\nAnterpolation — point → grid (the scatter direction): deposit per-point quantities qs (q_p the value at x_p) back onto a field. Allocating anterpolate(grid, xs, qs[, scheme]) / in-place anterpolate!(field, xs, qs[, scheme]).\n\nBoth carry the usual allocating / in-place (!) pair. The α/β accumulate form (beta = 0 overwrite, beta ne 0 accumulate — the mul! convention, since they are the linear maps W / W^top) belongs to anterpolation alone: accumulation is what a scatter needs, and it is the direction that writes into a preallocated field, while a gather fills a fresh point-value section. So the pair is symmetric in name and adjointness, and asymmetric in three places: a gather yields point values while its adjoint returns a field; the accumulate weights are taken by the scatter alone; and only interpolation gets the callable f(x), because evaluation is pointwise while its adjoint is not (see the module docstring). The driving case is two-way coupling in the cloud-parcel DNS this package serves: the water-vapour mass and latent heat a droplet releases on condensation (or absorbs on evaporation) must be returned to the supersaturation and temperature fields.\n\nThe two operations are a conserving adjoint pair. Interpolation and anterpolation share the same stencil weights w, which form a partition of unity (sum w = 1); anterpolation is then the exact adjoint of interpolation, and — since sum w = 1 — the total deposited quantity is conserved: summed over the grid, the scattered field equals the input summed over the points, in each fiber component. Using mismatched weights injects a spurious source/dissipation term (Sundaram & Collins 1996). For the parcel application this is mandatory — total water and latent heat must be conserved as vapour passes between droplets and the fields — so the design fixes anterpolation as the adjoint of whatever interpolation kernel is in use. The function–measure duality underlying this adjointness — evaluation as pairing with a Dirac mass, f(x) = delta_x(f), and anterpolation as the pushforward of point masses against the normalized counting measure, tfrac1Msum_p q_p delta_x_p (regularized to tfrac1Msum_p q_p w(cdot - x_p), i.e. a regularized point source) — is given in full in the Interpolation module docstring.\n\nThose two facts — adjointness and a partition-of-unity reproduction — are the commuting identities below. The two inner products are concrete pairings against the measure each base carries: on point-values a b the counting measure makes langlecdotcdotrangle_mathrmpts a sum over the M points, while on fields f g the cell-volume measure makes langlecdotcdotrangle_mathrmgrid a volume integral over the box Omega (with cdot the fiber contraction — the ordinary product for scalars, the dot product for vectors). Both are normalized by the total measure — divided by M and by Omega respectively — which is what makes them intensive averages rather than extensive totals, and it is precisely that normalization the NM factor further down is accounting for:\n\nbegingathered\nlangle a brangle_mathrmpts = frac1M sum_p=1^M a_pcdot b_p \nlangle f grangle_mathrmgrid = frac1Omegaint_Omega f(x)cdot g(x)mathrmdx\n= frac1N sum_x f(x)cdot g(x)\nendgathered\n\nsummed over the M points and over the box Omega cut into N cells, as inner computes them; the integral equals the grid sum for a resolved product; otherwise the sum defines it. Writing operatornamesample_mathrmgrid for sampling a function at the grid nodes, operatornameeval_mathrmpts for evaluating it at the points, and phi for any polynomial in Pi_d (the polynomials up to the scheme's reproduction degree d), the adjointness and the polynomial reproduction read\n\nbegingathered\nsum_p (W f)_pcdot q_p = sum_x f(x)cdot(W^top q)(x) \nW bigl(operatornamesample_mathrmgrid phibigr) = operatornameeval_mathrmpts phi\nquad (phi in Pi_d)\nendgathered\n\nthe left is the plain transpose W^top — the partition-of-unity scatter anterpolate applies; normalizing each side by its base count gives the measure-weighted M langle W f qrangle_mathrmpts = N langle f W^top qrangle_mathrmgrid (N grid nodes, M points — the NM is just the two normalizations). Intrinsically the pair is a pullback–pushforward: writing iota  B_mathrmpoints to Omega for the map from the point base B_mathrmpoints to the torus Omega that carries each point to its location x_p, pulling the trivial bundle back along iota keeps the fiber and changes only the base, B_mathrmpoints times V = iota^*(Omega times V) — a base-change, not a fiber operation (decision 19):\n\nbeginCD\nB_mathrmpoints times V iota times mathrmid_V Omega times V \nVpiVV VVpiV \nB_mathrmpoints iota Omega\nendCD\n\nInterpolation is the pullback of the section, W = iota^* otimes mathrmid_V (fiber-preserving — the trivial bundle leaves the fiber by the identity; (Wf)_p = f(x_p)); anterpolation the pushforward of the dual measure, iota_* (iota_* q = tfrac1Msum_p q_p delta_x_p, the Dirac mass above regularized by the stencil — pushed forward against the normalized counting measure, the same one langlecdotcdotrangle_mathrmpts uses, which is what puts the 1M there and makes the identification below come out at NM rather than N). That pushforward lands in measures, while (NM) W^top q is a section; the two are identified only after regularizing by the stencil and taking the density with respect to the normalized grid measure, and with those two steps understood iota_* = (NM) W^top for the uniform measures, the scalar rescaling that turns the implemented transpose W^top into the exact L²-adjoint. They have opposite variance — iota pulls a function back but pushes a measure forward — so the adjointness is their adjunction langle iota^* f qrangle_mathrmpts = langle f iota_* qrangle_mathrmgrid. (Strictly W is iota^* after a reconstruction — the spline or band-limited spectral interpolant lifting grid samples to a section over Omega — which the reproduction identity above shows is exact on Pi_d.)\n\nTwo scheme families (decision 23). The scheme argument is an AbstractTransferScheme — the choice of kernel and representation that realizes W / W^top (the commutative-diagram view above: a scheme is how the Dirac delta_x_p is realized). Two families share the same interpolate/anterpolate verbs and the same adjointness contract:\n\nPhysical-domain splines (AbstractSplineScheme) are local kernels on the physical nodal values (the gather/scatter kernels below); approximate, conserving, the production default.\nSpectral-domain exact transfer acts on a field in spectral representation by the literal inverse/forward DFT f(x_p) = tfrac1Nsum_k hat f(k) e^mathrmi kcdot(x_p-o) and its adjoint (W^top q)(k) = sum_p q_p e^-mathrmi kcdot(x_p-o) (here N is the grid-point count, o the grid origin, and x_p the p-th sample point). The gather's sum_k runs over the full spectrum; the equivalent sum over the stored rfft half — carrying the Parseval weight alpha(k) (the per-mode multiplicity: 1 where a stored coefficient represents only itself, 2 where it stands for a conjugate pair the half-spectrum dropped) and a real part, which is what the code evaluates — is written out where DirectDFT is detailed below, and is worth reading before anyone reproduces the compact form against a half-spectrum array. DirectDFT is the exact O(NM) form — the definition of the spectral-domain pair, a transparent baseline, the reference for divergence-free interpolation (it reproduces the band-limited field exactly, matching to_physical on nodes), and the oracle for the fast NUFFT (the O(Nlog N + M) windowed gridding version, owned natively — spread → FFT → deconvolve, reusing the spline kernels with a window, an oversampled FieldTransformPlans, and an assignment_window-style deconvolution; exact to a requested tolerance varepsilon on the resolved band). The adjoint relation carries the measure factor as for the splines: M langle What f qrangle_mathrmpts = N langle hat f W^top qrangle_mathrmgrid.\n\nMethod: the local splines (the physical-domain family). The package uses the local high-order spline interpolation of Lalescu, Teaca & Carati (2010), as implemented in the TurTLE code (Lalescu et al. 2022). At its core it is Hermite interpolation with the node derivatives supplied by finite differences, which makes the interpolant simultaneously local, high-order, and smooth; in the cubic four-point case it reduces to the Catmull–Rom spline (Catmull & Rom 1974). Concretely f(x) is a tensor-product weighted sum sum w_i_1 w_i_2 w_i_3  f(c+i) over a compact (2n+2)^3 stencil (n the per-axis finite-difference half-width) — c the grid cell containing x and i the integer node offset within the stencil — with closed-form polynomial weights w. Lalescu's two hierarchies share this stencil: the smooth hierarchy (LalescuSpline(n, m)) is a two-point Hermite of degree 2m+1 that matches the value and the first m derivatives at the two bracketing nodes (C^m, 0 le m le 2n; m = 0 is linear, n = 1 m = 1 is Catmull–Rom), and the Lagrange hierarchy (LagrangeSpline(n)) is the C^0 degree-2n+1 interpolant of all 2n+2 nodes. Field derivatives needed for the velocity gradient / deformation tensor are obtained by differentiating the same local polynomial, not from a separate field. Three properties make it the right fit:\n\nSingle representation, no global precompute. The weights act directly on the real-space nodal values, so interpolation acts only on the physical representation — unlike the spectral-domain schemes (DirectDFT, NUFFT; decision 23, below), which act on the spectral representation directly and reconstruct the band-limited field exactly. (The B-spline interpolation of van Hinsberg et al. (2012) — whose coefficients require a global transform that, in a spectral code, costs one FFT — is the higher-accuracy local alternative still in reserve.) PLANNED — the van Hinsberg global B-spline (needs a design discussion before coding): a coefficient pre-transform, then a compact local stencil — higher accuracy per stencil width, at the cost of a global solve that breaks the \"single representation\" property, so it needs a coefficient-field type and a transform step. It would slot under AbstractTransferScheme but changes the data flow (a global precompute), so it is a recorded future direction, not a drop-in scheme. (The other once-planned alternative — exact spectral transfer — is done: the O(NM) DirectDFT oracle and the fast NUFFT, owned natively with no NonuniformFFTs.jl dependency, under AbstractTransferScheme; decision 23.) See decision 17 and the references below.\nGPU-friendly (the GPU-first invariant, §1): per-point interpolation is a gather of (2n+2)^3 neighbours plus a fused multiply–add — embarrassingly parallel, no FFT, no global solve, no communication beyond the periodic stencil wrap. Anterpolation is the matching scatter.\nTrivial, conservative anterpolation: the same closed-form w deposit a point's source over its stencil with sum w = 1 — exactly the conserving two-way-coupling feedback the parcel DNS needs.\n\nThe interpolation order is a tunable parameter (n, m), not hard-wired, so accuracy can be matched to the spectral resolution (standard practice for pseudospectral particle tracking). The transfer kernels are implemented as portable KernelAbstractions gather/scatter kernels (the scatter accumulates atomically); the smooth per-axis weights are generated for any (n, m) by a small linear solve — match the value and the order-1…m centred-finite-difference (Fornberg 1988) derivatives at the two bracketing nodes — and pinned to 1e-12 against TurTLE's beta_n1_m1-style weights (n ↔ interp_neighbours, m ↔ smoothness), with the closed-form Catmull–Rom (n = 1, m = 1) as an independent anchor. The smooth scheme reproduces polynomials to degree min(2m+1 2n) for n ge 1; the Lagrange scheme to degree 2n+1. The bound needs that scope because the two-point n = 0 stencil is linear and reproduces degree 1, where the formula would read 0.\n\nData types — the unstructured base, in the package's own language. Interpolation needs to name two new objects: off-grid locations and the field values there. The package names them in its base/fiber vocabulary, not as particles — deliberately, because GBGPeriodicFields is a math backend (§1): \"particle\"/\"tracer\" is the consumer's interpretation (a location with physical attributes and an equation of motion), not a primitive here. The organizing principle reuses the existing base/fiber split: a field is a section — fibers (scalar, FiberVector, FiberTensor2) over a base — and so far the base has always been the structured Grid3D. Interpolation adds the unstructured base and its section, reusing the same fibers; the maps between sections over the two bases are interpolation W and its adjoint anterpolation W^top (the same W/W^top used above).\n\nPoint{T} = SVector{3,T} is a single position; a base-space alias of FiberVector (same representation, different role). It is an alias, not a wrapper, so arithmetic stays StaticArrays-native (invariant 8); position vs. value is a distinction of role, not dispatch.\nPointSet holds M positions in structure-of-arrays layout (M, 3) (point index first, coordinate last — each coordinate axis contiguous across points, mirroring the field's fiber-last (Nx,Ny,Nz,3) for coalesced GPU gather). It is grid-free: periodicity wraps any coordinate, so positions are untethered from a grid, the field supplying it at transfer time.\nPointValues is the section over a PointSet, as three concrete types mirroring the field zoo — ScalarPointValues / VectorPointValues / TensorPointValues{R} under an abstract AbstractPointValues, SoA layout (M,) / (M,3) / (M,3,3). They are representation-free: a point value is always a physical-space quantity (even exact spectral interpolation produces physical values), so, unlike the field types, they have no physical/spectral split.\n\nAll wrap a generic AbstractArray and allocate via similar, so they are backend-generic (CPU, CUDA, AMDGPU); compatibility reduces to M-matching and matching tensor order (a check predicate in the Fields style). The types live in the Points module, importing Fields helpers via an explicit using ..Fields: ….\n\nImplemented and tested. The point types, their full fiberwise algebra (M2 + M3, generated by reusing FieldAlgebra's per-component builders over an extended section interface — §6 — and locked by a separate 122-method parity baseline, the 206-method field surface byte-unchanged), and all transfer schemes (the local splines plus the spectral DirectDFT/NUFFT, decision 23) are implemented and covered on CPU (Float64/Float32) and the GPU (ROCm, allowscalar(false)).\n\nWhy the code says \"anterpolation.\" The point→grid (scatter) operation is named differently in every community, with no dominant choice: spreading (immersed-boundary method, where it is explicitly the adjoint S = J^* of interpolation, built from the same kernel — Peskin 2002; also NUFFT type-1), deposition / charge assignment (particle-in-cell — Hockney & Eastwood 1988; Birdsall & Langdon 1991), scatter (data-parallel computing), projection / Particle-Source-In-Cell (particle-laden-flow DNS — Crowe, Sharma & Stock 1977), gridding (MRI and radio interferometry), and, in cloud microphysics, no operator noun at all — only the source/sink term it produces and the two-way coupling / thermodynamic feedback it enacts. We adopt anterpolation (Brandt & Lubrecht 1990) because it is the single term whose literal definition is \"the adjoint of interpolation\" — it names the operation by the property that matters, independent of application. The binding carries a docstring listing the synonyms — adjoint of interpolation; spreading; deposition; scatter; projection / PSI-Cell; gridding; two-way-coupling source/feedback — so a reader from any of those fields finds it. \"Extrapolation\" is deliberately rejected: extrapolation means evaluating outside the convex hull of the data, whereas a sample point lies inside a grid cell and the operation is a weighted distribution; the loose use of \"extrapolation\" for it (seen in a few papers) is a misnomer.\n\nBase-space motion: displace! and wrap! (decision 73). The transfer verbs above move field values between the grid and the points; nothing above moves the points. The consuming solver's trajectory update is exactly that missing operation — each position advanced along a weighted sum of vector values held over it, x_p leftarrow x_p + sum_j c_j v_j(p), where x_p is the position of point p, the v_j are VectorPointValues sections (each holding one vector value per point), and each c_j is one plain real number — one scalar per term of the sum, not a per-point array — and until this decision it could only be written by unwrapping the storage and broadcasting on raw arrays, outside every contract the layer states. The geometry says it is a legitimate operation and says where its boundaries are. On this package's flat periodic base with its one global orthonormal frame (§2), the tangent space at every point is canonically the fiber's Cartesian mathbb R^3, so a vector value over a point defines a displacement with no connection machinery: displacement is coordinate addition. It is a named operation and not a + method, because positions are points of the periodic domain, not vectors — an algebra on positions would assert a linear structure the base does not have, and the position-versus-value role split stays intact. And it comes in two forms the type structure itself dictates: a PointSet deliberately carries no grid, so displace! without a grid cannot wrap and is displacement in the universal cover mathbb R^3 — the unwrapped torus, where a       coordinate keeps growing instead of folding back (winding preserved — a mean-square displacement needs it), while displace! with a trailing Grid3D is displacement on the torus proper, the wrap fused into the same pass. The call wrap!(points, grid) is the zero-term case kept as its own named verb: reduction to the canonical representative in the half-open domain o_a o_a + L_a) from each axis's origin over its length — with the floating-point endpoint folded (mod can return the period for a coordinate just below the origin) and non-finite coordinates passed through non-finite, so a diverged trajectory is never silently rewritten into an in-box position. Sampling never requires the wrap — the transfer kernels wrap their stencil indices, so interpolation is invariant under shifting a coordinate by whole periods — but canonicalization is load-bearing for precision on long runs (absolute floating-point resolution degrades as a coordinate grows), which is why the wrap is a package contract and not consumer hygiene. The coefficients are part of what the operation is, so they are not the α/β accumulation scalars and the operation takes no trailing α/β (the combine! precedent); there is deliberately no allocating sibling, displacement being motion of an existing base. The n-term form runs in one fused traversal — positions read once, each section once, positions written once — which collapses a K-step Adams–Bashforth update from K passes to one.\n\nReferences. The canonical list for the WHOLE document — every section and every docstring cites into it, which is why the citation policy and the check-docs DOI cross-check both name it §References. It sits here because the transfer literature is its largest single block, not because its scope is this section.\n\nC. C. Lalescu, B. Teaca, D. Carati, Implementation of high order spline interpolations for tracking test particles in discretized fields, J. Comput. Phys. 229(17) (2010) 5862–5869.\nC. C. Lalescu, B. Bramas, M. Rampp, M. Wilczek, An efficient particle tracking algorithm for large-scale parallel pseudo-spectral simulations of turbulence (TurTLE), Comput. Phys. Commun. 278 (2022) 108406; arXiv:2107.01104. Code: gitlab.mpcdf.mpg.de/TurTLE/turtle.\nM. A. T. van Hinsberg, J. H. M. ten Thije Boonkkamp, F. Toschi, H. J. H. Clercx, On the efficiency and accuracy of interpolation methods for spectral codes, SIAM J. Sci. Comput. 34(4) (2012) B479–B498.\nA. H. Barnett, J. Magland, L. af Klinteberg, A parallel nonuniform fast Fourier transform library based on an exponential of semicircle kernel, SIAM J. Sci. Comput. 41(5) (2019) C479–C504.\nB. Fornberg, Generation of finite difference formulas on arbitrarily spaced grids, Math. Comp. 51(184) (1988) 699–706.\nS. Sundaram, L. R. Collins, Numerical considerations in simulating a turbulent suspension of finite-volume particles, J. Comput. Phys. 124(2) (1996) 337–350.\nC. T. Crowe, M. P. Sharma, D. E. Stock, The Particle-Source-In-Cell (PSI-CELL) model for gas–droplet flows, J. Fluids Eng. 99(2) (1977) 325–332.\nA. Brandt, A. A. Lubrecht, Multilevel matrix multiplication and fast solution of integral equations, J. Comput. Phys. 90(2) (1990) 348–370 (an early use of \"anterpolation\" for the adjoint of interpolation).\nC. S. Peskin, The immersed boundary method, Acta Numerica 11 (2002) 479–517 (spreading = adjoint of interpolation).\nR. W. Hockney, J. W. Eastwood, Computer Simulation Using Particles, Adam Hilger, Bristol, 1988 (deposition / charge assignment; the NGP and CIC windows).\nC. K. Birdsall, A. B. Langdon, Plasma Physics via Computer Simulation, Adam Hilger, Bristol, 1991 (deposition / charge assignment).\nJ. M. Domínguez, A. J. C. Crespo, M. Gómez-Gesteira, J. C. Marongiu, Neighbour lists in smoothed particle hydrodynamics, Int. J. Numer. Methods Fluids 67(12) (2011) 2026–2042 (cell traversal order for neighbour search; corroborates decision 78's own measurement that lexicographic block order beats Z-order/Morton for this access pattern).\nE. Catmull, R. Rom, A class of local interpolating splines, in: Computer Aided Geometric Design, Academic Press, 1974, pp. 317–326.\nL. Grafakos, Classical Fourier Analysis, 3rd ed., Graduate Texts in Mathematics 249, Springer, 2014 (Thm 2.5.10: on a uniform periodic box every homogeneous linear operator is a Fourier multiplier — the math anchor for decision 19's taxonomy). It is cited by that anchor rather than by this section, and is kept in this list as the document's single Fourier-analysis reference.","title":"10. Interpolation and anterpolation (the grid↔point transfer layer)"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#11.-Convolution","page":"Architecture","text":"note: The current shape of the kernel protocol is here, not in the decision log\nThe decision log records how the kernel design arrived — decisions 19, 20, 21, 50, 51, 56, 60, 61 and 62 each changed part of it, and several supersede earlier ones. Reconstructing the present state from that chain is not the intended reading. This section is the current state: a kernel is its own kind of object, never a field; SpectralKernel is the abstract supertype of four storage classes — StoredKernel (one coefficient per mode), SeparableKernel (per-axis factors), RadialKernel (a symbol of lvert krvert^2) and ProductKernel (any two held unmultiplied); all four are interchangeable wherever a kernel is applied and agree bitwise on the host (on a device a complex-valued kernel agrees only to rounding), materialize_kernel expands any other class into the stored one (and materialize_kernel! into a StoredKernel that already exists), and inv and weighted_inner are the two entry points with narrower contracts.\n\nConvolution is the kernel-multiplier role of the measure structure: periodic (circular) convolution of a field with a kernel, in spectral representation. By the convolution theorem (§2), f star g is the coefficientwise product hat f cdot hat g; convolve/convolve! (equiv star, also exported as the infix operator ⋆) apply a kernel — a SpectralKernel, in any of its storage classes — whose transfer function hat g multiplies every fiber component (one method for scalar, vector, and tensor fields), with the α/β accumulate form and alias-safety of a coefficientwise op. It is the spectral sibling of DifferentialOperators (both are coefficientwise multipliers, §4) and the same-base counterpart of the Interpolation transfer (decision 19); offered under its true name convolve, never as * (invariant 1). Spectral representation only; physics (an LES filter, an integrating factor exp(-nu k^2 Delta t)) stays a consumer-built kernel (invariant 12). Six neutral builders ship: spectral_cutoff (the isotropic sharp low-pass), spectral_band (its annular generalization), radial_kernel (any real symbol of k^2, held as a closure), tophat_window (the box average of edges l_a, prod_a operatornamesinc(k_a l_a  2); decision 87), assignment_window (the prod_a operatornamesinc^2 CIC deconvolution window, the top-hat at the grid spacing composed with itself), and translation_kernel (the shift theorem's phase e^-mathrmi kcdot h, which displaces a field by a physical offset h). The first five are real and even in the wavenumber k; translation_kernel is the one complex kernel, and the reality condition a complex kernel must meet — the Hermitian symmetry hat g(-k) = overlinehat g(k), equivalently a real physical-space kernel g — is what forces its value at the Nyquist frequency to be a real attenuation rather than a phase (decision 28). Two fused forms extend the family without a stored kernel or an extra pass (decision 33): exponential_convolve!/exponential_convolve apply the exponential radial kernel exp(c k^2 + s hat w) evaluated lazily from the wavenumber arrays (for a real coefficient c le 0 the periodic Gaussian e^-cDelta; hat w an optional real per-mode exponent field entered as scale times field, s its scale), in single-field and weighted-two-field forms; and the kernel-composed combine! method convolves a weighted combination hat g cdot (alpha hat a + beta hat b) in the same fused pass — the decision-32 composition idiom applied to the combination. See decisions 20, 21, 22, 28, 33.","title":"11. Convolution"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#12.-Reductions","page":"Architecture","text":"Reductions is the L² / measure family — the third operation family, integrating a section over its base against the measure the base carries (Grid3D's uniform cell volume, PointSet's counting measure; decision 18). The integral (equiv intmathrmdmu) is the extensive sum; inner (equiv langlecdotcdotrangle), l2norm, and lpnorm are the normalized L² product and norms, and weighted_inner (equiv langlecdot w cdotrangle) is the spectral multiplier-weighted product langle f w grangle — the reduction-dual of convolve, generalizing inner (decision 27); mean/std/var/cov (extending Statistics) and maximum/minimum/extrema (extending Base) the order statistics, all acting on fields in physical representation and point-values (representation-free); cov returns the vector second central moment as a symmetric mathrmSym^2(V) tensor, which a fiber-preserving var would reduce to its basis-dependent diagonal. The scalar-only std/var additionally read from spectral representation through Parseval (decision 49), built from langle ffrangle - langle frangle^2 — Bessel-scaled for var, its square root for std — both terms already spectral-available. The reductions inner/l2norm also act on a field in spectral representation via Parseval's theorem (equal to the physical inner, no transform; the rfft half-spectrum weighting), and integral/mean via the zero (DC) Fourier mode — hat f(0) = sum_x f gives the mean hat f(0)N and the integral (OmegaN) hat f(0), no transform. The radial pair are spectral/physical duals: radial_spectrum (the shell-resolved inner — the power spectrum) and its Wiener–Khinchin dual radial_correlation (the two-point autocorrelation C(r) = mathcal F^-1(hat f^2)N), each with a two-field co-spectrum form. Five of them — integral, mean, maximum, minimum and extrema — additionally have a mapped form taking a leading function — a map applied to the section's fiber value at every base point, so mean(det, A) averages a determinant and maximum(norm, u) takes a supremum of magnitude, each reduced in one pass with no intermediate section (decision 30). Physics (kinetic energy tfrac12langle uurangle, dissipation, rms) stays consumer-side. See decisions 18, 21, 22, 30.","title":"12. Reductions"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Decision-log","page":"Architecture","text":"The decision log is its own document: docs/DECISIONS.md. It is append-only and is cited from here, and from the source, as \"decision N\".","title":"Decision log"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Fields","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Base-and-layout-types","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Base and layout types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Field-types","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Field types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Transform-plan-and-multiplier-types","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Transform-plan and multiplier types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Transform-operations","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Transform operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Field-accessor-functions","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Field accessor functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Field-allocation","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Field allocation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Moving-between-storage-families","page":"Fields","text":"Host and device are storage families, not representations: a field has both, and they vary independently — the representation says whether its values are the field or its Fourier coefficients, the family says which kind of array holds them. Moving between families is Adapt.jl's verb, extended here for every section, kernel and plan and re-exported, so a consumer needs nothing beyond using GBGPeriodicFields; and because it dispatches on a generic target, CUDA and AMDGPU each supply their own storage rule without this package depending on either. Whether a CUDA array sits in device or unified memory is a CUDA.jl setting rather than a different family; Running on a GPU explains it.","title":"Moving between storage families"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Moving-between-grid-resolutions","page":"Fields","text":"Two spectral fields over the same box whose grids differ in size name the same Fourier modes where they overlap, because a grid of N points over a domain of length L resolves 2π/L times the integers it stores. These two verbs move exactly those shared modes, leaving the rest of the destination untouched, at a cost set by the coarser field — the mode-space counterpart of the storage-family crossing above, and the route by which content computed on a small grid reaches a large one without a transform. Amplitudes are physical: the ratio of the two grids' point counts is applied inside, so the two are exact adjoints of each other.","title":"Moving between grid resolutions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#The-real-field-reality-condition","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"The real-field reality condition"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Removing-the-mean","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Removing the mean"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Comparing-sections","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Comparing sections"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Spectral-kernel-protocol","page":"Fields","text":"The kernel-taking operations dispatch on SpectralKernel, each accepting all four storage classes: convolve, the kernel-composed differential operators, and weighted_inner — the last subject to the separate requirement that its weight be real.","title":"Spectral kernel protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#FFT-thread-configuration","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"FFT thread configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#HDF5-persistence","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"HDF5 persistence"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Internal-API","page":"Fields","text":"These symbols are not part of the public interface; they are documented for contributors — field-allocation and compatibility helpers, the spectral-multiplier broadcast helpers, the broadcast machinery, and the storage-family helpers adapt rebuilds through.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Types","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Fields/#Functions","page":"Fields","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields","page":"Fields","text":"Fields\n\nField representation and transform core for scalar, vector, and second-order tensor fields on three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains, for CPU and GPU array backends. A field is a physical object, and the physical-space and spectral types (for example ScalarFieldPhysical and ScalarFieldSpectral) are two representations of the same field.\n\nThe parts are:\n\nGrid and layout — Grid3D describes the periodic domain geometry, and RealFourierLayout describes the real-to-complex transform layout.\nField types — the scalar, vector, and second-order tensor field types each come in a physical-space and a spectral representation. The two lowest orders carry their own names, ScalarFieldPhysical and VectorFieldPhysical; higher orders are TensorFieldPhysical{R} / TensorFieldSpectral{R}. The tensor order R is a type parameter of every field type.\nPersistence — write_field and read_field map a field to and from one self-describing HDF5 group (methods load with the HDF5 package, as a weak-dependency extension).\nTransforms — FieldTransformPlans holds the FFT plans, and to_physical and to_spectral move a field between the two representations. The allocating forms preserve their input, and the plain in-place ! forms may overwrite the source — except the four-argument scratch arity of to_physical!, which guarantees preservation by consuming a caller-supplied scratch instead; set_fft_threads! sets the thread count FFTW bakes into plans built afterwards.\nSpectral multipliers — FieldSpectralMultipliers holds the wavenumber arrays the differential operators apply as spectral multipliers.\nSpectral kernels — SpectralKernel is the supertype of every coefficientwise multiplier a convolve-style operation can apply, whatever storage it uses — including the stored class, which wraps a field of coefficients. It lives here, rather than with the kernels themselves, so every module that accepts one names a single type; Convolution defines the concrete classes.\nAllocation — similar_field returns uninitialized storage carrying a reference field's representation, tensor order, grid and backend, which is how an operation builds scratch or a result without naming a concrete field type.\nThe reality condition — reality_residual measures how far a spectrum is from a real field's, and reality_projection! projects it onto one, on the two stored self-conjugate planes where the half-spectrum can disagree with itself.\nMoving between grid resolutions — embed_modes! writes a coarser field's Fourier modes into a finer field over the same box and extract_modes! reads them back, each leaving the destination's other modes untouched and costing the coarse field rather than the fine one. Amplitudes are physical, so the pair are exact adjoints of each other.\nMoving between storage families — adapt moves a field, its multipliers, its kernels or its transform plans between host and device arrays (or any other Adapt.jl storage family), preserving the element type and everything else the object carries. Operations refuse a pair that disagrees rather than transferring one to meet the other, so that the cost of crossing the bus is always visible at the call site.\nBroadcasting — a dotted assignment writes into the destination's storage, an out-of-place dotted expression allocates a like field in the same representation, and physical and spectral representations cannot be mixed in one broadcast.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.Grid3D","page":"Fields","text":"Grid3D(\n  grid_size::NTuple{3, Int},\n  domain_size::NTuple{3, T},\n  grid_spacing::NTuple{3, T},\n  origin::NTuple{3, T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nGrid3D(\n  grid_size::NTuple{3, Int},\n  domain_size::NTuple{3, T};\n  origin::NTuple{3, T} = (zero(T), zero(T), zero(T)),\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nUniform three-dimensional grid for a periodic rectangular domain.\n\nThe grid is uniform, so the spacing is not independent: it must equal domain_size ./ grid_size along every axis. The two-argument constructor computes it and is the one to prefer; the four-argument form checks what it is handed and throws an ArgumentError on a spacing that contradicts the domain. Every grid size must be positive, every domain length finite and positive, and every origin coordinate finite.\n\nArguments\n\ngrid_size — the number of grid points along each axis, each positive.\ndomain_size — the length of the periodic box along each axis, each finite and positive.\ngrid_spacing — the four-argument form only: the spacing between adjacent points, which must equal domain_size ./ grid_size.\norigin — the physical coordinates of the first grid point, each finite: positional in the four-argument form, a keyword defaulting to zero in the two-argument form.\n\nFields\n\ngrid_size::NTuple{3, Int} — the number of grid points in each spatial direction.\ndomain_size::NTuple{3, T} — the length of the domain in each spatial direction.\ngrid_spacing::NTuple{3, T} — the spacing between adjacent grid points in each spatial direction. Stored rather than recomputed, but not independent — it must equal domain_size ./ grid_size, which the four-argument constructor enforces.\norigin::NTuple{3, T} — the physical coordinates of the first grid point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.Grid3D"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.RealFourierLayout","page":"Fields","text":"RealFourierLayout()\nRealFourierLayout(fft_dims::NTuple{3, Int})\n\nLayout convention for three-dimensional real-to-complex Fourier transforms.\n\nThe main role of the ordering is to specify the real-FFT half dimension: in dimension fft_dims[1], only the nonnegative frequencies are represented, while the other dimensions use the full complex FFT frequency ordering.\n\nThe zero-argument constructor uses fft_dims = (1, 2, 3).\n\nFields\n\nfft_dims — the tuple of transformed dimensions passed to the FFT planner, whose first entry is the halved (folded) axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.RealFourierLayout"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractField","page":"Fields","text":"AbstractField\n\nEither representation of a field: the union of AbstractFieldPhysical and AbstractFieldSpectral.\n\nIt names the argument type of the operations that do not care which representation they are handed — the grid, layout, element-type and tensor-order accessors, and the compatibility checks. An operation valid in only one representation is typed with that representation instead, which is how the method set encodes representation validity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractField"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldPhysical","page":"Fields","text":"AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R}\n\nAbstract supertype for fields represented by values in physical space.\n\nHere T<:AbstractFloat is the real scalar type associated with the field and R is the tensor order stored as an integer value parameter. Concrete subtypes provide the actual storage and metadata. The generic field interface obtains T and R through field_scalar_type and field_tensor_order, and obtains the stored data through field_data.\n\nnote: A field in physical representation owns its storage\nA concrete subtype's constructor rejects borrowed storage — a SubArray anywhere in the parent chain — so slicing a larger preallocated buffer and wrapping the view will throw. Ownership is what makes an aliased in-place call safe (FieldAlgebra). Allocate with similar_field, or copy the slice. Any owning array is accepted: Array, CuArray, ROCArray, or a reshape of one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldPhysical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldSpectral","page":"Fields","text":"AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R}\n\nAbstract supertype for fields represented by Fourier coefficients in spectral space.\n\nHere T<:AbstractFloat is the real scalar type associated with the field and R is the tensor order stored as an integer value parameter. Concrete subtypes provide the actual storage and metadata. The generic field interface obtains T and R through field_scalar_type and field_tensor_order, and obtains the stored data through field_data.\n\nnote: A field in spectral representation owns its storage\nA concrete subtype's constructor rejects borrowed storage — a SubArray anywhere in the parent chain — so slicing a larger preallocated buffer and wrapping the view will throw. Ownership is what makes an aliased in-place call safe (FieldAlgebra). Allocate with similar_field, or copy the slice. Any owning array is accepted: Array, CuArray, ROCArray, or a reshape of one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldSpectral"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.ScalarFieldPhysical","page":"Fields","text":"ScalarFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, 3},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalarFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, 3},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalarFieldPhysical(\n  f,\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout();\n  array_type = Array,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalarFieldPhysical(\n  family::Type{<:AbstractArray},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout(),\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalar field in physical representation, stored as its values on a three-dimensional physical grid.\n\nThe function constructor constructs a scalar field in physical representation by sampling a real-valued function on a three-dimensional physical grid.\n\nArguments\n\nf — the sampled function, called as f(x, y, z) at each grid point.\ngrid — the Grid3D the values are sampled on.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field.\narray_type — the array type the sampled values are stored in (Array on the host, a device array such as CuArray or ROCArray to build the field on a GPU).\n\nThe allocating constructor takes a storage family — Array, CuArray, ROCArray — in place of the array, and produces uninitialized value storage itself, on that family's backend, shaped by the grid and the tensor order. It exists for a caller holding only the domain context and no array or field to copy from; with a field in hand, similar_field is the shorter spelling. The element type is the grid's: a bare family takes it from there, a parameterized one must agree with it (a mixed pair is refused, never converted), any other parameter, a CUDA memory kind included, is ignored, and a complex parameter is always refused — complex storage is representation detail, not a user-facing element type.\n\nBroadcasting over scalar fields in physical representation is lifted to values_at_grid. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.values_at_grid, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new ScalarFieldPhysical.\n\nThe two-argument constructors use the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\nFields\n\nvalues_at_grid — the real scalar values, one per grid point.\ngrid — the Grid3D on which the values are sampled.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.ScalarFieldPhysical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.ScalarFieldSpectral","page":"Fields","text":"ScalarFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 3},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalarFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 3},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\n\nScalarFieldSpectral(\n  family::Type{<:AbstractArray},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout(),\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nScalar field in spectral representation, stored as its nonredundant real-to-complex Fourier coefficients.\n\nThe coefficient array has shape field_spectral_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(0)). The dimension fourier_layout.fft_dims[1] is the real-FFT half dimension.\n\nThe allocating constructor takes a storage family — Array, CuArray, ROCArray — in place of the array, and produces uninitialized coefficient storage (element type Complex{T}) itself, on that family's backend, shaped by the grid, the layout and the tensor order. It exists for a caller holding only the domain context and no array or field to copy from; with a field in hand, similar_field is the shorter spelling. The element type is the grid's: a bare family takes it from there, a parameterized one must agree with it (a mixed pair is refused, never converted), any other parameter, a CUDA memory kind included, is ignored, and a complex parameter is always refused — complex storage is representation detail, not a user-facing element type.\n\nBroadcasting over scalar fields in spectral representation is lifted to fourier_coefficients. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.fourier_coefficients, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new ScalarFieldSpectral. All such operations are coefficientwise; for example, .* does not represent a pseudospectral product of scalar fields.\n\nThe two-argument constructor uses the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\nArguments\n\nfourier_coefficients — the complex coefficients in real-FFT storage, of shape field_spectral_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(0)); the field keeps this array rather than copying it, and a view is refused, a field owning its storage.\nfamily — the allocating form only: the storage family (Array, CuArray, ROCArray) given in place of the array.\ngrid — the Grid3D of the physical domain the coefficients represent.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout; omitted, it is RealFourierLayout().\n\nFields\n\nfourier_coefficients — the complex Fourier coefficients, in real-FFT storage.\ngrid — the Grid3D of the physical domain the coefficients represent.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout giving the transformed dimensions and the real-FFT half dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.ScalarFieldSpectral"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.VectorFieldPhysical","page":"Fields","text":"VectorFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, 4},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVectorFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, 4},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVectorFieldPhysical(\n  f,\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout();\n  array_type = Array,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\n\nVectorFieldPhysical(\n  family::Type{<:AbstractArray},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout(),\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVector field in physical representation, stored as its components on a three-dimensional physical grid.\n\nThe function constructor constructs a vector field in physical representation by sampling a real vector-valued function on a three-dimensional physical grid.\n\nArguments\n\nf — the sampled function, called as f(x, y, z); it must return a three-component indexable object.\ngrid — the Grid3D the values are sampled on.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field.\narray_type — the array type the sampled values are stored in (Array on the host, a device array such as CuArray or ROCArray to build the field on a GPU).\n\nThe allocating constructor takes a storage family — Array, CuArray, ROCArray — in place of the array, and produces uninitialized value storage itself, on that family's backend, shaped by the grid and the tensor order. It exists for a caller holding only the domain context and no array or field to copy from; with a field in hand, similar_field is the shorter spelling. The element type is the grid's: a bare family takes it from there, a parameterized one must agree with it (a mixed pair is refused, never converted), any other parameter, a CUDA memory kind included, is ignored, and a complex parameter is always refused — complex storage is representation detail, not a user-facing element type.\n\nBroadcasting over vector fields in physical representation is lifted to values_at_grid. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.values_at_grid, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new VectorFieldPhysical. These operations are storage-wise and componentwise.\n\nThe two-argument constructors use the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\nFields\n\nvalues_at_grid — the real vector components, stored values_at_grid[i, j, k, α] with alpha = 1 2 3.\ngrid — the Grid3D on which the values are sampled.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.VectorFieldPhysical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.VectorFieldSpectral","page":"Fields","text":"VectorFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVectorFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, 4},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\n\nVectorFieldSpectral(\n  family::Type{<:AbstractArray},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout(),\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nVector field in spectral representation, stored as its nonredundant real-to-complex Fourier coefficients.\n\nThe allocating constructor takes a storage family — Array, CuArray, ROCArray — in place of the array, and produces uninitialized coefficient storage (element type Complex{T}) itself, on that family's backend, shaped by the grid, the layout and the tensor order. It exists for a caller holding only the domain context and no array or field to copy from; with a field in hand, similar_field is the shorter spelling. The element type is the grid's: a bare family takes it from there, a parameterized one must agree with it (a mixed pair is refused, never converted), any other parameter, a CUDA memory kind included, is ignored, and a complex parameter is always refused — complex storage is representation detail, not a user-facing element type.\n\nBroadcasting over vector fields in spectral representation is lifted to fourier_coefficients. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.fourier_coefficients, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new VectorFieldSpectral. These operations are storage-wise and componentwise. They do not represent pseudospectral products or tensor contractions.\n\nThe two-argument constructor uses the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\nArguments\n\nfourier_coefficients — the complex coefficients in real-FFT storage, of shape field_spectral_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(1)); the field keeps this array rather than copying it, and a view is refused, a field owning its storage.\nfamily — the allocating form only: the storage family (Array, CuArray, ROCArray) given in place of the array.\ngrid — the Grid3D of the physical domain the coefficients represent.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout; omitted, it is RealFourierLayout().\n\nFields\n\nfourier_coefficients — the complex vector Fourier coefficients in real-FFT storage, component index last, fourier_coefficients[i, j, k, α] with alpha = 1 2 3; its spatial part has shape field_spectral_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(0)) and the whole array field_spectral_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(1)).\ngrid — the Grid3D of the physical domain the coefficients represent.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout; its fft_dims[1] is the real-FFT half dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.VectorFieldSpectral"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.TensorFieldPhysical","page":"Fields","text":"TensorFieldPhysical{R}(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensorFieldPhysical{R}(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensorFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, N},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, N}\n\nTensorFieldPhysical(\n  values_at_grid::AbstractArray{T, N},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, N}\n\nTensorFieldPhysical{R}(\n  f,\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout();\n  array_type = Array,\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\n\nTensorFieldPhysical{R}(\n  family::Type{<:AbstractArray},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout(),\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensor field of order R ge 2 in physical representation, stored as its components on a three-dimensional physical grid.\n\nThe array has shape field_physical_shape(grid, Val(R)). The constructors without explicit {R} infer R as the array's dimension count minus three.\n\nThe function constructor constructs a tensor field of order R ge 2 in physical representation by sampling a real tensor-valued function on a three-dimensional physical grid.\n\nArguments\n\nf — the sampled function, called as f(x, y, z); it must return an indexable object with tensor-fiber shape fiber_shape(Val(R)).\ngrid — the Grid3D the values are sampled on.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field.\narray_type — the array type the sampled values are stored in (Array on the host, a device array such as CuArray or ROCArray to build the field on a GPU).\n\nThe allocating constructor takes a storage family — Array, CuArray, ROCArray — in place of the array, and produces uninitialized value storage itself, on that family's backend, shaped by the grid and the tensor order. It exists for a caller holding only the domain context and no array or field to copy from; with a field in hand, similar_field is the shorter spelling. The element type is the grid's: a bare family takes it from there, a parameterized one must agree with it (a mixed pair is refused, never converted), any other parameter, a CUDA memory kind included, is ignored, and a complex parameter is always refused — complex storage is representation detail, not a user-facing element type.\n\nBroadcasting over tensor fields in physical representation is lifted to values_at_grid. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.values_at_grid, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new TensorFieldPhysical. These operations are storage-wise and componentwise. They do not represent tensor contractions.\n\nThe two-argument constructors use the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\nFields\n\nvalues_at_grid — the real tensor components, stored values_at_grid[i, j, k, αs...] with each tensor index of length 3.\ngrid — the Grid3D on which the values are sampled.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout used when transforming the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.TensorFieldPhysical"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.TensorFieldSpectral","page":"Fields","text":"TensorFieldSpectral{R}(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensorFieldSpectral{R}(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {R, T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensorFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, N},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, N}\n\nTensorFieldSpectral(\n  fourier_coefficients::AbstractArray{Complex{T}, N},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, N}\n\n\nTensorFieldSpectral{R}(\n  family::Type{<:AbstractArray},\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout = RealFourierLayout(),\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nTensor field of order R ge 2 in spectral representation, stored as its nonredundant real-to-complex Fourier coefficients.\n\nThe allocating constructor takes a storage family — Array, CuArray, ROCArray — in place of the array, and produces uninitialized coefficient storage (element type Complex{T}) itself, on that family's backend, shaped by the grid, the layout and the tensor order. It exists for a caller holding only the domain context and no array or field to copy from; with a field in hand, similar_field is the shorter spelling. The element type is the grid's: a bare family takes it from there, a parameterized one must agree with it (a mixed pair is refused, never converted), any other parameter, a CUDA memory kind included, is ignored, and a complex parameter is always refused — complex storage is representation detail, not a user-facing element type.\n\nBroadcasting over tensor fields in spectral representation is lifted to fourier_coefficients. Thus dotted assignments such as @. destination = a - b + c write into destination.fourier_coefficients, while field-valued out-of-place dotted expressions return a new TensorFieldSpectral. These operations are storage-wise and componentwise. They do not represent pseudospectral products or tensor contractions.\n\nThe two-argument constructors use the default RealFourierLayout() for the layout.\n\nArguments\n\nfourier_coefficients — the complex coefficients in real-FFT storage, of shape field_spectral_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(R)); the field keeps this array rather than copying it, and a view is refused, a field owning its storage.\nfamily — the allocating form only: the storage family (Array, CuArray, ROCArray) given in place of the array.\ngrid — the Grid3D of the physical domain the coefficients represent.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout; omitted, it is RealFourierLayout().\n\nFields\n\nfourier_coefficients — the complex tensor Fourier coefficients in real-FFT storage, tensor indices last, fourier_coefficients[i, j, k, αs...] with each of length 3; its spatial part has shape field_spectral_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(0)) and the whole array field_spectral_shape(grid, fourier_layout, Val(R)).\ngrid — the Grid3D of the physical domain the coefficients represent.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout; its fft_dims[1] is the real-FFT half dimension. The constructors without an explicit {R} infer the tensor order R = N - 3 from the stored array's dimension count N.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.TensorFieldSpectral"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldTransformPlans","page":"Fields","text":"FieldTransformPlans(\n  physical::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n  spectral::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R};\n  fft_flags = nothing,\n  fft_timelimit = Inf,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nForward and inverse FFT plans connecting compatible fields represented in physical and spectral space.\n\nArguments\n\nphysical — the field supplying the real-space storage array to plan against.\nspectral — the field supplying the real-to-complex Fourier coefficient array.\nfft_flags — an optional backend-specific FFT planner flag.\nfft_timelimit — the planner time limit, used when fft_flags is given.\n\nOnly the spatial dimensions field_layout(physical).fft_dims are transformed; tensor-fiber dimensions are left untransformed.\n\nThe plans are backend-, array-type- and scalar-type-specific, and carry no tensor order: a real Fourier transform on a periodic box is defined on the box, and a rank-R field is that transform applied along a fiber, so one pair of plans serves scalar, vector and tensor fields alike. Construct a separate FieldTransformPlans object for each CPU/GPU backend and element type, but not for each tensor order.\n\nwarning: The default `ESTIMATE` planner can badly mis-plan single-precision CPU transforms\nOn the CPU the plans are FFTW plans, and with fft_flags unset FFTW picks the algorithm by its built-in ESTIMATE heuristic — instant, but a guess. On some CPUs that guess is far off: measured on a Zen 5 desktop, the Float32 transforms ran 2.3–5.1× slower than a timed plan at 256^3 (the widest gap single-threaded, narrowing as threads are added), and at 512^3 still slower than Float64 serially and at two threads, the gap closing (and reversing) only as more threads are added. For a long CPU run, pass fft_flags = FFTW.MEASURE (or FFTW.PATIENT): the one-time timed plan search costs seconds and removes the guess. MEASURE overwrites both reference arrays while planning, so construct the plans on scratch or zero-filled fields before seeding data. The setting is CPU-specific — the GPU FFT libraries take no planner flag and are unaffected — and the default stays ESTIMATE because it is instant, deterministic, and right for tests and short sessions.\n\nFields\n\ngrid::G — the Grid3D for which the plans were constructed.\nfourier_layout::L — the RealFourierLayout for which the plans were constructed.\nplan_forward_fft::PF — the plan transforming physical values to spectral coefficients, for one component.\nplan_inverse_fft::PI — the plan transforming spectral coefficients to physical values, for one component.\nfft_flags::Union{Nothing, UInt32} — the planner flag these plans were built with, or nothing for the default. It is retained rather than merely used, so that rebuilding these plans in another storage family can replay the intent they were created with — see adapt.\nfft_timelimit::Float64 — the planner time limit paired with fft_flags, retained for the same reason.\ncomponent_scratch::S — a component-sized buffer, or nothing. It exists only where a device backend would refuse a component's starting address, and is nothing for every grid whose component offsets align — which includes every grid a solver is likely to run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldTransformPlans"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralMultipliers","page":"Fields","text":"FieldSpectralMultipliers(\n  grid::Grid3D{T},\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n  wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3,\n  squared_wavenumber_1, squared_wavenumber_2, squared_wavenumber_3,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nFieldSpectralMultipliers(\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral{T},\n  wavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3,\n  squared_wavenumber_1, squared_wavenumber_2, squared_wavenumber_3,\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nFieldSpectralMultipliers(field::AbstractFieldSpectral{T}) where {T<:AbstractFloat}\n\nOne-dimensional Fourier wavenumber arrays for the operators applied coefficientwise in spectral representation — derivatives, the Laplacian, and the Helmholtz projection.\n\nArguments\n\ngrid, fourier_layout — the Grid3D and RealFourierLayout the multipliers are built for. The two-argument forms take them from field instead.\nwavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3 — the per-axis derivative wavenumbers, written a where the operators' formulas need to name them apart from the true wavenumber k. They are the angular wavenumbers of first derivatives, through multiplication by mathrmi a, and are zeroed at an even axis's Nyquist frequency.\nsquared_wavenumber_1, squared_wavenumber_2, squared_wavenumber_3 — the per-axis squared wavenumbers, used for the Laplacian through multiplication by -k^2. These keep the true k^2 at the Nyquist frequency, which is why the two families are stored rather than derived from each other.\nfield — in the one-argument convenience form, the field whose grid and layout the multipliers are built from; both families are then derived with the correct Nyquist treatment and allocated on that field's storage backend.\n\nEach array runs along one spatial axis in the FFT frequency ordering of fourier_layout; the half axis fourier_layout.fft_dims[1] stores only the nonnegative frequencies. The arrays must live on storage (CPU or GPU) matching the fields the multipliers are applied to.\n\nThe two array families are stored independently rather than squaring one to obtain the other, because on even grids they use different conventions for the Nyquist mode (the highest representable frequency). The two explicit constructors take both families from the caller and check only their lengths; the one-argument constructor derives them, which is what makes them canonical (see the Canonical parameter below).\n\nThe wavenumbers it derives are the physical angular wavenumbers k = 2pi n  L along each axis, with L the domain length and n running over the FFT frequency indices. On an even-length axis the highest stored mode is the Nyquist mode: its first-derivative wavenumber is set to zero, so that derivatives of real fields stay real, while its squared wavenumber keeps the true k^2, so that the Laplacian and Poisson-type solves stay correct. On an odd-length axis there is no exact Nyquist mode and no special treatment is applied.\n\nWhether the arrays were derived from the grid or supplied by the caller is recorded in the type, as a final Canonical parameter: the convenience constructor marks them canonical, the explicit ones do not. It is in the type rather than in a field because it decides, statically, whether two multiplier sets provably hold the same coordinates — two canonical sets on a matching grid and layout are derived the same way from the same inputs, so they agree by construction. That is what lets convolve collapse two radial kernels into one without testing anything at run time, and it is why a caller supplying its own coordinates (a sheared, time-dependent set, say) never has its kernels merged with one built on the grid's own wavenumbers.\n\nFields\n\ngrid, fourier_layout — the grid and Fourier layout above, stored as passed.\nwavenumber_1, wavenumber_2, wavenumber_3 — the derivative wavenumbers above.\nsquared_wavenumber_1, squared_wavenumber_2, squared_wavenumber_3 — the squared ones.\nwavenumber_view_1, wavenumber_view_2, wavenumber_view_3 — derived rather than passed: each is one of the three vectors above pre-oriented as a 3-D view along its own axis, holding no data of its own, so that a broadcast-route operator never rebuilds a reshape per call. Internal; reach them through _wavenumber_broadcast_view, never directly.\nsquared_wavenumber_view_1, squared_wavenumber_view_2, squared_wavenumber_view_3 — the same for the squared family, reached through _squared_wavenumber_broadcast.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralMultipliers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_physical!","page":"Fields","text":"to_physical!(\n  physical::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n  spectral::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n  transform_plans::FieldTransformPlans{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nto_physical!(\n  physical::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n  spectral::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n  transform_plans::FieldTransformPlans{T},\n  spectral_scratch::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nTransform a field from spectral space to physical space in place, returning the modified physical destination.\n\nThe three-argument form consumes its source; the four-argument form preserves it, at the price of one extra pass — the coefficients are copied into spectral_scratch and the transform runs from there, so spectral returns bitwise-unchanged with nothing allocated. It is the allocation-free member of the preserving contract that the allocating to_physical offers by copying into a fresh field: reach for it when the spectral coefficients are a state you keep — a solver's resolved state, a field transformed to physical space more than once — and the loop must not allocate.\n\nArguments\n\nphysical — the field that receives the transformed values.\nspectral — the source coefficients. The three-argument form may overwrite them as scratch; the four-argument form leaves them bitwise-unchanged.\ntransform_plans — the FieldTransformPlans supplying the inverse FFT plan.\nspectral_scratch — four-argument form only: a spectral field on the same grid and layout whose storage the transform consumes in spectral's place; on return its coefficients are undefined, exactly as the three-argument form leaves its source. It must not alias spectral — the same storage would defeat the preservation the argument exists to buy, so that call is refused rather than defined.\n\nwarning: The source is used as scratch\nThe inverse transform uses its input as scratch space and may overwrite it: on return the coefficients it ran from are left in an undefined state (the FFTW CPU backend destroys them — complex-to-real transforms have no input-preserving algorithm in the multidimensional case; some GPU backends happen to preserve them, but this is not guaranteed). In the three-argument form that input is spectral itself — use the four-argument form, or the allocating to_physical, when spectral must be preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_physical!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_physical","page":"Fields","text":"to_physical(\n  spectral::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n  transform_plans::FieldTransformPlans{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nAllocating form of to_physical!: allocate the result and return it. A field in spectral representation gives its physical representation on the same grid.\n\nUnlike the three-argument in-place form this one preserves spectral — it transforms a copy — which is the reason to reach for it: the inverse real transform may consume its input as scratch, and whether it does is backend-specific, so a caller that still needs the coefficients must not rely on that form. A loop that must not allocate gets the same preservation from the four-argument to_physical! with a caller-supplied scratch field instead. Here spectral is the source and transform_plans provides the inverse FFT plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_physical"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_spectral!","page":"Fields","text":"to_spectral!(\n  spectral::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n  physical::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n  transform_plans::FieldTransformPlans{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nTransform a field from physical space to spectral space in place, returning the modified spectral destination.\n\nArguments\n\nspectral — the field that receives the transformed coefficients.\nphysical — the source values, which the transform may overwrite as scratch.\ntransform_plans — the FieldTransformPlans supplying the forward FFT plan.\n\nThe forward transform is unnormalized and the inverse carries the 1N (N the number of grid points) — the AbstractFFTs convention. Every normalization elsewhere in the package is stated against it: the mean is the zero coefficient over N, adding a constant scales only that coefficient by N, and the Parseval sums carry 1N^2.\n\nwarning: The source may be overwritten\nThe forward transform may overwrite physical on some backends (for example rocFFT reserves the right to use real-to-complex inputs as scratch); the FFTW CPU backend preserves it, but do not rely on the source surviving. Use the allocating to_spectral when physical must be preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_spectral!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_spectral","page":"Fields","text":"to_spectral(\n  physical::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n  transform_plans::FieldTransformPlans{T},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nAllocating form of to_spectral!: allocate the result and return it. A field in physical representation gives its spectral representation on the same grid.\n\nUnlike the in-place form this one preserves physical — it transforms a copy — which is the reason to reach for it: the forward transform may consume its input as scratch on some backends, so a caller that still needs the values must not rely on the in-place form. Here physical is the source and transform_plans provides the forward FFT plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.to_spectral"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_data","page":"Fields","text":"field_data(field::AbstractField)\n\nReturn the array storing the field data.\n\nFor fields represented in physical space, this is the array of values on the grid. For fields represented in spectral space, this is the array of Fourier coefficients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_grid","page":"Fields","text":"field_grid(field::AbstractField)\n\nReturn the Grid3D associated with a field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_layout","page":"Fields","text":"field_layout(field::AbstractField)\n\nReturn the RealFourierLayout associated with a field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_layout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_scalar_type","page":"Fields","text":"field_scalar_type(\n  field::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nfield_scalar_type(\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nReturn the real scalar type T associated with a field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_scalar_type"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_tensor_order","page":"Fields","text":"field_tensor_order(\n  field::AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nfield_tensor_order(\n  field::AbstractFieldSpectral{T, R},\n) where {T<:AbstractFloat, R}\n\nReturn the tensor order R of a field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_tensor_order"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_physical_shape","page":"Fields","text":"field_physical_shape(grid::Grid3D, ::Val{R}) where {R}\n\nReturn the storage shape of a field of tensor order R represented in physical space on grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_physical_shape"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_spectral_shape","page":"Fields","text":"field_spectral_shape(\n  grid::Grid3D,\n  fourier_layout::RealFourierLayout,\n  ::Val{R},\n) where {R}\n\nReturn the storage shape of a field represented in nonredundant real-to-complex spectral space.\n\nArguments\n\ngrid — the Grid3D whose point count sets the spatial dimensions.\nfourier_layout — the RealFourierLayout deciding which axis carries the halved real transform.\nR — the tensor order, whose fiber dimensions are appended after the spatial ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.field_spectral_shape"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.similar_field","page":"Fields","text":"similar_field(reference::AbstractField, data)\n\nsimilar_field(reference::AbstractField, ::Val{R})\n\nConstruct a field with metadata copied from reference.\n\nWith storage array data, the field has the same representation and tensor order as reference. With Val{R}, new storage is allocated with the same representation and tensor order R.\n\nThis is used by out-of-place field broadcasting and by allocating algebraic operations. With no reference field in hand — only a grid, a layout and a backend — use the allocating type constructors instead, which take a storage family in place of the array (ScalarFieldPhysical and its five siblings).\n\nnote: Deliberately not an extension of `Base.similar`\nBase.similar's second positional argument means an element type or a set of dimensions, and here it is the tensor order — so the name would be borrowed while the convention that gives it meaning was broken. Worse, its most idiomatic spelling would mislead: similar(u, Float32) reads as a request for single precision, and this package never changes precision implicitly (a mixed pair is refused rather than promoted, and even the explicit adapt moves storage only). The data form is not an allocation at all — it wraps storage you supply, which Base.similar never does — so half of this operation could not carry the name in any case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.similar_field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#Adapt.adapt","page":"Fields","text":"adapt(storage, object)\n\nMove a section — a field or point-values — or its multipliers, its kernels, its point set or its transform plans to another storage family, host to device or device to host, returning an object of the same kind whose arrays live in the requested family. The element type is preserved exactly.\n\nA storage family is the kind of array holding the values — Array on the host, or one vendor's device array. Use this when something built on one family has to be used on another. Two fields combine arithmetically only when they share a family and an element type, and a mixed pair is refused rather than promoted or transferred, because a copy across the bus is a cost that belongs at the call site; adapt is that call site. The argument storage is the target array type (Array, CuArray, ROCArray, …) or any other Adapt.jl adaptor, and object is what to move — a field or point-values (the sections), or one of the companions a section is useless without: multipliers, a kernel, or transform plans.\n\nThe whole surface a solver needs is covered: the six field types, the point-value types, a PointSet, a FieldSpectralMultipliers, the four spectral-kernel classes (StoredKernel, SeparableKernel, RadialKernel and ProductKernel, all subtypes of SpectralKernel), and FieldTransformPlans. Everything the moved object carries besides its storage — the grid, the Fourier layout, the origin, the tensor order — is preserved by construction, since it is rebuilt through the same allocator the package uses everywhere else.\n\nElement-type conversion is deliberately not part of this: a transfer that silently changed precision would change what a solver computes. No operation in this package converts precision today.\n\nExamples\n\nu_device      = adapt(CuArray, u)                 # host  -> device\nu_host        = adapt(Array, u_device)            # device -> host\nmult_device   = adapt(CuArray, multipliers)       # must share the field's family\nplans_device  = adapt(CuArray, plans)             # rebuilt on the device\n\nwarning: Moving to the family an object is already in returns it unchanged\nFollowing Adapt.jl and convert, adapting to the family an object already belongs to is the identity: it returns the very same object, sharing storage, rather than a copy. Write copy when a distinct object is what is wanted.\n\nwarning: Transform plans are rebuilt, not moved, and that costs a planning pass\nAn FFT plan is a compiled schedule tied to one storage family and cannot be transferred, so adapting a FieldTransformPlans constructs new plans on the target. It needs nothing from the caller: the plans carry their own grid, layout and element type — and no tensor order — so the two arrays the planner needs as witnesses are allocated internally and released immediately. The planner request the plans were built with is replayed, with one exception. Planner flags are an FFTW concept — cuFFT rejects them outright — so a move to any non-host family does not apply them when building the new plan, but the returned plans still record the original flags, so a later move back to the host rebuilds them with the intent they were created with.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Adapt.adapt"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.adapt!","page":"Fields","text":"adapt!(destination, source)\n\nMove source into destination, which already holds storage in the family you want, and return destination.\n\nUse this in-place form when destination is already allocated; the allocating adapt returns a fresh result instead. There is no target argument here because there is nothing to name: the destination already is the target family, so the two cannot disagree.\n\nBoth arguments are sections of the same kind — two fields in the same representation, two point-value sections, or two point sets — agreeing in element type, tensor order, grid and Fourier layout, and differing at most in the storage family they live in. Everything except the family must match, because this moves storage and nothing else: precision is not converted here, exactly as it is not by adapt. A mismatch is refused with an ArgumentError naming the property that differs and both values, so a caller need not check the pair itself. A pair that differs in representation has no method rather than an error message, since a physical field and a spectral one are not the same section. Passing one section as both arguments is a no-op, and a source already in destination's family is an ordinary copy rather than an error, so generic code need not ask which side of the bus it is on.\n\nThe reason to reach for it is the return trip. Allocating the destination is most of what a device-to-host move costs — measured on an RTX 5070 Ti at 256^3, Float64, an allocating move runs at 51 GiB/s against the 190 GiB/s the same transfer reaches into a destination that already exists.\n\nOnly sections take this form. The adapt verb also moves a FieldSpectralMultipliers, a spectral kernel and a FieldTransformPlans, and those have no in-place spelling on purpose: they are built rather than filled — moving plans replans the transforms, and a radial kernel carries a closure — so there is no preallocated storage for a destination to offer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.adapt!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.embed_modes!","page":"Fields","text":"embed_modes!(destination::AbstractFieldSpectral, source::AbstractFieldSpectral)\nembed_modes!(destination::AbstractFieldSpectral, source::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n             α::Real, β::Real)\n\nWrite a coarser field's Fourier modes into a finer one over the same box, adding if β is nonzero, leave its other modes untouched, and return destination.\n\nTwo grids of different sizes over one box resolve the same physical wavenumbers where they overlap, so a field on the coarser grid names a subset of the finer grid's modes. This writes exactly those, which is how content computed cheaply on a small grid — a forcing, a prescribed initial condition — reaches a large one without a transform. The cost is the coarse field's size. Use extract_modes! for the other direction.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the finer field, written on the shared modes only. Passing the coarser field here is refused, which is what keeps the two directions from being confused at a call site.\nsource — the coarser field. It must match destination in scalar type, tensor order, storage family, domain size, origin and Fourier layout, and its grid must be no larger on any axis and smaller on at least one; every one of those is refused rather than converted.\nα — scales the source, 1 by default.\nβ — scales the destination on the shared modes before the source is added, 0 by default, which overwrites them. Modes outside the shared set are never read or written, whatever β is.\n\nAmplitudes are physical, not stored coefficients: the operation carries the ratio of the two grids' point counts, so the modes written describe the same waves they described on the coarse grid. Reading them back with extract_modes! returns what was written, and the two verbs are exact adjoints of each other under inner.\n\nwarning: The coarse grid's Nyquist modes are not transferred\nA mode at the coarse grid's Nyquist frequency on an even axis describes one sampled wave that the finer grid splits into two distinct modes, so the single coarse coefficient does not determine them. Those modes are left out of the transfer, and a caller that needs them must resolve the ambiguity itself. Content strictly below the coarse Nyquist frequency — which de-aliasing already guarantees in a pseudospectral solver — is unaffected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.embed_modes!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.extract_modes!","page":"Fields","text":"extract_modes!(destination::AbstractFieldSpectral, source::AbstractFieldSpectral)\nextract_modes!(destination::AbstractFieldSpectral, source::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n               α::Real, β::Real)\n\nRead a finer field's Fourier modes into a coarser field over the same box, leaving every other mode of destination untouched, and return destination.\n\nThis is the reverse of embed_modes!: it keeps the modes the coarse grid can hold and discards the rest, which is how the large scales of a field reach a small grid that something else computes on. The cost is the coarse field's size, so the fine field is never traversed.\n\nArguments\n\ndestination — the coarser field, written on the shared modes only. Passing the finer field here is refused.\nsource — the finer field, matched to destination exactly as embed_modes! requires.\nα — scales the source, 1 by default.\nβ — scales the destination on the shared modes before the source is added, 0 by default.\n\nAmplitudes are physical, as for embed_modes!. The result is the coarse field's own modes, not a band-limited copy of the fine field: to zero what the coarse grid cannot hold, build the destination with the modes already zero, or apply spectral_cutoff to the fine field first.\n\nwarning: The coarse grid's Nyquist modes are not transferred\nThe destination's Nyquist modes on an even axis are left exactly as they were, for the reason embed_modes! gives: the fine grid stores two modes there where the coarse grid stores one, and which of them the coarse coefficient should carry is not determined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.extract_modes!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.reality_residual","page":"Fields","text":"reality_residual(field::AbstractFieldSpectral) -> Real\n\nMeasure how far a field's coefficients are from being the spectrum of a real field, returning 0 exactly when they are one.\n\nA real field's spectrum satisfies hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k), and the real-to-complex layout exploits that by storing only half the modes — so for most of them the redundant partner is not stored and cannot disagree. Two planes are the exception: the folded axis's zero plane, and its Nyquist plane when that axis has even length, are each their own mirror image, so within them both partners are stored and the constraint becomes a relation between entries that a caller can break. This returns the largest such discrepancy,\n\nmax bigllvert hat f(k) - overlinehat f(-k) bigrrvert\n\nover those planes and every fiber component. A mode that is its own partner — the corners where each axis sits at zero or, on an even-length axis, at Nyquist; eight of them when all three axes are even, fewer when an axis is odd and so has no Nyquist plane — must be real, and this reports twice its imaginary part, that being the same discrepancy applied to a self-paired entry.\n\nThe residual is in the units of the stored coefficients, which the forward transform leaves unnormalized: a Fourier mode of physical amplitude a is stored with magnitude about a M, M the number of grid points, not a. So judge it against the field's largest stored coefficient, not against a physical amplitude.\n\nReach for it where responsibility for the coefficients changes hands: after writing or accepting them — and after any step that scales the coefficients in place, because a spectrum built by transforming a real field satisfies the constraint only to round-off (FFTW is exact at some power-of-two sizes and ulp-level elsewhere; rocFFT and cuFFT are ulp-level at every size), and a forcing proportional to the field amplifies that residual where viscosity does not damp it. The projection reality_projection! removes it exactly. The cost is one or two planes, O(N^2), per-axis count squared, not the O(N^3 log N) of the round trip below.\n\nIt reports rather than repairs, so the caller decides; the repair is reality_projection!, an explicit verb costing the same one or two planes. A physical round trip — to_physical followed by to_spectral — also projects, since the inverse real transform discards what no real field has, but it costs two transforms, perturbs every mode by round-off, and resets this residual only to the transform's own floor, the forward pass reseeding it.\n\nnote: The Nyquist plane is reported, never zeroed\nSome codes symmetrize by zeroing the Nyquist planes outright. This does not: the package keeps Nyquist content and treats the two wavenumber families as disagreeing there on purpose, so a violation on that plane is named like any other and what to do about it stays the caller's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.reality_residual"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.reality_projection!","page":"Fields","text":"reality_projection!(destination::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n                    source::AbstractFieldSpectral) -> destination\nreality_projection!(field::AbstractFieldSpectral) -> field\n\nProject a field's coefficients onto the spectrum of a real field, so that reality_residual of the result is exactly zero — the reality-condition twin of helmholtz_projection!.\n\nIn physical space nothing visible changes: the inverse real transform already discards the part of the coefficients that no real field has, so the image of field is unchanged. What changes is the stored spectrum. The real-to-complex layout stores both members of a conjugate pair only on the folded axis's zero plane and, for an even-length axis, its Nyquist plane; there the condition hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k) is a relation a caller — or a library's round-off — can break, and this replaces each stored pair by its projection,\n\nbeginaligned\nhat f(k) hat f(-k) leftarrow tfrac12 bigl( hat f(k) + overlinehat f(-k) bigr) \noverlinetfrac12 bigl( hat f(k) + overlinehat f(-k) bigr)\nendaligned\n\nwith a self-paired corner mode reduced to its real part by the same formula. Every other coefficient is untouched. This is the orthogonal projector onto real-field spectra in the Parseval inner product: linear, exactly idempotent, and it commutes with every per-mode multiplier m satisfying m(-k) = overlinem(k) — the differential operators, the exponential and radial kernels, the translation phases.\n\nReach for it where round-off on those planes is amplified, not damped. A spectrum freshly transformed from real data satisfies the condition only to round-off, and a solver whose forcing is proportional to the field grows that round-off exponentially; projecting once per step where the state is deposited removes it exactly, which a physical round trip does not, since the forward transform reseeds it. The physical representation has no method: there the projection is the identity.\n\nThe destination may be the source, and the one-argument form projects in place; the cost is then one or two planes, O(N^2), per-axis count squared. With a distinct destination the whole source is copied first, so that form costs a field copy, O(N^3), before the projection — the same shape as the two-argument helmholtz_projection!, kept so the twins read alike, but not the cheap one. The two fields must share a grid, layout, tensor order, element type and storage family, and a mismatch is refused rather than converted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.reality_projection!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.mean_free_projection!","page":"Fields","text":"mean_free_projection!(field::AbstractFieldSpectral) -> field\n\nRemove a field's mean exactly, in place: the orthogonal projector onto fields of zero mean, the companion of reality_projection! and of helmholtz_projection!.\n\nIn physical space it subtracts the average over the domain Omega from every component,\n\nf(x) leftarrow f(x) - frac1lvertOmegarvert int_Omega f(y)  mathrmdy\n\nand in spectral representation that is setting the zero Fourier coefficient hat f(0) of every fiber component to zero, both its real and imaginary parts, leaving every other coefficient untouched. The projector is linear, exactly idempotent, and orthogonal in the L^2 inner product, and the result's mean is exactly zero rather than zero to round-off.\n\nThe cost is writing one coefficient per fiber component, O(1) in the grid size, with no copy of the field and no value read back to the host. Subtracting the mean with subtract!(field, field, mean(field)) also touches only that coefficient, but it leaves round-off (the mean is scaled by the point count and back), reads the value back to the host, and clears only the real part, since the mean of a real field is real.\n\nThe physical representation has no method. There the mean occupies no single stored value, so the projection is a pass over the whole field, which subtract!(field, field, mean(field)) already is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.mean_free_projection!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#Base.:(==)-Tuple{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldPhysical}","page":"Fields","text":"==(a, b)\nisequal(a, b)\n\nCompare two fields, or two point-value sections, or two point sets, by what they hold: a == b is true when both are the same kind of section on the same base and every stored value is equal.\n\nFor fields, \"the same kind\" means the same representation and tensor order, and \"the same base\" the same Grid3D and RealFourierLayout; two fields differing in any of those are different fields and compare false. Point-value sections of different tensor orders or point counts, and point sets of different point counts, compare false for the same reason.\n\nSome pairs are refused with an ArgumentError rather than answered, because either answer could mislead. A field in physical representation compared with one in spectral representation may be two representations of one field, so false would be wrong and true would need a transform; transform one of them first. Sections of different element types or different storage families (host against device) are refused as every operation over two sections refuses them, rather than converted.\n\nThe values compare as Julia's arrays compare. Under ==, 0.0 equals -0.0 and NaN equals nothing, itself included. Under isequal, -0.0 differs from 0.0 and NaN equals NaN, which makes it the test for a bit-identical result. The hash of a section is consistent with isequal, and is built from the section's kind and base only, not its values, so it reads no stored value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:(==)"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.SpectralKernel","page":"Fields","text":"SpectralKernel{T}\n\nSupertype of every spectral kernel: a coefficientwise multiplier applied as the product hat f hat g of a field's coefficients hat f and the kernel's own multiplier hat g, in whichever storage its symbol admits. Convolution defines the concrete classes under Kernel storage classes — StoredKernel holding one coefficient per mode, SeparableKernel holding per-axis factors, RadialKernel holding the symbol itself, and ProductKernel holding two factors unmultiplied — and they differ only in cost, never in meaning. This supertype and the two generics below live here so that every module which applies a kernel — Convolution, DifferentialOperators, Reductions — can name one type, whichever of them is included first.\n\nA field in spectral representation is not a kernel. The two hold identical data, so nothing but a type can say which role is meant, and the distinction is load-bearing: the coefficientwise reciprocal of a kernel is the kernel that deconvolves by it, while the same operation on a field is one the package does not define. Wrap a field of coefficients in StoredKernel to declare the role, which is what materialize_kernel returns.\n\nA concrete kernel implements two methods: _kernel_data, returning the multiplier it contributes per mode, and _check_kernel_compatibility, checking it against the field it is applied to.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.SpectralKernel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.set_fft_threads!","page":"Fields","text":"set_fft_threads!(n)\n\nSet the number of threads FFTW uses and return n, effective for plans built afterwards (FFTW fixes the count at plan construction). Code that parallelizes across many fields at once should build plans under set_fft_threads!(1) to avoid FFTW×Julia thread oversubscription (more active threads than cores, contending instead of helping).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.set_fft_threads!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.write_field","page":"Fields","text":"write_field(parent::HDF5.H5DataStore, name::AbstractString, field::AbstractField)\n\nPersist field — any of the six field types, scalar / vector / tensor in either representation — as a new HDF5 group name under parent, returning the group it created. Use it to checkpoint a field for restart, save a full-resolution snapshot for later analysis, or pass a field to another tool; the reload with read_field is exact.\n\nThe group is self-describing: the field's numeric array becomes the dataset data, and its metadata become attributes — a type tag, a schema version, a representation tag (\"physical\" or \"spectral\"), the Grid3D geometry (grid_size, domain_size, origin), the Fourier layout (fft_dims), the element type, and the tensor order — so any HDF5 tool can read it. A physical field stores a real array; a field in spectral representation stores its complex half-spectrum. A device (GPU) array is copied to the host on write. The sugar parent[name] = field is equivalent.\n\nBecause parent is a handle you opened and own, this never opens or closes a file: a caller bundles a whole snapshot (velocity, vorticity, …) into one file under separate names and attaches its own time / iteration attributes — the package serializes the fields, and the caller owns the bundling and any notion of time.\n\nArguments\n\nparent — an open HDF5 File or Group to create the new subgroup under.\nname — the name of the group to create.\nfield — the field to write.\n\nProvided by a package extension, so it is available only when HDF5.jl is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.write_field"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.read_field","page":"Fields","text":"read_field(parent::HDF5.H5DataStore, name::AbstractString)\n\nReconstruct the field written by write_field from the HDF5 group name under parent, dispatching on the stored type attribute to return the reconstructed field in the matching type (ScalarFieldPhysical, VectorFieldSpectral, …). The reload is exact — the array data, the Grid3D geometry, the Fourier layout, the element type, and the tensor order are recovered bit-for-bit.\n\nThe reconstructed field is a host (CPU) object regardless of where the written one lived; move it to a device with adapt or adapt! afterward if needed.\n\nHere parent is an open HDF5 File or Group holding the group, and name is the name (or path) of that field group under parent.\n\nProvided by a package extension, so it is available only when HDF5.jl is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.read_field"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldPhysicalBroadcastStyle","page":"Fields","text":"FieldPhysicalBroadcastStyle{N}\n\nBroadcast style for fields in physical representation (N = 3 + R, with R the tensor order). A field stands for its storage array inside a broadcast, and the style materializes the result back into a physical-space field. See also FieldSpectralBroadcastStyle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldPhysicalBroadcastStyle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralBroadcastStyle","page":"Fields","text":"FieldSpectralBroadcastStyle{N}\n\nBroadcast style for fields in spectral representation (N = 3 + R, with R the tensor order); the spectral counterpart of FieldPhysicalBroadcastStyle. Combining the two styles is an error, so physical and spectral representations never mix in one broadcast.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.FieldSpectralBroadcastStyle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._allocated_field_storage-Union{Tuple{S}, Tuple{Type{<:AbstractArray}, Type{S}, NTuple{N, Int64} where N}} where S","page":"Fields","text":"_allocated_field_storage(family, ::Type{S}, shape)\n\nProduce an uninitialized array of element type S and shape shape in the storage family — the storage-producing step of the allocating (family, grid[, layout]) constructors. The family is reduced to its unparameterized wrapper first, so Array and Array{Float64} allocate identically once _check_family_element_type has vetted the declared element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._allocated_field_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._approx-Tuple{Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_approx(x)\n\nRound a number to four significant digits, for display.\n\nReserved for a RATIO — the NUFFT oversampling is the only current caller — and never for a coordinate. No compatibility check turns on a ratio, so rounding one cannot make two refused objects print the same string; a coordinate is the opposite case, which is why _show_domain prints those at full precision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._approx"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._axis_wavenumber_arrays-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray, Int64, T, Bool}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Fields","text":"_axis_wavenumber_arrays(reference, number_of_points, domain_length, half_dimension)\n\nBuild the per-axis derivative and squared wavenumber vectors with _axis_wavenumber_values on the host, then copy them onto the storage backend of reference (via similar + copyto!, avoiding scalar GPU writes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._axis_wavenumber_arrays"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._axis_wavenumber_values-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Int64, T, Bool}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Fields","text":"_axis_wavenumber_values(number_of_points, domain_length, half_dimension)\n\nReturn (derivative_wavenumber, squared_wavenumber) as host vectors for one spatial axis. The half_dimension flag marks the real-FFT half axis, which stores only the nonnegative frequencies. On even-length axes the derivative wavenumber is zeroed at the Nyquist mode (so derivatives of real fields stay real) while the squared wavenumber keeps the true k^2 (correct Laplacian) — the deliberate Nyquist split, stored as two independent families.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._axis_wavenumber_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_adapt_target-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_adapt_target(destination, source)\n\nCheck that two sections agree in everything a storage move must preserve — scalar type, tensor order, grid and Fourier layout — and deliberately not in storage family, which is the one thing the move exists to change.\n\nThis is _check_same_domain minus its backend clause. Reusing that check would refuse the pair adapt! is for, and dropping it would let a move rewrite a destination built for another grid. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_adapt_target"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_broadcast_field_compatibility-Tuple{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldPhysical}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_broadcast_field_compatibility(destination, argument)\n\nCheck, during field broadcasting, that argument is compatible with destination: same-representation fields must pass _check_field_compatibility; mixing representations throws; non-field arguments (numbers, plain arrays) pass. It recurses into a Broadcasted argument's operands, returning nothing or throwing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_broadcast_field_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_family_element_type-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{<:AbstractArray}, Grid3D{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Fields","text":"_check_family_element_type(family, grid)\n\nRefuse a storage family whose declared element type disagrees with the grid's — a mixed pair is refused, never converted — or is complex, because a field's element type is its real scalar type and complex storage is representation detail the spectral constructors allocate themselves. A bare family (Array, ROCArray) declares nothing and passes, the element type coming from the grid. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_family_element_type"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_compatibility","page":"Fields","text":"_check_field_compatibility(a::AbstractFieldPhysical, b::AbstractFieldPhysical)\n_check_field_compatibility(a::AbstractFieldSpectral, b::AbstractFieldSpectral)\n\nCheck that two fields in the same representation share scalar type, tensor order, grid, Fourier layout, and storage family.\n\nThis is the check for operations that require fully matching operands. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_compatibility"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_domain_compatibility","page":"Fields","text":"_check_field_domain_compatibility(a::AbstractFieldPhysical,\n                                  b::AbstractFieldPhysical)\n_check_field_domain_compatibility(a::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n                                  b::AbstractFieldSpectral)\n\nCheck that two fields in the same representation share scalar type, grid, Fourier layout, and storage family.\n\nTensor order is allowed to differ, so this is the check for representation-preserving operations that change order, such as gradient and divergence. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_domain_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_matches_context-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_field_matches_context(field::AbstractField, context)\n\nCheck that field was built for the same grid and Fourier layout as context.\n\nHere context is a precomputed spectral artifact carrying grid and fourier_layout fields, namely FieldTransformPlans or FieldSpectralMultipliers. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_field_matches_context"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_kernel_compatibility","page":"Fields","text":"_check_kernel_compatibility(field, kernel)\n\nCheck that kernel was built for field's grid and Fourier layout, throwing if not. Each storage class checks whatever it stored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_kernel_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_matching_context-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_matching_context(a, b)\n\nCheck that two participants in a spectral operation were built for the same grid and Fourier layout, throwing otherwise. This is the package's one context predicate: an operation that mixes objects from different domains is rejected at the entry point rather than producing a wrong answer or failing later inside a kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_matching_context"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_multipliers_compatibility-Tuple{AbstractFieldSpectral, FieldSpectralMultipliers}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_multipliers_compatibility(field::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n                                 multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nCheck that a spectral-space field and multipliers share scalar type, grid, Fourier layout, and storage family. Returns nothing or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_multipliers_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_same_domain-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_same_domain(a, b)\n\nThrow unless a and b share scalar type, grid, Fourier layout, and storage family (tensor order may differ). Shared body for the same-representation domain checks; the entry points that call it (_check_field_domain_compatibility, _check_field_compatibility) are each restricted to a single representation, so a physical/spectral pair has no matching method and is rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_same_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_shared_mode_compatibility-Tuple{AbstractFieldSpectral, AbstractFieldSpectral}","page":"Fields","text":"_check_shared_mode_compatibility(coarse, fine)\n\nCheck that two fields in spectral representation name the same Fourier modes where their grids overlap, and that coarse is the coarser of the two. Returns nothing or throws.\n\nEvery refusal reads metadata only, never a coefficient. The two fields must agree in scalar type, tensor order, storage family, domain size, origin and Fourier layout — a differing domain or origin means the two grids' modes are different physical waves whatever their counts, and a differing fold means the stored index of a mode is not the same function of its frequency.\n\nThe coarse grid must be no larger than the fine on every axis, and smaller on one. Equal grids are refused because their shared set would omit only the excluded Nyquist shell, which makes such a call a copy that silently leaves those planes stale; add! or combine! is the operation meant for two fields on one grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._check_shared_mode_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._component_offsets_align-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Grid3D, RealFourierLayout, Type{T}}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Fields","text":"_component_offsets_align(grid, layout, ::Type{T}) -> (host_aligned, device_aligned)\n\nReport whether the component views of a rank-R  0 field begin at offsets a transform library will accept, given a plan built for one component. Component c starts (c-1) whole components into the storage, so the question is entirely a property of the grid and the element type.\n\nThe two answers differ because the two backends ask different questions. A host (FFTW) plan records the alignment class of the pointer it was planned for and refuses a different one, for the real and the complex array alike; requiring each component stride to be a multiple of 64 bytes is conservative — it preserves whatever class the base pointer has — and every production grid satisfies it. A device (cuFFT) plan needs only the real pointer aligned to its complex element, which reduces to the real component stride being even, i.e. to the grid not being odd on all three axes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._component_offsets_align"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._component_view-Tuple{AbstractArray, Val{0}, Int64}","page":"Fields","text":"_component_view(data, ::Val{R}, component::Int)\n\nSlice one component of a section's storage, a three-dimensional array over the grid, so a rank-agnostic plan applies one component at a time. The fiber-last layout makes each component contiguous, which is what makes the slice a plain strided view.\n\nIndexing the fiber axes DIRECTLY, rather than reshaping the fiber into one trailing component axis first, is what keeps the transforms allocation-free. A reshape of an Array returns a new Array sharing the data, and that wrapper is heap-allocated: two of them per transform, one for the source and one for the destination, cost a constant 128 bytes per call whatever the rank or the grid — small enough to sit under test/allocations.jl's field-sized threshold, and reported from a caller whose own contract asserts literal zero. A rank-0 section is its own component and is returned untouched, which is also what it was before the plans became rank-agnostic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._component_view"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._context_grid","page":"Fields","text":"_context_grid(object)\n\nReturn an object's grid. Every object that participates in a spectral operation — a field, a multiplier set, a kernel of any storage class — answers this and its peer _context_layout, so one predicate (_check_matching_context) can compare any two of them. Convolution extends both for the kernel classes, which is what lets a kernel be checked against a field or a multiplier set without either side knowing the other's field names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._context_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._context_layout","page":"Fields","text":"_context_layout(object)\n\nReturn an object's Fourier layout, the peer of _context_grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._context_layout"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._field_physical_of_order-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Val{0}}","page":"Fields","text":"_field_physical_of_order(data, grid, layout, ::Val{R})\n_field_spectral_of_order(data, grid, layout, ::Val{R})\n\nWrap data as the physical (or spectral) field type of tensor order R on grid and layout.\n\nThese pick the concrete type from the order alone, which similar_field cannot do because it copies its metadata from a reference field. When adapt rebuilds a FieldTransformPlans there is no such reference — it has only the plans' own grid, layout and element type, the plans carrying no tensor order — so it needs this form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._field_physical_of_order"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._first_field-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}}","page":"Fields","text":"_first_field(x)\n\nReturn the first AbstractField found in x — a field, an arbitrary value, a Broadcasted tree, or an argument tuple — or nothing if there is none. Out-of-place field broadcasting uses it to pick the field whose grid/layout metadata seeds the allocated result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._first_field"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._init_fft_threads-Tuple{}","page":"Fields","text":"_init_fft_threads()\n\nSet FFTW's thread count to match Julia's (one when threading is off). FFTW bakes the thread count into each plan, so this runs at package load, before any FieldTransformPlans is built. Retune afterwards with set_fft_threads!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._init_fft_threads"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._is_canonical-Union{Tuple{FieldSpectralMultipliers{<:AbstractFloat, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, <:Any, Canonical}}, Tuple{Canonical}} where Canonical","page":"Fields","text":"_is_canonical(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nRead back whether a multiplier set's wavenumbers were derived from its grid, rather than supplied.\n\nThe answer is a type parameter, not a field, because it decides statically whether two multiplier sets provably hold the same coordinates — which is what lets convolve collapse two radial kernels without a runtime test. The flag has to be carried across a move deliberately by adapt: rebuilding through the public six-array constructor would mark the result non-canonical and silently disable that collapse, costing performance with no wrong answer to show for it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._is_canonical"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._kernel_data","page":"Fields","text":"_kernel_data(kernel)\n\nReturn the coefficientwise multiplier a kernel contributes to a pass. A kernel held as a field yields its stored array; a separable, radial or product kernel yields what its pass consumes — typically a lazy expression over the wavenumber arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._kernel_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._project_pair!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_project_pair!(data, ix, iy)\n\nProject one conjugate pair of data, at linear indices ix and iy, onto the reality condition: both members are replaced by the mean of the first and the conjugate of the second, which is the orthogonal projection of the pair onto hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k). A self-paired corner (ix == iy) gets the same formula against itself, i.e. its real part, and is read and written ONCE. All reads precede all writes, so the in-place call is exact; the kernel and the gated CPU loop both call this, so they agree bitwise, which is what the GPU parity suite and the threading lane compare.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._project_pair!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._reality_projection_planes!-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{KernelAbstractions.CPU, Any, Val{D}, Vararg{Any, 5}}} where D","page":"Fields","text":"_reality_projection_planes!(backend, data, ::Val{D}, planes, sizes, others,\n                            ncomp, P)\n\nProject every stored self-conjugate plane of data onto the reality condition, dispatching on backend: a KernelAbstractions launch of _reality_projection_kernel! on a device, and a gated loop over slabs on a CPU. D is the folded axis, planes the plane indices stored on it (the zero plane, and the Nyquist plane when the full axis is even), sizes the three stored extents, others the two full axes, ncomp the fiber component count and P the elements per component. Both arms call _project_pair! per pair, agreeing bitwise; returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._reality_projection_planes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._refuse_cross_representation_equality-Tuple{}","page":"Fields","text":"_refuse_cross_representation_equality()\n\nThrow the ArgumentError for comparing a field in physical representation with one in spectral representation, which may be two representations of one field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._refuse_cross_representation_equality"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._refuse_field_as_fiber_map-Tuple{Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_refuse_field_as_fiber_map(φ)\n\nRefuse a field handed to a fiber-map slot, and do nothing for anything else.\n\nA field in physical representation is callable — f(x) samples it at a position — so passing one where map!/map expect the fiber map φ type-checks and runs. What it then computes is nonsense of the worst kind: for a vector source the fiber is an SVector{3}, which the callable happily reads as a position, so the call silently interpolates the field at its own source's values and returns a plausible number. For a scalar source it fails, but as BoundsError: attempt to access Float64 at index [2], which names neither the argument nor the mistake.\n\nThere is nothing to protect: φ maps a fiber to a fiber, while a field is a section. Both field and point-value map! are covered, the point-value fiber being an SVector{3} at R = 1 exactly as a field's is.\n\nDispatch does the work, so the non-field path is a no-op the compiler removes; the refusal costs nothing in the pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._refuse_field_as_fiber_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._rfft_shape-Tuple{Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}, Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}}","page":"Fields","text":"_rfft_shape(grid_size::NTuple{3, Int}, fft_dims::NTuple{3, Int})\n\nReturn the shape of the nonredundant real-to-complex Fourier representation of a three-dimensional real field.\n\nHere grid_size is the physical grid size and fft_dims is the order of transformed dimensions. The dimension fft_dims[1] is the real-FFT half dimension: it is stored with length grid_size[fft_dims[1]] ÷ 2 + 1, while the other dimensions keep their physical-grid lengths.\n\nReturns a three-tuple giving the spectral array size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._rfft_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._section_equality-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_section_equality(compare, a, b)\n\nCompare two fields of one representation with compare (== or isequal) applied to their storage, after refusing a pair of different element types or storage families and answering false for a pair of different tensor orders, grids or layouts. Returns a Bool or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._section_equality"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_mode_axis-Tuple{Int64, Int64, Bool}","page":"Fields","text":"_shared_mode_axis(coarse_size, fine_size, is_folded)\n\nReturn the per-axis geometry the resolution verbs walk: the coarse stored extent, the two grid sizes and whether this is the folded axis, as one isbits tuple a kernel can carry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_mode_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_mode_index-Tuple{Int64, NTuple{4, Any}}","page":"Fields","text":"_shared_mode_index(stored_index, axis)\n\nMap a coarse stored index on one axis to the fine stored index of the SAME Fourier frequency, or return 0 for a coarse mode that is not shared.\n\nThe returned 0 is the coarse Nyquist of an even axis, which the shared set excludes. On a full axis the stored index runs over nonnegative frequencies and then wraps to negative ones, exactly as AbstractFFTs.fftfreq orders them; the folded axis stores nonnegative frequencies only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_mode_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_mode_pair-Tuple{Int64, Any, Any, Any, Int64, Int64, Int64}","page":"Fields","text":"_shared_mode_pair(work_item, axes, coarse_strides, fine_strides, components,\n                  coarse_elements, fine_elements)\n\nReturn the index pair (coarse, fine) a work item acts on, or (0, 0) when its mode is not shared.\n\nOne work item owns one coarse stored mode of one fiber component, so the walk costs the coarse field's size and the fine field is touched only where it is written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_mode_pair"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_modes!-Union{Tuple{ToFine}, Tuple{AbstractFieldSpectral, AbstractFieldSpectral, Val{ToFine}, Any, Any}} where ToFine","page":"Fields","text":"_shared_modes!(destination, source, ::Val{ToFine}, α, β)\n\nRun the shared body of embed_modes! and extract_modes!: check the pair, build the per-axis geometry, run the pass. ToFine says which of the two fields is the destination, which is the only thing the two verbs do differently besides their refusals and their scale factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_modes!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_modes_pass!-Union{Tuple{ToFine}, Tuple{KernelAbstractions.CPU, Any, Any, Val{ToFine}, Vararg{Any, 8}}} where ToFine","page":"Fields","text":"_shared_modes_pass!(backend, destination, source, ::Val{ToFine}, axes,\n                    coarse_strides, fine_strides, components, coarse_elements,\n                    fine_elements, α, β)\n\nWalk the shared modes, dispatching on backend: a KernelAbstractions launch on a device, and a gated loop over slabs on a CPU. ToFine is true for embed_modes! and false for extract_modes!; both arms call _write_shared_mode! per mode, agreeing bitwise, and the work item count is the coarse field's stored size times its component count. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._shared_modes_pass!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._show_domain-Tuple{IO, Grid3D, RealFourierLayout}","page":"Fields","text":"_show_domain(io, grid)\n_show_domain(io, grid, layout)\n\nPrint the parts of a grid and layout that differ from the ordinary box, and nothing when they do not.\n\nThe grid-only arity is for an object that carries a grid and no layout — a traversal scratch, which is bound to the grid its keys were computed for and has no Fourier structure at all.\n\nThe ordinary box is 0 2pi) on every axis with the fold on the first, which is what the tests, the examples and the benchmarks all use. Anything else is what makes two same-sized fields refuse to combine, so it is exactly what a display has to carry.\n\nCoordinates print at full precision, not rounded. A first draft rounded them to four significant digits, which reintroduced one axis-scale down the very defect this display exists to remove: the grid check is exact equality, so two boxes differing in the sixth digit are refused and would have rendered as the same string. Length is spent only where it buys that, since the ordinary box prints nothing at all here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._show_domain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._show_extent-Tuple{IO, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_show_extent(io, sizes)\n\nPrint a size tuple in the 64×64×64 form every display in the package uses for a grid or an array shape, so that one shape is spelled one way across the modules.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._show_extent"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._show_field_head-Tuple{IO, Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}}","page":"Fields","text":"_show_field_head(io, field)\n\nPrint a field's type head, ScalarFieldPhysical{Float64} or TensorFieldSpectral{2, Float64}.\n\nThe head is an abbreviation of typeof, not a copy of it — a valid partial application with the storage, grid and layout parameters dropped. The tensor types carry the order R as their first parameter and the scalar and vector names already imply theirs, so only the tensor head shows it. Shared by both display forms, which is the point: written twice, the REPL form dropped the order and two tensor fields of different rank showed the same head.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._show_field_head"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._similar_field_physical","page":"Fields","text":"_similar_field_physical(reference::AbstractFieldSpectral, data)\n\nConstruct a field represented in physical space with storage array data and metadata copied from the spectral-space reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._similar_field_physical"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._similar_field_spectral","page":"Fields","text":"_similar_field_spectral(reference::AbstractFieldPhysical, data)\n\nConstruct a field represented in spectral space with storage array data and metadata copied from the physical-space reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._similar_field_spectral"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._spectral_constant_scale-Union{Tuple{AbstractFieldSpectral{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:AbstractFloat","page":"Fields","text":"_spectral_constant_scale(field::AbstractFieldSpectral)\n\nReturn prod(grid_size), the factor by which a physical constant scales the zero Fourier coefficient under the unnormalized-forward FFT convention. Adding a real constant in spectral representation multiplies it by this scale and writes only the zero mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._spectral_constant_scale"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._spectral_zero_mode_component_data-Tuple{VectorFieldSpectral, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_spectral_zero_mode_component_data(field::VectorFieldSpectral, component)\n_spectral_zero_mode_component_data(field::TensorFieldSpectral{2}, first_index,\n                                   second_index)\n\nReturn a 1×1×1 view of the zero Fourier coefficient of one component of a vector or second-order tensor field in spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._spectral_zero_mode_component_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._spectral_zero_mode_data-Tuple{ScalarFieldSpectral}","page":"Fields","text":"_spectral_zero_mode_data(field::ScalarFieldSpectral)\n\nReturn a 1×1×1 view of the zero Fourier coefficient of a scalar field in spectral representation; constant additions in spectral representation write only here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._spectral_zero_mode_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._squared_wavenumber_broadcast-Tuple{FieldSpectralMultipliers}","page":"Fields","text":"_squared_wavenumber_broadcast(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers)\n\nReturn the total squared wavenumber k^2 = k_1^2 + k_2^2 + k_3^2 as a lazy Broadcasted over the three per-axis squared-wavenumber vectors (reshaped to the three spatial dimensions). Used by the Laplacian and the inverse Laplacian. The Helmholtz projection does not use it: the projector divides by the derivative family's own a cdot a, so its pass takes the three derivative wavenumbers and squares them inline.\n\nLazy on purpose: returning the unfused Broadcasted lets callers compose it into their own broadcasts over the coefficients hat s of a field in spectral representation (-k^2 hat s for the Laplacian, -hat s  k^2 for its inverse, …) without ever materializing the O(N^3) array. That keeps those operators allocation-free in place: no O(N^3) k^2 scratch is built, which on the GPU would otherwise accumulate in the memory pool on each call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._squared_wavenumber_broadcast"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._storage_prototype-Union{Tuple{S}, Tuple{Any, Type{S}}} where S","page":"Fields","text":"_storage_prototype(to, ::Type{S})\n\nReturn an empty array of element type S in the storage family to, to be grown with similar.\n\nThis is how adapt allocates a transform plan's witness arrays on the target without a host round trip: adapting a zero-length array costs nothing and yields a prototype of the right family, where allocating a full-size host array and adapting it would copy an entire field's worth of uninitialised memory across the bus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._storage_prototype"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._tensor2_component_data-Tuple{Union{TensorFieldPhysical{2, T} where T<:AbstractFloat, TensorFieldSpectral{2, T} where T<:AbstractFloat}, Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_tensor2_component_data(\n  field::Union{TensorFieldPhysical{2}, TensorFieldSpectral{2}},\n  first_index,\n  second_index,\n)\n\nReturn a view of the storage array corresponding to component (first_index, second_index) of a second-order tensor field.\n\nThe first three dimensions are spatial dimensions. The last two dimensions are the tensor-fiber indices. This helper is internal and works for second-order tensor fields in physical-space and spectral representations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._tensor2_component_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._unwrap_broadcast_argument-Tuple{Union{AbstractFieldPhysical, AbstractFieldSpectral}}","page":"Fields","text":"_unwrap_broadcast_argument(x)\n\nReplace each field in a broadcast expression with its storage array — recursing through a Broadcasted tree and leaving non-field arguments untouched — so a field-level broadcast lowers to a plain array broadcast on the underlying backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._unwrap_broadcast_argument"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._validate_fft_dims-Tuple{Tuple{Int64, Int64, Int64}}","page":"Fields","text":"_validate_fft_dims(d::NTuple{3, Int})\n\nCheck whether d is a valid ordering of the three spatial Fourier-transform dimensions. Returns true or false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._validate_fft_dims"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._vector_component_data-Tuple{Union{VectorFieldPhysical, VectorFieldSpectral}, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_vector_component_data(\n  field::Union{VectorFieldPhysical, VectorFieldSpectral},\n  component,\n)\n\nReturn a view of the storage array corresponding to vector component component.\n\nThe first three dimensions are spatial dimensions. The last dimension is the vector-fiber index. This helper is internal and works for vector fields in physical-space and spectral representations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._vector_component_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._wavenumber_broadcast_view-Tuple{FieldSpectralMultipliers, Val{1}}","page":"Fields","text":"_wavenumber_broadcast_view(multipliers::FieldSpectralMultipliers, ::Val{axis})\n\nReshape the per-axis derivative wavenumber array for axis 1, 2 or 3 so it broadcasts against the three spatial dimensions of a field in spectral representation. Used by the first-derivative differential operators (gradient, divergence, curl), applied as spectral multipliers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._wavenumber_broadcast_view"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Fields/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._write_shared_mode!-Tuple{Any, Any, Int64, Int64, Any, Any}","page":"Fields","text":"_write_shared_mode!(destination, source, destination_index, source_index, α, β)\n\nPerform the assignment both resolution verbs share, so the two directions and the two backends cannot drift: overwrite when β is zero, accumulate otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields._write_shared_mode!"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/","page":"Getting started","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"A short, runnable tour of the package. Every code block below is a doctest — the output shown is checked when the docs are built, so the tutorial cannot silently drift from the code.\n\nThe mental model (the Architecture page): a field is a real physical object — a section of a tensor bundle over a periodic box. Physical and spectral are two representations of the same object; the complex coefficient arrays are storage detail, never the object itself.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Grids-and-fields","page":"Getting started","text":"A field is built from a Grid3D (the periodic box) and a function sampled on it. The constructor sets the tensor order, and with it the fiber — the value space sitting over each point: ScalarFieldPhysical, VectorFieldPhysical, TensorFieldPhysical. Data is stored fiber-last: the three grid axes come first, then one axis of length 3 for each tensor order, so a scalar field is (Nx, Ny, Nz), a vector field (Nx, Ny, Nz, 3) and a second-order tensor field (Nx, Ny, Nz, 3, 3).\n\njulia> using GBGPeriodicFields\n\njulia> grid = Grid3D((16, 16, 16), (2π, 2π, 2π));\n\njulia> u = VectorFieldPhysical((x, y, z) -> (sin(x), cos(y), sin(z)), grid);\n\njulia> field_tensor_order(u)\n1\n\njulia> size(field_data(u))\n(16, 16, 16, 3)","title":"Grids and fields"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Representations:-physical-spectral","page":"Getting started","text":"A field goes to its spectral representation with to_spectral and back with to_physical, through a reusable FieldTransformPlans. The allocating forms preserve their input, so the round trip recovers the field.\n\nTwo pieces of scaffolding appear below, the spectral side being the one place storage is not simply \"the values\". A RealFourierLayout records how the real-to-complex transform is laid out: a real field's Fourier coefficients, by wavenumber vector, satisfy hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k), so only half the spectrum is stored, and the layout says which axis is the halved one (the first, by default). The helper field_spectral_shape then gives the array size that half-spectrum needs, and its Val(R) argument names the tensor order — Val(0) for a scalar field, Val(1) for a vector one — passed as a Val so the shape is known to the compiler rather than computed at run time.\n\njulia> layout = RealFourierLayout();\n\njulia> vector_shape = field_spectral_shape(grid, layout, Val(1));\n\njulia> spectral = VectorFieldSpectral(zeros(ComplexF64, vector_shape), grid);\n\njulia> plans = FieldTransformPlans(u, spectral);\n\njulia> uh = to_spectral(u, plans);\n\njulia> field_data(to_physical(uh, plans)) ≈ field_data(u)\ntrue","title":"Representations: physical ↔ spectral"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Differential-operators","page":"Getting started","text":"The differential operators are applied as spectral multipliers, acting on a field in spectral representation through a FieldSpectralMultipliers (the wavenumber arrays). They are exact on the band-limited field — e.g. the Laplacian of sin(x) is -sin(x):\n\njulia> φ = ScalarFieldPhysical((x, y, z) -> sin(x), grid);\n\njulia> scalar_shape = field_spectral_shape(grid, layout, Val(0));\n\njulia> scalar_spectral = ScalarFieldSpectral(zeros(ComplexF64, scalar_shape), grid);\n\njulia> multipliers = FieldSpectralMultipliers(scalar_spectral);\n\njulia> laplacian_φ = to_physical(laplacian(to_spectral(φ, plans), multipliers), plans);\n\njulia> field_data(laplacian_φ) ≈ -field_data(φ)\ntrue\n\nThe plans built above for the vector field u transformed this scalar field φ too. A real Fourier transform is defined on the box, and a field of tensor order R is that transform applied per component, so a FieldTransformPlans carries no tensor order and one pair serves every order: build one per storage family and element type.\n\nThe same pattern works for gradient, divergence, curl, symmetric_gradient, inverse_laplacian, and helmholtz_projection.","title":"Differential operators"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Fiberwise-algebra-and-L/measure-reductions","page":"Getting started","text":"The fiberwise (pointwise) algebra — dot, cross, norm, … — preserves the base. The reductions of the Reductions module integrate the base out by its measure: inner (langlecdotcdotrangle) is the normalized L² product; inner(u, u) is langle u^2rangle, the average of u^2 — single angle brackets mean that average (here langlesin^2rangle + langlecos^2rangle + langlesin^2rangle = tfrac32).\n\njulia> field_tensor_order(cross(u, u))    # u × u\n1\n\njulia> round(inner(u, u); digits = 4)     # ⟨|u|²⟩ = 1/2 + 1/2 + 1/2\n1.5\n\nSecond-order tensor fields complete the surface. The gradient of a vector field is one, and the tensor-only operations act on it fiberwise — the trace of nabla u is the divergence, which is a useful check that the two routes agree:\n\njulia> A = gradient(uh, multipliers);      # ∇u, a tensor field in spectral representation\n\njulia> field_tensor_order(A)\n2\n\njulia> round(inner(tr(A), divergence(uh, multipliers)); digits = 8) ==\n       round(inner(divergence(uh, multipliers), divergence(uh, multipliers)); digits = 8)\ntrue\n\nThe same quantity, twice the kinetic energy, resolved over wavenumber shells, is the radial power spectrum; its total equals inner by Parseval:\n\njulia> shells, spectrum = radial_spectrum(uh, multipliers);\n\njulia> round(sum(spectrum); digits = 4)   # the shells sum to inner(u, u)\n1.5","title":"Fiberwise algebra and L²/measure reductions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Convolution-(apply-a-Fourier-kernel)","page":"Getting started","text":"The convolve operation applies a kernel as the coefficientwise product. A kernel is not a field, and is held per mode, as per-axis factors, or as a function of the wavenumber magnitude: spectral_cutoff builds an isotropic sharp low-pass as the last, a RadialKernel storing no coefficients — here keeping wavenumbers k le 4:\n\njulia> cutoff = spectral_cutoff(multipliers, 4.0);\n\njulia> field_tensor_order(convolve(uh, cutoff))\n1","title":"Convolution (apply a Fourier kernel)"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Points:-interpolation-and-anterpolation","page":"Getting started","text":"A PointSet holds off-grid positions. The gather interpolate samples a field at those points; its adjoint anterpolate scatters per-point quantities back onto the grid.\n\njulia> points = PointSet([0.5 1.0 1.5; 2.0 3.0 4.0]);\n\njulia> npoints(points)\n2\n\njulia> sampled = interpolate(u, points);\n\njulia> point_value_tensor_order(sampled)\n1\n\njulia> scattered = anterpolate(grid, points, sampled);\n\njulia> size(field_data(scattered))\n(16, 16, 16, 3)\n\nNote that the adjoint takes the grid rather than a field as its first argument: it builds the field it returns, so it needs only the base to build it over, where interpolate needs the field it samples.","title":"Points: interpolation and anterpolation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorial/#Running-multithreaded","page":"Getting started","text":"CPU threading is on by default, but only if Julia itself has threads — the package threads nothing in a single-threaded session, and nothing in the code you write says so. Start Julia with them:\n\njulia -t 4 --project        # four threads\njulia -t auto --project     # one per core\n\nNothing else is required: the fiberwise algebra, the differential operators and the reductions all thread themselves from there, and the FFT plans pick up the same count when the package loads. The work is split only above a size threshold, since threading a small field costs more than it saves:\n\njulia> threading_cutoff()\n16384\n\nResults do not depend on how many threads you used. The threaded routes write each output element once, from the same fused expression a serial run uses, so they are bitwise identical to the serial answer — which is what lets you tune the thread count without re-baselining anything you compare against. The exceptions are by nature rather than oversight: anterpolate, radial_spectrum and radial_correlation accumulate with atomics, so the summation order varies between runs and the last bits with it.\n\nTo compare against a serial run without restarting Julia, scope it with with_threading; set_threading_cutoff! moves the threshold, and set_fft_threads! sets the FFT thread count alone, which is worth separating when the transforms and the pointwise work want different counts.\n\nThat is the whole surface in miniature: build a field, move between representations, apply operators / convolutions, reduce it, and transfer to and from points. The API reference documents every operation (with its computed formula), and the Architecture page explains why the package is shaped this way.","title":"Running multithreaded"},{"category":"section","location":"#GBGPeriodicFields.jl","page":"Home","text":"GBGPeriodicFields.jl is a package for representing, transforming, and manipulating scalar, vector, and tensor fields on three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains. The tensors are Cartesian — components on one fixed right-handed orthonormal frame, with no curvilinear coordinates or metric machinery — so this is an applied field toolkit rather than a general differential-geometry package.\n\nThe package is aimed primarily at fluid-dynamics and turbulence simulations. Fields are physical objects, while physical-space and nonredundant spectral representations are storage and manipulation choices. The implementation is designed for both CPU and GPU array backends, with HDF5 field persistence, and includes an unstructured point layer: evaluating fields at off-grid positions (interpolation) and depositing per-point quantities back onto the grid (anterpolation) — the field layer for Lagrangian-particle methods.\n\nThis package is not intended to be a ready-to-use DNS solver; rather, it is a toolkit for building fluid-dynamics and general field solvers.\n\nGBGHomogeneousTurbulence.jl is such a solver, and the package's primary consumer: a pseudospectral direct numerical simulation of homogeneous isotropic turbulence built on these periodic fields. Its documentation shows this package in earnest — driving a complete solver, not a worked example.\n\nThe GBG part in the name GBGPeriodicFields.jl comes from the city of Göteborg (Gothenburg, Sweden), where the primary development took place.\n\nA few words of vocabulary, used throughout the docs. A field — and its off-grid point-values — is a section: one fiber value (a scalar, a vector, or a second-order tensor) attached to every point of a base (the structured grid, or an unstructured set of points). The operations group as: pointwise algebra within the fiber; differential operators, applied as spectral multipliers; periodic convolution with a kernel; reductions against the base's own measure (a grid cell's volume, a point's count of one), giving a number or a radial profile; and the grid↔point transfer. Each group is catalogued under Main components below. Why they are grouped that way is the Architecture page; you do not need it to use the package.","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"This is an unregistered package, so it is not listed in the General Julia registry. To install it, run:\n\n]add https://github.com/GrigorySarnitsky/GBGPeriodicFields.jl\n\nor\n\nusing Pkg\n\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/GrigorySarnitsky/GBGPeriodicFields.jl\")\n\nThen you can use it as usual:\n\nusing GBGPeriodicFields\n\nNew here? Start with the Getting started tour — a short, runnable walkthrough of grids, fields, representations, operators, reductions, and the point transfer. For a full solver, the Examples page walks through a complete forced-HIT pseudospectral DNS built on the package — the performance-first, allocation-free pattern, with results.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Main-components","page":"Home","text":"FiberSections: the base-agnostic foundation — the Cartesian fiber types (FiberVector, FiberTensor2/3/4) naming the value at one base point, the AbstractFiberSection supertype fields and point-values share, and the threading primitive and code generators both reuse.\nFields: the core types — grids, fields in their physical and spectral representations, and the to_spectral/to_physical transforms between them. It also holds what the other modules build on: similar_field and the allocating constructors, broadcasting, HDF5 persistence, the spectral-kernel supertype, adapt/adapt! for moving a field, its wavenumber multipliers, kernels or FFT plans between host and device, and the check and projector for a real field's spectrum, reality_residual and reality_projection!.\nFieldAlgebra: fiberwise operations on fields — vector-space operations, scalar actions, vector and tensor products, contractions, pointwise norms, and tensor decompositions, plus map!/map for any other fiber map.\nDifferentialOperators: differential operators applied as spectral multipliers — gradient, divergence, curl, the inverse curl, the one-pass symmetric gradient, Laplacian, inverse Laplacian, and Helmholtz projection.\nConvolution: periodic convolution of a field with a kernel, in spectral representation — convolve/convolve! (infix ⋆) apply it as the coefficientwise product of the transforms. A kernel is not a field, and is stored per mode, per axis, by wavenumber magnitude, or as a product; spectral_cutoff/spectral_band, tophat_window, assignment_window, translation_kernel and radial_kernel build them, and exponential_convolve applies an exponential radial kernel without building one.\nPoints: the unstructured base — the Point, PointSet and point-value types, their fiberwise algebra mirroring the field operations in physical representation, the motion pair displace!/wrap!, and HDF5 persistence.\nInterpolation: the grid↔point transfer — interpolate (gather W) and anterpolate (scatter W^top), an adjoint pair, exactly L²-adjoint once rescaled by NM, grid points over points. The scheme is chosen: local physical-domain splines, the exact spectral DirectDFT, or the fast NUFFT. All run on CPU, CUDA and AMDGPU, and a scheme saves to HDF5.\nReductions: the L²/measure structure — reductions that integrate the base away against its measure, over fields and point-values alike: the extensive integral, the normalized inner/l2norm/lpnorm, the moments mean/std/var/cov, maximum/minimum/extrema, and the spectral-only, multiplier-weighted weighted_inner. Several also act on a field in spectral representation, with no transform. The radial pair radial_spectrum and radial_correlation resolve the inner product over wavenumber or separation shells.","title":"Main components"},{"category":"section","location":"#Documentation-structure","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Documentation structure"},{"category":"section","location":"#Start-here","page":"Home","text":"Getting started: the guided tour, from a Grid3D to a transform, an operator and a reduction.\nRunning on a GPU: fields on an NVIDIA or AMD device, and CUDA fields larger than GPU memory.\nExamples: a complete forced-HIT pseudospectral DNS built on the package.\nNotation: the single reference for every symbol the docstrings use.","title":"Start here"},{"category":"section","location":"#Design-and-internals","page":"Home","text":"Architecture: why the package is built as it is — the fiber-bundle model, representation validity (the method set is the spec), the code-generation architecture, the spectral machinery, CPU threading, and the grid↔point transfer.\nFormalization: the typed statements behind the prose — each operation's spaces and normalizations checked by expansion against the code, linked to the tests that verify them.\nTesting: how the tests are built and the per-operation coverage contract.\nPerformance: the performance model, externally-sourced best practices, the recorded optimization findings, and the tracked benchmark suite.","title":"Design & internals"},{"category":"section","location":"#API-reference","page":"Home","text":"Pages = [\n  \"api.md\",\n  \"api/GBGPeriodicFields.md\",\n  \"api/FiberSections.md\",\n  \"api/Fields.md\",\n  \"api/FieldAlgebra.md\",\n  \"api/DifferentialOperators.md\",\n  \"api/Convolution.md\",\n  \"api/Points.md\",\n  \"api/Interpolation.md\",\n  \"api/Reductions.md\",\n]","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Notation","page":"Notation","text":"The symbols used throughout the docstrings and the design documents, with the code object each one denotes. All mathematics is rendered LaTeX; a code identifier always appears in backticks and never inside a math span. This page is the single reference — a docstring introduces a symbol on first use and need not repeat the convention.","title":"Notation"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Conventions","page":"Notation","text":"Concept Symbol Convention\nImaginary unit mathrmi It is always upright — never bare italic i, never imath. Writing it upright frees i for use as an index.\nIndices i j k dots They are italic, taken in order, and used freely alongside mathrmi (upright versus italic tells them apart). As an index, k occurs only in component subscripts (Einstein-summed); the standalone k of the table below is always the wavenumber, so the two roles never meet.\nRepeated index a_i b_i An index repeated within a term is summed over the three components 1 2 3 (Einstein convention); an explicit sum is reserved for the grid, point, and mode sums below.\nPartial derivative partial_x_i, partial^2_x_i x_i The coordinate is subscripted, not the fiber index; a second derivative repeats the coordinate, so the Laplacian is Delta f = partial^2_x_i x_i f (with the repeated i summed).\nDifferential mathrmdx, mathrmdmu Upright mathrmd (matching the imaginary unit), with a thin space before it: int_Omega f mathrmdmu. Never a bare italic d.\nSummations sum_x, sum_p, sum_k They run over the N grid points, the M points, and the Fourier modes.\nFractions tfrac12, frac1N Every macro argument is braced, both numerator and denominator, so no argument boundary is ever ambiguous.\nSpacing juxtaposition Adjacent factors sit side by side (C_K varepsilon^23 kappa^-53), never separated by a thin space; a thin space appears only before the differential above.\nDelimiters Bigl( dots Bigr) Size delimiters by hand with the fixed-size \\bigl/\\Bigl … \\bigr/\\Bigr commands; the auto-sizing \\left/\\right pair is not used — it inserts a spurious gap the fixed-size forms do not. A system of cases is written with the cases environment, which sizes its own brace.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Base-and-fiber","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Code\nf, g a field: one value attached to every point of the base, that value — the fiber value — being a scalar, a vector, or a second-order tensor (a section of the tensor bundle, in the language the design docs use). One deliberate reuse: in Convolution, g and its transform hat g denote the kernel rather than a field — the standard symbol for a convolution kernel. A kernel is not a field, so read g as the kernel on that page and as a field everywhere else —\nx a position in the periodic box Omega (x_p is the p-th point) —\nq the value a point carries (q_p sits at x_p) — sampled from a field, or a quantity of the point's own that anterpolation, interpolation's adjoint, deposits onto the grid AbstractPointValues\nB the base of the bundle picture — the set a section attaches values to: a Grid3D's nodes, or a PointSet's points. A subscripted N_B is its point count (N or M); the bare glyph is never a scalar Grid3D / PointSet\nOmega, lvertOmegarvert the periodic box and its volume Grid3D\nN the grid-point count prod_i N_i, the product of the counts along the axes; on a uniform periodic grid this is equally the cell count, each node carrying the volume of one cell, which is why the docs use \"grid points\" and \"grid cells\" for the same N. A complexity expression may use either reading, and which one is always fixed by the shape it describes: the storage claims of the kernels and reductions — O(N^3) for a full field, O(N) for a per-axis array, O(N^2) for a plane — count per axis on the cubic grids they describe, while the transfer costs O(NM) and O(N log N + M) count the total. The two never mix inside one formula prod(grid_size)\nM the number of points npoints\nk a wavenumber vector (k_a is its a-th component, lvert krvert its magnitude) FieldSpectralMultipliers\na the derivative wavenumber vector (a_1 a_2 a_3 its components) — the family the first-derivative operators multiply by, equal to k except that it is zeroed at an even-length axis's Nyquist frequency, which keeps derivatives of real fields real. The Laplacian instead uses the true lvert krvert^2, and the two families are stored separately for that reason. Standing alone or with a numeric subscript the glyph is this vector; sitting in a subscript (k_a, Delta x_a) it is an ordinary per-axis index FieldSpectralMultipliers\nkappa a wavenumber-shell magnitude, kappa = lvert krvert — the radial coordinate a spectrum is resolved over radial_spectrum\nh a physical displacement (h_a is its component along axis a) translation_kernel\nDelta before a field it is the Laplacian; before a coordinate or parameter it reads as \"an increment of\": Delta x_a the grid spacing on axis a, Delta k the wavenumber-shell width (the box fundamental, in radial_spectrum), and Delta t a timestep — which appears only in the examples, the package itself carrying no notion of time laplacian, radial_spectrum\nhat f the field f in spectral representation: its Fourier coefficients as the forward transform stores them to_spectral(f)\n\nThe worked turbulence example sets the wavenumber vector bold, as mathbfk, because it follows the turbulence convention in which italic k is the turbulent kinetic energy. That override is local to the example and is declared there; everywhere else the wavenumber vector is the plain k of the table above.","title":"Base and fiber"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Operators","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Code\nnabla gradient gradient\nnablacdot divergence divergence\nnablatimes curl curl\nDelta, Delta^-1 the Laplacian and its inverse laplacian, inverse_laplacian\nmathbbP the Helmholtz (Leray) projection helmholtz_projection\nmathcalF, mathcalF^-1 the forward and inverse transforms to_spectral, to_physical\ncdot, times the fiber dot and cross products dot (⋅), cross (×)\nlanglecdotcdotrangle the L^2 inner product over the base's total measure inner\nlanglecdotrangle the base average of one argument, against the same measure mean\nlvertcdotrvert the pointwise (fiberwise) magnitude. In this table the bars are written \\lvert…\\rvert because a literal pipe inside a cell is eaten as a column delimiter; in running math outside a table the same symbol is written with plain single bars, which cannot be shown in this cell because the literal pipe would split it — note that LaTeX's backslash-bar is NOT that spelling, being the DOUBLE bar, the lVertcdotrVert of the next row norm\nlVertcdotrVert the L^2 norm l2norm\nint fmathrmdmu the integral against the base measure: cell volume on a grid, unit mass per point integral\nstar periodic convolution convolve\nW, W^top interpolation (gather) and its adjoint anterpolation (scatter). One deliberate reuse: the kernel builders tophat_window and assignment_window write W for their physical-space window — the box, and the linear cloud-in-cell \"tent\" a point deposits — and hat W for its transfer function, not the gather operator; the tent is the LalescuSpline(0, 0) weight, but the two are different objects interpolate, anterpolate","title":"Operators"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Scalar-weights","page":"Notation","text":"Two distinct quantities are written alpha, and the argument tells them apart: the caller's accumulation weight is always the bare alpha, while the half-spectrum Parseval weight is always written with its mode argument as alpha(k). They never denote the same thing. A third, purely local use appears in the Fields storage docstrings, where alpha = 1 2 3 numbers a stored fiber component; it names an array slot rather than a weight, and does not travel beyond those bullets.\n\nSymbol Meaning Code\nalpha, beta the caller's accumulation weights in textttdestination leftarrow alpha (dots) + beta textttdestination the trailing α, β arguments\nalpha(k) the half-spectrum Parseval weight of mode k: 1 on a self-conjugate plane of the folded axis, 2 elsewhere. The folded axis is the one the real-to-complex transform halves, storing only non-negative wavenumbers — it is the first entry of a RealFourierLayout's fft_dims, written fft_dims[1] throughout, and is the first axis by default; its self-conjugate planes are the two — zero and, on an even axis, Nyquist — that the folding maps to themselves, so a mode's conjugate partner lies in the same plane and is stored alongside it rather than being implied; those modes are therefore counted once, not twice, so that a sum over stored modes equals the sum over the full spectrum inner, radial_spectrum\nw a multiplier weighting an inner product mode by mode weighted_inner","title":"Scalar weights"},{"category":"section","location":"gbg/#gbg","page":"Documentation tooling","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.\n\nThe documentation of GBGPeriodicFields is held to the rulebook of GBGCodingTools v2.0.0-rc.37: a documentation rubric, the LaTeX idiom, the citation style, and a testing protocol. The rules themselves are on that package's site, each under a stable id. What follows is only what this package does differently from the defaults.","title":"Documentation tooling"},{"category":"section","location":"gbg/#What-GBGPeriodicFields-has-switched-off","page":"Documentation tooling","text":"Each entry names a rule this package does not hold itself to, and why. A rule listed here is read as the entry states it.\n\nRULE.DOC.RETIRED_CLAIMS — A decision log QUOTES a retired claim in order to record that it was retired: decision 20's entry says a kernel is itself a scalar field and is immediately followed by decision 60 superseding it. The ban and the log want opposite things from the same sentence, and the log is right. This is why the log is its own document: the rule can be narrowed to a FILE but not to a section, so splitting it turned an unfixable problem into a one-line switch. Every other rule still reads this file. (docs/DECISIONS.md)\nRULE.API.POSITIONAL — ONLY Grid3D's two-argument constructor's origin keyword. It shifts every node, so the rule points the other way; the owner weighed that against the churn on 2026-09-02 and kept the keyword, the four-argument constructor taking it positionally. Every other argument in this file still follows the rule. (src/Fields.jl)\nRULE.API.POSITIONAL — ONLY the array_type keyword of the constructors that build a field by sampling a function. It picks the storage family the result lives in, not any value; making it positional would be an interface change breaking the consumer's calls for a consistency gain only. Owner-decided 2026-09-25 (finding 2026-09-24-documentation-review-01-009). Every other argument in this file still follows the rule. (src/Fields.jl)\nRULE.API.POSITIONAL — ONLY the callable field's scheme keyword, field(x; scheme = …). A one-point convenience whose default is the usual case; owner-settled 2026-08-05, while interpolate/anterpolate keep scheme positional. Every other argument in this file still follows the rule. (src/Interpolation.jl)\nRULE.API.POSITIONAL — ONLY the corrected keyword of var, std and cov. These EXTEND Statistics, whose own methods spell it as a keyword; a positional form would break the drop-in standard-library use the package promises. Owner-decided 2026-09-25 (finding 2026-09-24-documentation-review-01-009). Every other argument in this file still follows the rule. (src/Reductions.jl)","title":"What GBGPeriodicFields has switched off"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Reductions/#Reductions","page":"Reductions","text":"","title":"Reductions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Reductions/#Integral-reductions","page":"Reductions","text":"Five of the reductions on this page also take a leading fiber map — the mapped form, as in mean(det, A) or maximum(norm, u), which reduces a nonlinear invariant without building it as a section. It is documented on integral, which names the five. Statistics gives var and std no such spelling, so neither does this package.","title":"Integral reductions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Reductions/#Inner-product-and-norm-reductions","page":"Reductions","text":"","title":"Inner-product and norm reductions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Reductions/#Moments-and-order-statistics","page":"Reductions","text":"These extend the standard-library generics — mean, std, var, and cov from Statistics, and maximum, minimum, and extrema from Base — rather than introducing package names, and are used exactly as their stdlib counterparts are.\n\nAll seven are in scope from using GBGPeriodicFields alone: the Statistics four are re-exported by the top-level module, as dot and norm are from LinearAlgebra, and the Base three are in scope already. That split is why two blocks follow rather than one.","title":"Moments and order statistics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Reductions/#Radial-reductions","page":"Reductions","text":"","title":"Radial reductions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Reductions/#Internal-API","page":"Reductions","text":"These symbols are not part of the public interface; they are documented for contributors — the fiberwise reduction machinery (the fiber map-reduce and its loop and tree forms) and the fixed reduction tree that makes a threaded reduction bitwise thread-count-invariant, together with the backend split that decides which of the two a reduction takes.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions","page":"Reductions","text":"Reductions\n\nThis module holds the L² / measure structure on sections, the third kind of operation beside the fiberwise algebra and the differential operators. Periodic convolution and the grid↔point transfer belong to this same family, as two further roles of the one integration structure. A reduction integrates a section over its base against the base's measure — the extensive one for integral, its unit-mass normalization for the intensive reductions: a Grid3D uses its uniform quadrature (the cell volume), and a PointSet uses its counting measure (unit weight per point, as a PointSet is grid-free). One set of formulas serves both fields and point-values, dispatched on AbstractFiberSection.\n\nThe measure reductions act on fields in physical representation and point-values — representation-free, physical-space quantities. Several also act on fields in spectral representation, returning the identical physical value with no inverse transform: inner/l2norm by Parseval's theorem, integral/mean from the zero (DC) Fourier mode, and the scalar-only std/var from Parseval. The remaining reductions (lpnorm, cov, maximum/minimum/extrema) stay physical-only.\n\nThe parts follow, each linking to where its formula lives.\n\nintegral — the extensive integral, the one quantity that scales with the base measure.\nmean — the intensive, fiber-preserving arithmetic average over the N grid cells or the M points (it extends Statistics; the mean of a vector field is a FiberVector).\nstd, var, cov — the base moments (extending Statistics, corrected = true by default). The std and var reductions are scalar-only: the sample standard deviation and variance are unambiguous only for a scalar section. The coordinate-free second moment of a vector field is the covariance tensor cov (R, a symmetric FiberTensor2), of which a per-component variance would be only the basis-dependent diagonal.\ninner — the normalized L² inner product langlecdotcdotrangle, a scalar that averages the fiber contraction over the N grid cells or the M points. l2norm and lpnorm are the norms derived from it, and weighted_inner is its spectral generalization, weighting the pairing by a kernel multiplier.\nmaximum, minimum, extrema — the L^infty end, extending Base. The bare forms take a scalar section; the mapped forms below lift that restriction for any order the reductions serve (R le 2: scalar, vector and second-order tensor sections are the supported surface, and a fiber-preserving reduction raises above it rather than returning a wrong answer).\nThe mapped forms — integral, mean, maximum, minimum and extrema also accept a leading function, applied to the fiber value at every base point: mean(det, A) averages a determinant over the base and maximum(norm, u) takes a supremum of magnitude, each in a single pass that never builds the mapped quantity as a section. Naming that map is also what makes an order statistic well posed on a vector or tensor section, where the bare maximum refuses to guess. They mirror the standard-library mean(f, itr) and maximum(f, itr) spellings and, mapping the fiber nonlinearly, are physical-only.\nradial_spectrum and radial_correlation — a Fourier-dual pair (Wiener–Khinchin) that resolves inner over a radial coordinate, the shell-binned power spectrum over k and its physical-space two-point correlation over r, each also in a two-field co-spectrum form.\n\nnote: The moments extend `Statistics`; the order statistics extend `Base`\nmean, std, var and cov extend Statistics rather than being owned here, and maximum, minimum and extrema extend Base. Either way they are used exactly as their standard-library counterparts are, and using GBGPeriodicFields alone puts all of them in scope: the top-level module re-exports the four Statistics generics, as it does dot and norm from LinearAlgebra, so a caller need not know which standard library a reduction's verb lives in.\n\nPhysics stays with the caller, who builds kinetic energy, enstrophy, dissipation, rms and turbulence intensity from these.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.integral","page":"Reductions","text":"integral(section)\nintegral(fiber_map, section)\n\nThe base integral int f mathrmdmu of a section f, passed as section (integral, intmathrmdmu) — the measure-weighted sum of the section over its base. It is the one extensive reduction, the only one that scales with the base measure; reach for inner or mean when you want an intensive, size-independent average instead. On a Grid3D the sum uses the uniform cell-volume quadrature, and on a PointSet it uses the unit-weight counting measure:\n\nint f mathrmdmu =\nbegincases\n  dfracOmegaN sum_x f(x)  textbase a  textttGrid3D text  the box  Omega text and its  N text cells4pt\n  sum_p f_p                           textbase a  textttPointSet text  no domain unit weight per point\nendcases\n\nwhere N is the number of grid cells and Omega the domain volume. The result is fiber-preserving: a scalar for a scalar section, a FiberVector for a vector section, and a FiberTensor2 for a tensor section. For a band-limited field the grid quadrature is exact, because the periodic trapezoidal rule integrates the represented modes exactly. For a field in spectral representation the same value comes from the zero (DC) Fourier mode with no inverse transform, int_Omega fmathrmdmu = tfracOmegaN hat f(0), since the DC coefficient hat f(0) = sum_x f(x) is real for a real field.\n\nThe mapped form\n\nGiven a leading function fiber_map, the integrand becomes that function of the section's fiber value at each base point rather than the fiber value itself:\n\nint_Omega (textttfiber_map circ f) mathrmdmu\n\nThe map receives the scalar for a scalar section, the FiberVector for a vector section, and the FiberTensor2 for a tensor section, so integral(det, A) integrates a determinant and integral(norm, u) a magnitude. The mapped value is formed one base point at a time and consumed by the reduction immediately, so nothing section-sized is built — where writing the same quantity as integral(det(A)) first materializes a whole determinant field. Passing identity recovers the bare form. The same leading function is taken by mean (which divides by the base count instead of weighting by the measure) and by maximum/minimum/extrema; for the order statistics it is also what makes them well posed above tensor order zero, the bare forms being scalar-only by design.\n\nThe mapped form is the cheaper route on both backends — one pass that allocates nothing, against a materialized section plus a second pass over it — by roughly two and a half times on a CPU and a quarter on a GPU. It gets there by different means on each, chosen internally: a fixed reduction tree over vectorized blocked loops on a CPU, a single fused pass on a GPU. That tree's shape does not depend on the thread count, so a CPU result is bitwise identical however many threads it runs on.\n\nnote: The fiber map's own cost can dominate\nThe fiber map's own cost can outweigh the choice of route. LinearAlgebra.norm applied to a fiber uses the overflow-safe scaled algorithm, several times the cost of the plain sqrtsum_i u_i^2 that the fieldwise norm evaluates, which is enough to make maximum(norm, u) costlier than lpnorm(u, Inf) on a CPU despite allocating nothing; passing a plain magnitude instead reverses that. Pick the map for the accuracy the result needs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.integral"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.inner","page":"Reductions","text":"inner(f, g)\n\nThe normalized L² inner product langle f grangle of two compatible sections (inner, langlecdotcdotrangle) — the fiber contraction f cdot g averaged over the base, returning a scalar. It is intensive (it matches mean), which makes it the natural pairing for homogeneous turbulence, where kinetic energy is tfrac12langle u urangle. For the norms derived from it use l2norm or lpnorm; to resolve the pairing over a wavenumber or separation shell use radial_spectrum or radial_correlation.\n\nFor two fields in spectral representation inner returns the same value by Parseval's theorem with no inverse transform, counting twice each stored mode whose conjugate the real transform omits. This p = 2 inner and l2norm are representation-invariant by Parseval, while lpnorm is physical-only.\n\nThe arguments f and g are two compatible sections of equal tensor order. On a grid the pairing is the equal-weight average over the N cells (Omega is the periodic box, Omega its volume); on a point-set it is the average over the M points. The grid's continuum form below is exact when the product f cdot g is resolved on the grid (the periodic trapezoidal rule integrates resolved modes exactly); under aliasing — both fields carrying Nyquist content, say — the discrete sum is the definition:\n\nbeginaligned\nlangle f grangle = frac1Omega int_Omega f(x) cdot g(x)mathrmdx\n  = frac1N sum_x f(x) cdot g(x)  (textttGrid3D) \nlangle f grangle = frac1M sum_p f_p cdot g_p  (textttPointSet)\nendaligned\n\nnote: The mixed spectral pair allocates one or two correction planes\nIn spectral representation inner(f, f) and l2norm run allocation-free, but the mixed inner(f, g) materializes O(N^2) correction planes, per-axis count squared — the zero plane of the folded axis, the one the real transform halves, and its Nyquist plane too when that axis is even, so an odd axis pays one plane and an even one two. That is small beside the field's O(N^3), per-axis count cubed, where inner(f, f) pays nothing. The fused alternative, a strided-view dot, has no AMD method (rocBLAS dotc), so one route serves every backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.inner"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.weighted_inner","page":"Reductions","text":"weighted_inner(f, w::SpectralKernel)\nweighted_inner(f, g, w::SpectralKernel)\nweighted_inner(f, weights::Tuple{Vararg{SpectralKernel}})\n\nThe L² pairing langle f w grangle of a field f with a field g filtered by a real Fourier multiplier w, both in spectral representation (weighted_inner, langlecdot w cdotrangle). It applies the spectral symbol w to g and reduces against f in one fused pass, materializing no weighted field — the reduction-dual of convolve, which instead forms w star g as a field, w there also naming the physical-space kernel whose transfer function it is. It generalizes inner (the w equiv 1 case); the two-argument form is the g = f case langle f w frangle.\n\nPhysically it pairs f with the filtered field w star g. By Parseval, with the rfft half-spectrum weight alpha(k) (1 on the folded axis's zero plane and, when that axis has even length, its Nyquist plane; 2 otherwise — the folded rfft axis is the layout's fft_dims[1], the first by default), N the number of grid points, and c a fiber-component index (not the exponent coefficient c of exponential_convolve!),\n\nbeginaligned\nlangle f w grangle = frac1N sum_x f(x) cdot (w star g)(x) \n= frac1N^2 sum_textstored  k alpha(k)  w(k) sum_c mathrmRebigl(hat f_c(k) overlinehat g_c(k)bigr)\nendaligned\n\nArguments\n\nf, g — two compatible, equal-order fields in spectral representation (g = f in the two-argument form).\nweights — in the tuple form, a tuple of such kernels, of any length. Up to four are answered in one traversal and one device readback; a longer tuple takes one traversal per group of four. On the CPU the results are bitwise-equal to calling the operation once per weight, and on a device they agree to rounding, a device reduction summing in its own order. The results are returned in the order given. An empty tuple answers () without reading the field, and every refusal the single-weight form makes is made here in any position.\nw — the weight: a kernel in any of the four storage classes of a SpectralKernel, holding a real multiplier w(k). All four give bitwise-identical results. The multiplier must be real, because langle f w grangle is an inner product only then: a kernel whose type says complex — translation_kernel's phase, for one — is rejected rather than silently contributing its real part. One class is exempt, and the exemption is a hazard in its own right — see the warning below. Build it with the kernel builders: a band mask (spectral_band/spectral_cutoff) gives an in-band (co-)energy, and radial_kernel(identity, multipliers) (w = k^2) gives the Dirichlet energy langle f -Delta grangle exactly (both keep the true Nyquist k^2); on fields with no Nyquist content this equals langlenabla f nabla grangle, the derivative multiplier zeroing only the then-empty Nyquist plane.\n\nIt is spectral-only, a Parseval reduction like the spectral inner.\n\nIt equals the naive textttinner(w star f w star g) only when w is an idempotent projection (a sharp band mask, w^2 = w); for a general w it is the single-weight pairing langle f w grangle above, not lVert w star frVert^2. The co-spectrum-like mixed form is symmetric in f, g and may be negative.\n\nIt is fused: the cost is a single weighted pass over the coefficients — the same traffic as one spectral inner — and its only allocation is a small per-axis weight vector (one wavenumber row, not a field). It is thus far cheaper than the radial reductions radial_spectrum / radial_correlation, which sweep every mode with a scatter-add binning pass (and, for the correlation, an inverse transform).\n\nnote: The unit weight and `inner` agree numerically, not bitwise\nlangle f 1 cdot frangle is langle f frangle, so a unit weight reproduces inner — measured to within 10^-15 relative, not to the last bit. Both reduce on the same threaded fixed tree, so the residue is the weighted pass carrying a multiplication by one through each mode rather than a different summation order. That matters where code comparing against a stored number swaps one for the other.\n\nwarning: A complex [`StoredKernel`](@ref) weight is silently reduced to its real part\nThree of the four kernel classes throw on a complex multiplier, their type settling the question for free. A StoredKernel cannot: its storage is Complex{T} by construction, so a materialized spectral_cutoff and a materialized translation_kernel have the same type. Telling them apart needs a full extra pass over the coefficients, which this reduction's single pass does not permit — so that class keeps the real-part behaviour, and a complex weight reaching it returns a number rather than raising. It is the one route by which a phase kernel can serve as a weight and answer a question you did not ask.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.weighted_inner"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.l2norm","page":"Reductions","text":"l2norm(f)\n\nThe normalized L² norm lVert frVert_2 of a section (l2norm, lVertcdotrVert_2) — the root-mean-square of the fiber magnitude f over the base, and the p = 2 case of lpnorm. It is sqrtlangle f frangle built on inner, so it inherits both bases: the grid cell-volume average and the point-set counting average.\n\nbeginaligned\nlVert frVert_2 = sqrtlangle f frangle = sqrtfrac1N sum_x f(x)^2  (textttGrid3D) \nlVert frVert_2 = sqrtfrac1M sum_p f_p^2  (textttPointSet)\nendaligned\n\nIt is defined on fields in either representation and on point-values (the spectral case through the Parseval inner).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.l2norm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.lpnorm","page":"Reductions","text":"lpnorm(section, p = 2)\n\nThe normalized L^p norm lVert frVert_p of a section f, passed as section (lpnorm, lVertcdotrVert_p), where f is the pointwise fiber magnitude (the fiberwise norm) — the L^p average of f over the base. The exponent p selects the norm: p = 2 (the default) reduces to l2norm, and p = Inf gives the supremum of the fiber magnitude (for example the maximum speed entering a CFL time-step bound). On a grid the average is the equal-weight sum over the N cells; on a point-set it is the sum over the M points:\n\nbeginaligned\nlVert frVert_p = Bigl(frac1N sum_x f(x)^pBigr)^1p  (textttGrid3D) \nlVert frVert_p = Bigl(frac1M sum_textpoints f^pBigr)^1p  (textttPointSet) \nlVert frVert_infty = sup_x f(x) = max_x f(x) \nendaligned\n\nIt is physical-space only at every exponent, p = 2 included; for a field in spectral representation use l2norm, which reads the same value off the coefficients through Parseval.\n\nThe exponent p must satisfy 1 le p le infty; outside that range the expression is not a norm and the call raises rather than returning a plausible number. The result carries the section's own element type — a Float32 section gives a Float32 norm at every exponent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.lpnorm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#Statistics.cov","page":"Reductions","text":"cov(u; corrected = true)\n\nThe covariance tensor R_ij = langle u_i u_jrangle of a vector section over its base, where u = u - bar u is the fluctuation about the base mean — the coordinate-free second central moment of a vector-valued field or point set, the vector analogue of the scalar var. It returns one symmetric FiberTensor2: the diagonal holds the per-component variances (the normal stresses, in a turbulence reading), the off-diagonal the component covariances (the shear stresses) that a per-component variance would discard.\n\nUse this for the second moment of a vector field. The var/std pair is deliberately scalar-only: a per-component var would be only the basis-dependent diagonal of this tensor, and operatornamestd = sqrtoperatornamevar has no canonical vector form, so the covariance tensor is the honest object. The mean bar u is subtracted first, so this is the covariance of the fluctuations, not the raw second moment langle u_i u_jrangle.\n\nArguments\n\nu — the vector section whose fluctuations the covariance tensor is built from.\ncorrected — divide by the base count minus one (the sample convention, the default, matching Statistics.cov); false divides by the count (the population covariance).\n\nIt is physical-space only, since the outer product u otimes u is nonlinear. It is computed in one mapped pass as textttmean(u otimes u) - bar u otimes bar u (Bessel-scaled by the base count minus one, as var is), over either base's measure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Statistics.cov"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#Statistics.mean","page":"Reductions","text":"mean(f)\nmean(fiber_map, f)\n\nThe arithmetic average of a section over its base, fiber-preserving: on a grid the equal-weight cell average tfrac1Nsum_x f(x) over the N cells, on a point-set the sample mean tfrac1Msum_p f_p over the M points — a vector field's mean is one FiberVector, a second-order tensor field's one FiberTensor2. The mapped form mean(fiber_map, f) averages (textttfiber_map circ f)(x) (for example mean(det, A)) in one fused pass with no intermediate section; the map is nonlinear in general, so the mapped forms exist on fields in physical representation and point-values only. On a field in spectral representation the mean is read from the zero (DC) Fourier mode in O(1), equal to the physical value with no transform. See integral for the extensive (measure-weighted) sibling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Statistics.mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#Statistics.std","page":"Reductions","text":"std(f; corrected = true)\n\nThe sample standard deviation of a scalar section: the square root of var, over the same bases var serves — a Grid3D or a PointSet — and, for a field, in spectral representation as well. Point values are representation-free, so that last clause reaches fields alone. Like var it is scalar-only — operatornamestd = sqrtoperatornamevar has no canonical form for a vector (the matrix square root of the covariance is not a standard deviation); use cov for the vector second central moment.\n\nArguments\n\nf — the scalar section the moment is taken over: a field in physical or spectral representation, or point values.\ncorrected — divide by the base count minus one (Bessel's correction, the default) rather than by the count, exactly as it does for var, of which this is the square root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Statistics.std"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#Statistics.var","page":"Reductions","text":"var(f; corrected = true)\n\nThe sample variance of a scalar section over its base: the Bessel-corrected mean (dividing by one less than the count, the standard correction that makes the sample variance an unbiased estimate of the population one) of the squared deviation from the average, tfrac1N-1sum_x (f(x) - langle frangle)^2 over a grid's N cells and tfrac1M-1sum_p over a point set's M points (matching Statistics.var's default correction).\n\nBoth var and std are scalar-only: the sample variance is unambiguous only for a scalar section. A vector field's coordinate-free second moment is the covariance tensor cov; a per-component variance would be only its basis-dependent diagonal, so it is not offered — calling var/std on a vector or tensor section raises a clear error pointing to cov.\n\nIn physical representation it runs as two fused passes — the mean, then the squared deviations — with no intermediate deviation section (the numerically stable form). In spectral representation it is read from Parseval: the population moment is langle f frangle - langle frangle^2 = N^-2sum_textstored  k ne 0 alpha(k) hat f_k^2 — the fluctuation energy, with N the number of grid cells, alpha(k) the half-spectrum multiplicity of mode k, and the N^-2 the normalization the inner product itself carries — and the default corrected = true multiplies it by N(N-1). Either way it equals the physical value with no inverse transform; that one-pass form loses precision to cancellation when the mean dominates the fluctuations, so prefer the physical form there.\n\nArguments\n\nf — the scalar section the moment is taken over: a field in physical or spectral representation, or point values.\ncorrected — divide by the count minus one (the sample convention, the default, matching Statistics.var) or by the count itself. A grid holds the complete population of a field rather than a sample of it, so corrected = false is often what a field diagnostic wants; the default follows the standard library rather than the base.\n\nSee std for its square root, cov for the vector second moment, and mean for the first moment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Statistics.var"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#Base.extrema","page":"Reductions","text":"extrema(f)\nextrema(fiber_map, f)\n\nThe pair (minimum(f), maximum(f)) of a scalar section over its base, in one call — and with a leading fiber map, the pair (minimum(fiber_map, f), maximum(fiber_map, f)), which is what makes the order statistics well posed above tensor order 0. See maximum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.extrema"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#Base.maximum","page":"Reductions","text":"maximum(f)\nmaximum(fiber_map, f)\n\nThe largest value of a scalar section over its base — a grid's cells or a point-set's points. The bare form exists only at tensor order zero: the maximum of a vector or tensor section is ill posed (which component?), and naming a fiber map in the mapped form maximum(fiber_map, f) is what makes it well posed — maximum(norm, u) takes the largest fiber magnitude in one fused pass with no intermediate section. It takes physical fields and point-values only. The materializing route to the same supremum is lpnorm(f, Inf). An order statistic has no neutral element, so reducing an empty section (a zero-point set) throws an ArgumentError, as for an empty collection in Base, rather than fabricating a value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.maximum"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#Base.minimum","page":"Reductions","text":"minimum(f)\nminimum(fiber_map, f)\n\nThe smallest value of a scalar section over its base — the mirror of maximum; see there for the bases, the scalar-only restriction, and the mapped form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.minimum"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.radial_spectrum","page":"Reductions","text":"radial_spectrum(f, multipliers)\nradial_spectrum(f, g, multipliers)\n\nThe radial (shell-binned) power spectrum resolves a field's L² energy over wavenumber-magnitude shells. Write P(kappa) for the one-argument power spectrum and P_fg(kappa) for the two-argument co-spectrum (cross-power) of two compatible, equal-order fields.\n\nArguments\n\nf, g — the fields, in spectral representation; the two-argument form takes them compatible and of equal tensor order.\nmultipliers — the per-axis wavenumbers k for the field's grid (FieldSpectralMultipliers).\n\nIt is spectral-only, a Parseval reduction like the spectral inner.\n\nIt returns (shells, spectrum), a pair of Vector{T} in the section's own scalar type and on the host whatever backend the field lives on. Here shells are the bin-center wavenumber magnitudes kappa, and spectrum[m + 1] sums the Parseval-weighted fiber (co-)energy over the modes in shell m, whose centre kappa_m is shells[m + 1] (vectors count from 1, shells from 0):\n\nbegingathered\nP(kappa_m) = frac1N^2 sum_substackk    mathrmround(kDelta k) = m alpha(k) sum_c hat f_c(k)^2 \nP_fg(kappa_m) = frac1N^2 sum_substackk    mathrmround(kDelta k) = m alpha(k) sum_c mathrmRebigl(hat f_c(k) overlinehat g_c(k)bigr)\nendgathered\n\nwith c indexing the fiber components, alpha(k) the Parseval weight (1 on the folded axis's zero and even-length Nyquist planes, 2 otherwise), shell width Delta k (the box fundamental), and the prefactor the inverse square of the total point count. Each form refines inner: sum_kappa P(kappa) = langle f frangle and sum_kappa P_fg(kappa) = langle f grangle — the L² (co-)energy split across isotropic k-shells one fundamental wavenumber wide (the box's smallest nonzero wavenumber, Delta k = 2pi  max_a L_a). The one-argument form is the g = f case (mathrmRe(hat f overlinehat f) = hat f^2); the co-spectrum is symmetric (P_fg = P_gf, mathrmRe being conjugation-symmetric) and, unlike the spectrum, may be negative in a shell (anticorrelated modes). In physical terms the kinetic-energy spectrum is E(kappa) = tfrac12 P(kappa) (integrating to the turbulent kinetic energy, half the L² energy); the package keeps the neutral name and leaves the physics to the caller.\n\nThe shells are CENTRED on their nominal wavenumber — shell m collects lvert krvert within pmDelta k2 of mDelta k — which is what makes kappa_m an energy-density sample AT kappa_m rather than at the band's edge. The abscissa is a property of the grid alone, identical for every field on it, so two spectra are directly comparable and a co-spectrum (which may be negative) is handled by the same rule.\n\nShells run from 0 to the corner wavenumber, so the sum covers all modes (an exact inner refinement); on a de-aliased field high-k shells are empty. On a non-cubic box shells beyond the inscribed sphere of the sampled band — shell index min_a k_a^mathrmNyq  Delta k, with k_a^mathrmNyq = lfloor N_a2 rfloor 2piL_a each axis's Nyquist wavenumber from its point count and length — are sampled by corner modes only; the total still equals inner. The shell width carries a second anisotropy artefact worth knowing: Delta k is taken from the box's largest side, so on a strongly anisotropic box it is finer than the mode spacing 2piL_a along every shorter axis, and a systematic fraction of shells is then empty — the profile combs, though its sum is unaffected. The two shells meet at a half-integer kDelta k, which an anisotropic box hits exactly; such a mode is assigned by round's ties-to-even, so the boundary belongs to neither shell by a fixed convention but to whichever has even index. The interval is deliberately not written half-open for that reason — a half-open bracket would claim round-half-up, which is not what the code does. The reduction is GPU-portable: a single scatter-add binning pass over the modes that derives each mode's shell, fiber contraction, and Parseval weight from its index — no full-grid staging array is built — costing more than a plain reduction like inner only by the binning — whose accumulators take atomic (concurrency-safe) updates, since several modes land in one shell — and a square root per mode.\n\nnote: For an exact spectral moment, do not weight the abscissa\nReporting each shell's energy-weighted mean wavenumber would make the first moment sum_m kappa_m E(kappa_m) exact, but it is the wrong tool: the abscissa would then depend on the field, so two spectra on one grid would arrive on different kappa grids, and a co-spectrum would divide by a weight that can vanish or change sign. Take the moment directly instead — weighted_inner against a radial symbol is exact and involves no binning at all:# ⟨f, |k| f⟩, exactly\nweighted_inner(f, radial_kernel(k2 -> sqrt(k2), multipliers))\n# ⟨f, −Δf⟩, the Dirichlet energy\nweighted_inner(f, radial_kernel(identity, multipliers))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.radial_spectrum"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.radial_correlation","page":"Reductions","text":"radial_correlation(f, plans)\nradial_correlation(f, g, plans)\n\nThe radial (shell-averaged) two-point correlation of a field is the physical-space dual of radial_spectrum under the Wiener–Khinchin theorem — the statement that the autocorrelation and the power spectrum are a Fourier-transform pair. The one-argument form is the autocorrelation C(r), and the two-argument form is the cross-correlation C_fg(r) = langle f(x)cdot g(x+r)rangle of two compatible, equal-order fields.\n\nArguments\n\nf, g — the fields, in spectral representation; the two-argument form takes them compatible and of equal tensor order.\nplans — transform plans for their grid and layout; a pair carries no tensor order and serves every rank.\n\nIt takes plans where its Fourier dual radial_spectrum takes multipliers, the one asymmetry in an otherwise mirrored pair: the spectrum weighs each mode where it already sits, while the correlation must come back to physical space, and an inverse transform needs a plan.\n\nIt returns (separations, correlation), a pair of host Vector{T} in the section's scalar type, as radial_spectrum does, where separations are the bin-center magnitudes r and correlation[i] is the value averaged over the minimum-image shell (each separation taken as its nearest periodic replica, so no distance exceeds half the box) r approx r. By the (cross-)correlation theorem the kernel forms the fiber-summed (cross-)power spectrum, inverse-transforms it, and normalizes:\n\nbegingathered\nC(r) = frac1N sum_x f(x)cdot f(x+r) = frac1N mathcal F^-1Bigl(textstylesum_c hat f_c^2Bigr)(r) \nC_fg(r) = frac1N sum_x f(x)cdot g(x+r) = frac1N mathcal F^-1Bigl(textstylesum_c overlinehat f_c hat g_cBigr)(r)\nendgathered\n\nwith c indexing the fiber components, followed by the shell average C(r_i) = textshell  i^-1 sum_r approx r_i C(r). At zero separation each is inner: C(0) = langle f^2rangle = textttinner(f f) and C_fg(0) = langle fcdot grangle = textttinner(f g) — inner resolved over physical separation, exactly as radial_spectrum resolves it over k (a Fourier pair up to the convention's 1N: mathcal F C = hat f^2N, mathcal F C_fg = overlinehat f hat gN). The one-argument form is the g = f case; the radial binning symmetrizes the cross-correlation — the pm r folding of the min-image shells averages C_fg(r) with C_fg(-r) = C_gf(r), so the result equals tfrac12(C_fg + C_gf) and is symmetric in f g.\n\nSeparations run from 0 to the minimum-image corner sqrtsum_a r_a^2, with each axis's reach, from its point count and spacing, r_a = lfloor N_a2rfloor Delta x_a (= L_a2 on an even axis, half a cell less on an odd one; sqrt3 L2 for an even cubic box) at the finest spacing min_a Delta x_a, the min(i N_a - i) fold making C periodic and even (C(r) = C(L - r)). This is the physical-space mirror of the spectrum's corner caveat: only r le min_a r_a — the sphere inscribed in the min-image box — is sampled in all directions; shells from there out to the corner keep only the box-diagonal directions. And in a finite periodic box the large-r tail is itself an artifact — there C reflects the field correlating with its own periodic image — so a caller extracts an integral scale (the area under the normalized correlation curve) only out to where C has decayed (e.g. the first zero crossing), not the full curve. As with the spectrum the total is unaffected: C(0) = textttinner(f f) exactly.\n\nIt takes spectral input only, since it needs the inverse transform, and it is GPU-portable: one inverse transform plus a single binning pass.\n\nnote: This one allocates a field, unlike `radial_spectrum`\nIts route builds a full power field and takes an allocating inverse transform, so it costs a field's worth of memory per call — a deliberate exception to the package's allocation-free default, guarded by a bounded rather than a zero assertion; radial_spectrum has no such cost, its binning kernel allocating only the shell accumulators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions.radial_correlation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiber_at-Union{Tuple{NC}, Tuple{Any, Any, Val{NC}}} where NC","page":"Reductions","text":"_fiber_at(components, index, ::Val{NC})\n\nGather the NC component values of one base point into a tuple, by linear index. The component arrays are the contiguous per-component slices of a fiber-last section, so each is a unit-stride stream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiber_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiber_loop_reduce-Union{Tuple{NC}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{Combine}, Tuple{FiberMap}, Tuple{FiberMap, Combine, Any, Type{V}, Val{NC}, Int64, Int64}} where {FiberMap, Combine, V, NC}","page":"Reductions","text":"_fiber_loop_reduce(fiber_map, combine, components, V, ::Val{NC}, lo, hi)\n\nReduce base points lo:hi on the CPU, assembling each fiber value of type V from components, applying fiber_map, and combining with combine. The recursion splits until a leaf of _FIBER_REDUCTION_BLOCK points, which runs as one vectorized loop.\n\nThe shape is Base's own mapreduce blocking, reproduced here because the generic path does not apply to a fused fiber gather: reducing the equivalent lazy Broadcasted forgoes the vectorized loop and runs several times slower, and the sequential alternative would give up pairwise summation's O(log n) error growth for O(n).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiber_loop_reduce"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiber_mapreduce-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Combine}, Tuple{FiberMap}, Tuple{FiberMap, Combine, Union{AbstractFieldPhysical{T, R}, AbstractPointValues{T, R}}}} where {FiberMap, Combine, T, R}","page":"Reductions","text":"_fiber_mapreduce(fiber_map, combine, section)\n\nReduce with combine over the base, applying fiber_map to the fiber value at each base point: the scalar itself for tensor order 0, the FiberVector for 1, the FiberTensor2 for 2. This backs the mapped forms of the reductions, so a nonlinear fiber invariant is reduced without materializing it as a section — the mapped value exists only per base point, in registers.\n\nThe fiber is gathered from the section's per-component arrays, because the fiber-last layout makes each component a contiguous slice but places the components of one base point a whole base apart, so a fiber cannot be reinterpreted into a StaticArray in place. How those components are then reduced is chosen per backend by _reduce_fiber — a vectorized blocked loop on a CPU, one fused pass over a lazy Broadcasted on a GPU — since the two reward opposite things.\n\nOne spelling to avoid either way: the multi-argument mapreduce(fiber_map, combine, components...) is fused by GPUArrays but falls back on the CPU to a route that materializes a full section-sized array.\n\nThe fiber_map::FiberMap type parameter — here and on every public form that forwards to this one — is required, not decoration. Julia declines to specialize on an argument of Function type that a method only passes along instead of calling, so without it the map arrives boxed and the reduction allocates on every call; naming its type in the signature forces the specialization that keeps the whole family allocation-free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiber_mapreduce"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiber_tree_reduce-Union{Tuple{NC}, Tuple{V}, Tuple{Combine}, Tuple{FiberMap}, Tuple{FiberMap, Combine, Any, Type{V}, Val{NC}, Int64, Int64, Int64}} where {FiberMap, Combine, V, NC}","page":"Reductions","text":"_fiber_tree_reduce(fiber_map, combine, components, V, ::Val{NC}, lo, hi, depth)\n\nThreaded form of _fiber_loop_reduce with the identical reduction tree: it splits at the same midpoints the serial recursion uses and combines left-before-right, evaluating the top depth levels as spawned tasks and every subtree below as the untouched serial recursion — so the result is bitwise-identical to serial at every thread count (the tree is a function of the element count alone, never of the schedule).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiber_tree_reduce"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiberwise-Union{Tuple{R}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractFiberSection{T, R}, Any, Any}} where {T, R}","page":"Reductions","text":"_fiberwise(section, whole_reduce, over_base_reduce)\n\nApply a reduction that keeps the fiber and integrates the base away, returning a fiber value: a scalar for tensor order 0, a FiberVector for 1, a FiberTensor2 for 2. The whole_reduce(data) helper is used for the scalar case (reduce the whole array); over_base_reduce(data, base_dims) reduces only the leading base dimensions for higher orders. The (tiny) reduced array is copied to the host before the SVector/SMatrix build, so no device array is scalar-indexed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._fiberwise"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._indexed_leaf_reduce-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{E}, Tuple{E, C, Int64, Int64}} where {E, C}","page":"Reductions","text":"_indexed_leaf_reduce(element, combine, lo, hi)\n\nReduce element(i) over i in lo:hi with combine, as one vectorized loop below the pairwise block size and by the fixed midpoint recursion above it — the array-kernel sibling of _fiber_loop_reduce, sharing its tree so accuracy and determinism carry over.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._indexed_leaf_reduce"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._indexed_tree_reduce-Union{Tuple{C}, Tuple{E}, Tuple{E, C, Int64, Int64}} where {E, C}","page":"Reductions","text":"_indexed_tree_reduce(element, combine, lo, hi)\n\nReduce element(i) over i in lo:hi, spawning the top _spawn_depth levels of the fixed midpoint tree when _use_threads allows and running _indexed_leaf_reduce below — the same tree at every thread count, so the result is bitwise thread-count-invariant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._indexed_tree_reduce"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._reduce_fiber","page":"Reductions","text":"_reduce_fiber(backend, fiber_map, combine, components, V, ::Val{NC})\n\nReduce a section's fiber values on backend, gathering each fiber of type V from its NC component arrays. The two backends take different routes because they reward opposite things: a CPU wants the vectorized blocked loop of _fiber_loop_reduce, while a GPU has no such loop to lose and is served by one fused pass over a lazy Broadcasted, which also spares it a section write and read-back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._reduce_fiber"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._spawn_depth-Tuple{}","page":"Reductions","text":"_spawn_depth()\n\nNumber of top tree levels _fiber_tree_reduce and _indexed_tree_reduce evaluate as spawned tasks: enough binary splits to give every thread work, plus one level of slack for load balancing. Zero when a single thread is available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._spawn_depth"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._tree_dims_reduce-Union{Tuple{B}, Tuple{F}, Tuple{F, Any, NTuple{B, Any}}} where {F, B}","page":"Reductions","text":"_tree_dims_reduce(scalar_reduce, data, base_count)\n\nApply scalar_reduce (a whole-array tree reduction) to each fiber component of data — the component slices over the leading base_count base axes are contiguous, so each reduces linearly — returning the array of per-component results a dims-style Base call gives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._tree_dims_reduce"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._tree_sum-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reductions","text":"_tree_sum(data)\n\nSum a CPU array (or contiguous view) through _indexed_tree_reduce's fixed pairwise tree — threaded above the gate, bitwise thread-count-invariant, and with no accumulator-typing peek (the first element seeds the accumulator inside the tree itself).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._tree_sum"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Reductions/#GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._tree_var-Tuple{Any}","page":"Reductions","text":"_tree_var(data; corrected)\n\nVariance through two fixed-tree passes — the mean, then the summed squared deviation — matching the corrected convention of Statistics.var.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Reductions._tree_var"},{"category":"section","location":"api/GBGPeriodicFields/#GBGPeriodicFields.jl","page":"GBGPeriodicFields","text":"","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/GBGPeriodicFields/#Re-exported-modules","page":"GBGPeriodicFields","text":"The top-level module includes and re-exports the public interface of:\n\nGBGPeriodicFields.FiberSections\nGBGPeriodicFields.Fields\nGBGPeriodicFields.FieldAlgebra\nGBGPeriodicFields.DifferentialOperators\nGBGPeriodicFields.Convolution\nGBGPeriodicFields.Points\nGBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation\nGBGPeriodicFields.Reductions","title":"Re-exported modules"},{"category":"module","location":"api/GBGPeriodicFields/#GBGPeriodicFields.GBGPeriodicFields","page":"GBGPeriodicFields","text":"GBGPeriodicFields\n\nGBGPeriodicFields carries real-valued scalar, vector and second-order-tensor fields on three-dimensional rectangular periodic domains, in physical-space and real-to-complex spectral representations, on CPU and GPU backends, with HDF5 field persistence.\n\nThe tensors are Cartesian — components on one fixed right-handed orthonormal frame, with no curvilinear coordinates or metric machinery — so this is an applied field toolkit rather than a general differential-geometry package.\n\nThese words recur in every operation's documentation:\n\na base is the set of locations a value is attached to: a Grid3D of nodes, or a PointSet of off-grid positions. A grid cell carries the quadrature weight a reduction integrates against; the values themselves sit on the nodes.\na fiber is the value at one such location — a scalar, a FiberVector, or a FiberTensor2.\na section puts the two together: it assigns one fiber to every point of a base, which is what a field and a set of point-values each are, and why one set of formulas serves both.\nthe resolved band is the set of wavenumbers a grid's resolution actually represents, excluding the Nyquist frequency of each even-length axis. Those planes are excluded because there the wavenumbers used for first derivatives are zeroed while the squared ones used for the Laplacian keep the true value — so every identity scoped \"on the resolved band\" holds off them and may fail on them. An odd-length axis has no exact Nyquist mode and so loses nothing.\na field is band-limited when its Fourier content vanishes above the wavenumbers the grid stores, so the finite trigonometric series those coefficients define represents it exactly. A product of two such fields is not band-limited, which is why the operations that form one are offered in physical representation only.\na section's scalar type is its real precision — the T <: AbstractFloat every field and point-value type is parameterized on, Float64 or Float32, read back by field_scalar_type. Its element type is what the storage array actually holds, and the two part company in spectral representation: physical storage holds T, spectral storage holds Complex{T} at the same scalar type. Precision is the scalar type; the complex storage is representation detail, so a field in spectral representation is no less a Float64 field.\na section's storage family is the kind of array its data lives in — Array on the host, CuArray or ROCArray on a device — taken as the array's underlying type rather than its wrapping, so a reshape of an Array is still the Array family. Two sections combine only within one storage family and one element type; a mixed pair is refused rather than converted, and adapt moves one explicitly — a field, the wavenumber multipliers and convolution kernels built for it, its transform plans, a point set or point values — into another family, never changing the element type.\n\nThe package is the field layer for pseudospectral fluid and turbulence solvers, and it carries a point layer that evaluates fields between grid nodes for Lagrangian-particle methods. A field is one real physical object; the physical and spectral forms are two representations of that same object.\n\nThis top-level module assembles its public interface from the submodules below. Each module's own docstring maps what it provides, so this list says what each is for rather than repeating it:\n\nFiberSections — the base-agnostic foundation both layers share: the fiber types FiberVector and FiberTensor2/FiberTensor3/FiberTensor4, which name the value at one base point and so belong to neither base in particular.\nFields — the field and grid types, the two representations and the transforms between them, allocation, broadcasting, HDF5 persistence, and the storage-family move adapt.\nFieldAlgebra — the fiberwise algebra on fields, including map!/map for any fiber map it does not name.\nDifferentialOperators — the differential operators, applied as spectral multipliers.\nConvolution — periodic convolution against a kernel, and the kernel builders. A kernel is its own kind of object rather than a field.\nPoints — the unstructured base: point-data types, their fiberwise algebra mirroring the field surface, spatial motion, and HDF5 persistence.\nInterpolation — the grid↔point transfer, interpolate and anterpolate as an adjoint pair, under a choice of scheme.\nReductions — the L²/measure reductions over fields and point-values, against the base's own measure.\n\nnote: Scalar, vector and second-order tensors only\nSections of tensor order R = 0 1 2 — scalar, vector and second-order tensor — are the supported surface: no operation reaches past it, though not all span all three. Some are defined at one order by their own mathematics — det and tr need a matrix, cov a vector, std, var and the bare maximum/minimum/extrema a scalar — and each says so in its own docstring. The field and point-value types are parameterized on R, so higher orders can be constructed and several layers serve them (storage, HDF5 persistence, the vector-space operations, inner, l2norm, and spline interpolation between grid and points). Others do not: the reductions returning a fiber or taking a fiber map (mean(det, A)), map, and sym/skew/dev are defined for R le 2 and throw above it.General R was deferred rather than declined, to ship a usable field layer for the second-order surface a pseudospectral solver actually needs. Above the ceiling an operation either works or raises — none returns a wrong answer — and the test suite checks that.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.GBGPeriodicFields"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Interpolation/#Interpolation","page":"Interpolation","text":"","title":"Interpolation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Interpolation/#Transfer-operations","page":"Interpolation","text":"","title":"Transfer operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Interpolation/#Traversal-order","page":"Interpolation","text":"An order is an option on the transfer operations above, not an operation on the sections themselves: it changes the sequence in which the points are visited and never the result.","title":"Traversal order"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Interpolation/#Transfer-scheme-types","page":"Interpolation","text":"","title":"Transfer-scheme types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Interpolation/#Scheme-persistence","page":"Interpolation","text":"","title":"Scheme persistence"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Interpolation/#Internal-API","page":"Interpolation","text":"These symbols are not part of the public interface; they are documented for contributors — the scheme supertype AbstractSplineScheme, the spline-weight generators (Fornberg finite differences plus a small linear solve), the periodic cell-finding, and the compatibility checks the transfer verbs route through.","title":"Internal API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Interpolation/#Types","page":"Interpolation","text":"","title":"Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Interpolation/#Functions","page":"Interpolation","text":"","title":"Functions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation","page":"Interpolation","text":"Interpolation\n\nTransfer between the grid sections of Fields and the point sections of Points: interpolation evaluates a field at off-grid positions, and its adjoint anterpolation deposits per-point quantities back onto the grid. A PointSet holds the positions and an AbstractPointValues the per-point values.\n\nInterpolation (interpolate) is the gather W, the linear map that evaluates a field f at the points: its value (Wf)_p = f(x_p) at the p-th point is f at that point's position x_p. Anterpolation (anterpolate) is the scatter W^top, the transpose of W, its adjoint for the unnormalized sums: it spreads per-point quantities q (with q_p the value carried at x_p) back onto the surrounding grid nodes with stencil weights, returning a whole field whose value (W^top q)(x) is what it deposits at grid position x. The two are exact transposes — pairing the gathered values against q over the M points equals pairing f against the scattered field over the N grid cells. Written with the normalized inner product langlecdotcdotrangle (inner, the fiber contraction averaged over its base), the factors M and N undo its 1M and 1N:\n\nbeginaligned\nM langle Wf q rangle_mathrmpts = sum_p (Wf)_p cdot q_p \n= sum_x f(x) cdot (W^top q)(x) = N langle f W^top q rangle_mathrmgrid\nendaligned\n\nThe middle sum, over the N grid nodes, is the physical-representation form. For the spectral schemes, whose scatter is the conjugate transpose, the same identity holds with the mode-wise pairing, one number by Parseval — which is why inner on a field in spectral representation returns the physical value.\n\nHere the subscripts name the two mean-normalized inner products — over the M sample points and over the N grid nodes. This holds because each scheme's scatter applies its own gather's transpose — the same stencil weights for the local splines, the conjugate Fourier sum for the spectral pair. Because those weights sum to one (a partition of unity), anterpolation conserves the total it deposits — the property that coupling a quantity back onto the flow needs. That is a property of the local-spline schemes, whose weights sum to one at every position, and it holds for DirectDFT too, by a different route: a constant is band-limited, so the exact spectral gather reproduces it exactly, which makes the equivalent nodal weights sum to one; and the deposited zero mode is exactly sum_p q_p, which is the conservation statement. Only NUFFT falls short, and only by its tolerance. Conservation holds only when both directions use the same weights; mismatched weights inject a spurious source (Sundaram & Collins 1996). The pair is symmetric in name and in adjointness, and asymmetric in three places, listed together because each is easy to meet as a surprise: a gather yields point values while its adjoint returns a field; the alpha/beta accumulation weights are taken by the scatter alone; and only the gather carries the callable sugar f(x), evaluation being pointwise where its adjoint is not. Each scheme's own docstring gives the gather and scatter weights it applies.\n\nA transfer scheme (an AbstractTransferScheme) fixes the weights and the representation it acts on. Two families ship: the physical-domain local splines of Lalescu, Teaca & Carati (2010) (the default CatmullRom), which act on a field in physical representation, and the spectral-domain exact DirectDFT / fast NUFFT that act on a field in spectral representation. The point-value data types and their fiberwise algebra live in the Points module.\n\nThe parts are:\n\nInterpolation (grid → point, gather; interpolate, W): interpolate and the in-place interpolate!, plus the callable f(x) (and its broadcast f.(xs)) as the ergonomic face of a single-point gather, which the physical-domain spline schemes carry and the spectral ones do not.\nAnterpolation (point → grid, scatter; anterpolate, W^top): anterpolate and the in-place anterpolate!, the latter with an alpha/beta accumulate form (beta = 0 overwrite, beta ne 0 accumulate, the mul! convention; accumulation is the common case for the scatter).\nTraversal order. point_traversal_order builds a PointTraversalOrder, a permutation saying which point each work item of a transfer handles, so that neighbouring work touches neighbouring memory. It is an option on the four verbs above and never changes their result. Points move, and an order goes stale as they do, so point_traversal_order! rebuilds one in place against a PointTraversalOrderScratch, allocating nothing that scales with the point count.\nScheme persistence. write_scheme and read_scheme map a scheme to and from one self-describing HDF5 group, so a checkpoint restores not only where a point set samples but how (methods load with the HDF5 package, as a weak-dependency extension). A scheme is a parameter record rather than a section, so the group carries attributes and no dataset: the weights are derived and recomputed on read. NUFFT is excluded by design — it owns plans built against a reference field, so it is rebuilt rather than stored.\n\nReferences\n\nC. C. Lalescu, B. Teaca, D. Carati, Implementation of high order spline interpolations for tracking test particles in discretized fields, J. Comput. Phys. 229(17) (2010) 5862–5869.\nS. Sundaram, L. R. Collins, Numerical considerations in simulating a turbulent suspension of finite-volume particles, J. Comput. Phys. 124(2) (1996) 337–350.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.interpolate!","page":"Interpolation","text":"interpolate!(out::AbstractPointValues, field::AbstractFieldPhysical,\n             points::PointSet, scheme::AbstractSplineScheme = CatmullRom;\n             traversal = nothing)\ninterpolate!(out::AbstractPointValues, field::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n             points::PointSet, scheme::DirectDFT)\ninterpolate!(out::AbstractPointValues, field::AbstractFieldSpectral,\n             points::PointSet, scheme::NUFFT; traversal = nothing)\n\nInterpolate field to the off-grid points, writing the gathered values into out; returns out. This is the in-place home of the gather W, and for a single position there is the callable field(x). The Interpolation module docstring explains the pair.\n\nUse this in-place form when out is already allocated; the allocating interpolate returns a fresh result instead.\n\nArguments\n\nout — the point-value section written with the gathered values. It must match field in tensor order and cover the same number of points as points. The local-spline transfer serves any tensor order the field types hold, order 3 included.\nfield — the field to sample, in the representation the scheme acts on.\npoints — the PointSet of positions to evaluate at.\nscheme — an AbstractTransferScheme, selecting the transfer kernel and the representation it acts on. The physical-domain local splines (CatmullRom and the other LalescuSpline / LagrangeSpline) gather the nodal values of a physical-space field over a compact stencil, while DirectDFT and NUFFT evaluate a field in spectral representation in the Fourier basis; each scheme's own docstring gives its weights.\ntraversal — an optional traversal order from point_traversal_order: a permutation of the point indices saying which point each work item handles. It changes only the sequence in which points are visited — the gather is bitwise unchanged — and is worth several times the throughput when the points are in an order unrelated to the grid. It must name every point exactly once and live in the same storage family as the field; nothing, the default, visits them in index order.\n\nThe spline schemes are exact at grid nodes and for polynomials up to the scheme's reproduction degree and approximate otherwise; the spectral schemes reconstruct the band-limited field exactly (DirectDFT) or to a set tolerance (NUFFT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.interpolate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.interpolate","page":"Interpolation","text":"interpolate(field::AbstractFieldPhysical, points, scheme = CatmullRom;\n            traversal = nothing)\ninterpolate(field::AbstractFieldSpectral, points, scheme::DirectDFT)\ninterpolate(field::AbstractFieldSpectral, points, scheme::NUFFT;\n            traversal = nothing)\n\nAllocating form of interpolate!: allocate a new AbstractPointValues of field's tensor order and return field sampled at points. See interpolate! for the schemes and their accuracy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.interpolate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldPhysical-Tuple{Any}","page":"Interpolation","text":"(field::AbstractFieldPhysical)(x; scheme = CatmullRom)\n\nEvaluate field at a single off-grid position x (a length-3 indexable), returning a scalar, an SVector{3}, or an SMatrix{3,3} for a scalar, vector, or second-order-tensor field. This is the ergonomic face of a one-point interpolate gather: it builds a one-point set and runs the spline kernel. For many points, call interpolate with a PointSet instead. The scheme keyword selects the transfer scheme, CatmullRom by default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Fields.AbstractFieldPhysical"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.anterpolate!","page":"Interpolation","text":"anterpolate!(field::AbstractFieldPhysical, points::PointSet,\n             quantities::AbstractPointValues,\n             scheme::AbstractSplineScheme = CatmullRom,\n             α::Real = true, β::Real = false; traversal = nothing)\nanterpolate!(field::AbstractFieldSpectral, points::PointSet,\n             quantities::AbstractPointValues, scheme::DirectDFT,\n             α::Real = true, β::Real = false)\nanterpolate!(field::AbstractFieldSpectral, points::PointSet,\n             quantities::AbstractPointValues, scheme::NUFFT,\n             α::Real = true, β::Real = false; traversal = nothing)\n\nDeposit (scatter) the per-point quantities onto field in place — the transpose of interpolate! under the same scheme, conjugated for a spectral one — computing textttfield = alpha (W^top q) + beta textttfield; returns field. This is the in-place home of the scatter W^top.\n\nUse this in-place form when field is already allocated; the allocating anterpolate returns a fresh result instead.\n\nBecause it applies the same weights as the matching gather, anterpolation is the exact transpose; the Interpolation module docstring explains the pair. For a local-spline scheme the weights also sum to exactly one at any position (a partition of unity), so the total deposited quantity is conserved. Other communities name this operation spreading (immersed-boundary methods), deposition or charge assignment (particle-in-cell), projection (PSI-Cell, particle-source-in-cell), or gridding (nonuniform FFTs); anterpolation follows Brandt & Lubrecht (1990).\n\nArguments\n\nfield — the field deposited onto, in the representation the scheme acts on. It shares its tensor order with quantities.\npoints — the PointSet the quantities sit at, covering as many points as quantities.\nquantities — the per-point values to deposit.\nscheme — an AbstractTransferScheme, selecting the transfer kernel and the representation: the physical-domain local splines (CatmullRom and the other LalescuSpline / LagrangeSpline) scatter onto a physical-space field, while DirectDFT and NUFFT deposit into a field in spectral representation; each scheme's own docstring gives the weights.\nα — scales the deposited contribution.\nβ — sets the accumulation: beta = 0 (default) overwrites field, beta ne 0 accumulates onto it (the mul! convention; accumulation is the common case for a two-way-coupling scatter — a deposit that accumulates onto a field the same points also sample).\ntraversal — an optional traversal order from point_traversal_order: a permutation of the point indices saying which point each work item handles. It changes only the sequence in which points are deposited, and since the accumulation is atomic its summation order was never fixed, so the result moves by no more than it already does between runs. It must name every point exactly once and live in the same storage family as the field; nothing, the default, visits them in index order.\n\nReferences\n\nA. Brandt, A. A. Lubrecht, Multilevel matrix multiplication and fast solution of integral equations, J. Comput. Phys. 90(2) (1990) 348–370 (an early use of \"anterpolation\" for the adjoint of interpolation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.anterpolate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.anterpolate","page":"Interpolation","text":"anterpolate(grid::Grid3D, points, quantities, scheme = CatmullRom;\n            traversal = nothing)\nanterpolate(reference::AbstractFieldSpectral, points, quantities, scheme::DirectDFT)\nanterpolate(reference::AbstractFieldSpectral, points, quantities, scheme::NUFFT;\n            traversal = nothing)\n\nAllocating form of anterpolate!: allocate a new field of quantities' tensor order, deposit (scatter) the per-point quantities from the positions in points onto it, and return it. The spline schemes deposit onto a physical-space field on grid; the spectral schemes deposit into a field in spectral representation like reference (same grid and layout). See anterpolate! for the schemes and the alpha/beta accumulate form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.anterpolate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.PointTraversalOrder","page":"Interpolation","text":"PointTraversalOrder(indices)\n\nA PointTraversalOrder validates a traversal order: a permutation of 1:M, for M points, saying which point each unit of a transfer's work handles. Build one with point_traversal_order, or wrap your own permutation by calling this constructor, which checks it.\n\nAn order changes only the sequence in which points are handled, never the result, and it moves no data. Entry i names the point that the i-th unit of work takes, so the point set and any point-values indexed alongside it keep their own indexing, and every result lands in the slot it would have landed in anyway.\n\nThe constructor throws an ArgumentError unless indices names every point in 1:M exactly once. It accepts any integer vector, including a UnitRange such as 1:M, and keeps the storage family it is given, since a transfer will not mix families. Use adapt to move one between families.\n\nwarning: The guarantee is made once, at construction\nWriting through indices afterwards can leave a duplicate or an out-of-range entry that no later call re-checks, and a transfer will then leave some points unhandled and others handled twice. Treat the field as read-only, and rebuild with point_traversal_order! rather than editing in place.\n\nFields\n\nindices — the permutation itself, one point index per unit of work, in the storage family the constructor was given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.PointTraversalOrder"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.PointTraversalOrderScratch","page":"Interpolation","text":"PointTraversalOrderScratch(grid::Grid3D, points::PointSet; tile_size = 4)\n\nA PointTraversalOrderScratch holds storage for point_traversal_order!, so a rebuild allocates nothing that grows with the point count.\n\nBuild one alongside the order it will refill, and hold both for as long as the point set lives. It is storage and nothing else: it holds no order, no transfer ever reads it, and two orders over the same grid and point count can share one — though not at the same time, since a refill overwrites it.\n\nArguments\n\ngrid — the Grid3D the order will be built against. It is stored, and a refill against a different grid is refused.\npoints — a PointSet of the size and storage family the refills will use. Only its point count, element type and storage family are read.\ntile_size — the block edge in grid cells, which fixes how many blocks the grid divides into and therefore how large the counters below are. A refill uses this value rather than taking one of its own.\n\nwarning: It is sized for one point count and one block layout\nA refill checks the point count and the block layout and refuses a mismatch rather than reading past an array. A point set that grows, or a different tile_size, needs a new scratch.\n\nFields\n\nkeys — the block each point falls in, one entry per point.\ncounts — how many points fall in each block.\noffsets — the running total of counts, giving each block its starting slot.\nseen — one mark per point, for the permutation check.\ntiles — the number of blocks along each axis.\ntile_size — the block edge the scratch was built with.\ngrid — the grid it was built for, compared against the rebuild's on every refill.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.PointTraversalOrderScratch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.point_traversal_order!","page":"Interpolation","text":"point_traversal_order!(traversal::PointTraversalOrder, grid::Grid3D,\n                       points::PointSet, scratch::PointTraversalOrderScratch)\n\nRebuild traversal in place for the current positions of points, and return it: a permutation of 1:M, for a set of M points, that groups points lying in the same block of grid cells.\n\nPass it as the traversal keyword of interpolate!, anterpolate! or their allocating siblings. A transfer's cost is dominated by where its points read and write rather than by the arithmetic on them, and points usually arrive in an order unrelated to the grid — the order they were seeded in, say — so consecutive units of work touch unrelated memory. Following a traversal order makes them touch neighbouring memory instead. The result is unchanged either way.\n\nIt is not always a gain. Points already in a lattice or a sweep order are local already, and ordering them adds a build for no benefit. Measure the pair on your own point set before adopting it.\n\nUse this in-place form when traversal is already allocated; the allocating point_traversal_order returns a fresh result instead. The result is the one that form would have produced, up to the sequence of points within a block, which neither form fixes.\n\nReach for it when the points move. An order describes where they were when it was built, so it goes stale as they move — never wrong, only progressively less useful — and a caller that rebuilds it periodically would otherwise allocate about 15 MiB at 256^3 with 10^6 points on every rebuild. On a device that memory is freed by a collector which runs on host pressure and cannot see it, so it accumulates. Here the result is traversal's own storage and everything else is scratch, so nothing allocated per rebuild grows with the point count: about 1.2 KiB of KernelAbstractions launch bookkeeping, measured unchanged from 10^3 points to 10^6.\n\nArguments\n\ntraversal — the PointTraversalOrder to overwrite, whose length fixes the point count.\ngrid — the grid whose cells the blocks are cut from, and whose origin the points are measured from, which must be the grid the scratch was built for. Points lying outside it are clamped into the edge blocks rather than refused, since an order is a hint and cannot change a result.\npoints — the point set whose current positions decide the order. Its storage family decides where the order is built and used.\nscratch — a PointTraversalOrderScratch built for this grid, point count and tile_size. The block edge comes from it rather than from a keyword here, so there is one source of truth for it.\n\nAll four must agree in point count, element type and storage family, and a mismatch is refused rather than converted.\n\nThe block edge tile_size (default 4), fixed when the scratch is built or passed to the allocating form as a keyword, is in grid cells, so each axis is divided into lceil N  texttttile_size rceil blocks for a grid of N cells along it. Smaller blocks group points more tightly and cost more: the number of blocks grows as the cube of the reciprocal edge, and with it both the build time and the working memory — 2 MiB of counters at the default on a 256^3 grid against 128 MiB at tile_size = 1. The best value follows the backend rather than the transfer scheme: a device is insensitive to it, while a CPU prefers coarser blocks, up to roughly 16.\n\nA build or rebuild costs one pass over the points, a running total over the blocks, and the permutation check — approx 027 ms on a device at 256^3 with 10^6 points, against 071 ms for one ordered gather there, so about a third of a single transfer. On a CPU the same build is approx 9 ms, so it pays back over more transfers; either figure is what sets how often a rebuild is worth doing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.point_traversal_order!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.point_traversal_order","page":"Interpolation","text":"point_traversal_order(grid::Grid3D, points::PointSet; tile_size = 4)\n\nBuild and return a fresh PointTraversalOrder for points on grid — the allocating form of point_traversal_order!, which says what an order is, when it pays, what tile_size trades and what a build costs.\n\nArguments\n\ngrid, points — as for the in-place form.\ntile_size — the block edge in grid cells (default 4), whose trade-off the in-place form describes.\n\nwarning: Rebuild with the in-place form, not this one\nEach call here allocates about 15 MiB at 256^3 with 10^6 points. Use this form for the first order and point_traversal_order! for every rebuild after it, which writes into storage you already hold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.point_traversal_order"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.AbstractTransferScheme","page":"Interpolation","text":"AbstractTransferScheme\n\nIt is the supertype of all grid↔point transfer schemes, the parameter that fixes how the adjoint pair interpolate (W, the gather that evaluates a field at each point) / anterpolate (W^top, its transpose, the scatter depositing per-point values onto the grid) is realized. A scheme is a choice of kernel and representation: the physical-domain local splines (AbstractSplineScheme — gather/scatter on the nodal values) and the spectral-domain transfers (the exact DirectDFT and the fast, approximate NUFFT — evaluate/deposit in the Fourier basis, acting on a field in spectral representation). They share the verbs, one adjointness contract and one documented non-uniformity, differing only in (kernel, representation).\n\nOne place that uniformity does not reach is the allocating anterpolate's leading argument, which names what the fresh destination is built from: a Grid3D suffices for the spline schemes, whose result is a field in physical representation, while the spectral schemes take a whole field in spectral representation, their destination needing that representation's layout, backend and element type too. Code polymorphic over the allocating form therefore has to branch; the in-place anterpolate! forms, where the caller owns the destination, are uniform.\n\nSchemes divide the same way over storage families. The splines and DirectDFT are plain values — weight coefficients in an SMatrix, or none at all — so a kernel captures them by value: one built on the host drives a transfer over device points with nothing copied, which is why adapt (documented with Fields) does not list them. NUFFT is the exception, and for the reason write_scheme also refuses it: it owns plans and buffers built from its reference field, so it already lives in that field's family.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.AbstractTransferScheme"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.LalescuSpline","page":"Interpolation","text":"LalescuSpline(n, m = 1; T = Float64)\n\nSmooth (C^m) local spline interpolation follows Lalescu, Teaca & Carati (2010), pinned to 1e-12 against the textttbeta_nntexttt_mm weights of TurTLE, the reference pseudospectral turbulence solver with Lagrangian particle tracking (its interp_neighbours = n, smoothness = m). Over a compact stencil of 2n+2 nodes at offsets -n dots n+1 (n ge 0, the first argument — the finite-difference half-width), the per-axis weights w_q(t) for fractional cell position t in 0 1) are the coefficients of the two-point Hermite interpolant of degree 2m+1: the polynomial that reproduces the field value and the derivatives of order 1 dots m at the two bracketing nodes (offsets 0 and 1), each derivative estimated by a centred finite difference over a 2n+1-node stencil about that node (Fornberg weights). The second argument m sets the smoothness, C^m with 0 le m le 2n. That bound makes the default m = 1 valid only for n ge 1, so the one-argument LalescuSpline(0) is rejected rather than silently repaired: the smallest stencil admits m = 0 alone, spelled LalescuSpline(0, 0) (the linear scheme). The weights are stored in the (2n+2) times (2m+2) SMatrix coefficients (w_q(t) = sum_r textttcoefficientsq r+1  t^r, the exponent r running over the polynomial degrees).\n\nIt reproduces polynomials up to degree min(2m+1 2n) (for n ge 1) wherever the whole stencil samples one branch of that polynomial — a periodic base wraps the stencil at the boundary, and the wrapped samples come from the opposite edge, which no non-periodic polynomial continues. Special cases: m = 0 is linear interpolation over the two central nodes (independent of n); n = 1 m = 1 is the Catmull–Rom spline (the default CatmullRom, quadratic precision). For the C^0 Lagrange hierarchy (degree 2n+1, all 2n+2 nodes interpolated) use LagrangeSpline. Used by interpolate (grid → point) and anterpolate (point → grid).\n\nArguments\n\nn — the finite-difference half-width, n ge 0: the stencil has 2n+2 nodes per axis.\nm — the smoothness, C^m with 0 le m le 2n; its default 1 is valid only for n ge 1, and the linear scheme on the smallest stencil is LalescuSpline(0, 0).\nT — a keyword: the element type the weight coefficients are stored in, Float64 by default.\n\nReferences\n\nC. C. Lalescu, B. Teaca, D. Carati, Implementation of high order spline interpolations for tracking test particles in discretized fields, J. Comput. Phys. 229(17) (2010) 5862–5869.\nC. C. Lalescu, B. Bramas, M. Rampp, M. Wilczek, An efficient particle tracking algorithm for large-scale parallel pseudo-spectral simulations of turbulence (TurTLE), Comput. Phys. Commun. 278 (2022) 108406; arXiv:2107.01104. Code: gitlab.mpcdf.mpg.de/TurTLE/turtle.\nB. Fornberg, Generation of finite difference formulas on arbitrarily spaced grids, Math. Comp. 51(184) (1988) 699–706.\n\nFields\n\nneighbours — the stencil half-width n, as passed.\nsmoothness — the continuity order m, as passed.\ncoefficients — the weight polynomial's coefficients, derived from (n m) rather than stored independently, which is why write_scheme persists the two parameters and no dataset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.LalescuSpline"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.CatmullRom","page":"Interpolation","text":"CatmullRom\n\nCatmullRom is the default: the cubic n = 1 m = 1 LalescuSpline, a four-point C^1 stencil that reproduces the Catmull–Rom spline (and quadratic polynomials exactly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.CatmullRom"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.LagrangeSpline","page":"Interpolation","text":"LagrangeSpline(n; T = Float64)\n\nThis is local, C^0 Lagrange interpolation: the unique degree-2n+1 polynomial through the 2n+2 nearest grid nodes (the stencil offsets -n dots n+1, n ge 0). The per-axis weights are the Lagrange cardinal polynomials, stored in the (2n+2) times (2n+2) SMatrix coefficients. The n argument is the stencil half-width: it fixes both the stencil, which reaches n nodes below a point and n + 1 above it, and the degree, so a larger n reproduces a higher degree at the cost of a wider gather. It reproduces polynomials up to degree 2n+1 exactly wherever the whole stencil samples one branch of that polynomial (a wrapped stencil draws from the opposite edge, which no non-periodic polynomial continues), but is only C^0 (the interpolant kinks at the nodes); for smooth derivatives use LalescuSpline instead. The case n = 0 is linear interpolation over the two bracketing nodes. Matches Lagrange_polys of TurTLE, the reference pseudospectral turbulence solver with Lagrangian particle tracking.\n\nArguments\n\nn — the stencil half-width, n ge 0: the stencil has 2n+2 nodes per axis and the interpolant degree 2n+1.\nT — a keyword: the element type the weight coefficients are stored in, Float64 by default.\n\nReferences\n\nC. C. Lalescu, B. Teaca, D. Carati, Implementation of high order spline interpolations for tracking test particles in discretized fields, J. Comput. Phys. 229(17) (2010) 5862–5869.\nC. C. Lalescu, B. Bramas, M. Rampp, M. Wilczek, An efficient particle tracking algorithm for large-scale parallel pseudo-spectral simulations of turbulence (TurTLE), Comput. Phys. Commun. 278 (2022) 108406; arXiv:2107.01104. Code: gitlab.mpcdf.mpg.de/TurTLE/turtle.\n\nFields\n\nneighbours — the stencil half-width n, as passed.\ncoefficients — the cardinal-basis weights, derived from n rather than stored independently, which is why write_scheme persists the parameter and no dataset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.LagrangeSpline"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.DirectDFT","page":"Interpolation","text":"DirectDFT()\n\nThe exact spectral-domain transfer scheme: it interpolates and anterpolates by the literal O(NM) discrete Fourier sum over the field's stored Fourier modes k and the M points (x_p the p-th point position, q_p the value carried there), with N = N_x N_y N_z the grid-point count — none of the fast scheme's apparatus (no spreading window, oversampled grid or FFT — the machinery of NUFFT, which it validates as the oracle). It defines the spectral-domain adjoint pair and is the transparent exact baseline; for many points or modes, prefer NUFFT. It acts on a field's Fourier coefficients, in spectral representation:\n\nbeginaligned\nf(x_p) = frac1N sum_k hat f(k)  e^mathrmi kcdot (x_p - o)\n        = frac1N sum_textstored k alpha(k)  mathrmRebigl(hat f(k)  e^mathrmi kcdot (x_p - o)bigr) \n(W^top q)(k) = sum_p q_p  e^-mathrmi kcdot (x_p - o)\nendaligned\n\nThe second form reconstructs the real field (o the grid origin) from the stored rfft half-spectrum with the Parseval weight alpha(k) = 1 on the self-conjugate planes — the zero plane of the folded axis, the one the real transform halves, and on an even-length one its Nyquist plane — and 2 elsewhere. The first equality reads an even axis's Nyquist amplitude split across pm k — its band-limited cosine form, which is what the stored-k sum with the weight alpha(k) implements; sub-Nyquist, and at the grid nodes, the two sides agree with no convention needed.\n\nInterpolation reproduces the band-limited field exactly, so it is pointwise divergence-free on the resolved band — the frequencies this grid represents, each even axis's Nyquist plane excluded — where the local splines are not, and matches to_physical at the grid nodes; anterpolation matches to_spectral from grid-node samples. It is stateless: pass DirectDFT() as the scheme.\n\nwarning: Anterpolating off-node points does not give a real field's spectrum at an even axis's Nyquist\nThe deposit (W^top q)(k) is evaluated at every stored mode, the Nyquist planes included, and it is the exact adjoint of the gather there. But an even-length axis identifies its Nyquist mode with its own negative, so a real field's deposited coefficients must satisfy hat g(-k) = overlinehat g(k) within that plane, and the sum above meets that condition only when every point sits on a grid node of that axis, a: off-node along it, the two sides differ by e^-2mathrmi k_a x_pa ne 1. The deposited array is then the half-spectrum of no real field, and to_physical — which is the Hermitian projector — silently keeps only its Hermitian part, so a transform round trip changes it. The same hazard translation_kernel documents for its Nyquist factor is why NUFFT projects that plane out; DirectDFT deliberately does not, keeping the literal adjoint instead. The resolved band is unaffected, which is the band a de-aliased spectral computation uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.DirectDFT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.NUFFT","page":"Interpolation","text":"NUFFT(reference::AbstractFieldSpectral;\n      tol = 1e-10, oversampling = 2, width = 0, beta = 0)\n\nThe fast, approximate spectral-domain transfer scheme: the O(Nlog N + M) windowed-gridding (nonuniform FFT) counterpart of DirectDFT for evaluating a field in spectral representation at scattered points and depositing point values back, agreeing with DirectDFT to a tolerance tol. It runs the transfer through an oversampled work grid and a single FFT — spreading through a smooth compact window then transforming for the deposit, and the reverse for the evaluation, deconvolving the coefficients before the transform and gathering through the window after it — so it is far cheaper than the exact O(NM) sum once the point and mode counts grow; for a few points or modes, DirectDFT is simpler and exact.\n\nBuild a plan once from a reference field in spectral representation, which fixes the grid, layout, element type, and tensor order, then pass it as the scheme to interpolate / anterpolate on fields like the reference.\n\nArguments\n\nreference — a field in spectral representation, fixing the grid, layout, element type and tensor order the plan is built for. The order is fixed with the rest because the plan stores oversampled field templates, which carry the fiber; a plan built at one order does not serve another.\ntol — the target accuracy; the window width and shape default to the values that reach it. It is a target, not a guaranteed bound: it must be finite and positive, and a value finer than the element type can resolve widens the window as far as the formula goes and then saturates at that resolution rather than erroring.\noversampling — the linear oversampling factor sigma of the work grid (default 2); must be at least 2. That is an implementation bound rather than a theoretical one — lower upsampling is standard with a correspondingly wider window — because the width and shape formulas here are fitted for sigma ge 2; a value of 1 leaves no spectral guard band, so the gridding breaks down.\nwidth — the gridding stencil width in nodes; 0 (default) picks it from tol.\nbeta — the window shape parameter, unrelated to the accumulation weight beta the transfer verbs take; 0 (default) picks it from the width.\n\nIt agrees with DirectDFT (hence to_physical / to_spectral at the grid nodes) to tol on the resolved band (the rfft Nyquist plane is projected out), shares its adjoint relation, and like it reconstructs the band-limited field (divergence-free on the resolved band — the package's derivative wavenumbers are zeroed at an even axis's Nyquist frequency while this scheme evaluates the true phase there, so a discretely divergence-free field carrying Nyquist content is not pointwise divergence-free in the continuum).\n\nwarning: A plan costs about eight times the field in memory, for its whole lifetime\nA NUFFT plan holds an oversampled work grid of about sigma^3 times the field's memory (eight times at the default oversampling sigma = 2) for its whole lifetime, which matters on a memory-tight GPU.\n\nReferences\n\nA. H. Barnett, J. Magland, L. af Klinteberg, A parallel nonuniform fast Fourier transform library based on an exponential of semicircle kernel, SIAM J. Sci. Comput. 41(5) (2019) C479–C504.\n\nFields\n\nwidth — the gridding stencil width in nodes, as resolved from tol.\nbeta — the window shape parameter, as resolved from tol and width.\ntotal_oversampling — the ratio of the oversampled point count to the reference grid's, which carries the transforms' normalization between the two grids.\nreference_grid, reference_layout — the base and Fourier layout the plan was built for, kept so a field handed to it can be checked rather than assumed compatible.\noversampled_grid, oversampled_plans — the work grid and its transform plans.\ndeconvolution — the per-mode reciprocal of the window's transform, dividing out the gridding.\noversampled_physical, oversampled_spectral — the work buffers, whose contents are scratch: the physical one is a template copied per call and never mutated in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.NUFFT"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.write_scheme","page":"Interpolation","text":"write_scheme(parent::HDF5.H5DataStore, name::AbstractString,\n             scheme::AbstractTransferScheme)\n\nPersist a grid↔point transfer scheme — the choice of kernel and representation that fixes how interpolate and anterpolate are realized — as a new HDF5 group name under parent, returning the group it created. Use it when a checkpoint must restore not only the positions and values of a point set but also how that set samples a field, so a restarted run transfers identically rather than falling back to a default. Reload with read_scheme.\n\nThe group is self-describing and holds no dataset: a scheme's weight coefficients are derived from its parameters, so the group carries a type tag, a schema version, the weight element type, and the defining parameters — the neighbourhood n and, for LalescuSpline, the smoothness m. Reading reconstructs through the ordinary constructor, which recomputes the weights and revalidates the parameters, so a group asserting an impossible combination is refused rather than producing a malformed scheme. The sugar parent[name] = scheme is equivalent. CatmullRom needs no identity of its own: it is LalescuSpline(1, 1) and round-trips as one, comparing equal to the alias.\n\nBecause parent is a handle you opened and own, this never opens or closes a file, and the package attaches no notion of time — the caller bundles a snapshot and owns any time / iteration attributes.\n\nArguments\n\nparent — an open HDF5 File or Group to create the new subgroup under.\nname — the name of the group to create.\nscheme — the scheme to write: a LalescuSpline, a LagrangeSpline, or DirectDFT.\n\nProvided by a package extension, so it is available only when HDF5.jl is loaded.\n\nwarning: NUFFT is deliberately not persistable\nEvery other scheme is its parameters, which is what makes a few attributes a faithful record. A NUFFT is not: it owns oversampled FFT plans and a deconvolution window built against a particular reference field, so a stored copy would be either a large duplicate of derived data or a set of parameters pretending to be the object. It is the same class as FieldTransformPlans, which is likewise rebuilt rather than moved when storage families change. Rebuild it from the reference field the caller restores anyway — NUFFT(reference; tol) — and write the tol in the caller's own snapshot if it varies. Passing one here throws an ArgumentError naming this.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.write_scheme"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.read_scheme","page":"Interpolation","text":"read_scheme(parent::HDF5.H5DataStore, name::AbstractString)\n\nReconstruct the transfer scheme written by write_scheme from the HDF5 group name under parent, dispatching on the stored type attribute to return it in the matching type. The reload is exact: the reconstructed scheme compares equal (==, and in fact ===) to the one written, because a scheme is fully determined by the parameters the group stores and the constructor recomputes the weights identically.\n\nHere parent is an open HDF5 File or Group holding the group, and name is the name (or path) of that group under parent. The group's schema is checked, not merely read: a file from a newer schema version, a group missing a parameter its type needs, an unrecognized type tag, or a parameter combination the scheme's own constructor rejects all raise an ArgumentError rather than producing a scheme that is not what the file claims.\n\nProvided by a package extension, so it is available only when HDF5.jl is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.read_scheme"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.AbstractSplineScheme","page":"Interpolation","text":"AbstractSplineScheme{N2, M2, T, L}\n\nIt is the supertype of the physical-domain local schemes over one shared stencil: the smooth LalescuSpline of Lalescu, Teaca & Carati (2010) and the C^0 LagrangeSpline, the classical Lagrange interpolant of the same nodes, matched to TurTLE's Lagrange_polys. Each acts on a field in physical representation, gathering and scattering its nodal values over a compact (2n+2)^3 stencil, n being the constructor's half-width argument, around the cell c that contains a point x_p. With t_a the point's fractional position along axis a and w the scheme's per-axis weight polynomials, interpolation is the tensor-product gather\n\nbeginaligned\nf(x_p) = sum_i_1 i_2 i_3 w_i_1(t_1)  w_i_2(t_2)  w_i_3(t_3) \nqquad fbigl(x_c + (i_1 i_2 i_3)bigr)\nendaligned\n\nand anterpolation is its transpose, adding w_i_1(t_1)  w_i_2(t_2)  w_i_3(t_3)  q_p (with q_p the value carried at x_p) onto each of those same nodes. A scheme is a tensor product of 1D weight polynomials: a compact stencil of N_2 = 2n+2 nodes, each weight a degree-(M_2-1) polynomial in the fractional cell position t in 0 1). The N_2 times M_2 coefficient matrix ([node, power]) is precomputed once into an SMatrix that the kernels take by value. It is one of the two families of AbstractTransferScheme; the spectral-domain DirectDFT / NUFFT are the other. Only the spline family has a shared supertype, its members differing in their weights alone. In the signature T is the coefficient type and L the matrix length N2 * M2.\n\nReferences\n\nC. C. Lalescu, B. Teaca, D. Carati, Implementation of high order spline interpolations for tracking test particles in discretized fields, J. Comput. Phys. 229(17) (2010) 5862–5869.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.AbstractSplineScheme"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._axis_weights-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{M2}, Tuple{N2}, Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{N2, M2, T}, T}} where {N2, M2, T}","page":"Interpolation","text":"_axis_weights(coefficients, t)\n\nEvaluate the per-axis spline weights w_q(t) for all N2 stencil nodes at fractional position t, as an SVector{N2}, by Horner over the M2 polynomial-coefficient columns of coefficients (M_2 = deg + 1); pure, allocation-free, so it runs in the transfer kernels on CPU and GPU.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._axis_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._check_nufft_compatibility-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{NUFFT{T}, AbstractFieldSpectral}} where T","page":"Interpolation","text":"_check_nufft_compatibility(scheme::NUFFT, field::AbstractFieldSpectral)\n\nCheck that a NUFFT plan may be used on field: same base, Fourier layout, scalar type, storage family and tensor order.\n\nThe plan precomputes an oversampled work grid, its transform plans and a deconvolution for one specific context. Applying it to a field from another context is not a shape error that surfaces on its own — a same-order field on a different domain has identical storage, so it silently receives the wrong deconvolution and a work grid that resolves different wavenumbers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._check_nufft_compatibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._check_transfer_colocation-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Interpolation","text":"_check_transfer_colocation(field, points, values)\n\nCheck that the three operands of a grid↔point transfer carry the same scalar type and live in the same storage family, throwing otherwise.\n\nA transfer selects its launch backend from the field and then hands the point and value arrays to that one kernel, so a host/device mixture reaches a launch that can only see one of them, and a mixed precision reaches an internal locator that requires one scalar type. Both are public input errors, and without this check both surfaced from inside the implementation instead of at the call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._check_transfer_colocation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._fornberg-Tuple{Float64, Vector{Float64}, Int64}","page":"Interpolation","text":"_fornberg(evaluation_point, nodes, max_order)\n\nReturn Fornberg (1988) finite-difference weights: weights[i, k+1] is the weight of nodes[i] in the order-k derivative (k = 0 1 dots up to max_order) of a function sampled at nodes, evaluated at evaluation_point, a host-side spline-weight helper.\n\nReferences\n\nB. Fornberg, Generation of finite difference formulas on arbitrarily spaced grids, Math. Comp. 51(184) (1988) 699–706.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._fornberg"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._hermite_weight_matrix-Tuple{Int64, Int64}","page":"Interpolation","text":"_hermite_weight_matrix(n, m)\n\nBuild the (2n+2) times (2m+2) matrix of smooth-spline weight-polynomial coefficients (row q is the weight polynomial for stencil offset q - 1 - n in -n dots n+1; column r+1 is the t^r coefficient). Each weight has degree 2m+1; together they form the two-point Hermite interpolant reproducing the value and the order-1 dots m derivatives at the two bracketing nodes (offsets 0 and 1), the derivatives taken from centred finite differences over a 2n+1-node stencil about each bracketing node. See LalescuSpline; pinned to 1e-12 against TurTLE's weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._hermite_weight_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._lagrange_weight_matrix-Tuple{Int64}","page":"Interpolation","text":"_lagrange_weight_matrix(n)\n\nBuild the (2n+2) times (2n+2) matrix of Lagrange cardinal-polynomial coefficients over the stencil offsets -n dots n+1 (row q is the cardinal polynomial of node q; column r+1 is the t^r coefficient). Each is the degree-2n+1 polynomial that is one at its own node and zero at the others (the inverse of the offsets' Vandermonde matrix). See LagrangeSpline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._lagrange_weight_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._locate-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, T, T, Int64}} where T","page":"Interpolation","text":"_locate(coordinate, origin, spacing, number_of_grid_points)\n\nReturn (base_cell, t) for one axis: the zero-based index base_cell of the left node of the cell containing coordinate, and the fractional position t in 0 1) within it. Periodic wrapping of the stencil comes later. It is pure, kernel-safe arithmetic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._locate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._scan_offsets!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Interpolation","text":"_scan_offsets!(offsets, counts)\n\nWrite the inclusive running total of counts into offsets, and return offsets.\n\nThe generic method is cumsum!, which is a parallel scan on a device and a serial loop on the host. The host method below threads it, because on a CPU this is the one stage of building a traversal order that KernelAbstractions does not parallelise, and its cost is set by the number of blocks — that is, by the grid — rather than by the point count. At 512^3 and the default tile_size it was a quarter of the build at eight threads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._scan_offsets!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._scan_offsets!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Vector{T}}} where T<:Integer","page":"Interpolation","text":"_scan_offsets!(offsets::Vector, counts::Vector)\n\nIt computes a threaded inclusive running total on the host: each chunk sums its slice, the chunk totals are scanned serially (there are as many as chunks), and each chunk then writes its slice offset by the total of everything before it.\n\nThe result is bitwise identical to cumsum!, and for integers a property, not a tolerance: addition is exact and associative, so regrouping the sums cannot change them. A threaded float reduction would need a fixed summation tree for thread-count invariance; this needs none.\n\nTwo details are load-bearing. The chunk accumulator is declared local, because a name already bound in the enclosing scope would be SHARED by every task rather than shadowed — a data race that produced wrong answers and ran 50× slower when this was first written. And the grand total is accumulated wide and checked against typemax, because cumsum! into a narrow integer is overflow-checked while a plain accumulation wraps: a wrapped total becomes a negative offset, which the placement kernel would use as an index inside @inbounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._scan_offsets!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/Interpolation/#GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._show_spline_eltype-Tuple{IO, GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation.AbstractSplineScheme}","page":"Interpolation","text":"_show_spline_eltype(io, scheme)\n\nPrint a spline scheme's element type as the T keyword its constructor takes, and only when it is not the default Float64.\n\nBoth halves are deliberate. Printing it keeps two schemes that differ only in precision — which are refused against each other — from rendering as one string. Omitting it at the default keeps the ordinary display a bare constructor call, and writing it as the keyword rather than as a type parameter keeps both forms valid Julia that reconstructs the scheme.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGPeriodicFields.Interpolation._show_spline_eltype"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/","page":"Testing","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Testing"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#Testing-—-GBGPeriodicFields","page":"Testing","text":"This is one of four design documents — three rationale companions and the typed FORMALIZATION.md behind them: ARCHITECTURE.md explains why the package is built the way it is, PERFORMANCE.md how it performs and is benchmarked, and this one explains how the tests are built and what they must cover. Read it before adding or changing tests. The goal is not a test count — it is that every claim the package makes is checked against something independent of the package.","title":"Testing — GBGPeriodicFields"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#1.-Philosophy","page":"Testing","text":"Verify against independent ground truth, never against the code's own other path. A test that compares one package routine to another package routine only proves they agree, not that either is correct.\n\nThat bar is enforced, not stated (decision 55). Every public value-returning operation carries a recorded oracle provenance in test/oracle_registry.jl, and two assertions hold the line: an operation with no entry fails the suite, and so does one whose only coverage is other package operations. The rule existed here for a year before anything checked it, which is how weighted_inner came to be verified entirely against inner, laplacian, gradient and convolve — every assertion sound, and none of them a value derived independently of the package.\n\nFour kinds of oracle are used:\n\nAnalytic oracles — for the differential operators. A field is taken to spectral representation, the operator applied, and the result compared to a known closed form: partial_x sin x = cos x, Delta sin x = -sin x, nabla times (0 0 sin x) = (0 -cos x 0), a Helmholtz-projected field is divergence-free. These catch sign, axis, and scaling errors that a self-consistent round-trip would silently reproduce.\nIndependent computation — a direct evaluation written out in the test, taking no package routine but the one under test: a per-point LU for det, an explicit sqrtsum_i u_i^2 for norm, a binned circshift autocorrelation for radial_correlation, an O(M^2) pair count for the point RDF. Slow and obviously correct, which is the trade being made.\nTrusted reference — for representation parity and for the GPU path. The CPU path is pinned by analytic oracles and the parity baseline; then another computation of the same quantity (the spectral form, or the same operation on a device array) is required to match it. This is still \"two computations of one quantity,\" not a thing against itself.\nStructural identities — relations the operators must satisfy for any input: the vector-calculus identities (nablacdot(nablatimes u) = 0, nablatimes(nablavarphi) = 0) and, on a periodic box, the adjoint relations (divergence as the negative gradient adjoint, self-adjoint curl, a self-adjoint negative-semidefinite Laplacian, an exactly idempotent self-adjoint Helmholtz projection — decision 42). Tested on randomized band-limited fields, they pin the discrete energy/entropy structure of the scheme.\n\nA fixture must be able to falsify the claim its test makes. A symmetric fixture cannot see an asymmetric defect, and a self-referential assertion — threaded against serial, device against host, compressed kernel against stored — cannot see a defect shared by both sides. Both classes shipped here: a gradient testset named for an index convention whose fixture had a diagonal gradient, and a tensor divergence with no analytic oracle at all and nine identical components. Where a convention is stated in a name or a docstring, the fixture must be capable of contradicting it — and since that is a mechanical property, generated_analytic.jl obtains it by construction rather than by care.\n\nThe public method set is the spec. Two mechanisms enforce this:\n\nthe parity baseline (test/method_parity.jl) locks the method counts two ways. The per-generic list (206 public methods) is enumerated — it names 28 generics in two modules — and is therefore blind to anything not on the list: decisions 48, 49 and 51 each added public surface that never moved it. Beside it sits a derived baseline (decision 53), one frozen count per module over every function that module owns, so any new method anywhere moves a number and must be refrozen deliberately — the pass-audit protocol applied to the method table. Prefer adding to the derived one; the enumerated one stays because it localizes a change to the generic that moved. The derived count asks about ownership (m.module === mod) over the surface public_surface.jl derives, not about a module's namespace: a method defined under a qualified name — Base.inv(kernel::SeparableKernel) = … — creates no binding to scan, so a namespace-based count silently missed it (decision 56). The two baselines count different denominators and are deliberately not compared;\nintentional-absence tests assert that invalid combinations do not exist (@test_throws MethodError), symmetrically across representations: nonlinear products are absent in spectral (field×field, division by a scalar field, pointwise norms), and the differential operators are absent in physical (differentiation is spectral-only — there are no finite-difference physical operators yet). A deliberate gap is a tested gap.\n\n\"Should work\" is not done. The minimum bar for any change is: the package loads, the parity baseline and ambiguity check pass, and the suite is green.","title":"1. Philosophy"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#A-GATE-and-a-SURVEY,-and-how-to-tell-which-a-test-is","page":"Testing","text":"Pkg.test() and test/exhaustive/ answer different questions, and the difference decides where any new test belongs.\n\n Pkg.test() test/exhaustive/\nthe question did this change break something we know about? is there something we do not know about?\nthe verdict binary — green, or the change does not ship a report, read by a person\nmay be non-green never yes, while a known limitation stands\ncadence every change occasionally, on demand\nits oracle specific, per claim (langle sin^2 rangle = 12) universal, per property (aliasing must not change the answer)\nwhat it holds claims, completeness guards, intentional absences products over the coverage axes\n\nFour rules follow, and they settle placement without further argument:\n\nA specific oracle goes in the gate. langle sin^2rangle = 12 is a claim about one operation: it must always hold, it costs microseconds, and a regression must block a commit.\nA universal oracle over a product goes in the survey. \"Aliasing must not change the answer\" ranges over every operation × every storage relation, and hand-picking instances of it is exactly how the aliasing axis came to hold two of its six values (decision 56).\nA completeness guard goes in the gate — the two ledgers, the oracle registry, the derived baselines. They are microseconds, and their whole purpose is to fail the moment an operation arrives without coverage. An occasional run cannot do that job.\nAnything that may legitimately be non-green goes in the survey. This is the decisive one. The aliasing failures cannot sit in the gate: they would have to be fixed or suppressed, and a suppressed failure is worse than an absent test because it reads as a pass.\n\nThe two are connected by a lifecycle: the survey discovers, a fix lands, the gate pins it forever, and rule 3's guards are what stop a new operation from skipping the first step.\n\nGetting the split backwards fails in both directions, and both have happened here. Products in the gate slow it until someone trims coverage; oracles in the survey let regressions ship because nobody ran it.\n\nNote that the GPU suite and the benchmarks are opt-in for an unrelated reason — they need hardware, or a quiet machine. \"Opt-in\" is not the axis; gate-versus-survey is.\n\nThe survey is a report, not a gate that has been silenced: it runs blind to what the package has decided, prints everything that does not work, and where a failure matches a recorded decision or a documented contract it prints the citation beside the failure rather than in place of it. It also reports what it could not construct, since a lane whose silence reads as a pass is the failure the whole file exists to prevent. See test/exhaustive/README.md.","title":"A GATE and a SURVEY, and how to tell which a test is"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#2.-Layout","page":"Testing","text":"test/runtests.jl includes each module's file inside its own wrapper module (module FieldAlgebraTests; include(\"fieldalgebra.jl\"); end), so per-file helpers and constants (comp, plans_for, grid, T) cannot collide. Every file is also runnable on its own (julia --project=. test/<file>.jl).\nPer-module behaviour files — fields.jl (including the rank-free transform plans and their component-offset alignment lane, decision 76; the real-field reality condition of decision 71 — reality_residual against an elementwise oracle written from the definition, over both element types, tensor orders 0/1/2, permuted folds and an odd folded axis, which has one self-conjugate plane rather than two, plus the pair-consistency property, the self-paired corners and the round-trip repair, plus decision 83's reality_projection!: the identity on an exact spectrum, an elementwise pair-mean oracle over the ORIGINAL coefficients, off-plane coefficients bitwise untouched, exact idempotence, round-trip agreement, linearity, commutation with the Laplacian symbol, the two-argument form, the domain refusal and the physical absence, plus decision 90's mean_free_projection!: the zero coefficient exactly zero in both parts, every other coefficient bitwise untouched, agreement with a physical-space average oracle, exact idempotence and the physical absence; and the decision-45 Grid3D uniformity check — a contradictory grid_spacing and a non-positive size are rejected, an equivalent-arithmetic spacing accepted, at both element types — and the decision-64 scratch-arity testset: bitwise source preservation, bitwise equality with the allocating form, the aliasing and context refusals), equality.jl (decision 91: sections compared by value against raw arrays built independently, the signed-zero and NaN semantics, and the refusals across representations and precisions), fieldalgebra.jl (the M2 + M3 surface, incl. the weighted combination combine!, the determinant det, and the arbitrary-φ map! / map — decision 48 — against the named operations as oracles, order changes, multi-input, α/β, aliasing, and the physical-only absence), differentialoperators.jl (the differential operators, incl. the fused symmetric_gradient), convolution.jl (periodic convolution + the kernel/coordinate builders spectral_cutoff / spectral_band / radial_kernel / translation_kernel, and the kernel storage classes — convolve! is walked over all four classes (StoredKernel, SeparableKernel, RadialKernel and the ProductKernel that composing across the families gives), each required to agree bitwise with its materialized twin, with the class list derived from the type hierarchy so a fifth class fails until it is given a specimen (and a small testset pins the hierarchy's head public, since decision 65 exports SpectralKernel — the type every kernel-taking signature dispatches on — beside the four classes that were already exported); the builders themselves are single-carrier operations since decision 66, so they sit in context_entrypoints.jl's exemption list rather than its invocation list, a foreign kernel being caught where it meets a field; the kernel-composed helmholtz_projection and divergence arities are walked over the separable and radial classes but not the product one; and weighted_inner is walked over all four classes since decision 62, each required to agree bitwise with its own materialized form (the two-class signature it had before was a narrow signature, not a constraint — and both rationales recorded for it were wrong). Tensor orders 0/1/2 and the composition cases pinning which class each pairing lands in are covered alongside), interpolation.jl (the point-data types), pointvalues_algebra.jl (the point-value M2 + M3 surface, incl. det and the representation-free map! / map), hdf5.jl also covers transfer-scheme persistence (decision 75 — nine schemes round-tripped by === plus a bitwise behavioural check, eight malformed-group refusals, the NUFFT refusal, and a completeness guard over every concrete scheme type, itself verified by perturbation), displacement.jl (spatial motion, decision 73 — displace! against a raw-array oracle, the fused torus arity bitwise against cover-then-wrap!, and the wrap contract's endpoint / non-finite / origin pins), caller_counts.jl (operand counts the caller chooses, decision 88 — add!/subtract!, map!, displace! and the tuple weighted_inner past their group sizes and past 32 operands, bitwise against the ungrouped arithmetic, with the destination aliasing a later operand), interpolation_transfer.jl (the physical-domain spline transfer — the smooth LalescuSpline/CatmullRom and the C^0 LagrangeSpline — interpolation / anterpolation kernels), transfer_spectral.jl (the spectral-domain transfer schemes — the exact DirectDFT and the fast NUFFT), and reductions.jl (the L²/measure reductions + the multiplier-weighted weighted_inner, including its tuple-of-weights form (decision 72) — fused bitwise-equal to the separate calls for every count from one to five over a heterogeneous tuple holding one weight of each storage class, the order property that reversing the weights reverses the results and nothing else, @inferred for the concrete NTuple, both refusals in any position, and the empty tuple — + the radial reductions radial_spectrum / radial_correlation, incl. the two-field co-spectra, on fields and point-values; and — decision 49 — the scalar-only std/var with their spectral Parseval form, the scalar-only errors, and the covariance tensor cov vs a per-point covariance with its symmetry and per-component diagonal), rdf.jl (the Phase-2 point pair-correlation: assignment_window + the FFT g(r) recipe vs direct counting), display.jl (decision 82 — the display layer by properties, the sharpest being discrimination: two objects a check refuses must render differently), shared_modes.jl (decision 85 — the resolution verbs, against three independent oracles: an ANALYTIC one, where a field of named modes embedded into a finer grid is compared with the same function sampled there, through the transforms rather than through the index map; ADJOINTNESS, langle Eu wrangle = langle u Rwrangle, which no single-direction bug satisfies and which pins the physical-amplitude scaling; and the amplitude of a single cosine measured in physical space across resolutions — plus the excluded Nyquist shell, the untouched non-shared modes, and every structural refusal including the transposed argument order), plus eltype_genericity.jl (Float64/Float32), the cross-file permuted-layout lane (decision 41) — a permuted rfft layout testset in fields.jl (a transform round-trip + both multiplier families vs rfftfreq/fftfreq, odd and even folds), differentialoperators.jl (analytic gradient/Laplacian), convolution.jl (the translation kernel's circshift oracle, a live Nyquist factor on the folded axis), transfer_spectral.jl (DirectDFT at the nodes + NUFFT vs its oracle), reductions.jl (Parseval/shell/weighted + radial_correlation, odd and even folds), and hdf5.jl (fft_dims persistence) — every layer whose correctness rests on the fold following fft_dims, adjointness.jl (operator structure on random fields), randomized.jl (property-based: algebraic laws on random fields / point-values / kernels), allocations.jl (a comprehensive @allocated audit, both element types. Two bars, and the difference matters: most rows are pinned below a field, which is what catches a materialized temporary, while the four radial-symbol operators are pinned at literally zero on the serial lane since decision 70 — the generous bar is what let 144 B a call hide under it, visible only to a consumer counting bytes per step. The audit covers the entire in-place ! surface — the differential operators (incl. symmetric_gradient!), transforms, convolve!, the M2+M3 algebra add!/combine!/mul!/multiply!/divide!/dot!/cross!/transpose!/sym!/skew!/tr!/det! over physical+spectral, all tensor orders, the constant/α/β/reversed variants, the point-value algebra (incl. det!), and the spline interpolate!/anterpolate! — allocates sim 0 bytes; the hot-loop reductions (physical inner, the fused weighted_inner, maximum, integral/mean, and the spectral zero-mode integral/mean) do not materialize a field; and the documented allocators are bounded — norm!/lpnorm/radial_correlation (its power field + the allocating transform; dev! left the list when decision 39 fused its trace into a register, and std/var left it when decision 49 rewrote them as two fused passes — measured at 16 bytes, not a field; radial_spectrum is pinned tight, its staging fused into the binning kernel by decision 34), and the spectral vector inner's O(N^2) Parseval-correction planes, rocBLAS offering no dotc over a strided ROCArray view; guards the GPU hot-loop discipline, decisions 24/25/34), pass_audit.jl (the element-traffic ledger, decision 36: a CountingArray harness measures every audited operation's exact per-array read/write counts against a frozen fused-ideal table — an above-ideal count must carry a named holder — catching the unfused-recompute class no other layer sees; CPU-only, PASS_AUDIT_PRINT=1 regenerates the cells), Both ledgers derive their own completeness (decision 53): every exported in-place (!) operation must have a row in allocations.jl and in pass_audit.jl, or be named in that file's exemption list with a reason, the requirement being read from the export list at run time rather than remembered. A new in-place operation therefore cannot ship without a deliberate decision, and a stale exemption naming a nonexistent operation fails too. It found four operations audited nowhere while their own siblings were — subtract! beside add!, skew!/dev! beside sym!, norm! beside det!/tr!. metadata_sweep.jl (decision 53: a generated sweep asserting that every field-producing operation propagates the grid, spectral layout, element type and tensor order of its inputs, over four folds and both element types — 1016 assertions with a free oracle, since it checks metadata rather than numbers. It also varies order-carrying argument slots independently, the sampling the rest of the suite lacks: every other fixture builds its field, point-values and plan from one R, so the suite runs down the diagonal of a cube and two real bugs lived off it. Verified by mutation — dropping the layout in materialize_kernel or in similar_field's vector arity fails it, and both of those survived the whole suite before it existed), generated_analytic.jl (decision 53: closed-form oracles over a fixture family that is asymmetric by construction — every component/axis slot draws a distinct amplitude, frequency and phase, so a transposed index, a swapped axis or a dropped term shows up by signature rather than by luck of the fixture. One rule per operator, written once as a formula in the fixture's own parameters and instantiated automatically, so the oracle stays free while the symmetry that hid two real defects is gone. Verified by mutation: transposing gradient's destination packing gives 12 failures, contracting divergence's wrong tensor index gives 2 — both of which the whole suite passed before), public_surface.jl (not a testset: the shared derivation of the package's public surface — every function a package module owns methods of, extensions of external generics included — which the two ledgers and the oracle registry each derive their required set from), context_matrix.jl and context_entrypoints.jl (decision 58: the context-mismatch matrix, in two tiers with derived denominators. Tier 1 takes the participant set from methods(_context_grid) and checks every ORDERED PAIR against every mismatch axis, so a new participant adds a row and a column on its own and one with no builder fails rather than sitting out. Tier 2 derives the (operation, carrier-kind) pairs from the method table and requires each to be invoked or exempted with a reason. The two are complementary and each was proven so: neutering the shared predicate fails Tier 1; removing a single entry point's check while the predicate stands fails only Tier 2. The domain axis is the one a hand-written test is least likely to include — the two grids have the SAME SHAPE, so every array involved is identically sized and nothing structural distinguishes them), contracts.jl (decision 56: the negative matrix for the strict compatibility protocol — context mismatch at identical shape, numeric domains, constructor invariants, transfer colocation, the inversion zero policy, and the R le 2 ceiling lane whose property is that above the ceiling every entry point works or throws, never returns a wrong value), results_format.jl (decision 56: the committed benchmark report must parse as GFM — every table's alignment and data rows must match its header's column count), oracle_registry.jl (decision 55: the recorded oracle provenance of every public value-returning operation — analytic, independent, property, relational or structural, each naming the file its strongest oracle lives in. An operation with no entry fails, and so does one covered only against other package operations; the second assertion is the standing bar, not a snapshot of today), aqua.jl (package checks), threading.jl (CPU threading; runs in both the default and a JULIA_NUM_THREADS lane; covers the KernelAbstractions kernels separately — bitwise for the disjoint-write gather, to a tolerance for the two Atomix accumulators — and asserts the decision-40 reductions bitwise thread-count-invariant, in-process and across a subprocess at a different -t), and jet.jl (JET @test_call static analysis of the public operations), and hdf5.jl (the write_field/read_field and write_points/read_points HDF5 persistence round trips — every field type, every point type, the self-description attributes, and the corrupted-group error paths for both families — run with the HDF5 weak dependency loaded).\ntest/method_parity.jl — the spec-lock: per-generic method counts (the 206-method field surface and the separate 122-method point-value surface) plus detect_ambiguities (from Test) over the whole package. The explicit multiply! quartet exists precisely to keep that ambiguity set empty. The frozen counts, both the enumerated and the derived per-module ones, live in test/method_parity_baseline.toml, each moved number refrozen there with its reason.\nThe four audits are declared to GBGCodingTools (since 2026-09-27): the allocation audit, the traffic audit, the oracle registry and method parity call the tool's engines, GBGCodingTools.TestHarness, which were extracted from these files, so the rulebook's RULE.TEST.ALLOC_ZERO, PASS_AUDIT, ORACLE_INDEPENDENT and METHOD_PARITY bind the package. Each file keeps its own rows, frozen counts and exemptions; only the machinery moved.\ntest/Project.toml — the test environment's dependencies, deliberately backend-free (see §5).\ntest/gpu/ — the opt-in GPU suite (see §5): one shared suite.jl and a thin entry per lane — rocm/, cuda/, and cuda-unified/, which runs the same suite with every CUDA array in unified memory (§4, axis 17).\nDoctests — the Getting-started tutorial (docs/src/tutorial.md) is written as jldoctest blocks, run and verified by doctest = true in docs/make.jl (outside Pkg.test()): a third layer of checked examples that cannot drift from the code (a wrong expected output fails the build).","title":"2. Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#3.-Writing-a-test-here","page":"Testing","text":"Helpers and conventions. Build fields from coordinate functions (ScalarFieldPhysical((x,y,z) -> …, grid)); take component slices with the local comp/tcomp helpers; build matching transform plans with a plans_for(field) helper that allocates the spectral scratch with similar(field_data(field), …) so it inherits the field's backend. Compare with field_data(result) ≈ expected.\n\nTolerances — the part that bites. ≈ (isapprox) uses a relative tolerance with atol = 0 by default:\n\nFor an O(1) result, bare ≈ is correct.\nFor a result that is mathematically ~zero, bare ≈ degenerates to an almost-exact comparison and will fail on nothing but rounding. Either assert maximum(abs, data) < 1e-12, or pass an explicit atol. Also choose inputs so the expected result is not accidentally zero — e.g. an axis-aligned field (sin x sin y sin z) is irrotational and longitudinal, so both curl and the Helmholtz projection return ~0 and test nothing. Use a field whose components depend on more than one coordinate.\nFor cross-backend comparison (GPU vs CPU), compare with isapprox(Array(device), host; rtol, atol) carrying a small absolute floor — never bitwise. The two run the same source expression but not necessarily the same arithmetic: a device compiler may contract x + c * v into a fused multiply-add, one rounding where the host takes two, so results differ in the last bit. Measured for displace! on CUDA, where the device result equals the host's muladd form exactly (PERFORMANCE.md finding (z)). Bitwise is a same-backend contract — it is what thread-count invariance asserts — and claiming it across backends is what made the first displace! GPU assertions fail. A wrapped coordinate needs more than a tolerance: compare the difference modulo the box, since a last-bit disagreement arriving at the wrap can place the two sides on opposite edges and read as a whole box length.\nThe same trap appears for inner products of independent fields (e.g. langle nablavarphi urangle): they are near-orthogonal, so the common value of two equal inner products is small relative to the operand magnitudes. Scale the floor by the Cauchy–Schwarz product lVert arVert lVert brVert, not by the (tiny) result.\n\nPick the right oracle. Operators → analytic closed form. Representation parity and backend parity → trusted CPU reference. Never assert an operator only by inverting itself.","title":"3. Writing a test here"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#4.-The-coverage-contract","page":"Testing","text":"For each public operation, a test must establish:\n\n[ ] Physical-space correctness against an analytic or hand-computed result.\n[ ] Spectral-space behaviour — correctness where the operation is linear in the     field (valid in both representations); an asserted intentional absence     (@test_throws MethodError) where it is nonlinear (physical-only).\n[ ] Both element types — Float64 and Float32. (Float32 is the realistic GPU     precision; consumer GPUs run Float64 far slower.)\n[ ] An anisotropic domain — at least one test with L_x ne L_y ne L_z, so the 2piL     wavenumber scaling is actually exercised and a dropped length factor is visible.\n[ ] The in-place ! form with a caller-supplied destination, including the     aliased in-place branch for the by-construction alias-safe operations (cross, transpose, sym/skew/dev,     the curl wrapper) — and, for mul!, the LinearAlgebra no-alias contract. An op whose kernel is     alias-safe by construction (the one-pass helmholtz_projection!, decision 32) asserts the     aliased call bitwise equal to the non-aliased one instead — a stronger check than the     guard's tolerance.\n[ ] Element traffic (decision 36): a new or rewritten operation gets a pass_audit.jl row —     its exact per-array read and write counts measured through the CountingArray harness and     frozen beside the fused ideal, with a named holder for any deliberate gap. This is the layer     that catches an unfused recompute, which correctness, allocation, and static analysis all pass.\n[ ] Allocation-free (invariant 13): every in-place ! operator gets an @allocated assertion in     allocations.jl — on preallocated buffers with a distinct destination it allocates sim 0     field-sized bytes, both element types. Run on the CPU: there a materialized field's full bytes     are counted, whereas GPU @allocated reports only the host wrapper of a device buffer and would miss     the materialization that wedges a hot loop (the code is generic, so a field-sized materialization     is backend-symmetric — though a device run also carries small, size-independent launch bookkeeping     the CPU audit cannot see, absorbed by the fraction-of-a-field threshold because it does not scale     with the grid). A deliberate materializer (norm!/lpnorm, radial_correlation, the     mixed spectral inner(f, g)) instead gets a bounded guard that catches an O(N)/O(textshells) regression.\n[ ] Both α/β branches for the five-argument kernels — overwrite (beta = 0, which must     ignore the destination's prior contents, NaN included) and accumulate (beta ne 0).\n[ ] GPU parity — the result matches the CPU path on a device array, under     allowscalar(false).\n[ ] Metadata survives anything that ALLOCATES its result — the grid, the Fourier layout, the     origin and the tensor order. An operation that returns a fresh section must carry all four, and     the way to guarantee it is to build the result through the package's own allocator rather than     through a two-argument constructor, which defaults the layout. This axis was missing when map     shipped, and map(φ, field) was consequently broken for every permuted RealFourierLayout from     its first day (decision 48). It applies to the whole allocating surface, not only to obviously     metadata-carrying operations, and it is what test/adapt.jl asserts across three folds, a     non-zero origin, both element types, both representations and all three tensor orders.\n\nThe storage-family axis, and a vendor limit on it. Moving a section between storage families (decision 63) is exercised on CPU with a custom adaptor, because adapting to the family a section already occupies is deliberately the identity and would test nothing. The device half is in the GPU suite, which now moves whole workspaces — field, multipliers, kernels and plans — through the shipped interface rather than through hand-written twins. A permuted RealFourierLayout cannot be PLANNED on CUDA: cuFFT rejects any non-strictly-increasing FFT region, so only the default (1 2 3) fold reaches plan_rfft there, and a permuted-fold device test that transforms would fail in the vendor library rather than in anything the package controls. Everything that does not build a plan is unaffected, and the GPU suite exercises exactly that: it moves a permuted-fold spectral field to the device and checks inner and radial_spectrum against the host. So the restriction is on the transform, not on the layout — a device test needing an actual transform on a folded layout must use the default one.\n\nGrid coverage. Use a mixed-parity grid (even and odd axis lengths, e.g. (8, 7, 6)) so the Nyquist split is exercised; where the Nyquist mode matters, include a field that actually populates it and confirm the derivative of a real field stays real.\n\nThe coverage axes — the explicit denominator (2026-07-23). The checklist above is exhaustive only over the axes it names; a bug class living on an unnamed axis is invisible to it by construction. Three defects taught this in one week (decisions 41/42 and the NUFFT layout wrapper): each sat on an input-space axis that existed in the type system yet was never varied by any test — the rfft fold fft_dims had exactly one value ever constructed, and the adjointness fixtures deliberately dodged the Nyquist band. So the denominator is written down. Every operation's coverage is judged against the full axis list:\n\nrepresentation (physical/spectral, including the asserted intentional absences);\ntensor order R;\nelement type (Float64/Float32) on the CPU lane — GPU parity alone leaves the default Pkg.test() blind to the Float32 numerics;\nrfft layout — the fold must follow a non-default fft_dims (the permuted-layout lane, §2);\naxis parity (even and odd, folded and unfolded);\ngrid-size cubicity (distinct extents, e.g. (8, 6, 4) — a cubic-only suite cannot see a transposed extent);\ndomain-length anisotropy (distinct per-axis 2piL factors appearing in the oracle's own formula, so a dropped factor cannot cancel);\norigin ne 0 where the operation consumes coordinates (the transfer layer);\ndegenerate bases (an empty PointSet; an axis of length 1 or 2) — pinned or asserted-absent;\nα/β including the alpha = 0 beta ne 0 branch (the pure beta-rescale);\naliasing patterns per arity (dest === a and dest === b are different hazards);\nNyquist content — at least one fixture populating the Nyquist planes, including the mixed case  (Nyquist on one axis, resolved on another); a deliberately band-limited fixture states why  (the dissipation identity is the standing example);\nbackend (GPU parity — remembering parity is shared-bug-blind, so an analytic oracle on some  CPU lane must also cover the non-default shapes);\nthread count (bitwise for the gated routes, tolerance for the two Atomix accumulators);\nkernel storage class, for every entry point that takes one — StoredKernel, SeparableKernel,  RadialKernel and the cross-family ProductKernel must agree bitwise with the materialized  twin, since decision 60 made these four an abstract hierarchy rather than one stored form. The  lane exists (§2); this row records it as an axis, because the class is chosen by the builder  a consumer calls and so varies independently of every axis above it;\nmultiplier provenance — canonical (derived from the grid by the convenience constructor) versus  custom (supplied through the six-array form, which validates lengths and not values). Decision 61  made this a type parameter, so it selects dispatch: two canonical sets collapse under convolve  while any custom set keeps both operands. It is an axis rather than a case because a kernel built on  a custom set is admissible input to every kernel-taking entry point, not just to composition.\nstorage family — Array on the host against a device array (CuArray, ROCArray). Decision 63  made crossing it an explicit operation, adapt, so a section, its multipliers, its kernels and its  plans are all admissible in either family, and decision 69 added adapt! for a destination that  already exists (sections only). The CPU lane drives adapt with a custom adaptor, since  adapting to the family one already occupies is the identity and would exercise nothing; for  adapt! the gate proves everything except the crossing — destination returned and filled,  metadata untouched, each mismatch refused, the aliased no-op, and zero allocation — because a  host-only run has no second family to cross into. The crossing itself, in both directions and for  the point layer, is the GPU suite's. Partial NA on one combination, with its reason: a permuted  RealFourierLayout cannot be planned on CUDA, cuFFT rejecting a non-strictly-increasing FFT  region, so a device test that transforms on a folded layout must use the default one. Operations  that build no plan are unrestricted, and the GPU suite does cross the two axes there. That is a vendor limit  rather than a package one, and it makes the permuted folds of decision 41 a CPU-side generality.\nOn CUDA the axis has a second value inside one family: device against unified memory  (decision 84), selected by a CUDA.jl preference rather than by any argument. The lane  test/gpu/cuda-unified refuses to run unless the preference took effect, asserts that every  allocating route lands in unified memory, runs a working set of 1.25 times the card, and then the  whole GPU suite — 494 of 494 and 44 of 44 on 2026-09-17. It cannot re-check invariant 6, because  CUDA.jl does not honour allowscalar(false) for unified arrays, so it adds to the device lane and  never replaces it. Per-field placement is not an admissible input: the family constructors read only  an element type, and their docstrings say so.\ncomponent-offset alignment, per FFT backend (decision 76). A rank-R field's component c  begins at element offset (c-1)prod_a N_a, and a transform library may refuse a pointer whose  alignment differs from the one it planned for — so the plans, which now carry no tensor order and  are applied one component at a time, must work at every offset. The two backends ask different  questions and the axis has a value for each: on the host FFTW records an alignment class, and a  grid whose component stride is not a multiple of 64 bytes is planned UNALIGNED; on a device  cuFFT needs only the real pointer aligned to its complex element, which fails exactly when the  grid is odd on all three axes, and such a grid transforms through a component-sized buffer  instead. The file test/fields.jl carries the lane — every rank over (8,8,8), (8,7,6), (7,5,3) and  (9,9,9), plus the predicate itself asserted directly — and the GPU suite carries the staged half,  since the host never reaches it.\nstencil width parity, for any scheme placing a stencil by an integer offset. A width-W  window around a point at mathrmbase + t, t in 0 1), has the W nearest nodes at one  fixed offset only when W is even; for odd W that set is centred on mathrmbase  while t  12 and on mathrmbase+1 after, so a fixed offset sits a whole cell off centre.\nThe NUFFT shipped that way, and every tol selecting an odd width missed its target by  10^2–10^3, a tighter request returning a worse result than a looser one. It stayed  invisible because every NUFFT(…; tol = …) call in the suite picked an even width, leaving the  odd branch never numerically exercised — a parameter with two regimes, tested in one.  test/regressions/nufft-odd-width-mis-centered.jl carries the lane.\nthe adjoint verb, wherever an operation ships as a pair — the transfer's interpolate and  anterpolate, the transforms' to_spectral and to_physical, a kernel and its inv,  write_* and read_*. An axis the two verbs share — the transfer scheme, the stencil width,  the grid, the element type — is not swept until it has been swept on each of them, and  \"swept the axis\" is false until then.\nThis axis is recorded because a benchmark sweep taught it, which is why it is also named in  the coverage contract of PERFORMANCE.md §5. Finding (ad) varied the scheme on  the gather and left the scatter pinned at CatmullRom, so the width-8 scatter was never measured  at any width — and that is the cell which then regressed 145–148times at 512^3 and  shipped for two days (finding (am)). The axis existed in the code with exactly one value ever  taken, which is the shape this list exists to catch. It generalises past the transfer: an adjoint  pair is two configurations, never one.\nthe object's own display, for every type that stores a field-sized array. A section's largest  field is its data, so the default struct display prints it — not a cosmetic problem but a  correctness one, since the same string is what an exception interpolating the object carries.\nThe axis has two values, both to be swept: the compact two-argument show, which a container, a  $(...) interpolation and an error message use, and the three-argument MIME\"text/plain\" form  the REPL uses.\nIts acceptance test is discrimination — any two objects a compatibility check refuses must  render differently, a display that collides on exactly the pairs it exists to explain being worse  than none. Decision 82 measured four mutually incompatible fields rendering as one string. A  derived gate enforces the axis rather than a list of types, so a new type ships covered or fails.\nthe grid-size ratio, for any operation relating two grids over one box (decision 85). One  ratio is not a sweep: a coarse grid half the fine one shares almost every mode, while a block a  tenth its size shares almost none, and an index map can be right for one and wrong for the other.\nThe axis carries three values that are not interchangeable — a ratio near one, a ratio of several,  and a coarse grid whose axes are of mixed parity against a fine grid of different parity — because  what the map must get right is where each frequency lands when the two grids disagree about which  index holds it. Each value is swept for BOTH verbs, by axis 20's rule.\nthe operand count, for any operation taking as many operands as its caller passes — add!  and subtract! over sections, map! over sources, displace! over terms, weighted_inner over  a tuple of weights (decision 88). A count of two or three, which is all a solver usually passes,  exercises none of what breaks: each of these works through its operands in fixed-size groups,  and Julia takes a generic path for tuples of 32 or more entries.\nThe axis carries three values, all to be swept: a count below the group size, a count just past  it, and a count past 32. At each, the result must equal the ungrouped arithmetic exactly, a  destination that is also a LATER operand must give the same answer, and the serial allocation  must stay zero. An allocation row must build its call with every operand written out: a splat  of more than 32 values allocates at the call site, before the package is entered, and would  measure the test.\n\nThe operator inverse_curl (decision 77) is covered on every axis this list names that applies to it: both element types, three grids including an anisotropic mixed-parity one, the aliased in-place call asserted bitwise, the zero mode, the gauge, and — because it is a gated CPU/device pair — bitwise thread-count invariance and the decision-47 reachability probe in test/threading.jl. Its oracle is a closed-form pair differentiated by hand rather than the package's own curl, per decision 55.\n\nA transfer's TRAVERSAL ORDER is a coverage axis (decision 78): the four point-indexed verbs each have two routes through one kernel. The file test/point_traversal_order.jl covers it by the property rather than by a sample — the gather is BITWISE equal with and without an order, and again under a reversed one, so the invariance belongs to the order rather than to the one the builder happens to produce.\n\nThe in-place rebuild (decision 80) is checked the same way, and the choice matters: point_traversal_order! is compared to point_traversal_order block-wise, not index-wise. Both forms place points within a block through atomics, which fix no order inside it, so an index-wise comparison would assert a property neither form has and would fail intermittently — it would be testing the atomics rather than the operation. What the transfer actually sees is the sequence of blocks, and that is deterministic. The lane also moves the points with displace! and rebuilds, since following the points is the whole reason the form exists, and asserts the refusals for each of the three peers that can disagree — the order, the point set and the scratch.\n\nTwo promise kinds are gate-enforced with derived denominators. test/peer_rejections.jl requires every operation with a reachable _check_* predicate, minus the carrier gate's registry, to refuse a peer on a foreign context; test/absence_gate.jl calls every representation-asymmetric operation on the side invariant 1 leaves absent and requires the refusal. Both carry completeness assertions in both directions, so a new validating or asymmetric operation fails the suite until its disposition is decided.\n\nThe value lane is audited PER METHOD by test/exhaustive/method_ledger.jl, which asks of every (operation, method) cell whether an assertion reached it — derived from coverage, not declared. It reads 572 covered, 3 closed by a recorded reason, 0 uncovered. So a new operation needs a row that actually dispatches to each of its methods, and the survey fails when a cell has neither a row nor a recorded reason.\n\nThe rule. A new type parameter, constructor argument, or admissible input region is a new axis: it gets a row in this list and a lane in the suite in the same change, or an explicit NA with its reason. \"Exhaustive\" means exhaustive over this written list — an axis missing from the list is the failure mode, so extending the list is part of extending the type system. The cross-file permuted-layout lane (§2) and the anisotropic-box operator and radial testsets are the reference implementations.","title":"4. The coverage contract"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#5.-GPU-testing","page":"Testing","text":"GPU testing is opt-in and dependency-free for the package. The default Pkg.test() stays backend-free and portable; a GPU backend is only ever a test dependency. This is possible because the data layer is generic over AbstractArray, FFT plans are built through AbstractFFTs (dispatched on the array type), and allocations inherit their backend via similar — so a field on a ROCArray/CuArray just works.\n\nPer-backend environments live under test/gpu/ (rocm/ and cuda/). Each is instantiated only on matching hardware (AMD at home, NVIDIA at the office); a no-GPU CI box never installs a vendor stack.\nA single backend-agnostic test/gpu/suite.jl holds the test logic. A thin per-backend entry script (test/gpu/<backend>/runtests.jl) supplies to_device, device_functional, GPU_BACKEND, and calls <Backend>.allowscalar(false) before including the suite. With scalar indexing disabled, any stray scalar index into device memory inside a package method throws — turning invariant 6 into a checked guarantee rather than a convention.\nStrategy: run the public surface on device arrays and compare against the trusted CPU result (matches, with an atol floor per §3). If no device is present the suite warns and skips.\nManifests are gitignored (repo convention — none are tracked); each environment reproduces from its Project.toml plus a [compat] bound, via Pkg.instantiate().\n\nRun it on hardware:\n\njulia --project=test/gpu/rocm -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'   # one-time\njulia --project=test/gpu/rocm test/gpu/rocm/runtests.jl\n\nAdding a backend = mirror rocm/ (see test/gpu/README.md); the suite needs no changes.","title":"5. GPU testing"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#6.-Maintenance","page":"Testing","text":"After any code-generation change, re-verify the method set is unchanged: update the counts in test/method_parity_baseline.toml only with a deliberate, reviewed reason, and keep detect_ambiguities empty.\nKeep the test summary in CLAUDE.md (roadmap item 4) roughly current.\nAqua.jl (test/aqua.jl) runs Aqua.test_all on every Pkg.test() — ambiguities, unbound type parameters, undefined exports, project/compat consistency, stale deps, type piracy, persistent tasks. Keep it green; among other things it enforces that every dependency carries a [compat] bound.\nJET.jl (test/jet.jl) runs @test_call on the public operations, filtered to the package's own modules — a per-Julia-version static check for inference-detected errors (MethodError, BoundsError, undefined variables). It is left uncapped in test/Project.toml so each CI Julia version resolves a compatible JET. Note it analyzes the running version, so it does not catch version-divergence (that is the CI matrix's job).\nCI (.github/workflows/CI.yml) is manual-only — workflow_dispatch plus release tags, not every push: each commit is authored locally and the full matrix is run before pushing, so a push-triggered run would only re-verify what is already green (a tagged release additionally exercises a from-scratch clean-environment resolve, the one thing a single local run cannot give). The matrix is Julia 1.12 (the compat floor) at -t1 and current stable at -t4, so both lanes run on one Julia and differ only in thread count — running them locally needs no manifest juggling. The floor was 1.11 until it was dropped: the package ran faster there, but every operation cost several times more to compile (_assign! alone 4.5×), which made the local suite take about six times as long for a Julia nothing in the fleet runs.\nPerformance is tracked separately, not by Pkg.test(): the opt-in benchmark/ suite and its coverage contract live in PERFORMANCE.md (the third rationale companion).","title":"6. Maintenance"},{"category":"section","location":"testing/#7.-Current-coverage-and-known-gaps","page":"Testing","text":"Honest status, so the contract above reads as the target rather than a description.\n\nCovered: FFT round-trips, the transforms' input contracts — the allocating forms preserving their inputs, and the decision-64 scratch arity's bitwise preservation, its bitwise equality with the allocating form, and its aliasing and context refusals; spectral multipliers cross-checked against AbstractFFTs.fftfreq/rfftfreq; the Nyquist split both at the multiplier level and end-to-end (the derivative wavenumber zeroed vs the squared wavenumber kept at the Nyquist mode); M2 + M3 algebra and contractions in both representations, including the weighted combination combine! (physical + spectral, alias-safe) and the physical-only determinant det (against a per-point LU oracle, with its spectral absence asserted); the spectral zero-mode constant invariant; transfer-scheme HDF5 persistence (decision 75) — the parameter round trip asserted with === and reinforced behaviourally, every malformed-group path refused for its own reason, and a perturbation-verified guard that a new scheme family cannot ship unpersistable; the allocating constructors (decision 74) — each (family, grid[, layout]) field type-identical to its data-built twin and to similar_field of that twin, across both element types, two layout folds, orders through R = 4, the default-layout arity, a transform round trip through freshly allocated scratch, and the two refusals (a parameterized family disagreeing with the grid's element type; a complex parameter); the point-value algebra — the Points module's M2 + M3 mirror of the physical field surface (storage-wise broadcasting), checked per-point on CPU across Float64/Float32 (test/pointvalues_algebra.jl) with the point-data types in test/interpolation.jl; spatial motion (test/displacement.jl, decision 73) — displace! against an independent raw-array oracle over both element types and one-to-three terms, the destination/source form with its source preserved and its aliased call agreeing, the fused torus arity bitwise-equal to cover-then-wrap!, in-box membership on an anisotropic box, the wrap contract's three pins (the folded endpoint with the mod(-1e-20, 2π) === 2π assumption asserted, non-finite pass-through for NaN/±Inf, the origin-shifted fundamental domain), sampling's whole-period invariance at tolerance, the mixed-precision / mismatched-count / mixed-family refusals, and the threading override bitwise; the in-place ! forms with caller-supplied destinations and the aliased in-place calls (cross!, transpose!, sym!/skew!/dev!, curl! — alias-safe by construction since decisions 38/39, bitwise-identical to non-aliased), plus the guard-free aliased helmholtz_projection! (bitwise-equal to non-aliased; decision 32); the kernel-composed operator forms (helmholtz_projection!/divergence! with a trailing kernel and α/β, equal to the sequential operator-then-convolve! composition, with the alpha = -1 sign fold and kernel-aliasing cases); the intentional absences in both representations (nonlinear products absent in spectral, differential operators absent in physical) and the physical/spectral symmetry of every linear field/fiber operation; the differential operators by analytic round-trip, including an anisotropic (2pi 4pi 6pi) box that exercises the 2piL wavenumber scaling; the fused symmetric_gradient (matching sym(gradient) exactly, plus its own analytic diagonal on the anisotropic box); the vector-calculus identities nablacdot(nablatimes u) = 0 and nablatimes(nablavarphi) = 0; element-type genericity on CPU (Float64 and Float32, asserting element-type preservation through operations); the 206-method field and 122-method point-value parity baselines and zero ambiguities; Aqua.test_all (ambiguities, unbound type parameters, undefined exports, Project/compat consistency, stale dependencies, type piracy, persistent tasks); the operator adjoint/structural identities on randomized band-limited fields — divergence as the negative gradient adjoint, self-adjoint curl, a self-adjoint negative-semidefinite Laplacian (with the dissipation identity), and an exactly idempotent, self-adjoint Helmholtz projection (decision 42; the full-spectrum Nyquist-content identities live in differentialoperators.jl) — with the L² inner product computed inline (the global L² inner now lives in the Reductions module, below); and GPU parity on ROCm across Float64 and Float32 (allowscalar(false)) — the public field surface (including the mul! beta = 0 / beta ne 0 branches), confirmed on an AMD Radeon, plus the PointValues algebra, the interpolation / anterpolation transfer (gather, atomic scatter, accumulate, plus a heavy atomic-contention case), the spectral transfer (DirectDFT and NUFFT), convolution (apply a Fourier kernel), and the L²/measure reductions added alongside; and CPU threading — every threaded assignment is bitwise-identical to serial (field ops, the codegen contractions/products including mul!'s beta=0/betane0 branches, transpose/sym/skew/dev, norms, and the differential operators), exercised in both the default and a JULIA_NUM_THREADS=4 lane (test/threading.jl), with the override / cutoff gate and the GPU materialize! fallback also asserted — and, separately, the KernelAbstractions kernels, which thread by KA's own partitioning rather than through _assign! and so cannot be checked by toggling with_threading at all: the disjoint-write gather is asserted bitwise, including against a subprocess launched at a different thread count, while the two Atomix accumulators (the anterpolation scatter, the shell binning) are held to a tolerance because a nondeterministic summation order perturbs their last bits. The helmholtz_projection! operator is checked with the gated operations instead, decision 40 having given it a CPU loop twin that the with_threading toggle does reach, and across thread counts as well (ARCHITECTURE.md §9); and the L²/measure reductions (test/reductions.jl): integral/mean/inner/l2norm/lpnorm/std/var/maximum/minimum/extrema on physical fields and point-values, against analytic oracles (langle sinrangle = 0, langle sin^2rangle = tfrac12) and manual references, across Float64/Float32, with the arithmetic-mean semantics on both bases (tfrac1Nsum grid, tfrac1Msum points), the extensive/intensive relation between integral and mean — the one is the other times the base measure — (incl. an anisotropic box), the energy identity tfrac12 textttinner(uu) = tfrac12langle u^2rangle, the spectral Parseval inner/l2norm (equal to the physical inner, scalar/vector/tensor, on even- and odd-N_x grids (N_x the first-axis point count) to hit both the Nyquist and no-Nyquist branches), the multiplier-weighted weighted_inner langle f w grangle (the w equiv 1 reduction to inner, the w = k^2 Dirichlet energy checked against both -langle f Delta frangle and langle nabla f nabla frangle, the idempotent-band identity lVert w star frVert^2, and the f leftrightarrow g symmetry — scalar/vector/tensor, spectral-only), the spectral zero-mode integral/mean (equal to the physical reductions, read from the DC coefficient; scalar/vector/tensor, even/odd grids, with lpnorm/maximum still absent in spectral representation — std/var are not absent, having gained their Parseval form in decision 49), the shell-resolved Parseval inner radial_spectrum (the radial power spectrum P(kappa) (kappa the wavenumber-shell magnitude), checked by the exact refinement sum_kappa P(kappa) = langle f frangle over scalar/vector/tensor on even-N_x and odd-N_x grids, plus the single-mode shell placement and its spectral-only absence), its physical-space dual radial_correlation (the Wiener–Khinchin two-point autocorrelation, checked against an independent binned-circshift autocorrelation and C(0) = textttinner(ff), all orders / both grids), the two-field co-spectra radial_spectrum(f, g) / radial_correlation(f, g) (the cross-power / cross-correlation, refining the mixed inner(f, g): analytical oracles — an orthogonal pair cos x perp sin x ⇒ zero, and cos x pm cos 2x ⇒ pmtfrac12 in adjacent shells cancelling to textttinner = 0 (the co-spectrum may be negative); the f leftrightarrow g symmetry; the g = f reduction to the single-field forms (bit-identical); and an independent binned-circshift cross-correlation on a random pair — scalar/vector/tensor, both grids; the shared k/r shell binning is a single scatter-add pass — KernelAbstractions / Atomix, device-clean, no scalar indexing (decision 25) — so these oracles plus the GPU parity suite also cover that histogram kernel), the mapped forms (a leading function applied to the fiber value at every base point, decision 30: the analytic invariants of a constant diagonal tensor — det = 24, operatornametr = 9 — the identity map reproducing the bare fiber-valued reductions, the analytic langle sin^2rangle = tfrac12, the coincidence max_n lVert u_nrVert = lVert urVert_infty linking maximum(norm, u) to the materializing lpnorm(u, Inf), a random tensor against an independent explicit per-point reduction, the counting-measure forms on point-values, and the spectral absence of the whole family), and the intentional absence of the measure reductions in spectral representation; and convolution (test/convolution.jl): periodic convolution as the coefficientwise product hat f cdot hat g with a StoredKernel, checked against the translation kernel exp(-mathrmi k_a m Delta x_a) (a shift of m cells, with Delta x_a the grid spacing along axis a) Leftrightarrow circshift along all three axes, on scalar and vector fields (an independent physical oracle), the identity / scaling / cutoff kernels over scalar, vector, and tensor (R=2) fields (the 5-D fiber broadcast), sharp-cutoff idempotence, kernel-composition associativity, the α/β accumulate form (incl. R=2), in-place aliasing safety, a mixed-parity (8,7,6) grid (odd axis + Nyquist), the lazy exponential kernel exponential_convolve! (decision 33: equality against the same kernel stored and applied with convolve!, the independent single-mode eigenvalue oracle e^ccos x, the α/β accumulate, the scale-times-field offset, both two-operand forms equal to the sequential combine!-then-convolve pair, and the in-place filtered accumulate), the kernel-composed combine! (fused equals sequential over scalar and vector fields, the b = textttdest accumulate, the kernel aliasing the destination, and the physical absence), the spectral_band annular band-pass (spectral_cutoff as its wavenumber_low = 0 case, idempotent, a k^2 membership oracle) and radial_kernel (the builder for an arbitrary real function of k^2, checked at sigma = mathrmidentity against the analytic sum, real-valued), across Float64/Float32, and the physical-space intentional absence on fields in physical representation; tophat_window, the box average of decision 87 (the box mean of a single mode, cos(k cdot x) prod_a operatornamesinc(k_a l_a  2), as an independent oracle on an anisotropic box with edges that are not whole cells; a domain-sized box keeping the mean alone; a zero edge as the identity, bitwise; a scalar edge equal to the cube; assignment_window equal, bitwise, to the cell-wide window composed with itself; and the refusal of negative and non-finite edges); and — for translation_kernel, the displacement kernel of decision 28 — the same circshift oracle but on a random field, which unlike the band-limited fields above carries Nyquist content and so exercises the one frequency where the kernel is a real attenuation rather than a phase (a whole-cell displacement, odd and even, along each axis), the exact DirectDFT evaluation at the displaced nodes as the oracle for a sub-cell displacement, an analytic displaced cosine, the reality check that a displaced half-spectrum survives a transform round trip (the inverse transform is the Hermitian projector, so a kernel built from the true Nyquist phase would move), the Nyquist attenuation itself (a half-cell displacement annihilates cos(4x) on an 8-point 2pi axis; a whole-cell one flips its sign), unit modulus on the resolved band, composition and inversion, the fiber broadcast over vector and tensor fields, and the mixed-parity grid; the point pair-correlation (rdf.jl, decision 22 Phase 2): assignment_window against its prod operatornamesinc^2 formula, and the FFT g(r) recipe (anterpolate density → deconvolve → radial_correlation) validated against an independent direct O(M^2) physical-space pair count on a clustered set (the clustering peak within ~10% and the g → 1 baseline); and property-based tests (randomized.jl): algebraic laws on random fields, point-values, and kernels over several trials — vector-space and contraction/product identities, transpose/sym/skew/dev, the reduction laws (inner symmetry, linearity, Cauchy–Schwarz, textttintegral = Omegacdottextttmean), convolution bilinearity/commutativity, and the operator identities nablacdot(nablatimes u)=0, nablatimes(nabla varphi)=0 — which catch the index/sign bugs that specific analytic functions can mask.\n\nGaps:\n\nDocstring doctests — the Getting-started tutorial is doctested (§2, the third checked layer), but the per-function docstrings in src/ do not yet carry their own jldoctest examples.\nEven-broader randomized coverage — randomized.jl now covers the algebra, reductions, and convolution laws on random CPU data; extending property checks onto the device, and to more element types, is possible but lower-value (the laws are formula-level and backend-independent).","title":"7. Current coverage and known gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/","page":"Formalization","text":"info: Written by AI, not regularly reviewed\nThis page is written by AI and is not reviewed regularly by a human.","title":"Formalization"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Formalization-—-GBGPeriodicFields","page":"Formalization","text":"This page is the formal backing for the mathematical statements made informally in Architecture, the module docstrings, and the API pages. For each nontrivial operation it records a typed statement — every object given its space, every operator its domain and codomain — and checks each claim by expansion, with the definitions taken from the implementation (src/) and a link to the test that verifies the claim numerically.\n\nIt exists as a precision check, not as a page a user reads. Writing a statement formally forces every type and normalization into the open, which is exactly where imprecision in the prose becomes visible: the all-ones mathbf1 that was secretly two different vectors, or a section space written mathbb R^N that had quietly flattened the fiber away. These are hand-verified notes, not machine-checked proofs — the deterministic check is the linked test (the test suite plays the role of the compiler), so a statement here with no test is a coverage gap to close.\n\nnote: Coordinate forms live here\nThe coordinate-free preference governs the user-facing prose. This page is the sanctioned home for the coordinate/matrix/component expansions — a basis, the matrix entries W_pn, the all-ones mathbf1_N / mathbf1_M — because checking precision requires expanding into components. The statements here must still be correct; they need not be coordinate-free.","title":"Formalization — GBGPeriodicFields"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Declarations","page":"Formalization","text":"Fixed throughout (definitions read from src/):\n\nbeginaligned\nV        = (mathbb R^3)^otimes R   textfiber tensor order  R (dim 3^R)\nGamma_G = mathbb R^N_xotimesmathbb R^N_yotimesmathbb R^N_zotimes V textgrid sections  N = N_x N_y N_z text cells\nGamma_P = mathbb R^M otimes V        textpoint sections  M text scattered points\nendaligned\n\nA grid cell is a triple (i_1 i_2 i_3) of per-axis indices (a standalone k is reserved for the wavenumber below; as a subscripted component index — varepsilon_ijk, A_ikB_kj — it keeps its ordinary meaning, the two never colliding since one is a free symbol and the other always sits in a subscript run). The same rule governs a, which below is the derivative wavenumber — the Nyquist-zeroed family, kept apart from the true k — when it stands alone or carries a numeric component subscript (a_1 a_2 a_3), and is an ordinary per-axis index when it sits in a subscript or a product bound (k_a, Delta x_a, prod_a=1^3). So the scalar field over the grid is the 3-axis tensor product mathbb R^N_xotimesmathbb R^N_yotimesmathbb R^N_z (the array f_i_1 i_2 i_3) — one real per cell. As a vector space this is mathbb R^N with N = N_x N_y N_z: a column-major flat index n collapses (i_1 i_2 i_3), and the global pairings below use that flat n (the per-axis structure is irrelevant to them — it is what the tensor-product stencil weights w_i_1w_i_2w_i_3 (the per-axis transfer weights, defined in the interpolation section below) and the differential multipliers mathrmi k_a (k_a a component of the wavenumber k, set up with the transforms below) act on). The factor otimes V then attaches the fiber, so a grid section is a V-value at each cell (cong V^N; a single real only in the scalar case R = 0). The point base mathbb R^M is unstructured — M scattered points, no product — which is exactly the structured-to-unstructured nature of the transfer. A point's position x_p in mathbb R^3 is a separate object from the value it carries: the positions are the PointSet geometry (mathbb R^M otimes mathbb R^3 cong mathbb R^3M, themselves a vector-valued section over the points) and enter only through the transfer weights w_n(x_p) (the interpolation stencil weight of grid node n at position x_p, defined in the interpolation section), whereas Gamma_P is the space of values — a V per point. So Gamma_P is mathbb R^3M only when the sampled field is a vector (V = mathbb R^3); in general it is mathbb R^M otimes V, just as the grid value space is not mathbb R^3N.\n\nThe transform mathcal F (to_spectral, inverse to_physical) is the rfft isomorphism between the physical (nodal) and spectral representations of a grid section; hat f = mathcal F f. Its codomain needs naming, because the obvious candidate is wrong: mathcal F is not onto the stored complex array mathbb C^N_k_x N_y N_z otimes V, which has strictly more real dimensions than Gamma_G. The image is the Hermitian-constrained subspace: the planes the folded axis maps to itself under k mapsto -k (the zero plane, and on an even axis the Nyquist plane) are closed under conjugation, so each stored coefficient there has its partner stored too and the pair must satisfy hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k). Individual coefficients on those planes are not real — only the self-paired modes, k = -k, are. That constraint is exactly what makes the map a bijection onto its image and the round trip exact. Every \"coefficients of a real field\" statement below means an element of that subspace, which is also why a kernel must be Hermitian for its result to be one. With the fiber contraction cdot  V times V to mathbb R and the wavenumber k, the inner products are the package's normalized reductions (inner, src/Reductions.jl = dot(data, data) / _base_count):\n\nbegingathered\nlangle a brangle_mathrmpts = frac1M sum_p=1^M a_pcdot b_p \nlangle f grangle_mathrmgrid = frac1N sum_n=1^N f_ncdot g_n \nendgathered","title":"Declarations"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Representations-and-transforms-(Fields)","page":"Formalization","text":"Design rationale: Architecture §4.\n\nThe transform mathcal F is forward unnormalized, inverse carrying 1N (AbstractFFTs convention, plan_rfft/plan_irfft), with k ranging over the wavenumbers and x over the N grid nodes — where x denotes a node's offset from the grid origin o, not its absolute position. A grid carries an arbitrary origin, so the absolute node position is o + x; writing the pair in the offset coordinate is what makes it agree with the spectral transfer below, whose phase e^mathrmikcdot(x_p - o) subtracts that origin explicitly because a point x_p is given in absolute coordinates:\n\nbegingathered\nhat f(k) = sum_x f(x) e^-mathrmi kcdot x \nf(x) = frac1N sum_k hat f(k) e^mathrmi kcdot x\nendgathered\n\nso mathcal F^-1mathcal F = mathrmid (exact to round-off). Two consequences used elsewhere:\n\nHalf-spectrum + conjugate symmetry. f real Rightarrow hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k), so only k_x ge 0 is stored (_rfft_shape: length N_x2+1 on the rfft axis — the folded axis is fft_dims[1], the first by default, and x names it throughout); the k_x = 0 plane, and on an even axis the Nyquist plane (the highest stored frequency, k_x = piDelta x), are self-conjugate.\nAdding a real constant c touches only the zero mode, scaled by N: mathcal F(f + c) = hat f + c N delta_k0 (_spectral_constant_scale = prod(grid_size)), since the unnormalized forward sums N copies of c at k = 0. checkmark\n\nThe scratch arity to_physical!(physical, spectral, plans, spectral_scratch) (decision 64) adds nothing to this map — it computes the same mathcal F^-1 — and one contract: spectral returns bitwise-unchanged, the copy into the scratch being what the transform consumes instead, with no allocation. Aliasing scratch with source is refused, the shared storage making the contract unsatisfiable. checkmark\n\nThe reality condition (decision 71). A half-spectrum is the spectrum of a REAL field iff it is Hermitian, hat f(-k) = overlinehat f(k). The rfft stores only non-negative wavenumbers on the folded axis, so for almost every mode the partner is not stored and the condition is automatic; it can be violated only on a self-conjugate plane of the folded axis — the zero plane, and on an even-length axis the Nyquist plane — where a mode's partner lies in the same plane. The reality_residual diagnostic measures the departure,\n\nbeginaligned\ntextttreality_residual(hat f) = max_k in textself-conjugate planes \nqquad bigllvert hat f(k) - overlinehat f(-k) bigrrvert \nendaligned\n\nwhich is zero when the coefficients are a real field's. A spectrum the package itself transforms from real data satisfies the condition only to round-off — FFTW is exact at some power-of-two sizes and ulp-level elsewhere, rocFFT and cuFFT ulp-level at every size (measured 2026-09-05) — so \"zero for anything the package produces\" holds to the ulp and no further. It reports and never repairs; the repair is the projector below. Cost is O(N^2) — one or two planes — against the O(N^3 log N) of a physical round trip, which is what makes it usable as a check.\n\nThe reality projection (decision 83). Let mathcal H be the real-linear subspace of stored half-spectra satisfying the condition on the self-conjugate planes. The operation reality_projection! is the map that, on every stored plane and for every pair k -k within it, sets\n\nbeginaligned\nhat f(k) hat f(-k) leftarrow tfrac12 bigl( hat f(k) + overlinehat f(-k) bigr) \noverlinetfrac12 bigl( hat f(k) + overlinehat f(-k) bigr)\nendaligned\n\na self-paired corner reducing to operatornameRe hat f(k) by the same formula, and leaves every other coefficient unchanged. Writing the pair as a point of mathbb C^2 cong mathbb R^4, the condition is the two-dimensional real subspace (a bar a) and the map is its orthogonal projection in the Euclidean inner product — which is the Parseval inner product restricted to the plane, each stored coefficient counted once — so P is mathbb R-linear, P^2 = P exactly in floating point (a projected pair is bit-identical under a second application), and textttreality_residual(P hat f) = 0 exactly, since both members are written from one value m as m and bar m. For a per-mode multiplier m with m(-k) = overlinem(k) — every differential operator's symbol, the exponential and radial kernels, the translation phases — P(m hat f) = m P hat f, because the multiplier maps mathcal H to itself. The physical round trip mathcal F mathcal F^-1 is the same projector up to the transform's round-off; the inverse real transform discards the anti-Hermitian part, and the forward transform reseeds it at the ulp. checkmark\n\nBacks the decision log, decision 83 and the reality_projection! docstring. Checked by test/fields.jl (reality_projection!) — the identity on an exact spectrum, agreement with an elementwise pair-mean oracle over the ORIGINAL coefficients, off-plane coefficients bitwise untouched, exact idempotence, round-trip agreement to 64 varepsilon, linearity, commutation with the Laplacian symbol, the two-argument form, the domain refusal and the physical absence — over both element types, tensor orders 0/1/2, permuted folds and an odd folded axis; and test/gpu/suite.jl (device against host, the device result exactly Hermitian).\n\nThe mean-free projection (decision 90). For a field f on the box Omega, let Q be Q f = f - lvertOmegarvert^-1 int_Omega f mathrmdx, applied per fiber component. Under the forward-unnormalized transform the zero coefficient is hat f(0) = sum_x f(x), the sum over the M grid points, and the discrete mean is hat f(0)  M; so Q sets hat f(0) leftarrow 0 in every component and leaves every other coefficient unchanged. That write is exactly what mean_free_projection! performs. The map Q is linear, and Q^2 = Q bit for bit, a zero written twice being the same zero. It is orthogonal in the Parseval inner product, since the zero mode's coefficient is orthogonal to every other's, and the result's mean is 0  M = 0 exactly, with no rounding. Both parts are cleared: the zero coefficient of a real field is real, so an imaginary part there belongs to no real field's mean. checkmark\n\nThe divergence has exactly zero mean. For a vector field u, the computed zero coefficient of nablacdot u is mathrmi (a_1 hat u_1 + a_2 hat u_2 + a_3 hat u_3) at k = 0, where every derivative wavenumber a_j is exactly 0 for multipliers built from the grid; a product of a real zero with a finite complex number is zero, so the coefficient is exactly zero (possibly -0), matching int_Omega nablacdot u mathrmdx = 0 for a periodic field. The same holds componentwise for a second-order tensor, and in the kernel-composed form with beta = 0; with beta ne 0 the zero coefficient is beta times the destination's. checkmark\n\nBacks the decision log, decision 90, and the mean_free_projection! and divergence! docstrings. Checked by test/fields.jl (mean_free_projection!) — the zero coefficient exactly zero in both parts, every other coefficient bitwise untouched, agreement with the values less their arithmetic average computed in physical space, exact idempotence and the physical absence — over both element types, tensor orders 0/1/2, permuted folds and odd axes; test/differentialoperators.jl (the divergence's zero mean, vector and tensor, plain and kernel-composed); and test/gpu/suite.jl.\n\nBacks Architecture §4 and the Fields transform docstrings. Checked by test/fields.jl — FFT round-trip, multipliers vs fftfreq/rfftfreq, the Nyquist behaviour, transform input-preservation, and the scratch arity's bitwise preservation, its bitwise equality with the allocating form, and its aliasing and context refusals — and test/fieldalgebra.jl (the constant/zero-mode invariant).","title":"Representations and transforms (Fields)"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Fiberwise-algebra-(FieldAlgebra-/-FiberSections)","page":"Formalization","text":"Design rationale: Architecture §2, §6.\n\nThe vertical operations act inside the fiber V at each base point, identically over grid and point bases (one set of generated kernels, src/FiberSections.jl). A FiberVector is mathbb R^3, a FiberTensor2 genuinely a 3times3 matrix, so component notation is the natural form here (a repeated index is summed, and varepsilon_ijk is the Levi-Civita symbol):\n\nbeginaligned\nucdot v = u_i v_i  A  B = A_ijB_ij  (utimes v)_i = varepsilon_ijk u_j v_k\n(Au)_i = A_iju_j  (uA)_i = u_j A_ji  (AB)_ij = A_ikB_kj\n(A^top)_ij = A_ji  u = sqrtu_i u_i  A = sqrtA_ijA_ij\nendaligned\n\nThe decompositions are operatornamesym(A)=tfrac12(A+A^top), operatornameskew(A)=tfrac12(A-A^top), operatornamedev(A)=A-tfrac13(operatornametrA) I, so operatornamesym+operatornameskew=mathrmid and operatornametroperatornamedev(A)=0. The trace operatornametr(A)=A_ii — the first tensor invariant that dev removes — is exposed as tr/tr!, and being linear it is representation-preserving (the trace of the spectral coefficients is the spectral representation of operatornametrA). The third invariant, the determinant det A=varepsilon_ijkA_1iA_2jA_3k=I_3 (det/det!), is a homogeneous cubic form in the components, hence nonlinear — physical-space only, like the norm — and multiplicative det(AB)=det A det B; it reuses the one shared formula builder, so it mirrors tr onto both fields and point-values. checkmark The mul! family carries the α/β accumulate textttdest=alpha(cdot)+beta textttdest, and the weighted combination alpha a+beta b (combine!) is its linear two-field peer — coefficientwise, hence representation-preserving and alias-safe, with alpha=beta=1 the plain add!.\n\nBacks Architecture §2, §6, decision 27, and the FieldAlgebra docstrings. Checked by test/fieldalgebra.jl (and test/pointvalues_algebra.jl for the identical point-value mirror) — dot/cross, tensor products with α/β, transpose, norms, the trace, the determinant (against a per-point LU oracle), the weighted combination, and the sym/skew/dev identities.\n\nThe arbitrary fiber map (map!/map, decision 48) lifts a chosen map on the fibers, varphi  V_1 times dots times V_m to U, to a section map applied pointwise over the base: at each base point x, textttdestination(x) = varphibigl(s_1(x) dots s_m(x)bigr) for sources s_1 dots s_m. The output fiber space U — written U rather than W, which this page reserves for the gather operator — hence destination's tensor order — is varphi's codomain, which need not match the inputs' (varphi = det sends V = mathbb R^3times3 to mathbb R). It is the section-valued companion of the mapped reductions (which instead reduce varphi over the base) and the arbitrary-varphi generalization of the named vertical operations above; a general varphi is nonlinear, so — like det and the norm — it is physical-space only on fields (invariant 1), and representation-free on point-values. The single-source form carries the α/β accumulate textttdestination = alpha varphi(textttsource) + beta textttdestination. checkmark\n\nBacks Architecture §2, decision 48, and the map! / map docstring. Checked by test/fieldalgebra.jl and test/pointvalues_algebra.jl (against the named operations as oracles, expcircdet hand references, order changes, multi-input, α/β, aliasing, and the physical-only absence), GPU parity in test/gpu/suite.jl, allocation-freedom in test/allocations.jl, and the fused-ideal element traffic in test/pass_audit.jl.","title":"Fiberwise algebra (FieldAlgebra / FiberSections)"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Differential-operators-(DifferentialOperators)","page":"Formalization","text":"Design rationale: Architecture §4.\n\nEach operator L is diagonalized by mathcal F into a pointwise multiplier hat L — the intertwiner square mathcal Fcirc L = hat Lcircmathcal F — applied coefficientwise in spectral representation (src/DifferentialOperators.jl; a below is the derivative-wavenumber vector of the Nyquist split that follows, k the true wavenumber):\n\nbeginaligned\nnabla to mathrmia  nablacdot to mathrmiacdot  nablatimes to mathrmiatimes\nDelta to -k^2  Delta^-1 to -k^-2 (0text at k=0)  mathbb P to mathrmid - fraca a^topa cdot a (mathrmidtext where  acdot a = 0)\nendaligned\n\nNyquist split (even axes, deliberate — FieldSpectralMultipliers): the derivative wavenumber is zeroed at Nyquist (the Nyquist mode is a real cosine, so mathrmia_N taken from the true wavenumber would break reality), while the squared wavenumber keeps the true k^2 (correct Delta / Delta^-1). The a in mathbb P's multiplier above is that derivative wavenumber — the projector is built entirely from that one family (decision 42), and sub-Nyquist a = k. Identities, each a one-line spectral check:\n\nbegingathered\nnablacdot(nablatimes u) = 0  bigl(acdot(atimeshat u)=0bigr) qquad\nnablatimes(nablaphi) = 0  bigl(atimes mathrmiahatphi=0bigr) \nmathbb P^2 = mathbb P qquad nablacdot(mathbb P u) = 0 quadcheckmark\nendgathered\n\nall four unconditional — the first two because curl, divergence, and gradient share the one derivative-wavenumber family, the last two because mathbb P = mathrmid - (a a^top)(a cdot a) is built entirely from that same family (decision 42), the Nyquist planes included. The dissipation identity alone is scoped: on the resolved band — every mode off the even-axis Nyquist planes —\n\nlangle Deltaphiphirangle = -lVertnablaphirVert^2 le 0 quadcheckmark\n\nsince Delta keeps the true k^2 (invariant 5) against nabla's Nyquist-zeroed derivative family; with Nyquist content only the sign langleDeltaphiphirangle le 0 survives (test/adjointness.jl scopes its dissipation fixtures sub-Nyquist and asserts the sign unconditionally; the projector identities are pinned with full-spectrum Nyquist content in test/differentialoperators.jl).\n\n(mathbb P and Delta are self-adjoint — their multipliers mathrmid-(a a^top)(a cdot a), -k^2 are real symmetric.)\n\nThe divergence is the negative adjoint of the gradient, the one relation here between sections of different tensor order — nabla  Gamma_G^R=0 to Gamma_G^R=1 and nablacdot  Gamma_G^R=1 to Gamma_G^R=0, so the two inner products below are taken in different spaces (scalar sections on the right, vector sections on the left):\n\nlangle nablaphi urangle_Gamma^R=1 = -langle phi nablacdot urangle_Gamma^R=0\nquadcheckmark\n\nbecause the gradient's multiplier is mathrmia and the divergence's the contraction mathrmiacdot with the same derivative-wavenumber family a: moving the factor across the Hermitian pairing conjugates it, and overlinemathrmia = -mathrmia for real a supplies the sign. Unconditional — it needs only that both operators read one derivative family, the Nyquist planes included, which invariant 5 guarantees. Pinned in test/adjointness.jl.\n\nThe inverse curl inverts nablatimes on the solenoidal subspace. Writing a for the derivative wavenumber and mathbb S = hat u  acdothat u = 0 hat u(0) = 0 for the discretely divergence-free, mean-free fields, the operator is\n\n(nablatimes)^-1hat w(k) = fracmathrmi atimeshat wlvert arvert^2\nqquad k ne 0 quadcheckmark\n\nand zero at k = 0. Two statements hold, and the second is the reason numerator and denominator must come from the SAME wavenumber family. First, the range is mathbb S: expanding the triple product, acdot(atimeshat w) = 0 identically, so the result is divergence-free at every mode, which fixes the gauge that nablatimes u = w leaves free. Second, on mathbb S it is a true inverse — from mathrmiatimes(mathrmiatimeshat u) = a(acdothat u) - lvert arvert^2hat u and acdothat u = 0,\n\n(nablatimes)^-1(nablatimes) hat u = hat u quadtextfor  hat u in mathbb S\nquadcheckmark\n\nexactly, at every mode including the Nyquist planes, because the cancellation is between two copies of the same lvert arvert^2 rather than between the derivative family and the true lvert krvert^2. A non-solenoidal input has its longitudinal part annihilated rather than reported, which is the compatibility condition nablacdot w = 0 stated as a projection. Backs inverse_curl; checked by test/differentialoperators.jl against the closed-form pair u = (sin y sin z sin x), w = nablatimes u = (-cos z -cos x -cos y).\n\nThe curl is self-adjoint on the same pairing,\n\nlangle nablatimes u vrangle = langle u nablatimes vrangle quadcheckmark\n\nbecause its multiplier is mathrmi times the real antisymmetric matrix a_times (the cross-product matrix of the derivative wavenumber a, so a_times^top = -a_times): the transpose flips the antisymmetric matrix's sign and conjugating the pairing's other slot flips mathrmi's, and the two cancel. Unconditional — it needs only that one derivative-wavenumber family, Nyquist planes included. Pinned in test/adjointness.jl.\n\nThe symmetric gradient operatornamesymnabla (symmetric_gradient) is the fused symmetric peer of nablatimes (which takes the antisymmetric part of nabla u): its multiplier is the symmetric part of nabla u's multiplier mathrmia_jhat u_i,\n\nbegingathered\nhat S_ij = tfracmathrmi2 (a_jhat u_i + a_ihat u_j) \nS = operatornamesym(nabla u) quadcheckmark\nendgathered\n\nformed without materializing the full gradient tensor and sharing the derivative-wavenumber Nyquist zeroing.\n\nThe kernel-composed forms (decision 32): for a scalar Fourier symbol hat g  K to mathbb C (K the stored mode set; the kernel convolve! takes) and the operator multipliers above, the scalar commutes through both the matrix symbol and the contraction — per mode k, with hat u(k) in mathbb C^3,\n\nbegingathered\nhat g Bigl(mathrmid - fraca a^topa cdot aBigr) hat u\n  = Bigl(mathrmid - fraca a^topa cdot aBigr) (hat g hat u) in mathbb C^3 \nhat g  bigl(mathrmiacdothat ubigr) = mathrmiacdot(hat g hat u) in mathbb C\nqquadcheckmark\nendgathered\n\nso the composition \"operator then convolve\" is itself a single Fourier multiplier (the product of symbols), and helmholtz_projection!(dest, f, m, kernel, α, β) / divergence!(dest, f, m, kernel, α, β) compute alpha hat g  hat L hat u + beta  widehattextttdest in one pass. Alias safety of the one-pass projection: the kernel is a mode-local map — the values written at mode k depend only on values read at mode k, and every read of a mode precedes its writes — so destination === field computes the same function (bitwise-tested). The Nyquist split is inherited unchanged: the composed symbol is a product of per-mode scalars, so each factor keeps its own family's Nyquist value.\n\nBacks Architecture §4, §7, decisions 27, 32. Checked by test/differentialoperators.jl (analytic round-trips, the Nyquist split, nablacdot(nablatimes)=0, nablatimesnabla=0, operatornamesymnabla u=operatornamesym(nabla u), the fused-equals-sequential kernel compositions, and the bitwise aliased projection) and test/adjointness.jl (self-adjointness, exactly idempotent projection, negative-semidefinite Laplacian).","title":"Differential operators (DifferentialOperators)"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Convolution-(Convolution)","page":"Formalization","text":"Design rationale: Architecture §11.\n\nBy the convolution theorem, periodic convolution is the coefficientwise spectral product; convolve applies a scalar kernel hat g (a SpectralKernel, broadcast over the fiber) with α/β accumulate (src/Convolution.jl):\n\nbegingathered\nmathcal F(f star g) = hat fcdothat g \nwidehattextttdest = alpha hat f hat g + beta widehattextttdest quadcheckmark\nendgathered\n\nThis is exactly the product invariant 1 bans as * — it is convolution, not the (absent) pointwise spectral product. The neutral kernel builders: spectral_cutoff is the sharp low-pass hat g(k)=1 for kle k_c (else 0), idempotent (hat g^2=hat g, a projection); spectral_band its annular k_mathrmlolekle k_mathrmhi generalization (cutoff is k_mathrmlo=0); radial_kernel the builder taking any real function of k^2 (its sigma = mathrmidentity case being that coordinate itself); and assignment_window the linear/CIC mass-assignment window\n\nbegingathered\nhat W(k) = prod_a=1^3operatornamesinc^2Bigl(tfrack_a Delta x_a2Bigr) \noperatornamesinc y = tfracsin yy\nendgathered\n\nundone by dividing (deconvolution).\n\nThe fifth builder, tophat_window (decision 87), is the box average of edges l_a: the transform of the box's normalized indicator W_l(x) = prod_a l_a^-1 mathbf1biglx_a le l_a2bigr,\n\nhat W_l(k) = prod_a=1^3 operatornamesincBigl(tfrack_a l_a2Bigr)\n\nwith k_a the true wavenumber component. Three statements, each expanded against the code and tested (test/convolution.jl, \"tophat_window: the box average of a chosen width\"):\n\nThe box mean of one mode. Per axis, tfrac1lint_-l2^l2 e^mathrmi k (x - s) mathrmds = e^mathrmi k x operatornamesinc(k l2), so W_l star cos(k cdot x) = cos(k cdot x) prod_a operatornamesinc(k_a l_a2), the test's oracle on an anisotropic box with edges that are not whole cells. checkmark\nThe two limits. At l_a = L_a the argument k_a l_a2 = pi n_a makes every factor with n_a ne 0 vanish, so a box the size of the domain keeps k = 0 alone; at l_a = 0 each factor is exactly 1 (the code's ifelse at a zero argument), so the window is the identity bitwise. checkmark\nThe cloud-in-cell window is its square. assignment_window is convolve of two windows at the grid spacing, and composing separable kernels multiplies their factors, so hat W = prod_a operatornamesinc^2(k_a Delta x_a2), bitwise the former closed form. checkmark\n\nThe sixth builder, translation_kernel, is the shift theorem's multiplier: for a physical displacement h = (h_1 h_2 h_3) it is the transform of the Dirac delta displaced to h, so convolving carries the field f at position x to f(x - h). Write k_a for the true signed wavenumber of axis a, a_a for the derivative wavenumber (equal to k_a except at the Nyquist frequency of an even-length axis, where it is zeroed — invariant 5), N_a for the number of points on that axis and Delta x_a for its grid spacing. The implemented multiplier is the separable product\n\nhat g_h(k) = prod_a=1^3\n  bigl  cos(k_a h_a) - mathrmi sin(a_a h_a)  bigr\n\nThree statements, each expanded against the code and tested:\n\nA pure phase away from Nyquist. Where no component of k sits at a Nyquist frequency we have a_a = k_a, and cos is even, so each factor collapses to cos(k_a h_a) - mathrmi sin(k_a h_a) = e^-mathrmi k_a h_a, whence hat g_h(k) = e^-mathrmi k cdot h, of unit modulus. checkmark\nReality: the kernel is Hermitian. hat g_h(-k) = overlinehat g_h(k) on the discrete frequency torus, so convolve carries a real field to a real field. Each non-Nyquist factor conjugates under k_a mapsto -k_a; each Nyquist factor is the real number cos(pi h_a  Delta x_a), hence its own conjugate — which is what the statement needs, the Nyquist frequency being its own negative modulo N_a. The two candidate kernels fail here or below: the true phase e^-mathrmi kcdot h is not Hermitian at Nyquist for a fractional displacement, and the all-derivative-wavenumber kernel is Hermitian but carries the wrong value. checkmark\nWhole-cell displacements are exact. For h_a = m_a Delta x_a with m in mathbb Z^3 every factor is the exact phase — the Nyquist one reducing to cos(pi m_a) = (-1)^m_a — and the convolution is exactly the index shift, circshift by m cells. checkmark\n\nThe exponential radial kernel (exponential_convolve!, decision 33) is the symbol hat g(k) = exp(c k^2 + s hat w(k)) with c s in mathbb R and hat w a real-valued per-mode field, evaluated lazily (never materialized). Three statements, each expanded against the code and tested:\n\nReality is automatic. k^2 is real and even in k and hat w is required real and (as a stored half-spectrum of a real field) Hermitian, so hat g is real and even — hat g(-k) = hat g(k) = overlinehat g(k) — and a real field stays real for every c s; no Nyquist split arises (the exponent uses the true squared wavenumber, invariant 5's Laplacian family). checkmark\nIt is the heat semigroup's symbol. The Laplacian's symbol is -k^2, so the operator e^-cDelta has symbol exp(-c cdot (-k^2)) = exp(ck^2) — for c le 0 the periodic Gaussian smoothing at length sqrt-2c — exact on the grid because operator and exponent share the same wavenumber family. checkmark\nA scalar exponent offset is redundant. exp(ck^2 + s_0) = e^s_0 exp(ck^2) for a constant s_0, which is the accumulate weight alpha's job — hence the deliberate absence of a scalar-offset method; only the per-mode s hat w earns a signature slot. checkmark\n\nThe kernel-composed combination (combine!(dest, α, a, β, b, kernel) and the exponential two-operand form) is the distribution of a diagonal symbol over a linear combination — per mode, hat g (alpha hat a + beta hat b) = alpha hat g hat a + beta hat g hat b — computed as the left-hand side in one coefficientwise pass; with b = textttdest it is the filtered accumulate, alias-safe because the map is mode-local. checkmark\n\nKernel storage is a representation, not a meaning. A kernel is the multiplier function hat g  K to mathbb C on the stored half-spectrum K; how its values reach an operation is a storage choice, and the package carries four. Write k_a for the signed wavenumber of axis a and k^2 = sum_a k_a^2:\n\nstored (StoredKernel), hat g tabulated one coefficient per mode (O(N^3) storage) — the class materialize_kernel returns, and the wrapper that gives a field of coefficients the kernel role;\nseparable (SeparableKernel), hat g(k) = prod_a=1^3 g_a(k_a) held as its three factors g_a  mathbb R to mathbb C tabulated per axis (O(N)) — the class translation_kernel and assignment_window return, both being products over axes by construction;\nradial (RadialKernel), hat g(k) = sigma(k^2) held as the real symbol sigma  mathbb R to mathbb R itself and evaluated per mode (O(1)) — the class spectral_cutoff, spectral_band and radial_kernel return;\na product (ProductKernel), hat g = hat g_1 hat g_2 for any two kernels held unmultiplied and evaluated in one pass — what a composition gives whenever it cannot be collapsed into a single kernel.\n\nTwo statements, each expanded against the code and tested:\n\nThe classes are interchangeable. For every entry point taking a kernel, and every storage class, the computed coefficients are those of the same hat g. The associations are chosen to make this hold bitwise, not merely to rounding: the radial route sums (kappa_1^2 + kappa_2^2) + kappa_3^2 in the order the stored path's broadcast associates it, and the separable route multiplies its factors in axis order. checkmark\nComposition is closed within the two compressed families but not the stored one. Radial composed with radial is radial when both are radial in the same k^2 — that is, over canonical multiplier sets of one concrete type — canonical meaning built by the convenience constructor from the grid itself, as opposed to a caller's own set supplied through the explicit six-array form — which is the hypothesis decision 61 made a dispatch condition; sigma_1 sigma_2 is then again a function of that k^2, while a pairing that fails it keeps both operands as a ProductKernel rather than silently evaluating sigma_2 at the first set's coordinates. Separable composed with separable is separable, per axis; and radial composed with separable is a ProductKernel, since a general sigma(k^2) admits no factorization over axes (the exponential is the exception) and prod_a g_a no reduction to k^2. Stored composed with stored is deliberately not closed: multiplying the coefficients would be an O(N^3) pass building a third array, so the pair is kept as a ProductKernel too. Composition therefore never forces a materialization. checkmark\n\nThe reciprocal inv is the pointwise 1hat g, defined on every class and closed within each: separable per axis, radial in the symbol, product factorwise, and stored coefficientwise — the last with an explicit zero check, since it is the one class that can test its coefficients. It is what deconvolves: hat g^-1hat g = 1 wherever hat g is nonzero, which is why a sharp cutoff has no inverse and a window has one on its support.\n\nBacks Architecture §11, decisions 20–22, 28, 33, 50, 51, 60, 61. Checked by test/convolution.jl — the translation kernel e^-mathrmi k_a m Delta x_a Leftrightarrow circshift (a shift of m cells; an independent physical oracle), cutoff idempotence, and the physical-space intentional absence (the operatornamesinc^2 window is checked by test/rdf.jl); and for translation_kernel, circshift on a random field (one with Nyquist content, so the Nyquist factor is exercised), the exact DirectDFT evaluation at the displaced nodes for a sub-cell displacement, and the reality check that the displaced half-spectrum survives a transform round trip (the inverse transform being the Hermitian projector); and for the decision-33 pair, the stored-kernel equality oracles, the single-mode eigenvalue e^ccos x, and fused-equals-sequential for both two-operand forms; and for the storage classes, the walk over every kernel-taking entry point that requires each class to agree bitwise with the materialized product, together with the composition cases that pin which class each pairing lands in.","title":"Convolution (Convolution)"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Reductions-(Reductions)","page":"Formalization","text":"Design rationale: Architecture §12.\n\nThe L²/measure structure (src/Reductions.jl). The integral is extensive (against the base measure — grid cell volume, point counting); inner is the normalized L² product (declared above); textttl2norm = sqrtlangle ffrangle:\n\nbegingathered\nint fmathrmdmu = fracOmegaNsum_n f_n (mathrmgrid)quad sum_p f_p (mathrmpoints) \nint fmathrmdmu = mathrmmeasurecdottextttmean quadcheckmark\nendgathered\n\nintegral and mean in spectral representation (the zero mode, no transform): the unnormalized forward gives the DC coefficient hat f(0) = sum_x f_x (real for a real field), so\n\nbegingathered\ntextttmean(f) = frachat f(0)N \nint_Omega fmathrmdmu = fracOmegaN hat f(0) quadcheckmark\nendgathered\n\nBoth equal the physical reductions (since sum_x f_x = hat f(0)), read from the same zero-mode slot the constant-add writes; lpnorm/maximum have no single-coefficient form.\n\nVariance, standard deviation, and covariance (decision 49). The reductions operatornamevar/operatornamestd are scalar-only. The population second central moment is what Parseval gives directly, as the fluctuation energy carried by every mode but the mean:\n\nbeginaligned\noperatornamevar(f)bigm_textttcorrected=false = langle f frangle - langle frangle^2 \n= frac1N^2sum_textstored kne 0 alpha(k) hat f(k)^2 quadcheckmark\nendaligned\n\nwith alpha(k) the rfft half-spectrum multiplicity of mode k and the 1N^2 the normalization langlecdotcdotrangle itself carries. The default applies Bessel's correction on top,\n\noperatornamevar(f) = fracN_BN_B-1Bigl(langle f frangle - langle frangle^2Bigr)\n  quadcheckmark\n\nwith N_B the count of base points — N cells for a grid, M points for a point set (the subscript ties it to the base B of the bundle picture, rather than reusing that glyph for a scalar), var being defined on both exactly as cov is — so the identity reads from both representations, with operatornamestd = sqrtoperatornamevar. A vector section's coordinate-free second central moment is the covariance tensor R = operatornamecov(u) in operatornameSym^2(mathbb R^3),\n\nbeginaligned\nR_ij = fracN_BN_B-1Bigl(textttmean(u otimes u) - bar u otimes bar uBigr)_ij \n= frac1N_B-1sum_n (u_ni - bar u_i)(u_nj - bar u_j) quadcheckmark\nendaligned\n\nwhere N_B is again that count — N cells for a grid, M points for a point set — since cov is defined on both. The result is a symmetric FiberTensor2 whose diagonal is the per-component variance a fiber-preserving var would have returned; operatornamestd = sqrtoperatornamevar has no canonical vector form, so it is not offered. Physical-only (the outer product is nonlinear). Checked by test/reductions.jl (scalar var/std vs a two-pass reference and the equal spectral form; cov vs a per-point covariance, its symmetry, and its per-component diagonal) and GPU parity in test/gpu/suite.jl.\n\nThe mapped forms (decision 30). Write V for the fiber — the vector space over one base point, so V = mathbbR at tensor order 0, mathbbR^3 at order 1, mathbbR^3times3 at order 2 — and f_n in V for the fiber value of the section at base point n. Given a map varphi  V to U into any space U carrying the reducing operation (W on this page is the gather operator, so the generic codomain takes another letter), the mapped reductions are the bare ones applied to varphi circ f:\n\nbegingathered\ntextttmean(varphi f) = frac1Nsum_n varphi(f_n) \nint_Omega (varphi circ f)mathrmdmu = fracOmegaNsum_n varphi(f_n) (mathrmgrid) quad\nsum_p varphi(f_p) (mathrmpoints) quadcheckmark\nendgathered\n\nThe order statistics reduce the same mapped values, with a maximum or a minimum in place of the sum. Two consequences are worth stating because they are what the tests pin. Taking the identity map recovers the bare reduction exactly, textttmean(mathrmid f) = textttmean(f), including its fiber-valued result at order 1 and 2. And with varphi = lVertcdotrVert the order statistic coincides with the existing supremum norm, max_n lvert f_nrvert = lVert frVert_infty, so maximum(norm, u) and lpnorm(u, Inf) are the same number by different routes — the first without materializing varphi circ f as a section. The mapped forms exist only on fields in physical representation and point-values: varphi is nonlinear in general, so it has no coefficientwise meaning on a spectral representation (invariant 1). Tests: test/reductions.jl (the analytic diagonal-tensor invariants, the identity-map agreement, the lpnorm coincidence, an independent per-point reduction, and the spectral absence), test/allocations.jl (the family allocates zero bytes), test/gpu/suite.jl (device parity).\n\ninner in spectral representation (Parseval, no transform): the discrete Parseval identity tfrac1Nsum_x f_xcdot g_x = tfrac1N^2sum_textall khat f(k) overlinehat g(k) together with the rfft half-spectrum (each k_x0 mode stands for its conjugate partner, so it counts twice; the self-conjugate k_x=0 and even-Nyquist planes count once) gives\n\nbeginaligned\nlangle f grangle = frac1N^2 mathrmReBigl(2sum_k_xge0hat f overlinehat g \n-sum_textzero planehat f overlinehat g-sum_textNyquist planehat f overlinehat gBigr) quadcheckmark\nendaligned\n\n(the Nyquist sum only for even N_x). radial_spectrum bins this with the half-spectrum weight alpha(k) (1 on the self-conjugate planes, 2 else) over k-shells, and radial_correlation is the Wiener–Khinchin dual (the inverse transform mathcal F^-1 is to_physical, the normalized inverse):\n\nbegingathered\nP(kappa_m) = frac1N^2sum_mathrmround(kDelta k)=malpha(k)sum_c hat f_c(k)^2 \nC(r) = frac1N mathcal F^-1Bigl(textstylesum_chat f_c^2Bigr)(r)\nendgathered\n\nwith P(kappa) the shell power spectrum (the consumer's physics energy spectrum is E(kappa)=tfrac12P(kappa)), each refining inner exactly: sum_m P(kappa_m) = langle ffrangle and C(0) = langle ffrangle. checkmark The two-field forms (cross-power sum_cmathrmRe(hat f_coverlinehat g_c), cross-correlation C_fg(r)=tfrac1Nmathcal F^-1(overlinehat f hat g)(r)) refine the mixed langle fgrangle (sum P_fg=C_fg(0)=langle fgrangle), are symmetric in fg, and may be negative.\n\nweighted_inner applies a real Fourier multiplier w(k) inside the same Parseval sum,\n\nlangle f w grangle = frac1N^2sum_textstored k alpha(k) w(k)sum_c mathrmRebigl(hat f_c overlinehat g_cbigr) quadcheckmark\n\ngeneralizing inner (the wequiv1 case) — the reduction-dual of convolve. Given a tuple of weights it answers all of them in one traversal, and the identity that licenses the fusion is that each answer is the one the separate call would give:\n\ntextttweighted_inner(f (w_1dotsw_n))_j = textttweighted_inner(f w_j)\nqquad j = 1dotsn \n\nbitwise, not merely to roundoff — the coefficient reads are shared while each weight is still evaluated per mode, so no summation order changes (decision 72). For w=k^2 (the squared-wavenumber symbol, true k^2 at Nyquist) it is the Dirichlet energy langle f-Delta grangle; and since w is real it equals the naive langle wstar f wstar grangle iff w is idempotent (w^2=w, a sharp band), not for a general w. Symmetric in fg, may be negative, spectral-only. The realness of w is a hypothesis of the statement, not a convenience: with a complex multiplier the pairing is not an inner product, so the operation refuses one wherever the kernel's type settles the question (decision 62). The one class whose type cannot settle it is StoredKernel, whose storage is complex whatever multiplier it holds; there the real part is taken, as documented.\n\nlpnorm is the p-family around that L^2 pair: on a grid lVert frVert_p = bigl(tfrac1Nsum_n lvert f_nrvert^pbigr)^1p and on a point-set the M-normalized analogue, with lvert f_nrvert the fiber Euclidean magnitude; p = 2 delegates to textttl2norm = sqrtlangle ffrangle and p = infty is max_n lvert f_nrvert (src/Reductions.jl). Physical fields and point-values only. checkmark Checked by test/reductions.jl (the p = 2 delegation to l2norm and the p = infty coincidence with maximum(norm, u)).\n\nBacks Architecture §12, decisions 18/21/22/27/30/49/62. Checked by test/reductions.jl — langlesinrangle=0, langlesin^2rangle=tfrac12, the spectral integral/mean against the physical reductions (langle crangle=c, int cmathrmdx=cOmega), sum_i P(kappa_i)=langle ffrangle, C(0)=langle ffrangle against an independent binned-circshift oracle, the co-spectrum signs, and weighted_inner against langle f-Delta grangle and the idempotent-band identity.","title":"Reductions (Reductions)"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Interpolation-/-anterpolation-(Interpolation)","page":"Formalization","text":"Design rationale: Architecture §10.\n\nThe base gather W_b  mathbb R^N to mathbb R^M has entries (W_b)_pn = w_n(x_p) (the local stencil weights, src/Interpolation.jl), a partition of unity — for each point the row sums to one, sum_n (W_b)_pn = 1, i.e. W_b mathbf1_N = mathbf1_M with mathbf1_N in mathbb R^N and mathbf1_M in mathbb R^M (two distinct all-ones vectors). Both transfer maps are fiber-preserving (the trivial bundle leaves the fiber by the identity):\n\nbegingathered\nW = W_b otimes mathrmid_V  Gamma_G to Gamma_P \nW^top = W_b^top otimes mathrmid_V  Gamma_P to Gamma_G \nendgathered\n\nAdjoint, raw (counting) form. For f in Gamma_G, q in Gamma_P:\n\nbeginaligned\nsum_p (Wf)_pcdot q_p\n  = sum_p sum_n (W_b)_pn (f_ncdot q_p) \n  = sum_n f_ncdot (W^top q)_n qquadcheckmark\nendaligned\n\nAdjoint, normalized inner. Dividing the two sides by M and N:\n\nbeginaligned\nM langle Wf qrangle_mathrmpts\n  = sum_p (Wf)_pcdot q_p \n  = sum_n f_ncdot(W^topq)_n \n  = N langle f W^top qrangle_mathrmgrid qquadcheckmark\nendaligned\n\nSo the bare langle Wf qrangle_mathrmpts = langle f W^topqrangle_mathrmgrid is false unless N = M — the two sides differ by NM. This is the slip the formalization catches: it type-checks, but the expansion does not match. The clean adjunction uses the measure-adjoint iota_* = (NM) W^top:\n\nbeginaligned\nlangle f iota_* qrangle_mathrmgrid\n  = frac1Nsum_n f_ncdottfracNM (W^topq)_n \n  = frac1Msum_n f_ncdot(W^topq)_n \n  = langle Wf qrangle_mathrmpts qquadcheckmark\nendaligned\n\nConservation (sum the deposit over the grid, per fiber component):\n\nbeginaligned\nsum_n (W^top q)_n\n  = sum_nsum_p (W_b)_pn q_p \n  = sum_p Bigl(textstylesum_n (W_b)_pnBigr) q_p \n  = sum_p q_p in V qquadcheckmark\nendaligned\n\n(uses the partition-of-unity row sum sum_n (W_b)_pn = 1).\n\nBacks Architecture §10 and the Interpolation module docstring. Checked by test/interpolation_transfer.jl — the measure-weighted adjointness M langle Wf qrangle_mathrmpts = N langle f W^topqrangle_mathrmgrid, conservation (partition of unity), and polynomial reproduction.","title":"Interpolation / anterpolation (Interpolation)"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Spectral-transfer:-DirectDFT-/-NUFFT-(Interpolation)","page":"Formalization","text":"Design rationale: Architecture §10, decision 23.\n\nThe exact Fourier transfer between a field's spectral coefficients and off-grid points, and its fast windowed form — the spectral siblings of the spline W / W^top above. DirectDFT is the literal O(NM) sum (src/Interpolation.jl; o the grid origin, alpha the half-spectrum Parseval weight, 1 on the self-conjugate planes and 2 else):\n\nbegingathered\ntexttype-2 (interpolate)quad f(x_p) = frac1Nsum_textstored kalpha(k) mathrmRebigl(hat f(k) e^mathrmi kcdot(x_p-o)bigr) \ntexttype-1 (anterpolate)quad (W^topq)(k) = sum_p q_p e^-mathrmi kcdot(x_p-o) \nendgathered\n\nType-2 reproduces the band-limited field exactly (it matches to_physical at the grid nodes). Type-1's codomain is the full stored-coefficient array, not mathcal F's Hermitian-constrained image: for off-node points on an even axis the deposit is the half-spectrum of no real field, so read it as a functional on the stored modes. The adjointness below is with respect to the alpha-weighted pairing on those modes, and holds mode for mode. The pair satisfies the same measure-weighted adjoint as the splines, M langle What f qrangle_mathrmpts = N langle hat f W^topqrangle_mathrmgrid. checkmark NUFFT is its O(Nlog N + M) form — for the deposit, spread → oversampled (sigma=2) FFT → deconvolve by 1hatpsi (window psi, transform hatpsi) — exact to a tolerance varepsilon against DirectDFT on the resolved band (the rfft Nyquist is projected out), an exact adjoint pair there.\n\nBacks Architecture §10, decision 23. Checked by test/transfer_spectral.jl — interpolate == to_physical at the nodes, exact off-grid reconstruction, the adjoint relation (R = 0 1 2), and NUFFT vs DirectDFT to varepsilon.","title":"Spectral transfer: DirectDFT / NUFFT (Interpolation)"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Moving-between-grid-resolutions-(Fields,-decision-85)","page":"Formalization","text":"Design rationale: the decision log, decision 85.\n\nLet one box carry two grids, N_c points per axis and N_f ge N_c, over the same domain of length L and the same origin. Both resolve wavenumbers on the lattice 2piL times the integers, so the coarse grid's frequencies are a subset of the fine grid's, naming the same physical modes. Write T(N) for the space of fields the grid of N points represents, and S for the shared frequencies: every coarse frequency EXCEPT the Nyquist shell k_a = N_c2 of an even axis, which is excluded because the coarse grid identifies +N_c2 with -N_c2 while the fine grid stores them apart, leaving the fine pair undetermined by the coarse datum.\n\nWith that exclusion T_S is a genuine subspace of T(N_f), and the two verbs are the inclusion and its adjoint:\n\nbeginaligned\nE  T_S to T(N_f) quad (E u)(k) = u(k) text for  k in S quad 0 text otherwise\nR  T(N_f) to T_S quad (R w)(k) = w(k) text for  k in S\nendaligned\n\nBoth are stated on physical amplitudes: a stored coefficient is prod N times the amplitude of its mode (invariant 4), so the implementations carry the ratio of the two point counts, and the statements below are false for raw coefficient copying by a factor of (N_cN_f)^2.\n\nStatement 1 (retraction). R E = mathrmid_T_S, so the round trip returns the coarse field on the shared modes. Verified in test/shared_modes.jl, \"the round trip is the identity on the coarse field\".\n\nStatement 2 (adjointness, exactly). For u in T_S and w in T(N_f),\n\nlangle E u wrangle_T(N_f) = langle u R wrangle_T_S\n\nwith no measure factor — unlike the grid↔point transfer, whose W/W^top pair carries NM. The half-spectrum multiplicity alpha(k) appears on both sides at the same modes and cancels. Verified in test/shared_modes.jl, \"the two verbs are exact adjoints\", bitwise in mathbbF_64 on a cubic grid and to rounding otherwise.\n\nStatement 3 (E R is the orthogonal projector onto T_S). E R is idempotent and self-adjoint on T(N_f); it is the same operator spectral_cutoff applies through convolve when its band is S, which is why the package offers no third verb for it.\n\nStatement 4 (reality is preserved). S is symmetric under k mapsto -k, and the folded axis's stored self-conjugate plane maps onto its counterpart index for index, so E and R carry a real field's spectrum to a real field's spectrum. Verified by reality_residual in test/shared_modes.jl's analytic testset.","title":"Moving between grid resolutions (Fields, decision 85)"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Storage-families-(Fields,-Points,-Convolution)","page":"Formalization","text":"Design rationale: the decision log, decision 63.\n\nA storage family is the kind of array holding a section's values — Array on the host, or one vendor's device array. It is not part of the mathematical object: a section is a map from the base to the fiber, and which allocator holds its samples is no more intrinsic than which page of RAM they occupy. Writing mathcalS for a section and mathcalA for a family, the move is therefore stated as an identity, not a computation:\n\nbeginaligned\noperatornameadapt(mathcalA mathcalS) = mathcalS \nmathcalS(n) = mathcalS(n) quad forall n in textbase \nmathcalC(mathcalS) = mathcalC(mathcalS)\nendaligned\n\nHere mathcalC(mathcalS) is the context of a section — everything it carries besides its samples, namely the grid, the Fourier layout, the origin and the tensor order — and the element type is likewise unchanged. Only the family differs. Two consequences follow and are what the tests assert. First, the move commutes with every operation: for an operation Phi defined on both families, Phi(operatornameadapt(mathcalA mathcalS)) = operatornameadapt(mathcalA Phi(mathcalS)) up to the floating-point reassociation any backend is free to make, which is why the device suite can compare a moved workspace against the host result at all. Second, it is idempotent, and on the family a section already occupies it is the identity map on objects, operatornameadapt(mathcalA mathcalS) equiv mathcalS, sharing storage rather than copying. checkmark\n\nThe same statement covers the companions a section cannot be used without, none of which is itself a section. A FieldSpectralMultipliers and each of the four kernel classes move by the identity above with mathcalC reading their own context, so a moved kernel denotes the same multiplier, hat g_mathcalA = hat g as a function of the mode, and therefore computes the same convolution on the target family as on the source. checkmark\n\nPrecision is deliberately not of this kind and is not offered here: changing the element type changes the values, and — because Grid3D stores the domain size, spacing and origin at the field's own element type — it changes mathcalC as well, so it is a different statement rather than a weaker one.\n\nFieldTransformPlans is the one companion that is rebuilt rather than moved, because an FFT plan is not a section: it is a compiled schedule bound to one storage family. It is nonetheless determined by data it already carries — the grid, the layout and its element type — so the rebuild needs no argument, and a plan is independent of the arrays it was planned against, which is what lets the witnesses be released immediately.\n\nBacks the decision log, decision 63. Checked by test/adapt.jl — value and context preservation across three layout folds, a non-zero origin, both element types, both representations and R = 0 1 2; the identity case; the point layer (PointSet and all three point-value types); each of the four kernel classes, asserted through the convolution it computes rather than through its storage; the Canonical parameter of the multipliers; and a rebuilt plan transforming equally. Commutation with the operations is checked on device in test/gpu/suite.jl, where a moved workspace reproduces the host curl and convolve.","title":"Storage families (Fields, Points, Convolution)"},{"category":"section","location":"formalization/#Base-space-motion:-displacement-and-the-canonical-representative-(decision-73)","page":"Formalization","text":"Setting. The base is the flat torus mathbb T^3 = mathbb R^3  Lambda with lattice Lambda = L_1mathbb Z times L_2mathbb Z times L_3mathbb Z and a chosen fundamental domain D = o_1 o_1 + L_1) times o_2 o_2 + L_2) times o_3 o_3 + L_3), where L_a is the domain extent and o_a the origin along axis a (both carried by Grid3D). A PointSet stores a representative X = (x_p)_p=1^M in (mathbb R^3)^M of a configuration of M points; a VectorPointValues is a section v of the (trivialized) tangent bundle — trivialized meaning the displacement at each point is read in the single fixed Cartesian frame the flat torus supplies, so no curvilinear machinery enters — over that configuration, one vector v(p) in mathbb R^3 per point.\n\nStatement 1 (cover displacement). For real coefficients c_1 dots c_n and sections v_1 dots v_n, the operation displace! without a grid computes, per point,\n\nx_p leftarrow x_p + sum_j=1^n c_j v_j(p) \n\nevaluated left-to-right in one fused pass. Because the torus is flat and the frame global, this is the exponential map of the flat metric — which carries a point along a vector — lifted to the universal cover, the unwrapped torus, in which a coordinate keeps growing instead of folding back. No choice of connection (a rule for comparing vectors over different points) enters, and the operation is exact linear algebra per point. checkmark\n\nStatement 2 (canonical representative). The operation wrap! computes, per point and axis,\n\nx_pa leftarrow operatornamemod(x_pa - o_a L_a) + o_a in o_a o_a + L_a) \n\nthe unique representative of x_pa + L_amathbb Z in the fundamental domain — in exact arithmetic. In floating point, mod may return exactly L_a (measured: mod(-1e-20, 2π) === 2π), and that value is folded to 0 before the origin is restored, so membership in the half-open interval holds for every finite input; a non-finite coordinate maps to a non-finite coordinate (NaN fixed, pminfty mapsto textttNaN), never into D.\n\nStatement 3 (the fused torus arity). displace! with a grid computes Statement 2 applied to the result of Statement 1, in one pass, and is bitwise equal to the sequential pair: the fused form performs the same floating-point operations in the same order per element, only in registers rather than through memory. checkmark\n\nStatement 4 (deck invariance of sampling, with its limit). Interpolation is invariant under the deck group — the whole-period lattice shifts that map the unwrapped picture onto itself: for any such shift x mapsto x + k cdot L (componentwise, k in mathbb Z^3) the transfer kernels wrap their stencil indices, so the sampled value is unchanged in exact arithmetic. In floating point the two evaluations differ by roundoff of the offset computation, so the test asserts closeness, not equality; and the statement does not extend to coordinates of arbitrary magnitude, where cell-index arithmetic leaves its exact range. This bounded form is the honest version of \"positions need no wrap\".\n\nBacks Architecture §10 and the decision log, decision 73. Checked by test/displacement.jl — the raw-array oracle for Statement 1 (single- and three-term, both element types), the endpoint/non-finite/origin pins for Statement 2, the bitwise equality of Statement 3, and the tolerance form of Statement 4; GPU parity in test/gpu/suite.jl.","title":"Base-space motion: displacement and the canonical representative (decision 73)"}]
}
